  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'hard', 1, 'Use the digits 4, 7 and 2 once each to make the smallest number you can with three decimal places, like 0.123.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"0.247"'::jsonb, 'Put the smallest digit in the tenths place, the next smallest in the hundredths and the largest in the thousandths: 0.247.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which number is closest to 1?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.98"},{"id":"b","text":"1.03"},{"id":"c","text":"0.9"},{"id":"d","text":"1.1"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '0.98 is 0.02 below 1, 1.03 is 0.03 above, and 0.9 and 1.1 are both 0.1 away. So 0.98 is closest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'very_hard', 1, 'What number does the dot on the number line show?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"5.68"'::jsonb, 'From 5.6 to 5.7 is 10 hundredths, so each jump is 0.01. The dot is 8 jumps past 5.6, so it is at 5.68.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":5.6,"to":5.7,"jumps":10,"dotAt":8}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these numbers are between 0.3 and 0.31? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.305"},{"id":"b","text":"0.35"},{"id":"c","text":"0.309"},{"id":"d","text":"0.299"},{"id":"e","text":"0.301"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Write the ends with three places: 0.300 and 0.310. 0.305, 0.309 and 0.301 fit between them. 0.35 (0.350) is too big and 0.299 is too small.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'very_easy', 1, '1.5 is greater than 1.2.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Both have 1 one. 5 tenths is more than 2 tenths, so 1.5 is greater.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which decimal does the dot on the number line show?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.9"},{"id":"b","text":"9"},{"id":"c","text":"0.09"},{"id":"d","text":"0.1"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each of the 10 jumps from 0 to 1 is one tenth. The dot is 9 jumps along, so it is 0.9.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":0,"to":1,"jumps":10,"dotAt":9}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which number is the largest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3.1"},{"id":"b","text":"1.3"},{"id":"c","text":"0.3"},{"id":"d","text":"1.1"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Look at the ones first. 3.1 has 3 ones and the others have 1 or 0, so 3.1 is the largest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'easy', 1, 'Which is larger, 6.4 or 6.38?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6.4"'::jsonb, 'More digits doesn''t mean bigger. 6.4 is 6.40, and 40 hundredths is more than 38 hundredths, so 6.4 is larger.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the table. Put the parcels in order from lightest to heaviest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Parcel B"},{"id":"b","text":"Parcel C"},{"id":"c","text":"Parcel A"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Write the masses with two places: 1.25, 1.60, 1.70. So B is lightest, then C, then A.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Parcel","Mass (kg)"],"rows":[["Parcel A",1.7],["Parcel B",1.25],["Parcel C",1.6]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'easy', 1, '0.5 = ___ hundredths', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"50"'::jsonb, '5 tenths is the same as 50 hundredths, because each tenth is 10 hundredths.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'medium', 1, 'Which number is between 0.7 and 0.8?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.75"},{"id":"b","text":"0.07"},{"id":"c","text":"0.85"},{"id":"d","text":"0.078"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '0.7 is 0.70 and 0.8 is 0.80. 0.75 sits between them. 0.07 and 0.078 are less than 0.1, and 0.85 is too big.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'medium', 1, 'What number does the dot on the number line show?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"1.3"'::jsonb, 'From 0 to 2 there are 20 jumps, so each jump is 0.1. The dot is 13 jumps along, which is 1.3.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":0,"to":2,"jumps":20,"dotAt":13}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 0.4? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.40"},{"id":"b","text":"0.400"},{"id":"c","text":"0.04"},{"id":"d","text":"four tenths"},{"id":"e","text":"4.0"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Zeros on the end after the decimal don''t change the value, so 0.40 and 0.400 equal 0.4, and so does four tenths. 0.04 is 4 hundredths and 4.0 is 4 ones.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'hard', 1, 'Put these decimals in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.065"},{"id":"b","text":"0.56"},{"id":"c","text":"0.6"},{"id":"d","text":"0.605"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Write them with three places: 0.065, 0.560, 0.600, 0.605. Now compare them like whole numbers.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030331', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the table. On which day did the most rain fall?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Monday"},{"id":"b","text":"Tuesday"},{"id":"c","text":"Wednesday"},{"id":"d","text":"Thursday"}]'::jsonb, '"d"'::jsonb, 'Write them with two places: 12.50, 12.05, 12.45, 12.60. The largest is 12.60, so Thursday.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Day","Rain (mm)"],"rows":[["Monday",12.5],["Tuesday",12.05],["Wednesday",12.45],["Thursday",12.6]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030332', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'hard', 1, '3.09 is greater than 3.1, because 9 is bigger than 1.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Compare the tenths first. 3.09 has 0 tenths and 3.1 has 1 tenth, so 3.1 is greater.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030333', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the smallest number with three decimal places that is greater than 0.6?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"0.601"'::jsonb, '0.6 is 0.600. The next number with three decimal places is 1 thousandth more: 0.601.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030334', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'very_hard', 1, 'In a gymnastics event, the scores were 8.75, 8.7, 8.705 and 8.075. Which score came third?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8.7"},{"id":"b","text":"8.705"},{"id":"c","text":"8.075"},{"id":"d","text":"8.75"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Write them with three places: 8.750, 8.700, 8.705, 8.075. From highest: 8.750, 8.705, 8.700, 8.075. Third is 8.7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030335', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050303', 'very_hard', 1, 'Type the missing digit to make this true: 5.4___7 > 5.487', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"9"'::jsonb, 'The ones and tenths are the same, so compare hundredths. 8 would give 5.487, which is equal, not greater. So the digit must be 9: 5.497.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030401', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_easy', 1, 'What fraction of the bar is shaded?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3/8","three eighths"]'::jsonb, 'The bar has 8 equal parts and 3 are shaded, so 3/8 is shaded.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"bar","parts":8,"shaded":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030402', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_easy', 1, '1/2 is bigger than 1/4.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Cutting something into 2 equal parts makes bigger pieces than cutting it into 4, so 1/2 is bigger.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030403', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_easy', 1, '2/5 + 1/5 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3/5","three fifths"]'::jsonb, 'The parts are the same size (fifths), so add the tops: 2 fifths + 1 fifth = 3 fifths.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030404', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'easy', 1, 'Which fraction is the largest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/10"},{"id":"b","text":"7/10"},{"id":"c","text":"5/10"},{"id":"d","text":"1/10"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'They are all tenths, so the one with the most tenths is largest: 7/10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030405', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'easy', 1, 'Which fraction is equal to 1/2?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/6"},{"id":"b","text":"2/6"},{"id":"c","text":"3/5"},{"id":"d","text":"4/10"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '3 is half of 6, so 3/6 is one half. 4/10 would need 5 tenths to be a half.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030406', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'easy', 1, 'What fraction is shown by the dot on the number line?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2/5","two fifths"]'::jsonb, 'The line from 0 to 1 is cut into 5 equal jumps, so each jump is 1/5. The dot is 2 jumps along: 2/5.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":0,"to":1,"jumps":5,"dotAt":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030407', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'medium', 1, 'Put these fractions in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/8"},{"id":"b","text":"1/5"},{"id":"c","text":"1/3"},{"id":"d","text":"1/2"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'When the top number is 1, a bigger bottom number means smaller pieces. So 1/8 is smallest and 1/2 is largest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030408', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'medium', 1, '7/8 − 3/8 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4/8","1/2","one half"]'::jsonb, 'Both are eighths, so subtract the tops: 7 eighths − 3 eighths = 4 eighths, which is also one half.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030409', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'medium', 1, 'Which fractions are bigger than 1/2? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/4"},{"id":"b","text":"2/5"},{"id":"c","text":"5/8"},{"id":"d","text":"1/3"},{"id":"e","text":"4/6"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Half of 4 is 2, half of 8 is 4 and half of 6 is 3, so 3/4, 5/8 and 4/6 are more than half. 2/5 and 1/3 are less.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030410', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'hard', 1, 'Which is bigger, 2/3 or 3/4?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2/3"},{"id":"b","text":"3/4"},{"id":"c","text":"They are equal"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '2/3 is 1/3 away from a whole and 3/4 is only 1/4 away. 1/4 is a smaller gap, so 3/4 is bigger.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030411', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'hard', 1, '3/5 + 4/5 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7/5","1 2/5"]'::jsonb, '3 fifths + 4 fifths = 7 fifths. 5 fifths make 1 whole, so 7/5 is the same as 1 and 2/5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030412', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'hard', 1, '5/6 is closer to 1 whole than 3/4 is.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '5/6 is 1/6 short of a whole and 3/4 is 1/4 short. 1/6 is smaller than 1/4, so 5/6 is closer to 1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030413', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these fractions in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/10"},{"id":"b","text":"2/5"},{"id":"c","text":"1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"5/8"},{"id":"e","text":"3/4"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Compare each with 1/2. 3/10 and 2/5 (= 4/10) are less than half, and 3/10 is smaller. 5/8 and 3/4 (= 6/8) are more than half, and 5/8 is smaller.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030414', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_hard', 1, 'A pizza is cut into 8 equal slices. Ava ate 3/8 of the pizza and Ben ate 2/8. What fraction of the pizza is left?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3/8","three eighths"]'::jsonb, 'They ate 3/8 + 2/8 = 5/8. The whole pizza is 8/8, so 8/8 − 5/8 = 3/8 is left.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030415', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which fraction is the largest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2/3"},{"id":"b","text":"5/6"},{"id":"c","text":"7/10"},{"id":"d","text":"3/4"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Look at how far each is from 1 whole: 1/3, 1/6, 3/10 and 1/4. The smallest gap is 1/6, so 5/6 is the largest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030416', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_easy', 1, 'What fraction of the circle is shaded?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5/6"},{"id":"b","text":"1/6"},{"id":"c","text":"6/5"},{"id":"d","text":"1/5"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The circle has 6 equal parts and 5 are shaded, so 5/6 is shaded.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"circle","parts":6,"shaded":5}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030417', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_easy', 1, '1/3 is bigger than 1/2.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Cutting something into 3 equal parts makes smaller pieces than cutting it into 2, so 1/3 is smaller than 1/2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030418', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_easy', 1, '3/7 + 2/7 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5/7","five sevenths"]'::jsonb, 'The parts are the same size (sevenths), so add the tops: 3 sevenths + 2 sevenths = 5 sevenths.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030419', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'easy', 1, 'Which fraction is the smallest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5/8"},{"id":"b","text":"2/8"},{"id":"c","text":"7/8"},{"id":"d","text":"4/8"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'They are all eighths, so the one with the fewest eighths is smallest: 2/8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030420', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'easy', 1, '1/2 = ___/10', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"5"'::jsonb, 'Half of 10 is 5, so one half is the same as 5 tenths: 1/2 = 5/10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030421', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'easy', 1, 'What fraction is shown by the dot on the number line?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5/8","five eighths"]'::jsonb, 'The line from 0 to 1 is cut into 8 equal jumps, so each jump is 1/8. The dot is 5 jumps along: 5/8.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":0,"to":1,"jumps":8,"dotAt":5}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030422', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'medium', 1, 'Put these fractions in order from largest to smallest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/2"},{"id":"b","text":"1/4"},{"id":"c","text":"1/6"},{"id":"d","text":"1/10"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'When the top number is 1, a smaller bottom number means bigger pieces. So 1/2 is largest and 1/10 is smallest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030423', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'medium', 1, '9/10 − 4/10 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5/10","1/2","one half","five tenths"]'::jsonb, 'Both are tenths, so subtract the tops: 9 tenths − 4 tenths = 5 tenths, which is also one half.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030424', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'medium', 1, 'Which fractions are smaller than 1/2? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/8"},{"id":"b","text":"4/6"},{"id":"c","text":"2/10"},{"id":"d","text":"5/8"},{"id":"e","text":"2/5"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Half of 8 is 4, half of 10 is 5 and half of 5 is 2 and a half, so 3/8, 2/10 and 2/5 are less than half. 4/6 and 5/8 are more.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030425', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'hard', 1, 'Which is bigger, 4/5 or 7/10?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4/5"},{"id":"b","text":"7/10"},{"id":"c","text":"They are equal"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Change fifths to tenths: 4/5 is the same as 8/10. 8/10 is more than 7/10, so 4/5 is bigger.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030426', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'hard', 1, '5/6 + 4/6 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9/6","1 3/6","1 1/2","3/2"]'::jsonb, '5 sixths + 4 sixths = 9 sixths. 6 sixths make 1 whole, so 9/6 is the same as 1 and 3/6, or 1 and a half.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030427', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'hard', 1, '2/3 is the same as 4/6.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Cut each third in half and you get sixths. 2 thirds become 4 sixths, so 2/3 = 4/6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030428', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these fractions in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/4"},{"id":"b","text":"3/8"},{"id":"c","text":"1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"7/10"},{"id":"e","text":"4/5"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, '1/4 is 2/8, which is less than 3/8, and both are less than half. 7/10 and 4/5 (= 8/10) are more than half, and 7/10 is smaller.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030429', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_hard', 1, 'A cake is cut into 12 equal pieces. Jack eats 5/12 of the cake and Mia eats 4/12. What fraction of the cake is left?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3/12","1/4","one quarter","a quarter"]'::jsonb, 'They ate 5/12 + 4/12 = 9/12. The whole cake is 12/12, so 12/12 − 9/12 = 3/12 is left, which is also 1/4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030430', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050304', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which fraction is closest to 1 whole?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7/8"},{"id":"b","text":"9/10"},{"id":"c","text":"4/5"},{"id":"d","text":"2/3"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Look at how far each is from 1 whole: 1/8, 1/10, 1/5 and 1/3. The smallest gap is 1/10, so 9/10 is closest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030501', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the dots. How many dots are in 1/2 of them?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"10"'::jsonb, 'There are 2 rows of 10, so 20 dots. Half of 20 is 10, which is one row.', '{"kind":"array","rows":2,"columns":10}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030502', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is 1/10 of 50?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"5"'::jsonb, 'To find one tenth, divide by 10: 50 ÷ 10 = 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030503', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_easy', 1, 'To find 1/4 of a number, you can halve it and then halve it again.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Half of a half is a quarter. For example, half of 20 is 10, and half of 10 is 5, which is 1/4 of 20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030504', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'easy', 1, 'What is 1/4 of 24?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, 'Divide by 4: 24 ÷ 4 = 6. Or halve 24 to get 12, then halve again to get 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030505', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'easy', 1, 'What is 1/5 of 35?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5"},{"id":"b","text":"7"},{"id":"c","text":"30"},{"id":"d","text":"175"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'To find one fifth, divide by 5: 35 ÷ 5 = 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030506', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the ribbon on the ruler. Ella cuts off 1/2 of it. How long is the piece she cuts off?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","5 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The ribbon is 10 cm long. Half of 10 cm is 5 cm.', '{"kind":"ruler","cm":12,"length":10,"object":"ribbon"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030507', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'medium', 1, 'What is 1/4 of 1 kilogram, in grams?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["250","250 g"]'::jsonb, '1 kg = 1000 g. 1000 ÷ 4 = 250, so 1/4 of a kilogram is 250 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030508', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the water in the jug. Kai pours out 1/5 of it. How many millilitres does he pour out?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["100","100 mL"]'::jsonb, 'The jug has 500 mL of water. 1/5 of 500 is 500 ÷ 5 = 100 mL.', '{"kind":"jug","max":1000,"value":500,"step":100,"unit":"mL"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030509', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'medium', 1, 'What is 1/10 of $30?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$3"},{"id":"b","text":"$10"},{"id":"c","text":"$0.30"},{"id":"d","text":"$300"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Divide by 10: $30 ÷ 10 = $3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030510', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'hard', 1, 'What is 1/4 of 2 hours, in minutes?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30","30 minutes"]'::jsonb, '2 hours = 120 minutes. 120 ÷ 4 = 30 minutes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030511', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 6? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/2 of 12"},{"id":"b","text":"1/4 of 24"},{"id":"c","text":"1/5 of 25"},{"id":"d","text":"1/10 of 60"},{"id":"e","text":"1/4 of 20"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '12 ÷ 2 = 6, 24 ÷ 4 = 6 and 60 ÷ 10 = 6. But 25 ÷ 5 = 5 and 20 ÷ 4 = 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030512', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'hard', 1, 'What is 1/5 of 1 metre, in centimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["20","20 cm"]'::jsonb, '1 m = 100 cm. 100 ÷ 5 = 20, so 1/5 of a metre is 20 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030513', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_hard', 1, 'In a class of 30 students, 1/2 play netball, 1/5 play cricket and the rest go swimming. How many go swimming?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"9"'::jsonb, '1/2 of 30 is 15 and 1/5 of 30 is 6. 15 + 6 = 21 play a sport, so 30 − 21 = 9 go swimming.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030514', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which is more: 1/4 of 80 or 1/5 of 90?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/4 of 80"},{"id":"b","text":"1/5 of 90"},{"id":"c","text":"They are equal"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1/4 of 80 is 80 ÷ 4 = 20. 1/5 of 90 is 90 ÷ 5 = 18. 20 is more.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030515', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_hard', 1, '1/10 of a number is 7. What is the number?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"70"'::jsonb, 'If one tenth is 7, then all ten tenths are 10 × 7 = 70.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030516', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the dots. How many dots are in 1/4 of them?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"5"'::jsonb, 'There are 4 rows of 5, so 20 dots. One quarter is one of the 4 rows, which is 5 dots (20 ÷ 4 = 5).', '{"kind":"array","rows":4,"columns":5}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030517', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is 1/2 of 90?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"45"},{"id":"b","text":"40"},{"id":"c","text":"180"},{"id":"d","text":"50"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'To find one half, divide by 2: 90 ÷ 2 = 45.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030518', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_easy', 1, 'To find 1/10 of a number, you divide it by 10.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'One tenth means 1 of 10 equal parts, so you share the number into 10 parts by dividing by 10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030519', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'easy', 1, 'What is 1/5 of 40?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"8"'::jsonb, 'To find one fifth, divide by 5: 40 ÷ 5 = 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030520', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'easy', 1, 'What is 1/4 of 36?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"9"},{"id":"b","text":"12"},{"id":"c","text":"6"},{"id":"d","text":"32"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Divide by 4: 36 ÷ 4 = 9. Or halve 36 to get 18, then halve again to get 9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030521', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the ribbon on the ruler. Ben cuts it into 4 equal pieces. How long is each piece?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3","3 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The ribbon is 12 cm long. Each piece is 1/4 of it: 12 ÷ 4 = 3 cm.', '{"kind":"ruler","cm":15,"length":12,"object":"ribbon"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030522', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'medium', 1, 'What is 1/5 of 1 kilogram, in grams?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["200","200 g"]'::jsonb, '1 kg = 1000 g. 1000 ÷ 5 = 200, so 1/5 of a kilogram is 200 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030523', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the milk in the jug. Ava uses 1/4 of it in a smoothie. How many millilitres does she use?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["200","200 mL"]'::jsonb, 'The jug has 800 mL of milk. 1/4 of 800 is 800 ÷ 4 = 200 mL.', '{"kind":"jug","max":1000,"value":800,"step":100,"unit":"mL"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030524', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'medium', 1, 'What is 1/10 of 1 kilometre, in metres?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"100 m"},{"id":"b","text":"10 m"},{"id":"c","text":"1000 m"},{"id":"d","text":"1 m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1 km = 1000 m. 1000 ÷ 10 = 100, so 1/10 of a kilometre is 100 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030525', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'hard', 1, 'What is 1/5 of an hour, in minutes?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12 minutes"]'::jsonb, '1 hour = 60 minutes. 60 ÷ 5 = 12 minutes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030526', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 8? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/2 of 16"},{"id":"b","text":"1/4 of 40"},{"id":"c","text":"1/5 of 40"},{"id":"d","text":"1/10 of 80"},{"id":"e","text":"1/5 of 45"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, '16 ÷ 2 = 8, 40 ÷ 5 = 8 and 80 ÷ 10 = 8. But 40 ÷ 4 = 10 and 45 ÷ 5 = 9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030527', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'hard', 1, 'What is 1/4 of $10?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2.50","$2.50","2.5","$2.5"]'::jsonb, 'Halve $10 to get $5, then halve again to get $2.50.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030528', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_hard', 1, 'At a sausage sizzle, 60 sausages were cooked. 1/2 were sold in the first hour and 1/5 in the second hour. How many sausages were left?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"18"'::jsonb, '1/2 of 60 is 30 and 1/5 of 60 is 12. 30 + 12 = 42 were sold, so 60 − 42 = 18 were left.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030529', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which is more: 1/5 of 60 or 1/10 of 130?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/5 of 60"},{"id":"b","text":"1/10 of 130"},{"id":"c","text":"They are equal"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '1/5 of 60 is 60 ÷ 5 = 12. 1/10 of 130 is 130 ÷ 10 = 13. 13 is more.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030530', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050305', 'very_hard', 1, '1/4 of a number is 9. What is the number?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"36"'::jsonb, 'If one quarter is 9, then all four quarters are 4 × 9 = 36.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030601', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_easy', 1, 'What does 50% of something mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"half of it"},{"id":"b","text":"a quarter of it"},{"id":"c","text":"a tenth of it"},{"id":"d","text":"all of it"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Per cent means out of 100. 50 out of 100 is one half.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030602', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_easy', 1, 'What percentage of the bar is shaded?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30%","30"]'::jsonb, '3 out of 10 parts are shaded. 3 out of 10 is the same as 30 out of 100, which is 30%.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"bar","parts":10,"shaded":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030603', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_easy', 1, '100% of something means all of it.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '100% means 100 out of 100, which is the whole thing.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030604', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'easy', 1, 'Write 25% as a fraction in its simplest form.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/4","one quarter","a quarter"]'::jsonb, '25% is 25 out of 100. Four lots of 25 make 100, so 25% is one quarter, 1/4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030605', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'easy', 1, 'Which decimal is equal to 75%?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.75"},{"id":"b","text":"7.5"},{"id":"c","text":"0.075"},{"id":"d","text":"75"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '75% is 75 hundredths, which is written 0.75.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030606', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are equal to one half? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"50%"},{"id":"b","text":"0.5"},{"id":"c","text":"5%"},{"id":"d","text":"2/4"},{"id":"e","text":"0.05"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '50%, 0.5 and 2/4 are all one half. 5% and 0.05 are only 5 out of 100.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030607', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'medium', 1, 'What is 50% of 64?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"32"'::jsonb, '50% is one half. Half of 64 is 32.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030608', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'medium', 1, 'What is 10% of 90?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"9"'::jsonb, '10% is one tenth. Divide by 10: 90 ÷ 10 = 9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030609', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'medium', 1, 'What percentage of the circle is shaded?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"25%"},{"id":"b","text":"4%"},{"id":"c","text":"40%"},{"id":"d","text":"14%"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1 out of 4 equal parts is shaded. One quarter is 25 out of 100, which is 25%.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"circle","parts":4,"shaded":1}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030610', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'hard', 1, 'What is 25% of 48?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"12"'::jsonb, '25% is one quarter. Halve 48 to get 24, then halve again to get 12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030611', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'hard', 1, 'Put these in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"10%"},{"id":"b","text":"0.2"},{"id":"c","text":"1/4"},{"id":"d","text":"0.6"},{"id":"e","text":"3/4"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to decimals: 10% = 0.1, 0.2, 1/4 = 0.25, 0.6 and 3/4 = 0.75.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030612', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'hard', 1, 'A $40 T-shirt has 10% off. How much money do you save?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$4"},{"id":"b","text":"$10"},{"id":"c","text":"$36"},{"id":"d","text":"$0.40"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '10% is one tenth. $40 ÷ 10 = $4 saved. ($36 is the new price, not the saving.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030613', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_hard', 1, 'There are 28 students in 5K. 75% of them walk to school. How many students walk?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"21"'::jsonb, '25% of 28 is 28 ÷ 4 = 7. 75% is three lots of 25%, so 3 × 7 = 21 students.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030614', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_hard', 1, 'A $60 board game is 25% off. What is the sale price?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$15"},{"id":"b","text":"$45"},{"id":"c","text":"$35"},{"id":"d","text":"$50"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '25% of $60 is $60 ÷ 4 = $15 off. The sale price is $60 − $15 = $45.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030615', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"10% of 50 is 5"},{"id":"b","text":"25% of 20 is 4"},{"id":"c","text":"50% of 30 is 15"},{"id":"d","text":"75% of 8 is 6"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, '50 ÷ 10 = 5, half of 30 is 15, and a quarter of 8 is 2, so 75% is 6. But 25% of 20 is 20 ÷ 4 = 5, not 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030616', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_easy', 1, 'What does per cent mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"out of 100"},{"id":"b","text":"out of 10"},{"id":"c","text":"out of 1000"},{"id":"d","text":"times 100"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Cent means 100, like 100 cents in a dollar. Per cent means out of 100.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030617', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_easy', 1, 'What percentage of the bar is shaded?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"70%"},{"id":"b","text":"7%"},{"id":"c","text":"30%"},{"id":"d","text":"17%"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '7 out of 10 parts are shaded. 7 out of 10 is the same as 70 out of 100, which is 70%.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"bar","parts":10,"shaded":7}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030618', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_easy', 1, '0% of something means none of it.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '0% means 0 out of 100, which is nothing at all.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030619', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'easy', 1, 'Write 10% as a fraction in its simplest form.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/10","one tenth","a tenth"]'::jsonb, '10% is 10 out of 100. Ten lots of 10 make 100, so 10% is one tenth, 1/10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030620', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'easy', 1, 'Which percentage is equal to 0.25?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"25%"},{"id":"b","text":"2.5%"},{"id":"c","text":"0.25%"},{"id":"d","text":"250%"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '0.25 is 25 hundredths, which is 25 out of 100, or 25%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030621', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 3/4? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"75%"},{"id":"b","text":"0.75"},{"id":"c","text":"34%"},{"id":"d","text":"0.34"},{"id":"e","text":"6/8"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, '3/4 is 75 out of 100, so it is 75% and 0.75. 6/8 is also 3/4 because both numbers are doubled. 34% and 0.34 just use the digits 3 and 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030622', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'medium', 1, 'What is 10% of 250?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"25"'::jsonb, '10% is one tenth. Divide by 10: 250 ÷ 10 = 25.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030623', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'medium', 1, 'What is 50% of $18?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$9"},{"id":"b","text":"$36"},{"id":"c","text":"$50"},{"id":"d","text":"$1.80"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '50% is one half. Half of $18 is $9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030624', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'medium', 1, 'What percentage of the circle is shaded?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["75%","75"]'::jsonb, '3 out of 4 equal parts are shaded. Each quarter is 25%, so 3 quarters is 75%.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"circle","parts":4,"shaded":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030625', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'hard', 1, 'What is 25% of 64?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"16"'::jsonb, '25% is one quarter. Halve 64 to get 32, then halve again to get 16.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030626', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'hard', 1, 'Put these in order from largest to smallest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"100%"},{"id":"b","text":"0.8"},{"id":"c","text":"3/4"},{"id":"d","text":"50%"},{"id":"e","text":"0.1"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to decimals: 100% = 1, 0.8, 3/4 = 0.75, 50% = 0.5 and 0.1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030627', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'hard', 1, 'A $120 jacket has 10% off. What is the sale price?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$108"},{"id":"b","text":"$12"},{"id":"c","text":"$110"},{"id":"d","text":"$118"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '10% of $120 is $120 ÷ 10 = $12 off. The sale price is $120 − $12 = $108. ($12 is the saving, not the price.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030628', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_hard', 1, 'There are 32 students in 5W. 25% of them have a pet dog. How many students do NOT have a pet dog?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"24"'::jsonb, '25% of 32 is 32 ÷ 4 = 8 students with a dog. 32 − 8 = 24 students without one.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030629', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which is more: 10% of 300 or 50% of 50?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"10% of 300"},{"id":"b","text":"50% of 50"},{"id":"c","text":"They are equal"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '10% of 300 is 300 ÷ 10 = 30. 50% of 50 is half of 50, which is 25. 30 is more.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005030630', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050306', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"50% of 90 is 45"},{"id":"b","text":"25% of 40 is 10"},{"id":"c","text":"10% of 70 is 0.7"},{"id":"d","text":"75% of 20 is 15"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Half of 90 is 45, a quarter of 40 is 10, and a quarter of 20 is 5, so 75% is 15. But 10% of 70 is 70 ÷ 10 = 7, not 0.7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which unit is best for measuring the distance from Sydney to Newcastle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"millimetres"},{"id":"b","text":"centimetres"},{"id":"c","text":"metres"},{"id":"d","text":"kilometres"}]'::jsonb, '"d"'::jsonb, 'Sydney to Newcastle is a long way, more than 100 km, so kilometres are the best unit.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many centimetres long is the ribbon?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9","9 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The ribbon starts at 0 and ends at 9, so it is 9 cm long.', '{"kind":"ruler","cm":12,"length":9,"object":"ribbon"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_easy', 1, '1 kilometre is the same as 1000 metres.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Kilo means one thousand, so 1 kilometre is 1000 metres.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'easy', 1, '3 km = ___ m', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"3000"'::jsonb, 'Each kilometre is 1000 m, so 3 km is 3 × 1000 = 3000 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'easy', 1, 'What is the perimeter of this rectangle in centimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["26","26 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Perimeter is the distance all the way around: 8 + 5 + 8 + 5 = 26 cm.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":8,"height":5,"top":"8 cm","side":"5 cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'easy', 1, 'Which device is best for measuring the length of the school oval?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a 30 cm ruler"},{"id":"b","text":"a trundle wheel"},{"id":"c","text":"a kitchen scale"},{"id":"d","text":"a measuring jug"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A trundle wheel clicks every metre as you walk, so it is good for long distances like an oval. A ruler is too short.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'medium', 1, 'How many millimetres long is the pencil?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["125","125 mm"]'::jsonb, 'The pencil ends halfway between 12 and 13, so it is 12.5 cm. Each centimetre is 10 mm, so 12.5 cm is 125 mm.', '{"kind":"ruler","cm":15,"length":12.5,"object":"pencil"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'medium', 1, 'Put these distances in order from shortest to longest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"950 m"},{"id":"b","text":"1.2 km"},{"id":"c","text":"1500 m"},{"id":"d","text":"2 km"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to metres: 950 m, 1.2 km = 1200 m, 1500 m, 2 km = 2000 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'medium', 1, 'What is the perimeter of this triangle in centimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["21","21 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Add the three sides: 8 + 7 + 6 = 21 cm.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[3,5]],"sideLabels":["8 cm","7 cm","6 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are the same as 2.5 km? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2500 m"},{"id":"b","text":"2 km 500 m"},{"id":"c","text":"250 m"},{"id":"d","text":"2 km 50 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '0.5 km is half of 1000 m, which is 500 m. So 2.5 km is 2 km 500 m, or 2500 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'hard', 1, 'A rectangular paddock is 120 m long and 75 m wide. How many metres of fencing are needed to go all the way around it?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["390","390 m"]'::jsonb, 'Add one length and one width: 120 + 75 = 195 m. Double it for the other two sides: 195 × 2 = 390 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'hard', 1, 'Which length is the same as 4.6 m?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"46 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"460 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"4600 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"406 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '4 m is 400 cm and 0.6 m is 60 cm, so 4.6 m is 460 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Two sides of this garden bed are not marked. What is its perimeter in metres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["22","22 m"]'::jsonb, 'The unmarked flat side is 6 − 3 = 3 m. The unmarked upright side is 5 − 2 = 3 m. Perimeter: 6 + 2 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 5 = 22 m.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[6,2],[3,2],[3,5],[0,5]],"sideLabels":["6 m","2 m","","","3 m","5 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_hard', 1, 'A square has the same perimeter as a rectangle that is 10 cm long and 6 cm wide. How long is each side of the square, in centimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","8 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The rectangle''s perimeter is 10 + 6 + 10 + 6 = 32 cm. A square has 4 equal sides, so each is 32 ÷ 4 = 8 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Ella walks 750 m to school and 750 m home again each day, Monday to Friday. How many kilometres does she walk in the school week?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7.5","7.5 km"]'::jsonb, 'Each day she walks 750 + 750 = 1500 m. In 5 days that is 1500 × 5 = 7500 m, which is 7.5 km.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which unit is best for measuring the length of an ant?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"millimetres"},{"id":"b","text":"metres"},{"id":"c","text":"kilometres"},{"id":"d","text":"litres"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'An ant is tiny, only a few millimetres long, so millimetres are the best unit. Litres measure capacity, not length.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many centimetres long is the pencil?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","7 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The pencil starts at 0 and ends at 7, so it is 7 cm long.', '{"kind":"ruler","cm":10,"length":7,"object":"pencil"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_easy', 1, '1 centimetre is the same as 10 millimetres.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Each centimetre on a ruler is split into 10 small marks, which are millimetres.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'easy', 1, '5 m = ___ cm', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"500"'::jsonb, 'Each metre is 100 cm, so 5 m is 5 × 100 = 500 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'easy', 1, 'What is the perimeter of this rectangle in metres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["24","24 m"]'::jsonb, 'Perimeter is the distance all the way around: 9 + 3 + 9 + 3 = 24 m.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":9,"height":3,"top":"9 m","side":"3 m"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'easy', 1, 'Which is the best estimate of the height of a classroom door?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2 m"},{"id":"b","text":"20 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"20 m"},{"id":"d","text":"2 km"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A door is a bit taller than an adult, about 2 m. 20 cm is shorter than a ruler and 20 m is taller than a house.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'medium', 1, 'How many millimetres long is the ribbon?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"65 mm"},{"id":"b","text":"6.5 mm"},{"id":"c","text":"650 mm"},{"id":"d","text":"56 mm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The ribbon ends halfway between 6 and 7, so it is 6.5 cm. Each centimetre is 10 mm, so 6.5 cm is 65 mm.', '{"kind":"ruler","cm":10,"length":6.5,"object":"ribbon"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'medium', 1, 'Put these lengths in order from shortest to longest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"45 mm"},{"id":"b","text":"6 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"0.5 m"},{"id":"d","text":"75 cm"},{"id":"e","text":"1 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to centimetres: 45 mm = 4.5 cm, 6 cm, 0.5 m = 50 cm, 75 cm, 1 m = 100 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'medium', 1, 'What is the perimeter of this shape in centimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["24","24 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Add all four sides: 10 + 5 + 4 + 5 = 24 cm.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[7,4],[3,4]],"sideLabels":["10 cm","5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are longer than 1.5 km? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"1600 m"},{"id":"b","text":"1 km 450 m"},{"id":"c","text":"1.05 km"},{"id":"d","text":"2000 m"},{"id":"e","text":"1499 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","d"]'::jsonb, '1.5 km is 1500 m. 1600 m and 2000 m are longer. 1 km 450 m is 1450 m, 1.05 km is 1050 m and 1499 m is just short.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'hard', 1, 'A rectangular swimming pool is 25 m long and 12 m wide. Ella walks once all the way around its edge. How far does she walk?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["74","74 m"]'::jsonb, 'One length and one width make 25 + 12 = 37 m. Going all the way around is double that: 37 × 2 = 74 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'hard', 1, 'Which length is the same as 3.05 m?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"305 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"350 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"3050 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"35 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '3 m is 300 cm and 0.05 m is 5 cm, so 3.05 m is 305 cm. (350 cm would be 3.5 m.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Two sides of this vegetable patch are not marked. What is its perimeter in metres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["26","26 m"]'::jsonb, 'The unmarked flat side is 7 − 4 = 3 m. The unmarked upright side is 6 − 3 = 3 m. Perimeter: 7 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 4 + 6 = 26 m.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[7,0],[7,3],[4,3],[4,6],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["7 m","3 m","","","4 m","6 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_hard', 1, 'A square has a perimeter of 36 cm. A rectangle has the same perimeter and is 12 cm long. How wide is the rectangle, in centimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","6 cm"]'::jsonb, 'A length and a width make half the perimeter: 36 ÷ 2 = 18 cm. The length is 12 cm, so the width is 18 − 12 = 6 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Jai runs 4 laps of a 400 m track every day for 5 days. How many kilometres does he run altogether?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","8 km"]'::jsonb, 'Each day he runs 4 × 400 = 1600 m. In 5 days that is 1600 × 5 = 8000 m, which is 8 km.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the shaded rectangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12 cm²","12 cm2","12 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'There are 3 rows of 4 shaded squares. 3 × 4 = 12, so the area is 12 cm².', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":4,"cells":[[0,0],[1,0],[2,0],[3,0],[0,1],[1,1],[2,1],[3,1],[0,2],[1,2],[2,2],[3,2]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which unit would you use to measure the area of a classroom floor?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"square centimetres"},{"id":"b","text":"square metres"},{"id":"c","text":"metres"},{"id":"d","text":"kilograms"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A classroom floor is big, so square metres (m²) are best. Metres measure length, not area.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Area measures how much flat surface a shape covers.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Area is the amount of surface inside a shape, like how much carpet covers a floor.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'easy', 1, 'What is the area of this rectangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["28","28 cm²","28 cm2","28 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Area of a rectangle = length × width = 7 × 4 = 28 cm².', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":7,"height":4,"top":"7 cm","side":"4 cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'easy', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the shaded shape?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"10 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"12 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"8 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"14 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Count the shaded squares: 4 in the bottom row, 4 in the next row and 2 on top. 4 + 4 + 2 = 10 cm².', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":5,"cells":[[0,0],[1,0],[2,0],[3,0],[0,1],[1,1],[2,1],[3,1],[0,2],[1,2]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'easy', 1, 'Area of a rectangle = length × ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["width","breadth"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply the length by the width to find how many squares cover a rectangle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'medium', 1, 'A rectangular rug is 3 m long and 2 m wide. What is its area in square metres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","6 m²","6 m2","6 square metres"]'::jsonb, 'Area = length × width = 3 × 2 = 6 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'medium', 1, 'What is the area of this square garden?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"12 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"24 m²"},{"id":"c","text":"36 m²"},{"id":"d","text":"36 m"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'Area = 6 × 6 = 36 m². 24 is the perimeter, not the area, and area is in square metres, not metres.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":6,"height":6,"top":"6 m","side":"6 m"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'medium', 1, 'Put these shapes in order from smallest to largest area.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"a 2 cm by 3 cm rectangle"},{"id":"b","text":"a 4 cm by 2 cm rectangle"},{"id":"c","text":"a 3 cm by 3 cm square"},{"id":"d","text":"a 5 cm by 2 cm rectangle"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Work out each area: 2 × 3 = 6, 4 × 2 = 8, 3 × 3 = 9 and 5 × 2 = 10 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'hard', 1, 'Which rectangles have an area of 24 cm²? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"6 cm by 4 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"8 cm by 3 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"12 cm by 2 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"10 cm by 4 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '6 × 4, 8 × 3 and 12 × 2 all make 24. But 10 × 4 = 40.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'hard', 1, 'This rectangle has an area of 32 m². Its missing side is ___ m long.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["4","4 m"]'::jsonb, 'Length × width = area, so 8 × ? = 32. Since 8 × 4 = 32, the missing side is 4 m.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":8,"height":4,"top":"8 m","side":"? m"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'hard', 1, 'A 4 m by 4 m square has a larger area than a 6 m by 2 m rectangle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The square is 4 × 4 = 16 m² and the rectangle is 6 × 2 = 12 m². Their perimeters are both 16 m, but the areas are different.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the area of this L-shaped deck?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["33","33 m²","33 m2","33 square metres"]'::jsonb, 'Split it into two rectangles. The bottom one is 8 × 3 = 24 m². The top one is 3 × 3 = 9 m². 24 + 9 = 33 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[8,3],[3,3],[3,6],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["8 m","3 m","5 m","3 m","3 m","6 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Jack''s lawn is 12 m long and 8 m wide. In the middle is a vegetable patch 3 m long and 2 m wide. How many square metres of grass are there?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["90","90 m²","90 m2","90 square metres"]'::jsonb, 'The whole lawn is 12 × 8 = 96 m². The patch is 3 × 2 = 6 m². Grass: 96 − 6 = 90 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangle has a perimeter of 20 cm. Which one has the biggest area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"9 cm by 1 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"8 cm by 2 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"6 cm by 4 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"5 cm by 5 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"d"'::jsonb, 'All have a perimeter of 20 cm. Areas: 9, 16, 24 and 25 cm². The square, 5 cm by 5 cm, has the biggest area.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the shaded rectangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["10","10 cm²","10 cm2","10 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'There are 2 rows of 5 shaded squares. 2 × 5 = 10, so the area is 10 cm².', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":4,"cells":[[0,0],[1,0],[2,0],[3,0],[4,0],[0,1],[1,1],[2,1],[3,1],[4,1]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which unit would you use to measure the area of a postage stamp?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"square centimetres"},{"id":"b","text":"square metres"},{"id":"c","text":"square kilometres"},{"id":"d","text":"centimetres"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A stamp is small, so square centimetres (cm²) are best. Centimetres measure length, not area.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_easy', 1, '1 m² is the area of a square with sides 1 metre long.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A square metre is exactly the space covered by a square 1 m long and 1 m wide.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'easy', 1, 'What is the area of this rectangle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"40 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"26 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"13 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"40 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area = length × width = 8 × 5 = 40 cm². 26 is the perimeter, and area is in square centimetres, not centimetres.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":8,"height":5,"top":"8 cm","side":"5 cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'easy', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the shaded shape?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9","9 cm²","9 cm2","9 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Count the shaded squares: 5 in the bottom row, then 2 and 2 above. 5 + 2 + 2 = 9 cm².', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":5,"cells":[[0,0],[1,0],[2,0],[3,0],[4,0],[0,1],[1,1],[0,2],[1,2]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'easy', 1, 'A rectangle 5 cm long and 3 cm wide has an area of ___ cm².', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["15","15 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'Area = length × width = 5 × 3 = 15 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'medium', 1, 'A rectangular school garden bed is 4 m long and 3 m wide. What is its area in square metres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12 m²","12 m2","12 square metres"]'::jsonb, 'Area = length × width = 4 × 3 = 12 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'medium', 1, 'What is the area of this square tile?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["49","49 cm²","49 cm2","49 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Area = 7 × 7 = 49 cm². (28 cm would be the perimeter, not the area.)', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":7,"height":7,"top":"7 cm","side":"7 cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'medium', 1, 'Put these rectangles in order from largest to smallest area.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"6 m by 5 m"},{"id":"b","text":"7 m by 4 m"},{"id":"c","text":"9 m by 3 m"},{"id":"d","text":"5 m by 5 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Work out each area: 6 × 5 = 30, 7 × 4 = 28, 9 × 3 = 27 and 5 × 5 = 25 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'hard', 1, 'Which rectangles have an area of 36 m²? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"9 m by 4 m"},{"id":"b","text":"6 m by 6 m"},{"id":"c","text":"12 m by 3 m"},{"id":"d","text":"10 m by 6 m"},{"id":"e","text":"8 m by 5 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '9 × 4, 6 × 6 and 12 × 3 all make 36. But 10 × 6 = 60 and 8 × 5 = 40.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'hard', 1, 'This rectangle has an area of 45 cm². Its missing side is ___ cm long.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["9","9 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Length × width = area, so ? × 5 = 45. Since 9 × 5 = 45, the missing side is 9 cm.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":9,"height":5,"top":"? cm","side":"5 cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'hard', 1, 'Two rectangles with the same perimeter always have the same area.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'A 5 cm by 1 cm rectangle and a 3 cm by 3 cm square both have a perimeter of 12 cm, but their areas are 5 cm² and 9 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the area of this L-shape?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["22","22 cm²","22 cm2","22 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Split it into two rectangles. The bottom one is 6 × 2 = 12 cm². The upright one above it is 2 × 5 = 10 cm². 12 + 10 = 22 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[6,2],[2,2],[2,7],[0,7]],"sideLabels":["6 cm","2 cm","4 cm","5 cm","2 cm","7 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bedroom floor is 6 m long and 5 m wide. Carpet costs $20 for each square metre. How much will it cost to carpet the floor?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["600","$600"]'::jsonb, 'The floor is 6 × 5 = 30 m². Each square metre costs $20, so 30 × $20 = $600.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Each of these rectangles has an area of 24 cm². Which one has the smallest perimeter?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4 cm by 6 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"3 cm by 8 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"2 cm by 12 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"1 cm by 24 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Perimeters: 4 + 6 + 4 + 6 = 20, 3 + 8 + 3 + 8 = 22, 2 + 12 + 2 + 12 = 28 and 1 + 24 + 1 + 24 = 50 cm. The closest to a square, 4 cm by 6 cm, has the smallest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_easy', 1, 'Cutting a rectangle along a diagonal makes two triangles that are the same size.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A diagonal cuts a rectangle exactly in half, so the two triangles match and each has half the area.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_easy', 1, 'A square has an area of 10 cm². It is cut in half along a diagonal. What is the area of each triangle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"10 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"20 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"2 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each triangle is half of the square. Half of 10 cm² is 5 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_easy', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². The triangle is half of the 4 by 4 square. What is the area of the triangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","8 cm²","8 cm2","8 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'The whole square is 4 × 4 = 16 cm². Half of 16 is 8, so the triangle is 8 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[4,0],[4,4]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'easy', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the triangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12 cm²","12 cm2","12 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'The triangle is half of a 6 by 4 rectangle. 6 × 4 = 24, and half of 24 is 12 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[0,4]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'easy', 1, 'A parallelogram can be cut and rearranged into a ___ with the same area.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["rectangle","a rectangle"]'::jsonb, 'Cut the triangle off one slanted end and slide it to the other end. The pieces make a rectangle with the same area.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'easy', 1, 'A rectangle has an area of 18 m². A triangle is exactly half of this rectangle. What is the triangle''s area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"9 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"36 m²"},{"id":"c","text":"18 m²"},{"id":"d","text":"6 m²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Half of 18 m² is 9 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'medium', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the parallelogram?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["15","15 cm²","15 cm2","15 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Cut the triangle off the left end and move it to the right end. You get a rectangle 5 long and 3 high: 5 × 3 = 15 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[5,0],[7,3],[2,3]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'medium', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the triangle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"16 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"32 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"12 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"20 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The triangle fits in a rectangle 8 long and 4 high, which is 32 cm². The triangle is half of that: 16 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[3,4]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'medium', 1, 'A parallelogram with a base of 6 cm and a height of 4 cm has the same area as a rectangle 6 cm long and 4 cm wide.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Cut the slanted end off the parallelogram and move it across to make a 6 cm by 4 cm rectangle. Both are 24 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'hard', 1, 'What is the area of this right-angled triangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["20","20 cm²","20 cm2","20 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'It is half of an 8 cm by 5 cm rectangle. 8 × 5 = 40, and half of 40 is 20 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[0,5]],"sideLabels":["8 cm","","5 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these have an area of 12 cm²? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"a triangle with base 6 cm and height 4 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"a parallelogram with base 4 cm and height 3 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"a rectangle 6 cm long and 2 cm wide"},{"id":"d","text":"a triangle with base 3 cm and height 4 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Triangle: half of 6 × 4 = 12. Parallelogram: 4 × 3 = 12. Rectangle: 6 × 2 = 12. The last triangle is half of 3 × 4, only 6 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'hard', 1, 'Put these shapes in order from smallest to largest area.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"a triangle with base 4 cm and height 3 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"a square with sides of 3 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"a parallelogram with base 5 cm and height 2 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"a rectangle 4 cm long and 3 cm wide"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Triangle: half of 4 × 3 = 6. Square: 3 × 3 = 9. Parallelogram: 5 × 2 = 10. Rectangle: 4 × 3 = 12 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of this triangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["10","10 cm²","10 cm2","10 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'The base is 4 cm. The height is how far the top corner is straight up from the base line: 5 cm. Area = half of 4 × 5 = 10 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[4,0],[7,5]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_hard', 1, 'A triangle has an area of 15 cm² and a base of 6 cm. How tall is it, in centimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","5 cm"]'::jsonb, 'A triangle is half of a rectangle, so the rectangle would be 15 × 2 = 30 cm². 6 × ? = 30, so the height is 5 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of this house shape?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["33","33 cm²","33 cm2","33 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Split it into a rectangle and a triangle roof. Rectangle: 6 × 4 = 24 cm². Roof: base 6, height 3, so half of 6 × 3 = 9 cm². 24 + 9 = 33 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[6,4],[3,7],[0,4]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_easy', 1, 'A triangle that is exactly half of a rectangle has half the rectangle''s area.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'If the triangle covers exactly half of the rectangle, it covers half of its squares, so its area is half.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_easy', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². The triangle is half of a 6 by 2 rectangle. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"12 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"8 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"3 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The rectangle is 6 × 2 = 12 cm². Half of 12 is 6, so the triangle is 6 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[6,2]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_easy', 1, 'Two matching triangles fit together to make a rectangle with an area of 14 m². What is the area of one triangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","7 m²","7 m2","7 square metres"]'::jsonb, 'The two triangles are the same size, so each is half of 14 m²: 14 ÷ 2 = 7 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'easy', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the triangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["10","10 cm²","10 cm2","10 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'The triangle is half of a 4 by 5 rectangle. 4 × 5 = 20, and half of 20 is 10 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[4,0],[0,5]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'easy', 1, 'A triangle drawn inside a rectangle with the same base and height covers ___ of the rectangle.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["half","one half","1/2","a half"]'::jsonb, 'Cut the rectangle around a triangle and the leftover pieces fit together to make another triangle the same size, so the triangle is half.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'easy', 1, 'A parallelogram is cut and rearranged into a rectangle 7 cm long and 3 cm wide. What is the area of the parallelogram?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"21 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"10 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"20 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"42 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Moving the pieces doesn''t change the area. The rectangle is 7 × 3 = 21 cm², so the parallelogram is 21 cm² too.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'medium', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the parallelogram?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["16","16 cm²","16 cm2","16 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Cut the triangle off one slanted end and move it to the other end. You get a rectangle 4 long and 4 high: 4 × 4 = 16 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[4,0],[7,4],[3,4]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'medium', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the triangle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"15 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"30 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"11 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"20 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The triangle fits in a rectangle 6 long and 5 high, which is 30 cm². The triangle is half of that: 15 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[4,5]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'medium', 1, 'To find the area of a parallelogram, you multiply the lengths of its two slanted sides.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Multiply the base by the height, which is measured straight up from the base, not along the slanted side.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'hard', 1, 'What is the area of this right-angled triangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["27","27 m²","27 m2","27 square metres"]'::jsonb, 'It is half of a 9 m by 6 m rectangle. 9 × 6 = 54, and half of 54 is 27 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[9,0],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["9 m","","6 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these have an area of 20 cm²? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"a parallelogram with base 5 cm and height 4 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"a triangle with base 10 cm and height 4 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"a triangle with base 5 cm and height 4 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"a rectangle 10 cm long and 2 cm wide"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Parallelogram: 5 × 4 = 20. First triangle: half of 10 × 4 = 20. Rectangle: 10 × 2 = 20. The second triangle is half of 5 × 4, only 10 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'hard', 1, 'Put these shapes in order from smallest to largest area.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"a triangle with base 6 cm and height 2 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"a square with sides of 3 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"a triangle with base 5 cm and height 4 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"a parallelogram with base 4 cm and height 3 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'First triangle: half of 6 × 2 = 6. Square: 3 × 3 = 9. Second triangle: half of 5 × 4 = 10. Parallelogram: 4 × 3 = 12 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of this triangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","6 cm²","6 cm2","6 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'The base is 3 cm. The height is how far the top corner is straight up from the base line: 4 cm. Area = half of 3 × 4 = 6 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[3,0],[7,4]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_hard', 1, 'A parallelogram has an area of 28 cm² and a height of 4 cm. How long is its base, in centimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","7 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Area of a parallelogram = base × height, so ? × 4 = 28. Since 7 × 4 = 28, the base is 7 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of this shape?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["26","26 cm²","26 cm2","26 square centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Split it into a rectangle and a triangle. Rectangle: 5 × 4 = 20 cm². Triangle on the right: base 3, height 4, so half of 3 × 4 = 6 cm². 20 + 6 = 26 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[5,4],[0,4]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050401', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many small cubes make this block?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, 'There is one layer with 2 rows of 3 cubes. 2 × 3 = 6 cubes.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":3,"width":2,"height":1}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050402', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which unit measures how much water a bucket holds?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"litres"},{"id":"b","text":"metres"},{"id":"c","text":"kilograms"},{"id":"d","text":"square metres"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Capacity, how much a container holds, is measured in litres and millilitres.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050403', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_easy', 1, '1 litre is the same as 1000 millilitres.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Milli means one thousandth, so there are 1000 mL in 1 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050404', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'easy', 1, 'How much water is in the jug, in millilitres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["700","700 mL"]'::jsonb, 'The water reaches the 700 mL mark.', '{"kind":"jug","max":1000,"value":700,"step":100,"unit":"mL"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050405', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'easy', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. What is the volume of this block?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12 cm³","12 cm3","12 cubic centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Each layer has 2 rows of 3 cubes, which is 6. There are 2 layers, so 6 × 2 = 12 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":3,"width":2,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050406', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'easy', 1, '2 L = ___ mL', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"2000"'::jsonb, 'Each litre is 1000 mL, so 2 L is 2 × 1000 = 2000 mL.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050407', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'medium', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. What is the volume of this rectangular prism?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"24 cm³"},{"id":"b","text":"9 cm³"},{"id":"c","text":"12 cm³"},{"id":"d","text":"18 cm³"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The bottom layer has 3 rows of 4 cubes, which is 12. There are 2 layers, so 12 × 2 = 24 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":4,"width":3,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050408', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'medium', 1, 'How much water is in the jug?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1.5 L"},{"id":"b","text":"1.25 L"},{"id":"c","text":"1.75 L"},{"id":"d","text":"15 L"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The water reaches the mark between 1.25 L and 1.75 L, which is 1.5 L.', '{"kind":"jug","max":2,"value":1.5,"step":0.25,"unit":"L"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050409', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'medium', 1, 'Put these amounts in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"250 mL"},{"id":"b","text":"0.5 L"},{"id":"c","text":"750 mL"},{"id":"d","text":"1.2 L"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to mL: 250 mL, 0.5 L = 500 mL, 750 mL, 1.2 L = 1200 mL.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050410', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'hard', 1, 'A box is 5 cm long, 4 cm wide and 3 cm high. How many 1 cm cubes will fill it?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"60"'::jsonb, 'The bottom layer holds 5 × 4 = 20 cubes. There are 3 layers, so 20 × 3 = 60 cubes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050411', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are the same as 1.5 L? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"1500 mL"},{"id":"b","text":"1 L 500 mL"},{"id":"c","text":"150 mL"},{"id":"d","text":"1 L 50 mL"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '0.5 L is half of 1000 mL, which is 500 mL. So 1.5 L is 1 L 500 mL, or 1500 mL.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050412', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'hard', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. Some cubes are hidden at the back. What is the volume of this prism?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["36","36 cm³","36 cm3","36 cubic centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Each layer has 2 rows of 6 cubes, which is 12. There are 3 layers, so 12 × 3 = 36 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":6,"width":2,"height":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050413', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_hard', 1, 'A 2 L bottle of water is poured into cups that hold 300 mL each. How many cups can be filled right to the top?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, '2 L is 2000 mL. 6 cups use 6 × 300 = 1800 mL, leaving 200 mL. That is not enough for another full cup, so 6 cups.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050414', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_hard', 1, 'Mia wants to build a prism twice as tall as this one, with the same length and width. How many cubes will she need altogether?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"48"'::jsonb, 'Each layer has 2 rows of 4, which is 8 cubes. This prism has 3 layers; twice as tall is 6 layers. 8 × 6 = 48 cubes.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":4,"width":2,"height":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050415', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangular prism has a volume of 40 cm³. It is 5 cm long and 2 cm wide. How high is it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"8 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"33 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"20 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'One layer is 5 × 2 = 10 cubes. 40 cubes make 40 ÷ 10 = 4 layers, so it is 4 cm high.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050416', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many small cubes make this block?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"4"'::jsonb, 'There is one layer with 2 rows of 2 cubes. 2 × 2 = 4 cubes.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":2,"width":2,"height":1}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050417', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which unit is best for measuring the amount of medicine on a spoon?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"millilitres"},{"id":"b","text":"litres"},{"id":"c","text":"kilograms"},{"id":"d","text":"centimetres"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A spoon holds only a small amount, so millilitres are best. A litre is far too big.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050418', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_easy', 1, 'Half a litre is the same as 500 millilitres.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '1 litre is 1000 mL, and half of 1000 is 500, so half a litre is 500 mL.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050419', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'easy', 1, 'How much juice is in the jug, in millilitres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["300","300 mL"]'::jsonb, 'The juice reaches the 300 mL mark.', '{"kind":"jug","max":1000,"value":300,"step":100,"unit":"mL"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050420', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'easy', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. What is the volume of this block?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["16","16 cm³","16 cm3","16 cubic centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Each layer has 2 rows of 4 cubes, which is 8. There are 2 layers, so 8 × 2 = 16 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":4,"width":2,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050421', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'easy', 1, '3000 mL = ___ L', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"3"'::jsonb, 'Every 1000 mL makes 1 L, so 3000 mL is 3000 ÷ 1000 = 3 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050422', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'medium', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. What is the volume of this rectangular prism?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"20 cm³"},{"id":"b","text":"9 cm³"},{"id":"c","text":"10 cm³"},{"id":"d","text":"14 cm³"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The bottom layer has 2 rows of 5 cubes, which is 10. There are 2 layers, so 10 × 2 = 20 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":5,"width":2,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050423', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'medium', 1, 'How much water is in the jug?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.75 L"},{"id":"b","text":"0.5 L"},{"id":"c","text":"1 L"},{"id":"d","text":"75 L"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each mark is 0.25 L. The water reaches the third mark: 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 L.', '{"kind":"jug","max":2,"value":0.75,"step":0.25,"unit":"L"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050424', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'medium', 1, 'Put these amounts in order from largest to smallest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"2 L"},{"id":"b","text":"1750 mL"},{"id":"c","text":"1.5 L"},{"id":"d","text":"600 mL"},{"id":"e","text":"0.25 L"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to mL: 2 L = 2000 mL, 1750 mL, 1.5 L = 1500 mL, 600 mL, 0.25 L = 250 mL.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050425', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'hard', 1, 'A box is 7 cm long, 3 cm wide and 2 cm high. How many 1 cm cubes will fill it?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"42"'::jsonb, 'The bottom layer holds 7 × 3 = 21 cubes. There are 2 layers, so 21 × 2 = 42 cubes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050426', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these hold more than 1 L? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"1200 mL"},{"id":"b","text":"0.9 L"},{"id":"c","text":"1 L 50 mL"},{"id":"d","text":"999 mL"},{"id":"e","text":"1.5 L"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, '1 L is 1000 mL. 1200 mL, 1 L 50 mL (1050 mL) and 1.5 L (1500 mL) are more. 0.9 L is 900 mL and 999 mL is just short.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050427', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'hard', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. Some cubes are hidden at the back. What is the volume of this cube?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["27","27 cm³","27 cm3","27 cubic centimetres"]'::jsonb, 'Each layer has 3 rows of 3 cubes, which is 9. There are 3 layers, so 9 × 3 = 27 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":3,"width":3,"height":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050428', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_hard', 1, 'A 1.5 L jug of cordial is poured into glasses that hold 200 mL each. How many glasses can be filled right to the top?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"7"'::jsonb, '1.5 L is 1500 mL. 7 glasses use 7 × 200 = 1400 mL, leaving 100 mL. That is not enough for another full glass, so 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050429', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_hard', 1, 'Leo wants to build a prism with the same length and height as this one, but twice as wide. How many cubes will he need altogether?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"24"'::jsonb, 'Twice as wide is 4 rows instead of 2. Each layer would have 4 rows of 3, which is 12 cubes, and there are 2 layers: 12 × 2 = 24 cubes.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":3,"width":2,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050430', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050504', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangular prism has a volume of 36 cm³. It is 3 cm long and 3 cm wide. How high is it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"6 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"12 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"30 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'One layer is 3 × 3 = 9 cubes. 36 cubes make 36 ÷ 9 = 4 layers, so it is 4 cm high.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050501', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which unit would you use to measure the mass of a big bag of potatoes?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"kilograms"},{"id":"b","text":"grams"},{"id":"c","text":"millilitres"},{"id":"d","text":"metres"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A big bag of potatoes is heavy, so kilograms are best. Grams are for light things like a biscuit.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050502', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_easy', 1, '1 kilogram is the same as 1000 grams.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Kilo means one thousand, so 1 kg = 1000 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050503', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_easy', 1, 'What mass does the scale show?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3","3 kg"]'::jsonb, 'The needle points to 3, and the scale is in kg, so 3 kg.', '{"kind":"scale","max":5,"value":3,"step":1,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050504', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'easy', 1, 'How many grams of flour are on the scale?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["400","400 g"]'::jsonb, 'The needle points to 400, and the scale is in grams, so 400 g.', '{"kind":"scale","max":1000,"value":400,"step":100,"unit":"g"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050505', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'easy', 1, '4 kg = ___ g', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"4000"'::jsonb, 'Each kilogram is 1000 g, so 4 kg is 4 × 1000 = 4000 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050506', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these has a mass of about 1 tonne?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a small car"},{"id":"b","text":"a cat"},{"id":"c","text":"an apple"},{"id":"d","text":"a school bag"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A tonne is 1000 kg. A small car has a mass of about 1 tonne; the others are much lighter.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050507', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'medium', 1, 'What mass does the scale show?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3.5 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"3.05 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"35 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"4.5 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The needle is on the mark halfway between 3 and 4, which is 3.5 kg.', '{"kind":"scale","max":5,"value":3.5,"step":0.5,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050508', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'medium', 1, 'Put these masses in order from lightest to heaviest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"90 g"},{"id":"b","text":"0.5 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"800 g"},{"id":"d","text":"1.25 kg"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to grams: 90 g, 0.5 kg = 500 g, 800 g, 1.25 kg = 1250 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050509', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'medium', 1, 'How many grams are there in 2.5 kg?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2500","2500 g"]'::jsonb, '2 kg is 2000 g and 0.5 kg is 500 g, so 2.5 kg is 2500 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050510', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'hard', 1, 'The needle is halfway between two numbered marks. What mass does the scale show, in grams?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["650","650 g"]'::jsonb, 'The needle is halfway between 600 and 700. Halfway is 50 more than 600, so 650 g.', '{"kind":"scale","max":1000,"value":650,"step":100,"unit":"g"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050511', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are the same as 3 kg 250 g? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"3250 g"},{"id":"b","text":"3.25 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"325 g"},{"id":"d","text":"3.025 kg"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '3 kg is 3000 g, and 3000 + 250 = 3250 g. 250 g is 0.25 kg, so it is also 3.25 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050512', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'hard', 1, 'A box holds 6 tins of tomatoes. Each tin has a mass of 400 g. What is the total mass of the tins, in kilograms?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2.4","2.4 kg"]'::jsonb, '6 × 400 = 2400 g. 2400 g is 2 kg and 400 g, which is 2.4 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050513', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_hard', 1, 'A truck can carry 2 tonnes. It is already carrying 1350 kg. How many more kilograms can it carry?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["650","650 kg"]'::jsonb, '2 tonnes is 2000 kg. 2000 − 1350 = 650 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050514', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_hard', 1, 'Ruby has a 1.5 kg bag of flour. She uses 350 g for scones and 400 g for a cake. How much flour is left?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"750 g"},{"id":"b","text":"1150 g"},{"id":"c","text":"1.1 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"850 g"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1.5 kg is 1500 g. She uses 350 + 400 = 750 g. 1500 − 750 = 750 g left.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050515', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_hard', 1, 'The scale shows the mass of 8 cans that all weigh the same. What is the mass of one can, in grams?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["500","500 g"]'::jsonb, 'The scale shows 4 kg, which is 4000 g. 4000 ÷ 8 = 500 g for each can.', '{"kind":"scale","max":5,"value":4,"step":0.5,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050516', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which unit would you use to measure the mass of a strawberry?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"grams"},{"id":"b","text":"kilograms"},{"id":"c","text":"tonnes"},{"id":"d","text":"litres"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A strawberry is very light, so grams are best. Kilograms and tonnes are for heavy things.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050517', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_easy', 1, '1 tonne is the same as 1000 kilograms.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A tonne is used for very heavy things, and 1 tonne = 1000 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050518', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_easy', 1, 'What mass does the scale show?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","6 kg"]'::jsonb, 'The needle points to 6, and the scale is in kg, so 6 kg.', '{"kind":"scale","max":10,"value":6,"step":1,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050519', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'easy', 1, 'How much butter is on the scale?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"700 g"},{"id":"b","text":"7 g"},{"id":"c","text":"70 g"},{"id":"d","text":"700 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The needle points to 700, and the scale is in grams, so 700 g.', '{"kind":"scale","max":1000,"value":700,"step":100,"unit":"g"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050520', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'easy', 1, '5000 g = ___ kg', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"5"'::jsonb, 'Every 1000 g makes 1 kg, so 5000 g is 5000 ÷ 1000 = 5 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050521', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these would you measure in tonnes?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a loaded truck"},{"id":"b","text":"a loaf of bread"},{"id":"c","text":"a pencil"},{"id":"d","text":"a bag of apples"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A tonne is 1000 kg, which suits very heavy things like a loaded truck. The others are measured in grams or kilograms.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050522', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'medium', 1, 'What mass does the scale show, in kilograms?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2.5","2.5 kg"]'::jsonb, 'The needle is on the mark halfway between 2 and 3, which is 2.5 kg.', '{"kind":"scale","max":4,"value":2.5,"step":0.5,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050523', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'medium', 1, 'Put these masses in order from heaviest to lightest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"2 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"1.8 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"1500 g"},{"id":"d","text":"0.75 kg"},{"id":"e","text":"600 g"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to grams: 2 kg = 2000 g, 1.8 kg = 1800 g, 1500 g, 0.75 kg = 750 g, 600 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050524', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'medium', 1, 'How many grams are there in 1.75 kg?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1750","1750 g"]'::jsonb, '1 kg is 1000 g and 0.75 kg is 750 g, so 1.75 kg is 1750 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050525', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'hard', 1, 'Each mark on the scale is 0.25 kg. What mass does it show?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1.25 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"1.2 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"1.5 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"12.5 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The needle is one mark past 1 kg. One mark is 0.25 kg, so it shows 1 + 0.25 = 1.25 kg.', '{"kind":"scale","max":2,"value":1.25,"step":0.25,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050526', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are the same as 2 kg 75 g? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2075 g"},{"id":"b","text":"2.075 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"2.75 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"275 g"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '2 kg is 2000 g, and 2000 + 75 = 2075 g. 75 g is 0.075 kg, so it is also 2.075 kg. 2.75 kg would be 2 kg 750 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050527', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'hard', 1, 'A bag holds 8 apples. Each apple has a mass of 150 g. What is the total mass of the apples, in kilograms?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1.2","1.2 kg"]'::jsonb, '8 × 150 = 1200 g. 1200 g is 1 kg and 200 g, which is 1.2 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050528', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_hard', 1, 'A lift can carry 1 tonne. Six people with a total mass of 480 kg are in it. How many more kilograms can it carry?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["520","520 kg"]'::jsonb, '1 tonne is 1000 kg. 1000 − 480 = 520 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050529', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_hard', 1, 'Ali has a 2 kg bag of rice. He cooks 450 g on Monday and 300 g on Tuesday. How much rice is left?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1250 g"},{"id":"b","text":"1750 g"},{"id":"c","text":"1.75 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"750 g"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '2 kg is 2000 g. He cooks 450 + 300 = 750 g. 2000 − 750 = 1250 g left.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050530', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050505', 'very_hard', 1, 'The scale shows the mass of 6 bags of lollies that all weigh the same. What is the mass of one bag, in grams?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["250","250 g"]'::jsonb, 'The scale shows 1.5 kg, which is 1500 g. 1500 ÷ 6 = 250 g for each bag.', '{"kind":"scale","max":3,"value":1.5,"step":0.25,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050601', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which time is in the afternoon?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3:00 pm"},{"id":"b","text":"3:00 am"},{"id":"c","text":"9:00 am"},{"id":"d","text":"11:00 am"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'pm times are from midday to midnight, so 3:00 pm is in the afternoon. am times are before midday.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050602', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_easy', 1, 'In 24-hour time, 13:00 is 1:00 pm.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'After midday the hours keep counting past 12. 13 − 12 = 1, so 13:00 is 1:00 pm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050603', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_easy', 1, 'Kai''s swimming lesson starts at this time in the afternoon. Which time is it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2:30 am"},{"id":"b","text":"2:30 pm"},{"id":"c","text":"6:10 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"3:30 pm"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The clock shows half past 2. It is in the afternoon, so it is 2:30 pm.', '{"kind":"clock","hour":2,"minute":30}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050604', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'easy', 1, 'Write 5:00 pm in 24-hour time.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["17:00","1700","17 00"]'::jsonb, 'For pm times, add 12 to the hour: 5 + 12 = 17, so 17:00.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050605', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'easy', 1, 'School assembly starts at this time in the morning. In 24-hour time that is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["09:15","9:15","0915"]'::jsonb, 'The clock shows quarter past 9. Morning times keep the same hour in 24-hour time, written 09:15.', '{"kind":"clock","hour":9,"minute":15}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050606', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'easy', 1, 'Put these 24-hour times in order from earliest to latest in one day.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"06:45"},{"id":"b","text":"11:20"},{"id":"c","text":"14:05"},{"id":"d","text":"21:30"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'In 24-hour time a bigger hour is always later: 06:45, 11:20, 14:05 (2:05 pm), 21:30 (9:30 pm).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050607', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'medium', 1, 'What is 20:15 in 12-hour time?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8:15 pm"},{"id":"b","text":"8:15 am"},{"id":"c","text":"10:15 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"2:15 pm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '20 is more than 12, so it is pm. 20 − 12 = 8, so it is 8:15 pm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050608', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'medium', 1, 'A movie starts at 6:40 pm and finishes at 8:15 pm. How many minutes long is it?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["95","95 minutes"]'::jsonb, '6:40 to 7:00 is 20 minutes. 7:00 to 8:00 is 60 minutes. 8:00 to 8:15 is 15 minutes. 20 + 60 + 15 = 95 minutes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050609', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'medium', 1, 'Ava''s bus leaves at this time in the morning. The trip takes 25 minutes. What time does she arrive?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["11:15","11:15 am","11:15am"]'::jsonb, 'The clock shows 10:50. 10 minutes takes it to 11:00, and 15 more minutes makes 11:15 am.', '{"kind":"clock","hour":10,"minute":50}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050610', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the timetable. How many minutes does the train take from Central to Parramatta?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["33","33 minutes"]'::jsonb, 'From 14:25 to 14:58 is 58 − 25 = 33 minutes.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Station","Train leaves"],"rows":[["Central","14:25"],["Strathfield","14:41"],["Parramatta","14:58"],["Blacktown","15:16"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050611', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the timetable. When does the train leave Strathfield, in 12-hour time?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2:41 pm"},{"id":"b","text":"4:41 pm"},{"id":"c","text":"2:41 am"},{"id":"d","text":"12:41 pm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '14:41 is after midday, so it is pm. 14 − 12 = 2, so it is 2:41 pm.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Station","Train leaves"],"rows":[["Central","14:25"],["Strathfield","14:41"],["Parramatta","14:58"],["Blacktown","15:16"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050612', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these times are after midday? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"13:10"},{"id":"b","text":"12:30 pm"},{"id":"c","text":"9:30 am"},{"id":"d","text":"01:30"},{"id":"e","text":"16:00"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, '13:10 and 16:00 have hours bigger than 12, and 12:30 pm is half an hour after midday. 9:30 am and 01:30 are in the morning.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050613', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_hard', 1, 'A plane leaves Sydney at 22:50. The flight takes 4 hours 30 minutes. What is the time in Sydney when it lands? Use 24-hour time.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["03:20","3:20","0320"]'::jsonb, '22:50 plus 4 hours is 02:50 the next morning (it goes past midnight). 30 minutes more is 03:20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050614', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_hard', 1, 'Dad puts a roast in the oven at 4:35 pm. It needs 1 hour 50 minutes to cook. When will it be ready?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6:25 pm"},{"id":"b","text":"6:15 pm"},{"id":"c","text":"5:25 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"6:35 pm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '4:35 plus 1 hour is 5:35. 25 more minutes is 6:00, and the last 25 minutes makes 6:25 pm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050615', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the table. How many minutes was Noah away from home?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["225","225 minutes"]'::jsonb, 'From 08:10 to 11:10 is 3 hours, which is 180 minutes. 11:10 to 11:55 is 45 minutes. 180 + 45 = 225 minutes.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Noah''s Saturday","Time"],"rows":[["Leaves home","08:10"],["Cricket starts","08:45"],["Cricket ends","11:20"],["Gets home","11:55"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050616', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which time is in the morning?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7:30 am"},{"id":"b","text":"7:30 pm"},{"id":"c","text":"12:30 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"11:00 pm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'am times are from midnight to midday, so 7:30 am is in the morning. pm times are after midday.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050617', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_easy', 1, 'In 24-hour time, 18:00 is 6:00 pm.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'After midday the hours keep counting past 12. 18 − 12 = 6, so 18:00 is 6:00 pm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050618', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_easy', 1, 'Mia eats dinner at this time in the evening. Which time is it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7:45 pm"},{"id":"b","text":"7:45 am"},{"id":"c","text":"9:35 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"8:45 pm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The minute hand is on the 9, which is 45 minutes, and the hour hand is just before the 8. That is 7:45, and evening is pm.', '{"kind":"clock","hour":7,"minute":45}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050619', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'easy', 1, 'Write 9:00 pm in 24-hour time.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["21:00","2100","21 00"]'::jsonb, 'For pm times, add 12 to the hour: 9 + 12 = 21, so 21:00.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050620', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'easy', 1, 'Netball training starts at this time in the afternoon. In 24-hour time that is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["16:20","1620"]'::jsonb, 'The clock shows 4:20. It is in the afternoon, so add 12 to the hour: 4 + 12 = 16, which gives 16:20.', '{"kind":"clock","hour":4,"minute":20}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050621', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'easy', 1, 'Put these times in order from earliest to latest in one day.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"5:15 am"},{"id":"b","text":"08:30"},{"id":"c","text":"12:10 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"15:45"},{"id":"e","text":"10:20 pm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to the same kind of time: 5:15 am, 8:30 am, 12:10 pm, 3:45 pm (15:45), then 10:20 pm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050622', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'medium', 1, 'What is 15:40 in 12-hour time?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3:40 pm"},{"id":"b","text":"5:40 pm"},{"id":"c","text":"3:40 am"},{"id":"d","text":"1:40 pm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '15 is more than 12, so it is pm. 15 − 12 = 3, so it is 3:40 pm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050623', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'medium', 1, 'A cricket match starts at 1:20 pm and finishes at 4:05 pm. How many minutes long is it?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["165","165 minutes"]'::jsonb, '1:20 to 4:20 would be 3 hours, which is 180 minutes. It finished 15 minutes earlier than 4:20, so 180 − 15 = 165 minutes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050624', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'medium', 1, 'Tom puts a cake in the oven at this time in the morning. It takes 40 minutes to bake. What time is it ready?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12:15","12:15 pm","12:15pm"]'::jsonb, 'The clock shows 11:35. 25 minutes takes it to 12:00, and 15 more minutes makes 12:15 pm.', '{"kind":"clock","hour":11,"minute":35}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050625', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the timetable. How many minutes does the bus take from Manly to Mona Vale?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["52","52 minutes"]'::jsonb, 'From 07:48 to 08:00 is 12 minutes, and 08:00 to 08:40 is 40 minutes. 12 + 40 = 52 minutes.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Bus stop","Bus leaves"],"rows":[["Manly","07:48"],["Dee Why","08:06"],["Collaroy","08:19"],["Mona Vale","08:40"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050626', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the timetable. Which part of the trip takes the longest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Collaroy to Mona Vale"},{"id":"b","text":"Manly to Dee Why"},{"id":"c","text":"Dee Why to Collaroy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Manly to Dee Why is 18 minutes, Dee Why to Collaroy is 13 minutes and Collaroy to Mona Vale is 21 minutes.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Bus stop","Bus leaves"],"rows":[["Manly","07:48"],["Dee Why","08:06"],["Collaroy","08:19"],["Mona Vale","08:40"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050627', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these times are before midday? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"10:45"},{"id":"b","text":"00:30"},{"id":"c","text":"12:15"},{"id":"d","text":"11:59 am"},{"id":"e","text":"13:00"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '10:45 and 11:59 am are in the morning, and 00:30 is half an hour after midnight. 12:15 is after midday and 13:00 is 1:00 pm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050628', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bus leaves Sydney at 21:35 and arrives in Melbourne 11 hours 40 minutes later. What time does it arrive? Use 24-hour time.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["09:15","9:15","0915"]'::jsonb, '21:35 plus 11 hours is 08:35 the next morning (it goes past midnight). 40 minutes more is 09:15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050629', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_hard', 1, 'A movie is 2 hours 15 minutes long. It must finish by 9:00 pm. What is the latest time it can start?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6:45 pm"},{"id":"b","text":"7:15 pm"},{"id":"c","text":"6:15 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"7:45 pm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Work backwards from 9:00 pm. Take away 2 hours to get 7:00 pm, then 15 minutes more to get 6:45 pm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005050630', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050506', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the table. How many minutes was Lily away from home?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["280","280 minutes"]'::jsonb, 'From 09:25 to 13:25 is 4 hours, which is 240 minutes. 13:25 to 14:05 is 40 minutes. 240 + 40 = 280 minutes.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Lily''s Sunday","Time"],"rows":[["Leaves for the beach","09:25"],["Goes swimming","10:00"],["Eats lunch","12:30"],["Gets home","14:05"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_easy', 1, 'What type of triangle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"equilateral"},{"id":"b","text":"isosceles"},{"id":"c","text":"scalene"},{"id":"d","text":"right-angled"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'All three sides are 6 cm. A triangle with three equal sides is equilateral.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[3,5]],"sideLabels":["6 cm","6 cm","6 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_easy', 1, 'A quadrilateral is any shape with four straight sides.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Quad means four. Squares, rectangles and kites are all quadrilaterals.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many right angles does this rectangle have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4","four"]'::jsonb, 'Every corner of a rectangle is a right angle, and it has 4 corners.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[7,0],[7,4],[0,4]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'easy', 1, 'Which kind of triangle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"right-angled"},{"id":"b","text":"equilateral"},{"id":"c","text":"obtuse-angled"},{"id":"d","text":"acute-angled"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The corner at the bottom left is a square corner, a right angle. So it is a right-angled triangle.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[0,5]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'easy', 1, 'A triangle with no equal sides is called a ___ triangle.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"scalene"'::jsonb, 'Scalene triangles have three different side lengths. Isosceles have two equal sides and equilateral have three.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'easy', 1, 'What is this shape called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"parallelogram"},{"id":"b","text":"trapezium"},{"id":"c","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"d","text":"kite"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It has two pairs of parallel sides, and the sides are not all equal, so it is a parallelogram.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[8,4],[2,4]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these shapes always have two pairs of parallel sides? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"square"},{"id":"b","text":"rectangle"},{"id":"c","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"d","text":"kite"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Squares, rectangles and rhombuses all have opposite sides parallel. A kite does not have parallel sides.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'medium', 1, 'This triangle is isosceles.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Two of its sides are 7 cm. A triangle with exactly two equal sides is isosceles.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[3,6]],"sideLabels":["6 cm","7 cm","7 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'medium', 1, 'What is this shape called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"kite"},{"id":"b","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"c","text":"trapezium"},{"id":"d","text":"parallelogram"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It has two pairs of equal sides that sit next to each other, the two long ones and the two short ones, and no parallel sides. That makes it a kite.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[3,0],[6,5],[3,7],[0,5]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'hard', 1, 'Every square is also a rectangle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A rectangle is a quadrilateral with four right angles. A square has four right angles too, so it is a special rectangle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'hard', 1, 'I have four equal sides, but my angles are not right angles. What shape am I?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"b","text":"square"},{"id":"c","text":"rectangle"},{"id":"d","text":"kite"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A square also has four equal sides, but its angles are right angles. Four equal sides without right angles is a rhombus.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'hard', 1, 'Put these shapes in order from fewest to most lines of symmetry.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"scalene triangle"},{"id":"b","text":"isosceles triangle"},{"id":"c","text":"equilateral triangle"},{"id":"d","text":"square"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A scalene triangle has 0 lines of symmetry, an isosceles triangle has 1, an equilateral triangle has 3 and a square has 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which words describe this triangle? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"right-angled"},{"id":"b","text":"isosceles"},{"id":"c","text":"equilateral"},{"id":"d","text":"scalene"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'It has a right angle at the bottom left, and two sides of 5 cm, so it is right-angled and isosceles. The long side is longer than 5 cm, so it is not equilateral.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[5,0],[0,5]],"sideLabels":["5 cm","","5 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which quadrilateral has diagonals that are the same length and cross at right angles?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"square"},{"id":"b","text":"rectangle"},{"id":"c","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"d","text":"kite"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A rectangle''s diagonals are equal but don''t cross at right angles. A rhombus''s cross at right angles but aren''t equal. A square''s do both.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_hard', 1, 'A triangle can have two right angles.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'If two corners were right angles, the two sides going up from them would be parallel and would never meet to make the third corner.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the side lengths. What type of triangle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"scalene"},{"id":"b","text":"isosceles"},{"id":"c","text":"equilateral"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The sides are 8 cm, 10 cm and 6 cm, all different. A triangle with no equal sides is scalene.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["8 cm","10 cm","6 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_easy', 1, 'A rhombus has four equal sides.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Every side of a rhombus is the same length, like a square that has been pushed over.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many lines of symmetry does this square have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4","four"]'::jsonb, 'A square can be folded in half 4 ways: across the middle both ways and along both diagonals.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[5,0],[5,5],[0,5]],"sideLabels":["5 cm","5 cm","5 cm","5 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'easy', 1, 'Which kind of triangle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"obtuse-angled"},{"id":"b","text":"right-angled"},{"id":"c","text":"acute-angled"},{"id":"d","text":"equilateral"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The corner at the right end of the short bottom side is wider than a right angle, so it is an obtuse-angled triangle.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[4,0],[10,3]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'easy', 1, 'A triangle with exactly two equal sides is called an ___ triangle.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"isosceles"'::jsonb, 'Isosceles triangles have two equal sides. Equilateral triangles have three, and scalene triangles have none.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'easy', 1, 'What is this shape called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"trapezium"},{"id":"b","text":"parallelogram"},{"id":"c","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"d","text":"kite"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It has one pair of parallel sides (the top and the bottom) and the other two sides slope in. That makes it a trapezium.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[6,4],[2,4]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these shapes always have four right angles? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"square"},{"id":"b","text":"rectangle"},{"id":"c","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"d","text":"parallelogram"},{"id":"e","text":"kite"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Squares and rectangles always have four right angles. A rhombus, parallelogram or kite can have slanted corners.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'medium', 1, 'All four sides are 5 cm and the corners are not right angles. What is this shape called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"b","text":"kite"},{"id":"c","text":"trapezium"},{"id":"d","text":"rectangle"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A quadrilateral with four equal sides is a rhombus. It isn''t a square because its angles aren''t right angles.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[3,0],[6,4],[3,8],[0,4]],"sideLabels":["5 cm","5 cm","5 cm","5 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'medium', 1, 'This triangle is scalene.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Two of its sides are 5 cm, so it has two equal sides. That makes it isosceles, not scalene.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[4,3]],"sideLabels":["8 cm","5 cm","5 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'hard', 1, 'Every rhombus is a parallelogram.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A parallelogram has two pairs of parallel sides. A rhombus has its opposite sides parallel too, so it is a special parallelogram.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'hard', 1, 'I have two pairs of equal sides that sit next to each other, and one line of symmetry. What shape am I?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"kite"},{"id":"b","text":"rectangle"},{"id":"c","text":"parallelogram"},{"id":"d","text":"trapezium"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A kite has two short equal sides next to each other and two long equal sides next to each other. It folds in half along one line.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'hard', 1, 'Put these shapes in order from fewest to most lines of symmetry.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"parallelogram"},{"id":"b","text":"kite"},{"id":"c","text":"rectangle"},{"id":"d","text":"equilateral triangle"},{"id":"e","text":"square"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'A parallelogram (that isn''t a rectangle or rhombus) has 0 lines of symmetry, a kite has 1, a rectangle has 2, an equilateral triangle has 3 and a square has 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"A square is a rhombus"},{"id":"b","text":"A rectangle is a parallelogram"},{"id":"c","text":"A kite is a parallelogram"},{"id":"d","text":"A trapezium has two pairs of parallel sides"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'A square has four equal sides, so it is a rhombus. A rectangle has two pairs of parallel sides, so it is a parallelogram. A kite has no parallel sides and a trapezium has only one pair.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which quadrilateral has diagonals that cross at right angles, where only one diagonal is cut in half by the other?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"kite"},{"id":"b","text":"square"},{"id":"c","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"d","text":"rectangle"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A kite''s diagonals cross at right angles, and the long one cuts the short one in half but not the other way round. In a square and a rhombus both diagonals are cut in half.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050601', 'very_hard', 1, 'An equilateral triangle can have a right angle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'All three angles of an equilateral triangle are the same size. If one were a right angle, all three would be, and three right angles are too wide to close up into a triangle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is this object called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"cube"},{"id":"b","text":"square"},{"id":"c","text":"sphere"},{"id":"d","text":"cylinder"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It is a 3D object with 6 square faces, so it is a cube. A square is a flat 2D shape.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"cube"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_easy', 1, 'A sphere has no flat faces.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A sphere, like a ball, has one curved surface and no flat faces.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is this object called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"b","text":"triangular pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"rectangular prism"},{"id":"d","text":"cone"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It has two matching triangle ends joined by rectangles, so it is a triangular prism.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'easy', 1, 'How many faces does this square pyramid have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","five"]'::jsonb, 'It has 1 square base and 4 triangle faces. 1 + 4 = 5 faces.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"square_pyramid"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'easy', 1, 'A prism is named after the shape of its ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["base","bases","ends","end"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular prism has triangle bases at each end, and a rectangular prism has rectangle bases.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'easy', 1, 'How many edges does a cube have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","twelve"]'::jsonb, 'A cube has 4 edges on top, 4 on the bottom and 4 going up the sides. 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'medium', 1, 'How many vertices (corners) does this rectangular prism have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","eight"]'::jsonb, 'It has 4 corners on the top and 4 on the bottom. 4 + 4 = 8 vertices.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"rectangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'medium', 1, 'A net is made of 1 square and 4 triangles. What object does it fold into?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"b","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"c","text":"cube"},{"id":"d","text":"triangular pyramid"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The square is the base, and the 4 triangles fold up to meet at a point. That is a square pyramid.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these objects are prisms? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"cube"},{"id":"b","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"c","text":"rectangular prism"},{"id":"d","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"e","text":"cone"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Prisms have two matching ends joined by rectangles. A cube is a rectangular prism with square faces. Pyramids and cones come to a point.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'hard', 1, 'A net is made of 2 triangles and 3 rectangles. What object does it fold into?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"b","text":"triangular pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"d","text":"rectangular prism"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The 2 triangles are the matching ends, and the 3 rectangles join them. That is a triangular prism.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'hard', 1, 'How many edges does this triangular pyramid have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","six"]'::jsonb, 'The triangle base has 3 edges, and 3 more edges go up to the top point. 3 + 3 = 6 edges.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_pyramid"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'hard', 1, 'A cone is a pyramid.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'A pyramid has a flat base with straight sides and flat triangle faces. A cone has a circle base and a curved surface, so it is not a pyramid.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these objects in order from fewest to most faces.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"triangular pyramid"},{"id":"b","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"rectangular prism"},{"id":"d","text":"hexagonal prism"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Triangular pyramid: 4 faces. Square pyramid: 5. Rectangular prism: 6. Hexagonal prism: 2 hexagons + 6 rectangles = 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_hard', 1, 'A prism has bases that are hexagons (6 sides). How many faces does it have altogether?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","eight"]'::jsonb, 'There are 2 hexagon bases, and one rectangle joins each side of the hexagon: 6 rectangles. 2 + 6 = 8 faces.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_hard', 1, 'You look down at this square pyramid from directly above. What do you see?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a square with lines from each corner to the middle"},{"id":"b","text":"a triangle"},{"id":"c","text":"a circle with a dot in the middle"},{"id":"d","text":"a square with nothing inside it"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'From above you see the square base, and the 4 sloping edges run from each corner up to the top point in the middle.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"square_pyramid"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is this object called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"cylinder"},{"id":"b","text":"cone"},{"id":"c","text":"circle"},{"id":"d","text":"sphere"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It has two flat circle ends joined by one curved surface, like a can of soup. That is a cylinder. A circle is a flat 2D shape.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"cylinder"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_easy', 1, 'A cube has 6 faces.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A cube has a top, a bottom and 4 sides, all squares. 1 + 1 + 4 = 6 faces.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many flat faces does this cone have?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1"},{"id":"b","text":"0"},{"id":"c","text":"2"},{"id":"d","text":"3"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A cone has one flat circle base. The rest is a curved surface that comes to a point.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"cone"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'easy', 1, 'How many faces does this triangular prism have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","five"]'::jsonb, 'It has 2 triangle ends and 3 rectangle faces joining them. 2 + 3 = 5 faces.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'easy', 1, 'A pyramid is named after the shape of its ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["base","bottom face"]'::jsonb, 'A square pyramid has a square base and a triangular pyramid has a triangle base. The other faces are triangles that meet at a point.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'easy', 1, 'How many vertices (corners) does a square pyramid have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","five"]'::jsonb, 'There are 4 corners around the square base and 1 at the top point. 4 + 1 = 5 vertices.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'medium', 1, 'How many edges does this triangular prism have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9","nine"]'::jsonb, 'Each triangle end has 3 edges, which makes 6, and 3 more edges join the two ends. 6 + 3 = 9 edges.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'medium', 1, 'A net is made of 6 squares that are all the same size. What object does it fold into?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"cube"},{"id":"b","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"d","text":"cylinder"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A cube has 6 square faces, so a net of 6 equal squares folds into a cube.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these objects are pyramids? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"b","text":"triangular pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"cone"},{"id":"d","text":"cube"},{"id":"e","text":"triangular prism"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Pyramids have a flat base with straight sides and triangle faces that meet at a point. A cone has a curved surface, and the cube and prism have two matching ends.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'hard', 1, 'A net is made of 4 triangles that are all the same. What object does it fold into?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"triangular pyramid"},{"id":"b","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"d","text":"cube"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'One triangle is the base and the other 3 fold up to meet at a point. That is a triangular pyramid.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'hard', 1, 'How many edges does this rectangular prism have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","twelve"]'::jsonb, 'It has 4 edges around the top, 4 around the bottom and 4 going up the sides. 4 + 4 + 4 = 12 edges.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"rectangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'hard', 1, 'A cylinder is a prism.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'A prism has flat faces joining its two ends. A cylinder''s ends are joined by a curved surface, so it is not a prism.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these objects in order from fewest to most vertices.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"triangular pyramid"},{"id":"b","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"d","text":"cube"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Triangular pyramid: 3 + 1 = 4 vertices. Square pyramid: 4 + 1 = 5. Triangular prism: 3 + 3 = 6. Cube: 4 + 4 = 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_hard', 1, 'A pyramid has a base with 6 sides (a hexagon). How many edges does it have altogether?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","twelve"]'::jsonb, 'The hexagon base has 6 edges, and one edge goes from each of its 6 corners up to the top point. 6 + 6 = 12 edges.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050602', 'very_hard', 1, 'You look at this triangular prism straight on from one of its ends. What shape do you see?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a triangle"},{"id":"b","text":"a rectangle"},{"id":"c","text":"a square"},{"id":"d","text":"a circle"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each end of a triangular prism is a triangle, so looking straight at an end you see a triangle.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_easy', 1, 'What type of angle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"right angle"},{"id":"b","text":"acute angle"},{"id":"c","text":"obtuse angle"},{"id":"d","text":"straight angle"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The square mark in the corner shows a right angle, like the corner of a book. It is 90°.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":90}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_easy', 1, 'An acute angle is smaller than a right angle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Acute angles are between 0° and 90°, so they are smaller than a right angle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word describes this angle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"acute"},{"id":"b","text":"obtuse"},{"id":"c","text":"right"},{"id":"d","text":"reflex"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The arms are closer together than a right angle, so it is smaller than 90°. That makes it acute.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":40}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'easy', 1, 'A right angle measures ___ degrees.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["90","90°"]'::jsonb, 'A right angle is a quarter of a full turn: 360 ÷ 4 = 90°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'easy', 1, 'Which is the best estimate for the size of this angle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"130°"},{"id":"b","text":"50°"},{"id":"c","text":"90°"},{"id":"d","text":"230°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The angle is wider than a right angle (90°) but less than a straight line (180°), so it is obtuse. 130° fits. 50° is acute and 230° is reflex.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":130}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'easy', 1, 'Put these angles in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"acute angle"},{"id":"b","text":"right angle"},{"id":"c","text":"obtuse angle"},{"id":"d","text":"straight angle"},{"id":"e","text":"reflex angle"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Acute is less than 90°, right is 90°, obtuse is between 90° and 180°, straight is 180° and reflex is between 180° and 360°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'medium', 1, 'How many degrees are in a straight angle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["180","180°"]'::jsonb, 'A straight angle is half a full turn: 360 ÷ 2 = 180°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'medium', 1, 'What type of angle is marked?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"reflex"},{"id":"b","text":"obtuse"},{"id":"c","text":"acute"},{"id":"d","text":"straight"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The marked turn goes past a straight line (180°) but not all the way round (360°). That is a reflex angle.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":250}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these angles are obtuse? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"95°"},{"id":"b","text":"120°"},{"id":"c","text":"175°"},{"id":"d","text":"85°"},{"id":"e","text":"190°"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Obtuse angles are more than 90° and less than 180°: 95°, 120° and 175°. 85° is acute and 190° is reflex.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'hard', 1, 'Two angles sit side by side on a straight line. One of them is 110°. How big is the other angle, in degrees?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["70","70°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles on a straight line add up to 180°. 180 − 110 = 70°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'hard', 1, 'Angle a is more than half of a right angle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Half of a right angle is 45°. Angle a is wider than that (it is about 60°), so the statement is true.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":60,"label":"a"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'hard', 1, 'Angles around a point add up to ___ degrees.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["360","360°"]'::jsonb, 'The angles around a point make one full turn, which is 360°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_hard', 1, 'Three angles meet at a point. Two of them are 150° and 90°. How big is the third angle, in degrees?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["120","120°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles at a point add up to 360°. 150 + 90 = 240, and 360 − 240 = 120°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_hard', 1, 'A straight line is split into three angles. Two of them are 35° and 90°. How big is the third angle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"55°"},{"id":"b","text":"65°"},{"id":"c","text":"235°"},{"id":"d","text":"145°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Angles on a straight line add up to 180°. 35 + 90 = 125, and 180 − 125 = 55°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_hard', 1, 'The clock shows 2 o''clock. How many degrees is the smaller angle between the hands?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["60","60°"]'::jsonb, 'A full turn is 360°, and there are 12 numbers, so each gap is 360 ÷ 12 = 30°. From 12 to 2 is 2 gaps: 2 × 30 = 60°.', '{"kind":"clock","hour":2,"minute":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_easy', 1, 'What type of angle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"obtuse angle"},{"id":"b","text":"acute angle"},{"id":"c","text":"right angle"},{"id":"d","text":"straight angle"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The arms open wider than a right angle but not as far as a straight line, so it is an obtuse angle.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":120}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_easy', 1, 'An obtuse angle is bigger than a right angle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Obtuse angles are between 90° and 180°, so they are bigger than a right angle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_easy', 1, 'What type of angle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"straight angle"},{"id":"b","text":"right angle"},{"id":"c","text":"reflex angle"},{"id":"d","text":"acute angle"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The two arms make one straight line. That is a straight angle, 180°.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":180}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'easy', 1, 'A full turn measures ___ degrees.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["360","360°"]'::jsonb, 'A full turn goes all the way around, which is 360°. That is 4 right angles: 4 × 90 = 360.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'easy', 1, 'Which is the best estimate for the size of this angle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"35°"},{"id":"b","text":"90°"},{"id":"c","text":"145°"},{"id":"d","text":"135°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The angle is much smaller than a right angle (90°), so it is acute. 35° fits. 135° and 145° are obtuse.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":35}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'easy', 1, 'Put these angles in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"half a right angle"},{"id":"b","text":"a right angle"},{"id":"c","text":"an angle of 150°"},{"id":"d","text":"a straight angle"},{"id":"e","text":"three quarters of a full turn"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Half a right angle is 45°, a right angle is 90°, then 150°, a straight angle is 180° and three quarters of a full turn is 270°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'medium', 1, 'How many degrees are in half a right angle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["45","45°"]'::jsonb, 'A right angle is 90°. Half of 90 is 45°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'medium', 1, 'What type of angle is marked?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"reflex"},{"id":"b","text":"obtuse"},{"id":"c","text":"acute"},{"id":"d","text":"right"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The marked turn goes past a straight line (180°) but not all the way round (360°). That is a reflex angle.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":300}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these angles are acute? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"45°"},{"id":"b","text":"89°"},{"id":"c","text":"90°"},{"id":"d","text":"12°"},{"id":"e","text":"100°"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Acute angles are less than 90°: 45°, 89° and 12°. 90° is a right angle and 100° is obtuse.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'hard', 1, 'Two angles sit side by side on a straight line. One of them is 65°. How big is the other angle, in degrees?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["115","115°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles on a straight line add up to 180°. 180 − 65 = 115°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'hard', 1, 'Angle b is smaller than a right angle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The arms of angle b open wider than a square corner, so it is more than 90°. It is obtuse, not smaller than a right angle.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":100,"label":"b"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'hard', 1, 'A right angle is split into two angles. One of them is 35°. How big is the other?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"55°"},{"id":"b","text":"65°"},{"id":"c","text":"145°"},{"id":"d","text":"325°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The two angles together make 90°. 90 − 35 = 55°. (145° would be for a straight line.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_hard', 1, 'Four angles meet at a point. Three of them are 90°, 100° and 80°. How big is the fourth angle, in degrees?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["90","90°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles at a point add up to 360°. 90 + 100 + 80 = 270, and 360 − 270 = 90°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_hard', 1, 'A straight angle is split into three equal angles. How big is each one?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"60°"},{"id":"b","text":"90°"},{"id":"c","text":"120°"},{"id":"d","text":"45°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A straight angle is 180°. 180 ÷ 3 = 60°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050603', 'very_hard', 1, 'The clock shows 4 o''clock. How many degrees is the smaller angle between the hands?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["120","120°"]'::jsonb, 'A full turn is 360° and there are 12 numbers, so each gap is 360 ÷ 12 = 30°. From 12 to 4 is 4 gaps: 4 × 30 = 120°.', '{"kind":"clock","hour":4,"minute":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060401', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_easy', 1, 'What are the coordinates of point A?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(4, 2)"},{"id":"b","text":"(2, 4)"},{"id":"c","text":"(4, 4)"},{"id":"d","text":"(2, 2)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Go across first, then up. A is 4 across and 2 up, so it is at (4, 2).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":5,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":4,"y":2,"label":"A"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060402', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_easy', 1, 'In the coordinates (3, 5), the first number tells you how far across to go.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Coordinates are written (across, up). So (3, 5) means 3 across and 5 up.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060403', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which point is at (5, 2)?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A"},{"id":"b","text":"B"},{"id":"c","text":"C"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Go 5 across and 2 up. That lands on point B.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":1,"y":3,"label":"A"},{"x":5,"y":2,"label":"B"},{"x":2,"y":5,"label":"C"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060404', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'easy', 1, 'What is the x-coordinate of point P?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, 'The x-coordinate is how far across the point is. P is 6 across (and 3 up), so its x-coordinate is 6.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":6,"y":3,"label":"P"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060405', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'easy', 1, 'The point (0, 0), where the x-axis and the y-axis meet, is called the ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["origin","the origin"]'::jsonb, 'The origin is the starting point of the grid, at (0, 0).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060406', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'easy', 1, 'What are the coordinates of point K?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(0, 4)"},{"id":"b","text":"(4, 0)"},{"id":"c","text":"(4, 4)"},{"id":"d","text":"(1, 4)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'K is on the y-axis, so it is 0 across. It is 4 up. So K is at (0, 4).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":0,"y":4,"label":"K"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060407', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'medium', 1, 'How many units apart are points A and B?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4","4 units"]'::jsonb, 'A is at (2, 1) and B is at (2, 5). They are in the same column, so count up: 5 − 1 = 4 units.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":2,"y":1,"label":"A"},{"x":2,"y":5,"label":"B"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060408', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'medium', 1, 'Which points are on the same horizontal line as (3, 4)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"(7, 4)"},{"id":"b","text":"(0, 4)"},{"id":"c","text":"(3, 7)"},{"id":"d","text":"(4, 3)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Points on the same horizontal line are the same height, so they have the same second number, 4: (7, 4) and (0, 4).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060409', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'medium', 1, 'Start at (2, 3). Move 4 units right and 2 units up. What are your new coordinates?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["(6, 5)","(6,5)","6, 5","6,5"]'::jsonb, 'Moving right adds to the first number: 2 + 4 = 6. Moving up adds to the second number: 3 + 2 = 5. So (6, 5).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060410', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'hard', 1, 'ABCD is a rectangle. Where must point D go?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(1, 4)"},{"id":"b","text":"(4, 1)"},{"id":"c","text":"(4, 5)"},{"id":"d","text":"(5, 5)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'D must be above A (across 1) and level with C (up 4). So D is at (1, 4).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":1,"y":1,"label":"A"},{"x":5,"y":1,"label":"B"},{"x":5,"y":4,"label":"C"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060411', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'hard', 1, 'Which describes the path drawn from S to E?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4 up, then 5 right"},{"id":"b","text":"5 up, then 4 right"},{"id":"c","text":"4 right, then 5 up"},{"id":"d","text":"1 up, then 6 right"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The path goes from (1, 1) up to (1, 5), which is 4 up. Then it goes right to (6, 5), which is 5 right.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":7,"axes":true,"path":[[1,1],[1,5],[6,5]],"points":[{"x":1,"y":1,"label":"S"},{"x":6,"y":5,"label":"E"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060412', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'hard', 1, '(2, 7) and (7, 2) are the same point.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '(2, 7) is 2 across and 7 up, but (7, 2) is 7 across and 2 up. The order matters, so they are different points.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060413', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_hard', 1, 'Point M is exactly halfway between A and B. What are the coordinates of M?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["(4, 2)","(4,2)","4, 2","4,2"]'::jsonb, 'A is at (1, 2) and B is at (7, 2), which are 6 units apart. Halfway is 3 units from A: 1 + 3 = 4. M stays at height 2, so (4, 2).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":5,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":1,"y":2,"label":"A"},{"x":7,"y":2,"label":"B"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060414', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these points in order from closest to the y-axis to furthest from it.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"(1, 6)"},{"id":"b","text":"(3, 0)"},{"id":"c","text":"(5, 2)"},{"id":"d","text":"(8, 1)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The distance from the y-axis is the first number, how far across: 1, 3, 5, then 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060415', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_hard', 1, 'A square has three of its corners at (2, 1), (6, 1) and (6, 5). What is the area of the square, in square units?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["16","16 square units"]'::jsonb, 'From (2, 1) to (6, 1) is 6 − 2 = 4 units, so each side is 4 units. Area = 4 × 4 = 16 square units.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060416', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_easy', 1, 'What are the coordinates of point B?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(3, 5)"},{"id":"b","text":"(5, 3)"},{"id":"c","text":"(3, 3)"},{"id":"d","text":"(5, 5)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Go across first, then up. B is 3 across and 5 up, so it is at (3, 5).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":3,"y":5,"label":"B"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060417', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_easy', 1, 'To plot the point (4, 1), you go 1 across and 4 up.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Coordinates are written (across, up). So (4, 1) means 4 across and 1 up, not the other way round.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060418', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which point is at (2, 4)?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A"},{"id":"b","text":"B"},{"id":"c","text":"C"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Go 2 across and 4 up. That lands on point A. (B is at (4, 2), the numbers swapped.)', '{"kind":"grid","columns":7,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":2,"y":4,"label":"A"},{"x":4,"y":2,"label":"B"},{"x":6,"y":1,"label":"C"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060419', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'easy', 1, 'What is the y-coordinate of point Q?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"4"'::jsonb, 'The y-coordinate is how far up the point is. Q is 5 across and 4 up, so its y-coordinate is 4.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":5,"y":4,"label":"Q"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060420', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'easy', 1, 'The axis that runs straight across the bottom of the grid is called the ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["x-axis","x axis","horizontal axis"]'::jsonb, 'The x-axis runs across (left to right). The y-axis runs up the side.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060421', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'easy', 1, 'What are the coordinates of point R?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(5, 0)"},{"id":"b","text":"(0, 5)"},{"id":"c","text":"(5, 5)"},{"id":"d","text":"(5, 1)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'R is on the x-axis, so it is 0 up. It is 5 across. So R is at (5, 0).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":5,"y":0,"label":"R"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060422', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'medium', 1, 'How many units apart are points A and B?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","6 units"]'::jsonb, 'A is at (1, 3) and B is at (7, 3). They are on the same row, so count across: 7 − 1 = 6 units.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":1,"y":3,"label":"A"},{"x":7,"y":3,"label":"B"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060423', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'medium', 1, 'Which points are on the same vertical line as (5, 2)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"(5, 6)"},{"id":"b","text":"(5, 0)"},{"id":"c","text":"(2, 5)"},{"id":"d","text":"(6, 2)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Points on the same vertical line are the same distance across, so they have the same first number, 5: (5, 6) and (5, 0).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060424', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'medium', 1, 'Start at (6, 4). Move 3 units left and 1 unit down. What are your new coordinates?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["(3, 3)","(3,3)","3, 3","3,3"]'::jsonb, 'Moving left takes away from the first number: 6 − 3 = 3. Moving down takes away from the second number: 4 − 1 = 3. So (3, 3).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060425', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'hard', 1, 'PQRS is a square. Where must point S go?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(2, 5)"},{"id":"b","text":"(5, 2)"},{"id":"c","text":"(1, 5)"},{"id":"d","text":"(6, 6)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'S must be straight above P (2 across) and level with R (5 up). So S is at (2, 5), and every side is 4 units long.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":2,"y":1,"label":"P"},{"x":6,"y":1,"label":"Q"},{"x":6,"y":5,"label":"R"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060426', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'hard', 1, 'Which describes the path drawn from S to E?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5 right, then 4 up"},{"id":"b","text":"4 right, then 5 up"},{"id":"c","text":"5 up, then 4 right"},{"id":"d","text":"2 up, then 5 right"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The path goes from (0, 2) right to (5, 2), which is 5 right. Then it goes up to (5, 6), which is 4 up.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":7,"axes":true,"path":[[0,2],[5,2],[5,6]],"points":[{"x":0,"y":2,"label":"S"},{"x":5,"y":6,"label":"E"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060427', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'hard', 1, 'The point (0, 6) is on the x-axis.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '(0, 6) is 0 across and 6 up, so it sits on the y-axis, the line going up the side. Points on the x-axis have 0 as their second number.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060428', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_hard', 1, 'Point C is 2 units to the right of the point exactly halfway between A and B. What are the coordinates of C?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["(5, 4)","(5,4)","5, 4","5,4"]'::jsonb, 'A is at (3, 1) and B is at (3, 7), 6 units apart. Halfway is 3 units up from A: (3, 4). 2 units right of that is (5, 4).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":8,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":3,"y":1,"label":"A"},{"x":3,"y":7,"label":"B"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060429', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangle has corners at (1, 1), (7, 1), (7, 4) and (1, 4). What is its perimeter, in units?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["18","18 units"]'::jsonb, 'From (1, 1) to (7, 1) is 7 − 1 = 6 units long. From (7, 1) to (7, 4) is 4 − 1 = 3 units. Perimeter: 6 + 3 + 6 + 3 = 18 units.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005060430', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050604', 'very_hard', 1, 'Point P is at (4, 3). Point Q is 3 units above P. Point R is 2 units to the left of Q. Where is R?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(2, 6)"},{"id":"b","text":"(6, 6)"},{"id":"c","text":"(1, 6)"},{"id":"d","text":"(2, 1)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '3 units above P adds 3 to the second number: Q is at (4, 6). 2 units left takes 2 from the first number: R is at (2, 6).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the picture graph. How many children chose dogs?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"8"'::jsonb, 'Each star stands for 2 children. Dogs has 4 stars, and 4 × 2 = 8 children.', '{"kind":"picture_graph","title":"Favourite pets in 5M","thing":"children","each":2,"rows":[{"label":"Dogs","count":4},{"label":"Cats","count":3},{"label":"Fish","count":1}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_easy', 1, 'In a picture graph where each symbol stands for 10, three symbols mean 30.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Each symbol is worth 10, so 3 symbols are 3 × 10 = 30.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the column graph. How many cans were collected on Tuesday?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"20"'::jsonb, 'The Tuesday column reaches the line marked 20.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Cans collected","columns":[{"label":"Mon","value":15},{"label":"Tue","value":20},{"label":"Wed","value":10}],"step":5}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the picture graph. How many more children chose dogs than cats?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"2"'::jsonb, 'Dogs has 1 more star than cats. Each star is 2 children, so that is 2 more children (8 − 6 = 2).', '{"kind":"picture_graph","title":"Favourite pets in 5M","thing":"children","each":2,"rows":[{"label":"Dogs","count":4},{"label":"Cats","count":3},{"label":"Fish","count":1}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'easy', 1, 'A picture graph uses one symbol for every 5 votes. How many symbols show 30 votes?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5"},{"id":"b","text":"6"},{"id":"c","text":"30"},{"id":"d","text":"25"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Each symbol is 5 votes. 30 ÷ 5 = 6 symbols.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the column graph. The numbers up the side go up in equal steps. How big is each step?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"10"'::jsonb, 'The numbers on the side go 0, 10, 20, 30 and so on, so each step is 10.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Kilometres cycled","columns":[{"label":"Lily","value":40},{"label":"Max","value":70},{"label":"Zara","value":50}],"step":10}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'medium', 1, 'Jai counted 120, 80, 150 and 60 cars on four days. Which scale suits a column graph of this data best?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"each line stands for 1 car"},{"id":"b","text":"each line stands for 10 cars"},{"id":"c","text":"each line stands for 100 cars"},{"id":"d","text":"each line stands for 3 cars"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Counting by 10s reaches 150 in 15 lines and all four numbers land on a line. By 1s or 3s you''d need far too many lines; by 100s the columns would be hard to read.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the picture graph. How many fish did Bo catch?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"20"'::jsonb, 'Each star stands for 4 fish. Bo has 5 stars, and 5 × 4 = 20 fish.', '{"kind":"picture_graph","title":"Fish caught","thing":"fish","each":4,"rows":[{"label":"Ana","count":3},{"label":"Bo","count":5},{"label":"Cy","count":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the column graph. How many people visited the museum on Tuesday?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"125"'::jsonb, 'The scale goes up by 25. The Tuesday column reaches the line marked 125.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Museum visitors","columns":[{"label":"Mon","value":75},{"label":"Tue","value":125},{"label":"Wed","value":100},{"label":"Thu","value":50}],"step":25}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the column graph. How many people visited the museum altogether over the four days?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"350"'::jsonb, 'Read each column: 75 + 125 + 100 + 50 = 350 visitors.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Museum visitors","columns":[{"label":"Mon","value":75},{"label":"Tue","value":125},{"label":"Wed","value":100},{"label":"Thu","value":50}],"step":25}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'hard', 1, 'In a picture graph where each symbol stands for 4 people, half a symbol stands for 2 people.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Half of 4 is 2, so half a symbol shows 2 people.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'hard', 1, 'In a picture graph, each symbol stands for 6 ice creams sold. Thursday shows 3 and a half symbols. How many ice creams were sold on Thursday?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"21"'::jsonb, '3 symbols are 3 × 6 = 18. Half a symbol is half of 6, which is 3. 18 + 3 = 21.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_hard', 1, 'Mel wants to show 18, 24, 30 and 36 in a picture graph using whole symbols only. Which key works?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1 symbol = 4"},{"id":"b","text":"1 symbol = 5"},{"id":"c","text":"1 symbol = 6"},{"id":"d","text":"1 symbol = 10"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, '18, 24, 30 and 36 are all multiples of 6 (3, 4, 5 and 6 symbols). 18 and 30 aren''t multiples of 4, and 18 isn''t a multiple of 5 or 10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the column graph. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"5M recycled twice as many cans as 5K"},{"id":"b","text":"6T recycled 50 more cans than 6R"},{"id":"c","text":"5K recycled the fewest cans"},{"id":"d","text":"The total is 1000 cans"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, '5M has 300, which is double 5K''s 150, and 150 is the smallest. 6T (200) recycled 50 fewer than 6R (250), and the total is 150 + 300 + 250 + 200 = 900.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Cans recycled","columns":[{"label":"5K","value":150},{"label":"5M","value":300},{"label":"6R","value":250},{"label":"6T","value":200}],"step":50}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_hard', 1, 'On a column graph the lines go up by 20. A column stops exactly halfway between the 60 line and the 80 line. What number does it show?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"70"'::jsonb, 'Halfway between 60 and 80 is 70, because 70 is 10 more than 60 and 10 less than 80.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the picture graph. How many books did Leo read?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"30"'::jsonb, 'Each star stands for 5 books. Leo has 6 stars, and 6 × 5 = 30 books.', '{"kind":"picture_graph","title":"Books read in Term 2","thing":"books","each":5,"rows":[{"label":"Ava","count":3},{"label":"Leo","count":6},{"label":"Kim","count":4}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_easy', 1, 'In a picture graph where each symbol stands for 5, four symbols mean 9.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Each symbol is worth 5, so 4 symbols are 4 × 5 = 20, not 9. (9 comes from adding 4 and 5.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the column graph. Which team scored 40 goals?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Stars"},{"id":"b","text":"Swans"},{"id":"c","text":"Kites"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The Stars column reaches the line marked 40. Swans scored 30 and Kites scored 50.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Netball goals this season","columns":[{"label":"Swans","value":30},{"label":"Kites","value":50},{"label":"Stars","value":40}],"step":10}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the picture graph. How many books did Ava and Kim read altogether?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"35"'::jsonb, 'Ava has 3 stars and Kim has 4, so 7 stars. Each star is 5 books: 7 × 5 = 35 books.', '{"kind":"picture_graph","title":"Books read in Term 2","thing":"books","each":5,"rows":[{"label":"Ava","count":3},{"label":"Leo","count":6},{"label":"Kim","count":4}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'easy', 1, 'A picture graph uses one symbol for every 10 people. How many symbols show 70 people?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7"},{"id":"b","text":"70"},{"id":"c","text":"10"},{"id":"d","text":"17"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each symbol is 10 people. 70 ÷ 10 = 7 symbols.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the column graph. How many more litres were used on Tuesday than on Monday?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["40","40 L"]'::jsonb, 'The scale goes up by 20. Tuesday reaches 100 and Monday reaches 60. 100 − 60 = 40 L.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Litres of water used in the garden","columns":[{"label":"Mon","value":60},{"label":"Tue","value":100},{"label":"Wed","value":80}],"step":20}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'medium', 1, 'Sally counted 25, 45, 30 and 50 birds in four surveys. Which scale suits a column graph of this data best?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"each line stands for 5 birds"},{"id":"b","text":"each line stands for 1 bird"},{"id":"c","text":"each line stands for 100 birds"},{"id":"d","text":"each line stands for 20 birds"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'All four numbers are multiples of 5, so they land on a line, and 50 needs only 10 lines. By 1s you''d need 50 lines; by 20s or 100s the numbers fall between lines.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the picture graph. How many laps did Ivy run?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"18"'::jsonb, 'Each star stands for 3 laps. Ivy has 6 stars, and 6 × 3 = 18 laps.', '{"kind":"picture_graph","title":"Laps of the oval","thing":"laps","each":3,"rows":[{"label":"Tom","count":4},{"label":"Ivy","count":6},{"label":"Raj","count":5}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the column graph. How many tickets were sold on Saturday?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"250"'::jsonb, 'The scale goes up by 50. The Saturday column reaches the line marked 250.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"School concert tickets sold","columns":[{"label":"Thu","value":100},{"label":"Fri","value":150},{"label":"Sat","value":250},{"label":"Sun","value":200}],"step":50}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the column graph. How many tickets were sold altogether over the four days?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"700"'::jsonb, 'Read each column: 100 + 150 + 250 + 200 = 700 tickets.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"School concert tickets sold","columns":[{"label":"Thu","value":100},{"label":"Fri","value":150},{"label":"Sat","value":250},{"label":"Sun","value":200}],"step":50}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'hard', 1, 'In a picture graph where each symbol stands for 8 cars, a quarter of a symbol stands for 2 cars.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A quarter of 8 is 8 ÷ 4 = 2, so a quarter of a symbol shows 2 cars.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'hard', 1, 'In a picture graph, each symbol stands for 20 kangaroos. How should 50 kangaroos be shown?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2 and a half symbols"},{"id":"b","text":"5 symbols"},{"id":"c","text":"2 symbols"},{"id":"d","text":"3 and a half symbols"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '2 symbols are 2 × 20 = 40 kangaroos. 10 more is half of 20, so add half a symbol: 2 and a half symbols.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_hard', 1, 'In a picture graph, 1 symbol stands for 6 people. How should 27 people be shown?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4 and a half symbols"},{"id":"b","text":"4 symbols"},{"id":"c","text":"27 symbols"},{"id":"d","text":"5 and a half symbols"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '4 symbols are 4 × 6 = 24 people. 3 more is half of 6, so add half a symbol: 4 and a half symbols.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the column graph. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"4B collected three times as much as 6D"},{"id":"b","text":"5C collected 40 kg more than 3A"},{"id":"c","text":"3A and 6D together collected as much as 5C"},{"id":"d","text":"The total is 300 kg"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '4B has 120, which is 3 × 40. 5C (100) is 40 more than 3A (60). 3A + 6D = 60 + 40 = 100, the same as 5C. The total is 60 + 120 + 100 + 40 = 320 kg, not 300.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Kilograms of rubbish collected","columns":[{"label":"3A","value":60},{"label":"4B","value":120},{"label":"5C","value":100},{"label":"6D","value":40}],"step":20}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050701', 'very_hard', 1, 'On a column graph the lines go up by 5. The tallest column shows 85. How many lines up from 0 is the top of that column?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"17"'::jsonb, 'Each line is 5 more than the one below. 85 ÷ 5 = 17, so the top is 17 lines up.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. What was the temperature at 9am?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["16","16°C","16 °C","16 degrees"]'::jsonb, 'Find 9am along the bottom, go up to the line, then across to the side: 16°C.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Dubbo (°C)","points":[{"label":"6am","value":12},{"label":"9am","value":16},{"label":"12pm","value":24},{"label":"3pm","value":28},{"label":"6pm","value":20}],"step":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. At what time was it hottest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"9am"},{"id":"b","text":"12pm"},{"id":"c","text":"3pm"},{"id":"d","text":"6pm"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'The highest point on the line is at 3pm, at 28°C.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Dubbo (°C)","points":[{"label":"6am","value":12},{"label":"9am","value":16},{"label":"12pm","value":24},{"label":"3pm","value":28},{"label":"6pm","value":20}],"step":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_easy', 1, 'A line graph is a good way to show how something changes over time.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A line graph joins the points in time order, so you can see the line go up and down as time passes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. By how many degrees did the temperature rise from 6am to 12pm?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12°C","12 °C","12 degrees"]'::jsonb, 'It was 12°C at 6am and 24°C at 12pm. 24 − 12 = 12 degrees.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Dubbo (°C)","points":[{"label":"6am","value":12},{"label":"9am","value":16},{"label":"12pm","value":24},{"label":"3pm","value":28},{"label":"6pm","value":20}],"step":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'easy', 1, 'Put these events on a timeline in order, earliest first.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"First Fleet arrives in Sydney Cove (1788)"},{"id":"b","text":"Gold is found near Bathurst (1851)"},{"id":"c","text":"Federation of Australia (1901)"},{"id":"d","text":"Sydney Opera House opens (1973)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Order the years from smallest to largest: 1788, 1851, 1901, 1973.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'easy', 1, 'The table shows koalas counted in a national park each year. Between which two years did the number go up?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2021 to 2022"},{"id":"b","text":"2022 to 2023"},{"id":"c","text":"2023 to 2024"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'The count went down from 48 to 42 to 36, then up from 36 to 45 between 2023 and 2024.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Year","Koalas counted"],"rows":[["2021",48],["2022",42],["2023",36],["2024",45]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the line graph. In which week did the plant grow the most?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Week 1 to Week 2"},{"id":"b","text":"Week 2 to Week 3"},{"id":"c","text":"Week 3 to Week 4"},{"id":"d","text":"Week 4 to Week 5"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The line is steepest from Week 2 to Week 3: it grew from 4 cm to 10 cm, which is 6 cm. Every other week it grew 2 cm.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Plant height (cm)","points":[{"label":"Week 1","value":2},{"label":"Week 2","value":4},{"label":"Week 3","value":10},{"label":"Week 4","value":12},{"label":"Week 5","value":14}],"step":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'medium', 1, 'On a timeline, the First Fleet arrived in 1788 and Federation happened in 1901. How many years apart are these events?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["113","113 years"]'::jsonb, '1901 − 1788: count up 12 years to 1800, then 101 years to 1901. 12 + 101 = 113 years.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the line graph. How much did the plant grow altogether from Week 1 to Week 5?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12 cm"]'::jsonb, 'It was 2 cm in Week 1 and 14 cm in Week 5. 14 − 2 = 12 cm.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Plant height (cm)","points":[{"label":"Week 1","value":2},{"label":"Week 2","value":4},{"label":"Week 3","value":10},{"label":"Week 4","value":12},{"label":"Week 5","value":14}],"step":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The water level fell on Tuesday and on Wednesday"},{"id":"b","text":"The level stayed the same from Wednesday to Thursday"},{"id":"c","text":"It probably rained between Thursday and Friday"},{"id":"d","text":"The tank was fullest on Monday"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The line goes down to Wednesday, is flat to Thursday, then jumps up to 900 L on Friday, so water was added, probably by rain. Friday (900 L), not Monday, was fullest.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Water in a farm tank (L)","points":[{"label":"Mon","value":800},{"label":"Tue","value":650},{"label":"Wed","value":500},{"label":"Thu","value":500},{"label":"Fri","value":900}],"step":100}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. How many litres of water were used from Monday to Wednesday?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["300","300 L"]'::jsonb, 'It went from 800 L on Monday to 500 L on Wednesday. 800 − 500 = 300 L.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Water in a farm tank (L)","points":[{"label":"Mon","value":800},{"label":"Tue","value":650},{"label":"Wed","value":500},{"label":"Thu","value":500},{"label":"Fri","value":900}],"step":100}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'hard', 1, 'A timeline from 1950 to 2000 is split into 5 equal parts. Each part stands for 10 years.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'From 1950 to 2000 is 50 years. 50 ÷ 5 = 10 years for each part.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. Between which two days did the amount of water change the most?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mon to Tue"},{"id":"b","text":"Tue to Wed"},{"id":"c","text":"Wed to Thu"},{"id":"d","text":"Thu to Fri"}]'::jsonb, '"d"'::jsonb, 'The changes are 150 L down, 150 L down, no change, then 400 L up. The biggest change is Thursday to Friday.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Water in a farm tank (L)","points":[{"label":"Mon","value":800},{"label":"Tue","value":650},{"label":"Wed","value":500},{"label":"Thu","value":500},{"label":"Fri","value":900}],"step":100}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_hard', 1, 'A line graph shows a puppy''s mass going up steadily, from 4 kg at 2 months old to 10 kg at 6 months old. About what was its mass at 4 months old?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"7 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"8 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"5 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '4 months is halfway between 2 and 6 months. Halfway between 4 kg and 10 kg is 7 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_hard', 1, 'The table shows events for a timeline of Opal''s life. Which event is exactly halfway between Opal being born and the family moving?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Opal''s sister is born"},{"id":"b","text":"Opal starts school"},{"id":"c","text":"None of them"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'From 2012 to 2020 is 8 years. Half of 8 is 4, and 2012 + 4 = 2016, the year her sister was born.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Event","Year"],"rows":[["Opal is born",2012],["Opal''s sister is born",2016],["Opal starts school",2018],["Family moves to Wollongong",2020]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. What was the temperature at 12pm?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30","30°C","30 °C","30 degrees"]'::jsonb, 'Find 12pm along the bottom, go up to the line, then across to the side: 30°C.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Broken Hill (°C)","points":[{"label":"6am","value":15},{"label":"9am","value":20},{"label":"12pm","value":30},{"label":"3pm","value":35},{"label":"6pm","value":25},{"label":"9pm","value":20}],"step":5}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. At what time was it coolest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6am"},{"id":"b","text":"9am"},{"id":"c","text":"6pm"},{"id":"d","text":"9pm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The lowest point on the line is at 6am, at 15°C.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Broken Hill (°C)","points":[{"label":"6am","value":15},{"label":"9am","value":20},{"label":"12pm","value":30},{"label":"3pm","value":35},{"label":"6pm","value":25},{"label":"9pm","value":20}],"step":5}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_easy', 1, 'On a timeline, events are placed in the order they happened.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A timeline is like a number line for dates. Earlier events go first and later events go further along.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. By how many degrees did the temperature drop from 3pm to 9pm?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["15","15°C","15 °C","15 degrees"]'::jsonb, 'It was 35°C at 3pm and 20°C at 9pm. 35 − 20 = 15 degrees.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Broken Hill (°C)","points":[{"label":"6am","value":15},{"label":"9am","value":20},{"label":"12pm","value":30},{"label":"3pm","value":35},{"label":"6pm","value":25},{"label":"9pm","value":20}],"step":5}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'easy', 1, 'Put these events on a timeline in order, earliest first.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Captain Cook sails along the east coast (1770)"},{"id":"b","text":"Sydney Harbour Bridge opens (1932)"},{"id":"c","text":"First TV broadcast in Australia (1956)"},{"id":"d","text":"Sydney Olympic Games (2000)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Order the years from smallest to largest: 1770, 1932, 1956, 2000.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'easy', 1, 'The table shows platypus sightings in a creek each year. Between which two years did the number go down?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2020 to 2021"},{"id":"b","text":"2021 to 2022"},{"id":"c","text":"2022 to 2023"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The count went up from 15 to 22, then down from 22 to 19 between 2021 and 2022, then up again to 26.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Year","Platypus sightings"],"rows":[["2020",15],["2021",22],["2022",19],["2023",26]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the line graph. Between which months did the puppy gain the most mass?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Month 1 to Month 2"},{"id":"b","text":"Month 2 to Month 3"},{"id":"c","text":"Month 3 to Month 4"},{"id":"d","text":"Month 4 to Month 5"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The line is steepest from Month 2 to Month 3: it went from 4 kg to 7 kg, a gain of 3 kg. The other gains are 2 kg, 2 kg and 1 kg.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Puppy''s mass (kg)","points":[{"label":"Month 1","value":2},{"label":"Month 2","value":4},{"label":"Month 3","value":7},{"label":"Month 4","value":9},{"label":"Month 5","value":10}],"step":1}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'medium', 1, 'On a timeline, the Sydney Harbour Bridge opened in 1932 and the Sydney Opera House opened in 1973. How many years apart are these events?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["41","41 years"]'::jsonb, '1973 − 1932: count up 8 years to 1940, then 33 years to 1973. 8 + 33 = 41 years.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the line graph. How much mass did the puppy gain altogether from Month 1 to Month 5?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","8 kg"]'::jsonb, 'It was 2 kg in Month 1 and 10 kg in Month 5. 10 − 2 = 8 kg.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Puppy''s mass (kg)","points":[{"label":"Month 1","value":2},{"label":"Month 2","value":4},{"label":"Month 3","value":7},{"label":"Month 4","value":9},{"label":"Month 5","value":10}],"step":1}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The number of cars went up from 7am to 9am"},{"id":"b","text":"The number stayed the same from 9am to 10am"},{"id":"c","text":"The car park was fullest at 11am"},{"id":"d","text":"There were 20 fewer cars at 11am than at 10am"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'The line rises to 60 at 9am, stays flat to 10am, then falls to 40 at 11am, which is 20 fewer. The car park was fullest at 9am and 10am, not 11am.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Cars in the school car park","points":[{"label":"7am","value":10},{"label":"8am","value":40},{"label":"9am","value":60},{"label":"10am","value":60},{"label":"11am","value":40}],"step":10}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. How many more cars were there at 9am than at 7am?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"50"'::jsonb, 'There were 60 cars at 9am and 10 cars at 7am. 60 − 10 = 50 more cars.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Cars in the school car park","points":[{"label":"7am","value":10},{"label":"8am","value":40},{"label":"9am","value":60},{"label":"10am","value":60},{"label":"11am","value":40}],"step":10}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'hard', 1, 'A timeline from 1800 to 2000 is split into 4 equal parts. Each part stands for 40 years.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'From 1800 to 2000 is 200 years. 200 ÷ 4 = 50, so each part stands for 50 years, not 40.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. Between which two times did the number of cars change the most?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7am to 8am"},{"id":"b","text":"8am to 9am"},{"id":"c","text":"9am to 10am"},{"id":"d","text":"10am to 11am"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The changes are 30 up, 20 up, no change, then 20 down. The biggest change is 7am to 8am.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Cars in the school car park","points":[{"label":"7am","value":10},{"label":"8am","value":40},{"label":"9am","value":60},{"label":"10am","value":60},{"label":"11am","value":40}],"step":10}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_hard', 1, 'A line graph shows the water in a dam rising steadily from 200 megalitres in January to 500 megalitres in April. About how much water was in the dam in March?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"400 megalitres"},{"id":"b","text":"350 megalitres"},{"id":"c","text":"300 megalitres"},{"id":"d","text":"450 megalitres"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'January to April is 3 months, and the water rose 300 megalitres, so about 100 each month: 300 in February and 400 in March.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050702', 'very_hard', 1, 'The table shows events for a family timeline. How old was Grandpa when Mum was born?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["28","28 years","28 years old"]'::jsonb, 'Grandpa was born in 1958 and Mum in 1986. 1986 − 1958: count up 2 to 1960, then 26 to 1986. 2 + 26 = 28 years old.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Event","Year"],"rows":[["Grandpa is born",1958],["Grandpa moves to Orange",1970],["Mum is born",1986],["Ted is born",2014]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the probability of this spinner landing on red?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/4"},{"id":"b","text":"1/3"},{"id":"c","text":"3/4"},{"id":"d","text":"1/2"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 4 equal parts and 1 is red, so the probability is 1 out of 4, or 1/4.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","blue","green","yellow"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_easy', 1, 'An event that is impossible has a probability of 0.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Probability goes from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word best describes a probability of 1?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"certain"},{"id":"b","text":"impossible"},{"id":"c","text":"even chance"},{"id":"d","text":"unlikely"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A probability of 1 means it will definitely happen, so it is certain.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'easy', 1, 'You pick one counter from this bag without looking. What is the probability of picking red? Write it as a fraction.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3/8","three eighths"]'::jsonb, 'There are 3 + 5 = 8 counters and 3 are red, so the probability is 3/8.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":3},{"colour":"blue","count":5}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'easy', 1, 'You roll a normal dice numbered 1 to 6. What is the probability of rolling a 4? Write it as a fraction.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/6","one sixth"]'::jsonb, 'There are 6 equally likely numbers and only one of them is 4, so the probability is 1/6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'easy', 1, 'Put these chance words in order from least likely to most likely.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"impossible"},{"id":"b","text":"unlikely"},{"id":"c","text":"even chance"},{"id":"d","text":"likely"},{"id":"e","text":"certain"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Impossible has probability 0, even chance is 1/2 and certain is 1. Unlikely sits between 0 and 1/2, and likely between 1/2 and 1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'medium', 1, 'What is the probability of this spinner landing on blue?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4/6"},{"id":"b","text":"1/6"},{"id":"c","text":"2/6"},{"id":"d","text":"4/2"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 6 equal parts and 4 are blue, so the probability is 4/6 (which is the same as 2/3).', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["blue","blue","red","blue","green","blue"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'medium', 1, 'You roll a normal dice numbered 1 to 6. Which of these have a probability of 1/2? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rolling an even number"},{"id":"b","text":"Rolling a number less than 4"},{"id":"c","text":"Rolling a 6"},{"id":"d","text":"Rolling a number greater than 2"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Even numbers are 2, 4, 6 (3 out of 6) and less than 4 is 1, 2, 3 (3 out of 6), both 1/2. A 6 is 1/6, and greater than 2 is 4/6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'medium', 1, 'Noah spun a spinner 20 times. It landed on green 5 times. What fraction of his spins landed on green?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5/20","1/4"]'::jsonb, '5 out of 20 spins is 5/20, which is the same as 1/4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'hard', 1, 'A probability of 1/2 is the same as a 50% chance.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '1/2 is 50 out of 100, which is 50%. It is an even chance.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'hard', 1, 'You pick one counter from this bag without looking. What is the probability that it is NOT green?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5/10"},{"id":"b","text":"2/10"},{"id":"c","text":"3/10"},{"id":"d","text":"8/10"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 10 counters. 2 red + 3 blue = 5 are not green, so the probability is 5/10.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":2},{"colour":"blue","count":3},{"colour":"green","count":5}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'hard', 1, 'Ella is going to flip a coin 50 times. About how many heads should she expect?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"25"'::jsonb, 'Heads has a probability of 1/2, and half of 50 is 25. She may not get exactly 25, but that is the best guess.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which two colours are equally likely on this spinner?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"red and blue"},{"id":"b","text":"red and yellow"},{"id":"c","text":"yellow and blue"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Count the parts: red 3, blue 3 and yellow 2. Red and blue both have 3 out of 8.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","yellow","blue","red","blue","yellow","red","blue"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bag has 12 counters. The probability of picking a red counter is 1/4. How many red counters are in the bag?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"3"'::jsonb, '1/4 of the counters are red, and 1/4 of 12 is 12 ÷ 4 = 3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_hard', 1, 'A spinner has 10 equal parts: 5 green, 3 orange and 2 purple. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The probability of green is 1/2"},{"id":"b","text":"Orange is more likely than purple"},{"id":"c","text":"The probability of purple is 1/5"},{"id":"d","text":"Landing on green is certain"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Green is 5/10 = 1/2, orange (3) beats purple (2), and purple is 2/10 = 1/5. Green is not certain because orange and purple are possible too.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the probability of this spinner landing on yellow?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/2"},{"id":"b","text":"1/4"},{"id":"c","text":"3/4"},{"id":"d","text":"1"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 4 equal parts and 2 are yellow. 2 out of 4 is the same as 1/2.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["green","yellow","green","yellow"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_easy', 1, 'A probability of 0.9 means something is very likely to happen.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Probability goes from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain). 0.9 is very close to 1, so it is very likely.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_easy', 1, 'You roll a normal dice numbered 1 to 6. Which word describes the chance of rolling a 7?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"impossible"},{"id":"b","text":"unlikely"},{"id":"c","text":"even chance"},{"id":"d","text":"certain"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A normal dice has no 7 on it, so rolling a 7 can''t happen. It is impossible, with a probability of 0.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'easy', 1, 'You pick one counter from this bag without looking. What is the probability of picking purple? Write it as a fraction.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2/6","1/3","one third"]'::jsonb, 'There are 4 + 2 = 6 counters and 2 are purple, so the probability is 2/6, which is the same as 1/3.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"green","count":4},{"colour":"purple","count":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'easy', 1, 'You flip a coin. What is the probability of getting tails? Write it as a fraction.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/2","one half","a half"]'::jsonb, 'A coin has 2 sides that are equally likely, and one is tails, so the probability is 1/2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'easy', 1, 'Put these probabilities in order from least likely to most likely.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0"},{"id":"b","text":"1/4"},{"id":"c","text":"1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"3/4"},{"id":"e","text":"1"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, '0 is impossible and 1 is certain. 1/4 is less than 1/2, and 3/4 is more than 1/2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'medium', 1, 'What is the probability of this spinner landing on orange?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5/8"},{"id":"b","text":"3/8"},{"id":"c","text":"5/3"},{"id":"d","text":"1/8"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 8 equal parts. Count the orange ones: 5. So the probability is 5/8.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","orange","orange","red","orange","orange","purple","orange"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'medium', 1, 'You roll a normal dice numbered 1 to 6. Which of these have a probability of 2/6? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rolling a 1 or a 2"},{"id":"b","text":"Rolling a number greater than 4"},{"id":"c","text":"Rolling an odd number"},{"id":"d","text":"Rolling a 5"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '1 or 2 is 2 numbers out of 6, and greater than 4 is 5 or 6, also 2 out of 6. Odd numbers are 1, 3, 5 (3 out of 6), and a 5 is 1 out of 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'medium', 1, 'Isla took a counter from a bag 40 times, putting it back each time. She got blue 10 times. What fraction of her picks were blue?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["10/40","1/4"]'::jsonb, '10 out of 40 picks is 10/40, which is the same as 1/4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'hard', 1, 'A probability of 0.3 is more likely than a probability of 1/2.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '1/2 is the same as 0.5. 0.3 is less than 0.5, so a probability of 0.3 is less likely.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'hard', 1, 'You pick one counter from this bag without looking. Which of these is true?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Blue is twice as likely as red"},{"id":"b","text":"Red is more likely than yellow"},{"id":"c","text":"The probability of yellow is 1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"Picking blue is certain"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 12 counters: 6 blue, 3 red and 3 yellow. Blue has twice as many as red, so it is twice as likely. Red and yellow are equally likely, and yellow is 3/12.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":3},{"colour":"yellow","count":3},{"colour":"blue","count":6}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'hard', 1, 'Max is going to roll a normal dice 60 times. About how many times should he expect to roll a 6?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"10"'::jsonb, 'Rolling a 6 has a probability of 1/6, and 1/6 of 60 is 60 ÷ 6 = 10. He may not get exactly 10, but that is the best guess.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which colour is most likely on this spinner, and what is its probability?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"blue, 4/10"},{"id":"b","text":"blue, 4/6"},{"id":"c","text":"green, 3/10"},{"id":"d","text":"yellow, 3/7"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Count the 10 parts: blue 4, green 3 and yellow 3. Blue is most likely, with 4 out of all 10 parts: 4/10.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["blue","green","blue","yellow","green","blue","yellow","blue","green","yellow"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bag has 20 counters. The probability of picking a green counter is 1/5. How many counters are NOT green?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"16"'::jsonb, '1/5 of 20 is 20 ÷ 5 = 4 green counters. 20 − 4 = 16 counters are not green.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005070330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000050703', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bag has 6 red, 4 blue and 2 white counters. You pick one without looking. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The probability of red is 1/2"},{"id":"b","text":"Blue is twice as likely as white"},{"id":"c","text":"The probability of white is 1/6"},{"id":"d","text":"Picking a black counter is impossible"},{"id":"e","text":"Picking blue is likely"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'There are 12 counters. Red is 6/12 = 1/2, blue (4) is twice white (2), white is 2/12 = 1/6, and there are no black ones. Blue is only 4/12, less than half, so it is unlikely, not likely.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the value of the 7 in 2 745 316?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7000"},{"id":"b","text":"70 000"},{"id":"c","text":"700 000"},{"id":"d","text":"7 000 000"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'Read the places from the right: ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten thousands, hundred thousands. The 7 is in the hundred thousands place, so it is worth 700 000.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_easy', 1, '7 is a prime number.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '7 has exactly two factors, 1 and 7, so it is prime.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_easy', 1, 'What number is the dot on?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["-3","−3"]'::jsonb, 'Each jump is 1. The dot is 2 jumps to the right of −5, so it is on −3.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":-5,"to":5,"jumps":10,"dotAt":2,"labelEvery":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'easy', 1, 'Write four million, sixty thousand and twelve in numerals.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4 060 012","4060012"]'::jsonb, '4 millions, 0 hundred thousands, 6 ten thousands, 0 thousands, 0 hundreds, 1 ten and 2 ones: 4 060 012.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are composite numbers? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"9"},{"id":"b","text":"13"},{"id":"c","text":"15"},{"id":"d","text":"21"},{"id":"e","text":"2"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A composite number has more than two factors. 9 = 3 × 3, 15 = 3 × 5 and 21 = 3 × 7 are composite. 13 and 2 are prime.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'easy', 1, 'Put these temperatures in order from coldest to warmest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"−8°C"},{"id":"b","text":"−3°C"},{"id":"c","text":"0°C"},{"id":"d","text":"4°C"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Below zero, the bigger the number after the minus sign, the colder it is. So −8 is colder than −3, then 0, then 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'medium', 1, 'The triangular numbers start 1, 3, 6, 10. What is the 6th triangular number?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"21"'::jsonb, 'Each one adds the next counting number: 1, 3, 6, 10, then 10 + 5 = 15, then 15 + 6 = 21.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'medium', 1, 'Round 6 482 951 to the nearest hundred thousand.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6 400 000"},{"id":"b","text":"6 500 000"},{"id":"c","text":"6 480 000"},{"id":"d","text":"7 000 000"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '6 482 951 is between 6 400 000 and 6 500 000. The ten thousands digit is 8, so round up to 6 500 000.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'medium', 1, 'The temperature in Orange was −4°C at dawn. It rose 9 degrees by lunch, to ___ °C.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"5"'::jsonb, 'Count up from −4: 4 degrees gets to 0, and 5 more gets to 5. 4 + 5 = 9, so it is 5°C.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'hard', 1, 'Which number is both a square number and a triangular number?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"16"},{"id":"b","text":"36"},{"id":"c","text":"25"},{"id":"d","text":"45"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '36 = 6 × 6, so it is square, and 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 + 7 + 8 = 36, so it is triangular. 16 and 25 are only square; 45 is only triangular.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'hard', 1, 'How many thousands are there in 3 400 000?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"3400"'::jsonb, '3 000 000 is 3000 thousands and 400 000 is 400 thousands, so there are 3400 thousands.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'hard', 1, '−7 is greater than −2.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'On a number line, −7 is further left than −2, so −7 is smaller. −2 is the greater number.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the smallest prime number greater than 50?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"53"'::jsonb, '51 = 3 × 17 and 52 is even, so neither is prime. 53 has only the factors 1 and 53, so it is the answer.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_hard', 1, 'At 6 am in Thredbo it was −6°C. By 2 pm it was 11°C. By how many degrees did the temperature rise?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"17"'::jsonb, 'From −6 up to 0 is 6 degrees, and from 0 up to 11 is 11 degrees. 6 + 11 = 17 degrees.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Every square number has an odd number of factors"},{"id":"b","text":"1 is a prime number"},{"id":"c","text":"Two triangular numbers next to each other add to a square number"},{"id":"d","text":"All prime numbers are odd"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, '16 has the factors 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 (five of them), because 4 × 4 only counts once. 3 + 6 = 9 and 6 + 10 = 16 are squares. 1 has only one factor, so it is not prime, and 2 is a prime that is even.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the value of the 4 in 8 413 562?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4000"},{"id":"b","text":"40 000"},{"id":"c","text":"400 000"},{"id":"d","text":"4 000 000"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'Read the places from the right: ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten thousands, hundred thousands. The 4 is in the hundred thousands place, so it is worth 400 000.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_easy', 1, '15 is a prime number.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '15 has the factors 1, 3, 5 and 15. That is more than two factors, so it is composite, not prime.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_easy', 1, 'What number is the dot on?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["-6","−6"]'::jsonb, 'Each jump is 1. The dot is 4 jumps to the right of −10, so it is on −6.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":-10,"to":0,"jumps":10,"dotAt":4,"labelEvery":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'easy', 1, 'Write seven million, two hundred and five thousand and forty in numerals.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7 205 040","7205040"]'::jsonb, '7 millions, then 205 thousands, then 0 hundreds, 4 tens and 0 ones: 7 205 040.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are prime numbers? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"11"},{"id":"b","text":"21"},{"id":"c","text":"29"},{"id":"d","text":"27"},{"id":"e","text":"31"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, '11, 29 and 31 each have only two factors: 1 and themselves. 21 = 3 × 7 and 27 = 3 × 9, so they are composite.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'easy', 1, 'Put these numbers in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"−9"},{"id":"b","text":"−4"},{"id":"c","text":"−1"},{"id":"d","text":"3"},{"id":"e","text":"6"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Negative numbers are to the left of 0 on a number line. The further from 0 a negative number is, the smaller it is: −9, −4, −1, then 3 and 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'medium', 1, 'The square numbers start 1, 4, 9, 16. What is the 7th square number?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"49"'::jsonb, 'The 7th square number is 7 × 7 = 49. (1 × 1, 2 × 2, 3 × 3 and so on.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'medium', 1, 'Round 3 749 600 to the nearest million.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3 000 000"},{"id":"b","text":"4 000 000"},{"id":"c","text":"3 700 000"},{"id":"d","text":"3 750 000"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '3 749 600 is between 3 000 000 and 4 000 000. The hundred thousands digit is 7, which is 5 or more, so round up to 4 000 000.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'medium', 1, 'At midnight in Cooma it was 3°C. By 5 am the temperature had dropped 8 degrees, to ___ °C.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["-5","−5"]'::jsonb, 'Count down from 3: 3 degrees gets to 0, and 5 more gets to −5. 3 + 5 = 8, so it is −5°C.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'hard', 1, 'How many hundreds are there in 2 750 000?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["27 500","27500"]'::jsonb, 'Divide by 100 to find how many hundreds: 2 750 000 ÷ 100 = 27 500.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these is a triangular number?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"28"},{"id":"b","text":"24"},{"id":"c","text":"32"},{"id":"d","text":"18"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Triangular numbers add the next counting number each time: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, 36. 28 = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 + 7. The others are not on the list.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'hard', 1, '−12 is less than −15.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'On a number line, −15 is further left than −12, so −15 is smaller. −12 is the greater number.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the largest prime number less than 100?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"97"'::jsonb, '99 = 9 × 11 and 98 is even, so neither is prime. 97 cannot be divided evenly by 2, 3, 5 or 7, so it is prime.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_hard', 1, 'The lowest temperature one night at Perisher was −9°C. The next afternoon it was 23 degrees warmer. What was the temperature in the afternoon?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["14","14°C","14 °C"]'::jsonb, 'From −9 up to 0 uses 9 degrees. 23 − 9 = 14 degrees are left, so it reached 14°C.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006040130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Two odd prime numbers always add to an even number"},{"id":"b","text":"Every number ending in 5, except 5 itself, is composite"},{"id":"c","text":"There is a square number between 50 and 60"},{"id":"d","text":"The 10th triangular number is 55"},{"id":"e","text":"49 is a prime number"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Odd + odd is always even. A number ending in 5 can be divided by 5, so if it is bigger than 5 it has more than two factors. 1 + 2 + … + 10 = 55. But 7 × 7 = 49 and 8 × 8 = 64, so no square number is between 50 and 60, and 49 = 7 × 7 is not prime.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_easy', 1, '4.5 + 2.3 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6.8"'::jsonb, 'Add the ones: 4 + 2 = 6. Add the tenths: 5 + 3 = 8 tenths. That makes 6.8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_easy', 1, '25 000 + 13 000 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["38 000","38000"]'::jsonb, 'Think in thousands: 25 thousand + 13 thousand = 38 thousand, which is 38 000.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Taking 3.4 away from 9.6 leaves 6.2.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Ones: 9 − 3 = 6. Tenths: 6 − 4 = 2. So 9.6 − 3.4 = 6.2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'easy', 1, 'Which is the best estimate of 4.9 + 7.2 + 3.1?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"12"},{"id":"b","text":"15"},{"id":"c","text":"17"},{"id":"d","text":"13"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Round each one to the nearest whole: 5 + 7 + 3 = 15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'easy', 1, '12.75 + 6.5 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"19.25"'::jsonb, 'Line up the decimal points: 12.75 + 6.50. 12 + 6 = 18 and 0.75 + 0.5 = 1.25, so 18 + 1.25 = 19.25.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'easy', 1, '8 − 2.65 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"5.35"'::jsonb, 'Count up from 2.65: 0.35 gets to 3, then 5 more gets to 8. 0.35 + 5 = 5.35.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'medium', 1, '246 385 + 178 940 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["425 325","425325"]'::jsonb, 'Add the thousands: 246 000 + 178 000 = 424 000. Add the rest: 385 + 940 = 1325. 424 000 + 1325 = 425 325.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'medium', 1, 'Ella buys a drink for $3.85 and a sandwich for $6.40. How much change does she get from $20?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9.75","$9.75"]'::jsonb, 'She spends $3.85 + $6.40 = $10.25. Then $20 − $10.25 = $9.75 change.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'medium', 1, '15.2 − ___ = 7.85', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"7.35"'::jsonb, 'The missing number is the gap between 7.85 and 15.2. Count up: 0.15 to 8, then 7.2 more to 15.2. 0.15 + 7.2 = 7.35.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'hard', 1, '500 000 − 268 475 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["231 525","231525"]'::jsonb, 'Count up from 268 475: 25 to 268 500, 1500 to 270 000, then 230 000 to 500 000. 25 + 1500 + 230 000 = 231 525.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'hard', 1, 'Leo ran 3.75 km on Monday, 4.6 km on Tuesday and 2.85 km on Wednesday. How far did he run altogether?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"11.2 km"},{"id":"b","text":"10.2 km"},{"id":"c","text":"11.12 km"},{"id":"d","text":"9.96 km"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '3.75 + 4.6 = 8.35, and 8.35 + 2.85 = 11.2 km. Line up the decimal points so 4.6 is 4.60.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these make exactly 10? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"3.65 + 6.35"},{"id":"b","text":"7.2 + 2.08"},{"id":"c","text":"12.5 − 2.5"},{"id":"d","text":"4.75 + 5.25"},{"id":"e","text":"11 − 1.1"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, '3.65 + 6.35, 12.5 − 2.5 and 4.75 + 5.25 all make 10. 7.2 + 2.08 is 9.28 and 11 − 1.1 is 9.9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_hard', 1, 'A water tank held 1250.5 L. 368.75 L was used on Monday and 415.6 L on Tuesday. How many litres are left?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["466.15","466.15 L"]'::jsonb, 'Used: 368.75 + 415.6 = 784.35 L. Left: 1250.5 − 784.35 = 466.15 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_hard', 1, 'A town''s population grew from 98 640 to 103 215. By how many people did it grow?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4575","4 575"]'::jsonb, 'Count up from 98 640: 1360 to 100 000, then 3215 to 103 215. 1360 + 3215 = 4575.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_hard', 1, '4.8 + ___ = 10 − 1.65', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"3.55"'::jsonb, 'First work out the right side: 10 − 1.65 = 8.35. Then 4.8 + ? = 8.35, so ? = 8.35 − 4.8 = 3.55.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_easy', 1, '6.2 + 3.5 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"9.7"'::jsonb, 'Add the ones: 6 + 3 = 9. Add the tenths: 2 + 5 = 7 tenths. That makes 9.7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_easy', 1, '40 000 + 27 000 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["67 000","67000"]'::jsonb, 'Think in thousands: 40 thousand + 27 thousand = 67 thousand, which is 67 000.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Adding 1.6 and 2.7 gives 4.3.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Ones: 1 + 2 = 3. Tenths: 6 + 7 = 13 tenths, which is 1.3. 3 + 1.3 = 4.3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'easy', 1, 'Which is the best estimate of 19.8 − 6.1 + 2.9?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"17"},{"id":"b","text":"15"},{"id":"c","text":"19"},{"id":"d","text":"13"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Round each one to the nearest whole: 20 − 6 + 3 = 17.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'easy', 1, '23.48 + 5.7 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"29.18"'::jsonb, 'Line up the decimal points: 23.48 + 5.70. 23 + 5 = 28 and 0.48 + 0.7 = 1.18, so 28 + 1.18 = 29.18.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'easy', 1, '10 − 4.36 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"5.64"'::jsonb, 'Count up from 4.36: 0.64 gets to 5, then 5 more gets to 10. 0.64 + 5 = 5.64.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'medium', 1, '389 476 + 245 819 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["635 295","635295"]'::jsonb, 'Add the thousands: 389 000 + 245 000 = 634 000. Add the rest: 476 + 819 = 1295. 634 000 + 1295 = 635 295.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'medium', 1, 'Noah buys a meat pie for $4.75 and a juice for $2.90. How much change does he get from $10?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2.35","$2.35"]'::jsonb, 'He spends $4.75 + $2.90 = $7.65. Then $10 − $7.65 = $2.35 change.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'medium', 1, '___ − 3.48 = 6.9', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"10.38"'::jsonb, 'The missing number is 3.48 more than 6.9. Line up the points: 6.90 + 3.48 = 10.38.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'hard', 1, '700 000 − 356 289 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["343 711","343711"]'::jsonb, 'Count up from 356 289: 11 to 356 300, 700 to 357 000, then 343 000 to 700 000. 11 + 700 + 343 000 = 343 711.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'hard', 1, 'Three parcels have masses of 2.85 kg, 1.4 kg and 3.75 kg. What is their total mass?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"7.9 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"6.74 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"8.1 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Line up the decimal points so 1.4 is 1.40. 2.85 + 1.40 = 4.25, and 4.25 + 3.75 = 8.00, which is 8 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these make exactly 5? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2.45 + 2.55"},{"id":"b","text":"1.7 + 3.4"},{"id":"c","text":"8.25 − 3.25"},{"id":"d","text":"6 − 0.1"},{"id":"e","text":"3.08 + 1.92"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, '2.45 + 2.55, 8.25 − 3.25 and 3.08 + 1.92 all make 5. 1.7 + 3.4 is 5.1 and 6 − 0.1 is 5.9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_hard', 1, 'A school raised $2450.80 at its fete. It spent $618.35 on prizes and $295.60 on food. How much money is left?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1536.85","$1536.85"]'::jsonb, 'Spent: $618.35 + $295.60 = $913.95. Left: $2450.80 − $913.95 = $1536.85.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_hard', 1, 'The crowd at a cricket Test was 47 385 on day one and 39 860 on day two. How many more people came on day one?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7525","7 525"]'::jsonb, 'Count up from 39 860: 140 to 40 000, then 7385 to 47 385. 140 + 7385 = 7525.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060103', 'very_hard', 1, '12.5 − ___ = 3.75 + 4.6', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"4.15"'::jsonb, 'First work out the right side: 3.75 + 4.6 = 8.35. Then 12.5 − ? = 8.35, so ? = 12.5 − 8.35 = 4.15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_easy', 1, '3.2 × 3 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"9.6"'::jsonb, '3 × 3 = 9 and 3 × 0.2 = 0.6, so 3.2 × 3 = 9.6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_easy', 1, '6 × 700 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"4200"'::jsonb, '6 × 7 = 42, and 6 × 7 hundreds = 42 hundreds, which is 4200.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_easy', 1, 'When you multiply a number by 100, each digit moves two places to the left.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Multiplying by 10 moves each digit one place left, so multiplying by 100 moves it two places. For example, 3.5 × 100 = 350.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'easy', 1, '4.6 ÷ 2 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"2.3"'::jsonb, 'Half of 4 is 2 and half of 0.6 is 0.3, so 4.6 ÷ 2 = 2.3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'easy', 1, 'How many dots are in this array?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"96"'::jsonb, 'There are 8 rows of 12. 8 × 10 = 80 and 8 × 2 = 16, so 80 + 16 = 96.', '{"kind":"array","rows":8,"columns":12}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'easy', 1, 'What is 2.5 × 10?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"25"},{"id":"b","text":"2.50"},{"id":"c","text":"250"},{"id":"d","text":"0.25"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Multiplying by 10 moves each digit one place to the left: 2.5 becomes 25.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'medium', 1, '324 × 6 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"1944"'::jsonb, '300 × 6 = 1800, 20 × 6 = 120 and 4 × 6 = 24. 1800 + 120 + 24 = 1944.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'medium', 1, 'A packet of 8 muesli bars costs $5.60. How much does each bar cost?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.70","$0.70","0.7","70c","70 cents"]'::jsonb, '$5.60 is 560 cents. 560 ÷ 8 = 70, so each bar costs 70 cents, or $0.70.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'medium', 1, '1356 ÷ 4 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"339"'::jsonb, '1200 ÷ 4 = 300, 120 ÷ 4 = 30 and 36 ÷ 4 = 9. 300 + 30 + 9 = 339.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'hard', 1, '47 × 26 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"1222"'::jsonb, '47 × 20 = 940 and 47 × 6 = 282. 940 + 282 = 1222.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'hard', 1, '7.35 × 4 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"29.4"'::jsonb, '7 × 4 = 28 and 0.35 × 4 = 1.4. 28 + 1.4 = 29.4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'hard', 1, '214 students are going on an excursion. Each bus seats 45 students. How many buses are needed?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4"},{"id":"b","text":"5"},{"id":"c","text":"4.75"},{"id":"d","text":"6"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '4 buses seat 4 × 45 = 180 students, which leaves 34 without a seat. They need one more bus, so 5 buses.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_hard', 1, 'A ribbon 12.6 m long is cut into 7 equal pieces. How long is each piece in metres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1.8","1.8 m"]'::jsonb, '12.6 is 126 tenths. 126 ÷ 7 = 18, so each piece is 18 tenths of a metre, which is 1.8 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_hard', 1, 'Netball uniforms cost $38.50 each. How much do 9 uniforms cost?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["346.50","$346.50","346.5"]'::jsonb, '9 × $38 = $342 and 9 × $0.50 = $4.50. $342 + $4.50 = $346.50.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these give the same answer as 36 × 25? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"9 × 100"},{"id":"b","text":"36 × 100 ÷ 4"},{"id":"c","text":"18 × 50"},{"id":"d","text":"30 × 25 + 6"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '36 × 25 = 900. 9 × 100 = 900, 3600 ÷ 4 = 900 and 18 × 50 = 900 (halve one, double the other). 30 × 25 + 6 is only 756.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_easy', 1, '4.1 × 2 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"8.2"'::jsonb, '2 × 4 = 8 and 2 × 0.1 = 0.2, so 4.1 × 2 = 8.2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_easy', 1, '8 × 600 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"4800"'::jsonb, '8 × 6 = 48, and 8 × 6 hundreds = 48 hundreds, which is 4800.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_easy', 1, 'When you divide a number by 10, each digit moves one place to the right.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Dividing by 10 makes each digit worth ten times less, so it moves one place right. For example, 45 ÷ 10 = 4.5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'easy', 1, '9.3 ÷ 3 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"3.1"'::jsonb, '9 ÷ 3 = 3 and 0.3 ÷ 3 = 0.1, so 9.3 ÷ 3 = 3.1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'easy', 1, 'How many dots are in this array?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"77"'::jsonb, 'There are 7 rows of 11. 7 × 10 = 70 and 7 × 1 = 7, so 70 + 7 = 77.', '{"kind":"array","rows":7,"columns":11}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'easy', 1, 'What is 0.46 × 100?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"46"},{"id":"b","text":"4.6"},{"id":"c","text":"460"},{"id":"d","text":"0.0046"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Multiplying by 100 moves each digit two places to the left: 0.46 becomes 46.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'medium', 1, '517 × 7 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"3619"'::jsonb, '500 × 7 = 3500, 10 × 7 = 70 and 7 × 7 = 49. 3500 + 70 + 49 = 3619.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'medium', 1, 'A pack of 6 bottles of water costs $8.70. How much does each bottle cost?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1.45","$1.45"]'::jsonb, '$8.70 is 870 cents. 870 ÷ 6 = 145, so each bottle costs 145 cents, or $1.45.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'medium', 1, '2184 ÷ 6 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"364"'::jsonb, '1800 ÷ 6 = 300, 360 ÷ 6 = 60 and 24 ÷ 6 = 4. 300 + 60 + 4 = 364.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'hard', 1, '38 × 54 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"2052"'::jsonb, '38 × 50 = 1900 and 38 × 4 = 152. 1900 + 152 = 2052.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'hard', 1, '6.25 × 8 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"50"'::jsonb, '6 × 8 = 48 and 0.25 × 8 = 2. 48 + 2 = 50.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'hard', 1, 'A farmer packs 350 eggs into cartons of 12. How many cartons can be completely filled?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"29"},{"id":"b","text":"30"},{"id":"c","text":"29.17"},{"id":"d","text":"28"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '29 × 12 = 348, which leaves 2 eggs. 2 eggs can''t fill a carton, so only 29 cartons are full.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_hard', 1, '9 friends share a $157.50 restaurant bill equally. How much does each friend pay?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["17.50","$17.50","17.5"]'::jsonb, '$157.50 ÷ 9: 9 × $17 = $153, which leaves $4.50. $4.50 ÷ 9 = $0.50. So each pays $17.50.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_hard', 1, 'A cricket bat costs $64.95. How much do 6 bats cost?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["389.70","$389.70","389.7"]'::jsonb, '6 × $65 = $390, but each bat is 5 cents less than $65. 6 × 5c = 30c, so $390 − $0.30 = $389.70.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060102', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these give the same answer as 48 × 15? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"24 × 30"},{"id":"b","text":"48 × 10 + 48 × 5"},{"id":"c","text":"50 × 15 − 2"},{"id":"d","text":"16 × 45"},{"id":"e","text":"40 × 15 + 8"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '48 × 15 = 720. 24 × 30 = 720 (halve one, double the other), 480 + 240 = 720 and 16 × 45 = 720 (divide one by 3, multiply the other by 3). 50 × 15 − 2 = 748 and 40 × 15 + 8 = 608.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_easy', 1, '3 + 4 × 2 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"11"'::jsonb, 'Multiply before you add: 4 × 2 = 8, then 3 + 8 = 11.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_easy', 1, 'In 10 − 2 × 3, you do the multiplication first.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Multiplication and division come before addition and subtraction, so do 2 × 3 = 6 first, then 10 − 6 = 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_easy', 1, '6 × ___ = 42', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"7"'::jsonb, '6 × 7 = 42.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'easy', 1, '(5 + 3) × 4 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"32"'::jsonb, 'Brackets first: 5 + 3 = 8. Then 8 × 4 = 32.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'easy', 1, '20 − 12 ÷ 4 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"17"'::jsonb, 'Divide before you subtract: 12 ÷ 4 = 3, then 20 − 3 = 17.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'easy', 1, 'Which number sentence shows 6 packs of 4 juice boxes, plus 3 single juice boxes?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6 × 4 + 3"},{"id":"b","text":"6 + 4 × 3"},{"id":"c","text":"(6 + 3) × 4"},{"id":"d","text":"6 × (4 + 3)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '6 packs of 4 is 6 × 4, then add the 3 single boxes: 6 × 4 + 3 = 27.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'medium', 1, '18 ÷ (2 + 4) × 5 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"15"'::jsonb, 'Brackets first: 2 + 4 = 6. Then work left to right: 18 ÷ 6 = 3, and 3 × 5 = 15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'medium', 1, '___ × 6 = 8 × 9', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"12"'::jsonb, '8 × 9 = 72, so ? × 6 = 72. 72 ÷ 6 = 12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'medium', 1, '4 + 6 × 2 = 20', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Multiply first: 6 × 2 = 12, then 4 + 12 = 16, not 20. You only get 20 if you add first, which is the wrong order.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'hard', 1, '50 − (3 + 7) × 4 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"10"'::jsonb, 'Brackets: 3 + 7 = 10. Multiply: 10 × 4 = 40. Subtract: 50 − 40 = 10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'hard', 1, 'Where should the brackets go to make 2 + 6 × 3 − 1 equal 23?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(2 + 6) × 3 − 1"},{"id":"b","text":"2 + 6 × (3 − 1)"},{"id":"c","text":"(2 + 6 × 3) − 1"},{"id":"d","text":"2 + (6 × 3 − 1)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '(2 + 6) × 3 − 1 = 8 × 3 − 1 = 24 − 1 = 23. The others give 14, 19 and 19.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'hard', 1, '36 ÷ ___ + 5 = 9', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"9"'::jsonb, 'The division part must make 4, because 4 + 5 = 9. 36 ÷ 9 = 4, so the missing number is 9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Zoo tickets cost $12 for adults and $7 for children. What is the total cost for 3 adults and 5 children?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["71","$71"]'::jsonb, '3 × 12 + 5 × 7 = 36 + 35 = 71, so it costs $71.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_hard', 1, '(___ + 4) × 3 = 45', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"11"'::jsonb, 'Something times 3 is 45, so the bracket makes 15. ? + 4 = 15, so ? = 11.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 24? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"4 + 4 × 5"},{"id":"b","text":"(4 + 4) × 5"},{"id":"c","text":"48 ÷ (4 − 2)"},{"id":"d","text":"48 ÷ 4 − 2"},{"id":"e","text":"30 − 12 ÷ 2"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, '4 + 20 = 24, 48 ÷ 2 = 24 and 30 − 6 = 24. But (4 + 4) × 5 = 40 and 48 ÷ 4 − 2 = 12 − 2 = 10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_easy', 1, '5 + 6 × 3 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"23"'::jsonb, 'Multiply before you add: 6 × 3 = 18, then 5 + 18 = 23.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_easy', 1, 'In (8 + 2) × 5, you work out the brackets first.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Brackets always come first: 8 + 2 = 10, then 10 × 5 = 50.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_easy', 1, '___ × 8 = 56', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"7"'::jsonb, '7 × 8 = 56.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'easy', 1, '(9 − 4) × 6 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"30"'::jsonb, 'Brackets first: 9 − 4 = 5. Then 5 × 6 = 30.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'easy', 1, '15 + 24 ÷ 6 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"19"'::jsonb, 'Divide before you add: 24 ÷ 6 = 4, then 15 + 4 = 19.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'easy', 1, 'Which number sentence shows 5 bags of 6 apples, with 4 apples eaten?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5 × 6 − 4"},{"id":"b","text":"5 − 6 × 4"},{"id":"c","text":"(5 − 4) × 6"},{"id":"d","text":"5 × (6 − 4)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '5 bags of 6 is 5 × 6 = 30 apples, then take away the 4 eaten: 5 × 6 − 4 = 26.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'medium', 1, '48 ÷ (2 + 6) × 7 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"42"'::jsonb, 'Brackets first: 2 + 6 = 8. Then work left to right: 48 ÷ 8 = 6, and 6 × 7 = 42.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'medium', 1, '___ × 4 = 6 × 10', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"15"'::jsonb, '6 × 10 = 60, so ? × 4 = 60. 60 ÷ 4 = 15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'medium', 1, '12 − 4 × 2 = 16', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Multiply first: 4 × 2 = 8, then 12 − 8 = 4, not 16. You only get 16 if you subtract first, which is the wrong order.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'hard', 1, '60 − (8 − 3) × 9 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"15"'::jsonb, 'Brackets: 8 − 3 = 5. Multiply: 5 × 9 = 45. Subtract: 60 − 45 = 15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'hard', 1, 'Where should the brackets go to make 3 + 5 × 4 − 2 equal 13?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3 + 5 × (4 − 2)"},{"id":"b","text":"(3 + 5) × 4 − 2"},{"id":"c","text":"(3 + 5) × (4 − 2)"},{"id":"d","text":"(3 + 5 × 4) − 2"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '3 + 5 × (4 − 2) = 3 + 5 × 2 = 3 + 10 = 13. The others give 30, 16 and 21.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'hard', 1, '48 ÷ ___ − 2 = 6', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, 'The division part must make 8, because 8 − 2 = 6. 48 ÷ 6 = 8, so the missing number is 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Movie tickets cost $15 for adults and $9 for children. A family buys 2 adult and 4 child tickets and pays with $100. How much change do they get?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["34","$34"]'::jsonb, 'The tickets cost 2 × 15 + 4 × 9 = 30 + 36 = 66 dollars. Change: 100 − 66 = 34, so $34.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_hard', 1, '(___ − 5) × 4 = 36', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"14"'::jsonb, 'Something times 4 is 36, so the bracket makes 9. ? − 5 = 9, so ? = 14.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006010130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 30? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"6 + 4 × 6"},{"id":"b","text":"(6 + 4) × 6"},{"id":"c","text":"90 ÷ (6 − 3)"},{"id":"d","text":"90 ÷ 6 − 3"},{"id":"e","text":"50 − 40 ÷ 2"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, '6 + 24 = 30, 90 ÷ 3 = 30 and 50 − 20 = 30. But (6 + 4) × 6 = 60 and 90 ÷ 6 − 3 = 15 − 3 = 12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020401', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the value of the 5 in 3.285?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5 tenths"},{"id":"b","text":"5 hundredths"},{"id":"c","text":"5 thousandths"},{"id":"d","text":"5 ones"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'After the decimal point the places are tenths, hundredths, then thousandths. The 5 is third, so it is 5 thousandths.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020402', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_easy', 1, '0.6 is the same as 0.600.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Zeros at the end after the decimal point don''t change the value. 6 tenths is 600 thousandths.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020403', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which is bigger, 0.45 or 0.405?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.45"},{"id":"b","text":"0.405"},{"id":"c","text":"They are the same"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Write both with three places: 0.450 and 0.405. 450 thousandths is more than 405 thousandths.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020404', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'easy', 1, 'Put these decimals in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.307"},{"id":"b","text":"0.37"},{"id":"c","text":"0.703"},{"id":"d","text":"0.73"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Write them all with three places: 0.307, 0.370, 0.703, 0.730. Then compare like whole numbers.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020405', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'easy', 1, 'Write 47 thousandths as a decimal.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.047",".047"]'::jsonb, '47 thousandths is 0 tenths, 4 hundredths and 7 thousandths: 0.047.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020406', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'easy', 1, 'What number is the dot on?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.36",".36"]'::jsonb, 'From 0.3 to 0.4 is split into 10 jumps, so each jump is 0.01. The dot is 6 jumps past 0.3, which is 0.36.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":0.3,"to":0.4,"jumps":10,"dotAt":6}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020407', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are greater than 0.5? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.51"},{"id":"b","text":"0.499"},{"id":"c","text":"0.6"},{"id":"d","text":"0.095"},{"id":"e","text":"0.505"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Compare the tenths first. 0.51, 0.6 and 0.505 all have 5 or more tenths and something extra. 0.499 and 0.095 are less than 0.5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020408', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'medium', 1, '0.8 = ___ thousandths', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"800"'::jsonb, '0.8 is 8 tenths. Each tenth is 100 thousandths, so 8 tenths is 800 thousandths.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020409', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'medium', 1, 'Which decimal is between 1.25 and 1.26?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1.255"},{"id":"b","text":"1.3"},{"id":"c","text":"1.205"},{"id":"d","text":"1.265"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Write the ends as 1.250 and 1.260. 1.255 is between them. 1.205 is too small, and 1.265 and 1.3 are too big.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020410', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'hard', 1, 'Round 4.386 to the nearest hundredth.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"4.39"'::jsonb, '4.386 is between 4.38 and 4.39. The thousandths digit is 6, so round up to 4.39.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020411', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'hard', 1, 'In a 100 m race, the times were 14.08 s, 14.8 s, 14.008 s and 14.18 s. Which time was the fastest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"14.08 s"},{"id":"b","text":"14.8 s"},{"id":"c","text":"14.008 s"},{"id":"d","text":"14.18 s"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'The fastest time is the smallest number. With three places they are 14.080, 14.800, 14.008 and 14.180. The smallest is 14.008 s.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020412', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'hard', 1, '0.09 is greater than 0.1 because 9 is greater than 1.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '0.1 is 1 tenth, which is 10 hundredths. 0.09 is only 9 hundredths, so 0.1 is greater.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020413', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these masses in order from heaviest to lightest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"1.5 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"1.45 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"1.405 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"1.054 kg"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Write them with three places: 1.500, 1.450, 1.405, 1.054. From biggest to smallest: 1.5, 1.45, 1.405, 1.054.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020414', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_hard', 1, 'Use the digits 2, 7 and 5 once each to make the largest number less than 1 with three decimal places.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.752",".752"]'::jsonb, 'To be less than 1 it starts with 0. Put the biggest digit in the tenths, the next in the hundredths and the smallest in the thousandths: 0.752.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020415', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_hard', 1, 'What number is exactly halfway between 2.35 and 2.4?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"2.375"'::jsonb, 'Write them as 2.350 and 2.400. They are 50 thousandths apart, and half of that is 25 thousandths. 2.350 + 0.025 = 2.375.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020416', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the value of the 8 in 5.183?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8 tenths"},{"id":"b","text":"8 hundredths"},{"id":"c","text":"8 thousandths"},{"id":"d","text":"8 ones"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'After the decimal point the places are tenths, hundredths, then thousandths. The 8 is second, so it is 8 hundredths.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020417', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_easy', 1, '0.7 is greater than 0.69.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Write both with two places: 0.70 and 0.69. 70 hundredths is more than 69 hundredths.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020418', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write 3 tenths and 4 thousandths as a decimal.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.304",".304"]'::jsonb, '3 tenths, 0 hundredths and 4 thousandths. Remember the 0 in the hundredths place: 0.304.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020419', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'easy', 1, 'Put these decimals in order from largest to smallest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.92"},{"id":"b","text":"0.9"},{"id":"c","text":"0.209"},{"id":"d","text":"0.092"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Write them all with three places: 0.920, 0.900, 0.209, 0.092. Then compare them like whole numbers.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020420', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'easy', 1, 'What number is the dot on?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2.54"]'::jsonb, 'From 2.5 to 2.6 is split into 10 jumps, so each jump is 0.01. The dot is 4 jumps past 2.5, which is 2.54.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":2.5,"to":2.6,"jumps":10,"dotAt":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020421', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'easy', 1, '0.035 = ___ thousandths', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"35"'::jsonb, '0.035 is 0 tenths, 3 hundredths and 5 thousandths. 3 hundredths is 30 thousandths, so it is 35 thousandths altogether.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020422', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are less than 0.3? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.29"},{"id":"b","text":"0.031"},{"id":"c","text":"0.31"},{"id":"d","text":"0.299"},{"id":"e","text":"0.3"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Write them with three places: 0.290, 0.031, 0.310, 0.299 and 0.300. Only 0.290, 0.031 and 0.299 are less than 0.300.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020423', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'medium', 1, 'Which decimal is between 0.7 and 0.71?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.705"},{"id":"b","text":"0.75"},{"id":"c","text":"0.071"},{"id":"d","text":"0.715"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Write the ends as 0.700 and 0.710. 0.705 is between them. 0.071 is too small, and 0.715 and 0.75 are too big.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020424', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'medium', 1, 'Round 7.649 to the nearest tenth.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"7.6"'::jsonb, '7.649 is between 7.6 and 7.7. Look at the hundredths digit: it is 4, which is less than 5, so round down to 7.6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020425', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'hard', 1, 'What is 0.01 more than 3.296?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"3.306"'::jsonb, '0.01 is 1 hundredth. 3.296 has 9 hundredths, and one more makes 10 hundredths, which is 1 tenth. So 3.296 + 0.01 = 3.306.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020426', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'hard', 1, 'In the long jump, Ava jumped 3.405 m, Bree 3.45 m, Chloe 3.054 m and Dani 3.4 m. Who jumped the furthest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Ava"},{"id":"b","text":"Bree"},{"id":"c","text":"Chloe"},{"id":"d","text":"Dani"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'With three places the jumps are 3.405, 3.450, 3.054 and 3.400. The biggest is 3.450, so Bree jumped furthest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020427', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'hard', 1, '0.125 is greater than 0.13 because it has more digits.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'More digits does not mean bigger. Write 0.13 as 0.130. 130 thousandths is more than 125 thousandths, so 0.13 is greater.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020428', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these decimals in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"2.009"},{"id":"b","text":"2.09"},{"id":"c","text":"2.1"},{"id":"d","text":"2.19"},{"id":"e","text":"2.901"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Write them with three places: 2.009, 2.090, 2.100, 2.190, 2.901. Then compare them like whole numbers.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020429', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_hard', 1, 'I am a number with three decimal places. Rounded to the nearest hundredth I am 4.27. I am as large as I can be. What number am I?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"4.274"'::jsonb, 'Numbers from 4.265 up to 4.274 round to 4.27. 4.275 would round up to 4.28, so the largest is 4.274.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020430', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060204', 'very_hard', 1, 'The number line from 1.2 to 1.3 is split into 4 equal jumps. What number is the dot on?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"1.275"'::jsonb, '1.2 to 1.3 is 0.1, which is 100 thousandths. 100 ÷ 4 = 25, so each jump is 0.025. Three jumps is 0.075, and 1.2 + 0.075 = 1.275.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":1.2,"to":1.3,"jumps":4,"dotAt":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_easy', 1, '2/5 + 1/5 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3/5"]'::jsonb, 'The pieces are the same size (fifths), so add the tops: 2 fifths + 1 fifth = 3 fifths.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_easy', 1, '3/8 is less than 1/2.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '1/2 is the same as 4/8, and 3/8 is less than 4/8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which fraction is equal to the shaded part?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2/3"},{"id":"b","text":"4/10"},{"id":"c","text":"3/4"},{"id":"d","text":"1/6"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '4 of 6 equal parts are shaded: 4/6. Divide the top and bottom by 2 to get 2/3.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"circle","parts":6,"shaded":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'easy', 1, '1/2 + 1/4 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3/4"]'::jsonb, '1/2 is the same as 2/4. 2/4 + 1/4 = 3/4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'easy', 1, 'Which fraction is greater, 2/3 or 5/6?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2/3"},{"id":"b","text":"5/6"},{"id":"c","text":"They are the same"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Change 2/3 into sixths: 2/3 = 4/6. 5/6 is more than 4/6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'easy', 1, '3/5 = ___/10', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, '5 × 2 = 10, so multiply the top by 2 as well: 3 × 2 = 6. 3/5 = 6/10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'medium', 1, 'Put these fractions in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/10"},{"id":"b","text":"2/5"},{"id":"c","text":"1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"3/4"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'As decimals: 3/10 = 0.3, 2/5 = 0.4, 1/2 = 0.5 and 3/4 = 0.75.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'medium', 1, '7/8 − 1/4 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5/8"]'::jsonb, '1/4 is the same as 2/8. 7/8 − 2/8 = 5/8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'medium', 1, 'What number is the dot on? Write it as a fraction or mixed number.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1 1/3","4/3"]'::jsonb, '0 to 2 is split into 6 jumps, so each whole has 3 jumps and each jump is 1/3. The dot is 4 jumps along: 4/3, which is 1 1/3.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":0,"to":2,"jumps":6,"dotAt":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'hard', 1, '2/3 + 5/6 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1 1/2","3/2","9/6","1 3/6"]'::jsonb, '2/3 is the same as 4/6. 4/6 + 5/6 = 9/6, which is 1 whole and 3/6, or 1 1/2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these fractions are greater than 3/5? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2/3"},{"id":"b","text":"7/10"},{"id":"c","text":"1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"5/8"},{"id":"e","text":"4/10"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '3/5 = 0.6. 2/3 is about 0.67, 7/10 = 0.7 and 5/8 = 0.625 are all bigger. 1/2 = 0.5 and 4/10 = 0.4 are smaller.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'hard', 1, 'Sam ate 3/8 of a pizza and Ruby ate 1/4 of it. What fraction of the pizza is left?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/8"},{"id":"b","text":"5/8"},{"id":"c","text":"4/12"},{"id":"d","text":"1/2"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1/4 = 2/8, so they ate 3/8 + 2/8 = 5/8. The whole pizza is 8/8, so 8/8 − 5/8 = 3/8 is left.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_hard', 1, '2 1/4 − 5/8 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1 5/8","13/8"]'::jsonb, 'Change to eighths: 2 1/4 = 18/8. 18/8 − 5/8 = 13/8, which is 1 5/8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these fractions in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"5/8"},{"id":"b","text":"2/3"},{"id":"c","text":"3/4"},{"id":"d","text":"5/6"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Each is short of 1 by a piece: 3/8, 1/3, 1/4 and 1/6. The smaller the missing piece, the bigger the fraction: 5/8, 2/3, 3/4, 5/6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Sophie has 1 1/2 cups of flour. She uses 3/4 of a cup for a cake and 5/8 of a cup for biscuits. How much flour is left?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/8","1/8 cup"]'::jsonb, 'Change to eighths: 1 1/2 = 12/8, 3/4 = 6/8. She uses 6/8 + 5/8 = 11/8. 12/8 − 11/8 = 1/8 of a cup is left.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_easy', 1, '3/7 + 2/7 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5/7"]'::jsonb, 'The pieces are the same size (sevenths), so add the tops: 3 sevenths + 2 sevenths = 5 sevenths.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_easy', 1, '5/6 is more than 1/2.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '1/2 is the same as 3/6, and 5/6 is more than 3/6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which fraction is equal to the shaded part?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/4"},{"id":"b","text":"6/10"},{"id":"c","text":"2/3"},{"id":"d","text":"1/8"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '6 of 8 equal parts are shaded: 6/8. Divide the top and bottom by 2 to get 3/4.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"circle","parts":8,"shaded":6}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'easy', 1, '1/3 + 1/6 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/2","3/6"]'::jsonb, '1/3 is the same as 2/6. 2/6 + 1/6 = 3/6, which simplifies to 1/2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'easy', 1, 'Which fraction is greater, 3/4 or 5/8?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/4"},{"id":"b","text":"5/8"},{"id":"c","text":"They are the same"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Change 3/4 into eighths: 3/4 = 6/8. 6/8 is more than 5/8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'easy', 1, '2/3 = ___/12', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"8"'::jsonb, '3 × 4 = 12, so multiply the top by 4 as well: 2 × 4 = 8. 2/3 = 8/12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'medium', 1, 'Put these fractions in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/5"},{"id":"b","text":"3/10"},{"id":"c","text":"1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"4/5"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them to tenths: 1/5 = 2/10, 3/10, 1/2 = 5/10 and 4/5 = 8/10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'medium', 1, '5/6 − 1/3 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/2","3/6"]'::jsonb, '1/3 is the same as 2/6. 5/6 − 2/6 = 3/6, which simplifies to 1/2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'medium', 1, 'What number is the dot on? Write it as a fraction or mixed number.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2 1/4","9/4"]'::jsonb, '0 to 3 is split into 12 jumps, so each whole has 4 jumps and each jump is 1/4. The dot is 9 jumps along: 9/4, which is 2 1/4.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":0,"to":3,"jumps":12,"dotAt":9}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'hard', 1, '3/4 + 5/8 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1 3/8","11/8"]'::jsonb, '3/4 is the same as 6/8. 6/8 + 5/8 = 11/8, which is 1 whole and 3/8: 1 3/8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these fractions are less than 2/3? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/5"},{"id":"b","text":"5/6"},{"id":"c","text":"1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"7/12"},{"id":"e","text":"3/4"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, '2/3 is about 0.67. 3/5 = 0.6, 1/2 = 0.5 and 7/12 (less than 8/12) are smaller. 5/6 and 3/4 are bigger than 2/3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'hard', 1, 'Lily read 1/3 of her book on Saturday and 5/12 of it on Sunday. What fraction of the book is left to read?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/4"},{"id":"b","text":"3/4"},{"id":"c","text":"6/15"},{"id":"d","text":"1/3"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1/3 = 4/12, so she read 4/12 + 5/12 = 9/12. The whole book is 12/12, so 12/12 − 9/12 = 3/12 = 1/4 is left.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_hard', 1, '3 1/3 − 5/6 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2 1/2","5/2","15/6","2 3/6"]'::jsonb, 'Change to sixths: 3 1/3 = 20/6. 20/6 − 5/6 = 15/6, which is 2 3/6, or 2 1/2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these fractions in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/5"},{"id":"b","text":"5/8"},{"id":"c","text":"2/3"},{"id":"d","text":"7/10"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'As decimals: 3/5 = 0.6, 5/8 = 0.625, 2/3 is about 0.667 and 7/10 = 0.7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Jack has a plank 2 m long. He cuts off 3/4 of a metre, then another 7/8 of a metre. How much of the plank is left?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3/8","3/8 m"]'::jsonb, 'Change to eighths: 2 m = 16/8 and 3/4 = 6/8. He cuts off 6/8 + 7/8 = 13/8. 16/8 − 13/8 = 3/8 of a metre is left.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is 1/5 of 30?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, 'Find one fifth by sharing into 5 equal groups: 30 ÷ 5 = 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_easy', 1, '1/4 of an hour is 15 minutes.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'An hour is 60 minutes, and 60 ÷ 4 = 15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many dots make up 1/3 of this array?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"8"'::jsonb, 'There are 3 equal rows, so one row is 1/3 of the array. Each row has 8 dots.', '{"kind":"array","rows":3,"columns":8}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'easy', 1, 'What is 3/4 of 32?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"24"'::jsonb, 'One quarter of 32 is 32 ÷ 4 = 8. Three quarters is 3 × 8 = 24.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'easy', 1, 'What is 2/5 of $50?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$20"},{"id":"b","text":"$10"},{"id":"c","text":"$25"},{"id":"d","text":"$40"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'One fifth of $50 is $10, so two fifths is 2 × $10 = $20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'easy', 1, '1/10 of 1 kg = ___ g', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"100"'::jsonb, '1 kg is 1000 g, and 1000 ÷ 10 = 100 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'medium', 1, 'What is 5/6 of 42?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"35"'::jsonb, 'One sixth of 42 is 42 ÷ 6 = 7. Five sixths is 5 × 7 = 35.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'medium', 1, 'How many minutes is 2/3 of an hour?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["40","40 minutes"]'::jsonb, 'One third of 60 minutes is 20 minutes, so two thirds is 40 minutes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'medium', 1, 'The jug shows how much juice Kai poured. He drinks 1/3 of it. How many millilitres does he drink?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["200","200 mL"]'::jsonb, 'The jug shows 600 mL. One third of 600 is 600 ÷ 3 = 200 mL.', '{"kind":"jug","max":1000,"value":600,"step":100,"unit":"mL"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'hard', 1, 'A netball team played 20 games and won 3/5 of them. There were no draws. How many games did they lose?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"8"'::jsonb, 'One fifth of 20 is 4, so they won 3 × 4 = 12 games. 20 − 12 = 8 games lost.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these is the biggest amount?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/8 of 64"},{"id":"b","text":"2/3 of 30"},{"id":"c","text":"3/4 of 28"},{"id":"d","text":"5/6 of 24"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '3/8 of 64 = 3 × 8 = 24. 2/3 of 30 = 20. 3/4 of 28 = 21. 5/6 of 24 = 20. The biggest is 24.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'hard', 1, 'How many metres is 7/10 of 2 km?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1400","1400 m"]'::jsonb, '2 km is 2000 m. One tenth of 2000 is 200, so seven tenths is 7 × 200 = 1400 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_hard', 1, '2/5 of a number is 18. What is the number?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"45"'::jsonb, 'If 2 fifths is 18, then 1 fifth is 18 ÷ 2 = 9. The whole number is 5 fifths: 5 × 9 = 45.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Ari had $60. He spent 1/4 of it on a book, then 1/3 of what was left on lunch. How much money does he have now?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30","$30"]'::jsonb, 'The book cost 1/4 of $60 = $15, leaving $45. Lunch cost 1/3 of $45 = $15, leaving $45 − $15 = $30.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_hard', 1, 'The scale shows a bag of flour. A recipe uses 3/4 of the bag. How many grams of flour does the recipe use?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1125","1125 g"]'::jsonb, 'The scale shows 1.5 kg, which is 1500 g. One quarter of 1500 is 375, so three quarters is 3 × 375 = 1125 g.', '{"kind":"scale","max":2,"value":1.5,"step":0.25,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is 1/6 of 42?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"7"'::jsonb, 'Find one sixth by sharing into 6 equal groups: 42 ÷ 6 = 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_easy', 1, '1/2 of a kilometre is 500 metres.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A kilometre is 1000 metres, and half of 1000 is 500.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many dots make up 1/4 of this array?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, 'There are 4 equal rows, so one row is 1/4 of the array. Each row has 6 dots.', '{"kind":"array","rows":4,"columns":6}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'easy', 1, 'What is 2/3 of 27?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"18"'::jsonb, 'One third of 27 is 27 ÷ 3 = 9. Two thirds is 2 × 9 = 18.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'easy', 1, 'What is 3/10 of $80?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$24"},{"id":"b","text":"$8"},{"id":"c","text":"$30"},{"id":"d","text":"$27"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'One tenth of $80 is $8, so three tenths is 3 × $8 = $24.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'easy', 1, '1/4 of 1 L = ___ mL', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"250"'::jsonb, '1 L is 1000 mL, and 1000 ÷ 4 = 250 mL.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'medium', 1, 'What is 7/8 of 56?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"49"'::jsonb, 'One eighth of 56 is 56 ÷ 8 = 7. Seven eighths is 7 × 7 = 49.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'medium', 1, 'How many minutes is 3/4 of 2 hours?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["90","90 minutes"]'::jsonb, '2 hours is 120 minutes. One quarter of 120 is 30, so three quarters is 3 × 30 = 90 minutes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'medium', 1, 'The scale shows a bag of rice. Mum uses 3/4 of it. How many grams does she use?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["600","600 g"]'::jsonb, 'The scale shows 800 g. One quarter of 800 is 200, so three quarters is 3 × 200 = 600 g.', '{"kind":"scale","max":1000,"value":800,"step":100,"unit":"g"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'hard', 1, 'There are 30 students in 6M. 2/5 of them walk to school. How many students do not walk?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"18"'::jsonb, 'One fifth of 30 is 6, so 2 × 6 = 12 walk. 30 − 12 = 18 do not walk.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these is the smallest amount?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2/3 of 33"},{"id":"b","text":"3/5 of 40"},{"id":"c","text":"5/8 of 32"},{"id":"d","text":"3/4 of 24"}]'::jsonb, '"d"'::jsonb, '2/3 of 33 = 2 × 11 = 22. 3/5 of 40 = 3 × 8 = 24. 5/8 of 32 = 5 × 4 = 20. 3/4 of 24 = 3 × 6 = 18. The smallest is 18.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'hard', 1, 'How many centimetres is 3/5 of 4 m?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["240","240 cm"]'::jsonb, '4 m is 400 cm. One fifth of 400 is 80, so three fifths is 3 × 80 = 240 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_hard', 1, '3/4 of a number is 27. What is the number?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"36"'::jsonb, 'If 3 quarters is 27, then 1 quarter is 27 ÷ 3 = 9. The whole number is 4 quarters: 4 × 9 = 36.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Grace had 48 stickers. She gave 1/3 of them to her brother, then gave 1/4 of what was left to a friend. How many stickers does she have now?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"24"'::jsonb, '1/3 of 48 is 16, leaving 48 − 16 = 32. 1/4 of 32 is 8, leaving 32 − 8 = 24 stickers.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060202', 'very_hard', 1, 'The jug shows how much water Ari has. He pours out 5/6 of it. How many millilitres does he pour out?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1000","1000 mL"]'::jsonb, 'The jug shows 1.2 L, which is 1200 mL. One sixth of 1200 is 200, so five sixths is 5 × 200 = 1000 mL.', '{"kind":"jug","max":2,"value":1.2,"step":0.2,"unit":"L"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_easy', 1, '50% is the same as which fraction?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/2"},{"id":"b","text":"1/5"},{"id":"c","text":"1/50"},{"id":"d","text":"1/4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Per cent means out of 100. 50 out of 100 is half, so 50% = 1/2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is 10% of 80?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"8"'::jsonb, '10% is one tenth. 80 ÷ 10 = 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_easy', 1, '25% is the same as 0.25.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '25% means 25 hundredths, and 25 hundredths is written 0.25.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'easy', 1, 'What percentage of the bar is shaded?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["75","75%"]'::jsonb, '3 of the 4 equal parts are shaded, which is 3/4. 3/4 = 75/100, so 75% is shaded.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"bar","parts":4,"shaded":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'easy', 1, 'What is 25% of 60?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"15"'::jsonb, '25% is one quarter. 60 ÷ 4 = 15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'easy', 1, '0.1 = ___%', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"10"'::jsonb, '0.1 is one tenth, which is 10 hundredths, so it is 10%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'medium', 1, 'A $40 T-shirt has 10% off. How many dollars is the discount?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4","$4"]'::jsonb, '10% is one tenth. One tenth of $40 is $4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 75%? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/4"},{"id":"b","text":"0.75"},{"id":"c","text":"0.075"},{"id":"d","text":"75/100"},{"id":"e","text":"7/5"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '75% means 75 out of 100: 75/100, which is 0.75 and also 3/4. 0.075 is only 75 thousandths, and 7/5 is more than 1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'medium', 1, 'Put these in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/10"},{"id":"b","text":"0.2"},{"id":"c","text":"25%"},{"id":"d","text":"3/4"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to decimals: 1/10 = 0.1, 0.2, 25% = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'hard', 1, 'Runners cost $80. They are on sale at 25% off. What is the sale price in dollars?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["60","$60"]'::jsonb, '25% is one quarter, and a quarter of $80 is $20. $80 − $20 = $60.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'hard', 1, 'Which is greater: 30% of 50, or 50% of 30?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"30% of 50"},{"id":"b","text":"50% of 30"},{"id":"c","text":"They are the same"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, '30% of 50 is 3 × 5 = 15 (10% of 50 is 5). 50% of 30 is half of 30, which is also 15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'hard', 1, 'What is 75% of 120?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"90"'::jsonb, '25% of 120 is 120 ÷ 4 = 30. 75% is three times that: 3 × 30 = 90.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_hard', 1, 'A school has 400 students. 45% of them walk to school. How many students do not walk?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"220"'::jsonb, 'If 45% walk, then 100% − 45% = 55% do not. 1% of 400 is 4, so 55% is 55 × 4 = 220.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_hard', 1, 'A $120 bike is 25% off at one shop. Another shop sells the same bike for $95. Which is cheaper, and by how much?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The 25% off shop, by $5"},{"id":"b","text":"The $95 shop, by $5"},{"id":"c","text":"The 25% off shop, by $25"},{"id":"d","text":"They cost the same"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '25% of $120 is $30, so the sale price is $120 − $30 = $90. $90 is $5 cheaper than $95.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Mia scored 18 out of 20 on a spelling test. What percentage did she score?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["90","90%"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply the top and bottom by 5 to get out of 100: 18/20 = 90/100, which is 90%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_easy', 1, '25% is the same as which fraction?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/4"},{"id":"b","text":"1/25"},{"id":"c","text":"1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"2/5"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '25% means 25 out of 100. Four lots of 25 make 100, so 25% is one quarter: 1/4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is 50% of 64?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"32"'::jsonb, '50% is one half. Half of 64 is 32.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_easy', 1, '100% of something means all of it.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Per cent means out of 100, so 100% is 100 out of 100: the whole amount.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'easy', 1, 'What percentage of the bar is shaded?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30","30%"]'::jsonb, '3 of the 10 equal parts are shaded, which is 3/10. 3/10 = 30/100, so 30% is shaded.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"bar","parts":10,"shaded":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'easy', 1, 'What is 10% of 350?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"35"'::jsonb, '10% is one tenth. 350 ÷ 10 = 35.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'easy', 1, '0.4 = ___%', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"40"'::jsonb, '0.4 is 4 tenths, which is 40 hundredths, so it is 40%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'medium', 1, 'A $60 video game has 25% off. How many dollars is the discount?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["15","$15"]'::jsonb, '25% is one quarter. One quarter of $60 is $60 ÷ 4 = $15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 20%? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/5"},{"id":"b","text":"0.2"},{"id":"c","text":"0.02"},{"id":"d","text":"20/100"},{"id":"e","text":"2/5"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '20% means 20 out of 100: 20/100, which is 0.2 and also 1/5. 0.02 is only 2 hundredths, and 2/5 is 40%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'medium', 1, 'Put these in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.05"},{"id":"b","text":"1/10"},{"id":"c","text":"30%"},{"id":"d","text":"1/2"},{"id":"e","text":"0.8"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to decimals: 0.05, 1/10 = 0.1, 30% = 0.3, 1/2 = 0.5 and 0.8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'hard', 1, 'A $90 jacket is on sale at 10% off. What is the sale price in dollars?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["81","$81"]'::jsonb, '10% of $90 is $9. Take the discount off: $90 − $9 = $81.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'hard', 1, 'Which is greater: 20% of 80, or 80% of 25?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"20% of 80"},{"id":"b","text":"80% of 25"},{"id":"c","text":"They are the same"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '20% of 80: 10% is 8, so 20% is 16. 80% of 25: 10% is 2.5, so 80% is 8 × 2.5 = 20. 20 is greater than 16.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'hard', 1, 'What is 30% of 150?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"45"'::jsonb, '10% of 150 is 15. 30% is three times that: 3 × 15 = 45.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_hard', 1, 'There are 28 students in 6K. 25% of them play cricket. How many students in 6K do not play cricket?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"21"'::jsonb, '25% is one quarter, and a quarter of 28 is 7. So 7 play cricket and 28 − 7 = 21 do not.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_hard', 1, 'A shop puts the price of a $40 hat up by 10%. Later it takes 10% off the new price. What is the final price?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$39.60"},{"id":"b","text":"$40"},{"id":"c","text":"$44"},{"id":"d","text":"$36"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '10% of $40 is $4, so the new price is $44. Then 10% of $44 is $4.40, so the final price is $44 − $4.40 = $39.60. It does not go back to $40, because the second 10% is of a bigger amount.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006020330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Josh got 21 out of 25 on a maths quiz. What percentage did he get?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["84","84%"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply the top and bottom by 4 to get out of 100: 21/25 = 84/100, which is 84%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many metres are in 1 kilometre?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"100"},{"id":"b","text":"1000"},{"id":"c","text":"10"},{"id":"d","text":"10 000"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Kilo means one thousand, so 1 kilometre is 1000 metres.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the ribbon on the ruler. How long is it in millimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["90","90 mm"]'::jsonb, 'The ribbon reaches the 9 cm mark. Each centimetre is 10 mm, so 9 × 10 = 90 mm.', '{"kind":"ruler","cm":15,"length":9,"object":"ribbon"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_easy', 1, '1 centimetre is the same length as 10 millimetres.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Every centimetre is split into 10 millimetres.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'easy', 1, '3.5 m = ___ cm', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["350","350 cm"]'::jsonb, 'There are 100 cm in 1 metre, so 3.5 × 100 = 350 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. What is its perimeter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["23","23 m"]'::jsonb, 'Add all four sides: 7.5 + 4 + 7.5 + 4 = 23 m.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":7,"height":4,"top":"7.5 m","side":"4 m"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'easy', 1, 'Which unit is best for measuring the distance from Sydney to Newcastle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"kilometres"},{"id":"b","text":"metres"},{"id":"c","text":"centimetres"},{"id":"d","text":"millimetres"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Sydney to Newcastle is a long way (more than 100 km), so kilometres are the best unit.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'medium', 1, '2.75 km = ___ m', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["2750","2750 m"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1000 m in 1 km, so 2.75 × 1000 = 2750 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'medium', 1, 'Put these lengths in order from shortest to longest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.4 m"},{"id":"b","text":"45 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"480 mm"},{"id":"d","text":"0.5 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to centimetres: 0.4 m = 40 cm, 45 cm, 480 mm = 48 cm and 0.5 m = 50 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the shape. All its corners are right angles. What is its perimeter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["28","28 m"]'::jsonb, 'Add every side: 8 + 3 + 4 + 3 + 4 + 6 = 28 m.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[8,3],[4,3],[4,6],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["8 m","3 m","4 m","3 m","4 m","6 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'hard', 1, 'A square paddock has a perimeter of 1.2 km. How long is each side, in metres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["300","300 m"]'::jsonb, '1.2 km is 1200 m. A square has 4 equal sides, so 1200 ÷ 4 = 300 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are the same length as 1.5 m? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"150 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"1500 mm"},{"id":"c","text":"15 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"0.0015 km"},{"id":"e","text":"0.15 km"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '1.5 m × 100 = 150 cm, 1.5 m × 1000 = 1500 mm, and 1.5 ÷ 1000 = 0.0015 km. 15 cm and 0.15 km (150 m) are wrong.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'hard', 1, 'Mia runs 4 laps of a 400 m track, then runs another 1.3 km. How far does she run altogether?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2.9 km"},{"id":"b","text":"1.7 km"},{"id":"c","text":"5.3 km"},{"id":"d","text":"2.09 km"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '4 × 400 m = 1600 m, and 1.3 km = 1300 m. 1600 + 1300 = 2900 m, which is 2.9 km.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the shape. All its corners are right angles, but some sides are not labelled. What is its perimeter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["34","34 m"]'::jsonb, 'The missing sides are 10 − 6 = 4 m, 7 − 4 = 3 m and 6 m. So 10 + 4 + 4 + 3 + 6 + 7 = 34 m. (The perimeter is the same as a 10 m by 7 m rectangle: 2 × 17 = 34 m.)', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[10,4],[6,4],[6,7],[0,7]],"sideLabels":["10 m","4 m","","","","7 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangular garden bed is 3.6 m long and 85 cm wide. What is its perimeter in centimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["890","890 cm"]'::jsonb, '3.6 m = 360 cm. The perimeter is 360 + 85 + 360 + 85 = 890 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Ella''s rope is 3.08 m long and Sam''s rope is 308 mm long. Ella''s rope is 10 times as long as Sam''s.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '3.08 m = 3080 mm, and 3080 is 10 times 308. So Ella''s rope is 10 times as long.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many millimetres are in 1 metre?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1000"},{"id":"b","text":"100"},{"id":"c","text":"10"},{"id":"d","text":"10 000"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1 metre is 100 cm and each centimetre is 10 mm, so 1 metre is 100 × 10 = 1000 mm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_easy', 1, '1.2 km is longer than 1100 m.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '1.2 km is 1.2 × 1000 = 1200 m, and 1200 m is longer than 1100 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which unit is best for measuring the thickness of a $1 coin?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"millimetres"},{"id":"b","text":"centimetres"},{"id":"c","text":"metres"},{"id":"d","text":"kilometres"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A coin is very thin, only a few millimetres thick, so millimetres are the best unit.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'easy', 1, '4.2 cm = ___ mm', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["42","42 mm"]'::jsonb, 'There are 10 mm in 1 cm, so 4.2 × 10 = 42 mm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the pencil on the ruler. How long is it in metres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.13",".13","0.13 m"]'::jsonb, 'The pencil reaches the 13 cm mark. There are 100 cm in a metre, so 13 cm = 13 ÷ 100 = 0.13 m.', '{"kind":"ruler","cm":15,"length":13,"object":"pencil"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. What is its perimeter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30","30 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Add all four sides: 9.5 + 5.5 + 9.5 + 5.5 = 30 cm.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":9,"height":5,"top":"9.5 cm","side":"5.5 cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'medium', 1, '1450 m = ___ km', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["1.45","1.45 km"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1000 m in 1 km, so 1450 ÷ 1000 = 1.45 km.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'medium', 1, 'Put these lengths in order from shortest to longest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.07 m"},{"id":"b","text":"8 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"95 mm"},{"id":"d","text":"0.1 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to millimetres: 0.07 m = 70 mm, 8 cm = 80 mm, 95 mm and 0.1 m = 100 mm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the shape. All its corners are right angles. What is its perimeter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["32","32 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Add every side: 9 + 4 + 4 + 3 + 5 + 7 = 32 cm.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[9,0],[9,4],[5,4],[5,7],[0,7]],"sideLabels":["9 cm","4 cm","4 cm","3 cm","5 cm","7 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'hard', 1, 'A rectangular paddock is 0.4 km long and 150 m wide. What is its perimeter, in metres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1100","1100 m"]'::jsonb, '0.4 km is 400 m. The perimeter is 400 + 150 + 400 + 150 = 1100 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are the same length as 2.4 km? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2400 m"},{"id":"b","text":"240 000 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"24 000 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"2 400 000 mm"},{"id":"e","text":"240 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '2.4 km × 1000 = 2400 m. 2400 m × 100 = 240 000 cm, and 2400 m × 1000 = 2 400 000 mm. 24 000 cm is only 240 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'hard', 1, 'Jo walks 650 m to the bus stop, rides 3.8 km on the bus, then walks 450 m to school. How far does she travel altogether?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4.9 km"},{"id":"b","text":"4.09 km"},{"id":"c","text":"5.9 km"},{"id":"d","text":"1.48 km"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Change to metres: 650 + 3800 + 450 = 4900 m, which is 4.9 km.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the shape. All its corners are right angles, and the gap cut into the top is 3 m deep. What is its perimeter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["38","38 m"]'::jsonb, 'The gap is 10 − 3 − 3 = 4 m wide, and both of its sides are 3 m. So 10 + 6 + 3 + 3 + 4 + 3 + 3 + 6 = 38 m.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[10,6],[7,6],[7,3],[3,3],[3,6],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["10 m","6 m","3 m","","","","3 m","6 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangular swimming pool is 25 m long and 850 cm wide. What is its perimeter in metres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["67","67 m"]'::jsonb, '850 cm = 8.5 m. The perimeter is 25 + 8.5 + 25 + 8.5 = 67 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060501', 'very_hard', 1, 'A 2.5 km bike track is 50 times as long as a 50 m pool.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '2.5 km = 2500 m, and 2500 ÷ 50 = 50. So the track is 50 times as long.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"24 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"20 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"10 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"24 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area of a rectangle = length × width = 6 × 4 = 24 cm². Area is in square units, so not just cm.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":6,"height":4,"top":"6 cm","side":"4 cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Area is measured in square units, such as cm² and m².', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Area counts how many squares cover a surface, so it uses square units.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the shape?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["21","21 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'Split it into two rectangles: the bottom part is 6 × 2 = 12 squares and the top part is 3 × 3 = 9 squares. 12 + 9 = 21 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[7,1],[7,3],[4,3],[4,6],[1,6]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'easy', 1, '1 hectare = ___ m²', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["10 000","10000"]'::jsonb, 'A hectare is a square 100 m by 100 m, and 100 × 100 = 10 000 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["28","28 m²"]'::jsonb, 'Area = length × width = 8 × 3.5 = 28 m².', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":8,"height":3,"top":"8 m","side":"3.5 m"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'easy', 1, 'Which unit is best for measuring the area of a farm?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"hectares"},{"id":"b","text":"square centimetres"},{"id":"c","text":"millimetres"},{"id":"d","text":"millilitres"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A farm is very big, so hectares are best. Millimetres measure length and millilitres measure capacity.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the shape. All its corners are right angles. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["43","43 m²"]'::jsonb, 'Split it into two rectangles. The bottom one is 9 × 3 = 27 m². The top one is 4 × 4 = 16 m². 27 + 16 = 43 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[9,0],[9,3],[4,3],[4,7],[0,7]],"sideLabels":["9 m","3 m","5 m","4 m","4 m","7 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'medium', 1, 'A rectangle has an area of 54 cm² and a width of 6 cm. How long is it?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9","9 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Length × width = area, so length = 54 ÷ 6 = 9 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'medium', 1, 'Which rectangles have an area of 36 m²? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"4 m × 9 m"},{"id":"b","text":"6 m × 6 m"},{"id":"c","text":"10 m × 8 m"},{"id":"d","text":"12 m × 3 m"},{"id":"e","text":"7 m × 5 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '4 × 9, 6 × 6 and 12 × 3 all make 36. 10 × 8 = 80 (its perimeter is 36, not its area) and 7 × 5 = 35.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'hard', 1, 'A paddock is 400 m long and 250 m wide. What is its area in hectares?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["10","10 ha","10 hectares"]'::jsonb, '400 × 250 = 100 000 m². Each hectare is 10 000 m², so 100 000 ÷ 10 000 = 10 hectares.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the shape. All its corners are right angles. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"48 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"60 m²"},{"id":"c","text":"52 m²"},{"id":"d","text":"40 m²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The whole rectangle would be 10 × 6 = 60 m². The gap cut out of the top is 4 × 3 = 12 m². 60 − 12 = 48 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[10,6],[7,6],[7,3],[3,3],[3,6],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["10 m","6 m","3 m","3 m","4 m","3 m","3 m","6 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'hard', 1, 'If you double both the length and the width of a rectangle, its area doubles too.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Try 2 m × 3 m = 6 m². Doubled, it is 4 m × 6 m = 24 m², which is 4 times as big, not 2 times.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A backyard is 15 m by 10 m. A 6 m by 4 m swimming pool takes up part of it, and the rest is grass. Turf costs $9 per square metre. How much does it cost to turf the grass?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1134","$1134"]'::jsonb, 'Backyard: 15 × 10 = 150 m². Pool: 6 × 4 = 24 m². Grass: 150 − 24 = 126 m². Cost: 126 × $9 = $1134.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A square has a perimeter of 28 cm. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"49 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"28 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"196 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"56 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each side is 28 ÷ 4 = 7 cm, so the area is 7 × 7 = 49 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these areas in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"8000 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"1 hectare"},{"id":"c","text":"1.5 hectares"},{"id":"d","text":"20 000 m²"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to m²: 8000 m², 1 ha = 10 000 m², 1.5 ha = 15 000 m², then 20 000 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"35 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"24 m²"},{"id":"c","text":"12 m²"},{"id":"d","text":"35 m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area of a rectangle = length × width = 7 × 5 = 35 m². 24 m is the perimeter, and area needs square units.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":7,"height":5,"top":"7 m","side":"5 m"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_easy', 1, 'A square with sides of 1 m has an area of 1 m².', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '1 m × 1 m = 1 m². That is exactly what one square metre means.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Each small square is 1 cm². What is the area of the shape?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["16","16 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'Split it into two rectangles: the bottom part is 5 × 2 = 10 squares and the top part is 2 × 3 = 6 squares. 10 + 6 = 16 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[2,1],[7,1],[7,3],[4,3],[4,6],[2,6]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'easy', 1, '3 hectares = ___ m²', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["30 000","30000"]'::jsonb, '1 hectare is 10 000 m², so 3 hectares is 3 × 10 000 = 30 000 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["40.5","40.5 m²"]'::jsonb, 'Area = length × width = 9 × 4.5 = 36 + 4.5 = 40.5 m².', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":9,"height":4,"top":"9 m","side":"4.5 m"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'easy', 1, 'Which unit is best for measuring the area of a postage stamp?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"square centimetres"},{"id":"b","text":"square metres"},{"id":"c","text":"hectares"},{"id":"d","text":"centimetres"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A stamp is small, so square centimetres are best. Centimetres alone measure length, not area.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the shape. All its corners are right angles. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["64","64 m²"]'::jsonb, 'Split it into two rectangles. The bottom one is 10 × 4 = 40 m². The top one is 6 × 4 = 24 m². 40 + 24 = 64 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[10,4],[6,4],[6,8],[0,8]],"sideLabels":["10 m","4 m","4 m","4 m","6 m","8 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'medium', 1, 'A rectangular rug has an area of 72 m² and a length of 9 m. How wide is it?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","8 m"]'::jsonb, 'Length × width = area, so width = 72 ÷ 9 = 8 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'medium', 1, 'Which rectangles have an area of 48 cm²? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"6 cm × 8 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"4 cm × 12 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"10 cm × 14 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"16 cm × 3 cm"},{"id":"e","text":"7 cm × 7 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '6 × 8, 4 × 12 and 16 × 3 all make 48. 10 × 14 = 140 (its perimeter is 48, not its area) and 7 × 7 = 49.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'hard', 1, 'A park is 500 m long and 300 m wide. What is its area in hectares?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["15","15 ha","15 hectares"]'::jsonb, '500 × 300 = 150 000 m². Each hectare is 10 000 m², so 150 000 ÷ 10 000 = 15 hectares.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the T shape. All its corners are right angles. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"39 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"63 m²"},{"id":"c","text":"36 m²"},{"id":"d","text":"45 m²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The top bar is 9 × 3 = 27 m². The stem is 3 m wide and 4 m tall: 3 × 4 = 12 m². 27 + 12 = 39 m². (63 m² is the whole 9 by 7 box, gaps and all.)', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,4],[0,7],[9,7],[9,4],[6,4],[6,0],[3,0],[3,4]],"sideLabels":["3 m","9 m","3 m","3 m","4 m","3 m","4 m","3 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'hard', 1, 'Two rectangles with the same perimeter always have the same area.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'A 1 m × 5 m rectangle and a 3 m × 3 m square both have a perimeter of 12 m, but their areas are 5 m² and 9 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A classroom floor is 9 m by 7 m. A 3 m by 2 m storeroom in one corner is tiled, and the rest of the floor is carpeted. Carpet costs $35 per square metre. How much does the carpet cost?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1995","$1995"]'::jsonb, 'Whole floor: 9 × 7 = 63 m². Storeroom: 3 × 2 = 6 m². Carpet: 63 − 6 = 57 m². Cost: 57 × $35 = $1995.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A square has an area of 64 m². What is its perimeter?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"32 m"},{"id":"b","text":"16 m"},{"id":"c","text":"64 m"},{"id":"d","text":"256 m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '8 × 8 = 64, so each side is 8 m. The perimeter is 4 × 8 = 32 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these areas in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"5000 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"0.75 hectares"},{"id":"c","text":"1.2 hectares"},{"id":"d","text":"15 000 m²"},{"id":"e","text":"2 hectares"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to m²: 5000, 0.75 ha = 7500, 1.2 ha = 12 000, 15 000 and 2 ha = 20 000 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_easy', 1, 'This triangle is half of a rectangle. Each square is 1 cm². What is the area of the triangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'The rectangle around it is 6 × 4 = 24 cm². The triangle is half of that: 24 ÷ 2 = 12 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[7,1],[1,5]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_easy', 1, 'A triangle has half the area of a rectangle with the same base and height.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Two copies of the triangle fit together to make the rectangle, so the triangle is half of it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which rule gives the area of a parallelogram?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"base × perpendicular height"},{"id":"b","text":"base + height"},{"id":"c","text":"2 × (base + height)"},{"id":"d","text":"base × slanting side"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Cut the triangle off one end and move it to the other end. You get a rectangle, so the area is base × perpendicular height.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the parallelogram. Each square is 1 cm². What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["28","28 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'The base is 7 squares and the perpendicular height is 4 squares. Area = 7 × 4 = 28 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[8,1],[10,5],[3,5]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'easy', 1, 'Area of a triangle = ½ × base × ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["height","perpendicular height"]'::jsonb, 'A triangle is half a rectangle, so its area is ½ × base × height.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'easy', 1, 'The base of this triangle is 10 cm and its perpendicular height is 6 cm. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"30 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"60 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"16 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"32 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area = ½ × base × height = ½ × 10 × 6 = 30 cm². 60 cm² forgets to halve.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[11,1],[4,7]],"sideLabels":["10 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'medium', 1, 'A parallelogram has a base of 12 m and a perpendicular height of 5 m. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["60","60 m²"]'::jsonb, 'Area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height = 12 × 5 = 60 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'medium', 1, 'Each square is 1 m². What is the area of the triangle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"20 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"40 m²"},{"id":"c","text":"13 m²"},{"id":"d","text":"25 m²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The base is 8 squares long and the triangle is 5 squares high. Area = ½ × 8 × 5 = 20 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[9,1],[6,6]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'medium', 1, 'A parallelogram with a base of 8 cm and a slanting side of 5 cm must have an area of 40 cm².', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'You need the perpendicular (straight up) height, not the slanting side. The height is less than 5 cm, so the area is less than 40 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'hard', 1, 'A triangle has an area of 24 cm² and a base of 8 cm. What is its perpendicular height?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","6 cm"]'::jsonb, '½ × 8 × height = 24, so 4 × height = 24 and the height is 6 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these shapes have an area of 18 cm²? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"a triangle with base 6 cm and height 6 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"a parallelogram with base 6 cm and height 3 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"a triangle with base 9 cm and height 2 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"a rectangle 9 cm by 2 cm"},{"id":"e","text":"a triangle with base 3 cm and height 6 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '½ × 6 × 6 = 18, 6 × 3 = 18 and 9 × 2 = 18. The triangles with base 9, height 2 and base 3, height 6 are only 9 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'hard', 1, 'This shape is a rectangle with a triangle on top. The rectangle is 8 m by 4 m, and the whole shape is 7 m tall. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["44","44 m²"]'::jsonb, 'Rectangle: 8 × 4 = 32 m². The triangle has base 8 m and height 7 − 4 = 3 m, so ½ × 8 × 3 = 12 m². 32 + 12 = 44 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[9,1],[9,5],[5,8],[1,5]],"sideLabels":["8 m","4 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_hard', 1, 'A parallelogram and a triangle both have a base of 10 cm. The parallelogram''s height is 4 cm. The two shapes have the same area. What is the triangle''s height?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"4 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"2 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"20 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The parallelogram is 10 × 4 = 40 cm². For the triangle, ½ × 10 × height = 40, so 5 × height = 40 and the height is 8 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_hard', 1, 'This shape is a 10 m by 6 m rectangle with a triangle cut off the top. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["45","45 m²"]'::jsonb, 'The rectangle is 10 × 6 = 60 m². The cut-off triangle has base 10 m and height 6 − 3 = 3 m, so ½ × 10 × 3 = 15 m². 60 − 15 = 45 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[10,6],[0,3]],"sideLabels":["10 m","6 m","","3 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these shapes in order from smallest area to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"triangle: base 6 cm, height 5 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"parallelogram: base 4 cm, height 5 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"square: sides 5 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"triangle: base 10 cm, height 6 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Work out each area: ½ × 6 × 5 = 15, 4 × 5 = 20, 5 × 5 = 25 and ½ × 10 × 6 = 30 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_easy', 1, 'This triangle is half of a rectangle. Each square is 1 cm². What is the area of the triangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["16","16 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'The rectangle around it is 8 × 4 = 32 cm². The triangle is half of that: 32 ÷ 2 = 16 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[9,1],[9,5]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_easy', 1, 'To find the area of a parallelogram, multiply the base by the slanting side.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'You multiply the base by the perpendicular height, measured straight up at a right angle to the base, not along the slanting side.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_easy', 1, 'A rectangle has an area of 30 cm². What is the area of a triangle with the same base and height?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"15 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"30 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"60 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"10 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A triangle is half of the rectangle with the same base and height, so 30 ÷ 2 = 15 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the parallelogram. Each square is 1 cm². What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30","30 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'The base is 6 squares and the perpendicular height is 5 squares. Area = 6 × 5 = 30 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[7,1],[9,6],[3,6]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'easy', 1, 'A triangle with a base of 8 cm and a perpendicular height of 5 cm has an area of ___ cm².', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["20","20 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'Area = ½ × base × height = ½ × 8 × 5 = 20 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'easy', 1, 'The base of this triangle is 8 m and its perpendicular height is 6 m. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"24 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"48 m²"},{"id":"c","text":"14 m²"},{"id":"d","text":"28 m²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area = ½ × base × height = ½ × 8 × 6 = 24 m². 48 m² forgets to halve.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[9,1],[3,7]],"sideLabels":["8 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'medium', 1, 'A parallelogram has a base of 9 cm and a perpendicular height of 7 cm. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["63","63 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'Area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height = 9 × 7 = 63 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'medium', 1, 'Each square is 1 m². What is the area of the triangle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"18 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"36 m²"},{"id":"c","text":"12 m²"},{"id":"d","text":"27 m²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The base is 6 squares long. The height is measured straight up from the base line to the top corner: 6 squares. Area = ½ × 6 × 6 = 18 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[2,1],[8,1],[11,7]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'medium', 1, 'Two triangles with the same base and the same perpendicular height always have the same area, even if they look different.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The area only depends on ½ × base × height. If those are the same, the areas are the same, however the top corner leans.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'hard', 1, 'A parallelogram has an area of 56 m² and a base of 8 m. What is its perpendicular height?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","7 m"]'::jsonb, 'Base × height = area, so 8 × height = 56 and the height is 56 ÷ 8 = 7 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these shapes have an area of 24 cm²? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"a triangle with base 8 cm and height 6 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"a parallelogram with base 6 cm and height 4 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"a triangle with base 12 cm and height 2 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"a parallelogram with base 12 cm and height 2 cm"},{"id":"e","text":"a triangle with base 6 cm and height 4 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '½ × 8 × 6 = 24, 6 × 4 = 24 and 12 × 2 = 24. The triangles with base 12, height 2 and base 6, height 4 are only 12 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'hard', 1, 'This shape is an 8 m by 5 m rectangle with a right-angled triangle joined to its side. What is the area of the whole shape?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["47.5","47.5 m²"]'::jsonb, 'Rectangle: 8 × 5 = 40 m². The triangle has a base of 11 − 8 = 3 m and a height of 5 m, so ½ × 3 × 5 = 7.5 m². 40 + 7.5 = 47.5 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[11,5],[0,5]],"sideLabels":["8 m","","11 m","5 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_hard', 1, 'A triangle has a base of 12 cm and a perpendicular height of 5 cm. A parallelogram has the same area and a base of 6 cm. What is the parallelogram''s height?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"10 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"2.5 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"15 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The triangle is ½ × 12 × 5 = 30 cm². For the parallelogram, 6 × height = 30, so the height is 5 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_hard', 1, 'This shape is a 10 m by 6 m rectangle with a triangle cut off one corner. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["54","54 m²"]'::jsonb, 'The rectangle is 10 × 6 = 60 m². The cut-off triangle is 10 − 6 = 4 m across and 6 − 3 = 3 m high, so ½ × 4 × 3 = 6 m². 60 − 6 = 54 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[10,3],[6,6],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["10 m","3 m","","6 m","6 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these shapes in order from smallest area to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"parallelogram: base 3 cm, height 4 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"triangle: base 8 cm, height 4 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"triangle: base 5 cm, height 8 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"parallelogram: base 7 cm, height 3 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Work out each area: 3 × 4 = 12, ½ × 8 × 4 = 16, ½ × 5 × 8 = 20 and 7 × 3 = 21 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050401', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_easy', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. What is the volume of the block?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12 cm³"]'::jsonb, 'Each layer is 3 × 2 = 6 cubes, and there are 2 layers. 6 × 2 = 12 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":3,"width":2,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050402', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_easy', 1, 'A 1 cm³ container holds exactly 1 mL of water.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '1 cubic centimetre and 1 millilitre are the same amount of space.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050403', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many millilitres of water are in the jug?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1500 mL"},{"id":"b","text":"150 mL"},{"id":"c","text":"15 mL"},{"id":"d","text":"1.5 mL"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The water is at 1.5 L. There are 1000 mL in a litre, so 1.5 × 1000 = 1500 mL.', '{"kind":"jug","max":2,"value":1.5,"step":0.25,"unit":"L"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050404', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'easy', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. What is the volume of the block?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"24 cm³"},{"id":"b","text":"9 cm³"},{"id":"c","text":"52 cm³"},{"id":"d","text":"14 cm³"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Volume = length × width × height = 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":4,"width":3,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050405', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'easy', 1, '2.5 L = ___ mL', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["2500","2500 mL"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1000 mL in 1 L, so 2.5 × 1000 = 2500 mL.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050406', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'easy', 1, 'Which unit is best for measuring the volume of a shipping container?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"cubic metres (m³)"},{"id":"b","text":"cubic centimetres (cm³)"},{"id":"c","text":"millilitres (mL)"},{"id":"d","text":"millimetres (mm)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A shipping container is huge, so cubic metres are best. Millimetres measure length, not volume.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050407', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'medium', 1, 'A rectangular prism is 8 cm long, 5 cm wide and 3 cm high. What is its volume?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["120","120 cm³"]'::jsonb, 'Volume = 8 × 5 × 3 = 120 cm³.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050408', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'medium', 1, 'A box is the same size as this block, and each small cube is 1 cm³. How many millilitres of water would fill the box?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["60","60 mL"]'::jsonb, 'The volume is 5 × 3 × 4 = 60 cm³. Since 1 cm³ holds 1 mL, the box holds 60 mL.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":5,"width":3,"height":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050409', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'medium', 1, 'Put these amounts in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"450 mL"},{"id":"b","text":"0.5 L"},{"id":"c","text":"750 cm³"},{"id":"d","text":"1.2 L"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to mL: 450 mL, 0.5 L = 500 mL, 750 cm³ = 750 mL, 1.2 L = 1200 mL.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050410', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'hard', 1, 'A fish tank is 50 cm long, 30 cm wide and 40 cm high. How many litres of water does it hold when full?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["60","60 L"]'::jsonb, 'Volume = 50 × 30 × 40 = 60 000 cm³ = 60 000 mL. There are 1000 mL in a litre, so it holds 60 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050411', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these rectangular prisms have a volume of 48 cm³? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"4 cm × 4 cm × 3 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"6 cm × 2 cm × 4 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"8 cm × 3 cm × 2 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"12 cm × 2 cm × 3 cm"},{"id":"e","text":"10 cm × 4 cm × 2 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '4 × 4 × 3, 6 × 2 × 4 and 8 × 3 × 2 all make 48. 12 × 2 × 3 = 72 and 10 × 4 × 2 = 80.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050412', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'hard', 1, 'A water tank holds 1 m³. How many litres is that?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1000 L"},{"id":"b","text":"100 L"},{"id":"c","text":"10 L"},{"id":"d","text":"1 000 000 L"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1 m³ is 100 cm × 100 cm × 100 cm = 1 000 000 cm³, which is 1 000 000 mL. Divide by 1000 to get 1000 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050413', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_hard', 1, 'This block is made of 1 cm cubes. How many more cubes must be added on top to make it 6 cubes high?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30"]'::jsonb, 'Each layer has 5 × 2 = 10 cubes. It is 3 layers high now, so 3 more layers are needed: 3 × 10 = 30 cubes.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":5,"width":2,"height":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050414', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangular prism has a volume of 360 cm³. It is 10 cm long and 6 cm wide. How high is it?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","6 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The bottom layer is 10 × 6 = 60 cm³. 360 ÷ 60 = 6, so it is 6 cm high.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050415', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_hard', 1, 'A 2 L bottle of water is poured into cups that each hold 150 mL. How many cups can be filled right to the top?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"13"},{"id":"b","text":"14"},{"id":"c","text":"12"},{"id":"d","text":"15"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '2 L = 2000 mL. 13 × 150 = 1950 mL, and there isn''t enough left (50 mL) to fill a 14th cup. So 13 cups.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050416', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_easy', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. What is the volume of the block?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["18","18 cm³"]'::jsonb, 'Each layer is 3 × 3 = 9 cubes, and there are 2 layers. 9 × 2 = 18 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":3,"width":3,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050417', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_easy', 1, '1 litre is the same amount as 1000 cm³.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '1 L is 1000 mL, and each millilitre takes up 1 cm³, so 1 L = 1000 cm³.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050418', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many litres of water are in the jug?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.4 L"},{"id":"b","text":"4 L"},{"id":"c","text":"40 L"},{"id":"d","text":"0.04 L"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The water is at 400 mL. There are 1000 mL in a litre, so 400 ÷ 1000 = 0.4 L.', '{"kind":"jug","max":1000,"value":400,"step":100,"unit":"mL"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050419', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'easy', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. What is the volume of the block?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"20 cm³"},{"id":"b","text":"9 cm³"},{"id":"c","text":"24 cm³"},{"id":"d","text":"10 cm³"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Volume = length × width × height = 5 × 2 × 2 = 20 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":5,"width":2,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050420', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'easy', 1, '750 mL = ___ L', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["0.75",".75","0.75 L"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1000 mL in 1 L, so 750 ÷ 1000 = 0.75 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050421', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'easy', 1, 'Which unit is best for measuring a dose of cough medicine?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"millilitres (mL)"},{"id":"b","text":"litres (L)"},{"id":"c","text":"cubic metres (m³)"},{"id":"d","text":"kilograms (kg)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A dose of medicine is a small amount of liquid, so millilitres are best. Kilograms measure mass.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050422', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'medium', 1, 'A shoebox is 12 cm long, 5 cm wide and 4 cm high. What is its volume?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["240","240 cm³"]'::jsonb, 'Volume = 12 × 5 × 4 = 60 × 4 = 240 cm³.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050423', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'medium', 1, 'A container is the same size as this block, and each small cube is 1 cm³. How many millilitres of water would fill it?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["48","48 mL"]'::jsonb, 'The volume is 6 × 4 × 2 = 48 cm³. Since 1 cm³ holds 1 mL, the container holds 48 mL.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":6,"width":4,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050424', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'medium', 1, 'Put these amounts in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.2 L"},{"id":"b","text":"350 cm³"},{"id":"c","text":"400 mL"},{"id":"d","text":"0.45 L"},{"id":"e","text":"1 L"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to mL: 0.2 L = 200 mL, 350 cm³ = 350 mL, 400 mL, 0.45 L = 450 mL and 1 L = 1000 mL.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050425', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'hard', 1, 'An aquarium is 60 cm long, 25 cm wide and 30 cm high. How many litres of water does it hold when full?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["45","45 L"]'::jsonb, 'Volume = 60 × 25 × 30 = 45 000 cm³ = 45 000 mL. There are 1000 mL in a litre, so it holds 45 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050426', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these rectangular prisms have a volume of 60 cm³? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"4 cm × 4 cm × 4 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"5 cm × 4 cm × 3 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"12 cm × 5 cm × 2 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"6 cm × 5 cm × 2 cm"},{"id":"e","text":"10 cm × 3 cm × 2 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["b","d","e"]'::jsonb, '5 × 4 × 3, 6 × 5 × 2 and 10 × 3 × 2 all make 60. 4 × 4 × 4 = 64 and 12 × 5 × 2 = 120.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050427', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'hard', 1, 'A cube-shaped container has edges of 10 cm. How many litres of water does it hold?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1 L"},{"id":"b","text":"10 L"},{"id":"c","text":"100 L"},{"id":"d","text":"0.1 L"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Volume = 10 × 10 × 10 = 1000 cm³, which is 1000 mL. 1000 mL = 1 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050428', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_hard', 1, 'This block is made of 1 cm cubes. How many more cubes are needed to turn it into a cube that is 4 cubes long, 4 wide and 4 high?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["40"]'::jsonb, 'The block has 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cubes. A 4 by 4 by 4 cube needs 4 × 4 × 4 = 64 cubes. 64 − 24 = 40 more.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":4,"width":3,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050429', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangular prism has a volume of 480 cm³. It is 12 cm long and 8 cm wide. How high is it?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","5 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The bottom layer is 12 × 8 = 96 cm³. 480 ÷ 96 = 5, so it is 5 cm high.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050430', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060504', 'very_hard', 1, 'A 1.5 L jug of cordial is poured into glasses that each hold 200 mL. How many glasses can be filled right to the top?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7"},{"id":"b","text":"8"},{"id":"c","text":"7.5"},{"id":"d","text":"6"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1.5 L = 1500 mL. 7 × 200 = 1400 mL, and the 100 mL left is not enough to fill an 8th glass. So 7 glasses.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050501', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many kilograms are in 1 tonne?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1000"},{"id":"b","text":"100"},{"id":"c","text":"10"},{"id":"d","text":"10 000"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1 tonne is 1000 kilograms.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050502', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_easy', 1, 'What mass does the scale show?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3.5 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"3.05 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"35 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"4.5 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The pointer is halfway between 3 and 4, so it shows 3.5 kg.', '{"kind":"scale","max":5,"value":3.5,"step":0.5,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050503', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_easy', 1, 'The mass of a loaded truck is best measured in tonnes.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A loaded truck weighs thousands of kilograms, so tonnes are the best unit.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050504', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'easy', 1, '2.4 kg = ___ g', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["2400","2400 g"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1000 g in 1 kg, so 2.4 × 1000 = 2400 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050505', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the scale. What is this mass in kilograms?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.75","0.75 kg"]'::jsonb, 'The scale shows 750 g. Divide by 1000 to change grams to kilograms: 750 ÷ 1000 = 0.75 kg.', '{"kind":"scale","max":1000,"value":750,"step":50,"unit":"g"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050506', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'easy', 1, 'Which is the best estimate for the mass of a fully grown red kangaroo?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"60 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"60 g"},{"id":"c","text":"6 t"},{"id":"d","text":"600 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A big kangaroo is a bit like an adult person, so about 60 kg. 60 g is a mouse, and 6 t is heavier than an elephant.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050507', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'medium', 1, 'Put these masses in order from lightest to heaviest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.08 t"},{"id":"b","text":"95 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"0.1 t"},{"id":"d","text":"120 kg"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to kilograms: 0.08 t = 80 kg, 95 kg, 0.1 t = 100 kg, 120 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050508', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'medium', 1, '3 bags of potatoes each have a mass of 2.5 kg. What is their total mass in kilograms?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7.5","7.5 kg"]'::jsonb, '3 × 2.5 = 7.5 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050509', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'medium', 1, '3.2 t = ___ kg', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["3200","3200 kg"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1000 kg in 1 tonne, so 3.2 × 1000 = 3200 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050510', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'hard', 1, 'The scale shows 5 jars of honey that all have the same mass. What is the mass of one jar, in grams?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["500","500 g"]'::jsonb, 'The scale shows 2.5 kg, which is 2500 g. 2500 ÷ 5 = 500 g.', '{"kind":"scale","max":5,"value":2.5,"step":0.5,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050511', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are the same as 1.5 t? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"1500 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"150 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"1 500 000 g"},{"id":"d","text":"15 000 kg"},{"id":"e","text":"1 t 500 kg"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, '1.5 × 1000 = 1500 kg, which is 1 t and 500 kg. In grams, 1500 × 1000 = 1 500 000 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050512', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'hard', 1, 'A ute can safely carry 1 t. It already has 650 kg in the back. Which load can still be added safely?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8 bags of 40 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"8 bags of 50 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"4 bags of 100 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"9 bags of 40 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1 t = 1000 kg, so 1000 − 650 = 350 kg is left. 8 × 40 = 320 kg fits. 400 kg and 360 kg are too much.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050513', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_hard', 1, 'An empty truck has a mass of 4.6 t. It is loaded with 120 boxes that each have a mass of 25 kg. What is the total mass, in tonnes?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7.6","7.6 t"]'::jsonb, '120 × 25 = 3000 kg, which is 3 t. 4.6 + 3 = 7.6 t.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050514', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_hard', 1, 'The scale shows a bag of 24 apples. On average, each apple has a mass of 150 g.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The scale shows 3.6 kg, which is 3600 g. 3600 ÷ 24 = 150 g.', '{"kind":"scale","max":4,"value":3.6,"step":0.2,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050515', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_hard', 1, 'One cake needs 375 g of flour. Flour comes in 1 kg bags. How many bags must you buy to make 7 cakes?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3","3 bags"]'::jsonb, '7 × 375 = 2625 g, which is 2.625 kg. 2 bags is only 2 kg, so you need 3 bags.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050516', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many grams are in 1 kilogram?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1000"},{"id":"b","text":"100"},{"id":"c","text":"10"},{"id":"d","text":"10 000"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Kilo means one thousand, so 1 kilogram is 1000 grams.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050517', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_easy', 1, 'What mass does the scale show?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2.5 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"2.05 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"25 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"3.5 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The pointer is halfway between 2 and 3, so it shows 2.5 kg.', '{"kind":"scale","max":3,"value":2.5,"step":0.5,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050518', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_easy', 1, 'The mass of a grape is best measured in grams.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A grape is very light, only a few grams, so grams are the best unit.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050519', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'easy', 1, '1.75 kg = ___ g', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["1750","1750 g"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1000 g in 1 kg, so 1.75 × 1000 = 1750 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050520', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the scale. What is this mass in kilograms?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1.25","1.25 kg"]'::jsonb, 'The scale shows 1250 g. Divide by 1000 to change grams to kilograms: 1250 ÷ 1000 = 1.25 kg.', '{"kind":"scale","max":2000,"value":1250,"step":250,"unit":"g"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050521', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'easy', 1, 'Which is the best estimate for the mass of an adult wombat?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"30 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"30 g"},{"id":"c","text":"3 t"},{"id":"d","text":"300 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A wombat is about as heavy as a Year 6 student, so about 30 kg. 30 g is a mouse, and 3 t is as heavy as a car or two.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050522', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'medium', 1, 'Put these masses in order from lightest to heaviest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"450 g"},{"id":"b","text":"0.5 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"0.65 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"700 g"},{"id":"e","text":"1.2 kg"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to grams: 450 g, 0.5 kg = 500 g, 0.65 kg = 650 g, 700 g and 1.2 kg = 1200 g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050523', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'medium', 1, '4 bags of flour each have a mass of 1.25 kg. What is their total mass in kilograms?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","5 kg"]'::jsonb, '4 × 1.25 = 4 × 1 + 4 × 0.25 = 4 + 1 = 5 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050524', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'medium', 1, '4500 kg = ___ t', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["4.5","4.5 t"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1000 kg in 1 tonne, so 4500 ÷ 1000 = 4.5 t.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050525', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'hard', 1, 'The scale shows 6 tins of tomatoes that all have the same mass. What is the mass of one tin, in grams?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["400","400 g"]'::jsonb, 'The scale shows 2.4 kg, which is 2400 g. 2400 ÷ 6 = 400 g.', '{"kind":"scale","max":3,"value":2.4,"step":0.2,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050526', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are the same as 2.25 kg? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2250 g"},{"id":"b","text":"2 kg 250 g"},{"id":"c","text":"225 g"},{"id":"d","text":"0.00225 t"},{"id":"e","text":"22 500 g"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '2.25 × 1000 = 2250 g, which is 2 kg and 250 g. In tonnes, 2.25 ÷ 1000 = 0.00225 t.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050527', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'hard', 1, 'A lift can safely carry 800 kg. People with a total mass of 410 kg are already inside. Which group can still get in safely?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6 people of 60 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"5 people of 80 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"4 people of 100 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"2 people of 90 kg and a 250 kg crate"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '800 − 410 = 390 kg is left. 6 × 60 = 360 kg fits. 400 kg, 400 kg and 180 + 250 = 430 kg are all too much.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050528', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_hard', 1, 'An empty truck has a mass of 5.8 t. It is loaded with 80 bags of cement that each have a mass of 40 kg. What is the total mass, in tonnes?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9","9 t"]'::jsonb, '80 × 40 = 3200 kg, which is 3.2 t. 5.8 + 3.2 = 9 t.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050529', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_hard', 1, 'The scale shows a box of 30 oranges. On average, each orange has a mass of more than 160 g.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The scale shows 4.5 kg, which is 4500 g. 4500 ÷ 30 = 150 g, which is less than 160 g.', '{"kind":"scale","max":5,"value":4.5,"step":0.5,"unit":"kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050530', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060505', 'very_hard', 1, 'A wheelbarrow can carry at most 80 kg. A gardener needs to move 1.3 t of soil. What is the smallest number of trips she can make?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["17","17 trips"]'::jsonb, '1.3 t = 1300 kg. 16 trips carry 16 × 80 = 1280 kg, which leaves 20 kg. That needs one more trip, so 17 trips.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050601', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is 2:30 pm in 24-hour time?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"14:30"},{"id":"b","text":"02:30"},{"id":"c","text":"12:30"},{"id":"d","text":"16:30"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'For pm times after midday, add 12 to the hour: 2 + 12 = 14, so 14:30.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050602', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_easy', 1, 'The clock shows a time in the evening. What is it in 24-hour time?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"21:40"},{"id":"b","text":"09:40"},{"id":"c","text":"20:40"},{"id":"d","text":"21:08"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The clock shows 9:40. In the evening that is 9:40 pm, and 9 + 12 = 21, so 21:40.', '{"kind":"clock","hour":9,"minute":40}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050603', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_easy', 1, '00:15 is a quarter past midnight.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'In 24-hour time the day starts at 00:00 (midnight), so 00:15 is 15 minutes after midnight.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050604', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'easy', 1, 'How many minutes are there from 13:45 to 15:10?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["85","85 minutes"]'::jsonb, '13:45 to 14:00 is 15 minutes, 14:00 to 15:00 is 60 minutes, and 15:00 to 15:10 is 10 minutes. 15 + 60 + 10 = 85 minutes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050605', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'easy', 1, 'In 12-hour time, 18:25 is ___ pm.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["6:25","6:25 pm","6:25pm"]'::jsonb, '18 − 12 = 6, so 18:25 is 6:25 pm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050606', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the train timetable. How long does the train take to go from Hornsby to Woy Woy?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"39 minutes"},{"id":"b","text":"79 minutes"},{"id":"c","text":"21 minutes"},{"id":"d","text":"41 minutes"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '16:52 to 17:00 is 8 minutes, and 17:00 to 17:31 is 31 minutes. 8 + 31 = 39 minutes.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Station","Train departs"],"rows":[["Hornsby","16:52"],["Berowra","17:04"],["Woy Woy","17:31"],["Gosford","17:39"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050607', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the timetable. The train reaches Gosford before quarter to six in the evening.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Quarter to six in the evening is 5:45 pm, or 17:45. The train is at Gosford at 17:39, six minutes earlier.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Station","Train departs"],"rows":[["Hornsby","16:52"],["Berowra","17:04"],["Woy Woy","17:31"],["Gosford","17:39"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050608', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'medium', 1, 'A movie starts at 19:35 and runs for 2 hours 40 minutes. What time does it finish, in 24-hour time?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["22:15"]'::jsonb, '19:35 plus 2 hours is 21:35. Add 40 more minutes: 21:35 + 25 minutes = 22:00, and 15 more minutes gives 22:15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050609', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'medium', 1, 'Put these times in order from earliest to latest in the same day.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"06:50"},{"id":"b","text":"9:15 am"},{"id":"c","text":"13:05"},{"id":"d","text":"4:40 pm"},{"id":"e","text":"22:10"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to 24-hour time: 06:50, 09:15, 13:05, 16:40 and 22:10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050610', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'hard', 1, 'It is not daylight saving time. Perth is 2 hours behind Sydney. When it is 3:00 pm in Sydney, what time is it in Perth?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1:00 pm"},{"id":"b","text":"5:00 pm"},{"id":"c","text":"2:30 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"12:00 pm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Perth is behind, so take away 2 hours: 3:00 pm − 2 hours = 1:00 pm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050611', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'hard', 1, 'When it is not daylight saving time, which of these cities have the same time as Sydney? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Brisbane"},{"id":"b","text":"Melbourne"},{"id":"c","text":"Adelaide"},{"id":"d","text":"Perth"},{"id":"e","text":"Hobart"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, 'Queensland, Victoria, Tasmania and NSW share Australian Eastern Standard Time. Adelaide is half an hour behind and Perth is 2 hours behind.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050612', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'hard', 1, 'A coach leaves Dubbo at 09:48 and arrives in Sydney at 15:20. How long is the trip?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5 h 32 min"},{"id":"b","text":"6 h 32 min"},{"id":"c","text":"5 h 28 min"},{"id":"d","text":"6 h 28 min"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '09:48 to 14:48 is 5 hours. 14:48 to 15:20 is 12 + 20 = 32 minutes. So 5 h 32 min.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050613', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_hard', 1, 'It is not daylight saving time, and Perth is 2 hours behind Sydney. A plane leaves Sydney at 1:30 pm Sydney time and flies for 5 hours to Perth. What is the time in Perth when it lands?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4:30 pm","4:30pm","4:30","16:30"]'::jsonb, '1:30 pm + 5 hours = 6:30 pm Sydney time. Perth is 2 hours behind, so it is 4:30 pm in Perth.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050614', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the bus timetable. Which bus has the shortest trip?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the 08:05 bus"},{"id":"b","text":"the 06:45 bus"},{"id":"c","text":"the 07:20 bus"},{"id":"d","text":"the 08:40 bus"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The trips take 1 h 25 min (06:45), 1 h 30 min (07:20), 1 h 20 min (08:05) and 1 h 25 min (08:40). The 08:05 bus is quickest.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Bus leaves Wollongong","Bus arrives in Sydney"],"rows":[["06:45","08:10"],["07:20","08:50"],["08:05","09:25"],["08:40","10:05"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050615', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_hard', 1, 'Sydney is 30 minutes ahead of Adelaide. A cricket match in Adelaide starts at 10:30 am Adelaide time. To watch the start live, a fan in Sydney should turn on the TV at 10:00 am Sydney time.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Sydney is ahead, so its clocks show a later time. 10:30 am in Adelaide is 11:00 am in Sydney, not 10:00 am.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050616', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is 7:15 pm in 24-hour time?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"19:15"},{"id":"b","text":"07:15"},{"id":"c","text":"17:15"},{"id":"d","text":"21:15"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'For pm times after midday, add 12 to the hour: 7 + 12 = 19, so 19:15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050617', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_easy', 1, 'The clock shows a time in the afternoon. What is it in 24-hour time?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"16:25"},{"id":"b","text":"04:25"},{"id":"c","text":"14:25"},{"id":"d","text":"16:05"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The clock shows 4:25. In the afternoon that is 4:25 pm, and 4 + 12 = 16, so 16:25.', '{"kind":"clock","hour":4,"minute":25}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050618', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_easy', 1, '12:00 in 24-hour time is midday.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'In 24-hour time 00:00 is midnight and 12:00 is midday (noon).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050619', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'easy', 1, 'How many minutes are there from 10:35 to 12:05?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["90","90 minutes"]'::jsonb, '10:35 to 11:00 is 25 minutes, 11:00 to 12:00 is 60 minutes, and 12:00 to 12:05 is 5 minutes. 25 + 60 + 5 = 90 minutes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050620', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'easy', 1, 'In 12-hour time, 21:50 is ___ pm.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["9:50","9:50 pm","9:50pm"]'::jsonb, '21 − 12 = 9, so 21:50 is 9:50 pm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050621', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the ferry timetable. How long does the ferry take to go from Circular Quay to Mosman Bay?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"37 minutes"},{"id":"b","text":"47 minutes"},{"id":"c","text":"33 minutes"},{"id":"d","text":"27 minutes"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '09:10 to 09:47 is 47 − 10 = 37 minutes.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Wharf","Ferry departs"],"rows":[["Circular Quay","09:10"],["Kirribilli","09:18"],["Neutral Bay","09:29"],["Mosman Bay","09:47"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050622', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the timetable. The ferry takes longer from Kirribilli to Neutral Bay than from Neutral Bay to Mosman Bay.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Kirribilli to Neutral Bay is 09:18 to 09:29, which is 11 minutes. Neutral Bay to Mosman Bay is 09:29 to 09:47, which is 18 minutes. So the second part is longer.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Wharf","Ferry departs"],"rows":[["Circular Quay","09:10"],["Kirribilli","09:18"],["Neutral Bay","09:29"],["Mosman Bay","09:47"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050623', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'medium', 1, 'A concert starts at 18:50 and runs for 2 hours 55 minutes. What time does it finish, in 24-hour time?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["21:45"]'::jsonb, '18:50 plus 2 hours is 20:50. Add 55 more minutes: 10 minutes gets to 21:00, and 45 more gives 21:45.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050624', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'medium', 1, 'Put these times in order from earliest to latest in the same day.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"00:40"},{"id":"b","text":"5:30 am"},{"id":"c","text":"11:55 am"},{"id":"d","text":"12:20"},{"id":"e","text":"3:10 pm"},{"id":"f","text":"20:05"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e","f"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to 24-hour time: 00:40, 05:30, 11:55, 12:20, 15:10 and 20:05.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050625', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'hard', 1, 'It is not daylight saving time. Adelaide is half an hour behind Sydney. When it is 9:00 am in Sydney, what time is it in Adelaide?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8:30 am"},{"id":"b","text":"9:30 am"},{"id":"c","text":"7:00 am"},{"id":"d","text":"8:00 am"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Adelaide is behind, so take away half an hour: 9:00 am − 30 minutes = 8:30 am.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050626', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'hard', 1, 'In summer, some states move their clocks forward one hour for daylight saving. Which of these do? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Victoria"},{"id":"b","text":"Queensland"},{"id":"c","text":"South Australia"},{"id":"d","text":"Western Australia"},{"id":"e","text":"Tasmania"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'NSW, the ACT, Victoria, South Australia and Tasmania use daylight saving. Queensland, Western Australia and the Northern Territory do not.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050627', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'hard', 1, 'A train leaves Central at 07:38 and arrives in Newcastle at 10:16. How long is the trip?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2 h 38 min"},{"id":"b","text":"3 h 22 min"},{"id":"c","text":"2 h 22 min"},{"id":"d","text":"3 h 38 min"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '07:38 to 09:38 is 2 hours. 09:38 to 10:16 is 22 + 16 = 38 minutes. So 2 h 38 min.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050628', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_hard', 1, 'It is not daylight saving time, and Perth is 2 hours behind Sydney. A plane leaves Perth at 9:15 am Perth time and flies for 4 hours 20 minutes to Sydney. What is the time in Sydney when it lands?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3:35 pm","3:35pm","3:35","15:35"]'::jsonb, '9:15 am + 4 h 20 min = 1:35 pm Perth time. Sydney is 2 hours ahead, so it is 3:35 pm in Sydney.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050629', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the bus timetable. Which bus has the longest trip?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the 07:35 bus"},{"id":"b","text":"the 07:05 bus"},{"id":"c","text":"the 08:10 bus"},{"id":"d","text":"the 08:45 bus"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The trips take 47 min (07:05), 54 min (07:35), 48 min (08:10) and 44 min (08:45). The 07:35 bus takes longest.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Bus leaves Penrith","Bus arrives Parramatta"],"rows":[["07:05","07:52"],["07:35","08:29"],["08:10","08:58"],["08:45","09:29"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006050630', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060506', 'very_hard', 1, 'It is not daylight saving time, and Perth is 2 hours behind Brisbane. A footy match in Brisbane starts at 7:30 pm Brisbane time. To watch the start live, a fan in Perth should turn on the TV at 5:30 pm Perth time.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Perth clocks show a time 2 hours earlier than Brisbane clocks. 7:30 pm − 2 hours = 5:30 pm in Perth.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_easy', 1, 'What type of angle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"obtuse"},{"id":"b","text":"acute"},{"id":"c","text":"right"},{"id":"d","text":"reflex"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It is bigger than a right angle (90°) but smaller than a straight angle (180°), so it is obtuse.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":135}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Angles on a straight line add up to 180°.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A straight line is half a full turn, and half of 360° is 180°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which is the best estimate for the size of this angle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"60°"},{"id":"b","text":"120°"},{"id":"c","text":"30°"},{"id":"d","text":"160°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It is smaller than a right angle, so it is acute. It is about two-thirds of a right angle, so 60° is the best fit. 30° would be much narrower.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":60}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'easy', 1, 'This 110° angle and another angle sit together on a straight line. How big is the other angle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["70","70°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles on a straight line add to 180°, so 180 − 110 = 70°.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":110,"label":"110°"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'easy', 1, 'What type of angle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"reflex"},{"id":"b","text":"obtuse"},{"id":"c","text":"straight"},{"id":"d","text":"acute"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It is more than a straight angle (180°) but less than a full turn (360°), so it is a reflex angle.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":250}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'easy', 1, 'Angles at a point add up to ___°.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["360","360°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles all the way around a point make a full turn, which is 360°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'medium', 1, 'Three angles meet at a point. Two of them are 120° and 95°. How big is the third angle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["145","145°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles at a point add to 360°. 120 + 95 = 215, and 360 − 215 = 145°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'medium', 1, 'When two straight lines cross, the angles opposite each other (vertically opposite angles) are equal.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Each pair of opposite angles shares a partner on a straight line, so they must be the same size.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'medium', 1, 'Put these angle types in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"acute"},{"id":"b","text":"right"},{"id":"c","text":"obtuse"},{"id":"d","text":"straight"},{"id":"e","text":"reflex"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Acute is less than 90°, right is 90°, obtuse is between 90° and 180°, straight is 180° and reflex is more than 180°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'hard', 1, 'Two angles of a triangle are 48° and 77°. How big is the third angle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["55","55°"]'::jsonb, 'The angles in a triangle add to 180°. 48 + 77 = 125, and 180 − 125 = 55°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'hard', 1, 'Two straight lines cross. One of the four angles is 40°. What are the other three angles, going around?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"140°, 40°, 140°"},{"id":"b","text":"40°, 40°, 40°"},{"id":"c","text":"140°, 140°, 140°"},{"id":"d","text":"50°, 40°, 50°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The angles next to 40° are on a straight line with it, so 180 − 40 = 140°. The opposite angle is equal, 40°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'hard', 1, 'Which sets of angles could be the three angles of a triangle? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"60°, 60°, 60°"},{"id":"b","text":"90°, 45°, 45°"},{"id":"c","text":"100°, 50°, 40°"},{"id":"d","text":"30°, 30°, 120°"},{"id":"e","text":"90°, 90°, 10°"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'The three angles must add to exactly 180°. 100 + 50 + 40 = 190 and 90 + 90 + 10 = 190, so those two can''t work.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_hard', 1, 'This 125° angle is one angle of an isosceles triangle. The other two angles are equal. How big is each of them?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["27.5","27.5°"]'::jsonb, '180 − 125 = 55° is left for the other two angles. They are equal, so 55 ÷ 2 = 27.5° each.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":125,"label":"125°"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Three angles meet at a point. The second angle is twice the first, and the third is three times the first. How big is the first angle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"60°"},{"id":"b","text":"30°"},{"id":"c","text":"120°"},{"id":"d","text":"45°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Together they make 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 lots of the first angle, and that is 360°. 360 ÷ 6 = 60°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_hard', 1, 'A straight line is split into three angles: this 72° angle, a right angle and one more. How big is the last angle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["18","18°"]'::jsonb, 'The three angles add to 180°. 180 − 90 − 72 = 18°.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":72,"label":"72°"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_easy', 1, 'What type of angle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"acute"},{"id":"b","text":"obtuse"},{"id":"c","text":"right"},{"id":"d","text":"reflex"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It is smaller than a right angle (90°), so it is acute.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":40}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_easy', 1, 'A full turn is 360°.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Turning all the way around back to where you started is 360°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which is the best estimate for the size of this angle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"150°"},{"id":"b","text":"30°"},{"id":"c","text":"90°"},{"id":"d","text":"210°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It is bigger than a right angle but smaller than a straight line, so it is obtuse. It is close to a straight line, so 150° fits best. 210° would be reflex.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":150}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'easy', 1, 'This 65° angle and another angle sit together on a straight line. How big is the other angle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["115","115°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles on a straight line add to 180°, so 180 − 65 = 115°.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":65,"label":"65°"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'easy', 1, 'What type of angle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"straight"},{"id":"b","text":"reflex"},{"id":"c","text":"obtuse"},{"id":"d","text":"right"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The two arms make one straight line. That is a straight angle, exactly 180°.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":180}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'easy', 1, 'Two right angles side by side make a ___ angle.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["straight","180°","180"]'::jsonb, '90° + 90° = 180°, which is a straight angle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'medium', 1, 'Four angles meet at a point. Three of them are 90°, 75° and 110°. How big is the fourth angle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["85","85°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles at a point add to 360°. 90 + 75 + 110 = 275, and 360 − 275 = 85°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'medium', 1, 'If two equal angles sit together on a straight line, each one is a right angle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'They add to 180°, and 180 ÷ 2 = 90°, so each one is a right angle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'medium', 1, 'Put these angles in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"one third of a right angle"},{"id":"b","text":"a right angle"},{"id":"c","text":"a straight angle"},{"id":"d","text":"three quarters of a full turn"},{"id":"e","text":"a full turn"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them to degrees: 90 ÷ 3 = 30°, 90°, 180°, 3/4 of 360 = 270°, and 360°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'hard', 1, 'Two angles of a triangle are 64° and 58°. How big is the third angle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["58","58°"]'::jsonb, 'The angles in a triangle add to 180°. 64 + 58 = 122, and 180 − 122 = 58°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'hard', 1, 'Two straight lines cross. One of the four angles is 115°. What are the other three angles, going around?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"65°, 115°, 65°"},{"id":"b","text":"115°, 115°, 115°"},{"id":"c","text":"65°, 65°, 65°"},{"id":"d","text":"25°, 115°, 25°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The angles next to 115° are on a straight line with it, so 180 − 115 = 65°. The opposite angle is equal, 115°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'hard', 1, 'Which sets of angles could be the three angles of a triangle? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"70°, 70°, 40°"},{"id":"b","text":"90°, 60°, 30°"},{"id":"c","text":"110°, 50°, 30°"},{"id":"d","text":"45°, 45°, 100°"},{"id":"e","text":"20°, 20°, 140°"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, 'The three angles must add to exactly 180°. 110 + 50 + 30 = 190 and 45 + 45 + 100 = 190, so those two can''t work.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_hard', 1, 'This 50° angle is the top angle of an isosceles triangle. The other two angles are equal. How big is each of them?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["65","65°"]'::jsonb, '180 − 50 = 130° is left for the other two angles. They are equal, so 130 ÷ 2 = 65° each.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":50,"label":"50°"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Two angles sit together on a straight line. One is 4 times as big as the other. How big is the smaller angle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"36°"},{"id":"b","text":"45°"},{"id":"c","text":"20°"},{"id":"d","text":"144°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Together they make 1 + 4 = 5 lots of the smaller angle, and that is 180°. 180 ÷ 5 = 36°. (The bigger one is 144°.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Three angles meet at a point: this 38° angle, a straight angle and one more. How big is the last angle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["142","142°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles at a point add to 360°. A straight angle is 180°. 360 − 180 − 38 = 142°.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":38,"label":"38°"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the triangle. What is the best name for it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"equilateral"},{"id":"b","text":"isosceles"},{"id":"c","text":"scalene"},{"id":"d","text":"right-angled"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'All three sides are 6 cm. A triangle with three equal sides is equilateral.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[2,1],[10,1],[6,8]],"sideLabels":["6 cm","6 cm","6 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_easy', 1, 'Every square is also a rectangle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A rectangle has four right angles and opposite sides equal. A square has all of that, so it is a special rectangle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is this quadrilateral called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"trapezium"},{"id":"b","text":"parallelogram"},{"id":"c","text":"kite"},{"id":"d","text":"rhombus"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The top and bottom are parallel, but the slanting sides are not. A quadrilateral with one pair of parallel sides is a trapezium.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[11,1],[8,6],[3,6]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'easy', 1, 'Which words describe this triangle? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"scalene"},{"id":"b","text":"obtuse-angled"},{"id":"c","text":"equilateral"},{"id":"d","text":"right-angled"},{"id":"e","text":"isosceles"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'All three sides are different, so it is scalene. The angle at the bottom right is bigger than a right angle, so it is obtuse-angled.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[7,1],[11,5]],"sideLabels":["6 cm","5.7 cm","10.8 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'easy', 1, 'How many pairs of parallel sides does a rhombus have?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2"},{"id":"b","text":"1"},{"id":"c","text":"0"},{"id":"d","text":"4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A rhombus is a parallelogram, so both pairs of opposite sides are parallel: 2 pairs.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'easy', 1, 'A triangle with exactly two equal sides is called an ___ triangle.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["isosceles"]'::jsonb, 'Isosceles triangles have two equal sides and two equal angles.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'medium', 1, 'Look at this parallelogram. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Opposite sides are parallel"},{"id":"b","text":"Opposite sides are equal"},{"id":"c","text":"All its angles are right angles"},{"id":"d","text":"All four sides are equal"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'In a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel and equal. Its angles are not right angles, and the slanting sides are shorter than the top and bottom.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[8,1],[11,6],[4,6]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'medium', 1, 'A triangle can have two right angles.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The angles in a triangle add to 180°. Two right angles already make 180°, leaving nothing for the third angle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'medium', 1, 'A rhombus is a parallelogram with…', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"four equal sides"},{"id":"b","text":"four right angles"},{"id":"c","text":"only one pair of parallel sides"},{"id":"d","text":"no equal sides"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A rhombus has opposite sides parallel like any parallelogram, and all four sides are equal.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'hard', 1, 'This shape is a kite. How many lines of symmetry does it have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1","one"]'::jsonb, 'A kite has one line of symmetry, running from the top point to the bottom point. Fold along it and the halves match.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[6,1],[9,5],[6,8],[3,5]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'hard', 1, 'Put these shape names in order from the most general to the most special.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"quadrilateral"},{"id":"b","text":"parallelogram"},{"id":"c","text":"rectangle"},{"id":"d","text":"square"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Every square is a rectangle, every rectangle is a parallelogram, and every parallelogram is a quadrilateral.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'hard', 1, 'A quadrilateral''s diagonals are equal in length and cut each other in half, but they do not cross at right angles. What is it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"rectangle"},{"id":"b","text":"square"},{"id":"c","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"d","text":"kite"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Equal diagonals that cut each other in half make a rectangle. If they also crossed at right angles it would be a square, and a rhombus''s diagonals are not equal.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are always true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Every rhombus is a parallelogram"},{"id":"b","text":"Every rectangle is a square"},{"id":"c","text":"Every square is a rhombus"},{"id":"d","text":"A rectangle''s diagonals are equal in length"},{"id":"e","text":"Every kite has four equal sides"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A rhombus has two pairs of parallel sides, a square has four equal sides, and a rectangle''s diagonals are equal. Most rectangles are not squares and most kites don''t have four equal sides.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_hard', 1, 'This right-angled triangle has two equal sides. What size is each of its other two angles?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"45°"},{"id":"b","text":"90°"},{"id":"c","text":"60°"},{"id":"d","text":"30°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The angles add to 180°. Take away the right angle: 180 − 90 = 90°. The two equal sides make the other two angles equal, so 90 ÷ 2 = 45°.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[7,1],[1,7]],"sideLabels":["6 cm","","6 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_hard', 1, 'A triangle has angles of 35° and 55°. Is it acute-angled, right-angled or obtuse-angled?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["right-angled","right angled","right"]'::jsonb, 'The third angle is 180 − 35 − 55 = 90°, so the triangle is right-angled.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the triangle. What is the best name for it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"isosceles"},{"id":"b","text":"equilateral"},{"id":"c","text":"scalene"},{"id":"d","text":"right-angled"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Two sides are 7.2 cm and one is 8 cm. A triangle with two equal sides is isosceles.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[2,1],[10,1],[6,7]],"sideLabels":["8 cm","7.2 cm","7.2 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_easy', 1, 'Every rectangle is a parallelogram.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A parallelogram has two pairs of parallel sides. A rectangle has that too, so it is a special parallelogram with right angles.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is this quadrilateral called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"b","text":"trapezium"},{"id":"c","text":"kite"},{"id":"d","text":"rectangle"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'All four sides are 5 cm, opposite sides are parallel, and the angles are not right angles. That makes it a rhombus.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[6,1],[9,5],[4,5]],"sideLabels":["5 cm","5 cm","5 cm","5 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'easy', 1, 'Which words describe this triangle? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"right-angled"},{"id":"b","text":"scalene"},{"id":"c","text":"isosceles"},{"id":"d","text":"equilateral"},{"id":"e","text":"obtuse-angled"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'The corner at the bottom left is a right angle, so it is right-angled. The sides are 6, 8 and 10 cm, all different, so it is scalene.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[9,1],[1,7]],"sideLabels":["8 cm","10 cm","6 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'easy', 1, 'How many pairs of parallel sides does a trapezium have?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1"},{"id":"b","text":"2"},{"id":"c","text":"0"},{"id":"d","text":"4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A trapezium has exactly one pair of parallel sides.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'easy', 1, 'A triangle with all three sides different lengths is called a ___ triangle.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["scalene"]'::jsonb, 'Scalene triangles have no equal sides and no equal angles.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'medium', 1, 'Look at this rectangle. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"All four angles are right angles"},{"id":"b","text":"Its two diagonals are equal in length"},{"id":"c","text":"All four sides are equal"},{"id":"d","text":"Opposite sides are parallel"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'A rectangle has four right angles, equal diagonals and opposite sides parallel. This one is longer than it is tall, so its sides are not all equal.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[10,1],[10,6],[1,6]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'medium', 1, 'A triangle can have two obtuse angles.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Each obtuse angle is more than 90°, so two of them would add to more than 180°. But all three angles of a triangle add to exactly 180°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these describes a kite?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"two pairs of equal sides that are next to each other"},{"id":"b","text":"two pairs of parallel sides"},{"id":"c","text":"four right angles"},{"id":"d","text":"exactly one pair of parallel sides"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A kite has two pairs of equal sides, and each pair sits side by side (not opposite each other).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'hard', 1, 'This shape is a rhombus. How many lines of symmetry does it have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2","two"]'::jsonb, 'A rhombus has 2 lines of symmetry: one through the top and bottom corners, and one through the left and right corners.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[6,1],[9,4.5],[6,8],[3,4.5]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'hard', 1, 'Put these shape names in order from the most general to the most special.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"quadrilateral"},{"id":"b","text":"parallelogram"},{"id":"c","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"d","text":"square"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Every square is a rhombus, every rhombus is a parallelogram, and every parallelogram is a quadrilateral.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'hard', 1, 'A quadrilateral''s diagonals cross at right angles and cut each other in half, but they are not equal in length. What is it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"rhombus"},{"id":"b","text":"square"},{"id":"c","text":"rectangle"},{"id":"d","text":"trapezium"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Diagonals that cut each other in half at right angles make a rhombus. If they were also equal it would be a square, and a rectangle''s diagonals don''t cross at right angles.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are always true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"A square''s diagonals are equal in length"},{"id":"b","text":"Every parallelogram is a rectangle"},{"id":"c","text":"A rhombus''s diagonals cross at right angles"},{"id":"d","text":"A trapezium has two pairs of parallel sides"},{"id":"e","text":"Every rectangle has four lines of symmetry"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, 'A square is a rectangle, so its diagonals are equal, and a rhombus''s diagonals always cross at right angles. Most parallelograms have no right angles, a trapezium has only one pair of parallel sides, and a rectangle that isn''t a square has only 2 lines of symmetry.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_hard', 1, 'This isosceles triangle has a 40° angle at the top. The two angles at the bottom are equal. How big is each of them?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["70","70°"]'::jsonb, 'The angles add to 180°. 180 − 40 = 140° is left for the two bottom angles. They are equal, so 140 ÷ 2 = 70° each.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[3.45,1],[8.55,1],[6,8]],"cornerLabels":["","","40°"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060302', 'very_hard', 1, 'A triangle has angles of 25° and 60°. Is it acute-angled, right-angled or obtuse-angled?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["obtuse-angled","obtuse angled","obtuse"]'::jsonb, 'The third angle is 180 − 25 − 60 = 95°. That is more than 90°, so the triangle is obtuse-angled.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is this object called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"b","text":"triangular pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"rectangular prism"},{"id":"d","text":"cone"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It has two triangle ends joined by rectangles, so it is a triangular prism.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many faces does this square pyramid have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","five"]'::jsonb, 'It has 1 square base and 4 triangle faces: 1 + 4 = 5 faces.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"square_pyramid"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_easy', 1, 'A prism has two matching end faces joined by rectangles.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'That is what makes a prism: two identical ends (bases) with rectangles around the sides.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'easy', 1, 'These six shaded squares make a net that folds into a cube.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'This is the cross-shaped net. The row of four wraps around the sides, and the squares above and below fold up to make the top and bottom.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":4,"rows":3,"cells":[[1,0],[0,1],[1,1],[2,1],[3,1],[1,2]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'easy', 1, 'How many edges does a triangular prism have?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"9"},{"id":"b","text":"6"},{"id":"c","text":"5"},{"id":"d","text":"12"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each triangle end has 3 edges (6 altogether), and 3 more edges join the ends. 6 + 3 = 9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'easy', 1, 'How many vertices (corners) does this triangular pyramid have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4","four"]'::jsonb, 'It has 3 corners around the triangle base and 1 at the top: 3 + 1 = 4.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_pyramid"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'medium', 1, 'These six shaded squares make a net that folds into a cube.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'In a 2 by 3 block, the squares fold on top of each other and leave two faces open, so it can''t make a cube.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":3,"rows":2,"cells":[[0,0],[1,0],[2,0],[0,1],[1,1],[2,1]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'medium', 1, 'A net is made of 2 pentagons and 5 rectangles. What object does it fold into?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"pentagonal prism"},{"id":"b","text":"pentagonal pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"hexagonal prism"},{"id":"d","text":"rectangular prism"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Two matching pentagon ends joined by rectangles make a pentagonal prism.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'medium', 1, 'Put these objects in order from fewest faces to most faces.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"triangular pyramid"},{"id":"b","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"rectangular prism"},{"id":"d","text":"hexagonal prism"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular pyramid has 4 faces, a square pyramid 5, a rectangular prism 6 and a hexagonal prism 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these objects have exactly 6 vertices? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"b","text":"pentagonal pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"cube"},{"id":"d","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"e","text":"hexagonal pyramid"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular prism has 3 + 3 = 6 vertices and a pentagonal pyramid has 5 + 1 = 6. A cube has 8, a square pyramid 5 and a hexagonal pyramid 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'hard', 1, 'A pyramid has a base with 8 sides (an octagon). How many faces does it have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9","nine"]'::jsonb, 'There is 1 octagon base and a triangle face on each of its 8 sides: 1 + 8 = 9 faces.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'hard', 1, 'Can these six shaded squares fold into a cube?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"No, two squares would overlap"},{"id":"b","text":"Yes, it makes a cube"},{"id":"c","text":"No, it has too few squares"},{"id":"d","text":"No, it has too many squares"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The row of four wraps around the sides. The two squares on top are side by side, so both try to become the top face and overlap, leaving the bottom open.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":4,"rows":3,"cells":[[0,1],[1,1],[2,1],[3,1],[0,2],[1,2]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_hard', 1, 'A prism has 18 edges. How many faces does it have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","eight"]'::jsonb, 'A prism has 3 edges for every side of its base, so the base has 18 ÷ 3 = 6 sides (a hexagonal prism). It has 2 hexagon ends and 6 rectangles: 8 faces.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which object has 7 faces, 15 edges and 10 vertices?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"pentagonal prism"},{"id":"b","text":"hexagonal pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"hexagonal prism"},{"id":"d","text":"pentagonal pyramid"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A pentagonal prism has 2 pentagons and 5 rectangles (7 faces), 5 + 5 + 5 = 15 edges and 5 + 5 = 10 vertices.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_hard', 1, 'Imagine slicing straight through this prism, parallel to its triangular ends. What 2D shape is the cut face?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["triangle","a triangle"]'::jsonb, 'A slice parallel to the ends of a prism is always the same shape as the ends, so it is a triangle.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is this object called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"triangular pyramid"},{"id":"b","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"c","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"d","text":"cone"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It has a triangle base and three triangle faces that meet at a point, so it is a triangular pyramid.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_pyramid"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many edges does this rectangular prism have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","twelve"]'::jsonb, 'It has 4 edges around the top, 4 around the bottom and 4 going up the sides: 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"rectangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_easy', 1, 'A pyramid has one base, and all its other faces are triangles that meet at a point.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'That is what makes a pyramid: one base, with triangles rising from each side to meet at the top.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'easy', 1, 'These six shaded squares make a net that folds into a cube.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The row of four wraps around the sides. One square above the row folds down to make the top and one square below folds up to make the bottom, so it closes up into a cube.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":4,"rows":3,"cells":[[0,1],[1,1],[2,1],[3,1],[0,2],[3,0]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'easy', 1, 'How many vertices (corners) does a rectangular prism have?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8"},{"id":"b","text":"6"},{"id":"c","text":"12"},{"id":"d","text":"4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It has 4 corners on the top face and 4 on the bottom face: 4 + 4 = 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'easy', 1, 'How many faces does this triangular prism have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","five"]'::jsonb, 'It has 2 triangle ends and 3 rectangles around the sides: 2 + 3 = 5 faces.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'medium', 1, 'These six shaded squares make a net that folds into a cube.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Only four faces fit around the sides of a cube. A row of five squares wraps around and the fifth square overlaps the first, leaving a face open.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":5,"rows":3,"cells":[[0,1],[1,1],[2,1],[3,1],[4,1],[2,2]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'medium', 1, 'A net is made of 1 hexagon and 6 triangles. What object does it fold into?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"hexagonal pyramid"},{"id":"b","text":"hexagonal prism"},{"id":"c","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"d","text":"pentagonal pyramid"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'One hexagon base with a triangle on each of its 6 sides folds into a hexagonal pyramid.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'medium', 1, 'Put these objects in order from fewest edges to most edges.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"triangular pyramid"},{"id":"b","text":"square pyramid"},{"id":"c","text":"triangular prism"},{"id":"d","text":"rectangular prism"},{"id":"e","text":"pentagonal prism"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular pyramid has 6 edges, a square pyramid 8, a triangular prism 9, a rectangular prism 12 and a pentagonal prism 15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these objects have exactly 8 faces? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"hexagonal prism"},{"id":"b","text":"pyramid with a 7-sided base"},{"id":"c","text":"pyramid with an 8-sided base"},{"id":"d","text":"cube"},{"id":"e","text":"pentagonal prism"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'A hexagonal prism has 2 + 6 = 8 faces and a pyramid with a 7-sided base has 1 + 7 = 8. The 8-sided pyramid has 9, a cube 6 and a pentagonal prism 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'hard', 1, 'A prism has a base with 7 sides. How many vertices does it have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["14","fourteen"]'::jsonb, 'Each of the two 7-sided ends has 7 corners, and there are no other corners. 7 + 7 = 14 vertices.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'hard', 1, 'Can these six shaded squares fold into a cube?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Yes, it makes a cube"},{"id":"b","text":"No, two squares would overlap"},{"id":"c","text":"No, it has too few squares"},{"id":"d","text":"No, a cube net needs a row of four"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'This staircase shape is one of the 11 nets of a cube. Fold each step down and the six squares close up with no gaps or overlaps. A net does not need a row of four.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":4,"rows":3,"cells":[[0,2],[1,2],[1,1],[2,1],[2,0],[3,0]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_hard', 1, 'A pyramid has 12 edges. How many faces does it have?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","seven"]'::jsonb, 'A pyramid has 2 edges for every side of its base (one around the base, one up to the top). 12 ÷ 2 = 6, so it is a hexagonal pyramid with 1 + 6 = 7 faces.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which object has 6 faces, 10 edges and 6 vertices?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"pentagonal pyramid"},{"id":"b","text":"pentagonal prism"},{"id":"c","text":"cube"},{"id":"d","text":"hexagonal pyramid"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A pentagonal pyramid has 1 pentagon and 5 triangles (6 faces), 5 + 5 = 10 edges and 5 + 1 = 6 vertices. A cube has 6 faces but 12 edges and 8 vertices.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060303', 'very_hard', 1, 'Imagine slicing straight across this square pyramid, parallel to its base. What 2D shape is the cut face?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["square","a square"]'::jsonb, 'A slice parallel to the base of a pyramid is the same shape as the base, only smaller. The base is a square, so the cut face is a square.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"square_pyramid"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030401', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_easy', 1, 'What are the coordinates of point A?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(4, 2)"},{"id":"b","text":"(2, 4)"},{"id":"c","text":"(4, 4)"},{"id":"d","text":"(2, 2)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Go across first, then up. A is 4 across and 2 up, so it is (4, 2).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":4,"y":2,"label":"A"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030402', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_easy', 1, 'In the coordinates (5, 3), the 5 tells you how far to go across, along the x-axis.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The first number is the x-coordinate (across) and the second is the y-coordinate (up or down).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030403', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_easy', 1, 'What number is the dot on?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"−3"},{"id":"b","text":"3"},{"id":"c","text":"−2"},{"id":"d","text":"−4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The dot is 3 steps to the left of 0, so it is on −3.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":-5,"to":5,"jumps":10,"dotAt":2,"labelEvery":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030404', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'easy', 1, 'Which point is at (6, 1)?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["Q"]'::jsonb, 'Go 6 across and 1 up. That is point Q. (Point R is at (1, 6), the numbers the other way round.)', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":3,"y":5,"label":"P"},{"x":6,"y":1,"label":"Q"},{"x":1,"y":6,"label":"R"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030405', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'easy', 1, 'The point (−2, 3) is in which quadrant?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"second"},{"id":"b","text":"first"},{"id":"c","text":"third"},{"id":"d","text":"fourth"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'x is negative (left) and y is positive (up). Left and up is the second quadrant, the top left.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030406', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'easy', 1, 'The point where the x-axis and y-axis cross is called the ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["origin","the origin"]'::jsonb, 'The origin is the point (0, 0), where the two axes cross.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030407', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'medium', 1, 'A, B and C are three corners of a square. What are the coordinates of the fourth corner?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["(2, 7)","(2,7)","2, 7","2,7"]'::jsonb, 'The fourth corner is straight above A and level with C: x = 2 like A, y = 7 like C. So it is (2, 7).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":8,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":2,"y":3,"label":"A"},{"x":6,"y":3,"label":"B"},{"x":6,"y":7,"label":"C"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030408', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these points have a negative x-coordinate? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−3, 2)"},{"id":"b","text":"(4, −1)"},{"id":"c","text":"(−5, −5)"},{"id":"d","text":"(0, 6)"},{"id":"e","text":"(−1, 0)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'The x-coordinate is the first number. It is negative in (−3, 2), (−5, −5) and (−1, 0).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030409', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'medium', 1, 'The point (0, −4) lies on the y-axis.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Its x-coordinate is 0, so it doesn''t move left or right. It sits on the y-axis, 4 below the origin.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030410', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'hard', 1, 'Start at (3, −2). Move 5 units left and then 4 units up. Where do you finish?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−2, 2)"},{"id":"b","text":"(8, 2)"},{"id":"c","text":"(−2, −6)"},{"id":"d","text":"(7, −7)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Left changes x: 3 − 5 = −2. Up changes y: −2 + 4 = 2. You finish at (−2, 2).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030411', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'hard', 1, 'Walk along the grid lines from A to B, then from B to C. How many units do you walk?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["10","10 units"]'::jsonb, 'A to B is 7 − 1 = 6 units across. B to C is 6 − 2 = 4 units up. 6 + 4 = 10 units.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":10,"rows":8,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":1,"y":2,"label":"A"},{"x":7,"y":2,"label":"B"},{"x":7,"y":6,"label":"C"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030412', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'hard', 1, 'Put these points in order from left to right on the coordinate plane.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−6, 1)"},{"id":"b","text":"(−2, 5)"},{"id":"c","text":"(0, −3)"},{"id":"d","text":"(3, 2)"},{"id":"e","text":"(7, −4)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Left to right depends only on the x-coordinate (the first number): −6, −2, 0, 3, 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030413', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangle has three corners at (−3, 2), (4, 2) and (4, −1). Where is the fourth corner?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−3, −1)"},{"id":"b","text":"(−1, −3)"},{"id":"c","text":"(3, −1)"},{"id":"d","text":"(−3, 1)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The fourth corner is below (−3, 2) and level with (4, −1). So x = −3 and y = −1: (−3, −1).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030414', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_hard', 1, 'How many units apart are the points (−4, 3) and (5, 3)?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9","9 units"]'::jsonb, 'Both points have y = 3, so count across: from −4 to 0 is 4 units and from 0 to 5 is 5 units. 4 + 5 = 9 units.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030415', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_hard', 1, 'Point T is reflected (flipped) across the y-axis. Where does it land?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−5, 3)"},{"id":"b","text":"(5, −3)"},{"id":"c","text":"(−5, −3)"},{"id":"d","text":"(3, 5)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'T is at (5, 3). Flipping across the y-axis moves it to the same distance on the left, so x becomes −5 and y stays 3: (−5, 3).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":5,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":5,"y":3,"label":"T"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030416', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_easy', 1, 'What are the coordinates of point B?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(1, 5)"},{"id":"b","text":"(5, 1)"},{"id":"c","text":"(1, 1)"},{"id":"d","text":"(5, 5)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Go across first, then up. B is 1 across and 5 up, so it is (1, 5).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":1,"y":5,"label":"B"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030417', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_easy', 1, 'The origin has the coordinates (0, 0).', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The origin is where the axes cross, 0 across and 0 up.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030418', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_easy', 1, 'What number is the dot on?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"−5"},{"id":"b","text":"5"},{"id":"c","text":"−4"},{"id":"d","text":"−6"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The dot is 5 steps to the left of 0, so it is on −5.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":-6,"to":4,"jumps":10,"dotAt":1,"labelEvery":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030419', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'easy', 1, 'Which point is at (4, 2)?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["L"]'::jsonb, 'Go 4 across and 2 up. That is point L. (Point K is at (2, 4), the numbers the other way round.)', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":2,"y":4,"label":"K"},{"x":4,"y":2,"label":"L"},{"x":5,"y":5,"label":"M"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030420', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'easy', 1, 'The point (3, −4) is in which quadrant?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"fourth"},{"id":"b","text":"first"},{"id":"c","text":"second"},{"id":"d","text":"third"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'x is positive (right) and y is negative (down). Right and down is the fourth quadrant, the bottom right.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030421', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'easy', 1, 'The axis that runs across the page, left to right, is called the ___-axis.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["x"]'::jsonb, 'The x-axis runs across (horizontal) and the y-axis runs up and down (vertical).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030422', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'medium', 1, 'What are the coordinates of the point exactly halfway between P and Q?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["(4, 2)","(4,2)","4, 2","4,2"]'::jsonb, 'P is at (1, 2) and Q is at (7, 2). They are 6 units apart, so halfway is 3 units from P: x = 1 + 3 = 4. y stays 2, so (4, 2).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":1,"y":2,"label":"P"},{"x":7,"y":2,"label":"Q"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030423', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these points have a negative y-coordinate? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"(2, −3)"},{"id":"b","text":"(−4, 1)"},{"id":"c","text":"(−1, −6)"},{"id":"d","text":"(5, 0)"},{"id":"e","text":"(0, −2)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'The y-coordinate is the second number. It is negative in (2, −3), (−1, −6) and (0, −2).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030424', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'medium', 1, 'The point (−3, 0) lies on the y-axis.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Its y-coordinate is 0, so it doesn''t move up or down. It sits on the x-axis, 3 to the left of the origin.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030425', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'hard', 1, 'Start at (−4, 1). Move 6 units right and then 5 units down. Where do you finish?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(2, −4)"},{"id":"b","text":"(−10, 6)"},{"id":"c","text":"(2, 6)"},{"id":"d","text":"(−4, −4)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Right changes x: −4 + 6 = 2. Down changes y: 1 − 5 = −4. You finish at (2, −4).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030426', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'hard', 1, 'A, B and C are three corners of a rectangle. What is the perimeter of the rectangle, in grid units?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["24","24 units"]'::jsonb, 'A to B is 6 − 1 = 5 units, and B to C is 9 − 2 = 7 units. The perimeter is 5 + 7 + 5 + 7 = 24 units.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":10,"rows":8,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":2,"y":6,"label":"A"},{"x":2,"y":1,"label":"B"},{"x":9,"y":1,"label":"C"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030427', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'hard', 1, 'Put these points in order from bottom to top on the coordinate plane.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"(4, −5)"},{"id":"b","text":"(−2, −1)"},{"id":"c","text":"(6, 0)"},{"id":"d","text":"(−7, 3)"},{"id":"e","text":"(1, 8)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Bottom to top depends only on the y-coordinate (the second number): −5, −1, 0, 3, 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030428', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_hard', 1, 'A square has three corners at (−2, −1), (3, −1) and (3, 4). Where is the fourth corner?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−2, 4)"},{"id":"b","text":"(4, −2)"},{"id":"c","text":"(−1, 4)"},{"id":"d","text":"(−2, −4)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The fourth corner is above (−2, −1) and level with (3, 4). So x = −2 and y = 4: (−2, 4). Each side is 5 units.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030429', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_hard', 1, 'How many units apart are the points (2, −5) and (2, 3)?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","8 units"]'::jsonb, 'Both points have x = 2, so count up: from −5 to 0 is 5 units and from 0 to 3 is 3 units. 5 + 3 = 8 units.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006030430', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060304', 'very_hard', 1, 'Point R is reflected (flipped) across the x-axis. Where does it land?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(3, −4)"},{"id":"b","text":"(−3, 4)"},{"id":"c","text":"(−3, −4)"},{"id":"d","text":"(4, 3)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'R is at (3, 4). Flipping across the x-axis moves it the same distance below the axis, so y becomes −4 and x stays 3: (3, −4).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":5,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":3,"y":4,"label":"R"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the picture graph. How many kangaroos were spotted on Monday?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"30"'::jsonb, 'Monday has 3 stars and each star stands for 10 kangaroos. 3 × 10 = 30.', '{"kind":"picture_graph","title":"Kangaroos spotted","thing":"kangaroos","each":10,"rows":[{"label":"Mon","count":3},{"label":"Tue","count":5},{"label":"Wed","count":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_easy', 1, 'On a graph where 1 square stands for 5 cars, 3 squares show 15 cars.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Each square is 5 cars, so 3 squares is 3 × 5 = 15 cars.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_easy', 1, 'The side of a column graph is marked 0, 10, 20, 30, 40. What is the scale going up in?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"ones"},{"id":"b","text":"fives"},{"id":"c","text":"tens"},{"id":"d","text":"forties"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'Each mark is 10 more than the one before, so the scale goes up in tens.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the graph. How much does each gap between the numbers on the side stand for?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"10 L"},{"id":"b","text":"20 L"},{"id":"c","text":"5 L"},{"id":"d","text":"1 L"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The side goes 0, 20, 40, 60 and so on, so each gap stands for 20 L.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Water used (L)","step":20,"columns":[{"label":"Mon","value":40},{"label":"Tue","value":80},{"label":"Wed","value":60},{"label":"Thu","value":100}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the graph. How many litres were used on Wednesday?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["60","60 L"]'::jsonb, 'The Wednesday column reaches the line marked 60, so 60 L.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Water used (L)","step":20,"columns":[{"label":"Mon","value":40},{"label":"Tue","value":80},{"label":"Wed","value":60},{"label":"Thu","value":100}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'easy', 1, 'In a picture graph, each car symbol stands for 15 cars. How many symbols should you draw for 60 cars?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"4"'::jsonb, '60 ÷ 15 = 4, so draw 4 symbols.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'medium', 1, 'A class collected 12, 27, 35 and 48 cans. Which scale is best for the side of their column graph?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1 mark = 1 can, up to 50"},{"id":"b","text":"1 mark = 5 cans, up to 50"},{"id":"c","text":"1 mark = 100 cans, up to 500"},{"id":"d","text":"1 mark = 2 cans, up to 30"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Going up in 5s to 50 fits every amount with 10 marks. Going up in 1s needs 50 marks, going up in 100s squashes every column, and stopping at 30 is too short for 35 and 48.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'medium', 1, 'In a picture graph, each book symbol stands for 8 books. How many symbols show 28 books?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3.5","3 1/2","3 and a half"]'::jsonb, '28 ÷ 8 = 3 with 4 left over. 4 is half of 8, so draw 3 whole symbols and half a symbol: 3.5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'medium', 1, 'On a graph whose scale goes up in 50s, a column ending halfway between 100 and 150 shows 125.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Halfway between 100 and 150 is 125, because 150 − 100 = 50 and half of 50 is 25.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the graph. How many more sausages were sold in Term 3 than in Term 1?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"75"'::jsonb, 'The scale goes up in 25s. Term 3 is 150 and Term 1 is 75, so 150 − 75 = 75 more.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Sausages sold at the school sizzle","step":25,"columns":[{"label":"Term 1","value":75},{"label":"Term 2","value":125},{"label":"Term 3","value":150},{"label":"Term 4","value":100}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the picture graph. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Tom scored 42 goals"},{"id":"b","text":"Ella scored 4 goals"},{"id":"c","text":"Ava scored 6 more goals than Ella"},{"id":"d","text":"Ella and Ava scored 54 goals altogether"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Each picture is 6 goals. Ella 4 × 6 = 24, Tom 7 × 6 = 42, Ava 5 × 6 = 30. Ava has one more picture than Ella, which is 6 goals, and 24 + 30 = 54.', '{"kind":"picture_graph","title":"Goals scored this season","thing":"goals","each":6,"rows":[{"label":"Ella","count":4},{"label":"Tom","count":7},{"label":"Ava","count":5}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'hard', 1, 'Ming is graphing visitors to Taronga Zoo. Each square on her grid stands for 500 visitors. How many squares tall is the column for 4500 visitors?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"9"'::jsonb, '4500 ÷ 500 = 9, so the column is 9 squares tall.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_hard', 1, 'A picture graph uses one symbol for every 20 people. One row shows 6 whole symbols and a quarter of a symbol. How many people is that?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"125"'::jsonb, '6 whole symbols are 6 × 20 = 120 people. A quarter of a symbol is 20 ÷ 4 = 5 people. 120 + 5 = 125.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Jai wants to graph 35, 70, 85 and 120. His grid is only 10 squares tall. Which scale lets every column fit?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1 square = 5"},{"id":"b","text":"1 square = 2"},{"id":"c","text":"1 square = 20"},{"id":"d","text":"1 square = 10"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'The tallest column is 120. With 1 square = 20 it needs 120 ÷ 20 = 6 squares, which fits. 1 square = 10 needs 12 squares, and 5 or 2 need even more.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the graph. How many books were borrowed in the whole year?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3400","3 400"]'::jsonb, 'The scale goes up in 200s, so a column halfway between two lines is 100 more. Term 2 is 900 and Term 3 is 1100. 600 + 900 + 1100 + 800 = 3400.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Library books borrowed","step":200,"columns":[{"label":"Term 1","value":600},{"label":"Term 2","value":900},{"label":"Term 3","value":1100},{"label":"Term 4","value":800}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the picture graph. How many books did 6B read?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"30"'::jsonb, '6B has 6 stars and each star stands for 5 books. 6 × 5 = 30.', '{"kind":"picture_graph","title":"Books read in Book Week","thing":"books","each":5,"rows":[{"label":"6A","count":4},{"label":"6B","count":6},{"label":"6C","count":3}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_easy', 1, 'On a picture graph where each symbol stands for 2 dogs, half a symbol stands for 1 dog.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A whole symbol is 2 dogs, and half of 2 is 1, so half a symbol is 1 dog.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_easy', 1, 'The side of a column graph is marked 0, 50, 100, 150, 200. What is the scale going up in?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"fifties"},{"id":"b","text":"tens"},{"id":"c","text":"fives"},{"id":"d","text":"hundreds"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each mark is 50 more than the one before, so the scale goes up in fifties.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the graph. How much rain fell in February?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["125","125 mm"]'::jsonb, 'The scale goes up in 25s. The February column reaches the line marked 125, so 125 mm.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Rainfall in Lismore (mm)","step":25,"columns":[{"label":"Jan","value":50},{"label":"Feb","value":125},{"label":"Mar","value":75},{"label":"Apr","value":100}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the graph. Which month had twice as much rain as January?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"April"},{"id":"b","text":"February"},{"id":"c","text":"March"},{"id":"d","text":"No month"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'January had 50 mm, and twice 50 is 100. April''s column reaches 100 mm.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Rainfall in Lismore (mm)","step":25,"columns":[{"label":"Jan","value":50},{"label":"Feb","value":125},{"label":"Mar","value":75},{"label":"Apr","value":100}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'easy', 1, 'In a picture graph, each person symbol stands for 25 people. How many symbols should you draw for 150 people?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, '150 ÷ 25 = 6, so draw 6 symbols.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'medium', 1, 'A canteen sold 120, 340, 275 and 410 icy poles in four terms. Which scale is best for the side of their column graph?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1 mark = 50, up to 450"},{"id":"b","text":"1 mark = 1, up to 450"},{"id":"c","text":"1 mark = 500, up to 5000"},{"id":"d","text":"1 mark = 50, up to 300"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Going up in 50s to 450 fits every amount with 9 marks. Going up in 1s needs 450 marks, going up in 500s squashes every column, and stopping at 300 is too short for 340 and 410.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'medium', 1, 'In a picture graph, each bird symbol stands for 6 birds. How many symbols show 15 birds?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2.5","2 1/2","2 and a half"]'::jsonb, '15 ÷ 6 = 2 with 3 left over. 3 is half of 6, so draw 2 whole symbols and half a symbol: 2.5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'medium', 1, 'On a graph whose scale goes up in 20s, a column ending halfway between 60 and 80 shows 65.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Halfway between 60 and 80 is 70, because 80 − 60 = 20 and half of 20 is 10. 60 + 10 = 70, not 65.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the picture graph. How many more fish were caught on Sunday than on Monday?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"24"'::jsonb, 'Each picture is 4 fish. Sunday has 9 pictures and Monday has 3, so Sunday has 6 more pictures: 6 × 4 = 24 more fish.', '{"kind":"picture_graph","title":"Fish caught at the jetty","thing":"fish","each":4,"rows":[{"label":"Sat","count":6},{"label":"Sun","count":9},{"label":"Mon","count":3}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the picture graph. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"36 fish were caught on Sunday"},{"id":"b","text":"6 fish were caught on Saturday"},{"id":"c","text":"72 fish were caught over the three days"},{"id":"d","text":"Monday''s catch was a third of Saturday''s"},{"id":"e","text":"24 fish were caught on Saturday"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Each picture is 4 fish. Saturday 6 × 4 = 24, Sunday 9 × 4 = 36, Monday 3 × 4 = 12. Altogether 24 + 36 + 12 = 72. Monday''s 12 is half of Saturday''s 24, not a third.', '{"kind":"picture_graph","title":"Fish caught at the jetty","thing":"fish","each":4,"rows":[{"label":"Sat","count":6},{"label":"Sun","count":9},{"label":"Mon","count":3}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'hard', 1, 'Leah is graphing how much water her family uses. Each square on her grid stands for 250 L. How many squares tall is the column for 3000 L?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"12"'::jsonb, '3000 ÷ 250 = 12, because 4 squares make 1000 L and 3 × 4 = 12. So the column is 12 squares tall.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_hard', 1, 'A picture graph uses one symbol for every 12 cars. One row shows 4 whole symbols and three quarters of a symbol. How many cars is that?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"57"'::jsonb, '4 whole symbols are 4 × 12 = 48 cars. A quarter of a symbol is 12 ÷ 4 = 3 cars, so three quarters is 9 cars. 48 + 9 = 57.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Tia wants to graph 45, 90, 150 and 210. Her grid is only 8 squares tall. Which scale lets every column fit?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1 square = 30"},{"id":"b","text":"1 square = 25"},{"id":"c","text":"1 square = 20"},{"id":"d","text":"1 square = 10"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The tallest column is 210. With 1 square = 30 it needs 210 ÷ 30 = 7 squares, which fits. 1 square = 25 needs more than 8 squares, and 20 or 10 need even more.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the graph. How many passengers travelled on the ferry over the four days?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6500","6 500"]'::jsonb, 'The scale goes up in 500s, so a column halfway between two lines is 250 more. Tue is 1250 and Thu is 1750. 1500 + 1250 + 2000 + 1750 = 6500.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Ferry passengers","step":500,"columns":[{"label":"Mon","value":1500},{"label":"Tue","value":1250},{"label":"Wed","value":2000},{"label":"Thu","value":1750}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. What was the temperature at 12 pm?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["24","24°C","24 °C"]'::jsonb, 'Find 12 pm along the bottom and go up to the line. It meets the line at 24, so it was 24°C.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Sydney (°C)","step":2,"points":[{"label":"6 am","value":14},{"label":"9 am","value":18},{"label":"12 pm","value":24},{"label":"3 pm","value":26},{"label":"6 pm","value":21},{"label":"9 pm","value":17}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. At which time was it warmest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"9 am"},{"id":"b","text":"12 pm"},{"id":"c","text":"3 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"6 pm"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'The highest point on the line is at 3 pm, at 26°C.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Sydney (°C)","step":2,"points":[{"label":"6 am","value":14},{"label":"9 am","value":18},{"label":"12 pm","value":24},{"label":"3 pm","value":26},{"label":"6 pm","value":21},{"label":"9 pm","value":17}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_easy', 1, 'A line graph is a good way to show how something changes over time.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The line joins the readings in time order, so you can see when the value goes up or down.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. By how many degrees did the temperature rise from 6 am to 12 pm?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"10"'::jsonb, 'It was 14°C at 6 am and 24°C at 12 pm. 24 − 14 = 10 degrees.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Sydney (°C)","step":2,"points":[{"label":"6 am","value":14},{"label":"9 am","value":18},{"label":"12 pm","value":24},{"label":"3 pm","value":26},{"label":"6 pm","value":21},{"label":"9 pm","value":17}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'easy', 1, 'Put these events in order on a timeline, earliest first.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"First Fleet arrives (1788)"},{"id":"b","text":"Gold found near Bathurst (1851)"},{"id":"c","text":"Federation (1901)"},{"id":"d","text":"Sydney Harbour Bridge opens (1932)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Compare the years: 1788, then 1851, then 1901, then 1932.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. What was the puppy''s mass in Week 3?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","5 kg"]'::jsonb, 'Find Wk 3 along the bottom and go up to the line. It meets the line at 5, so 5 kg.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Puppy''s mass (kg)","step":1,"points":[{"label":"Wk 1","value":2},{"label":"Wk 2","value":3},{"label":"Wk 3","value":5},{"label":"Wk 4","value":6},{"label":"Wk 5","value":8},{"label":"Wk 6","value":9}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the line graph. Between which two times did the temperature fall the most?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"12 pm and 3 pm"},{"id":"b","text":"3 pm and 6 pm"},{"id":"c","text":"6 pm and 9 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"6 am and 9 am"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The line goes down after 3 pm. From 3 pm to 6 pm it falls 26 − 21 = 5 degrees, and from 6 pm to 9 pm it falls 21 − 17 = 4 degrees.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Sydney (°C)","step":2,"points":[{"label":"6 am","value":14},{"label":"9 am","value":18},{"label":"12 pm","value":24},{"label":"3 pm","value":26},{"label":"6 pm","value":21},{"label":"9 pm","value":17}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'medium', 1, 'The table is a timeline of events in NSW. How many years after Federation did the Sydney Harbour Bridge open?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["31","31 years"]'::jsonb, 'Federation was in 1901 and the bridge opened in 1932. 1932 − 1901 = 31 years.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Year","Event"],"rows":[["1788","First Fleet arrives"],["1851","Gold found near Bathurst"],["1901","Federation"],["1932","Sydney Harbour Bridge opens"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the line graph. How much mass did the puppy gain from Week 1 to Week 6?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","7 kg"]'::jsonb, 'The puppy was 2 kg in Week 1 and 9 kg in Week 6. 9 − 2 = 7 kg.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Puppy''s mass (kg)","step":1,"points":[{"label":"Wk 1","value":2},{"label":"Wk 2","value":3},{"label":"Wk 3","value":5},{"label":"Wk 4","value":6},{"label":"Wk 5","value":8},{"label":"Wk 6","value":9}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. The puppy gained the same amount of mass every week.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Some weeks it gained 1 kg (Week 1 to 2) and some weeks 2 kg (Week 2 to 3). The line is steeper in some places.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Puppy''s mass (kg)","step":1,"points":[{"label":"Wk 1","value":2},{"label":"Wk 2","value":3},{"label":"Wk 3","value":5},{"label":"Wk 4","value":6},{"label":"Wk 5","value":8},{"label":"Wk 6","value":9}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'hard', 1, 'The table is a timeline of events in NSW. How many years passed between the First Fleet arriving and Federation?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["113","113 years"]'::jsonb, '1901 − 1788: count up 12 years to 1800, then 101 years to 1901. 12 + 101 = 113 years.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Year","Event"],"rows":[["1788","First Fleet arrives"],["1851","Gold found near Bathurst"],["1901","Federation"],["1932","Sydney Harbour Bridge opens"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The highest temperature was 26°C"},{"id":"b","text":"It was warmer at 9 pm than at 6 am"},{"id":"c","text":"The temperature fell between 12 pm and 3 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"The highest and lowest temperatures were 12 degrees apart"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'The top of the line is 26°C. 9 pm (17°C) is warmer than 6 am (14°C). From 12 pm to 3 pm it rose, not fell. 26 − 14 = 12 degrees.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Sydney (°C)","step":2,"points":[{"label":"6 am","value":14},{"label":"9 am","value":18},{"label":"12 pm","value":24},{"label":"3 pm","value":26},{"label":"6 pm","value":21},{"label":"9 pm","value":17}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. In which month did the tank first hold more water than the month before?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["April","Apr"]'::jsonb, 'The level falls from Jan to Feb to Mar (800, 650, 500). In April it goes up to 550, the first rise.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Water in the tank (L)","step":100,"points":[{"label":"Jan","value":800},{"label":"Feb","value":650},{"label":"Mar","value":500},{"label":"Apr","value":550},{"label":"May","value":900},{"label":"Jun","value":1000}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. What is the difference between the most and the least water in the tank?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["500","500 L"]'::jsonb, 'The most is 1000 L in June and the least is 500 L in March. 1000 − 500 = 500 L.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Water in the tank (L)","step":100,"points":[{"label":"Jan","value":800},{"label":"Feb","value":650},{"label":"Mar","value":500},{"label":"Apr","value":550},{"label":"May","value":900},{"label":"Jun","value":1000}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. Between which two months did the amount of water change the most?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Jan and Feb"},{"id":"b","text":"Mar and Apr"},{"id":"c","text":"Apr and May"},{"id":"d","text":"May and Jun"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'Find the steepest part of the line. Jan to Feb: 150 L. Mar to Apr: 50 L. Apr to May: 900 − 550 = 350 L. May to Jun: 100 L.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Water in the tank (L)","step":100,"points":[{"label":"Jan","value":800},{"label":"Feb","value":650},{"label":"Mar","value":500},{"label":"Apr","value":550},{"label":"May","value":900},{"label":"Jun","value":1000}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. What was the temperature at 12 pm?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["14","14°C","14 °C"]'::jsonb, 'Find 12 pm along the bottom and go up to the line. It meets the line at 14, so it was 14°C.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Canberra (°C)","step":2,"points":[{"label":"6 am","value":2},{"label":"9 am","value":8},{"label":"12 pm","value":14},{"label":"3 pm","value":16},{"label":"6 pm","value":12},{"label":"9 pm","value":6}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. At which time was it coldest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6 am"},{"id":"b","text":"9 am"},{"id":"c","text":"6 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"9 pm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The lowest point on the line is at 6 am, at 2°C.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Canberra (°C)","step":2,"points":[{"label":"6 am","value":2},{"label":"9 am","value":8},{"label":"12 pm","value":14},{"label":"3 pm","value":16},{"label":"6 pm","value":12},{"label":"9 pm","value":6}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_easy', 1, 'On a timeline, events are placed in the order they happened.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A timeline is like a number line for dates: earlier events go first and later events go after them.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. How tall was the sunflower in Week 5?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["130","130 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The scale goes up in 20s. In Week 5 the line is halfway between 120 and 140, so it was 130 cm.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Height of a sunflower (cm)","step":20,"points":[{"label":"Wk 1","value":10},{"label":"Wk 2","value":30},{"label":"Wk 3","value":60},{"label":"Wk 4","value":100},{"label":"Wk 5","value":130},{"label":"Wk 6","value":150}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'easy', 1, 'Put these NSW events in order on a timeline, earliest first.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Explorers cross the Blue Mountains (1813)"},{"id":"b","text":"First railway in NSW opens (1855)"},{"id":"c","text":"Sydney Opera House opens (1973)"},{"id":"d","text":"Sydney Olympic Games (2000)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Compare the years: 1813, then 1855, then 1973, then 2000.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the line graph. How many centimetres did the sunflower grow from Week 2 to Week 4?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["70","70 cm"]'::jsonb, 'It was 30 cm in Week 2 and 100 cm in Week 4. 100 − 30 = 70 cm.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Height of a sunflower (cm)","step":20,"points":[{"label":"Wk 1","value":10},{"label":"Wk 2","value":30},{"label":"Wk 3","value":60},{"label":"Wk 4","value":100},{"label":"Wk 5","value":130},{"label":"Wk 6","value":150}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the line graph. Between which two times did the temperature fall the most?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3 pm and 6 pm"},{"id":"b","text":"6 pm and 9 pm"},{"id":"c","text":"12 pm and 3 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"6 am and 9 am"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The line goes down after 3 pm. From 3 pm to 6 pm it falls 16 − 12 = 4 degrees, and from 6 pm to 9 pm it falls 12 − 6 = 6 degrees.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Canberra (°C)","step":2,"points":[{"label":"6 am","value":2},{"label":"9 am","value":8},{"label":"12 pm","value":14},{"label":"3 pm","value":16},{"label":"6 pm","value":12},{"label":"9 pm","value":6}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'medium', 1, 'The table is a timeline of events in NSW. How many years after the first NSW railway did the Sydney Opera House open?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["118","118 years"]'::jsonb, 'The railway opened in 1855 and the Opera House in 1973. 1973 − 1855: 45 years to 1900, then 73 more to 1973. 45 + 73 = 118 years.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Year","Event"],"rows":[["1813","Explorers cross the Blue Mountains"],["1855","First railway in NSW opens"],["1973","Sydney Opera House opens"],["2000","Sydney Olympic Games"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the line graph. How much did the sunflower grow from Week 1 to Week 6?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["140","140 cm"]'::jsonb, 'It was 10 cm in Week 1 and 150 cm in Week 6. 150 − 10 = 140 cm.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Height of a sunflower (cm)","step":20,"points":[{"label":"Wk 1","value":10},{"label":"Wk 2","value":30},{"label":"Wk 3","value":60},{"label":"Wk 4","value":100},{"label":"Wk 5","value":130},{"label":"Wk 6","value":150}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. The sunflower grew the most between Week 3 and Week 4.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Each week it grew 20, 30, 40, 30 and 20 cm. The biggest jump, 40 cm, is from Week 3 (60 cm) to Week 4 (100 cm), where the line is steepest.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Height of a sunflower (cm)","step":20,"points":[{"label":"Wk 1","value":10},{"label":"Wk 2","value":30},{"label":"Wk 3","value":60},{"label":"Wk 4","value":100},{"label":"Wk 5","value":130},{"label":"Wk 6","value":150}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'hard', 1, 'The table is a timeline of events in NSW. How many years passed between the explorers crossing the Blue Mountains and the Sydney Olympic Games?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["187","187 years"]'::jsonb, '2000 − 1813: count up 87 years to 1900, then 100 years to 2000. 87 + 100 = 187 years.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Year","Event"],"rows":[["1813","Explorers cross the Blue Mountains"],["1855","First railway in NSW opens"],["1973","Sydney Opera House opens"],["2000","Sydney Olympic Games"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The temperature went up three times in a row from 6 am"},{"id":"b","text":"It was warmer at 9 pm than at 9 am"},{"id":"c","text":"The temperature dropped 10 degrees from 3 pm to 9 pm"},{"id":"d","text":"The temperature at 6 pm was 12°C"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'It rose from 6 am to 9 am, 9 am to 12 pm and 12 pm to 3 pm. At 9 pm it was 6°C, colder than 8°C at 9 am. From 16°C at 3 pm to 6°C at 9 pm is a drop of 10 degrees, and 6 pm was 12°C.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Temperature in Canberra (°C)","step":2,"points":[{"label":"6 am","value":2},{"label":"9 am","value":8},{"label":"12 pm","value":14},{"label":"3 pm","value":16},{"label":"6 pm","value":12},{"label":"9 pm","value":6}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. In which month was the dam first fuller than the month before?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["May"]'::jsonb, 'The level falls from Jan to Apr (70, 60, 45, 40). In May it goes up to 55%, the first rise.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"How full the dam is (%)","step":10,"points":[{"label":"Jan","value":70},{"label":"Feb","value":60},{"label":"Mar","value":45},{"label":"Apr","value":40},{"label":"May","value":55},{"label":"Jun","value":80}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. How many percent fuller was the dam at its fullest than at its emptiest?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["40","40%"]'::jsonb, 'The fullest is 80% in June and the emptiest is 40% in April. 80 − 40 = 40, so 40% fuller.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"How full the dam is (%)","step":10,"points":[{"label":"Jan","value":70},{"label":"Feb","value":60},{"label":"Mar","value":45},{"label":"Apr","value":40},{"label":"May","value":55},{"label":"Jun","value":80}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060602', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the line graph. Between which two months did the level fall the most?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Jan and Feb"},{"id":"b","text":"Feb and Mar"},{"id":"c","text":"Mar and Apr"},{"id":"d","text":"May and Jun"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Only Jan to Apr show falls. Jan to Feb: 10. Feb to Mar: 60 − 45 = 15 (the line ends halfway between 40 and 50). Mar to Apr: 5. May to Jun is a rise, not a fall.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"How full the dam is (%)","step":10,"points":[{"label":"Jan","value":70},{"label":"Feb","value":60},{"label":"Mar","value":45},{"label":"Apr","value":40},{"label":"May","value":55},{"label":"Jun","value":80}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the chance of this spinner landing on red? Write it as a fraction.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/4"]'::jsonb, 'There are 4 equal sections and 1 is red, so the chance is 1/4.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","blue","green","yellow"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_easy', 1, 'A probability of 0 means the event is impossible.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Probability goes from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_easy', 1, 'When you flip a coin, what is the chance of getting heads, as a percentage?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"50%"},{"id":"b","text":"25%"},{"id":"c","text":"100%"},{"id":"d","text":"10%"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Heads is 1 of 2 equally likely outcomes. 1/2 = 50%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'easy', 1, 'You pick one counter from this bag without looking. What is the probability it is red, as a decimal?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.3",".3"]'::jsonb, 'There are 3 + 5 + 2 = 10 counters and 3 are red. 3/10 = 0.3.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":3},{"colour":"blue","count":5},{"colour":"green","count":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'easy', 1, 'What is the probability of rolling a 4 on a normal six-sided dice? Write it as a fraction.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/6"]'::jsonb, 'There are 6 equally likely faces and only one is a 4, so the probability is 1/6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'easy', 1, 'Put these probabilities in order from least likely to most likely.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0"},{"id":"b","text":"0.2"},{"id":"c","text":"1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"75%"},{"id":"e","text":"1"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them to decimals: 0, 0.2, 0.5, 0.75, 1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'medium', 1, 'What is the probability of this spinner landing on blue?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/2"},{"id":"b","text":"4/5"},{"id":"c","text":"1/4"},{"id":"d","text":"3/8"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '4 of the 8 equal sections are blue. 4/8 = 1/2.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","blue","red","blue","yellow","blue","red","blue"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'medium', 1, 'What is the probability of rolling an even number on a normal dice, as a percentage?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["50","50%"]'::jsonb, '3 of the 6 faces are even (2, 4, 6). 3/6 = 1/2 = 50%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'medium', 1, 'You pick one counter from this bag without looking. What is the chance it is purple, as a percentage?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["25","25%"]'::jsonb, 'There are 20 counters and 5 are purple. 5/20 = 1/4 = 25%.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"purple","count":5},{"colour":"orange","count":15}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'hard', 1, 'Zara rolled a dice 60 times. She rolled a 6 twelve times. What fraction of her rolls were 6s, in simplest form?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/5"]'::jsonb, '12 out of 60 is 12/60. Divide the top and bottom by 12: 1/5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'hard', 1, 'Ben spun a spinner 50 times and it landed on green 20 times. As a percentage, how often did it land on green?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"40%"},{"id":"b","text":"20%"},{"id":"c","text":"50%"},{"id":"d","text":"30%"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '20 out of 50 is 20/50. Double the top and bottom: 40/100 = 40%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'hard', 1, 'You pick one counter from this bag. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The chance of green is 0.5"},{"id":"b","text":"The chance of red is 20%"},{"id":"c","text":"The chance of yellow is 3/5"},{"id":"d","text":"The chance of red or yellow is 1/2"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'There are 20 counters. Green 10/20 = 0.5. Red 4/20 = 20%. Yellow 6/20 = 3/10, not 3/5. Red or yellow 10/20 = 1/2.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":4},{"colour":"yellow","count":6},{"colour":"green","count":10}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bag has 24 counters. The probability of picking a blue counter is 0.375. How many counters are blue?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"9"'::jsonb, '0.375 is 3/8. One eighth of 24 is 3, so three eighths is 3 × 3 = 9 blue counters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_hard', 1, 'Mae spun a spinner 40 times: red 21 times, blue 9 times and green 10 times. Which spinner did she most likely use?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Half red, a quarter blue, a quarter green"},{"id":"b","text":"A third each of red, blue and green"},{"id":"c","text":"Three quarters red, an eighth blue, an eighth green"},{"id":"d","text":"Half blue, a quarter red, a quarter green"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Red came up about 20 times out of 40 (about half). Blue and green each came up about 10 times out of 40 (about a quarter).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bag has 3 red and 5 blue marbles. How many red marbles must be added to make the chance of red 50%?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"2"'::jsonb, 'A 50% chance means the same number of red and blue. There are 5 blue, so you need 5 red. 5 − 3 = 2 more red marbles.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the chance of this spinner landing on green? Write it as a fraction.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/6"]'::jsonb, 'There are 6 equal sections and 1 is green, so the chance is 1/6.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","blue","green","yellow","purple","orange"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_easy', 1, 'A probability of 1 means the event is certain.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Probability goes from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the chance of rolling a number less than 7 on a normal six-sided dice?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"certain"},{"id":"b","text":"impossible"},{"id":"c","text":"even chance"},{"id":"d","text":"unlikely"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Every face (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6) is less than 7, so it is certain to happen. Its probability is 1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'easy', 1, 'You pick one counter from this bag without looking. What is the probability it is blue, as a decimal?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.75",".75"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1 + 3 = 4 counters and 3 are blue. 3/4 = 0.75.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":1},{"colour":"blue","count":3}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'easy', 1, 'The letters of the word NETBALL are written on 7 cards. You pick one card without looking. What is the probability it is an L? Write it as a fraction.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2/7"]'::jsonb, 'There are 7 cards and 2 of them are L, so the probability is 2/7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'easy', 1, 'Put these probabilities in order from least likely to most likely.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.1"},{"id":"b","text":"1/4"},{"id":"c","text":"40%"},{"id":"d","text":"0.6"},{"id":"e","text":"9/10"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change them to decimals: 0.1, 0.25, 0.4, 0.6, 0.9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'medium', 1, 'What is the probability of this spinner landing on yellow?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2/3"},{"id":"b","text":"4/2"},{"id":"c","text":"1/3"},{"id":"d","text":"4/10"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '4 of the 6 equal sections are yellow. 4/6 = 2/3.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["yellow","green","yellow","yellow","green","yellow"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'medium', 1, 'What is the probability of rolling a number greater than 4 on a normal dice? Write it as a fraction in simplest form.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/3"]'::jsonb, 'Only 5 and 6 are greater than 4, so 2 of the 6 faces. 2/6 = 1/3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'medium', 1, 'You pick one counter from this bag without looking. What is the chance it is green, as a percentage?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["70","70%"]'::jsonb, 'There are 10 counters and 7 are green. 7/10 = 70/100 = 70%.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":3},{"colour":"green","count":7}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'hard', 1, 'Kate tossed a coin 80 times and got 36 heads. What fraction of her tosses were heads, in simplest form?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9/20"]'::jsonb, '36 out of 80 is 36/80. Divide the top and bottom by 4: 9/20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'hard', 1, 'Omar spun a spinner 60 times and it landed on blue 15 times. As a percentage, how often did it land on blue?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"25%"},{"id":"b","text":"15%"},{"id":"c","text":"60%"},{"id":"d","text":"45%"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '15 out of 60 is 15/60, which simplifies to 1/4. 1/4 = 25%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'hard', 1, 'You pick one counter from this bag. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The chance of blue is 50%"},{"id":"b","text":"The chance of yellow is 1/3"},{"id":"c","text":"The chance of red is 0.2"},{"id":"d","text":"The chance of not getting red is 5/6"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'There are 12 counters. Blue 6/12 = 50%. Yellow 4/12 = 1/3. Red 2/12 = 1/6, which is not 0.2. Not red is 10/12 = 5/6.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"blue","count":6},{"colour":"yellow","count":4},{"colour":"red","count":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bag has 40 marbles. The probability of picking a green marble is 0.15. How many marbles are green?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, '0.15 is 15%. 10% of 40 is 4 and 5% is 2, so 15% of 40 is 4 + 2 = 6 green marbles.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_hard', 1, 'Sam rolls a normal dice 120 times. About how many times would you expect him to roll a 5?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"about 20"},{"id":"b","text":"about 5"},{"id":"c","text":"about 60"},{"id":"d","text":"about 50"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The chance of a 5 is 1/6 on each roll. 1/6 of 120 is 120 ÷ 6 = 20, so about 20 times.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006060330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000060603', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bag has 6 green and 4 white counters. How many white counters must be added to make the probability of green 0.4?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"5"'::jsonb, '0.4 = 2/5, so the 6 green counters must be 2 fifths of the bag. 1 fifth is 3, so the bag needs 15 counters. It has 10, so add 5 white counters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"becaus"},{"id":"b","text":"because"},{"id":"c","text":"becuz"},{"id":"d","text":"becoz"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Because'' is a tricky word to remember: b-e-c-a-u-s-e. It has ''cause'' at the end.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_easy', 1, 'The plural of ''cat'' is ''cats''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'For most words, you just add -s to show more than one: one cat, two cats.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_easy', 1, 'jump + ing = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"jumping"'::jsonb, '''Jump'' ends in two consonants, so you just add -ing: jumping.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'easy', 1, 'Write the plural of ''box''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"boxes"'::jsonb, 'Words that end in x, s, sh or ch add -es to make the plural: one box, two boxes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'easy', 1, 'Which word correctly finishes the sentence? ''I can ___ the birds singing.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"here"},{"id":"b","text":"hear"},{"id":"c","text":"hair"},{"id":"d","text":"heer"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Hear'' is what you do with your ear. Notice ''ear'' is inside ''hear''. ''Here'' means a place.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'easy', 1, 'Put these syllables in order to spell ''butterfly''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"but"},{"id":"b","text":"ter"},{"id":"c","text":"fly"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Clap the word as you say it: but-ter-fly. Breaking a long word into syllables helps you spell it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'medium', 1, 'Add ''ing'' to the word ''hop''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"hopping"'::jsonb, '''Hop'' has a short vowel and ends in one consonant, so you double the p before adding -ing: hopping.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'medium', 1, 'What is the plural of ''baby''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"babys"},{"id":"b","text":"babies"},{"id":"c","text":"babyes"},{"id":"d","text":"babeys"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'When a word ends in a consonant then y, change the y to i and add -es: baby becomes babies.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'medium', 1, 'The prefix ''un'' means ''not''. un + happy = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"unhappy"'::jsonb, 'A prefix goes at the front of a word and the base word does not change: un + happy = unhappy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses ''they''re'', ''their'' and ''there'' correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"They''re going to leave their bags over there."},{"id":"b","text":"There going to leave they''re bags over their."},{"id":"c","text":"Their going to leave there bags over they''re."},{"id":"d","text":"They''re going to leave there bags over their."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''They''re'' means ''they are''. ''Their'' means belonging to them. ''There'' is a place (it has ''here'' inside it).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'hard', 1, 'Add ''ed'' to the word ''carry''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"carried"'::jsonb, '''Carry'' ends in a consonant then y, so change the y to i before adding -ed: carried.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'hard', 1, 'The plural of ''leaf'' is ''leafs''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Many words ending in f change the f to v and add -es: one leaf, two leaves.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which word means ''full of care'' and is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"carefull"},{"id":"b","text":"careful"},{"id":"c","text":"carfull"},{"id":"d","text":"carful"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The suffix -ful has only one l. Keep the whole base word ''care'' and add -ful: careful.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_hard', 1, 'happy + ly = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"happily"'::jsonb, '''Happy'' ends in a consonant then y, so change the y to i before adding -ly: happily.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Each word has a suffix added. Which ones are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"hoping"},{"id":"b","text":"hopeing"},{"id":"c","text":"running"},{"id":"d","text":"runing"},{"id":"e","text":"smiled"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Drop the silent e before -ing or -ed (hope → hoping, smile → smiled). Double the last letter after a short vowel (run → running).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"frend"},{"id":"b","text":"friend"},{"id":"c","text":"freind"},{"id":"d","text":"frind"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Friend'' is a tricky word: f-r-i-e-n-d. A trick to remember it: a friend is there to the ''end''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_easy', 1, 'The plural of ''dish'' is ''dishes''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Words that end in sh add -es to make the plural, because ''dishs'' is hard to say: one dish, two dishes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_easy', 1, 'play + ed = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"played"'::jsonb, '''Play'' ends in a vowel then y, so you keep the y and just add -ed: played.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'easy', 1, 'Write the plural of ''lunch''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"lunches"'::jsonb, 'Words that end in ch add -es to make the plural: one lunch, two lunches.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'easy', 1, 'Which word correctly finishes the sentence? ''I ate ___ apples at recess.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to"},{"id":"b","text":"two"},{"id":"c","text":"too"},{"id":"d","text":"tow"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Two'' is the number 2. ''To'' shows where something goes, and ''too'' means also or very.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'easy', 1, 'Put these syllables in order to spell ''kangaroo''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"kan"},{"id":"b","text":"ga"},{"id":"c","text":"roo"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Clap the word as you say it: kan-ga-roo. Spell one syllable at a time.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'medium', 1, 'Add ''ed'' to the word ''stop''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"stopped"'::jsonb, '''Stop'' has a short vowel and ends in one consonant, so you double the p before adding -ed: stopped.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'medium', 1, 'What is the plural of ''pony''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"ponys"},{"id":"b","text":"ponies"},{"id":"c","text":"ponyes"},{"id":"d","text":"poneys"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Pony'' ends in a consonant then y, so change the y to i and add -es: ponies.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'medium', 1, 'The prefix ''re'' means ''again''. re + write = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"rewrite"'::jsonb, 'A prefix goes at the front and the base word stays the same: re + write = rewrite, which means write again.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses ''your'' and ''you''re'' correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"You''re going to need your raincoat today."},{"id":"b","text":"Your going to need you''re raincoat today."},{"id":"c","text":"You''re going to need you''re raincoat today."},{"id":"d","text":"Your going to need your raincoat today."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''You''re'' is short for ''you are'' (you are going). ''Your'' means belonging to you (your raincoat).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'hard', 1, 'Add ''est'' to the word ''big''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"biggest"'::jsonb, '''Big'' has a short vowel and ends in one consonant, so double the g before adding -est: biggest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'hard', 1, 'The plural of ''wolf'' is ''wolves''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Many words ending in f change the f to v and add -es: one wolf, a pack of wolves.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the plural of ''child''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"childs"},{"id":"b","text":"children"},{"id":"c","text":"childrens"},{"id":"d","text":"childes"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Some plurals don''t follow the rules and must be learnt. One child, many children. ''Childrens'' is never right.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_hard', 1, 'beauty + ful = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"beautiful"'::jsonb, '''Beauty'' ends in a consonant then y, so change the y to i before adding -ful (with one l): beautiful.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003110130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which plurals are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"cherries"},{"id":"b","text":"monkeys"},{"id":"c","text":"boxs"},{"id":"d","text":"foxes"},{"id":"e","text":"ladys"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Consonant + y changes to -ies (cherries, ladies). Vowel + y just adds -s (monkeys). Words ending in x add -es (boxes, foxes).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the same as ''big''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"tiny"},{"id":"b","text":"large"},{"id":"c","text":"short"},{"id":"d","text":"slow"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Large'' and ''big'' are synonyms: they mean the same thing.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the opposite of ''hot''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"warm"},{"id":"b","text":"cold"},{"id":"c","text":"sunny"},{"id":"d","text":"wet"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'An antonym is a word with the opposite meaning. The opposite of hot is cold.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_easy', 1, '''Happy'' and ''glad'' mean almost the same thing.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''Happy'' and ''glad'' are synonyms. Both describe feeling good.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'easy', 1, 'The opposite of ''early'' is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"late"'::jsonb, 'If you are not early, you are late. ''Early'' and ''late'' are antonyms.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'easy', 1, '''The kookaburra perched on a branch high in the gum tree.'' What does ''perched'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"sat or rested"},{"id":"b","text":"flew away"},{"id":"c","text":"sang loudly"},{"id":"d","text":"fell down"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The bird is on a branch, so it must be sitting there. ''Perched'' means sat or rested on something.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'easy', 1, 'Which words belong to the same word family as ''play''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"player"},{"id":"b","text":"playful"},{"id":"c","text":"plate"},{"id":"d","text":"replay"},{"id":"e","text":"plan"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Player, playful and replay all have the base word ''play''. ''Plate'' and ''plan'' only start with the same letters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from smallest to biggest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"tiny"},{"id":"b","text":"small"},{"id":"c","text":"large"},{"id":"d","text":"enormous"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Tiny is smaller than small. Large is big, and enormous is very, very big.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'medium', 1, '''The puppy was exhausted after the long walk and fell asleep on the mat.'' What does ''exhausted'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"very tired"},{"id":"b","text":"very hungry"},{"id":"c","text":"excited"},{"id":"d","text":"lost"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'After a long walk the puppy fell asleep, so ''exhausted'' must mean very tired.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'medium', 1, 'Which word is the opposite of ''ancient'': ''old'' or ''modern''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"modern"'::jsonb, '''Ancient'' means very old, so its opposite is ''modern'', which means new or from today.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'hard', 1, 'Which word describes someone who is never afraid?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"fearless"},{"id":"b","text":"fearful"},{"id":"c","text":"careless"},{"id":"d","text":"helpless"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The suffix -less means ''without''. Fearless means without fear. Fearful means full of fear.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'hard', 1, 'Which words could you use instead of ''said''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"whispered"},{"id":"b","text":"shouted"},{"id":"c","text":"walked"},{"id":"d","text":"replied"},{"id":"e","text":"jumped"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Whispered, shouted and replied are all ways of saying something. Walked and jumped are ways of moving.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'hard', 1, '''Furious'' is a stronger word than ''cross''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Both words mean angry, but ''cross'' is a little angry and ''furious'' is very angry.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_hard', 1, 'In science, what do we call an animal that eats only plants?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"carnivore"},{"id":"b","text":"herbivore"},{"id":"c","text":"omnivore"},{"id":"d","text":"predator"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A herbivore eats plants (think of ''herbs''). A carnivore eats meat and an omnivore eats both.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these words in order from coldest to hottest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"freezing"},{"id":"b","text":"cool"},{"id":"c","text":"warm"},{"id":"d","text":"boiling"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Freezing is colder than cool. Warm is a bit hot, and boiling is the hottest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_hard', 1, '''Mia was reluctant to jump into the cold pool, so she dipped one toe in first.'' What does ''reluctant'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"not keen"},{"id":"b","text":"very happy"},{"id":"c","text":"in a hurry"},{"id":"d","text":"too tall"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Mia only dipped one toe in, so she did not really want to jump. ''Reluctant'' means not keen.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the same as ''small''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"little"},{"id":"b","text":"huge"},{"id":"c","text":"loud"},{"id":"d","text":"fast"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Little'' and ''small'' are synonyms: they mean the same thing.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the opposite of ''full''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"empty"},{"id":"b","text":"heavy"},{"id":"c","text":"big"},{"id":"d","text":"round"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A full cup has lots in it and an empty cup has nothing. ''Full'' and ''empty'' are antonyms.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_easy', 1, '''Shout'' and ''whisper'' mean the same thing.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'To shout is to talk very loudly and to whisper is to talk very softly. They are opposites.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'easy', 1, 'The opposite of ''wet'' is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"dry"'::jsonb, 'After a swim you are wet, and a towel makes you dry. ''Wet'' and ''dry'' are antonyms.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'easy', 1, '''The wombat scurried into its burrow when it heard the dog bark.'' What does ''scurried'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"moved quickly"},{"id":"b","text":"fell asleep"},{"id":"c","text":"ate slowly"},{"id":"d","text":"sang"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The wombat heard a dog and wanted to get away, so ''scurried'' must mean moved quickly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'easy', 1, 'Which words belong to the same word family as ''help''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"helpful"},{"id":"b","text":"helper"},{"id":"c","text":"helmet"},{"id":"d","text":"unhelpful"},{"id":"e","text":"hello"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Helpful, helper and unhelpful all have the base word ''help''. ''Helmet'' and ''hello'' only start with the same letters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from slowest to fastest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"crawl"},{"id":"b","text":"walk"},{"id":"c","text":"jog"},{"id":"d","text":"sprint"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Crawling is slowest. Walking is faster, jogging is faster again, and a sprint is running as fast as you can.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'medium', 1, '''Jai was famished after swimming all morning, so he ate three sandwiches.'' What does ''famished'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"very hungry"},{"id":"b","text":"very cold"},{"id":"c","text":"sleepy"},{"id":"d","text":"sad"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Jai ate three sandwiches, so he must have been very hungry. ''Famished'' means very hungry.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'medium', 1, 'Which word is the opposite of ''brave'': ''bold'' or ''timid''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"timid"'::jsonb, 'A brave person is not afraid. A timid person is shy and easily scared, so ''timid'' is the opposite. ''Bold'' means almost the same as brave.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'hard', 1, 'Which word means ''full of colour''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"colourful"},{"id":"b","text":"colourless"},{"id":"c","text":"colouring"},{"id":"d","text":"discolour"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The suffix -ful means ''full of'', so colourful means full of colour. The suffix -less means without.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'hard', 1, 'Which words could you use instead of ''walked''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"strolled"},{"id":"b","text":"marched"},{"id":"c","text":"tiptoed"},{"id":"d","text":"laughed"},{"id":"e","text":"yelled"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Strolled, marched and tiptoed are all ways of walking. Laughed and yelled are sounds you make, not ways to walk.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'hard', 1, '''Chilly'' is a stronger word than ''freezing''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Both words mean cold, but ''chilly'' is a little cold and ''freezing'' is very cold. ''Freezing'' is the stronger word.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_hard', 1, 'In science, what do we call a mammal that carries its baby in a pouch?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a marsupial"},{"id":"b","text":"a reptile"},{"id":"c","text":"an insect"},{"id":"d","text":"an amphibian"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Kangaroos, koalas and wombats carry their babies in a pouch. These animals are called marsupials.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these words in order from saddest to happiest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"miserable"},{"id":"b","text":"sad"},{"id":"c","text":"happy"},{"id":"d","text":"overjoyed"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Miserable is very sad, so it comes first. Then sad, then happy, and overjoyed means extremely happy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003120130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031201', 'very_hard', 1, '''The bush track was so narrow that only one person could walk on it at a time.'' What does ''narrow'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"not wide"},{"id":"b","text":"very long"},{"id":"c","text":"muddy"},{"id":"d","text":"steep"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Only one person could fit on the track at a time, so it must have been thin. ''Narrow'' means not wide.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_easy', 1, 'What colour was Sam''s hat?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"blue"},{"id":"b","text":"red"},{"id":"c","text":"green"},{"id":"d","text":"yellow"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The first sentence says, ''Sam had a red hat.''', NULL, '{"title":"The Lost Hat","text":"Sam had a red hat. He wore it to the beach every day.\n\nOne windy morning, a big gust blew the hat off his head. It rolled across the sand and into the water. Sam ran after it, but the waves carried it away.\n\nThat afternoon, Sam''s dog, Biscuit, trotted up the beach. In his mouth was a soggy red hat. Sam laughed and gave Biscuit a big hug."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Sam''s hat blew away on a windy morning.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The text says, ''One windy morning, a big gust blew the hat off his head.''', NULL, '{"title":"The Lost Hat","text":"Sam had a red hat. He wore it to the beach every day.\n\nOne windy morning, a big gust blew the hat off his head. It rolled across the sand and into the water. Sam ran after it, but the waves carried it away.\n\nThat afternoon, Sam''s dog, Biscuit, trotted up the beach. In his mouth was a soggy red hat. Sam laughed and gave Biscuit a big hug."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_easy', 1, 'What was the name of Sam''s dog?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"Biscuit"'::jsonb, 'The text says, ''Sam''s dog, Biscuit, trotted up the beach.''', NULL, '{"title":"The Lost Hat","text":"Sam had a red hat. He wore it to the beach every day.\n\nOne windy morning, a big gust blew the hat off his head. It rolled across the sand and into the water. Sam ran after it, but the waves carried it away.\n\nThat afternoon, Sam''s dog, Biscuit, trotted up the beach. In his mouth was a soggy red hat. Sam laughed and gave Biscuit a big hug."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'easy', 1, 'What do echidnas eat?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"leaves and grass"},{"id":"b","text":"ants and termites"},{"id":"c","text":"fish"},{"id":"d","text":"berries"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says, ''Echidnas eat ants and termites.''', NULL, '{"title":"Echidnas","text":"Echidnas are small Australian animals covered in sharp spines. They have a long, thin snout and a sticky tongue.\n\nEchidnas eat ants and termites. They use their strong claws to dig into ant nests, then catch the insects with their tongue. Echidnas do not have teeth.\n\nAn echidna is a mammal that lays eggs. A baby echidna is called a puggle. It lives in a pouch on its mother''s belly until its spines start to grow."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'easy', 1, 'A baby echidna is called a ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"puggle"'::jsonb, 'The last paragraph says, ''A baby echidna is called a puggle.''', NULL, '{"title":"Echidnas","text":"Echidnas are small Australian animals covered in sharp spines. They have a long, thin snout and a sticky tongue.\n\nEchidnas eat ants and termites. They use their strong claws to dig into ant nests, then catch the insects with their tongue. Echidnas do not have teeth.\n\nAn echidna is a mammal that lays eggs. A baby echidna is called a puggle. It lives in a pouch on its mother''s belly until its spines start to grow."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these facts are in the text? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Echidnas lay eggs."},{"id":"b","text":"Echidnas have sharp spines."},{"id":"c","text":"Echidnas have big teeth."},{"id":"d","text":"Echidnas live in trees."},{"id":"e","text":"Echidnas have a sticky tongue."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, 'The text says echidnas lay eggs, have sharp spines and a sticky tongue. It says they do NOT have teeth, and it never says they live in trees.', NULL, '{"title":"Echidnas","text":"Echidnas are small Australian animals covered in sharp spines. They have a long, thin snout and a sticky tongue.\n\nEchidnas eat ants and termites. They use their strong claws to dig into ant nests, then catch the insects with their tongue. Echidnas do not have teeth.\n\nAn echidna is a mammal that lays eggs. A baby echidna is called a puggle. It lives in a pouch on its mother''s belly until its spines start to grow."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'medium', 1, 'Put these events in the order they happened.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Ava and Dad got up at six o''clock."},{"id":"b","text":"They baked lamingtons."},{"id":"c","text":"They arrived at the school hall."},{"id":"d","text":"Every lamington was sold."},{"id":"e","text":"Ava counted the money in the tin."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Follow the story from start to end: getting up, baking, arriving, selling out, then counting the money.', NULL, '{"title":"The Cake Sale","text":"Ava and her dad got up at six o''clock on Saturday. They baked three trays of lamingtons for the school cake sale.\n\nWhen they arrived at the school hall, people were already waiting in a long line. By ten o''clock, every lamington was gone.\n\n“We should have made more,” said Ava, counting the money in the tin. Dad smiled. “Next year we''ll bake six trays.”\n\nThe money they raised would help buy new books for the library."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'medium', 1, 'Why did Ava say, “We should have made more”?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The lamingtons sold out quickly."},{"id":"b","text":"The lamingtons were burnt."},{"id":"c","text":"Nobody came to the sale."},{"id":"d","text":"Dad ate them all."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'People lined up early and every lamington was gone by ten o''clock, so Ava wished they had more to sell.', NULL, '{"title":"The Cake Sale","text":"Ava and her dad got up at six o''clock on Saturday. They baked three trays of lamingtons for the school cake sale.\n\nWhen they arrived at the school hall, people were already waiting in a long line. By ten o''clock, every lamington was gone.\n\n“We should have made more,” said Ava, counting the money in the tin. Dad smiled. “Next year we''ll bake six trays.”\n\nThe money they raised would help buy new books for the library."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'medium', 1, 'How many trays of lamingtons did Ava and Dad bake this year?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3","three"]'::jsonb, 'The text says they baked three trays. Six trays is what Dad plans for next year.', NULL, '{"title":"The Cake Sale","text":"Ava and her dad got up at six o''clock on Saturday. They baked three trays of lamingtons for the school cake sale.\n\nWhen they arrived at the school hall, people were already waiting in a long line. By ten o''clock, every lamington was gone.\n\n“We should have made more,” said Ava, counting the money in the tin. Dad smiled. “Next year we''ll bake six trays.”\n\nThe money they raised would help buy new books for the library."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'hard', 1, 'Why do you think Grandpa could not look after the garden himself?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He uses a wheelchair and finds gardening hard."},{"id":"b","text":"He did not like vegetables."},{"id":"c","text":"He was away on holiday."},{"id":"d","text":"He had lost his gloves."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The text says Grandpa ''wheeled his chair'', which is a clue that he uses a wheelchair, so gardening would be hard for him.', NULL, '{"title":"Grandpa''s Garden","text":"Every afternoon, Grandpa wheeled his chair to the window and looked out at the vegetable garden. The tomato plants were drooping, and weeds poked up between the carrots.\n\nOn Saturday, Leo arrived with gloves, a trowel and a watering can. He pulled out the weeds and gave every plant a long drink. Grandpa watched from the window, pointing and calling out tips.\n\nWhen Leo came inside, muddy and sweaty, Grandpa''s eyes were shining. “It looks just like it used to,” he said softly."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'hard', 1, 'How did Grandpa feel at the end of the story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"happy and thankful"},{"id":"b","text":"angry"},{"id":"c","text":"bored"},{"id":"d","text":"scared"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'His ''eyes were shining'' and he said the garden looked like it used to. These clues show he was happy and thankful.', NULL, '{"title":"Grandpa''s Garden","text":"Every afternoon, Grandpa wheeled his chair to the window and looked out at the vegetable garden. The tomato plants were drooping, and weeds poked up between the carrots.\n\nOn Saturday, Leo arrived with gloves, a trowel and a watering can. He pulled out the weeds and gave every plant a long drink. Grandpa watched from the window, pointing and calling out tips.\n\nWhen Leo came inside, muddy and sweaty, Grandpa''s eyes were shining. “It looks just like it used to,” he said softly."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'hard', 1, 'The garden was neat and well cared for before Leo came.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Before Leo came, the tomato plants were drooping and weeds were growing, so the garden was not well cared for.', NULL, '{"title":"Grandpa''s Garden","text":"Every afternoon, Grandpa wheeled his chair to the window and looked out at the vegetable garden. The tomato plants were drooping, and weeds poked up between the carrots.\n\nOn Saturday, Leo arrived with gloves, a trowel and a watering can. He pulled out the weeds and gave every plant a long drink. Grandpa watched from the window, pointing and calling out tips.\n\nWhen Leo came inside, muddy and sweaty, Grandpa''s eyes were shining. “It looks just like it used to,” he said softly."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the main purpose of this text?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to give families information about the carnival"},{"id":"b","text":"to tell a story about a swimmer"},{"id":"c","text":"to explain how to swim"},{"id":"d","text":"to sell towels"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The notice tells readers when and where the carnival is, what to bring and how to get there. It gives information.', NULL, '{"title":"Swimming Carnival","text":"Kurrajong Public School Swimming Carnival\n\nWhen: Friday 14 February, 9 am to 2 pm\nWhere: Penrith Pool\n\nAll students should wear their house colour and bring a hat, sunscreen, a towel and a packed lunch. Students who are not swimming in a race will cheer from the stands.\n\nThe bus leaves school at 8:30 am. Students who miss the bus must be dropped at the pool by a parent.\n\nIf it rains, the carnival will be held on the following Friday."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_hard', 1, 'It rains on Friday 14 February. On what date will the carnival be held instead?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["21 February","Friday 21 February","21st February","February 21","21 Feb","21"]'::jsonb, 'If it rains, the carnival moves to the next Friday. One week after 14 February is 14 + 7 = 21 February.', NULL, '{"title":"Swimming Carnival","text":"Kurrajong Public School Swimming Carnival\n\nWhen: Friday 14 February, 9 am to 2 pm\nWhere: Penrith Pool\n\nAll students should wear their house colour and bring a hat, sunscreen, a towel and a packed lunch. Students who are not swimming in a race will cheer from the stands.\n\nThe bus leaves school at 8:30 am. Students who miss the bus must be dropped at the pool by a parent.\n\nIf it rains, the carnival will be held on the following Friday."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Jack is not swimming in any races. Which things should he still bring? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"a hat"},{"id":"b","text":"a packed lunch"},{"id":"c","text":"a towel"},{"id":"d","text":"a skateboard"},{"id":"e","text":"a pet"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The notice says ALL students bring a hat, sunscreen, a towel and lunch. Jack will still be there cheering from the stands.', NULL, '{"title":"Swimming Carnival","text":"Kurrajong Public School Swimming Carnival\n\nWhen: Friday 14 February, 9 am to 2 pm\nWhere: Penrith Pool\n\nAll students should wear their house colour and bring a hat, sunscreen, a towel and a packed lunch. Students who are not swimming in a race will cheer from the stands.\n\nThe bus leaves school at 8:30 am. Students who miss the bus must be dropped at the pool by a parent.\n\nIf it rains, the carnival will be held on the following Friday."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_easy', 1, 'What colour was Mila''s kite?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"red"},{"id":"b","text":"blue"},{"id":"c","text":"yellow"},{"id":"d","text":"green"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'The first sentence says, ''Mila got a yellow kite for her birthday.''', NULL, '{"title":"The Yellow Kite","text":"Mila got a yellow kite for her birthday. On Sunday she took it to the park with her big brother, Josh.\n\nAt first the kite would not fly. It kept crashing onto the grass. Josh told Mila to run into the wind. She ran as fast as she could, and the kite rose up, up, up into the sky.\n\n“It''s flying!” Mila shouted. The kite danced above the trees until the sun went down."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_easy', 1, 'The kite flew straight away on the first try.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The text says, ''At first the kite would not fly. It kept crashing onto the grass.''', NULL, '{"title":"The Yellow Kite","text":"Mila got a yellow kite for her birthday. On Sunday she took it to the park with her big brother, Josh.\n\nAt first the kite would not fly. It kept crashing onto the grass. Josh told Mila to run into the wind. She ran as fast as she could, and the kite rose up, up, up into the sky.\n\n“It''s flying!” Mila shouted. The kite danced above the trees until the sun went down."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Who went to the park with Mila?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["Josh","her brother","her big brother","brother"]'::jsonb, 'The text says she took the kite to the park with her big brother, Josh.', NULL, '{"title":"The Yellow Kite","text":"Mila got a yellow kite for her birthday. On Sunday she took it to the park with her big brother, Josh.\n\nAt first the kite would not fly. It kept crashing onto the grass. Josh told Mila to run into the wind. She ran as fast as she could, and the kite rose up, up, up into the sky.\n\n“It''s flying!” Mila shouted. The kite danced above the trees until the sun went down."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'easy', 1, 'What does a blue-tongue lizard do when it feels scared?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It sticks out its blue tongue and hisses."},{"id":"b","text":"It climbs a tall tree."},{"id":"c","text":"It changes colour."},{"id":"d","text":"It runs into the water."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says it opens its mouth wide, sticks out its blue tongue and hisses.', NULL, '{"title":"Blue-tongue Lizards","text":"Blue-tongue lizards live in many backyards in Australia. They have a short, fat body and short legs. Their most famous feature is their bright blue tongue.\n\nWhen a blue-tongue lizard feels scared, it opens its mouth wide, sticks out its blue tongue and hisses. This frightens away birds and other animals.\n\nBlue-tongue lizards eat snails, insects, flowers and fruit. Gardeners like them because they eat the snails that damage plants."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'easy', 1, 'Gardeners like blue-tongue lizards because they eat the ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["snails","snail"]'::jsonb, 'The last sentence says gardeners like them because they eat the snails that damage plants.', NULL, '{"title":"Blue-tongue Lizards","text":"Blue-tongue lizards live in many backyards in Australia. They have a short, fat body and short legs. Their most famous feature is their bright blue tongue.\n\nWhen a blue-tongue lizard feels scared, it opens its mouth wide, sticks out its blue tongue and hisses. This frightens away birds and other animals.\n\nBlue-tongue lizards eat snails, insects, flowers and fruit. Gardeners like them because they eat the snails that damage plants."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'easy', 1, 'Which foods do blue-tongue lizards eat, according to the text? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"snails"},{"id":"b","text":"insects"},{"id":"c","text":"fruit"},{"id":"d","text":"fish"},{"id":"e","text":"seeds"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The text says they eat snails, insects, flowers and fruit. Fish and seeds are not mentioned.', NULL, '{"title":"Blue-tongue Lizards","text":"Blue-tongue lizards live in many backyards in Australia. They have a short, fat body and short legs. Their most famous feature is their bright blue tongue.\n\nWhen a blue-tongue lizard feels scared, it opens its mouth wide, sticks out its blue tongue and hisses. This frightens away birds and other animals.\n\nBlue-tongue lizards eat snails, insects, flowers and fruit. Gardeners like them because they eat the snails that damage plants."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'medium', 1, 'Put these events in the order they happened.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Noah and Mum set up a table."},{"id":"b","text":"Only a few people stopped."},{"id":"c","text":"It began to rain."},{"id":"d","text":"Shoppers queued under the roof."},{"id":"e","text":"They had sold 80 sausages."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Follow the story from start to end: setting up, a slow start, the rain, the queue, then the total by lunchtime.', NULL, '{"title":"The Sausage Sizzle","text":"On Saturday morning, Noah and his mum set up a table outside the hardware shop. They were running a sausage sizzle to raise money for the Rural Fire Service.\n\nMum cooked the sausages on the barbecue while Noah put them in bread with sauce. At first only a few people stopped. Then the sky turned dark and it began to rain. Noah thought nobody would come.\n\nBut wet shoppers hurried under the shop''s roof and smelled the sausages. Soon there was a long queue. By lunchtime, they had sold 80 sausages."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'medium', 1, 'Why did Noah think nobody would come?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It started to rain."},{"id":"b","text":"The sausages were burnt."},{"id":"c","text":"The shop was closed."},{"id":"d","text":"They had no bread."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The text says the sky turned dark and it began to rain. Then ''Noah thought nobody would come.''', NULL, '{"title":"The Sausage Sizzle","text":"On Saturday morning, Noah and his mum set up a table outside the hardware shop. They were running a sausage sizzle to raise money for the Rural Fire Service.\n\nMum cooked the sausages on the barbecue while Noah put them in bread with sauce. At first only a few people stopped. Then the sky turned dark and it began to rain. Noah thought nobody would come.\n\nBut wet shoppers hurried under the shop''s roof and smelled the sausages. Soon there was a long queue. By lunchtime, they had sold 80 sausages."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'medium', 1, 'How many sausages had Noah and Mum sold by lunchtime?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["80","eighty"]'::jsonb, 'The last sentence says, ''By lunchtime, they had sold 80 sausages.''', NULL, '{"title":"The Sausage Sizzle","text":"On Saturday morning, Noah and his mum set up a table outside the hardware shop. They were running a sausage sizzle to raise money for the Rural Fire Service.\n\nMum cooked the sausages on the barbecue while Noah put them in bread with sauce. At first only a few people stopped. Then the sky turned dark and it began to rain. Noah thought nobody would come.\n\nBut wet shoppers hurried under the shop''s roof and smelled the sausages. Soon there was a long queue. By lunchtime, they had sold 80 sausages."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'hard', 1, 'How did Zoe feel at the start of the story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"scared"},{"id":"b","text":"bored"},{"id":"c","text":"angry"},{"id":"d","text":"sleepy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Her toes were curled over the edge, the water looked deep and everyone else had jumped in first. These clues show she was scared.', NULL, '{"title":"The Big Jump","text":"Zoe stood at the edge of the pool with her toes curled over the side. The water looked very deep. All the other children had already jumped in.\n\n“I''ll be right here,” said her teacher, holding out her hands.\n\nZoe took a big breath, held her nose and jumped. When she came up, her teacher was holding her arms. Zoe spluttered and wiped her eyes. Then she grinned from ear to ear. “Can I do it again?”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'hard', 1, 'Why did Zoe ask, “Can I do it again?”', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She found out jumping in was fun after all."},{"id":"b","text":"She wanted to get out of the pool."},{"id":"c","text":"She did not jump the first time."},{"id":"d","text":"Her teacher told her to say it."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Zoe ''grinned from ear to ear'' after she jumped. She enjoyed it, so she wanted another turn.', NULL, '{"title":"The Big Jump","text":"Zoe stood at the edge of the pool with her toes curled over the side. The water looked very deep. All the other children had already jumped in.\n\n“I''ll be right here,” said her teacher, holding out her hands.\n\nZoe took a big breath, held her nose and jumped. When she came up, her teacher was holding her arms. Zoe spluttered and wiped her eyes. Then she grinned from ear to ear. “Can I do it again?”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'hard', 1, 'Zoe was the first child to jump into the pool.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The text says, ''All the other children had already jumped in.'' Zoe was the last.', NULL, '{"title":"The Big Jump","text":"Zoe stood at the edge of the pool with her toes curled over the side. The water looked very deep. All the other children had already jumped in.\n\n“I''ll be right here,” said her teacher, holding out her hands.\n\nZoe took a big breath, held her nose and jumped. When she came up, her teacher was holding her arms. Zoe spluttered and wiped her eyes. Then she grinned from ear to ear. “Can I do it again?”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the main purpose of this text?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to tell families about the Book Week parade"},{"id":"b","text":"to tell a story about a book character"},{"id":"c","text":"to teach students how to read"},{"id":"d","text":"to sell costumes"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The notice says when and where the parade is, what to wear and bring, and who can come. It gives information.', NULL, '{"title":"Book Week Parade","text":"Wattle Grove Public School Book Week Parade\n\nWhen: Thursday 21 August, 9:30 am\nWhere: School oval (school hall if wet)\n\nStudents are invited to dress up as their favourite book character. Please bring the book your costume comes from so you can show it to your class.\n\nParents are welcome to watch. Seats will be set out under the trees.\n\nThe library will be closed all day so the teachers can set up the book fair. It will open again on Friday."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_hard', 1, 'It is raining on Thursday morning. Where will the parade be held?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["the school hall","school hall","the hall","hall","in the school hall","in the hall"]'::jsonb, 'The notice says the parade is on the school oval, but in the school hall if it is wet.', NULL, '{"title":"Book Week Parade","text":"Wattle Grove Public School Book Week Parade\n\nWhen: Thursday 21 August, 9:30 am\nWhere: School oval (school hall if wet)\n\nStudents are invited to dress up as their favourite book character. Please bring the book your costume comes from so you can show it to your class.\n\nParents are welcome to watch. Seats will be set out under the trees.\n\nThe library will be closed all day so the teachers can set up the book fair. It will open again on Friday."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are true, according to the notice? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Students should bring the book their costume comes from."},{"id":"b","text":"Parents can come and watch."},{"id":"c","text":"Students can borrow library books on Thursday."},{"id":"d","text":"The parade starts at 9:30 am."},{"id":"e","text":"The parade is on a Friday."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'The notice asks students to bring their book, welcomes parents and gives the time as 9:30 am. The library is closed on Thursday, and the parade is on Thursday.', NULL, '{"title":"Book Week Parade","text":"Wattle Grove Public School Book Week Parade\n\nWhen: Thursday 21 August, 9:30 am\nWhere: School oval (school hall if wet)\n\nStudents are invited to dress up as their favourite book character. Please bring the book your costume comes from so you can show it to your class.\n\nParents are welcome to watch. Seats will be set out under the trees.\n\nThe library will be closed all day so the teachers can set up the book fair. It will open again on Friday."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word rhymes with ''street'' in the poem?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"feet"},{"id":"b","text":"roof"},{"id":"c","text":"rain"},{"id":"d","text":"play"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Street'' and ''feet'' both end with the same sound: -eet.', NULL, '{"title":"Rainy Day","text":"Pitter, patter, on the roof,\nRain is falling, here''s the proof:\nPuddles growing on the street,\nSplashing under muddy feet.\n\nDucks go waddling, quack, quack, quack,\nWater dripping down their back.\nGrey clouds rumble, thunder rolls,\nFrogs are singing in their holes.\n\nWhen the sun comes out to play,\nRainbows chase the clouds away."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_easy', 1, 'This poem is about a hot, sunny day at the beach.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The poem talks about rain, puddles, clouds and thunder, so it is about a rainy day.', NULL, '{"title":"Rainy Day","text":"Pitter, patter, on the roof,\nRain is falling, here''s the proof:\nPuddles growing on the street,\nSplashing under muddy feet.\n\nDucks go waddling, quack, quack, quack,\nWater dripping down their back.\nGrey clouds rumble, thunder rolls,\nFrogs are singing in their holes.\n\nWhen the sun comes out to play,\nRainbows chase the clouds away."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word in the last two lines rhymes with ''play''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"away"'::jsonb, '''Play'' and ''away'' both end with the sound -ay.', NULL, '{"title":"Rainy Day","text":"Pitter, patter, on the roof,\nRain is falling, here''s the proof:\nPuddles growing on the street,\nSplashing under muddy feet.\n\nDucks go waddling, quack, quack, quack,\nWater dripping down their back.\nGrey clouds rumble, thunder rolls,\nFrogs are singing in their holes.\n\nWhen the sun comes out to play,\nRainbows chase the clouds away."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'easy', 1, 'How did Priya feel at the start of the story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"nervous"},{"id":"b","text":"angry"},{"id":"c","text":"excited"},{"id":"d","text":"sleepy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Priya held her bag tightly and stared at her shoes. These are clues that she felt nervous.', NULL, '{"title":"The New Girl","text":"On the first day of Term 2, a new girl stood by the classroom door. Her name was Priya. She held her bag tightly and stared at her shoes.\n\nAt lunch, Priya sat alone under the big fig tree. Mei walked over with her lunchbox. “Do you want to play handball with us?” she asked.\n\nPriya looked up and smiled for the first time all day. “Yes, please!” she said."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'easy', 1, 'Where does the story take place?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"at school"},{"id":"b","text":"at the beach"},{"id":"c","text":"at home"},{"id":"d","text":"at the shops"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The story happens at the classroom door and under a tree at lunch, so it is at school.', NULL, '{"title":"The New Girl","text":"On the first day of Term 2, a new girl stood by the classroom door. Her name was Priya. She held her bag tightly and stared at her shoes.\n\nAt lunch, Priya sat alone under the big fig tree. Mei walked over with her lunchbox. “Do you want to play handball with us?” she asked.\n\nPriya looked up and smiled for the first time all day. “Yes, please!” she said."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'easy', 1, 'Which words describe Mei? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"kind"},{"id":"b","text":"friendly"},{"id":"c","text":"mean"},{"id":"d","text":"lazy"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Mei walked over to someone sitting alone and asked her to play. That is kind and friendly.', NULL, '{"title":"The New Girl","text":"On the first day of Term 2, a new girl stood by the classroom door. Her name was Priya. She held her bag tightly and stared at her shoes.\n\nAt lunch, Priya sat alone under the big fig tree. Mei walked over with her lunchbox. “Do you want to play handball with us?” she asked.\n\nPriya looked up and smiled for the first time all day. “Yes, please!” she said."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'medium', 1, 'What kind of feeling (mood) does the writer create?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"spooky and mysterious"},{"id":"b","text":"happy and silly"},{"id":"c","text":"calm and sleepy"},{"id":"d","text":"angry"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Spider webs, shadows, creaking and something scurrying make the shed feel spooky and mysterious.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Shed","text":"The old shed at the bottom of the paddock leaned to one side like a tired horse. Its tin roof was rusty and full of holes. Spider webs hung in every corner, and the floor creaked with each step.\n\nInside, it smelled of dust and engine oil. A cracked window let in a thin stripe of sunlight. Somewhere in the shadows, something scratched and scurried."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'medium', 1, 'The shed ''leaned to one side like a tired horse''. What two things is the writer comparing?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the shed and a tired horse"},{"id":"b","text":"the paddock and a horse"},{"id":"c","text":"the roof and a spider"},{"id":"d","text":"the window and the sunlight"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The word ''like'' shows a comparison. The leaning shed is compared to a tired horse drooping to one side.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Shed","text":"The old shed at the bottom of the paddock leaned to one side like a tired horse. Its tin roof was rusty and full of holes. Spider webs hung in every corner, and the floor creaked with each step.\n\nInside, it smelled of dust and engine oil. A cracked window let in a thin stripe of sunlight. Somewhere in the shadows, something scratched and scurried."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'medium', 1, 'Which senses does the writer use to describe the shed? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"sight"},{"id":"b","text":"smell"},{"id":"c","text":"hearing"},{"id":"d","text":"taste"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Sight: rusty roof and sunlight. Smell: dust and engine oil. Hearing: creaking and scratching. Nothing is tasted.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Shed","text":"The old shed at the bottom of the paddock leaned to one side like a tired horse. Its tin roof was rusty and full of holes. Spider webs hung in every corner, and the floor creaked with each step.\n\nInside, it smelled of dust and engine oil. A cracked window let in a thin stripe of sunlight. Somewhere in the shadows, something scratched and scurried."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'hard', 1, 'Who is telling this story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a gum tree"},{"id":"b","text":"a child"},{"id":"c","text":"the man with the clipboard"},{"id":"d","text":"a bird"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The narrator says, ''I am the oldest gum tree in the park.''', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Gum Tree","text":"I am the oldest gum tree in the park. For a hundred years, I have watched children climb my branches and collect my fallen gumnuts.\n\nToday, a man came with a measuring tape and a clipboard. He tied a bright orange ribbon around my trunk. The children''s faces looked worried.\n\nI do not know what the ribbon means. But I hope I will still be here to give shade next summer."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'hard', 1, 'This story is told in the first person, using words like ''I'' and ''my''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The tree tells its own story with ''I'', ''my'' and ''me''. That is called first person.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Gum Tree","text":"I am the oldest gum tree in the park. For a hundred years, I have watched children climb my branches and collect my fallen gumnuts.\n\nToday, a man came with a measuring tape and a clipboard. He tied a bright orange ribbon around my trunk. The children''s faces looked worried.\n\nI do not know what the ribbon means. But I hope I will still be here to give shade next summer."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'hard', 1, 'Why might the children look worried?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"They think the tree might be cut down."},{"id":"b","text":"They lost their ball."},{"id":"c","text":"It was going to rain."},{"id":"d","text":"They were late for school."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A man measured the tree and tied a ribbon on it. The children may think this means the tree will be cut down.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Gum Tree","text":"I am the oldest gum tree in the park. For a hundred years, I have watched children climb my branches and collect my fallen gumnuts.\n\nToday, a man came with a measuring tape and a clipboard. He tied a bright orange ribbon around my trunk. The children''s faces looked worried.\n\nI do not know what the ribbon means. But I hope I will still be here to give shade next summer."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_hard', 1, 'What lesson does this poem teach?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Planning ahead pays off, and it is good to be kind."},{"id":"b","text":"Possums are faster than wombats."},{"id":"c","text":"Storms only happen in summer."},{"id":"d","text":"Never go outside at night."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Wombat''s hard work kept him safe in the storm, and he was kind to Possum even after Possum laughed at him.', NULL, '{"title":"Wombat and Possum","text":"Wombat dug his burrow deep,\nA cosy place to rest and sleep.\nPossum laughed, “Why work so hard?\nI''ll sleep up high in Gran''s backyard!”\n\nThen came a storm one winter night,\nThe wind blew hard with all its might.\nPossum''s branch began to sway,\nIt cracked and crashed and blew away!\n\nWet and cold, he knocked below.\nWombat smiled and said hello.\n“There''s room for two,” he said. “Come in!”\nAnd Possum crept in with a grin."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these events from the poem in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Wombat dug a deep burrow."},{"id":"b","text":"Possum laughed at Wombat."},{"id":"c","text":"A storm blew Possum''s branch away."},{"id":"d","text":"Wombat invited Possum in."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Each stanza tells one part: Wombat digs and Possum laughs, then the storm, then Wombat helps Possum.', NULL, '{"title":"Wombat and Possum","text":"Wombat dug his burrow deep,\nA cosy place to rest and sleep.\nPossum laughed, “Why work so hard?\nI''ll sleep up high in Gran''s backyard!”\n\nThen came a storm one winter night,\nThe wind blew hard with all its might.\nPossum''s branch began to sway,\nIt cracked and crashed and blew away!\n\nWet and cold, he knocked below.\nWombat smiled and said hello.\n“There''s room for two,” he said. “Come in!”\nAnd Possum crept in with a grin."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_hard', 1, 'How did Possum''s feelings about Wombat change?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"At first he laughed at Wombat, but by the end he was grateful."},{"id":"b","text":"At first he was grateful, but by the end he was angry."},{"id":"c","text":"He was scared of Wombat the whole time."},{"id":"d","text":"His feelings did not change at all."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'In stanza one Possum laughs at Wombat''s hard work. At the end he crept in ''with a grin'', glad of Wombat''s help.', NULL, '{"title":"Wombat and Possum","text":"Wombat dug his burrow deep,\nA cosy place to rest and sleep.\nPossum laughed, “Why work so hard?\nI''ll sleep up high in Gran''s backyard!”\n\nThen came a storm one winter night,\nThe wind blew hard with all its might.\nPossum''s branch began to sway,\nIt cracked and crashed and blew away!\n\nWet and cold, he knocked below.\nWombat smiled and said hello.\n“There''s room for two,” he said. “Come in!”\nAnd Possum crept in with a grin."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word rhymes with ''day'' in the poem?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"hay"},{"id":"b","text":"mud"},{"id":"c","text":"sheep"},{"id":"d","text":"west"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Day'' and ''hay'' both end with the same sound: -ay.', NULL, '{"title":"On the Farm","text":"The rooster crows at break of day,\nThe horses munch their sweet green hay,\nThe pigs roll round in sticky mud,\nThe cow lies down to chew her cud.\n\nThe ducks go paddling on the dam,\nThe farmer bottle-feeds a lamb,\nThe goats climb up the woodpile high,\nThe swallows swoop across the sky.\n\nThe sheepdog runs to round up sheep,\nThe kittens curl up fast asleep,\nAnd when the sun sinks in the west,\nThe farm and all its animals rest."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_easy', 1, 'This poem is set on a farm.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The poem talks about a rooster, horses, pigs, a cow and sheep, which all live on a farm.', NULL, '{"title":"On the Farm","text":"The rooster crows at break of day,\nThe horses munch their sweet green hay,\nThe pigs roll round in sticky mud,\nThe cow lies down to chew her cud.\n\nThe ducks go paddling on the dam,\nThe farmer bottle-feeds a lamb,\nThe goats climb up the woodpile high,\nThe swallows swoop across the sky.\n\nThe sheepdog runs to round up sheep,\nThe kittens curl up fast asleep,\nAnd when the sun sinks in the west,\nThe farm and all its animals rest."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word in the poem rhymes with ''sheep''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"asleep"'::jsonb, '''Sheep'' and ''asleep'' both end with the sound -eep.', NULL, '{"title":"On the Farm","text":"The rooster crows at break of day,\nThe horses munch their sweet green hay,\nThe pigs roll round in sticky mud,\nThe cow lies down to chew her cud.\n\nThe ducks go paddling on the dam,\nThe farmer bottle-feeds a lamb,\nThe goats climb up the woodpile high,\nThe swallows swoop across the sky.\n\nThe sheepdog runs to round up sheep,\nThe kittens curl up fast asleep,\nAnd when the sun sinks in the west,\nThe farm and all its animals rest."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'easy', 1, 'Which word best describes Tom?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"honest"},{"id":"b","text":"sneaky"},{"id":"c","text":"lazy"},{"id":"d","text":"rude"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Tom owned up to breaking the pot even though he wanted to hide. Mrs Lee even thanks him for being honest.', NULL, '{"title":"The Broken Pot","text":"Tom kicked his soccer ball too hard. It flew over the fence and smashed Mrs Lee''s flower pot.\n\nTom wanted to run and hide. Instead, he walked next door and knocked. “I''m sorry, Mrs Lee,” he said in a small voice. “I broke your pot. I''ll use my pocket money to buy a new one.”\n\nMrs Lee smiled. “Thank you for being honest, Tom. How about you help me plant the flowers again instead?”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'easy', 1, 'Where does this story take place?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"at Tom''s home and next door"},{"id":"b","text":"at school"},{"id":"c","text":"at the beach"},{"id":"d","text":"at a shop"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Tom kicks the ball over his fence into Mrs Lee''s yard, then walks next door, so the story happens at home.', NULL, '{"title":"The Broken Pot","text":"Tom kicked his soccer ball too hard. It flew over the fence and smashed Mrs Lee''s flower pot.\n\nTom wanted to run and hide. Instead, he walked next door and knocked. “I''m sorry, Mrs Lee,” he said in a small voice. “I broke your pot. I''ll use my pocket money to buy a new one.”\n\nMrs Lee smiled. “Thank you for being honest, Tom. How about you help me plant the flowers again instead?”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'easy', 1, 'Which clues show that Tom felt nervous about owning up? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"He wanted to run and hide."},{"id":"b","text":"He spoke in a small voice."},{"id":"c","text":"Mrs Lee smiled."},{"id":"d","text":"He kicked the ball."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Wanting to hide and speaking in a small voice show he was nervous. Mrs Lee smiling is about her, and kicking the ball happened before.', NULL, '{"title":"The Broken Pot","text":"Tom kicked his soccer ball too hard. It flew over the fence and smashed Mrs Lee''s flower pot.\n\nTom wanted to run and hide. Instead, he walked next door and knocked. “I''m sorry, Mrs Lee,” he said in a small voice. “I broke your pot. I''ll use my pocket money to buy a new one.”\n\nMrs Lee smiled. “Thank you for being honest, Tom. How about you help me plant the flowers again instead?”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'medium', 1, 'Which words best describe the feeling (mood) of this place?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"cool and peaceful"},{"id":"b","text":"hot and dry"},{"id":"c","text":"noisy and busy"},{"id":"d","text":"cold and icy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The rainforest is ''cool and green'' with soft dripping water and birdsong. It feels cool and peaceful.', NULL, '{"title":"In the Rainforest","text":"The rainforest was cool and green. Tall trees reached up like giant umbrellas, blocking out the sun. Vines twisted around their trunks like snakes.\n\nWater dripped from the leaves, plip, plop, onto the soft, damp ground. A whipbird cracked its call through the trees, and the air smelled of wet earth and moss. A bright blue butterfly drifted past, and far away a creek splashed over the rocks."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'medium', 1, '''Tall trees reached up like giant umbrellas.'' What are the trees being compared to?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"giant umbrellas"},{"id":"b","text":"snakes"},{"id":"c","text":"the sun"},{"id":"d","text":"moss"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The word ''like'' shows a comparison. The trees spread out over the forest and block the sun, just like umbrellas.', NULL, '{"title":"In the Rainforest","text":"The rainforest was cool and green. Tall trees reached up like giant umbrellas, blocking out the sun. Vines twisted around their trunks like snakes.\n\nWater dripped from the leaves, plip, plop, onto the soft, damp ground. A whipbird cracked its call through the trees, and the air smelled of wet earth and moss. A bright blue butterfly drifted past, and far away a creek splashed over the rocks."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'medium', 1, 'Which words from the text are sound words? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"plip"},{"id":"b","text":"plop"},{"id":"c","text":"cracked"},{"id":"d","text":"green"},{"id":"e","text":"tall"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Plip and plop are the sounds of dripping water, and the whipbird ''cracked'' its call. Green and tall describe how things look.', NULL, '{"title":"In the Rainforest","text":"The rainforest was cool and green. Tall trees reached up like giant umbrellas, blocking out the sun. Vines twisted around their trunks like snakes.\n\nWater dripped from the leaves, plip, plop, onto the soft, damp ground. A whipbird cracked its call through the trees, and the air smelled of wet earth and moss. A bright blue butterfly drifted past, and far away a creek splashed over the rocks."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'hard', 1, 'Who is telling this story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rusty the dog"},{"id":"b","text":"Ella"},{"id":"c","text":"the bus driver"},{"id":"d","text":"Ella''s mum"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The narrator says, ''I am Rusty, and I am a very good dog.'' The dog tells the story using ''I''.', NULL, '{"title":"Waiting at the Gate","text":"I am Rusty, and I am a very good dog. Every afternoon at three o''clock, I sit by the front gate and wait.\n\nToday, I waited and waited. The school bus came, but Ella did not get off. My tail stopped wagging. I lay down with my nose on my paws.\n\nThen I heard a car door. Ella ran up the path with a sticker on her jumper. “I went to the dentist, Rusty!” she said. I licked her face all over."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'hard', 1, 'Why did Rusty''s tail stop wagging?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Ella did not get off the bus, so he was sad and worried."},{"id":"b","text":"He was tired from running."},{"id":"c","text":"He did not like the bus."},{"id":"d","text":"Ella told him to stop."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Rusty waits for Ella every day. When she wasn''t on the bus, he stopped wagging and lay down, which shows he was sad and worried.', NULL, '{"title":"Waiting at the Gate","text":"I am Rusty, and I am a very good dog. Every afternoon at three o''clock, I sit by the front gate and wait.\n\nToday, I waited and waited. The school bus came, but Ella did not get off. My tail stopped wagging. I lay down with my nose on my paws.\n\nThen I heard a car door. Ella ran up the path with a sticker on her jumper. “I went to the dentist, Rusty!” she said. I licked her face all over."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'hard', 1, 'Ella was not on the bus because she went to the ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"dentist"'::jsonb, 'Ella tells Rusty, ''I went to the dentist, Rusty!'' Her sticker is another clue.', NULL, '{"title":"Waiting at the Gate","text":"I am Rusty, and I am a very good dog. Every afternoon at three o''clock, I sit by the front gate and wait.\n\nToday, I waited and waited. The school bus came, but Ella did not get off. My tail stopped wagging. I lay down with my nose on my paws.\n\nThen I heard a car door. Ella ran up the path with a sticker on her jumper. “I went to the dentist, Rusty!” she said. I licked her face all over."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_hard', 1, 'What lesson does this poem teach?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Friends are worth more than things."},{"id":"b","text":"Bottle caps are the best treasure."},{"id":"c","text":"Kookaburras do not like worms."},{"id":"d","text":"Always hide your things in a nest."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Crow kept his treasure but ended up alone. Kookaburra shared and had fun with friends. Crow learns that a friend is the best treasure.', NULL, '{"title":"Crow''s Treasure","text":"Crow had a shiny bottle cap,\nHe hid it in his nest.\n“I won''t share it with anyone,\nMy treasure is the best!”\n\nKookaburra found a juicy worm\nAnd called out to her friends.\nThey laughed and shared a feast together\nTill the day came to its end.\n\nCrow sat alone up in his tree,\nHis treasure cold and small.\n“A friend,” he sighed, “to laugh with me\nIs the best treasure of all.”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these events from the poem in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Crow hid his bottle cap in his nest."},{"id":"b","text":"Kookaburra called her friends to share a worm."},{"id":"c","text":"Crow sat alone in his tree."},{"id":"d","text":"Crow sighed that a friend is the best treasure."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Each stanza tells one part: Crow hides his treasure, Kookaburra shares, then Crow sits alone and learns his lesson.', NULL, '{"title":"Crow''s Treasure","text":"Crow had a shiny bottle cap,\nHe hid it in his nest.\n“I won''t share it with anyone,\nMy treasure is the best!”\n\nKookaburra found a juicy worm\nAnd called out to her friends.\nThey laughed and shared a feast together\nTill the day came to its end.\n\nCrow sat alone up in his tree,\nHis treasure cold and small.\n“A friend,” he sighed, “to laugh with me\nIs the best treasure of all.”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003130230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031302', 'very_hard', 1, 'How did Crow''s thinking change from the start to the end of the poem?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"At first he thought his bottle cap was the best treasure, but at the end he knew a friend was better."},{"id":"b","text":"At first he wanted friends, but at the end he only wanted the bottle cap."},{"id":"c","text":"He was happy and proud the whole time."},{"id":"d","text":"His thinking did not change at all."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'In stanza one Crow says ''My treasure is the best!'' In the last stanza he says a friend ''is the best treasure of all.''', NULL, '{"title":"Crow''s Treasure","text":"Crow had a shiny bottle cap,\nHe hid it in his nest.\n“I won''t share it with anyone,\nMy treasure is the best!”\n\nKookaburra found a juicy worm\nAnd called out to her friends.\nThey laughed and shared a feast together\nTill the day came to its end.\n\nCrow sat alone up in his tree,\nHis treasure cold and small.\n“A friend,” he sighed, “to laugh with me\nIs the best treasure of all.”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence is written correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"my dog is called rex."},{"id":"b","text":"My dog is called Rex."},{"id":"c","text":"My dog is called rex"},{"id":"d","text":"my Dog is called Rex."}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a full stop. Rex is a name, so it needs a capital letter too.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these words should always start with a capital letter? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"sydney"},{"id":"b","text":"monday"},{"id":"c","text":"banana"},{"id":"d","text":"emma"},{"id":"e","text":"school"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Names of places (Sydney), days (Monday) and people (Emma) always start with a capital letter.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'medium', 1, 'A sentence can end with a full stop, a question mark or an exclamation mark.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'All three can end a sentence. A question mark ends a question and an exclamation mark shows strong feeling.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'medium', 1, 'Type the punctuation mark that ends this question: Where are you going___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"?"'::jsonb, 'It''s a question, so it ends with a question mark: Where are you going?', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'medium', 1, 'Which word in this sentence should start with a capital letter? ''On saturday we went to the beach.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"we"},{"id":"b","text":"saturday"},{"id":"c","text":"went"},{"id":"d","text":"beach"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Days of the week always start with a capital letter: Saturday.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is written correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the cat sat on the mat."},{"id":"b","text":"The cat sat on the mat."},{"id":"c","text":"The cat sat on the mat"},{"id":"d","text":"the cat sat on the mat"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a full stop.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Every sentence should start with a capital letter.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Yes. The capital letter shows where a new sentence begins.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Type the mark that ends this sentence: We played cricket at lunch___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '[".","full stop"]'::jsonb, 'It''s a telling sentence, not a question, so it ends with a full stop.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'easy', 1, 'Which word in this sentence needs a capital letter? ''My friend lily has a pet rabbit.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"lily"'::jsonb, 'Lily is a person''s name, and names always start with a capital letter.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these words need a capital letter? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"april"},{"id":"b","text":"koala"},{"id":"c","text":"canberra"},{"id":"d","text":"pencil"},{"id":"e","text":"christmas"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Months (April), places (Canberra) and special days (Christmas) always start with a capital letter. Koala and pencil are ordinary words.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence should end with an exclamation mark?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Watch out for that snake"},{"id":"b","text":"I have a blue bag"},{"id":"c","text":"What is your name"},{"id":"d","text":"We eat lunch at one"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Watch out for that snake!'' is a warning that shows strong feeling, so it needs an exclamation mark.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence uses capital letters correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"On Friday, Ben went to Bondi Beach."},{"id":"b","text":"On friday, Ben went to Bondi beach."},{"id":"c","text":"on Friday, ben went to Bondi Beach."},{"id":"d","text":"On Friday, Ben Went to Bondi Beach."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first word, the day (Friday), the name (Ben) and the place (Bondi Beach) all need capitals. ''Went'' does not.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'medium', 1, 'Put the words in order to make a sentence.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Last"},{"id":"b","text":"week"},{"id":"c","text":"we"},{"id":"d","text":"went"},{"id":"e","text":"to"},{"id":"f","text":"Dubbo."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e","f"]'::jsonb, '''Last'' has the capital letter, so it comes first. ''Dubbo.'' has the full stop, so it comes last.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'medium', 1, 'How many capital letters should this sentence have? ''on monday, sam and i went to the museum in sydney.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"5"'::jsonb, 'On (first word), Monday (day), Sam (name), I (always a capital) and Sydney (place): 5 capitals.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the best way to write these two sentences?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I like apples. They are crunchy."},{"id":"b","text":"I like apples they are crunchy."},{"id":"c","text":"I like apples. they are crunchy."},{"id":"d","text":"I like apples, They are crunchy."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each sentence needs its own capital letter and full stop: ''I like apples.'' and ''They are crunchy.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these sentences are punctuated correctly? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Can you help me?"},{"id":"b","text":"Where is my hat."},{"id":"c","text":"The bus is late."},{"id":"d","text":"Look out!"},{"id":"e","text":"what time is it?"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, '''Where is my hat'' is a question, so it needs a question mark. ''what time is it?'' needs a capital W.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'hard', 1, 'In the sentence ''We saw a Kangaroo at the park.'', the word ''Kangaroo'' should have a capital letter.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '''Kangaroo'' is not a name of a particular animal or place, so it doesn''t need a capital: ''We saw a kangaroo at the park.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_hard', 1, 'How many sentences are in this text? ''The storm came at night. Rain hit the roof. Did you hear the thunder?''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1"},{"id":"b","text":"2"},{"id":"c","text":"3"},{"id":"d","text":"4"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'Count the end marks: two full stops and one question mark. That makes 3 sentences.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_hard', 1, 'How many capital letters are missing? ''we went camping at jervis bay. it rained all weekend.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"4"'::jsonb, 'We (first word), Jervis Bay (a place, two capitals) and It (start of the second sentence): 4 capitals are missing.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which words in this sentence need a capital letter? ''last easter, my aunty took me to the royal easter show.'' Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"last"},{"id":"b","text":"aunty"},{"id":"c","text":"royal"},{"id":"d","text":"show"},{"id":"e","text":"me"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, '''Last'' starts the sentence, and ''Royal Easter Show'' is the name of an event. ''My aunty'' isn''t a name, so ''aunty'' stays small.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word should start with a capital letter?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"tom"},{"id":"b","text":"cup"},{"id":"c","text":"hot"},{"id":"d","text":"run"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Tom is a person''s name, so it needs a capital letter: Tom.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_easy', 1, 'When the word ''I'' means yourself, it is always a capital letter.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'We always write ''I'' as a capital, like ''Mum and I went shopping.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Type the mark that ends this question: Which bus goes to Bathurst___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["?","question mark"]'::jsonb, 'It''s asking something, so it ends with a question mark.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'easy', 1, 'Which word in this sentence needs a capital letter? ''Our school holidays start in july.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"july"'::jsonb, 'July is the name of a month, and months always start with a capital letter.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'easy', 1, 'Put the words in order to make a sentence.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Koalas"},{"id":"b","text":"sleep"},{"id":"c","text":"in"},{"id":"d","text":"the"},{"id":"e","text":"day."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, '''Koalas'' has the capital letter, so it goes first. ''day.'' has the full stop, so it goes last.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentences end with the right mark? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Is it raining?"},{"id":"b","text":"I am eight years old."},{"id":"c","text":"How old are you."},{"id":"d","text":"Stop that dog!"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '''How old are you'' is a question, so it needs a question mark, not a full stop.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence is written correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Dad and I went fishing at Lake Macquarie."},{"id":"b","text":"dad and i went fishing at lake Macquarie."},{"id":"c","text":"Dad and i went fishing at Lake Macquarie."},{"id":"d","text":"Dad and I went Fishing at Lake Macquarie."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Capitals go on the first word, on ''I'', and on the place name Lake Macquarie. ''Fishing'' is an ordinary word.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'medium', 1, 'How many full stops does this text need? ''Our class has a garden we grow carrots and peas''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"2"'::jsonb, 'There are two ideas: ''Our class has a garden.'' and ''We grow carrots and peas.'' Each needs a full stop.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'medium', 1, '''Uluru'' should start with a capital letter because it is the name of a place.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Names of special places, like Uluru, always start with a capital letter.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'hard', 1, 'Where should the full stop go? ''The bell rang we lined up for assembly''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"after ''rang''"},{"id":"b","text":"after ''we''"},{"id":"c","text":"after ''lined''"},{"id":"d","text":"after ''up''"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''The bell rang.'' is one sentence and ''We lined up for assembly.'' is another. The full stop goes after ''rang''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140131', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'hard', 1, 'Which words in this sentence need a capital letter? ''my teacher, mr lee, lives in newcastle.'' Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"my"},{"id":"b","text":"teacher"},{"id":"c","text":"mr"},{"id":"d","text":"lee"},{"id":"e","text":"newcastle"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, '''My'' starts the sentence, ''Mr Lee'' is a name, and ''Newcastle'' is a place. ''Teacher'' is an ordinary word.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140132', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'hard', 1, 'Which mark best ends this sentence? ''Hooray, we won the grand final''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"an exclamation mark (!)"},{"id":"b","text":"a question mark (?)"},{"id":"c","text":"a comma (,)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Hooray'' shows strong, excited feeling, so an exclamation mark fits best.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140133', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which text is written correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"On Saturday we went to the beach. We swam in the waves."},{"id":"b","text":"On saturday we went to the beach. we swam in the waves."},{"id":"c","text":"On Saturday we went to the beach we swam in the waves."},{"id":"d","text":"On Saturday. We went to the beach we swam in the waves."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are two sentences, each with a capital at the start and a full stop at the end, and ''Saturday'' needs a capital.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140134', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Find the mistake. Which word should NOT have a capital letter? ''Last Week, Grace and I went to the zoo in Dubbo.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"week"'::jsonb, '''Week'' is an ordinary word in the middle of the sentence, so it should be small: ''Last week''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003140135', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031401', 'very_hard', 1, 'This is punctuated correctly: ''When is your birthday. Mine is in May?''', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The marks are swapped. It should be ''When is your birthday? Mine is in May.'' The question gets the question mark.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is a verb (a doing word)? ''The dog ran across the park.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"dog"},{"id":"b","text":"ran"},{"id":"c","text":"park"},{"id":"d","text":"the"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A verb tells what someone or something does. The dog ''ran'', so ''ran'' is the verb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_easy', 1, 'A sentence should start with a capital letter.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Every sentence begins with a capital letter and ends with a full stop, question mark or exclamation mark.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is written correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"my cat is called Tiger."},{"id":"b","text":"My cat is called Tiger."},{"id":"c","text":"My cat is called tiger"},{"id":"d","text":"my Cat is called tiger."}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The sentence starts with a capital, the name ''Tiger'' has a capital, and it ends with a full stop.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'easy', 1, 'Choose ''huge'' or ''quietly'' to fill the gap: The ___ elephant stomped through the jungle.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"huge"'::jsonb, 'We need a describing word for the elephant. ''Huge'' describes the elephant. ''Quietly'' doesn''t fit before a noun.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'easy', 1, 'Put the parts of a story in the right order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"orientation"},{"id":"b","text":"complication"},{"id":"c","text":"resolution"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'First the orientation introduces who, where and when. Then the complication is the problem. The resolution solves it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'easy', 1, 'Which words always need a capital letter? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Sydney"},{"id":"b","text":"Monday"},{"id":"c","text":"dog"},{"id":"d","text":"Ellie"},{"id":"e","text":"table"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Names of places, days and people need capitals: Sydney, Monday, Ellie. ''Dog'' and ''table'' are common nouns.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence has the speech marks in the right place?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"“Let''s go to the beach,” said Tom."},{"id":"b","text":"“Let''s go to the beach, said Tom.”"},{"id":"c","text":"Let''s go to the beach, “said Tom.”"},{"id":"d","text":"“Let''s go” to the beach, said Tom."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Speech marks go around only the words that are spoken: “Let''s go to the beach,”. ''said Tom'' is outside them.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'medium', 1, 'What usually happens in the orientation of a story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The characters and setting are introduced."},{"id":"b","text":"The problem is solved."},{"id":"c","text":"The problem happens."},{"id":"d","text":"The story ends."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The orientation is the beginning. It tells us who is in the story, where it happens and when.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'medium', 1, 'Which verb better shows that someone was very tired: ''trudged'' or ''skipped''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"trudged"'::jsonb, '''Trudged'' means walked slowly and heavily, like a tired person. ''Skipped'' sounds happy and full of energy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'hard', 1, 'Which noun group best helps the reader picture a scary forest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a dark, tangled forest"},{"id":"b","text":"a forest"},{"id":"c","text":"a nice forest"},{"id":"d","text":"forest dark"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Dark'' and ''tangled'' are describing words that make the forest sound scary. ''Nice'' doesn''t fit a scary story.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentences are punctuated correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"“Watch out!” yelled Jess."},{"id":"b","text":"“Can I come too?” asked Ben."},{"id":"c","text":"“I''m hungry” said Ali."},{"id":"d","text":"“it''s raining,” said Mum."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Spoken words start with a capital and end with a punctuation mark inside the speech marks. Ali''s needs a comma, and Mum''s needs a capital I.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'hard', 1, 'The complication is the part of the story where the problem is solved.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The complication is where the problem happens. The problem is solved in the resolution.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Orientation: Kai and his sister are camping in the bush. Complication: In the night they hear a growl, and their torch goes out. Which is the best resolution?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"They find a spare battery and see it was only a possum, so they laugh and go back to sleep."},{"id":"b","text":"Kai likes camping."},{"id":"c","text":"The next day was Tuesday."},{"id":"d","text":"They hear a growl."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A resolution solves the problem from the complication. Finding light and discovering the noise was a possum fixes both problems.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to make a short story.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Lily planted a tiny seed in a pot."},{"id":"b","text":"Every day she watered it and put it in the sun."},{"id":"c","text":"One morning, a green shoot poked out of the soil."},{"id":"d","text":"By summer, a bright sunflower stood taller than Lily."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Look for time clues: planting comes first, then ''Every day'', then ''One morning'', and ''By summer'' is last.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Write the past tense of ''find'': Yesterday, Zara ___ a strange note under her door.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"found"'::jsonb, '''Yesterday'' tells us it happened in the past. ''Find'' is a tricky verb: its past tense is ''found'', not ''finded''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is a describing word (an adjective)? ''Ben has a red bike.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Ben"},{"id":"b","text":"has"},{"id":"c","text":"red"},{"id":"d","text":"bike"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'A describing word tells us more about a noun. ''Red'' tells us what the bike looks like.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_easy', 1, 'A sentence that asks a question ends with a question mark.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Questions end with a question mark, like ''Where is my hat?''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is written correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"we went to bondi beach."},{"id":"b","text":"We went to Bondi Beach."},{"id":"c","text":"We went to bondi Beach"},{"id":"d","text":"we Went to Bondi beach."}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The sentence starts with a capital, the place name Bondi Beach has capitals, and it ends with a full stop.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'easy', 1, 'Choose ''loudly'' or ''green'' to fill the gap: The baby cried ___ all night.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"loudly"'::jsonb, 'We need a word that tells HOW the baby cried. ''Loudly'' tells how. ''Green'' is a colour and doesn''t fit.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'easy', 1, 'Put these words in order to make a sentence.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"The"},{"id":"b","text":"cat"},{"id":"c","text":"chased"},{"id":"d","text":"the"},{"id":"e","text":"mouse."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'A sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a full stop: The cat chased the mouse.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'easy', 1, 'Which words are verbs (doing words)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"swim"},{"id":"b","text":"jump"},{"id":"c","text":"chair"},{"id":"d","text":"sing"},{"id":"e","text":"blue"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Swim, jump and sing are things you can do. ''Chair'' is a thing (a noun) and ''blue'' is a colour.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence has the speech marks in the right place?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"“Where is my hat?” asked Lucy."},{"id":"b","text":"“Where is my hat? asked Lucy.”"},{"id":"c","text":"Where is my hat? “asked Lucy.”"},{"id":"d","text":"“Where is” my hat? asked Lucy."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Speech marks go around only the words Lucy says, and the question mark belongs inside them. ''asked Lucy'' is outside.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'medium', 1, 'What usually happens in the resolution of a story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The problem is solved."},{"id":"b","text":"The characters are introduced."},{"id":"c","text":"The problem first happens."},{"id":"d","text":"The title is written."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The resolution comes at the end. It is where the problem from the complication gets fixed.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'medium', 1, 'Which verb better shows someone moving very quietly: ''tiptoed'' or ''stomped''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"tiptoed"'::jsonb, 'To tiptoe is to walk softly on your toes so no one hears you. ''Stomped'' is loud and heavy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'hard', 1, 'Which noun group best helps the reader picture a happy birthday party?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a bright, noisy party with balloons"},{"id":"b","text":"a party"},{"id":"c","text":"a sad, quiet party"},{"id":"d","text":"party big"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Bright'', ''noisy'' and ''with balloons'' help the reader see and hear a fun party. ''Sad'' and ''quiet'' don''t fit.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'hard', 1, 'Which words need a capital letter? ''on friday, mia and i went to dubbo.'' Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"on"},{"id":"b","text":"friday"},{"id":"c","text":"mia"},{"id":"d","text":"i"},{"id":"e","text":"went"},{"id":"f","text":"dubbo"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","f"]'::jsonb, '''On'' starts the sentence, Friday is a day, Mia is a name, ''I'' is always a capital, and Dubbo is a place. ''Went'' does not.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'hard', 1, 'The orientation of a story tells the reader who is in it, where it happens and when.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The orientation is the beginning. It sets up the characters, the setting and the time.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Orientation: Ruby and Sam are at the beach. Complication: A big wave washes away their sandcastle just before the competition ends. Which is the best resolution?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"They quickly build a new castle higher up the sand and win a prize for teamwork."},{"id":"b","text":"Ruby likes the beach."},{"id":"c","text":"A big wave washes their sandcastle away."},{"id":"d","text":"It was a sunny day."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A resolution fixes the problem. Building a new castle in a safer spot solves the problem the wave caused.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to make a short story.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"On Monday, Finn found a puppy shivering in the rain."},{"id":"b","text":"He took it home and dried it with a towel."},{"id":"c","text":"The next day, he put up ''Found'' posters in his street."},{"id":"d","text":"On Friday, the puppy''s owner knocked on the door."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Look for time clues: ''On Monday'' starts it, then he takes the puppy home, ''The next day'' comes after, and ''On Friday'' is last.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Write the past tense of ''catch'': Last week, Jed ___ a huge fish at the lake.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"caught"'::jsonb, '''Last week'' tells us it is the past. ''Catch'' is a tricky verb: its past tense is ''caught'', not ''catched''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which of these is a fact?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Koalas live in Australia."},{"id":"b","text":"Koalas are the cutest animals."},{"id":"c","text":"Everyone loves koalas."},{"id":"d","text":"Koalas are boring."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A fact can be checked and proven true. The others are opinions: what someone thinks or feels.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_easy', 1, 'A heading tells the reader what a part of the text is about.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Headings help readers find information quickly by telling them the topic of each part.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which kind of text tells you how to make something?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a procedure, like a recipe"},{"id":"b","text":"a poem"},{"id":"c","text":"a fairy tale"},{"id":"d","text":"a joke"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A procedure gives steps in order so you can make or do something, like a recipe for pancakes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'easy', 1, 'Put the steps for brushing your teeth in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Wet your toothbrush."},{"id":"b","text":"Put toothpaste on the brush."},{"id":"c","text":"Brush all your teeth for two minutes."},{"id":"d","text":"Rinse your mouth."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'In a procedure the steps must be in order. You need toothpaste on the brush before you can brush, and you rinse at the end.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence uses commas in a list correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I packed a hat, a towel, sunscreen and a drink."},{"id":"b","text":"I packed, a hat a towel sunscreen, and a drink."},{"id":"c","text":"I packed a hat a towel sunscreen and a drink."},{"id":"d","text":"I, packed a hat, a towel sunscreen and a drink."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Put a comma between each thing in a list, and use ''and'' before the last thing (no comma needed before ''and'').', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'easy', 1, 'Choose ''because'' or ''but'': Frogs need water ___ their skin must stay wet.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"because"'::jsonb, 'The second part gives the reason frogs need water, so ''because'' is the right joining word.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'medium', 1, 'Which is the best heading for this paragraph? ''Platypuses eat worms, shrimp and insect larvae. They find their food at the bottom of rivers using their bill.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"What Platypuses Eat"},{"id":"b","text":"Platypus Babies"},{"id":"c","text":"Where Platypuses Sleep"},{"id":"d","text":"My Holiday"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Every sentence in the paragraph is about platypus food, so ''What Platypuses Eat'' fits best.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'medium', 1, 'Steps in a procedure often start with a command verb. Which steps start with a command verb? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mix the flour and milk."},{"id":"b","text":"The bowl is big."},{"id":"c","text":"Pour the batter into the pan."},{"id":"d","text":"Stir for one minute."},{"id":"e","text":"Pancakes are yummy."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Mix, pour and stir tell you what to do. They are command verbs. The other sentences don''t tell you to do anything.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'medium', 1, 'An information report uses facts, not made-up events.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'An information report teaches the reader about a real topic, so it uses true facts.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'hard', 1, 'Which technical word would you expect to find in a report about the water cycle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"evaporation"},{"id":"b","text":"yummy"},{"id":"c","text":"awesome"},{"id":"d","text":"suddenly"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Evaporation'' is a science word for water turning into vapour. Reports use exact, technical words like this.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'hard', 1, 'Join the two ideas with a conjunction: Wombats dig burrows ___ they have strong claws.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["because","as","since"]'::jsonb, 'The strong claws are the reason wombats can dig, so we use ''because'' to show the reason. ''Since'' and ''as'' also work.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence has the commas in the right places?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Australia has many deserts including the Simpson, the Tanami and the Gibson."},{"id":"b","text":"Australia has many deserts including, the Simpson the Tanami, and the Gibson."},{"id":"c","text":"Australia has many deserts including the Simpson the Tanami, and, the Gibson."},{"id":"d","text":"Australia, has many deserts, including the Simpson the Tanami and the Gibson."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The list is the three deserts. Put a comma after the first one, then ''and'' joins the last two.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence does NOT belong in an information report about kangaroos?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I think kangaroos are the best animal ever!"},{"id":"b","text":"Kangaroos are marsupials."},{"id":"c","text":"A baby kangaroo is called a joey."},{"id":"d","text":"Kangaroos use their strong tails for balance."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Reports give facts. ''I think kangaroos are the best animal ever!'' is the writer''s opinion, so it does not belong.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put the parts of a procedure in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Goal (what you will make)"},{"id":"b","text":"Materials (what you need)"},{"id":"c","text":"Method (the steps)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'A procedure first says what you will make, then lists what you need, then gives the steps to follow.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these would make a report about Uluru easier to read? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"headings"},{"id":"b","text":"a labelled photo or diagram"},{"id":"c","text":"facts sorted into paragraphs"},{"id":"d","text":"a ''Once upon a time'' opening"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Headings, labelled pictures and paragraphs help readers find facts. ''Once upon a time'' is for stories, not reports.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which of these is a fact?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The sun rises in the morning."},{"id":"b","text":"Mornings are the best time of day."},{"id":"c","text":"Everyone loves sunrise."},{"id":"d","text":"Sunsets are prettier than sunrises."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A fact can be checked. You can see the sun rise each morning. The others are what someone thinks or feels.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_easy', 1, 'A recipe is a kind of procedure.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A recipe tells you how to make food, step by step. That makes it a procedure.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which part of an information book lists the topics and the page numbers where they start?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the contents page"},{"id":"b","text":"the front cover"},{"id":"c","text":"the title"},{"id":"d","text":"a photo"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The contents page is near the front. It lists each topic and its page number so you can find it quickly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'easy', 1, 'Put the steps for making a cheese sandwich in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Get two slices of bread."},{"id":"b","text":"Spread butter on the bread."},{"id":"c","text":"Put cheese on one slice."},{"id":"d","text":"Put the other slice on top."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'You need bread before you can butter it, the cheese goes on next, and the top slice goes on last.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence uses commas in a list correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"At the zoo we saw lions, zebras, giraffes and seals."},{"id":"b","text":"At the zoo we saw, lions zebras giraffes, and seals."},{"id":"c","text":"At the zoo we saw lions zebras giraffes and seals."},{"id":"d","text":"At the zoo, we saw lions zebras, giraffes, and, seals."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Put a comma between the animals in the list, and use ''and'' before the last one.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'easy', 1, 'Choose ''and'' or ''but'': Penguins are birds, ___ they cannot fly.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"but"'::jsonb, 'Most birds can fly, so penguins not flying is a surprise. ''But'' joins two ideas that are different.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'medium', 1, 'Which is the best heading for this paragraph? ''Koalas live in eucalyptus forests in eastern Australia. They spend most of their time high up in the trees.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Where Koalas Live"},{"id":"b","text":"What Koalas Eat"},{"id":"c","text":"Baby Koalas"},{"id":"d","text":"A Day at the Zoo"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Both sentences tell us where koalas live, so ''Where Koalas Live'' fits best.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are command verbs you might see at the start of steps in a procedure? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"cut"},{"id":"b","text":"fold"},{"id":"c","text":"glue"},{"id":"d","text":"yesterday"},{"id":"e","text":"happy"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Cut, fold and glue tell you what to do. ''Yesterday'' is a time word and ''happy'' is a feeling.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'medium', 1, 'An information report should include made-up characters.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'An information report gives true facts about a real topic. Made-up characters belong in stories.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'hard', 1, 'Which technical word would you expect to find in a report about insects?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"antennae"},{"id":"b","text":"yummy"},{"id":"c","text":"scary"},{"id":"d","text":"wonderful"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Antennae'' is a science word for the feelers on an insect''s head. Reports use exact, technical words like this.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'hard', 1, 'Choose ''so'' or ''because'': It was very hot, ___ we stayed in the shade.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"so"'::jsonb, 'Staying in the shade is the result of the heat. ''So'' shows what happened because of the first part.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence has the commas in the right places?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The Aboriginal flag is black, red and yellow."},{"id":"b","text":"The Aboriginal flag is, black red, and yellow."},{"id":"c","text":"The Aboriginal flag, is black red and yellow."},{"id":"d","text":"The Aboriginal flag is black red, and, yellow."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The list is the three colours. Put a comma after the first colour, then ''and'' joins the last two.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence does NOT belong in an information report about the Sydney Harbour Bridge?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The bridge is the most beautiful thing I have ever seen!"},{"id":"b","text":"The bridge opened in 1932."},{"id":"c","text":"Cars, trains and people cross the bridge."},{"id":"d","text":"The bridge is made of steel."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Reports give facts. ''The most beautiful thing I have ever seen'' is the writer''s opinion, so it does not belong.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to explain how a frog grows.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"A female frog lays eggs in the water."},{"id":"b","text":"Tadpoles hatch from the eggs."},{"id":"c","text":"The tadpoles grow legs and lose their tails."},{"id":"d","text":"The young frogs climb out of the water."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A life cycle goes in order: eggs first, then tadpoles, then tadpoles change, and finally young frogs leave the water.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentences would fit in an information report about platypuses? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Platypuses live in rivers and creeks."},{"id":"b","text":"A platypus swims using its front feet."},{"id":"c","text":"Once upon a time, a platypus got lost."},{"id":"d","text":"A female platypus lays eggs."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Reports give true facts written in the present tense. ''Once upon a time'' is how a made-up story begins.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is an opinion?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Pizza is the best food in the world."},{"id":"b","text":"Pizza has cheese on it."},{"id":"c","text":"Pizza is cooked in an oven."},{"id":"d","text":"Pizza is often cut into slices."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''The best food in the world'' is what someone thinks. Other people might disagree, so it is an opinion.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_easy', 1, 'A persuasive text tries to make the reader agree with the writer.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'To persuade means to try to get someone to agree with you or do something.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word makes a writer sound sure?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"definitely"},{"id":"b","text":"maybe"},{"id":"c","text":"perhaps"},{"id":"d","text":"sometimes"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Definitely'' sounds certain. ''Maybe'' and ''perhaps'' sound unsure, which is not very persuasive.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'easy', 1, 'Choose ''because'' or ''but'': We should have a longer lunch ___ we need more time to play.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"because"'::jsonb, '''Because'' joins the opinion to a reason. The second part explains why lunch should be longer.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentences are facts? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Netball is played with a ball."},{"id":"b","text":"Netball is the most fun sport."},{"id":"c","text":"A netball team has seven players on the court."},{"id":"d","text":"Everyone should love netball."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, 'Facts can be checked: netball uses a ball and has seven players on court. ''Most fun'' and ''should love'' are opinions.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'easy', 1, '''Our school needs a vegetable garden. It would teach us about healthy food.'' What is the writer''s opinion?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The school needs a vegetable garden."},{"id":"b","text":"Vegetables are green."},{"id":"c","text":"Gardens need water."},{"id":"d","text":"Lunch is at one o''clock."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The writer believes the school needs a vegetable garden. Teaching about healthy food is the reason given.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'medium', 1, 'Put the parts of a persuasive text in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Introduction: state your opinion"},{"id":"b","text":"Reasons with examples"},{"id":"c","text":"Conclusion: repeat your opinion"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Start by saying what you think, then give your reasons, then finish by saying your opinion again strongly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'medium', 1, 'Which is the strongest reason to support ''Dogs make great pets''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Dogs keep you active because they need a walk every day."},{"id":"b","text":"Dogs are dogs."},{"id":"c","text":"My neighbour has a dog."},{"id":"d","text":"Dogs have four legs."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A strong reason explains why the opinion is true. Keeping you active is a real benefit of owning a dog.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'medium', 1, '''Firstly'' is a good word to start the first reason in a persuasive text.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''Firstly'' tells the reader that your first reason is coming. Then you can use ''Secondly'' and ''Finally''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'hard', 1, 'Which connective best fills the gap? ''Plastic bags can hurt sea turtles. ___, we should use reusable bags.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Therefore"},{"id":"b","text":"Once upon a time"},{"id":"c","text":"Meanwhile"},{"id":"d","text":"Suddenly"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Therefore'' means ''so'' or ''because of this''. It shows what we should do because of the first sentence.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'hard', 1, 'Which words or phrases are persuasive? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"must"},{"id":"b","text":"everyone knows"},{"id":"c","text":"it is essential"},{"id":"d","text":"a little bit"},{"id":"e","text":"I''m not sure"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '''Must'', ''everyone knows'' and ''it is essential'' sound strong and certain. ''A little bit'' and ''I''m not sure'' sound weak.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'hard', 1, 'Choose ''must'' or ''might'' to make this sentence more persuasive: Children ___ wear a hat in the sun.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"must"'::jsonb, '''Must'' is a strong word that tells the reader it is important. ''Might'' sounds unsure.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_hard', 1, '''Our school should have a pet chicken. Firstly, chickens lay eggs. Secondly, they eat our food scraps. Finally, ___'' Which is the best final reason?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"looking after a chicken would teach us to be responsible."},{"id":"b","text":"chickens are birds."},{"id":"c","text":"I had eggs for breakfast."},{"id":"d","text":"my favourite colour is red."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A good reason shows why the school should have a chicken. Learning to be responsible is a benefit; the others don''t support the opinion.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses a fact to support an opinion?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"We should save water because NSW often has droughts."},{"id":"b","text":"Water is the best drink ever."},{"id":"c","text":"Everyone hates wasting water."},{"id":"d","text":"I think water is nice."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''NSW often has droughts'' is a fact that can be checked, and it gives a reason for the opinion ''We should save water''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these words in the order a writer would use them to give three reasons and then finish.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Firstly"},{"id":"b","text":"Secondly"},{"id":"c","text":"Finally"},{"id":"d","text":"In conclusion"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, '''Firstly'', ''Secondly'' and ''Finally'' introduce the three reasons. ''In conclusion'' starts the ending.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is an opinion?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Winter is the worst season."},{"id":"b","text":"Winter comes after autumn."},{"id":"c","text":"Winter days are shorter than summer days."},{"id":"d","text":"In Australia, winter starts in June."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''The worst season'' is what someone thinks. Other people might love winter, so it is an opinion.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_easy', 1, 'An opinion is something that can be checked and proven true.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'A fact can be checked and proven. An opinion is what someone thinks or feels, and others might disagree.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word makes a writer sound unsure?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"perhaps"},{"id":"b","text":"certainly"},{"id":"c","text":"must"},{"id":"d","text":"always"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Perhaps'' means maybe, so it sounds unsure. ''Certainly'', ''must'' and ''always'' sound sure.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'easy', 1, 'Choose ''so'' or ''but'': Hats protect us from the sun, ___ we should wear them outside.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"so"'::jsonb, 'Wearing hats is the result of them protecting us. ''So'' joins a reason to what we should do.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentences are opinions? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Cats are better than dogs."},{"id":"b","text":"Cats have whiskers."},{"id":"c","text":"Cats are the cutest pets."},{"id":"d","text":"Cats can climb trees."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, '''Better than dogs'' and ''the cutest'' are what someone thinks. Whiskers and climbing can be checked, so they are facts.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'easy', 1, '''Every school should have a big library. Books help children learn new things.'' What reason does the writer give?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Books help children learn new things."},{"id":"b","text":"Every school should have a big library."},{"id":"c","text":"Libraries have shelves."},{"id":"d","text":"Children like lunch."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The opinion is that schools should have a big library. The reason is why: books help children learn new things.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'medium', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to make a short persuasive text.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"I believe our class should go on an excursion to the zoo."},{"id":"b","text":"Firstly, we would learn about animals."},{"id":"c","text":"Secondly, it would be a fun day together."},{"id":"d","text":"That is why we must go to the zoo."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Start with the opinion, then ''Firstly'' and ''Secondly'' give the reasons, and the last sentence says the opinion again.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'medium', 1, 'Which is the strongest reason to support ''We should plant more trees at school''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Trees give us shade on hot days and homes for birds."},{"id":"b","text":"Trees are trees."},{"id":"c","text":"I saw a tree yesterday."},{"id":"d","text":"Trees have leaves."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A strong reason shows why the opinion is a good idea. Shade and homes for birds are real benefits.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'medium', 1, '''In conclusion'' is a good way to start the last part of a persuasive text.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''In conclusion'' tells the reader you are finishing and are about to sum up your opinion.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'hard', 1, 'Which words best fill the gap? ''Lots of rubbish ends up in the ocean. ___ we must put our litter in the bin.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"That is why"},{"id":"b","text":"Once upon a time"},{"id":"c","text":"Last week"},{"id":"d","text":"Suddenly"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''That is why'' shows what we should do because of the first sentence. The others are for telling stories.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentences sound strong and certain? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"We must act now."},{"id":"b","text":"It is clear that this is important."},{"id":"c","text":"I guess maybe it is okay."},{"id":"d","text":"Everyone needs to help."},{"id":"e","text":"It might be a bit good."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '''Must'', ''it is clear'' and ''everyone needs to'' sound sure. ''I guess maybe'' and ''might be a bit'' sound unsure.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'hard', 1, 'Choose ''should'' or ''could'' to make this sentence more persuasive: Everyone ___ wear a helmet when riding a bike.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"should"'::jsonb, '''Should'' tells the reader what is the right thing to do. ''Could'' only says it is possible, so it is weaker.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which is the best conclusion for a persuasive text arguing that recess should be longer?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"In conclusion, a longer recess would help us play, rest and be ready to learn."},{"id":"b","text":"Recess is at eleven o''clock."},{"id":"c","text":"I like apples."},{"id":"d","text":"Firstly, recess is fun."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A conclusion starts with a word like ''In conclusion'' and sums up the opinion and reasons. ''Firstly'' belongs at the start of the reasons.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_hard', 1, 'A writer thinks ''Dogs should be allowed at the beach.'' Which sentences are reasons that support this opinion? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Dogs need exercise, and running on the sand is great for them."},{"id":"b","text":"Families could enjoy the beach with their pets."},{"id":"c","text":"Some dogs are brown."},{"id":"d","text":"The beach has sand."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise for dogs and family time are reasons why dogs should be allowed. Brown dogs and sand are facts that don''t support the opinion.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000003150330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000031503', 'very_hard', 1, '''Children should help with chores at home. Firstly, it teaches them to be responsible. Secondly, ___'' Which is the best second reason?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"it gives parents more time to spend with the family."},{"id":"b","text":"my favourite food is pasta."},{"id":"c","text":"chores are jobs."},{"id":"d","text":"I have a sister."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A good reason explains why children should help. Giving the family more time together is a benefit; the others don''t support the opinion.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is the verb in this sentence? ''The kookaburra laughed loudly.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"kookaburra"},{"id":"b","text":"laughed"},{"id":"c","text":"loudly"},{"id":"d","text":"the"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The verb is the action. The kookaburra ''laughed''. ''Loudly'' tells how it laughed.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_easy', 1, 'The names of people and places need capital letters.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Names like Ellie, Perth and Bondi Beach are proper nouns, so they start with capital letters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which part of a story introduces the characters and the setting?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"orientation"},{"id":"b","text":"complication"},{"id":"c","text":"resolution"},{"id":"d","text":"title"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The orientation comes first. It tells the reader who, where and when.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'easy', 1, 'Choose the stronger verb, ''stomped'' or ''went'': The angry giant ___ across the valley.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"stomped"'::jsonb, '''Stomped'' shows how the giant moved, heavy and angry. ''Went'' doesn''t help the reader picture it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"“I found a fossil!” shouted Amir."},{"id":"b","text":"“I found a fossil! shouted Amir.”"},{"id":"c","text":"I found a fossil! “shouted Amir”."},{"id":"d","text":"“I found a fossil” shouted Amir!"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Only the spoken words go inside the speech marks, and the exclamation mark belongs to them, so it goes inside too.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'easy', 1, 'Which words are adjectives in this sentence? ''The tiny, frightened possum hid in the hollow log.'' Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"tiny"},{"id":"b","text":"frightened"},{"id":"c","text":"hollow"},{"id":"d","text":"hid"},{"id":"e","text":"possum"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Adjectives describe nouns. Tiny and frightened describe the possum, and hollow describes the log. ''Hid'' is a verb and ''possum'' is a noun.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'medium', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to make a story.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Ned and Luca paddled their canoe out onto the lake."},{"id":"b","text":"Halfway across, dark clouds rolled in and the wind picked up."},{"id":"c","text":"They paddled hard and steered towards a small island."},{"id":"d","text":"Safe on the shore, they waited for the storm to pass."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The story goes from orientation (paddling out) to complication (the storm) to resolution (reaching safety). ''Halfway across'' and ''Safe on the shore'' are clues.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'medium', 1, 'Which noun group gives the reader the clearest picture?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a rusty old bike with a squeaky bell"},{"id":"b","text":"a bike"},{"id":"c","text":"the bike thing"},{"id":"d","text":"some bike"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Adding adjectives (rusty, old) and a phrase (with a squeaky bell) to the noun ''bike'' helps the reader see it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'medium', 1, 'Write the past tense of ''blow'': Last night, the wind ___ the gate open.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"blew"'::jsonb, '''Last night'' means the past. ''Blow'' is an irregular verb: its past tense is ''blew'', not ''blowed''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'hard', 1, 'When a different character starts speaking in a story, what should the writer do?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Start a new line"},{"id":"b","text":"Leave out the speech marks"},{"id":"c","text":"Write it in capital letters"},{"id":"d","text":"Use a full stop instead of speech marks"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each time a new person speaks, start a new line. This helps the reader keep track of who is talking.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentences are punctuated correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"“Where are you going?” asked Gran."},{"id":"b","text":"Dad said, “Dinner is ready.”"},{"id":"c","text":"“I can''t find my shoes”, said Leo."},{"id":"d","text":"“hurry up!” called Tess."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Gran''s and Dad''s are correct. Leo''s comma should go inside the speech marks, and Tess''s spoken words need a capital H.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'hard', 1, 'A good complication creates a problem that the characters must deal with.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The complication is the problem in the middle of the story. It gives the characters something to solve in the resolution.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence SHOWS that a character is scared, instead of just telling us?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mia''s hands trembled as she reached for the door handle."},{"id":"b","text":"Mia was scared."},{"id":"c","text":"Mia felt very, very scared."},{"id":"d","text":"Mia was a girl who was scared."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Trembling hands let the reader work out that Mia is scared. The other sentences just tell us.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which is the best way to improve this sentence? ''The dog went down the road.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The scruffy dog bolted down the dusty road."},{"id":"b","text":"The dog went down the road fast."},{"id":"c","text":"Dog went road."},{"id":"d","text":"The dog went down the road and went down the road."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A stronger verb (bolted) and adjectives (scruffy, dusty) make a much clearer picture for the reader.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which words need a capital letter? ''last easter, jack and i went to the show in sydney.'' Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"last"},{"id":"b","text":"jack"},{"id":"c","text":"i"},{"id":"d","text":"sydney"},{"id":"e","text":"went"},{"id":"f","text":"show"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, '''Last'' starts the sentence, Jack is a name, ''I'' is always a capital, and Sydney is a place. (Easter needs one too!) ''Went'' and ''show'' do not.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word tells HOW the turtle moved? ''The turtle crawled slowly up the beach.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"turtle"},{"id":"b","text":"crawled"},{"id":"c","text":"slowly"},{"id":"d","text":"beach"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, '''Slowly'' is an adverb. It tells how the turtle crawled.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_easy', 1, 'The word ''I'' is always written as a capital letter.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'When you write about yourself, ''I'' is always a capital letter, even in the middle of a sentence.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which part of a story is where the problem happens?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"complication"},{"id":"b","text":"orientation"},{"id":"c","text":"resolution"},{"id":"d","text":"title"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The complication comes after the orientation. It is where something goes wrong for the characters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'easy', 1, 'Choose the stronger verb, ''gobbled'' or ''ate'': The hungry dog ___ its dinner in seconds.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"gobbled"'::jsonb, '''Gobbled'' shows the dog ate fast and greedily. ''Ate'' doesn''t help the reader picture it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"“Can we go to the pool?” asked Hana."},{"id":"b","text":"“Can we go to the pool? asked Hana.”"},{"id":"c","text":"“Can we go to the pool”? asked Hana."},{"id":"d","text":"Can we go to the pool? “asked Hana.”"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Only Hana''s words go inside the speech marks, and her question mark belongs to them, so it goes inside too.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'easy', 1, 'Which words are nouns in this sentence? ''The farmer drove his tractor across the muddy paddock.'' Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"farmer"},{"id":"b","text":"tractor"},{"id":"c","text":"paddock"},{"id":"d","text":"drove"},{"id":"e","text":"muddy"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Nouns name people, places and things: farmer, tractor and paddock. ''Drove'' is a verb and ''muddy'' is an adjective.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'medium', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to make a story.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Early one morning, Asha set off on her bike to deliver newspapers."},{"id":"b","text":"Halfway down Bell Street, her chain snapped."},{"id":"c","text":"Luckily, Mr Ngata from the bike shop saw her and fixed it."},{"id":"d","text":"By eight o''clock, every paper was delivered."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The time clues help: ''Early one morning'' starts it, ''Halfway'' is the problem, ''Luckily'' brings help, and ''By eight o''clock'' ends it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'medium', 1, 'Which noun group gives the reader the clearest picture?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"an enormous green crocodile with yellow eyes"},{"id":"b","text":"a crocodile"},{"id":"c","text":"the croc thing"},{"id":"d","text":"some animal"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Adding adjectives (enormous, green) and a phrase (with yellow eyes) to ''crocodile'' helps the reader see it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'medium', 1, 'Write the past tense of ''swim'': Yesterday, the dolphins ___ beside our boat.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"swam"'::jsonb, '''Yesterday'' means the past. ''Swim'' is an irregular verb: its past tense is ''swam'', not ''swimmed''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence stays in the past tense the whole way through?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Kara opened the box and gasped."},{"id":"b","text":"Kara opens the box and gasped."},{"id":"c","text":"Kara opened the box and gasps."},{"id":"d","text":"Kara will open the box and gasped."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Opened'' and ''gasped'' are both past tense. The other sentences mix past with present or future.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentences are punctuated correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mum asked, “Have you fed the dog?”"},{"id":"b","text":"“I''m so tired,” yawned Pete."},{"id":"c","text":"“Look at that rainbow” said Kim."},{"id":"d","text":"“we''re late!” cried Sam."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Mum''s and Pete''s are correct. Kim''s needs a comma inside the speech marks, and Sam''s spoken words need a capital W.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'hard', 1, 'Every story must start with the words ''Once upon a time''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Stories can start in many ways, such as with dialogue, a sound or the setting. ''Once upon a time'' is mostly used for fairy tales.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence SHOWS that a character is excited, instead of just telling us?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Leo bounced on his toes and couldn''t stop grinning."},{"id":"b","text":"Leo was excited."},{"id":"c","text":"Leo felt really, really excited."},{"id":"d","text":"Leo is an excited boy."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Bouncing and grinning let the reader work out that Leo is excited. The other sentences just tell us.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses a simile (a comparison using ''like'' or ''as'')?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The waves were as tall as houses."},{"id":"b","text":"The waves were tall."},{"id":"c","text":"The waves crashed on the shore."},{"id":"d","text":"There were many waves."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''As tall as houses'' compares the waves to houses using ''as''. That makes it a simile and helps the reader picture them.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which words need a capital letter? ''last christmas, aunty meg took us to lake macquarie.'' Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"last"},{"id":"b","text":"christmas"},{"id":"c","text":"aunty"},{"id":"d","text":"meg"},{"id":"e","text":"took"},{"id":"f","text":"lake"},{"id":"g","text":"macquarie"},{"id":"h","text":"us"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","f","g"]'::jsonb, '''Last'' starts the sentence, Christmas is a special day, Aunty Meg is a name (with a title), and Lake Macquarie is a place. ''Took'' and ''us'' do not.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110401', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is a fact?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The Murray River is in Australia."},{"id":"b","text":"The Murray River is the prettiest river."},{"id":"c","text":"Rivers are boring."},{"id":"d","text":"Everyone should swim in rivers."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A fact can be checked. You can look at a map to see the Murray River is in Australia. The others are opinions.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110402', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_easy', 1, 'A procedure has steps written in order.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A procedure tells you how to do something, so the steps must be in the order you do them.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110403', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the job of a heading?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to tell the reader what a section is about"},{"id":"b","text":"to end the text"},{"id":"c","text":"to tell a joke"},{"id":"d","text":"to list the materials"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A heading sits above a section and tells you its topic, so you can find information quickly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110404', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence uses commas correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Wombats, koalas and kangaroos are marsupials."},{"id":"b","text":"Wombats koalas, and kangaroos are marsupials."},{"id":"c","text":"Wombats, koalas, and, kangaroos are marsupials."},{"id":"d","text":"Wombats koalas and kangaroos, are marsupials."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Put a comma between the items in the list. ''And'' joins the last two, so no comma is needed there.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110405', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'easy', 1, 'Put the steps for planting a seed in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Fill a pot with soil."},{"id":"b","text":"Make a small hole with your finger."},{"id":"c","text":"Drop the seed in and cover it."},{"id":"d","text":"Water the soil gently."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'You need soil before you can make a hole, the hole before the seed, and you water it at the end.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110406', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'easy', 1, 'Choose ''so'' or ''but'': Echidnas have no teeth, ___ they catch food with their sticky tongues.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"so"'::jsonb, 'Having no teeth is the reason they use their tongues. ''So'' shows a result.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110407', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'medium', 1, 'Which is the best subheading for this paragraph? ''Emus can run at about 50 kilometres per hour. Their long, strong legs help them travel long distances.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Speed and Movement"},{"id":"b","text":"What Emus Eat"},{"id":"c","text":"Emu Eggs"},{"id":"d","text":"My Favourite Bird"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The paragraph is all about how fast and far emus move, so ''Speed and Movement'' fits.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110408', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these belong in an information report about volcanoes? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"technical words like ''lava'' and ''magma''"},{"id":"b","text":"facts written in the present tense"},{"id":"c","text":"a diagram with labels"},{"id":"d","text":"a made-up hero who saves the day"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Reports use technical words, present-tense facts and labelled diagrams. Made-up characters belong in stories.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110409', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'medium', 1, 'In a procedure, steps usually begin with a command verb such as ''cut'', ''mix'' or ''pour''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Command verbs tell the reader exactly what to do, so procedures start their steps with them.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110410', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'hard', 1, 'Which conjunction best joins these ideas? ''Many frogs are in danger ___ their wetlands are being drained.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"because"},{"id":"b","text":"although"},{"id":"c","text":"until"},{"id":"d","text":"or"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The wetlands being drained is the reason frogs are in danger, so ''because'' shows cause and effect.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110411', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'hard', 1, 'Write the technical word: When water heats up and turns into vapour, it is called ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"evaporation"'::jsonb, 'Water turning into vapour (a gas) is called evaporation. It is an important step in the water cycle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110412', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the best topic sentence for a paragraph about what koalas eat?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Koalas eat mostly eucalyptus leaves."},{"id":"b","text":"Koalas are cute."},{"id":"c","text":"I saw a koala at the zoo."},{"id":"d","text":"Koalas sleep a lot."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A topic sentence tells what the paragraph is about. Only the first sentence is about koala food, and it is a fact.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110413', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses commas correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"To make a smoothie, you need a banana, milk, yoghurt and honey."},{"id":"b","text":"To make a smoothie you need, a banana milk, yoghurt, and honey."},{"id":"c","text":"To make, a smoothie you need a banana milk yoghurt, and honey."},{"id":"d","text":"To make a smoothie you need a banana milk yoghurt and, honey."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A comma goes after the opening phrase ''To make a smoothie'', then between each item in the list, with ''and'' before the last one.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110414', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put the parts of an information report in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Title"},{"id":"b","text":"General opening statement"},{"id":"c","text":"Paragraphs of facts with subheadings"},{"id":"d","text":"Concluding statement"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A report starts with a title and an opening that says what it is about, then gives facts in sections, and ends with a conclusion.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110415', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_hard', 1, 'A report on the Great Barrier Reef says: ''The reef is in Queensland. It is made of coral. I went there and it was awesome!'' Which changes would improve it? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Remove the personal opinion"},{"id":"b","text":"Add a subheading"},{"id":"c","text":"Add more facts, like how big it is"},{"id":"d","text":"Add a talking dragon"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Reports should be factual and organised. Remove ''it was awesome'', add subheadings and add more facts. A dragon belongs in a story.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110416', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is a fact?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Uluru is in the Northern Territory."},{"id":"b","text":"Uluru is the most beautiful place on Earth."},{"id":"c","text":"Everyone must visit Uluru."},{"id":"d","text":"Uluru is boring."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A fact can be checked on a map. The others are opinions: what someone thinks or feels.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110417', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_easy', 1, 'An information report is usually about made-up characters.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'An information report gives facts about a real topic, like an animal or a place. Made-up characters belong in stories.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110418', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which part of a non-fiction book explains the meaning of special words?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the glossary"},{"id":"b","text":"the contents page"},{"id":"c","text":"the front cover"},{"id":"d","text":"the title"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The glossary is a mini dictionary at the back of a book. It explains the technical words used in the text.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110419', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence uses commas correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"We packed tents, sleeping bags, torches and food."},{"id":"b","text":"We packed tents sleeping bags, torches, and, food."},{"id":"c","text":"We packed, tents sleeping bags torches and food."},{"id":"d","text":"We packed tents sleeping bags torches, and food."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Put a comma between the items in the list. ''And'' joins the last two, so no comma is needed there.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110420', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'easy', 1, 'Put the steps for making a paper plane in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Fold a sheet of paper in half the long way."},{"id":"b","text":"Fold the top corners down to the middle fold."},{"id":"c","text":"Fold each side down to make the wings."},{"id":"d","text":"Throw the plane gently forward."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'You fold the paper first, then shape the nose, then make the wings. You can only throw it once it is finished.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110421', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'easy', 1, 'Choose ''because'' or ''although'': Kangaroos can''t walk backwards easily ___ of their big tails and long feet.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"because"'::jsonb, 'Their big tails and feet are the reason, so ''because'' shows cause. ''Although'' would show a contrast.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110422', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'medium', 1, 'Which is the best subheading for this paragraph? ''Wombats dig long burrows with their strong claws. They sleep underground during the heat of the day.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Wombat Homes"},{"id":"b","text":"What Wombats Eat"},{"id":"c","text":"Wombat Babies"},{"id":"d","text":"My Pet"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The paragraph is about burrows and where wombats sleep, so ''Wombat Homes'' fits.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110423', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these belong in a procedure? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"a list of materials"},{"id":"b","text":"numbered steps"},{"id":"c","text":"command verbs like ''cut'' and ''stir''"},{"id":"d","text":"a villain who causes trouble"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Procedures list what you need, number the steps and start them with command verbs. Villains belong in stories.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110424', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'medium', 1, 'Information reports are usually written in the present tense.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Reports describe how things are now, for example ''Koalas eat eucalyptus leaves'', so they use the present tense.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110425', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'hard', 1, 'Which conjunction best fills the gap? ''___ the platypus lays eggs, it is a mammal.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Although"},{"id":"b","text":"Because"},{"id":"c","text":"So"},{"id":"d","text":"Until"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Most mammals don''t lay eggs, so this is a surprising contrast. ''Although'' joins two ideas that seem not to fit together.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110426', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'hard', 1, 'Write the technical word: When water vapour cools and turns back into tiny drops, forming clouds, it is called ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"condensation"'::jsonb, 'Water vapour cooling into drops is called condensation. You can see it on the outside of a cold glass.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110427', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the best topic sentence for a paragraph about how emu fathers care for their chicks?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Male emus sit on the eggs and look after the chicks."},{"id":"b","text":"Emus are funny birds."},{"id":"c","text":"I saw an emu once."},{"id":"d","text":"Emus can run fast."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A topic sentence tells what the paragraph is about. Only the first sentence is about emu fathers and chicks, and it is a fact.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110428', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses commas correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Before the storm arrived, we tied down the trampoline, the chairs and the bins."},{"id":"b","text":"Before the storm arrived we tied down, the trampoline the chairs, and the bins."},{"id":"c","text":"Before, the storm arrived we tied down the trampoline the chairs and, the bins."},{"id":"d","text":"Before the storm arrived we tied down the trampoline the chairs and the bins,"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A comma goes after the opening part ''Before the storm arrived'', then between the items in the list, with ''and'' before the last one.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110429', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to explain how a caterpillar becomes a butterfly.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"A butterfly lays eggs on a leaf."},{"id":"b","text":"A caterpillar hatches and eats lots of leaves."},{"id":"c","text":"The caterpillar makes a chrysalis around itself."},{"id":"d","text":"An adult butterfly comes out of the chrysalis."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A life cycle follows the order things happen: egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, then the adult butterfly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110430', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041104', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentences are written in a formal, factual way that suits an information report about bees? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Honey bees live in large groups called colonies."},{"id":"b","text":"The queen bee lays all the eggs in the hive."},{"id":"c","text":"Bees are super cool and I love them heaps!"},{"id":"d","text":"Worker bees collect nectar from flowers."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Reports use facts and formal language. ''Super cool'' and ''I love them heaps'' are chatty opinions, so that sentence doesn''t fit.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110501', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is an opinion?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Summer is the best season."},{"id":"b","text":"Summer comes after spring."},{"id":"c","text":"Summer in Australia starts in December."},{"id":"d","text":"Summer days are longer than winter days."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Best'' is what someone thinks, and others may disagree. The other sentences can be checked, so they are facts.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110502', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_easy', 1, 'In a persuasive text, the writer gives reasons to support an opinion.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Reasons explain why the writer''s opinion is right, which helps convince the reader.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110503', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word shows the writer feels most strongly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"must"},{"id":"b","text":"might"},{"id":"c","text":"could"},{"id":"d","text":"maybe"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Must'' is a strong, certain word. ''Might'', ''could'' and ''maybe'' sound unsure.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110504', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentences are facts? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Cricket is played with a bat and ball."},{"id":"b","text":"A cricket team has eleven players."},{"id":"c","text":"Cricket is boring to watch."},{"id":"d","text":"Cricket is the greatest sport ever."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Facts can be checked: cricket uses a bat and ball and has eleven players in a team. ''Boring'' and ''greatest'' are opinions.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110505', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'easy', 1, 'Choose ''Firstly'' or ''Finally'' to introduce the first reason: Students should walk to school. ___, walking keeps you fit and healthy.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"Firstly"'::jsonb, 'This is the first reason after the opinion, so ''Firstly'' is the right connective. ''Finally'' is for the last reason.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110506', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence would make the best opening for a persuasive text?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I strongly believe our school should have a class pet."},{"id":"b","text":"Rabbits are animals."},{"id":"c","text":"Some schools are big."},{"id":"d","text":"Pets eat food."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A persuasive text opens by clearly stating the writer''s opinion. ''I strongly believe…'' does this.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110507', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'medium', 1, 'Put the parts of a persuasive text in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Statement of opinion"},{"id":"b","text":"First reason with evidence"},{"id":"c","text":"Second reason with evidence"},{"id":"d","text":"Conclusion that restates the opinion"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'State your opinion, back it up with reasons and evidence, then sum up by saying your opinion again.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110508', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'medium', 1, 'Which reason best supports the opinion ''Recess should be longer''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Students need time to eat, play and rest, and 20 minutes is not enough for all three."},{"id":"b","text":"Recess should be longer because I like it."},{"id":"c","text":"Recess should be longer because it should."},{"id":"d","text":"Recess is recess."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A good reason explains why, with detail. The first option gives a clear reason; the others don''t explain anything.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110509', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'medium', 1, 'The connective ''therefore'' is used to show a result.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''Therefore'' means ''so'' or ''because of this''. It shows what follows from a reason.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110510', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the most persuasive sentence?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It is vital that we protect our koalas before it is too late."},{"id":"b","text":"Koalas are sort of important."},{"id":"c","text":"Maybe we could protect koalas."},{"id":"d","text":"Some people like koalas."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Vital'' and ''before it is too late'' are strong, urgent words that push the reader to act.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110511', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'hard', 1, 'Which are emotive words (words that stir up feelings) that a persuasive writer might use? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"heartbreaking"},{"id":"b","text":"cruel"},{"id":"c","text":"shocking"},{"id":"d","text":"rectangle"},{"id":"e","text":"Tuesday"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Heartbreaking, cruel and shocking make the reader feel strongly. ''Rectangle'' and ''Tuesday'' don''t create feelings.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110512', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'hard', 1, 'Choose ''Therefore'' or ''However'': Plastic straws harm sea life. ___, our canteen should stop selling them.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"Therefore"'::jsonb, 'Stopping straws is the result of the harm they cause, so ''Therefore'' fits. ''However'' would introduce an opposite idea.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110513', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence is a rhetorical question used to persuade?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Would you want to live in a world without trees?"},{"id":"b","text":"What time is it?"},{"id":"c","text":"Where is the library?"},{"id":"d","text":"How many legs does a spider have?"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A rhetorical question isn''t asked for an answer. It makes the reader think and agree with the writer.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110514', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_hard', 1, 'A writer argues that ''Our school should have a longer lunch break.'' Which sentence answers someone who disagrees?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Some people say we would lose learning time, but well-rested students learn faster."},{"id":"b","text":"Lunch is fun."},{"id":"c","text":"I like sandwiches."},{"id":"d","text":"Long lunches are long."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'This sentence names the other side''s argument (losing learning time) and then argues back. That makes the writer''s case stronger.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110515', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_hard', 1, '''Every student should learn to swim. Australia has thousands of beaches, rivers and pools. Swimming is the most fun thing ever. Learning to swim can save lives.'' Which sentences are strong reasons for the opinion? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Australia has thousands of beaches, rivers and pools."},{"id":"b","text":"Learning to swim can save lives."},{"id":"c","text":"Swimming is the most fun thing ever."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Being surrounded by water and saving lives are strong reasons. ''The most fun thing ever'' is just a personal feeling, so it is weak.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110516', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is an opinion?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Pancakes are the tastiest breakfast."},{"id":"b","text":"Pancakes are made with flour."},{"id":"c","text":"Pancakes are cooked in a pan."},{"id":"d","text":"Pancakes can be eaten with lemon and sugar."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''The tastiest'' is what someone thinks, and others may disagree. The other sentences can be checked, so they are facts.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110517', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_easy', 1, 'The conclusion of a persuasive text repeats the writer''s opinion.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The conclusion sums up the argument and states the opinion again so the reader remembers it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110518', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word sounds the most persuasive?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"essential"},{"id":"b","text":"okay"},{"id":"c","text":"fine"},{"id":"d","text":"alright"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Essential'' means absolutely needed, so it sounds strong. ''Okay'', ''fine'' and ''alright'' sound weak.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110519', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentences are opinions? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Netball is more exciting than soccer."},{"id":"b","text":"A netball team has seven players on the court."},{"id":"c","text":"Soccer is a waste of time."},{"id":"d","text":"A soccer team has eleven players on the field."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, '''More exciting'' and ''a waste of time'' are what someone thinks. The numbers of players can be checked, so they are facts.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110520', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'easy', 1, 'Choose ''Secondly'' or ''In conclusion'' to introduce the second reason: Our school should have a vegetable garden. Firstly, it teaches us science. ___, fresh vegetables are good for us.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"Secondly"'::jsonb, 'This is the second reason, straight after ''Firstly'', so ''Secondly'' is the right connective. ''In conclusion'' is for the ending.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110521', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence would make the best opening for a persuasive text about the school canteen?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I strongly believe our canteen should sell more healthy food."},{"id":"b","text":"The canteen is next to the hall."},{"id":"c","text":"Some people buy lunch."},{"id":"d","text":"Apples are fruit."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A persuasive text opens by clearly stating the writer''s opinion. ''I strongly believe…'' does this.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110522', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'medium', 1, 'Put these connectives in the order they would appear in a persuasive text.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Firstly"},{"id":"b","text":"In addition"},{"id":"c","text":"Finally"},{"id":"d","text":"In conclusion"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, '''Firstly'' starts the reasons, ''In addition'' adds another, ''Finally'' gives the last reason and ''In conclusion'' begins the ending.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110523', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence gives evidence to support the opinion ''Our town needs a skate park''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Last year, 200 local kids signed a petition asking for one."},{"id":"b","text":"Skate parks are cool."},{"id":"c","text":"I really want a skate park."},{"id":"d","text":"Skating is a sport."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Evidence is a fact that backs up the opinion. A petition signed by 200 kids shows real need for a skate park.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110524', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'medium', 1, 'Using facts and numbers can make a persuasive argument more convincing.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Facts and numbers show the reader the argument is based on real information, not just feelings.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110525', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the most persuasive sentence?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Every single day, rubbish washes onto our beautiful beaches, and we must stop it now."},{"id":"b","text":"There is some rubbish on beaches."},{"id":"c","text":"Beaches have sand."},{"id":"d","text":"Rubbish could maybe be a problem."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Every single day'', ''beautiful'' and ''we must stop it now'' are strong, urgent words that push the reader to act.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110526', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'hard', 1, 'Which connectives show a result (what happens because of something)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"therefore"},{"id":"b","text":"as a result"},{"id":"c","text":"so"},{"id":"d","text":"however"},{"id":"e","text":"meanwhile"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Therefore, as a result and so all show a result. ''However'' shows a contrast and ''meanwhile'' shows time.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110527', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'hard', 1, 'Choose ''However'' or ''Therefore'': Some people think homework is helpful. ___, too much homework leaves no time for sport and family.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"However"'::jsonb, 'The second sentence argues the opposite of the first, so ''However'' fits. ''Therefore'' would show a result.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110528', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which rhetorical question would best suit a persuasive text about saving koala habitat?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"How would you feel if your home was bulldozed?"},{"id":"b","text":"How many koalas live in zoos?"},{"id":"c","text":"When do koalas sleep?"},{"id":"d","text":"What colour is a koala?"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A rhetorical question is not asked for an answer. This one makes readers imagine losing their home, just like koalas do.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110529', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_hard', 1, 'A writer argues that ''Students should be allowed to wear sport uniform every day.'' Which sentence answers someone who disagrees?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Some say sport uniforms look less smart, but comfortable clothes help students move and play safely."},{"id":"b","text":"Sport uniforms are green."},{"id":"c","text":"I like sport."},{"id":"d","text":"Uniforms are clothes."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'This sentence names the other side''s argument (looking less smart) and then argues back, which makes the writer''s case stronger.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004110530', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041105', 'very_hard', 1, 'A writer believes ''Children should read every night.'' Which sentences would make the argument weaker? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"I suppose it could be okay, maybe."},{"id":"b","text":"I''m not really sure this matters."},{"id":"c","text":"Reading every night builds a bigger vocabulary."},{"id":"d","text":"It is essential that we start tonight."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Unsure words like ''I suppose'', ''maybe'' and ''not really sure'' weaken an argument. A clear reason and strong words make it stronger.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these words are nouns? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"table"},{"id":"b","text":"quickly"},{"id":"c","text":"dog"},{"id":"d","text":"jump"},{"id":"e","text":"city"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Nouns name people, places and things: table, dog and city. ''Quickly'' is an adverb and ''jump'' is a verb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'medium', 1, 'Which word is the verb in this sentence? ''The children laughed at the funny clown.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"children"},{"id":"b","text":"laughed"},{"id":"c","text":"funny"},{"id":"d","text":"clown"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A verb is a doing word. ''Laughed'' is what the children did.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'medium', 1, 'Which word is an adjective in this sentence? ''A tiny bird sang outside.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"tiny"},{"id":"b","text":"bird"},{"id":"c","text":"sang"},{"id":"d","text":"outside"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'An adjective describes a noun. ''Tiny'' describes the bird.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'medium', 1, 'In the sentence ''Mia runs fast'', the word ''runs'' is a verb.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''Runs'' is what Mia does, so it''s a verb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'medium', 1, 'Write the past tense of ''go'' to finish the sentence: Yesterday I ___ to the park.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"went"'::jsonb, 'The past tense of ''go'' is ''went'': Yesterday I went to the park.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is a noun?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"kangaroo"},{"id":"b","text":"quickly"},{"id":"c","text":"jumped"},{"id":"d","text":"soft"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A noun names a person, place or thing. A kangaroo is a thing (an animal).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_easy', 1, '''Happy'' is an adjective.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''Happy'' describes a noun, like ''a happy child'', so it is an adjective.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is a verb?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"swim"},{"id":"b","text":"blue"},{"id":"c","text":"chair"},{"id":"d","text":"slowly"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A verb is a doing word. You can swim, so ''swim'' is a verb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'easy', 1, 'What is the adjective in this sentence? ''The hungry koala ate the leaves.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"hungry"'::jsonb, 'An adjective describes a noun. ''Hungry'' tells us what the koala was like.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these words are verbs? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"kick"},{"id":"b","text":"shout"},{"id":"c","text":"green"},{"id":"d","text":"write"},{"id":"e","text":"garden"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Kick, shout and write are all things you can do. ''Green'' is an adjective and ''garden'' is a noun.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'easy', 1, 'Write the past tense of ''swim'' to finish the sentence: Yesterday we ___ at the beach.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"swam"'::jsonb, '''Swim'' doesn''t add -ed. Its past tense is ''swam'': Yesterday we swam at the beach.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'medium', 1, 'Which word is a noun in this sentence? ''The tall girl threw the ball.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"girl"},{"id":"b","text":"tall"},{"id":"c","text":"threw"},{"id":"d","text":"the"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Girl'' names a person, so it is a noun. ''Tall'' describes her and ''threw'' is what she did. (''Ball'' is a noun too.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'medium', 1, 'How many nouns are in this sentence? ''The farmer fed the cows and the sheep.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"3"'::jsonb, 'The nouns name a person and animals: farmer, cows and sheep. That is 3 nouns.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these words are adjectives? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"shiny"},{"id":"b","text":"bright"},{"id":"c","text":"river"},{"id":"d","text":"sang"},{"id":"e","text":"old"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, 'Shiny, bright and old describe nouns, like ''a shiny old bike''. ''River'' is a noun and ''sang'' is a verb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'hard', 1, 'What is the verb group in this sentence? ''Max will paint the fence tomorrow.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"will paint"},{"id":"b","text":"the fence"},{"id":"c","text":"tomorrow"},{"id":"d","text":"Max"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A verb group is the main verb and its helping verbs. ''Will paint'' tells us what Max is going to do.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'hard', 1, 'In the sentence ''Dad will fish at the lake.'', what kind of word is ''fish''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"verb"},{"id":"b","text":"noun"},{"id":"c","text":"adjective"},{"id":"d","text":"adverb"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Here ''fish'' is something Dad will do, so it is a verb. In ''I ate a fish'', it would be a noun.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'hard', 1, 'There are two adjectives in this sentence: ''The brave firefighter saved a frightened puppy.''', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''Brave'' describes the firefighter and ''frightened'' describes the puppy, so there are two adjectives.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Change the noun ''beauty'' into an adjective to finish the sentence: The garden looks ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"beautiful"'::jsonb, 'Adding -ful turns the noun ''beauty'' into the adjective ''beautiful'' (the y changes to i).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which words are nouns in this sentence? ''Three noisy cockatoos landed on the old fence.'' Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"cockatoos"},{"id":"b","text":"noisy"},{"id":"c","text":"fence"},{"id":"d","text":"landed"},{"id":"e","text":"old"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, 'Cockatoos and fence are things, so they are nouns. ''Noisy'' and ''old'' are adjectives and ''landed'' is a verb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these words in order to make a noun group.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"the"},{"id":"b","text":"three"},{"id":"c","text":"small"},{"id":"d","text":"green"},{"id":"e","text":"frogs"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'A noun group goes: little pointing word (the), number (three), size (small), colour (green), then the noun (frogs).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which of these words are nouns? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"teacher"},{"id":"b","text":"pencil"},{"id":"c","text":"sing"},{"id":"d","text":"Brisbane"},{"id":"e","text":"under"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Nouns name people, things and places: teacher, pencil and Brisbane. ''Sing'' is a verb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is an adjective?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"fluffy"},{"id":"b","text":"bark"},{"id":"c","text":"cat"},{"id":"d","text":"ran"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Fluffy'' describes a noun, like ''a fluffy cat'', so it is an adjective.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_easy', 1, 'In ''I jump high'', the word ''jump'' is a noun.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '''Jump'' is what I do, so here it is a verb, not a noun.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'easy', 1, 'What is the verb in this sentence? ''The wombat dug a deep hole.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"dug"'::jsonb, '''Dug'' is what the wombat did, so it is the verb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'easy', 1, 'Which adjective fits best? ''The ___ sun made us very hot.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"blazing"},{"id":"b","text":"freezing"},{"id":"c","text":"quietly"},{"id":"d","text":"jumped"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'We need a describing word that matches being hot. ''Blazing'' fits. ''Freezing'' is the opposite, and the others aren''t adjectives.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'easy', 1, 'Write the past tense of ''catch'' to finish the sentence: Ruby ___ the ball at netball.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"caught"'::jsonb, '''Catch'' doesn''t add -ed. Its past tense is ''caught''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'medium', 1, 'What kind of word is ''carefully'' in this sentence? ''She carried the eggs carefully.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"adverb"},{"id":"b","text":"adjective"},{"id":"c","text":"noun"},{"id":"d","text":"verb"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Carefully'' tells us how she carried the eggs. A word that tells how an action is done is an adverb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'medium', 1, 'How many verbs are in this sentence? ''Ali ran to the shop, bought milk and walked home.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"3"'::jsonb, 'Ali did three things: ran, bought and walked. That is 3 verbs.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are proper nouns (names that need a capital letter)? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mia"},{"id":"b","text":"river"},{"id":"c","text":"Tasmania"},{"id":"d","text":"Monday"},{"id":"e","text":"cake"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Proper nouns name a particular person, place or day: Mia, Tasmania and Monday. ''River'' and ''cake'' are common nouns.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence has both an adjective and an adverb?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The small dog barked loudly."},{"id":"b","text":"The dog barked."},{"id":"c","text":"Dogs bark at night."},{"id":"d","text":"The dog is in the yard."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Small'' describes the dog (adjective) and ''loudly'' tells how it barked (adverb).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120131', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'hard', 1, 'In ''The race was a win for our team.'', the word ''win'' is a verb.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Here ''a win'' is a thing the team got, so ''win'' is a noun. In ''We win'', it would be a verb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120132', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'hard', 1, 'Change the verb ''run'' into a noun for a person: The fastest ___ won a medal.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"runner"'::jsonb, 'Adding -er makes a noun for the person who does it. Double the n first: run → runner.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120133', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these words in order to make a noun group.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"two"},{"id":"b","text":"fluffy"},{"id":"c","text":"white"},{"id":"d","text":"lambs"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Number first (two), then what it is like (fluffy), then colour (white), and the noun (lambs) comes last.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120134', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_hard', 1, 'In ''The crowd cheered as the batter hit a six.'', which list has only nouns?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"crowd, batter, six"},{"id":"b","text":"cheered, hit"},{"id":"c","text":"crowd, cheered, six"},{"id":"d","text":"batter, hit, six"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Crowd, batter and six are things or people. ''Cheered'' and ''hit'' are verbs (things that were done).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120135', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which words are adjectives in this sentence? ''After the long game, the tired players drank cold water.'' Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"long"},{"id":"b","text":"game"},{"id":"c","text":"tired"},{"id":"d","text":"drank"},{"id":"e","text":"cold"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Long describes the game, tired describes the players and cold describes the water. ''Game'' is a noun and ''drank'' is a verb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'medium', 1, 'Put the words in order to make a sentence.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"The"},{"id":"b","text":"dog"},{"id":"c","text":"barked"},{"id":"d","text":"loudly."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The capital letter shows the first word and the full stop shows the last: The dog barked loudly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'medium', 1, 'Put these events in the order they happened.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Sam woke up and got dressed."},{"id":"b","text":"Sam ate breakfast."},{"id":"c","text":"Sam walked to school."},{"id":"d","text":"The school bell rang."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Sam got up, had breakfast, walked to school, and then the bell rang.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in alphabetical order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"emu"},{"id":"b","text":"kangaroo"},{"id":"c","text":"koala"},{"id":"d","text":"wombat"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Look at the first letters: e, k, k, w. ''Kangaroo'' and ''koala'' both start with k, so look at the second letters: a comes before o.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Put the words in order to make a sentence.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"My"},{"id":"b","text":"cat"},{"id":"c","text":"sleeps"},{"id":"d","text":"on"},{"id":"e","text":"the"},{"id":"f","text":"bed."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e","f"]'::jsonb, '''My'' has the capital letter so it starts, and ''bed.'' has the full stop so it ends: My cat sleeps on the bed.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which of these is a complete sentence?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The bird flew away."},{"id":"b","text":"The bird."},{"id":"c","text":"Flew away."},{"id":"d","text":"Away the."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A complete sentence says who or what (the bird) and what happened (flew away), and it makes sense on its own.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_easy', 1, '''Running down the road'' is a complete sentence.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'We don''t know who is running, so it doesn''t make sense on its own. ''Ella was running down the road.'' is complete.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'easy', 1, 'Which joining word fits best? ''I was hungry ___ I ate an apple.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"so"},{"id":"b","text":"but"},{"id":"c","text":"or"},{"id":"d","text":"because"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Being hungry led to eating an apple. ''So'' shows what happened as a result.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'easy', 1, 'Put these steps for making toast in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Put the bread in the toaster."},{"id":"b","text":"Wait for it to pop up."},{"id":"c","text":"Spread butter on the toast."},{"id":"d","text":"Eat the toast."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'You toast the bread first, wait for it, butter it, and eat it last.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'easy', 1, 'Type a joining word: I wanted to play outside ___ it was raining.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["but","yet"]'::jsonb, 'The two ideas don''t go together (wanting to play, but rain), so ''but'' joins them.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'medium', 1, 'Put the words in order to make a sentence.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"After"},{"id":"b","text":"school,"},{"id":"c","text":"Lily"},{"id":"d","text":"practised"},{"id":"e","text":"her"},{"id":"f","text":"netball"},{"id":"g","text":"skills."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e","f","g"]'::jsonb, '''After'' has the capital, so it starts. The comma after ''school'' shows where the time part ends: After school, Lily practised her netball skills.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are complete sentences? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The koala climbed the gum tree."},{"id":"b","text":"Under the bridge."},{"id":"c","text":"Sam laughed."},{"id":"d","text":"Because it was cold."},{"id":"e","text":"The waves crashed."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Each complete sentence tells who or what and what they did. ''Under the bridge.'' and ''Because it was cold.'' don''t make sense alone.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'medium', 1, 'Which word joins the two ideas in this sentence? ''We stayed inside because it was too hot.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"because"'::jsonb, '''Because'' joins ''We stayed inside'' to the reason, ''it was too hot''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'hard', 1, 'Put these sentences from a recount in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"First, we caught the bus to the museum."},{"id":"b","text":"Next, we looked at the dinosaur bones."},{"id":"c","text":"After lunch, we visited the gift shop."},{"id":"d","text":"Finally, we travelled back to school."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The time words show the order: First, Next, After lunch, then Finally.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence joins these two ideas best? ''The dog was muddy. Mum gave it a bath.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The dog was muddy, so Mum gave it a bath."},{"id":"b","text":"The dog was muddy, but Mum gave it a bath."},{"id":"c","text":"The dog was muddy or Mum gave it a bath."},{"id":"d","text":"Mum gave the dog a bath, so it was muddy."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The mud is the reason for the bath. ''So'' shows the bath happened because the dog was muddy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'hard', 1, '''When the bell rang.'' is a complete sentence.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'It leaves us asking ''What happened when the bell rang?'' It needs more, like ''When the bell rang, we went inside.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these parts in order to make a sentence.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Although it was raining,"},{"id":"b","text":"we"},{"id":"c","text":"still"},{"id":"d","text":"went"},{"id":"e","text":"swimming."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, '''Although'' has the capital and the comma ends that part, so it comes first: Although it was raining, we still went swimming.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these is a run-on sentence that needs fixing?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I finished my homework I went to bed."},{"id":"b","text":"I finished my homework, then I went to bed."},{"id":"c","text":"After I finished my homework, I went to bed."},{"id":"d","text":"I finished my homework and went to bed."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Two complete ideas are squashed together with nothing joining them. It needs a full stop or a joining word.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Type one joining word: You can have pasta ___ you can have pizza, but not both.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"or"'::jsonb, '''Or'' joins two choices when you can only pick one.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Put the words in order to make a sentence.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"The"},{"id":"b","text":"emu"},{"id":"c","text":"ran"},{"id":"d","text":"very"},{"id":"e","text":"fast."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, '''The'' has the capital letter and ''fast.'' has the full stop: The emu ran very fast.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word makes the sentence make sense? ''I brush my ___ every morning.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"teeth"},{"id":"b","text":"jump"},{"id":"c","text":"happy"},{"id":"d","text":"quickly"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'We need a thing you can brush. ''I brush my teeth every morning.'' makes sense.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_easy', 1, 'A sentence must make sense on its own.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Yes. A sentence is a complete idea that makes sense by itself.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'easy', 1, 'Put these steps for planting a seed in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Dig a small hole."},{"id":"b","text":"Drop the seed in the hole."},{"id":"c","text":"Cover it with soil."},{"id":"d","text":"Water it every day."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'You need a hole before the seed goes in, then you cover it, and then you water it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'easy', 1, 'Which word joins the two ideas? ''I like apples and I like pears.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"and"'::jsonb, '''And'' joins two ideas that go together.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these is NOT a complete sentence?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"In the morning before school."},{"id":"b","text":"We walked to the park."},{"id":"c","text":"Grandpa told a funny joke."},{"id":"d","text":"The sun is shining."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''In the morning before school.'' tells when, but not who did what. It doesn''t make sense alone.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'medium', 1, 'Type a joining word that gives a reason: I put on my raincoat ___ it was raining.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["because","as","since"]'::jsonb, 'The rain is the reason for the raincoat. ''Because'' joins the action to its reason.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'medium', 1, 'Put the words in order to make a sentence.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Every"},{"id":"b","text":"Saturday,"},{"id":"c","text":"Jake"},{"id":"d","text":"plays"},{"id":"e","text":"cricket"},{"id":"f","text":"with"},{"id":"g","text":"his"},{"id":"h","text":"dad."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e","f","g","h"]'::jsonb, '''Every'' has the capital, the comma ends the time part, and ''dad.'' has the full stop: Every Saturday, Jake plays cricket with his dad.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these joining words can give a reason? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"because"},{"id":"b","text":"since"},{"id":"c","text":"but"},{"id":"d","text":"or"},{"id":"e","text":"and"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '''Because'' and ''since'' give a reason, like ''since it was late''. ''But'' shows a difference, ''or'' a choice and ''and'' adds on.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the best way to join ''The film was long.'' and ''We enjoyed it.''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The film was long, but we enjoyed it."},{"id":"b","text":"The film was long, so we enjoyed it."},{"id":"c","text":"The film was long because we enjoyed it."},{"id":"d","text":"The film was long or we enjoyed it."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'You might expect a long film to be boring, but they enjoyed it. ''But'' joins two ideas that contrast.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'hard', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to tell the story.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"One sunny morning, Ari found a map in the attic."},{"id":"b","text":"He followed the map to the old fig tree."},{"id":"c","text":"He dug under the roots and found a tin box."},{"id":"d","text":"Inside the box was a note from his grandpa."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Ari must find the map before he can follow it, dig, and open the box.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'hard', 1, 'How many complete sentences are here? ''The storm hit the town. Trees fell over. While the power was out. People lit candles.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"3"'::jsonb, '''While the power was out.'' doesn''t make sense alone, so it isn''t a complete sentence. The other 3 are.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120231', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence has its words in a sensible order?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Before she went to bed, Zara read a book."},{"id":"b","text":"Zara read a book before bed she went to."},{"id":"c","text":"Before bed she, Zara read a book went."},{"id":"d","text":"Read a book Zara before she went to bed."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Before she went to bed'' tells when, and ''Zara read a book'' tells who did what. The others are jumbled.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120232', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentences join two ideas correctly? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"I was tired, so I went to bed early."},{"id":"b","text":"I was tired I went to bed early."},{"id":"c","text":"Because I was tired, I went to bed early."},{"id":"d","text":"I went to bed early because I was tired."},{"id":"e","text":"I was tired, I went to bed early."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Two complete ideas need a joining word like ''so'' or ''because''. A comma alone, or nothing at all, isn''t enough.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004120233', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041202', 'very_hard', 1, 'If you move ''when the siren sounds'' to the start of ''We leave the pool when the siren sounds.'', you need a comma after it.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'When the ''when'' part comes first, a comma marks where it ends: When the siren sounds, we leave the pool.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"beautiful"},{"id":"b","text":"beatiful"},{"id":"c","text":"beautifull"},{"id":"d","text":"butiful"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Beautiful'' has ''beau'' at the start (b-e-a-u) and the suffix -ful with one l at the end.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_easy', 1, 'The plural of ''bus'' is ''buses''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Words that end in s add -es to make the plural: one bus, two buses.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_easy', 1, 'help + ful = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"helpful"'::jsonb, 'Keep the base word ''help'' and add -ful with one l: helpful.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'easy', 1, 'Write the plural of ''party''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"parties"'::jsonb, '''Party'' ends in a consonant then y, so change the y to i and add -es: parties.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'easy', 1, 'Which word correctly finishes the sentence? ''Our team ___ the game on Saturday.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"one"},{"id":"b","text":"won"},{"id":"c","text":"wun"},{"id":"d","text":"wone"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Won'' is the past tense of ''win''. ''One'' sounds the same but is the number 1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'easy', 1, 'Put these word parts in order to make the word ''unhelpful''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"un"},{"id":"b","text":"help"},{"id":"c","text":"ful"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The prefix ''un'' goes at the front, then the base word ''help'', then the suffix ''ful'' at the end.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'medium', 1, 'Add ''ing'' to the word ''begin''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"beginning"'::jsonb, 'The last part of ''begin'' is stressed and has a short vowel before one consonant, so double the n: beginning.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'medium', 1, 'What is the plural of ''knife''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"knifes"},{"id":"b","text":"knives"},{"id":"c","text":"knifs"},{"id":"d","text":"knivs"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Many words ending in -fe change to -ves in the plural: one knife, two knives.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'medium', 1, 'Add a prefix to make a word that means ''go out of sight'': ___appear', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"dis"'::jsonb, 'The prefix ''dis'' means the opposite of. Disappear is the opposite of appear.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses the homophones correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The knight rode through the night."},{"id":"b","text":"The night rode through the knight."},{"id":"c","text":"The knight rode threw the night."},{"id":"d","text":"The nite rode through the knight."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A ''knight'' is a soldier on a horse and ''night'' is when it is dark. ''Through'' means in one side and out the other; ''threw'' is the past of ''throw''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'hard', 1, 'Add ''est'' to the word ''happy''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"happiest"'::jsonb, '''Happy'' ends in a consonant then y, so change the y to i before adding -est: happiest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'hard', 1, 'Which words are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"necessary"},{"id":"b","text":"neccessary"},{"id":"c","text":"separate"},{"id":"d","text":"seperate"},{"id":"e","text":"February"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Necessary has one c and two s''s. Separate has ''a rat'' in the middle. February has an r after the b.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which word correctly completes the sentence? ''The lifeguard''s ___ was clear: always swim between the flags.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"advice"},{"id":"b","text":"advise"},{"id":"c","text":"adviced"},{"id":"d","text":"advize"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Advice'' (with c) is a noun, a thing you give. ''Advise'' (with s) is the verb, the action of giving advice.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_hard', 1, 'gentle + ly = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"gently"'::jsonb, 'When a word ends in -le, drop the e and add -y: gentle becomes gently.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_hard', 1, 'The word ''disappointed'' is spelt with one s and two p''s.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'It is the prefix ''dis'' (one s) added to ''appoint'' (two p''s): dis + appointed = disappointed.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Wednesday"},{"id":"b","text":"Wensday"},{"id":"c","text":"Wednesdy"},{"id":"d","text":"Wendsday"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Wednesday'' has a silent d: W-e-d-n-e-s-d-a-y. Saying it slowly as ''Wed-nes-day'' helps you remember.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_easy', 1, 'The plural of ''church'' is ''churches''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Words that end in ch add -es to make the plural: one church, two churches.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_easy', 1, 'kind + ness = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"kindness"'::jsonb, 'Keep the base word ''kind'' and add the suffix -ness: kindness.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'easy', 1, 'Write the plural of ''city''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"cities"'::jsonb, '''City'' ends in a consonant then y, so change the y to i and add -es: cities.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'easy', 1, 'Which word correctly finishes the sentence? ''The wind ___ the leaves off the tree.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"blue"},{"id":"b","text":"blew"},{"id":"c","text":"bloo"},{"id":"d","text":"blewe"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Blew'' is the past tense of ''blow''. ''Blue'' sounds the same but is a colour.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'easy', 1, 'Put these word parts in order to make the word ''unkindly''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"un"},{"id":"b","text":"kind"},{"id":"c","text":"ly"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The prefix ''un'' goes at the front, then the base word ''kind'', then the suffix ''ly'' at the end.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'medium', 1, 'Add ''ing'' to the word ''forget''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"forgetting"'::jsonb, 'The last part of ''forget'' is stressed and has a short vowel before one consonant, so double the t: forgetting.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'medium', 1, 'What is the plural of ''half''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"halfs"},{"id":"b","text":"halves"},{"id":"c","text":"halfes"},{"id":"d","text":"halvs"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Many words ending in f change the f to v and add -es: one half, two halves.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'medium', 1, 'Add a prefix to make a word that means ''not possible'': ___possible', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"im"'::jsonb, 'The prefix ''im'' means ''not''. Impossible means not possible. We use ''im'' before many words starting with p.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses the homophones correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"We could see the sea from our hotel."},{"id":"b","text":"We could sea the see from our hotel."},{"id":"c","text":"We could see the see from our hotel."},{"id":"d","text":"We could sea the sea from our hotel."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''See'' is what you do with your eyes (it has two e''s, like two eyes). ''Sea'' is the ocean.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'hard', 1, 'Add ''ly'' to the word ''busy''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"busily"'::jsonb, '''Busy'' ends in a consonant then y, so change the y to i before adding -ly: busily.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'hard', 1, 'Which words are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"believe"},{"id":"b","text":"beleive"},{"id":"c","text":"library"},{"id":"d","text":"libary"},{"id":"e","text":"different"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Believe has ''lie'' in the middle. Library has an r after the b (li-brar-y). Different has two f''s and ''er'' in the middle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which word correctly completes the sentence? ''The library was so ___ that you could hear a pin drop.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"quiet"},{"id":"b","text":"quite"},{"id":"c","text":"quit"},{"id":"d","text":"quiete"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Quiet'' (i-e-t) means with little noise. ''Quite'' (i-t-e) means fairly or very, and ''quit'' means stop.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_hard', 1, 'argue + ment = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"argument"'::jsonb, 'This word breaks the usual rule: the e at the end of ''argue'' is dropped before -ment, so it is argument, not arguement.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004130130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041301', 'very_hard', 1, 'The plural of ''potato'' is ''potatoes''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Some words ending in o add -es to make the plural: one potato, two potatoes. Tomato and hero work the same way.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the same as ''quick''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"fast"},{"id":"b","text":"slow"},{"id":"c","text":"quiet"},{"id":"d","text":"heavy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Quick'' and ''fast'' are synonyms: they mean the same thing.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_easy', 1, '''Ancient'' and ''modern'' are antonyms.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Antonyms are opposites. Ancient means very old and modern means new.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the opposite of ''generous''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"selfish"},{"id":"b","text":"kind"},{"id":"c","text":"giving"},{"id":"d","text":"friendly"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A generous person shares. A selfish person keeps things for themselves, so ''selfish'' is the opposite.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'easy', 1, '''The thirsty hikers were delighted to find a cool stream.'' What does ''delighted'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"very pleased"},{"id":"b","text":"very tired"},{"id":"c","text":"lost"},{"id":"d","text":"angry"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Thirsty hikers would be happy to find water, so ''delighted'' means very pleased.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'easy', 1, 'The opposite of ''include'' is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["exclude","leave out"]'::jsonb, 'To include means to let in. To exclude means to leave out. The prefixes in- and ex- help show the opposite.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'easy', 1, 'Which words belong to the same word family as ''friend''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"friendly"},{"id":"b","text":"friendship"},{"id":"c","text":"unfriendly"},{"id":"d","text":"fried"},{"id":"e","text":"frighten"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Friendly, friendship and unfriendly all have the base word ''friend''. ''Fried'' and ''frighten'' just look similar.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from quietest to loudest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"silent"},{"id":"b","text":"quiet"},{"id":"c","text":"loud"},{"id":"d","text":"deafening"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Silent means no sound at all. Quiet is a little sound, loud is a lot, and deafening is so loud it hurts your ears.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'medium', 1, '''The scientist observed the ants for an hour and wrote notes about everything they did.'' What does ''observed'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"watched carefully"},{"id":"b","text":"stepped on"},{"id":"c","text":"fed"},{"id":"d","text":"ignored"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'She wrote notes about everything the ants did, so she must have watched them carefully.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'medium', 1, 'What word starting with ''a'' means ''the person who wrote a book''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"author"'::jsonb, 'The author is the person who writes a book. Their name is usually on the front cover.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'hard', 1, 'In geography, what is a ''peninsula''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"land almost surrounded by water"},{"id":"b","text":"a very tall mountain"},{"id":"c","text":"a large lake"},{"id":"d","text":"a dry desert"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A peninsula is land with water on most sides but still joined to the mainland, like Cape York in Queensland.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'hard', 1, 'Which words could replace ''good'' to make this sentence more precise? ''It was a good movie.'' Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"thrilling"},{"id":"b","text":"hilarious"},{"id":"c","text":"fascinating"},{"id":"d","text":"went"},{"id":"e","text":"table"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Thrilling, hilarious and fascinating are describing words that tell exactly what kind of good. ''Went'' and ''table'' are not describing words.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'hard', 1, 'The prefix ''pre'' means ''before''. What does ''preview'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to see before"},{"id":"b","text":"to see again"},{"id":"c","text":"to not see"},{"id":"d","text":"to see far away"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Pre'' means before and ''view'' means see, so a preview is seeing something before everyone else.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which word best fits? ''After losing the grand final by one goal, the netball team felt ___.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"devastated"},{"id":"b","text":"content"},{"id":"c","text":"cheerful"},{"id":"d","text":"bored"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Losing a grand final by just one goal would feel terrible. ''Devastated'' means extremely upset, which fits best.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these words in order from least likely to most likely.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"impossible"},{"id":"b","text":"unlikely"},{"id":"c","text":"likely"},{"id":"d","text":"certain"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Impossible means it can''t happen. Unlikely is a small chance, likely is a good chance, and certain means it will happen.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_hard', 1, '''Bio'' means ''life'' and ''graph'' means ''write''. Which word means ''the story of a person''s life'': ''biography'' or ''geography''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"biography"'::jsonb, 'Bio (life) + graph (write) makes biography, a written story of someone''s life. Geo means earth, so geography is about the Earth.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the same as ''begin''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"start"},{"id":"b","text":"finish"},{"id":"c","text":"stop"},{"id":"d","text":"wait"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Begin'' and ''start'' are synonyms: they mean the same thing.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_easy', 1, '''Gigantic'' and ''enormous'' are synonyms.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Synonyms mean the same. Gigantic and enormous both mean very, very big.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the opposite of ''arrive''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"depart"},{"id":"b","text":"come"},{"id":"c","text":"reach"},{"id":"d","text":"enter"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'To arrive is to get to a place. To depart is to leave it, so ''depart'' is the opposite.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'easy', 1, '''The magpie swooped at the cyclists, so they ducked and pedalled faster.'' What does ''swooped'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"flew down suddenly"},{"id":"b","text":"sang sweetly"},{"id":"c","text":"built a nest"},{"id":"d","text":"sat still"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The cyclists ducked, so the magpie must have come down at them fast. ''Swooped'' means flew down suddenly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'easy', 1, 'The opposite of ''visible'' is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"invisible"'::jsonb, 'Visible means able to be seen. The prefix in- means ''not'', so invisible means not able to be seen.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'easy', 1, 'Which words belong to the same word family as ''act''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"action"},{"id":"b","text":"actor"},{"id":"c","text":"react"},{"id":"d","text":"acorn"},{"id":"e","text":"attack"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Action, actor and react all have the base word ''act'' and are about doing. ''Acorn'' and ''attack'' just share some letters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from driest to wettest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"dry"},{"id":"b","text":"damp"},{"id":"c","text":"wet"},{"id":"d","text":"soaked"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Dry has no water. Damp is a little wet, wet has more water, and soaked means completely full of water.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'medium', 1, '''The ancient pots were fragile, so visitors were asked not to touch them.'' What does ''fragile'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"easily broken"},{"id":"b","text":"very heavy"},{"id":"c","text":"brightly coloured"},{"id":"d","text":"brand new"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Visitors were told not to touch them, which is a clue they could break. ''Fragile'' means easily broken.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'medium', 1, 'What word starting with ''i'' means ''the person who draws the pictures for a book''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"illustrator"'::jsonb, 'The author writes the words and the illustrator draws the pictures (the illustrations).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'hard', 1, 'In science, what is a ''predator''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"an animal that hunts other animals for food"},{"id":"b","text":"an animal that eats only plants"},{"id":"c","text":"a baby animal"},{"id":"d","text":"an animal that sleeps all winter"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A predator hunts and eats other animals, like a dingo or an eagle. The animal it hunts is called its prey.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'hard', 1, 'Which words could replace ''nice'' to make this sentence more precise? ''We had a nice day at the beach.'' Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"relaxing"},{"id":"b","text":"sunny"},{"id":"c","text":"wonderful"},{"id":"d","text":"quickly"},{"id":"e","text":"chair"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Relaxing, sunny and wonderful are describing words that tell exactly what kind of day it was. ''Quickly'' and ''chair'' don''t fit.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'hard', 1, 'The prefix ''tri'' means ''three''. What is a ''tripod''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a stand with three legs"},{"id":"b","text":"a stand with two legs"},{"id":"c","text":"a kind of boat"},{"id":"d","text":"a large tree"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Tri'' means three and ''pod'' means foot, so a tripod stands on three legs, like the stand for a camera.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which word best fits? ''The bushwalkers were ___ when the rescue helicopter finally found them after two cold nights.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"relieved"},{"id":"b","text":"annoyed"},{"id":"c","text":"bored"},{"id":"d","text":"jealous"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'After two cold nights lost, being found would take away all their worry. ''Relieved'' means glad that something bad is over.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"puddle"},{"id":"b","text":"pond"},{"id":"c","text":"lake"},{"id":"d","text":"ocean"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A puddle is tiny, a pond is small, a lake is much bigger, and an ocean is the largest body of water of all.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004140130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041401', 'very_hard', 1, '''Tele'' means ''far'' and ''scope'' means ''look''. Which word names a tool for looking at faraway things: ''telescope'' or ''microscope''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"telescope"'::jsonb, 'Tele (far) + scope (look) makes telescope, used to look at stars. ''Micro'' means small, so a microscope looks at tiny things.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Where did the Nguyen family go camping?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the Blue Mountains"},{"id":"b","text":"the beach"},{"id":"c","text":"their backyard"},{"id":"d","text":"the desert"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first sentence says they drove to a campsite in the Blue Mountains.', NULL, '{"title":"Bush Camp","text":"On the last weekend of the school holidays, the Nguyen family drove to a campsite in the Blue Mountains. Dad set up the tent while Kim and her little brother Tam gathered sticks for the fire.\n\nThat night, they toasted marshmallows and listened to the bush. A boobook owl called from a tall tree. Tam had never heard an owl before, and he sat very still so he would not scare it away.\n\nIn the morning, the family walked to a lookout. Mist filled the valley below like a bowl of white soup. Kim took a photo so she could show her class."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Tam had heard an owl many times before.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The text says Tam had never heard an owl before.', NULL, '{"title":"Bush Camp","text":"On the last weekend of the school holidays, the Nguyen family drove to a campsite in the Blue Mountains. Dad set up the tent while Kim and her little brother Tam gathered sticks for the fire.\n\nThat night, they toasted marshmallows and listened to the bush. A boobook owl called from a tall tree. Tam had never heard an owl before, and he sat very still so he would not scare it away.\n\nIn the morning, the family walked to a lookout. Mist filled the valley below like a bowl of white soup. Kim took a photo so she could show her class."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_easy', 1, 'What did the family toast over the fire?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["marshmallows","marshmallow"]'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says, ''That night, they toasted marshmallows.''', NULL, '{"title":"Bush Camp","text":"On the last weekend of the school holidays, the Nguyen family drove to a campsite in the Blue Mountains. Dad set up the tent while Kim and her little brother Tam gathered sticks for the fire.\n\nThat night, they toasted marshmallows and listened to the bush. A boobook owl called from a tall tree. Tam had never heard an owl before, and he sat very still so he would not scare it away.\n\nIn the morning, the family walked to a lookout. Mist filled the valley below like a bowl of white soup. Kim took a photo so she could show her class."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'easy', 1, 'What is the superb lyrebird famous for?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"copying other sounds"},{"id":"b","text":"flying long distances"},{"id":"c","text":"building nests in the sea"},{"id":"d","text":"eating fish"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The text says it is one of the best copycats in the animal world. It copies calls and other sounds.', NULL, '{"title":"Superb Lyrebirds","text":"The superb lyrebird lives in the forests of south-eastern Australia. It is famous for being one of the best copycats in the animal world.\n\nA male lyrebird can copy the calls of more than twenty other birds. Some lyrebirds have even copied sounds such as camera clicks and car alarms. The male sings loudly in winter to attract a mate. While he sings, he spreads his long, lacy tail feathers over his head and dances.\n\nLyrebirds spend most of their time on the ground, scratching through leaves for insects, worms and spiders. They can fly, but only for short distances."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'easy', 1, 'A male lyrebird sings loudly in ___ to attract a mate.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"winter"'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says, ''The male sings loudly in winter to attract a mate.''', NULL, '{"title":"Superb Lyrebirds","text":"The superb lyrebird lives in the forests of south-eastern Australia. It is famous for being one of the best copycats in the animal world.\n\nA male lyrebird can copy the calls of more than twenty other birds. Some lyrebirds have even copied sounds such as camera clicks and car alarms. The male sings loudly in winter to attract a mate. While he sings, he spreads his long, lacy tail feathers over his head and dances.\n\nLyrebirds spend most of their time on the ground, scratching through leaves for insects, worms and spiders. They can fly, but only for short distances."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'easy', 1, 'Which foods do lyrebirds eat, according to the text? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"insects"},{"id":"b","text":"worms"},{"id":"c","text":"spiders"},{"id":"d","text":"seeds"},{"id":"e","text":"fish"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The last paragraph says they scratch through leaves for insects, worms and spiders. Seeds and fish are not mentioned.', NULL, '{"title":"Superb Lyrebirds","text":"The superb lyrebird lives in the forests of south-eastern Australia. It is famous for being one of the best copycats in the animal world.\n\nA male lyrebird can copy the calls of more than twenty other birds. Some lyrebirds have even copied sounds such as camera clicks and car alarms. The male sings loudly in winter to attract a mate. While he sings, he spreads his long, lacy tail feathers over his head and dances.\n\nLyrebirds spend most of their time on the ground, scratching through leaves for insects, worms and spiders. They can fly, but only for short distances."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'medium', 1, 'What does ''His face went pale'' tell you about how Oscar felt?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"shocked and upset"},{"id":"b","text":"bored"},{"id":"c","text":"proud"},{"id":"d","text":"sleepy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'People''s faces can go pale when they get a nasty shock. Oscar had just broken the volcano he worked on for weeks.', NULL, '{"title":"The Science Fair","text":"Oscar had spent three weeks building a volcano for the science fair. He had painted it brown and green and practised the eruption five times in the backyard.\n\nOn the morning of the fair, Oscar tripped on the classroom step. The volcano slipped out of his arms and cracked down the middle. His face went pale.\n\nMs Rossi knelt beside him. “We have tape, glue and twenty minutes,” she said. Oscar took a deep breath and nodded.\n\nWhen the judges arrived, a jagged crack still ran down the volcano''s side. But when the red foam bubbled out of the top, the crowd cheered. Later, Oscar pinned a second-place ribbon to his shirt."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'medium', 1, 'Put these events in the order they happened.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Oscar practised the eruption in the backyard."},{"id":"b","text":"Oscar tripped on the classroom step."},{"id":"c","text":"Oscar and Ms Rossi fixed the volcano."},{"id":"d","text":"The red foam bubbled out of the top."},{"id":"e","text":"Oscar pinned on a second-place ribbon."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Follow the time clues: practising came before the fair, the trip happened that morning, then the repair, the eruption, and ''Later'' the ribbon.', NULL, '{"title":"The Science Fair","text":"Oscar had spent three weeks building a volcano for the science fair. He had painted it brown and green and practised the eruption five times in the backyard.\n\nOn the morning of the fair, Oscar tripped on the classroom step. The volcano slipped out of his arms and cracked down the middle. His face went pale.\n\nMs Rossi knelt beside him. “We have tape, glue and twenty minutes,” she said. Oscar took a deep breath and nodded.\n\nWhen the judges arrived, a jagged crack still ran down the volcano''s side. But when the red foam bubbled out of the top, the crowd cheered. Later, Oscar pinned a second-place ribbon to his shirt."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'medium', 1, 'Oscar''s volcano won first prize.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Oscar pinned a second-place ribbon to his shirt, so he came second.', NULL, '{"title":"The Science Fair","text":"Oscar had spent three weeks building a volcano for the science fair. He had painted it brown and green and practised the eruption five times in the backyard.\n\nOn the morning of the fair, Oscar tripped on the classroom step. The volcano slipped out of his arms and cracked down the middle. His face went pale.\n\nMs Rossi knelt beside him. “We have tape, glue and twenty minutes,” she said. Oscar took a deep breath and nodded.\n\nWhen the judges arrived, a jagged crack still ran down the volcano''s side. But when the red foam bubbled out of the top, the crowd cheered. Later, Oscar pinned a second-place ribbon to his shirt."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'hard', 1, 'What is the main reason Ruby wrote this letter?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to thank Aunty Jo and tell her how she used the binoculars"},{"id":"b","text":"to ask Aunty Jo for money"},{"id":"c","text":"to complain about her sore legs"},{"id":"d","text":"to explain how to catch fish"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Ruby starts by saying thank you, then describes the birds she saw with the binoculars Aunty Jo gave her.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter to Aunty Jo","text":"Dear Aunty Jo,\n\nThank you for the binoculars! I took them to Lake Macquarie on Sunday with Dad. We sat on the jetty for nearly two hours. My legs went numb, but it was worth it.\n\nFirst we saw pelicans gliding over the water. Then Dad pointed at a big bird sitting on top of a dead tree. Through the binoculars I could see its white head and its sharp, hooked beak. Suddenly it dived into the lake and came up with a fish in its claws!\n\nI have started a notebook where I write down every bird I see. So far I have nineteen. Next holidays, can you come with us?\n\nLove,\nRuby"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'hard', 1, '''My legs went numb, but it was worth it.'' What does Ruby mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Sitting still so long was uncomfortable, but seeing the birds made it worthwhile."},{"id":"b","text":"She hurt her legs and wanted to go home."},{"id":"c","text":"The jetty was broken."},{"id":"d","text":"She did not enjoy watching the birds."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Numb'' means she couldn''t feel her legs from sitting so long. ''Worth it'' means what she saw was better than the discomfort.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter to Aunty Jo","text":"Dear Aunty Jo,\n\nThank you for the binoculars! I took them to Lake Macquarie on Sunday with Dad. We sat on the jetty for nearly two hours. My legs went numb, but it was worth it.\n\nFirst we saw pelicans gliding over the water. Then Dad pointed at a big bird sitting on top of a dead tree. Through the binoculars I could see its white head and its sharp, hooked beak. Suddenly it dived into the lake and came up with a fish in its claws!\n\nI have started a notebook where I write down every bird I see. So far I have nineteen. Next holidays, can you come with us?\n\nLove,\nRuby"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'hard', 1, 'What can you tell about Ruby from her letter? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"She is patient."},{"id":"b","text":"She is interested in birds."},{"id":"c","text":"She is grateful to Aunty Jo."},{"id":"d","text":"She dislikes being outdoors."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'She waited nearly two hours (patient), keeps a bird notebook (interested) and says thank you (grateful). She enjoyed being outside.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter to Aunty Jo","text":"Dear Aunty Jo,\n\nThank you for the binoculars! I took them to Lake Macquarie on Sunday with Dad. We sat on the jetty for nearly two hours. My legs went numb, but it was worth it.\n\nFirst we saw pelicans gliding over the water. Then Dad pointed at a big bird sitting on top of a dead tree. Through the binoculars I could see its white head and its sharp, hooked beak. Suddenly it dived into the lake and came up with a fish in its claws!\n\nI have started a notebook where I write down every bird I see. So far I have nineteen. Next holidays, can you come with us?\n\nLove,\nRuby"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the main idea of this text?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Cane toads were brought in to help farmers, but they became a serious problem."},{"id":"b","text":"Cane toads are very good climbers."},{"id":"c","text":"Quolls love eating sausages."},{"id":"d","text":"Sugar cane grows in New South Wales."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The text explains why cane toads were brought in, why the plan failed, and the harm they now cause. That is the big idea.', NULL, '{"title":"Cane Toads","text":"Cane toads were brought to Queensland in 1935. Farmers hoped the toads would eat the beetles that were damaging their sugar cane. The plan did not work. The beetles lived high up on the cane stalks, and cane toads cannot climb.\n\nWith no natural enemies to stop them, the toads spread quickly. A female can lay up to 30 000 eggs at a time. Today, cane toads are found across northern Australia and into New South Wales.\n\nCane toads are poisonous. When a quoll, goanna or snake tries to eat one, it can die. Scientists are now teaching some quolls to avoid toads by feeding them toad sausages that make them feel sick but do not harm them."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_hard', 1, 'The toads could not eat the beetles because cane toads cannot ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["climb","climb up"]'::jsonb, 'The beetles lived high up on the cane stalks, and the text says cane toads cannot climb, so they could not reach them.', NULL, '{"title":"Cane Toads","text":"Cane toads were brought to Queensland in 1935. Farmers hoped the toads would eat the beetles that were damaging their sugar cane. The plan did not work. The beetles lived high up on the cane stalks, and cane toads cannot climb.\n\nWith no natural enemies to stop them, the toads spread quickly. A female can lay up to 30 000 eggs at a time. Today, cane toads are found across northern Australia and into New South Wales.\n\nCane toads are poisonous. When a quoll, goanna or snake tries to eat one, it can die. Scientists are now teaching some quolls to avoid toads by feeding them toad sausages that make them feel sick but do not harm them."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why would scientists want quolls to feel sick after eating a toad sausage?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"So the quolls learn to stay away from real cane toads."},{"id":"b","text":"So the quolls grow bigger."},{"id":"c","text":"To stop the quolls eating beetles."},{"id":"d","text":"Because the sausages are deadly poison."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'If a quoll feels sick after tasting toad, it learns not to eat toads. That could save its life when it meets a real, poisonous toad.', NULL, '{"title":"Cane Toads","text":"Cane toads were brought to Queensland in 1935. Farmers hoped the toads would eat the beetles that were damaging their sugar cane. The plan did not work. The beetles lived high up on the cane stalks, and cane toads cannot climb.\n\nWith no natural enemies to stop them, the toads spread quickly. A female can lay up to 30 000 eggs at a time. Today, cane toads are found across northern Australia and into New South Wales.\n\nCane toads are poisonous. When a quoll, goanna or snake tries to eat one, it can die. Scientists are now teaching some quolls to avoid toads by feeding them toad sausages that make them feel sick but do not harm them."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_easy', 1, 'How did Hamid and his grandmother get to the markets?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"by train"},{"id":"b","text":"by car"},{"id":"c","text":"by bus"},{"id":"d","text":"on foot"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first sentence says they ''caught the train to the Parramatta markets''.', NULL, '{"title":"Market Day","text":"Every second Saturday, Hamid and his grandmother caught the train to the Parramatta markets. Grandma always brought her green shopping trolley with one squeaky wheel.\n\nFirst they bought mangoes and bananas from Mr Tran''s fruit stall. Then they stopped at the bakery van for a warm cheese roll each. Hamid''s favourite stall sold old comic books, and he was always allowed to choose one.\n\nOn the train home, the trolley was so heavy that Hamid had to help Grandma push it up the ramp. “Next time,” Grandma laughed, “we buy fewer mangoes!”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Grandma''s shopping trolley was blue.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The text says Grandma brought her green shopping trolley.', NULL, '{"title":"Market Day","text":"Every second Saturday, Hamid and his grandmother caught the train to the Parramatta markets. Grandma always brought her green shopping trolley with one squeaky wheel.\n\nFirst they bought mangoes and bananas from Mr Tran''s fruit stall. Then they stopped at the bakery van for a warm cheese roll each. Hamid''s favourite stall sold old comic books, and he was always allowed to choose one.\n\nOn the train home, the trolley was so heavy that Hamid had to help Grandma push it up the ramp. “Next time,” Grandma laughed, “we buy fewer mangoes!”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_easy', 1, 'What did Hamid choose at his favourite stall?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["a comic book","comic book","comic","a comic","an old comic book","old comic book","comics"]'::jsonb, 'The text says his favourite stall sold old comic books, and he was allowed to choose one.', NULL, '{"title":"Market Day","text":"Every second Saturday, Hamid and his grandmother caught the train to the Parramatta markets. Grandma always brought her green shopping trolley with one squeaky wheel.\n\nFirst they bought mangoes and bananas from Mr Tran''s fruit stall. Then they stopped at the bakery van for a warm cheese roll each. Hamid''s favourite stall sold old comic books, and he was always allowed to choose one.\n\nOn the train home, the trolley was so heavy that Hamid had to help Grandma push it up the ramp. “Next time,” Grandma laughed, “we buy fewer mangoes!”"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'easy', 1, 'How do flying foxes find their way?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"with their big eyes and sense of smell"},{"id":"b","text":"by using echoes"},{"id":"c","text":"by following the moon"},{"id":"d","text":"by listening for other bats"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first paragraph says they do not use echoes. Instead, they use their big eyes and strong sense of smell.', NULL, '{"title":"Flying Foxes","text":"Flying foxes are large bats that live in Australia. They are also called fruit bats. Unlike many small bats, flying foxes do not use echoes to find their way. Instead, they use their big eyes and strong sense of smell.\n\nFlying foxes sleep during the day, hanging upside down in trees. Large groups, called camps, can have thousands of bats. At dusk they fly out to feed on nectar, pollen and fruit.\n\nFlying foxes are important for the bush. As they travel from tree to tree, they carry pollen and seeds, which helps new trees to grow."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'easy', 1, 'A large group of flying foxes is called a ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["camp","camps"]'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says, ''Large groups, called camps, can have thousands of bats.''', NULL, '{"title":"Flying Foxes","text":"Flying foxes are large bats that live in Australia. They are also called fruit bats. Unlike many small bats, flying foxes do not use echoes to find their way. Instead, they use their big eyes and strong sense of smell.\n\nFlying foxes sleep during the day, hanging upside down in trees. Large groups, called camps, can have thousands of bats. At dusk they fly out to feed on nectar, pollen and fruit.\n\nFlying foxes are important for the bush. As they travel from tree to tree, they carry pollen and seeds, which helps new trees to grow."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'easy', 1, 'Which foods do flying foxes eat, according to the text? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"nectar"},{"id":"b","text":"pollen"},{"id":"c","text":"fruit"},{"id":"d","text":"insects"},{"id":"e","text":"fish"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The text says they feed on nectar, pollen and fruit. Insects and fish are not mentioned.', NULL, '{"title":"Flying Foxes","text":"Flying foxes are large bats that live in Australia. They are also called fruit bats. Unlike many small bats, flying foxes do not use echoes to find their way. Instead, they use their big eyes and strong sense of smell.\n\nFlying foxes sleep during the day, hanging upside down in trees. Large groups, called camps, can have thousands of bats. At dusk they fly out to feed on nectar, pollen and fruit.\n\nFlying foxes are important for the bush. As they travel from tree to tree, they carry pollen and seeds, which helps new trees to grow."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'medium', 1, 'What does ''Her knees were shaking'' tell you about how Amira felt?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"nervous"},{"id":"b","text":"bored"},{"id":"c","text":"cold"},{"id":"d","text":"angry"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Amira was in the final two on a stage in front of everyone. Shaking knees are a clue that she was nervous.', NULL, '{"title":"The Spelling Bee","text":"Amira had practised her spelling list every night for a month. Her little sister even held up flash cards at breakfast.\n\nAt the school spelling bee, Amira got through round after round. Soon only she and a Year 6 boy named Harrison were left on the stage. Her knees were shaking.\n\n“Your word is ''rhythm'',” said the principal. Amira closed her eyes and pictured the flash card. “R-h-y-t-h-m,” she said slowly.\n\nThe hall burst into applause. Harrison smiled and shook her hand. Amira couldn''t stop grinning all the way home."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'medium', 1, 'Put these events in the order they happened.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Amira practised her list every night."},{"id":"b","text":"Amira got through round after round."},{"id":"c","text":"Only Amira and Harrison were left."},{"id":"d","text":"Amira spelt ''rhythm''."},{"id":"e","text":"Harrison shook Amira''s hand."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Practising happened for a month before the bee. Then the rounds, the final two, the last word, and the handshake after the applause.', NULL, '{"title":"The Spelling Bee","text":"Amira had practised her spelling list every night for a month. Her little sister even held up flash cards at breakfast.\n\nAt the school spelling bee, Amira got through round after round. Soon only she and a Year 6 boy named Harrison were left on the stage. Her knees were shaking.\n\n“Your word is ''rhythm'',” said the principal. Amira closed her eyes and pictured the flash card. “R-h-y-t-h-m,” she said slowly.\n\nThe hall burst into applause. Harrison smiled and shook her hand. Amira couldn''t stop grinning all the way home."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'medium', 1, 'Amira''s little sister helped her practise.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The text says her little sister held up flash cards at breakfast, which helped Amira practise.', NULL, '{"title":"The Spelling Bee","text":"Amira had practised her spelling list every night for a month. Her little sister even held up flash cards at breakfast.\n\nAt the school spelling bee, Amira got through round after round. Soon only she and a Year 6 boy named Harrison were left on the stage. Her knees were shaking.\n\n“Your word is ''rhythm'',” said the principal. Amira closed her eyes and pictured the flash card. “R-h-y-t-h-m,” she said slowly.\n\nThe hall burst into applause. Harrison smiled and shook her hand. Amira couldn''t stop grinning all the way home."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'hard', 1, 'What is the main reason Sami wrote this email?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to tell Mum and Dad what has been happening at camp"},{"id":"b","text":"to ask to come home early"},{"id":"c","text":"to complain about kayaking"},{"id":"d","text":"to explain how to do magic tricks"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Sami tells his parents about kayaking, the food and the talent show. He is sharing news from camp.', NULL, '{"title":"An Email from Camp","text":"Hi Mum and Dad,\n\nCamp at Myall Lakes is going well. Yesterday we went kayaking. Will and I shared a kayak, and we tipped over twice! The water was freezing, but by the end we could paddle in a straight line.\n\nThe food is not as good as yours, Dad. Last night''s spaghetti tasted like cardboard. Still, I ate two bowls because I was so hungry after the bushwalk.\n\nTonight is the talent show. I''m doing my magic tricks, so I''ve been practising with the cards you packed. Wish me luck!\n\nSee you Friday,\nSami"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'hard', 1, '''Last night''s spaghetti tasted like cardboard.'' What does Sami mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The spaghetti had very little flavour."},{"id":"b","text":"The spaghetti was served on cardboard plates."},{"id":"c","text":"The spaghetti was delicious."},{"id":"d","text":"The spaghetti was made from paper."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Cardboard has no taste, so Sami is saying the spaghetti was bland. He still ate it because he was hungry.', NULL, '{"title":"An Email from Camp","text":"Hi Mum and Dad,\n\nCamp at Myall Lakes is going well. Yesterday we went kayaking. Will and I shared a kayak, and we tipped over twice! The water was freezing, but by the end we could paddle in a straight line.\n\nThe food is not as good as yours, Dad. Last night''s spaghetti tasted like cardboard. Still, I ate two bowls because I was so hungry after the bushwalk.\n\nTonight is the talent show. I''m doing my magic tricks, so I''ve been practising with the cards you packed. Wish me luck!\n\nSee you Friday,\nSami"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'hard', 1, 'What can you tell about Sami from his email? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"He keeps trying when things are hard."},{"id":"b","text":"He has a sense of humour."},{"id":"c","text":"He likes doing magic."},{"id":"d","text":"He refused to eat dinner."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'He kept paddling after tipping over twice, jokes about the cardboard spaghetti and is doing magic in the talent show. He ate two bowls of dinner.', NULL, '{"title":"An Email from Camp","text":"Hi Mum and Dad,\n\nCamp at Myall Lakes is going well. Yesterday we went kayaking. Will and I shared a kayak, and we tipped over twice! The water was freezing, but by the end we could paddle in a straight line.\n\nThe food is not as good as yours, Dad. Last night''s spaghetti tasted like cardboard. Still, I ate two bowls because I was so hungry after the bushwalk.\n\nTonight is the talent show. I''m doing my magic tricks, so I''ve been practising with the cards you packed. Wish me luck!\n\nSee you Friday,\nSami"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the main idea of this text?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Many Australian plants can survive or even use fire, but fires that are too frequent can still harm them."},{"id":"b","text":"Bushfires are always good for the bush."},{"id":"c","text":"Banksias are the tallest plants in Australia."},{"id":"d","text":"Eucalyptus trees have thin bark."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The text explains how plants survive and use fire, then warns that fires happening too often can kill young plants. That is the big idea.', NULL, '{"title":"Plants and Bushfires","text":"Many Australian plants have amazing ways of surviving bushfires. Some eucalyptus trees have thick bark that protects them from the heat. After a fire, new green shoots sprout straight from their blackened trunks.\n\nOther plants need fire to spread their seeds. Some banksia cones stay tightly shut for years. The heat of a fire opens them, and the seeds fall onto the ash-covered ground. The ash makes the soil rich, and the fire has cleared away taller plants that would block the sunlight.\n\nThis does not mean fires are always good for the bush. Very hot fires that happen too often can kill young plants before they are old enough to make seeds."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Some banksia cones are opened by the ___ of a fire.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"heat"'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says, ''The heat of a fire opens them, and the seeds fall onto the ash-covered ground.''', NULL, '{"title":"Plants and Bushfires","text":"Many Australian plants have amazing ways of surviving bushfires. Some eucalyptus trees have thick bark that protects them from the heat. After a fire, new green shoots sprout straight from their blackened trunks.\n\nOther plants need fire to spread their seeds. Some banksia cones stay tightly shut for years. The heat of a fire opens them, and the seeds fall onto the ash-covered ground. The ash makes the soil rich, and the fire has cleared away taller plants that would block the sunlight.\n\nThis does not mean fires are always good for the bush. Very hot fires that happen too often can kill young plants before they are old enough to make seeds."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why can fires that happen too often be harmful, even to plants that need fire?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Young plants are burnt before they are old enough to make seeds."},{"id":"b","text":"The ash makes the soil too rich."},{"id":"c","text":"The bark becomes too thick."},{"id":"d","text":"Banksia cones open too slowly."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'If another fire comes before young plants make seeds, there are no new seeds to grow. Over time the plants could disappear.', NULL, '{"title":"Plants and Bushfires","text":"Many Australian plants have amazing ways of surviving bushfires. Some eucalyptus trees have thick bark that protects them from the heat. After a fire, new green shoots sprout straight from their blackened trunks.\n\nOther plants need fire to spread their seeds. Some banksia cones stay tightly shut for years. The heat of a fire opens them, and the seeds fall onto the ash-covered ground. The ash makes the soil rich, and the fire has cleared away taller plants that would block the sunlight.\n\nThis does not mean fires are always good for the bush. Very hot fires that happen too often can kill young plants before they are old enough to make seeds."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Where is this poem set?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"at the beach"},{"id":"b","text":"in a forest"},{"id":"c","text":"at school"},{"id":"d","text":"in the snow"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Seagulls, waves, sandcastles and lifesavers all tell us the poem is set at the beach.', NULL, '{"title":"Summer Beach","text":"Seagulls squabble, waves roll in,\nSalty spray upon my skin,\nSandcastles with moats so wide,\nWaiting for the rising tide.\n\nFlags are flapping, red and gold,\nLifesavers are brave and bold,\nSwimmers stay between the two,\nUnder skies of brightest blue.\n\nIce-cream dribbles down my hand,\nFootprints wander through the sand,\nSun goes down in orange light.\nGoodbye, beach, and goodnight!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word in the poem rhymes with ''hand''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"sand"'::jsonb, '''Hand'' and ''sand'' both end with the sound -and. They are a rhyming pair in the last stanza.', NULL, '{"title":"Summer Beach","text":"Seagulls squabble, waves roll in,\nSalty spray upon my skin,\nSandcastles with moats so wide,\nWaiting for the rising tide.\n\nFlags are flapping, red and gold,\nLifesavers are brave and bold,\nSwimmers stay between the two,\nUnder skies of brightest blue.\n\nIce-cream dribbles down my hand,\nFootprints wander through the sand,\nSun goes down in orange light.\nGoodbye, beach, and goodnight!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_easy', 1, 'The poem ends as the sun is going down.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The last stanza says, ''Sun goes down in orange light'' and then ''goodnight''.', NULL, '{"title":"Summer Beach","text":"Seagulls squabble, waves roll in,\nSalty spray upon my skin,\nSandcastles with moats so wide,\nWaiting for the rising tide.\n\nFlags are flapping, red and gold,\nLifesavers are brave and bold,\nSwimmers stay between the two,\nUnder skies of brightest blue.\n\nIce-cream dribbles down my hand,\nFootprints wander through the sand,\nSun goes down in orange light.\nGoodbye, beach, and goodnight!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'easy', 1, 'Which word best describes Jarrah?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"generous"},{"id":"b","text":"greedy"},{"id":"c","text":"lazy"},{"id":"d","text":"rude"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Jarrah used his own money to help the old man, so he is generous.', NULL, '{"title":"The Corner Shop","text":"Every Saturday, Jarrah helped Mr Patel at the corner shop. He stacked the shelves, swept the floor and always said hello to the customers by name.\n\nOne afternoon, an old man counted his coins at the counter and frowned. He was twenty cents short for his loaf of bread. Before Mr Patel could speak, Jarrah slipped a coin from his own pocket onto the counter.\n\n“Must have rolled under the till,” Jarrah said with a wink.\n\nThe old man''s eyes crinkled. “Thank you, young man,” he said quietly."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'easy', 1, 'Why did Jarrah say the coin ''must have rolled under the till''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"So the old man would not feel embarrassed"},{"id":"b","text":"Because he really found it on the floor"},{"id":"c","text":"Because Mr Patel told him to"},{"id":"d","text":"To trick Mr Patel"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The coin came from Jarrah''s own pocket. Pretending it was found let the old man accept help without feeling embarrassed. The wink is a clue.', NULL, '{"title":"The Corner Shop","text":"Every Saturday, Jarrah helped Mr Patel at the corner shop. He stacked the shelves, swept the floor and always said hello to the customers by name.\n\nOne afternoon, an old man counted his coins at the counter and frowned. He was twenty cents short for his loaf of bread. Before Mr Patel could speak, Jarrah slipped a coin from his own pocket onto the counter.\n\n“Must have rolled under the till,” Jarrah said with a wink.\n\nThe old man''s eyes crinkled. “Thank you, young man,” he said quietly."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'easy', 1, 'Which details show that Jarrah is a hard worker? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"He stacked the shelves."},{"id":"b","text":"He swept the floor."},{"id":"c","text":"He winked."},{"id":"d","text":"He helped every Saturday."},{"id":"e","text":"The old man frowned."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Stacking shelves, sweeping and helping every Saturday show hard work. A wink and the old man''s frown are not about work.', NULL, '{"title":"The Corner Shop","text":"Every Saturday, Jarrah helped Mr Patel at the corner shop. He stacked the shelves, swept the floor and always said hello to the customers by name.\n\nOne afternoon, an old man counted his coins at the counter and frowned. He was twenty cents short for his loaf of bread. Before Mr Patel could speak, Jarrah slipped a coin from his own pocket onto the counter.\n\n“Must have rolled under the till,” Jarrah said with a wink.\n\nThe old man''s eyes crinkled. “Thank you, young man,” he said quietly."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'medium', 1, '''The sky … turned the colour of a bruise.'' What colour is the writer describing?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"dark purple and grey"},{"id":"b","text":"bright blue"},{"id":"c","text":"sunny yellow"},{"id":"d","text":"pure white"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A bruise is dark purple and grey, like heavy storm clouds. The writer compares the sky to a bruise.', NULL, '{"title":"The Storm","text":"The sky over the paddock turned the colour of a bruise. Dry grass hissed as the wind raced through it, and the old windmill groaned on its rusty legs.\n\nThen the first fat drops hit the tin roof like a handful of stones. Within minutes, the rain was roaring. Water gushed from the gutters, and the thirsty red dirt drank it in.\n\nOn the verandah, Grandad held out his wrinkled hands and let the rain fill them. After two years of drought, he was laughing."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'medium', 1, 'Which words help you hear the storm? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"hissed"},{"id":"b","text":"groaned"},{"id":"c","text":"roaring"},{"id":"d","text":"wrinkled"},{"id":"e","text":"red"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Hissed, groaned and roaring are sound words. ''Wrinkled'' and ''red'' describe how things look.', NULL, '{"title":"The Storm","text":"The sky over the paddock turned the colour of a bruise. Dry grass hissed as the wind raced through it, and the old windmill groaned on its rusty legs.\n\nThen the first fat drops hit the tin roof like a handful of stones. Within minutes, the rain was roaring. Water gushed from the gutters, and the thirsty red dirt drank it in.\n\nOn the verandah, Grandad held out his wrinkled hands and let the rain fill them. After two years of drought, he was laughing."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'medium', 1, 'Why was Grandad laughing at the end?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He was happy that rain had finally come after the drought."},{"id":"b","text":"He thought the bruise was funny."},{"id":"c","text":"He was laughing at the windmill."},{"id":"d","text":"He was cold and wet."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The last sentence says there had been two years of drought. The rain meant the dry land would finally get water.', NULL, '{"title":"The Storm","text":"The sky over the paddock turned the colour of a bruise. Dry grass hissed as the wind raced through it, and the old windmill groaned on its rusty legs.\n\nThen the first fat drops hit the tin roof like a handful of stones. Within minutes, the rain was roaring. Water gushed from the gutters, and the thirsty red dirt drank it in.\n\nOn the verandah, Grandad held out his wrinkled hands and let the rain fill them. After two years of drought, he was laughing."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'hard', 1, 'How does Maddie feel about Ellie being made captain?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"jealous and upset"},{"id":"b","text":"proud of Ellie"},{"id":"c","text":"relieved"},{"id":"d","text":"scared"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Maddie couldn''t believe it and kicked her boots, which shows she was upset and felt she deserved it more.', NULL, '{"title":"The New Captain","text":"Maddie: I couldn''t believe it when Coach picked Ellie as captain. I had been to every training session all season. Ellie missed three! I kicked my boots across the change room.\n\nEllie: When Coach said my name, my stomach flipped. I had missed three training sessions while Mum was in hospital, and I thought Coach had forgotten about me. I wanted to tell Maddie how nervous I was, but she wouldn''t even look at me."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'hard', 1, 'Which information do we learn only from Ellie''s point of view?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Ellie missed training because her mum was in hospital."},{"id":"b","text":"Ellie missed three training sessions."},{"id":"c","text":"Coach picked Ellie as captain."},{"id":"d","text":"Maddie went to every training session."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Maddie knows Ellie missed training, but only Ellie tells us why: her mum was in hospital.', NULL, '{"title":"The New Captain","text":"Maddie: I couldn''t believe it when Coach picked Ellie as captain. I had been to every training session all season. Ellie missed three! I kicked my boots across the change room.\n\nEllie: When Coach said my name, my stomach flipped. I had missed three training sessions while Mum was in hospital, and I thought Coach had forgotten about me. I wanted to tell Maddie how nervous I was, but she wouldn''t even look at me."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'hard', 1, 'Both parts of this text are written in the first person.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Maddie and Ellie each tell their own part using ''I'' and ''my'', so both are first person.', NULL, '{"title":"The New Captain","text":"Maddie: I couldn''t believe it when Coach picked Ellie as captain. I had been to every training session all season. Ellie missed three! I kicked my boots across the change room.\n\nEllie: When Coach said my name, my stomach flipped. I had missed three training sessions while Mum was in hospital, and I thought Coach had forgotten about me. I wanted to tell Maddie how nervous I was, but she wouldn''t even look at me."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which features does this poem use? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"pairs of rhyming lines"},{"id":"b","text":"a regular, bouncy rhythm"},{"id":"c","text":"a first-person voice (''I'')"},{"id":"d","text":"speech marks for dialogue"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Lines rhyme in pairs (lies/skies, mud/flood), the beat repeats in every line, and the river speaks as ''I''. There is no dialogue.', NULL, '{"title":"The River''s Song","text":"I start as a trickle where mountain mist lies,\nI tumble down rocks under wide open skies,\nI wind through the valleys past cattle and sheep,\nI slow in the lowlands, grown wide and grown deep.\n\nI carry the rain and I carry the mud,\nI''m gentle in summer, I''m wild in a flood.\nAnd when I am tired from travelling far,\nI rest in the ocean beneath the first star."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put the river''s journey in the order it is told in the poem.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"a trickle in the mountains"},{"id":"b","text":"tumbling down rocks"},{"id":"c","text":"winding through valleys"},{"id":"d","text":"slowing in the lowlands"},{"id":"e","text":"resting in the ocean"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'The poem follows the river from where it starts in the mountains, downhill through the valleys and lowlands, to the ocean.', NULL, '{"title":"The River''s Song","text":"I start as a trickle where mountain mist lies,\nI tumble down rocks under wide open skies,\nI wind through the valleys past cattle and sheep,\nI slow in the lowlands, grown wide and grown deep.\n\nI carry the rain and I carry the mud,\nI''m gentle in summer, I''m wild in a flood.\nAnd when I am tired from travelling far,\nI rest in the ocean beneath the first star."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_hard', 1, '''I''m gentle in summer, I''m wild in a flood.'' Why does the poet use two opposite words here?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"To show the river can change from calm to dangerous"},{"id":"b","text":"To show the river is confused"},{"id":"c","text":"Because floods only happen in summer"},{"id":"d","text":"To make the poem shorter"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Putting ''gentle'' and ''wild'' side by side shows the contrast: the same river can be calm or powerful and dangerous.', NULL, '{"title":"The River''s Song","text":"I start as a trickle where mountain mist lies,\nI tumble down rocks under wide open skies,\nI wind through the valleys past cattle and sheep,\nI slow in the lowlands, grown wide and grown deep.\n\nI carry the rain and I carry the mud,\nI''m gentle in summer, I''m wild in a flood.\nAnd when I am tired from travelling far,\nI rest in the ocean beneath the first star."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_easy', 1, 'When is this poem set?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"at night"},{"id":"b","text":"in the morning"},{"id":"c","text":"at lunchtime"},{"id":"d","text":"in the afternoon"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The moon, waking possums, the boobook owl and the stars all show the poem is set at night.', NULL, '{"title":"Night in the Bush","text":"The moon climbs high, a silver light,\nThe possums wake to start the night,\nA frog croaks loudly by the creek,\nThe gum trees rustle, branches creak.\n\nA wallaby hops past our tent,\nIt sniffs the air to find a scent,\nThe breeze is cool upon my face,\nThe bush at night''s a magic place.\n\nA boobook calls from somewhere near,\nThe campfire crackles, warm and clear,\nThe stars come out to wink and shine,\nAnd I curl up, all snug and fine."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word in the poem rhymes with ''light''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"night"'::jsonb, '''Light'' and ''night'' both end with the sound -ight. They are the first rhyming pair in the poem.', NULL, '{"title":"Night in the Bush","text":"The moon climbs high, a silver light,\nThe possums wake to start the night,\nA frog croaks loudly by the creek,\nThe gum trees rustle, branches creak.\n\nA wallaby hops past our tent,\nIt sniffs the air to find a scent,\nThe breeze is cool upon my face,\nThe bush at night''s a magic place.\n\nA boobook calls from somewhere near,\nThe campfire crackles, warm and clear,\nThe stars come out to wink and shine,\nAnd I curl up, all snug and fine."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_easy', 1, 'In the poem, the stars are described as winking.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The poem says, ''The stars come out to wink and shine.'' Stars can''t really wink, which makes the image fun.', NULL, '{"title":"Night in the Bush","text":"The moon climbs high, a silver light,\nThe possums wake to start the night,\nA frog croaks loudly by the creek,\nThe gum trees rustle, branches creak.\n\nA wallaby hops past our tent,\nIt sniffs the air to find a scent,\nThe breeze is cool upon my face,\nThe bush at night''s a magic place.\n\nA boobook calls from somewhere near,\nThe campfire crackles, warm and clear,\nThe stars come out to wink and shine,\nAnd I curl up, all snug and fine."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'easy', 1, 'Which word best describes Tahlia?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"thoughtful"},{"id":"b","text":"selfish"},{"id":"c","text":"lazy"},{"id":"d","text":"bossy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Tahlia picked the new boy first and taught him how to hold the bat. She thought about how Zac would feel.', NULL, '{"title":"Tahlia''s Choice","text":"At sport, Ms Kelly asked Tahlia to pick a cricket team. Everyone expected her to choose the fastest bowlers first, like the other captain did.\n\nInstead, Tahlia called out, “Zac!” Zac was new and had never played cricket. He stared at her, then jogged over, smiling shyly.\n\nTahlia showed him how to hold the bat. When Zac hit his first four, the whole team cheered louder than they had all term."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'easy', 1, 'Why do you think Tahlia chose Zac first?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She wanted the new boy to feel welcome."},{"id":"b","text":"Zac was the fastest bowler."},{"id":"c","text":"Ms Kelly told her to pick Zac."},{"id":"d","text":"She wanted to lose the game."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Zac was new and had never played. Choosing him first, instead of the best players, would help him feel he belonged.', NULL, '{"title":"Tahlia''s Choice","text":"At sport, Ms Kelly asked Tahlia to pick a cricket team. Everyone expected her to choose the fastest bowlers first, like the other captain did.\n\nInstead, Tahlia called out, “Zac!” Zac was new and had never played cricket. He stared at her, then jogged over, smiling shyly.\n\nTahlia showed him how to hold the bat. When Zac hit his first four, the whole team cheered louder than they had all term."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'easy', 1, 'Which details show that Zac was surprised or shy at first? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"He stared at Tahlia."},{"id":"b","text":"He smiled shyly."},{"id":"c","text":"He hit a four."},{"id":"d","text":"The team cheered."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Staring shows surprise and ''smiling shyly'' shows he felt shy. Hitting a four and the cheering happened later.', NULL, '{"title":"Tahlia''s Choice","text":"At sport, Ms Kelly asked Tahlia to pick a cricket team. Everyone expected her to choose the fastest bowlers first, like the other captain did.\n\nInstead, Tahlia called out, “Zac!” Zac was new and had never played cricket. He stared at her, then jogged over, smiling shyly.\n\nTahlia showed him how to hold the bat. When Zac hit his first four, the whole team cheered louder than they had all term."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'medium', 1, '''The afternoon sun was a furnace.'' What does this tell you?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The day was extremely hot."},{"id":"b","text":"The sun was setting."},{"id":"c","text":"There was a fire in the street."},{"id":"d","text":"It was a cool, cloudy day."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A furnace is a very hot oven. The writer says the sun WAS a furnace to show how hot the day felt.', NULL, '{"title":"The Heatwave","text":"The afternoon sun was a furnace. Heat shimmered above the road like water, and the bitumen was soft and sticky under our thongs.\n\nThe cicadas screamed in the trees. Even the magpies had given up, sitting with their beaks wide open in the shade of the gum.\n\nInside, the old fan rattled and whirred, pushing the hot air from one side of the room to the other. Mum filled the paddling pool, and my sister and I lay in it like two lazy seals."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'medium', 1, '''My sister and I lay in it like two lazy seals.'' What are the children being compared to?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"lazy seals"},{"id":"b","text":"magpies"},{"id":"c","text":"cicadas"},{"id":"d","text":"an old fan"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The word ''like'' shows a comparison. The children lie still in the water, just like seals lazing about.', NULL, '{"title":"The Heatwave","text":"The afternoon sun was a furnace. Heat shimmered above the road like water, and the bitumen was soft and sticky under our thongs.\n\nThe cicadas screamed in the trees. Even the magpies had given up, sitting with their beaks wide open in the shade of the gum.\n\nInside, the old fan rattled and whirred, pushing the hot air from one side of the room to the other. Mum filled the paddling pool, and my sister and I lay in it like two lazy seals."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'medium', 1, 'Which details show how hot the day was? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Heat shimmered above the road."},{"id":"b","text":"The bitumen was soft and sticky."},{"id":"c","text":"The magpies sat with their beaks wide open."},{"id":"d","text":"The fan rattled and whirred."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Shimmering heat, melting bitumen and panting magpies all show the heat. A rattling, whirring fan is only about the noise it makes.', NULL, '{"title":"The Heatwave","text":"The afternoon sun was a furnace. Heat shimmered above the road like water, and the bitumen was soft and sticky under our thongs.\n\nThe cicadas screamed in the trees. Even the magpies had given up, sitting with their beaks wide open in the shade of the gum.\n\nInside, the old fan rattled and whirred, pushing the hot air from one side of the room to the other. Mum filled the paddling pool, and my sister and I lay in it like two lazy seals."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'hard', 1, 'How does Mr Cobb feel about the cricket balls?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"annoyed"},{"id":"b","text":"pleased"},{"id":"c","text":"excited"},{"id":"d","text":"scared"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A ball flattened his best tomato plant and he wants to ''teach those kids a lesson''. That shows he is annoyed.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Sides of the Fence","text":"Mr Cobb: Every week, a cricket ball lands in my vegetable patch. Last Tuesday one flattened my best tomato plant. I''ve kept the last three balls in my shed. Maybe that will teach those kids a lesson.\n\nLayla: Our backyard is tiny, so my brother and I have to play cricket right next to the fence. We''ve lost three balls this month! I made Mr Cobb a card to say sorry, but I''m too scared to knock on his door."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'hard', 1, 'Which information do we learn only from Layla''s point of view?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Layla made Mr Cobb a card to say sorry."},{"id":"b","text":"Cricket balls keep landing in the vegetable patch."},{"id":"c","text":"Three balls have gone over the fence."},{"id":"d","text":"Mr Cobb grows tomatoes."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Only Layla tells us about the sorry card. Mr Cobb doesn''t know about it, because she is too scared to knock on his door.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Sides of the Fence","text":"Mr Cobb: Every week, a cricket ball lands in my vegetable patch. Last Tuesday one flattened my best tomato plant. I''ve kept the last three balls in my shed. Maybe that will teach those kids a lesson.\n\nLayla: Our backyard is tiny, so my brother and I have to play cricket right next to the fence. We''ve lost three balls this month! I made Mr Cobb a card to say sorry, but I''m too scared to knock on his door."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'hard', 1, 'Mr Cobb knows that Layla feels sorry.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Layla made a card but is too scared to give it to him, so Mr Cobb doesn''t know she is sorry. Only the reader knows.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Sides of the Fence","text":"Mr Cobb: Every week, a cricket ball lands in my vegetable patch. Last Tuesday one flattened my best tomato plant. I''ve kept the last three balls in my shed. Maybe that will teach those kids a lesson.\n\nLayla: Our backyard is tiny, so my brother and I have to play cricket right next to the fence. We''ve lost three balls this month! I made Mr Cobb a card to say sorry, but I''m too scared to knock on his door."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the main message (theme) of this poem?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"If you keep trying, you can succeed even when things are hard."},{"id":"b","text":"Seeds should only grow in soil."},{"id":"c","text":"The wind is always right."},{"id":"d","text":"Flowers only open in summer."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The seed was alone with no soil, but it kept pushing ''again and again'' until it flowered. The last line says ''The ones who try can grow up tall.''', NULL, '{"title":"The Seed","text":"A seed fell down in a crack of stone,\nNo soil, no friends, it was all alone.\nThe wind said, “Give up, you''ll never grow!”\nThe seed just whispered, “Wait and know.”\n\nIt drank the dew and it sipped the rain,\nIt pushed through the dark again and again,\nTill one spring morning, small and bright,\nA flower opened in the light.\n\nNow children stop along the way\nTo see the flower that grew that day,\nAnd learn from something just as small:\nThe ones who try can grow up tall."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_hard', 1, 'In the poem, which non-human things speak like people? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"the wind"},{"id":"b","text":"the seed"},{"id":"c","text":"the stone"},{"id":"d","text":"the dew"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'The wind says, ''Give up, you''ll never grow!'' and the seed whispers, ''Wait and know.'' Giving things human actions is called personification.', NULL, '{"title":"The Seed","text":"A seed fell down in a crack of stone,\nNo soil, no friends, it was all alone.\nThe wind said, “Give up, you''ll never grow!”\nThe seed just whispered, “Wait and know.”\n\nIt drank the dew and it sipped the rain,\nIt pushed through the dark again and again,\nTill one spring morning, small and bright,\nA flower opened in the light.\n\nNow children stop along the way\nTo see the flower that grew that day,\nAnd learn from something just as small:\nThe ones who try can grow up tall."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000004150230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000041502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these events from the poem in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"A seed fell into a crack of stone."},{"id":"b","text":"The wind told the seed to give up."},{"id":"c","text":"The seed pushed through the dark again and again."},{"id":"d","text":"A flower opened one spring morning."},{"id":"e","text":"Children stopped to see the flower."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'The first stanza is the seed landing and the wind''s words, the second is the struggle and the flower, and the third is the children.', NULL, '{"title":"The Seed","text":"A seed fell down in a crack of stone,\nNo soil, no friends, it was all alone.\nThe wind said, “Give up, you''ll never grow!”\nThe seed just whispered, “Wait and know.”\n\nIt drank the dew and it sipped the rain,\nIt pushed through the dark again and again,\nTill one spring morning, small and bright,\nA flower opened in the light.\n\nNow children stop along the way\nTo see the flower that grew that day,\nAnd learn from something just as small:\nThe ones who try can grow up tall."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"becaus"},{"id":"b","text":"because"},{"id":"c","text":"becose"},{"id":"d","text":"beacuse"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Because'' is spelt b-e-c-a-u-s-e. A trick to remember it: Big Elephants Can Always Understand Small Elephants.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_easy', 1, 'The word ''knife'' has a silent k.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'We write the k in ''knife'' but we don''t say it. It starts with an ''n'' sound, just like ''knee'' and ''know''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Add the prefix un- to ''happy'' to make a new word: ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"unhappy"'::jsonb, 'A prefix goes on the front and the base word stays the same: un + happy = unhappy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'easy', 1, 'What is the plural of ''baby''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"babys"},{"id":"b","text":"babies"},{"id":"c","text":"babyes"},{"id":"d","text":"babyies"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'When a word ends in a consonant then y, change the y to i and add -es: baby becomes babies.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these words have a silent letter? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"thumb"},{"id":"b","text":"write"},{"id":"c","text":"plant"},{"id":"d","text":"listen"},{"id":"e","text":"bring"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'The b in ''thumb'', the w in ''write'' and the t in ''listen'' are silent. Every letter in ''plant'' and ''bring'' is sounded.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'easy', 1, 'Add the suffix -ful to ''hope''. Write the new word.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"hopeful"'::jsonb, 'The suffix -ful starts with a consonant, so we keep the e: hope + ful = hopeful.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'medium', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''From the hill we could see ___ house.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"there"},{"id":"b","text":"their"},{"id":"c","text":"they''re"},{"id":"d","text":"thier"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Their'' means belonging to them, like their house. ''There'' is a place and ''they''re'' is short for ''they are''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'medium', 1, 'Write the plural of ''leaf''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"leaves"'::jsonb, 'Many words ending in f change the f to v and add -es: leaf becomes leaves, just like wolf becomes wolves.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'medium', 1, 'Which is the correct spelling of ''make'' + -ing?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"makeing"},{"id":"b","text":"making"},{"id":"c","text":"makking"},{"id":"d","text":"makin"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'When a word ends in a silent e and the suffix starts with a vowel, drop the e: make + ing = making.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'hard', 1, 'The Greek root ''graph'' means write, and ''auto'' means self. Which word means a person''s name written by themselves?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"paragraph"},{"id":"b","text":"autograph"},{"id":"c","text":"photograph"},{"id":"d","text":"biography"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'auto (self) + graph (write) = autograph, a name you write yourself. A cricketer signs autographs for fans.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'hard', 1, 'The plural of ''potato'' is spelt ''potatos''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Some words ending in o add -es: potato becomes potatoes, and tomato becomes tomatoes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'hard', 1, 'Put these word parts in order to build the word ''unhelpfully''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"un"},{"id":"b","text":"help"},{"id":"c","text":"ful"},{"id":"d","text":"ly"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Start with the prefix un-, then the base word help, then the suffixes -ful and -ly: un + help + ful + ly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these words are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"necessary"},{"id":"b","text":"seperate"},{"id":"c","text":"definitely"},{"id":"d","text":"accomodate"},{"id":"e","text":"rhythm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, '''Necessary'' has one c and double s, ''definitely'' has the word ''finite'' inside it, and ''rhythm'' has no vowels except y. ''Separate'' has ''a rat'' in it, and ''accommodate'' has double c and double m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Add the suffix -ness to ''happy'' to make a noun. Write the new word.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"happiness"'::jsonb, '''Happy'' ends in a consonant then y, so change the y to i before adding the suffix: happy + ness = happiness.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why is ''hopping'' spelt with double p, but ''hoping'' with one p?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"''Hop'' has a short vowel and ends in one consonant, so the p doubles; ''hope'' ends in e, so the e is dropped"},{"id":"b","text":"Both words could be spelt either way"},{"id":"c","text":"Words about jumping always have a double letter"},{"id":"d","text":"''Hoping'' is short for ''hopeful''"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'hop (short o) + ing doubles the last consonant: hopping. hope (long o, ends in e) drops the e: hoping. The double p keeps the vowel short.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"frend"},{"id":"b","text":"freind"},{"id":"c","text":"friend"},{"id":"d","text":"frind"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, '''Friend'' is spelt f-r-i-e-n-d. A trick to remember it: you are my friend to the END.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_easy', 1, 'The word ''gnome'' has a silent g.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'We write the g in ''gnome'' but we start the word with an ''n'' sound, just like ''gnaw'' and ''gnat''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Add the prefix re- to ''build'' to make a word that means build again: ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"rebuild"'::jsonb, 'The prefix re- means again. It goes on the front and the base word stays the same: re + build = rebuild.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'easy', 1, 'What is the plural of ''church''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"churchs"},{"id":"b","text":"churches"},{"id":"c","text":"churchies"},{"id":"d","text":"churchez"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Words ending in ch, sh, s, x or z add -es so we can say the extra sound: church becomes churches.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'easy', 1, 'Which pairs are homophones (they sound the same but are spelt differently and mean different things)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"bare / bear"},{"id":"b","text":"flour / flower"},{"id":"c","text":"cat / cot"},{"id":"d","text":"week / weak"},{"id":"e","text":"ship / shop"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Bare and bear, flour and flower, and week and weak sound exactly the same. Cat and cot, and ship and shop, have different vowel sounds.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'easy', 1, 'Add the suffix -ment to ''enjoy''. Write the new word.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"enjoyment"'::jsonb, 'The suffix -ment starts with a consonant and ''enjoy'' has a vowel before the y, so nothing changes: enjoy + ment = enjoyment.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'medium', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''We ate ___ much cake at the party.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to"},{"id":"b","text":"too"},{"id":"c","text":"two"},{"id":"d","text":"tow"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Too'' with two o''s means more than enough, or also. ''To'' shows direction and ''two'' is the number 2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'medium', 1, 'Write the plural of ''half''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"halves"'::jsonb, 'Like many words ending in f, change the f to v and add -es: half becomes halves, just like calf becomes calves.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'medium', 1, 'Which is the correct spelling of ''swim'' + -ing?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"swiming"},{"id":"b","text":"swimming"},{"id":"c","text":"swimeing"},{"id":"d","text":"swimmming"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Swim'' has a short vowel and ends in one consonant, so double the m before -ing: swim + m + ing = swimming.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'hard', 1, 'The Greek root ''tele'' means far and ''scope'' means look. Which word names a tool for looking at things far away?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"telephone"},{"id":"b","text":"telescope"},{"id":"c","text":"microscope"},{"id":"d","text":"television"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'tele (far) + scope (look) = telescope, which lets you look at faraway stars and planets. A microscope looks at tiny things up close.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'hard', 1, 'The plural of ''piano'' is spelt ''pianoes''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Not every word ending in o adds -es. ''Piano'' just adds s: pianos, like photos and kangaroos.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'hard', 1, 'Put these word parts in order to build the word ''reappearance''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"re"},{"id":"b","text":"appear"},{"id":"c","text":"ance"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Start with the prefix re- (again), then the base word appear, then the suffix -ance: re + appear + ance.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these words are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"government"},{"id":"b","text":"Febuary"},{"id":"c","text":"library"},{"id":"d","text":"surprise"},{"id":"e","text":"probaly"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, '''Government'' has the n from ''govern'', ''library'' has two r''s and ''surprise'' starts with ''sur''. The correct spellings are ''February'' and ''probably''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Add the suffix -er to ''begin'' to name a person who is just starting. Write the new word.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"beginner"'::jsonb, 'Say be-GIN: the stress is on the last part, and it ends in one vowel and one consonant, so double the n: begin + n + er = beginner.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005110130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does ''enjoyed'' keep its y, but ''cried'' changes the y to i?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"''Enjoy'' has a vowel before the y, so the y stays; ''cry'' has a consonant before the y, so it changes to i"},{"id":"b","text":"''Enjoyed'' is a longer word, so it keeps all its letters"},{"id":"c","text":"Words about feelings always keep the y"},{"id":"d","text":"Both words could be spelt either way"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Look at the letter before the y. A vowel before y (enjoy, play, stay) keeps the y. A consonant before y (cry, try, carry) changes it to i: cried, tried, carried.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the same as ''enormous''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"tiny"},{"id":"b","text":"huge"},{"id":"c","text":"noisy"},{"id":"d","text":"heavy"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Enormous'' means very big, so the closest word is ''huge''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_easy', 1, '''Ancient'' means very old.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Something ancient comes from a very long time ago, like ancient rock art or ancient Egypt.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the opposite of ''generous''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"kind"},{"id":"b","text":"selfish"},{"id":"c","text":"giving"},{"id":"d","text":"rich"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A generous person shares freely. The opposite is someone who keeps things for themselves: selfish.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'easy', 1, '''The hikers were exhausted after climbing the mountain all day.'' What does ''exhausted'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"very tired"},{"id":"b","text":"very excited"},{"id":"c","text":"lost"},{"id":"d","text":"hungry"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'After climbing all day, they would be worn out. ''Exhausted'' means very tired.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'easy', 1, 'The prefix ''pre-'' means before. A preview is a look at something ___ it happens.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"before"'::jsonb, 'pre- means before, so a preview is a look before the real thing, like a movie trailer.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'easy', 1, 'Which words mean ''very happy''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"delighted"},{"id":"b","text":"miserable"},{"id":"c","text":"overjoyed"},{"id":"d","text":"thrilled"},{"id":"e","text":"furious"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Delighted, overjoyed and thrilled all mean very happy. Miserable means very sad and furious means very angry.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from least wet to most wet.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"damp"},{"id":"b","text":"wet"},{"id":"c","text":"drenched"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Damp is a little wet, wet is in the middle, and drenched means soaked right through.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'medium', 1, 'In science, what does ''evaporate'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"turn from a liquid into a gas"},{"id":"b","text":"turn from a gas into a liquid"},{"id":"c","text":"freeze into a solid"},{"id":"d","text":"sink to the bottom"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'When a puddle dries up in the sun, the water evaporates: it turns from a liquid into a gas called water vapour.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'medium', 1, 'In which sentence does ''bark'' mean the outer covering of a tree?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The dog''s bark woke the baby."},{"id":"b","text":"Koalas grip the rough bark of the gum tree."},{"id":"c","text":"I heard a fox bark in the night."},{"id":"d","text":"The coach began to bark orders at the team."}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Only the koala sentence is about a tree. In the others, ''bark'' is a sound or a sharp way of speaking.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'hard', 1, '''Bio'' means life and ''-logy'' means the study of. What is biology?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the study of living things"},{"id":"b","text":"the study of rocks"},{"id":"c","text":"the study of the stars"},{"id":"d","text":"a book about someone''s life"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Put the parts together: bio (life) + logy (study of) = the study of life, or living things.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'hard', 1, 'The suffix ''-less'' means without. Write one word that means ''without fear''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"fearless"'::jsonb, 'fear + less = fearless, which means without fear.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'hard', 1, 'Which word best describes how the tired soldier walked, slowly dragging his heavy boots through the mud?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"trudged"},{"id":"b","text":"skipped"},{"id":"c","text":"sprinted"},{"id":"d","text":"strolled"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Trudged'' means walked slowly and heavily because you are tired. Skipped and sprinted are fast, and strolled is relaxed.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_hard', 1, '''The council''s plan to cut down the old fig tree was controversial. Some residents cheered, while others wrote angry letters.'' What does ''controversial'' mean here?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"causing strong disagreement"},{"id":"b","text":"very expensive"},{"id":"c","text":"popular with everyone"},{"id":"d","text":"decided very quickly"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Some people cheered and others were angry, so people disagreed strongly. That is what ''controversial'' means.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_hard', 1, 'The Latin root ''port'' means carry. In which words does ''port'' mean carry? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"transport"},{"id":"b","text":"portable"},{"id":"c","text":"export"},{"id":"d","text":"porridge"},{"id":"e","text":"portrait"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Transport carries things across, portable means able to be carried, and export means to carry goods out of a country. Porridge and portrait just happen to share the letters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_hard', 1, '''Visible'' means able to be seen. Something that cannot be seen is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"invisible"'::jsonb, 'The prefix in- means not, so in + visible = invisible, not able to be seen.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the same as ''rapid''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"slow"},{"id":"b","text":"fast"},{"id":"c","text":"loud"},{"id":"d","text":"rough"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Rapid'' means very quick, like rapids in a river where the water rushes fast. The closest word is ''fast''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_easy', 1, '''Fragile'' means easily broken.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Boxes with glasses inside often say FRAGILE so people handle them gently, because they break easily.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the opposite of ''brave''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"fearless"},{"id":"b","text":"cowardly"},{"id":"c","text":"bold"},{"id":"d","text":"strong"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A brave person faces danger. The opposite is someone who runs away from danger: cowardly. Fearless and bold mean nearly the same as brave.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'easy', 1, '''The puppy was so curious that it sniffed every corner of the new house.'' What does ''curious'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"wanting to know or explore"},{"id":"b","text":"very sleepy"},{"id":"c","text":"scared"},{"id":"d","text":"naughty"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The puppy sniffed every corner because it wanted to find out about the new house. Curious means wanting to know or explore.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'easy', 1, 'The prefix ''re-'' means again. To reread a book is to read it ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"again"'::jsonb, 're- means again, so to reread is to read again, and to replay is to play again.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'easy', 1, 'Which words mean ''very small''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"tiny"},{"id":"b","text":"minute"},{"id":"c","text":"gigantic"},{"id":"d","text":"miniature"},{"id":"e","text":"massive"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Tiny, minute (said my-NEWT) and miniature all mean very small. Gigantic and massive mean very big.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from least hot to most hot.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"warm"},{"id":"b","text":"hot"},{"id":"c","text":"scorching"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Warm is a little hot, hot is in the middle, and scorching is so hot it could burn, like a 45 degree day in Bourke.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'medium', 1, 'In science, what is a ''predator''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"an animal that hunts and eats other animals"},{"id":"b","text":"an animal that is hunted by others"},{"id":"c","text":"a plant that grows in the shade"},{"id":"d","text":"an animal that sleeps all winter"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A predator hunts other animals for food, like a wedge-tailed eagle hunting a rabbit. The animal being hunted is called the prey.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'medium', 1, 'In which sentence does ''bat'' mean a flying animal?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Pass me the cricket bat."},{"id":"b","text":"A bat hung upside down in the cave."},{"id":"c","text":"It''s your turn to bat."},{"id":"d","text":"She didn''t bat an eyelid when the alarm went off."}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Only the cave sentence is about an animal hanging upside down. The others are about cricket or a saying that means not reacting.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'hard', 1, '''Geo'' means earth and ''-graphy'' means writing about or describing. What is geography?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the study of the earth, its places and its people"},{"id":"b","text":"the study of numbers"},{"id":"c","text":"writing the story of your own life"},{"id":"d","text":"the study of dinosaur bones"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Put the parts together: geo (earth) + graphy (describing) = describing the earth. In geography you learn about places, landforms and people.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'hard', 1, 'The suffix ''-ful'' means full of. Write one word that means ''full of colour''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["colourful","colorful"]'::jsonb, 'colour + ful = colourful. In Australia we spell ''colour'' with ou, and the suffix -ful has only one l.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'hard', 1, 'Which word best describes how the mouse moved, rushing along with quick, tiny steps so the cat wouldn''t hear?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"scurried"},{"id":"b","text":"stomped"},{"id":"c","text":"marched"},{"id":"d","text":"waddled"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Scurried'' means moved quickly with short, light steps. Stomped and marched are loud and heavy, and waddled is slow and wobbly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_hard', 1, '''The two classes reached a compromise: each would use the oval for half of lunchtime.'' What does ''compromise'' mean here?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"an agreement where each side gives up a little"},{"id":"b","text":"a fight that nobody wins"},{"id":"c","text":"a competition to decide a winner"},{"id":"d","text":"a promise never to share"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Neither class gets the whole oval, but both get some time. When each side gives up a little to agree, that is a compromise.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_hard', 1, 'The Latin root ''aqua'' means water. In which words does it carry that meaning? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"aquarium"},{"id":"b","text":"aquatic"},{"id":"c","text":"aqueduct"},{"id":"d","text":"acquire"},{"id":"e","text":"acrobat"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'An aquarium is a water tank, aquatic means living in water, and an aqueduct carries water. Acquire (to get) and acrobat are not about water.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005120130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051201', 'very_hard', 1, '''Possible'' means able to happen. Something that cannot happen is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"impossible"'::jsonb, 'The prefix in- (meaning not) changes to im- before a p, so it is easier to say: im + possible = impossible.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_easy', 1, 'What did the wind blow away?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Grandad''s hat"},{"id":"b","text":"Mia''s shoe"},{"id":"c","text":"the scoreboard"},{"id":"d","text":"a cricket ball"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first paragraph says a gust of wind snatched the hat off Grandad''s head.', NULL, '{"title":"The Lost Hat","text":"Mia''s grandfather had worn the same faded green hat to every cricket match for forty years. So when a gust of wind snatched it off his head at the Bathurst oval, Mia did not stop to think. She leapt over the fence and raced across the grass.\n\nThe hat tumbled past the boundary rope, past the scoreboard and towards the creek. Mia dived. Her fingers closed around the brim just before it reached the water.\n\nWhen she trotted back, muddy from knee to elbow, the whole crowd clapped. Grandad put the hat back on his head and grinned. \"Best catch of the day,\" he said."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Mia caught the hat before it reached the water.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The text says her fingers closed around the brim ''just before it reached the water''.', NULL, '{"title":"The Lost Hat","text":"Mia''s grandfather had worn the same faded green hat to every cricket match for forty years. So when a gust of wind snatched it off his head at the Bathurst oval, Mia did not stop to think. She leapt over the fence and raced across the grass.\n\nThe hat tumbled past the boundary rope, past the scoreboard and towards the creek. Mia dived. Her fingers closed around the brim just before it reached the water.\n\nWhen she trotted back, muddy from knee to elbow, the whole crowd clapped. Grandad put the hat back on his head and grinned. \"Best catch of the day,\" he said."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is a baby echidna called?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"puggle"'::jsonb, 'The last sentence says the tiny baby is called a puggle.', NULL, '{"title":"Echidnas","text":"The echidna is one of only a few mammals in the world that lay eggs. It is found all over Australia, from snowy mountains to hot deserts.\n\nAn echidna is covered in sharp spines, which protect it from predators. When it is frightened, it curls into a ball or digs quickly into the soil until only its spines show.\n\nEchidnas have no teeth. Instead, they use a long, sticky tongue to catch ants and termites. A female lays a single soft egg and keeps it in a pouch on her belly. After about ten days, a tiny baby called a puggle hatches."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'easy', 1, 'Why did the crowd clap?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mia had saved the hat with a great dive"},{"id":"b","text":"a batter hit a six"},{"id":"c","text":"Grandad won the match"},{"id":"d","text":"the rain had stopped"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The crowd clapped when Mia came back muddy with the hat. Grandad calls it the ''best catch of the day'', so they were cheering her dive.', NULL, '{"title":"The Lost Hat","text":"Mia''s grandfather had worn the same faded green hat to every cricket match for forty years. So when a gust of wind snatched it off his head at the Bathurst oval, Mia did not stop to think. She leapt over the fence and raced across the grass.\n\nThe hat tumbled past the boundary rope, past the scoreboard and towards the creek. Mia dived. Her fingers closed around the brim just before it reached the water.\n\nWhen she trotted back, muddy from knee to elbow, the whole crowd clapped. Grandad put the hat back on his head and grinned. \"Best catch of the day,\" he said."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'easy', 1, 'What is the main idea of the second paragraph?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"how echidnas protect themselves"},{"id":"b","text":"what echidnas eat"},{"id":"c","text":"where echidnas live"},{"id":"d","text":"how baby echidnas hatch"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph is all about spines, curling into a ball and digging in: ways echidnas stay safe from predators.', NULL, '{"title":"Echidnas","text":"The echidna is one of only a few mammals in the world that lay eggs. It is found all over Australia, from snowy mountains to hot deserts.\n\nAn echidna is covered in sharp spines, which protect it from predators. When it is frightened, it curls into a ball or digs quickly into the soil until only its spines show.\n\nEchidnas have no teeth. Instead, they use a long, sticky tongue to catch ants and termites. A female lays a single soft egg and keeps it in a pouch on her belly. After about ten days, a tiny baby called a puggle hatches."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'easy', 1, 'What is Ava''s main purpose in writing this letter?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to persuade the council to build a crossing"},{"id":"b","text":"to tell a funny story about her brother"},{"id":"c","text":"to explain how to cross a road"},{"id":"d","text":"to describe Seaview Public School"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Ava gives reasons and ends with ''I urge the council to act''. She wants to persuade the council to put in a crossing.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter to the Editor","text":"Dear Editor,\n\nEvery afternoon, hundreds of children cross Pacific Street to get to Seaview Public School. There is no pedestrian crossing, and cars often speed down the hill.\n\nLast week my younger brother had to jump back onto the footpath when a ute came around the corner too fast. He was lucky. Next time, someone might not be.\n\nA crossing with a lollipop person would cost far less than a hospital visit. Parents, teachers and students all agree it is needed. I urge the council to act before the end of Term 4, not after an accident.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAva Nguyen, Year 5"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'medium', 1, 'Which facts are stated in the text? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Echidnas lay eggs."},{"id":"b","text":"Echidnas eat ants and termites."},{"id":"c","text":"Echidnas live only in deserts."},{"id":"d","text":"Echidnas have sharp teeth."},{"id":"e","text":"A puggle hatches after about ten days."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, 'The text says echidnas lay eggs, eat ants and termites, and a puggle hatches after about ten days. It says they live in mountains and deserts, and that they have no teeth.', NULL, '{"title":"Echidnas","text":"The echidna is one of only a few mammals in the world that lay eggs. It is found all over Australia, from snowy mountains to hot deserts.\n\nAn echidna is covered in sharp spines, which protect it from predators. When it is frightened, it curls into a ball or digs quickly into the soil until only its spines show.\n\nEchidnas have no teeth. Instead, they use a long, sticky tongue to catch ants and termites. A female lays a single soft egg and keeps it in a pouch on her belly. After about ten days, a tiny baby called a puggle hatches."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'medium', 1, 'Why does Ava include the story about her younger brother?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to show that the danger is real"},{"id":"b","text":"to show that her brother is a fast runner"},{"id":"c","text":"to complain about utes"},{"id":"d","text":"to fill up space in the letter"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A true story about a near miss proves that speeding cars really are a danger, which makes her argument stronger.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter to the Editor","text":"Dear Editor,\n\nEvery afternoon, hundreds of children cross Pacific Street to get to Seaview Public School. There is no pedestrian crossing, and cars often speed down the hill.\n\nLast week my younger brother had to jump back onto the footpath when a ute came around the corner too fast. He was lucky. Next time, someone might not be.\n\nA crossing with a lollipop person would cost far less than a hospital visit. Parents, teachers and students all agree it is needed. I urge the council to act before the end of Term 4, not after an accident.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAva Nguyen, Year 5"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'medium', 1, 'How does Jayden feel at the start of the story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"worried"},{"id":"b","text":"excited"},{"id":"c","text":"bored"},{"id":"d","text":"angry"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'He had been ''dreading'' the carnival and said nothing while others talked about it. Dreading means feeling worried about something coming up.', NULL, '{"title":"The Swimming Carnival","text":"Jayden had been dreading the swimming carnival for weeks. While the other kids talked about ribbons and races, he stared at his lunchbox and said nothing.\n\nOn the day, he sat at the very back of the stand with his towel around his shoulders, even though the sun was blazing. When his name was called for the 50 metre freestyle, his stomach dropped.\n\nHe stood on the block. The water looked a long way down. Then he spotted Ms Patel at the end of the lane, giving him a thumbs-up.\n\nJayden came last, a whole lap behind. But when he climbed out, his friend Leo was waiting with a high five. \"You finished,\" Leo said. \"Last year you didn''t even get in.\"\n\nJayden looked at the water, then at Leo, and for the first time all day he smiled."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'hard', 1, '''He was lucky. Next time, someone might not be.'' What effect do these short sentences have?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"They make the warning feel serious and a little scary."},{"id":"b","text":"They make the letter sound funny."},{"id":"c","text":"They explain how the crossing would work."},{"id":"d","text":"They show that Ava is unsure what happened."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Short sentences make the reader stop and think. Saying someone ''might not be'' lucky next time hints that a child could be hurt.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter to the Editor","text":"Dear Editor,\n\nEvery afternoon, hundreds of children cross Pacific Street to get to Seaview Public School. There is no pedestrian crossing, and cars often speed down the hill.\n\nLast week my younger brother had to jump back onto the footpath when a ute came around the corner too fast. He was lucky. Next time, someone might not be.\n\nA crossing with a lollipop person would cost far less than a hospital visit. Parents, teachers and students all agree it is needed. I urge the council to act before the end of Term 4, not after an accident.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAva Nguyen, Year 5"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'hard', 1, 'Jayden kept his towel around his shoulders ''even though the sun was blazing''. What does this suggest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He was hiding because he felt nervous, not because he was cold."},{"id":"b","text":"He had forgotten to bring sunscreen."},{"id":"c","text":"The pool water was freezing."},{"id":"d","text":"He wanted to dry his hair."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It was hot, so he didn''t need the towel for warmth. Wrapping himself up at the back of the stand shows he wanted to hide.', NULL, '{"title":"The Swimming Carnival","text":"Jayden had been dreading the swimming carnival for weeks. While the other kids talked about ribbons and races, he stared at his lunchbox and said nothing.\n\nOn the day, he sat at the very back of the stand with his towel around his shoulders, even though the sun was blazing. When his name was called for the 50 metre freestyle, his stomach dropped.\n\nHe stood on the block. The water looked a long way down. Then he spotted Ms Patel at the end of the lane, giving him a thumbs-up.\n\nJayden came last, a whole lap behind. But when he climbed out, his friend Leo was waiting with a high five. \"You finished,\" Leo said. \"Last year you didn''t even get in.\"\n\nJayden looked at the water, then at Leo, and for the first time all day he smiled."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'hard', 1, 'Put these stages in the order they happen in the text.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Water evaporates."},{"id":"b","text":"Water vapour condenses into clouds."},{"id":"c","text":"Rain, hail or snow falls."},{"id":"d","text":"Water flows back to the ocean."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The signal words help: First the water evaporates, then it cools and condenses, then precipitation falls, and Finally it flows back to the sea.', NULL, '{"title":"The Water Cycle","text":"Water on Earth is always moving in a giant loop called the water cycle.\n\nFirst, the sun heats water in oceans, rivers and lakes. The water evaporates, which means it turns into an invisible gas called water vapour and rises into the air.\n\nAs the water vapour rises, it cools. The cooling makes it condense into tiny droplets, and millions of these droplets gather together to form clouds.\n\nWhen the droplets join and become too heavy, they fall as rain, hail or snow. This is called precipitation.\n\nFinally, the water flows across the land and into creeks, rivers and the ocean, where the sun warms it again. The cycle never stops, so the water you drink today might once have fallen on a dinosaur."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does Jayden smile at the end, even though he came last?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He realises that just finishing the race was a big step for him."},{"id":"b","text":"He thinks he will get a ribbon."},{"id":"c","text":"He is glad the carnival is over."},{"id":"d","text":"He thinks Leo is teasing him."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Leo reminds him that last year he ''didn''t even get in''. Jayden sees that finishing was a real achievement for him.', NULL, '{"title":"The Swimming Carnival","text":"Jayden had been dreading the swimming carnival for weeks. While the other kids talked about ribbons and races, he stared at his lunchbox and said nothing.\n\nOn the day, he sat at the very back of the stand with his towel around his shoulders, even though the sun was blazing. When his name was called for the 50 metre freestyle, his stomach dropped.\n\nHe stood on the block. The water looked a long way down. Then he spotted Ms Patel at the end of the lane, giving him a thumbs-up.\n\nJayden came last, a whole lap behind. But when he climbed out, his friend Leo was waiting with a high five. \"You finished,\" Leo said. \"Last year you didn''t even get in.\"\n\nJayden looked at the water, then at Leo, and for the first time all day he smiled."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_hard', 1, 'How is this text mainly organised?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"as a sequence of stages in a cycle"},{"id":"b","text":"as a problem and its solution"},{"id":"c","text":"as a comparison of two things"},{"id":"d","text":"as a list of opinions"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Words like First, As, When and Finally take us through each stage in order, and the last stage leads back to the first.', NULL, '{"title":"The Water Cycle","text":"Water on Earth is always moving in a giant loop called the water cycle.\n\nFirst, the sun heats water in oceans, rivers and lakes. The water evaporates, which means it turns into an invisible gas called water vapour and rises into the air.\n\nAs the water vapour rises, it cools. The cooling makes it condense into tiny droplets, and millions of these droplets gather together to form clouds.\n\nWhen the droplets join and become too heavy, they fall as rain, hail or snow. This is called precipitation.\n\nFinally, the water flows across the land and into creeks, rivers and the ocean, where the sun warms it again. The cycle never stops, so the water you drink today might once have fallen on a dinosaur."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does the author end with the idea that your drinking water might once have fallen on a dinosaur?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to show in an interesting way that the same water has been recycled for millions of years"},{"id":"b","text":"to prove that dinosaurs drank rainwater"},{"id":"c","text":"to warn readers not to drink tap water"},{"id":"d","text":"to start a new story about dinosaurs"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The cycle ''never stops'', so water is used again and again. The dinosaur idea is a surprising way to make readers picture how long that has been going on.', NULL, '{"title":"The Water Cycle","text":"Water on Earth is always moving in a giant loop called the water cycle.\n\nFirst, the sun heats water in oceans, rivers and lakes. The water evaporates, which means it turns into an invisible gas called water vapour and rises into the air.\n\nAs the water vapour rises, it cools. The cooling makes it condense into tiny droplets, and millions of these droplets gather together to form clouds.\n\nWhen the droplets join and become too heavy, they fall as rain, hail or snow. This is called precipitation.\n\nFinally, the water flows across the land and into creeks, rivers and the ocean, where the sun warms it again. The cycle never stops, so the water you drink today might once have fallen on a dinosaur."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Where did Sam find Ruby?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"at the animal nursery"},{"id":"b","text":"at the dagwood dog stall"},{"id":"c","text":"on a ride"},{"id":"d","text":"at a showbag stall"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The fifth paragraph says he ran to the animal nursery, and Ruby was sitting in the straw with a lamb.', NULL, '{"title":"Lost at the Show","text":"The Royal Easter Show was so crowded that Sam could barely see the rides over people''s heads. Mum had given him one job: hold Ruby''s hand and don''t let go.\n\nHe let go for three seconds, just long enough to pay for a dagwood dog. When he turned around, his little sister had vanished.\n\nSam''s heart thudded. He pushed past prams and showbag stalls, calling her name. Every small girl in a yellow raincoat made him spin around, but none of them was Ruby.\n\nThen he remembered how she had talked about the baby animals all week. He ran to the animal nursery. There, sitting in the straw, was Ruby, with a lamb asleep across her lap.\n\n\"Sam!\" she beamed. \"His name is Biscuit.\"\n\nSam sat down beside her and decided the dagwood dog could wait."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Lyrebirds can copy the calls of other birds.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says a male lyrebird can copy more than twenty other birds, such as kookaburras and whipbirds.', NULL, '{"title":"The Superb Lyrebird","text":"The superb lyrebird lives in the damp forests of south-eastern Australia, including the Blue Mountains and Royal National Park. It is famous for being one of the best copycats in the animal world.\n\nA male lyrebird can copy the calls of more than twenty other birds, such as kookaburras, whipbirds and currawongs. Some lyrebirds that live near people have even copied car alarms, camera shutters and chainsaws.\n\nThe male uses these sounds to attract a female. In winter, he builds a small mound of earth on the forest floor. He stands on it, spreads his long, lacy tail feathers over his head and sings his mixed-up song.\n\nLyrebirds also help the forest. As they scratch for worms and insects, they turn over huge amounts of fallen leaves, which helps them break down into rich soil."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_easy', 1, 'In step 4, what do you cover the worms with?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["damp newspaper","newspaper","wet newspaper","a sheet of damp newspaper"]'::jsonb, 'Step 4 says to cover the worms with the damp newspaper to keep them dark and moist.', NULL, '{"title":"How to Set Up a Worm Farm","text":"Goal: to turn food scraps into rich compost for the garden.\n\nYou will need:\n- a worm farm with stacking trays and a lid\n- a bucket of coconut fibre bedding\n- 1000 compost worms\n- a sheet of damp newspaper\n\nSteps:\n1. Place the worm farm in a shady spot, out of the hot afternoon sun.\n2. Soak the coconut fibre in water until it is damp, then spread it in the top tray.\n3. Gently tip the worms onto the bedding.\n4. Cover them with the damp newspaper to keep them dark and moist.\n5. Wait one week, then add small amounts of fruit and vegetable scraps.\n\nTip: Do not feed your worms onion, citrus or meat. Worms dislike these foods, and meat will attract flies."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'easy', 1, 'Why did Sam let go of Ruby''s hand?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to pay for a dagwood dog"},{"id":"b","text":"to go on a ride"},{"id":"c","text":"to pat a lamb"},{"id":"d","text":"to buy a showbag"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says he let go for three seconds, ''just long enough to pay for a dagwood dog''.', NULL, '{"title":"Lost at the Show","text":"The Royal Easter Show was so crowded that Sam could barely see the rides over people''s heads. Mum had given him one job: hold Ruby''s hand and don''t let go.\n\nHe let go for three seconds, just long enough to pay for a dagwood dog. When he turned around, his little sister had vanished.\n\nSam''s heart thudded. He pushed past prams and showbag stalls, calling her name. Every small girl in a yellow raincoat made him spin around, but none of them was Ruby.\n\nThen he remembered how she had talked about the baby animals all week. He ran to the animal nursery. There, sitting in the straw, was Ruby, with a lamb asleep across her lap.\n\n\"Sam!\" she beamed. \"His name is Biscuit.\"\n\nSam sat down beside her and decided the dagwood dog could wait."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'easy', 1, 'What is the main idea of the third paragraph?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"how a male lyrebird attracts a female"},{"id":"b","text":"where lyrebirds live"},{"id":"c","text":"what lyrebirds eat"},{"id":"d","text":"how lyrebirds help the forest"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The third paragraph begins ''The male uses these sounds to attract a female'' and then describes his mound, tail and song.', NULL, '{"title":"The Superb Lyrebird","text":"The superb lyrebird lives in the damp forests of south-eastern Australia, including the Blue Mountains and Royal National Park. It is famous for being one of the best copycats in the animal world.\n\nA male lyrebird can copy the calls of more than twenty other birds, such as kookaburras, whipbirds and currawongs. Some lyrebirds that live near people have even copied car alarms, camera shutters and chainsaws.\n\nThe male uses these sounds to attract a female. In winter, he builds a small mound of earth on the forest floor. He stands on it, spreads his long, lacy tail feathers over his head and sings his mixed-up song.\n\nLyrebirds also help the forest. As they scratch for worms and insects, they turn over huge amounts of fallen leaves, which helps them break down into rich soil."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'easy', 1, 'What is Oliver''s main purpose in writing this letter?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to persuade the principal to put a buddy bench in the playground"},{"id":"b","text":"to complain about a new girl in his class"},{"id":"c","text":"to explain how to run a cake stall"},{"id":"d","text":"to thank Mr Tran for painting"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Oliver says ''I am writing to ask if our school could put a buddy bench in the playground'', then gives reasons. He wants to persuade Mrs Rossi.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter to the Principal","text":"Dear Mrs Rossi,\n\nI am writing to ask if our school could put a buddy bench in the playground.\n\nA buddy bench is a seat where students can sit if they feel lonely or have no one to play with. When other students see someone on the bench, they go over and invite them to join in.\n\nLast term, a new girl in my class spent every lunchtime walking along the fence by herself. Nobody noticed until she told our teacher she wanted to change schools. A buddy bench could have helped her much sooner.\n\nThe bench would not cost much. Our class could raise the money with a cake stall, and Mr Tran has offered to paint it.\n\nPlease think about it. No one at Hillview should ever feel invisible.\n\nYours sincerely,\nOliver Burke, Year 5"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'medium', 1, 'Which facts are stated in the text? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Lyrebirds live in damp forests."},{"id":"b","text":"Some lyrebirds have copied car alarms."},{"id":"c","text":"Female lyrebirds build mounds of earth."},{"id":"d","text":"Lyrebirds eat mainly seeds and fruit."},{"id":"e","text":"Lyrebirds help fallen leaves turn into soil."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, 'The text says lyrebirds live in damp forests, have copied car alarms and help leaves break down into soil. It is the male that builds the mound, and they eat worms and insects.', NULL, '{"title":"The Superb Lyrebird","text":"The superb lyrebird lives in the damp forests of south-eastern Australia, including the Blue Mountains and Royal National Park. It is famous for being one of the best copycats in the animal world.\n\nA male lyrebird can copy the calls of more than twenty other birds, such as kookaburras, whipbirds and currawongs. Some lyrebirds that live near people have even copied car alarms, camera shutters and chainsaws.\n\nThe male uses these sounds to attract a female. In winter, he builds a small mound of earth on the forest floor. He stands on it, spreads his long, lacy tail feathers over his head and sings his mixed-up song.\n\nLyrebirds also help the forest. As they scratch for worms and insects, they turn over huge amounts of fallen leaves, which helps them break down into rich soil."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'medium', 1, 'Why does Oliver include the story about the new girl?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to show that a real student needed a buddy bench"},{"id":"b","text":"to tell the principal the girl''s name"},{"id":"c","text":"to show that the fence needs fixing"},{"id":"d","text":"to make the letter funny"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A true example of a lonely student who nearly left proves the problem is real. That makes his request stronger.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter to the Principal","text":"Dear Mrs Rossi,\n\nI am writing to ask if our school could put a buddy bench in the playground.\n\nA buddy bench is a seat where students can sit if they feel lonely or have no one to play with. When other students see someone on the bench, they go over and invite them to join in.\n\nLast term, a new girl in my class spent every lunchtime walking along the fence by herself. Nobody noticed until she told our teacher she wanted to change schools. A buddy bench could have helped her much sooner.\n\nThe bench would not cost much. Our class could raise the money with a cake stall, and Mr Tran has offered to paint it.\n\nPlease think about it. No one at Hillview should ever feel invisible.\n\nYours sincerely,\nOliver Burke, Year 5"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'medium', 1, 'Why did Hamish say no when Pop offered to help at the start?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He wanted to prove he could do it on his own."},{"id":"b","text":"He didn''t like his Pop."},{"id":"c","text":"Pop didn''t know how to make kites."},{"id":"d","text":"He had already bought a kite."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Hamish ''wanted to build the kite by himself'' and says he knows what he''s doing. He wanted to show he didn''t need help.', NULL, '{"title":"The Kite","text":"Hamish wanted to build the kite by himself. When Pop offered to help, Hamish shook his head. \"I watched a video,\" he said. \"I know what I''m doing.\"\n\nHe taped two sticks into a cross, stretched a garbage bag over them and tied on a long string. It looked perfect.\n\nAt Bondi Beach, the wind was strong and steady. Hamish ran along the sand, and the kite leapt into the air, wobbled, spun twice and smashed nose-first into the sand. One stick had snapped clean in half.\n\nHamish kicked the sand. Pop didn''t say anything. He just sat down, pulled a roll of tape from his pocket and held out his hand for the broken stick.\n\nThis time, Hamish passed it to him.\n\nBy the afternoon, a patched-up kite with a tail made from Pop''s old tie was dancing high above the waves."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'hard', 1, '''Every small girl in a yellow raincoat made him spin around.'' What can you work out from this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Ruby was wearing a yellow raincoat."},{"id":"b","text":"Sam was feeling dizzy from the rides."},{"id":"c","text":"Yellow raincoats were for sale at the show."},{"id":"d","text":"Sam was scared of girls in raincoats."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Sam is searching for Ruby, so he spins around whenever he sees someone who looks like her. That tells us Ruby had a yellow raincoat on.', NULL, '{"title":"Lost at the Show","text":"The Royal Easter Show was so crowded that Sam could barely see the rides over people''s heads. Mum had given him one job: hold Ruby''s hand and don''t let go.\n\nHe let go for three seconds, just long enough to pay for a dagwood dog. When he turned around, his little sister had vanished.\n\nSam''s heart thudded. He pushed past prams and showbag stalls, calling her name. Every small girl in a yellow raincoat made him spin around, but none of them was Ruby.\n\nThen he remembered how she had talked about the baby animals all week. He ran to the animal nursery. There, sitting in the straw, was Ruby, with a lamb asleep across her lap.\n\n\"Sam!\" she beamed. \"His name is Biscuit.\"\n\nSam sat down beside her and decided the dagwood dog could wait."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'hard', 1, '''This time, Hamish passed it to him.'' What does this sentence show?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Hamish is now ready to accept Pop''s help."},{"id":"b","text":"Hamish wants Pop to throw the stick away."},{"id":"c","text":"Hamish has given up on kites for good."},{"id":"d","text":"Pop has told Hamish off."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''This time'' reminds us that before, Hamish refused help. Handing over the broken stick shows he has changed his mind and will let Pop help.', NULL, '{"title":"The Kite","text":"Hamish wanted to build the kite by himself. When Pop offered to help, Hamish shook his head. \"I watched a video,\" he said. \"I know what I''m doing.\"\n\nHe taped two sticks into a cross, stretched a garbage bag over them and tied on a long string. It looked perfect.\n\nAt Bondi Beach, the wind was strong and steady. Hamish ran along the sand, and the kite leapt into the air, wobbled, spun twice and smashed nose-first into the sand. One stick had snapped clean in half.\n\nHamish kicked the sand. Pop didn''t say anything. He just sat down, pulled a roll of tape from his pocket and held out his hand for the broken stick.\n\nThis time, Hamish passed it to him.\n\nBy the afternoon, a patched-up kite with a tail made from Pop''s old tie was dancing high above the waves."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'hard', 1, 'Put these steps in the order the text gives them.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Put the worm farm in the shade."},{"id":"b","text":"Spread damp bedding in the top tray."},{"id":"c","text":"Tip the worms onto the bedding."},{"id":"d","text":"Cover the worms with damp newspaper."},{"id":"e","text":"Add small amounts of food scraps."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'The numbered steps 1 to 5 show the order: shade, bedding, worms, newspaper, then food after a week.', NULL, '{"title":"How to Set Up a Worm Farm","text":"Goal: to turn food scraps into rich compost for the garden.\n\nYou will need:\n- a worm farm with stacking trays and a lid\n- a bucket of coconut fibre bedding\n- 1000 compost worms\n- a sheet of damp newspaper\n\nSteps:\n1. Place the worm farm in a shady spot, out of the hot afternoon sun.\n2. Soak the coconut fibre in water until it is damp, then spread it in the top tray.\n3. Gently tip the worms onto the bedding.\n4. Cover them with the damp newspaper to keep them dark and moist.\n5. Wait one week, then add small amounts of fruit and vegetable scraps.\n\nTip: Do not feed your worms onion, citrus or meat. Worms dislike these foods, and meat will attract flies."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the main message of this story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Accepting help is not the same as failing, and working together can lead to success."},{"id":"b","text":"It is better to buy a kite than to make one."},{"id":"c","text":"Bondi Beach is too windy for kites."},{"id":"d","text":"You should never watch videos to learn things."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Hamish''s kite crashes when he works alone, but flies ''high above the waves'' once he lets Pop help. The story shows that accepting help can lead to success.', NULL, '{"title":"The Kite","text":"Hamish wanted to build the kite by himself. When Pop offered to help, Hamish shook his head. \"I watched a video,\" he said. \"I know what I''m doing.\"\n\nHe taped two sticks into a cross, stretched a garbage bag over them and tied on a long string. It looked perfect.\n\nAt Bondi Beach, the wind was strong and steady. Hamish ran along the sand, and the kite leapt into the air, wobbled, spun twice and smashed nose-first into the sand. One stick had snapped clean in half.\n\nHamish kicked the sand. Pop didn''t say anything. He just sat down, pulled a roll of tape from his pocket and held out his hand for the broken stick.\n\nThis time, Hamish passed it to him.\n\nBy the afternoon, a patched-up kite with a tail made from Pop''s old tie was dancing high above the waves."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Oliver ends with ''No one at Hillview should ever feel invisible.'' Why is this a strong ending?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It leaves the reader with a powerful feeling that links back to the lonely new girl."},{"id":"b","text":"It tells the principal how much the bench will cost."},{"id":"c","text":"It brings in a brand-new argument about painting."},{"id":"d","text":"It tells a joke to make the principal laugh."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The new girl walked alone and ''nobody noticed'', so she was invisible. Ending with this idea makes the principal feel how important the bench is.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter to the Principal","text":"Dear Mrs Rossi,\n\nI am writing to ask if our school could put a buddy bench in the playground.\n\nA buddy bench is a seat where students can sit if they feel lonely or have no one to play with. When other students see someone on the bench, they go over and invite them to join in.\n\nLast term, a new girl in my class spent every lunchtime walking along the fence by herself. Nobody noticed until she told our teacher she wanted to change schools. A buddy bench could have helped her much sooner.\n\nThe bench would not cost much. Our class could raise the money with a cake stall, and Mr Tran has offered to paint it.\n\nPlease think about it. No one at Hillview should ever feel invisible.\n\nYours sincerely,\nOliver Burke, Year 5"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051301', 'very_hard', 1, 'What type of text is this, and which features show it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a procedure, because it has a goal, a list of things you need and numbered steps"},{"id":"b","text":"a narrative, because it has characters and a problem"},{"id":"c","text":"a persuasive text, because it argues that everyone must buy worms"},{"id":"d","text":"an information report, because it describes every kind of worm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A procedure tells you how to do something. It has a goal, a materials list and numbered steps that start with command verbs like ''Place'', ''Soak'' and ''Wait''.', NULL, '{"title":"How to Set Up a Worm Farm","text":"Goal: to turn food scraps into rich compost for the garden.\n\nYou will need:\n- a worm farm with stacking trays and a lid\n- a bucket of coconut fibre bedding\n- 1000 compost worms\n- a sheet of damp newspaper\n\nSteps:\n1. Place the worm farm in a shady spot, out of the hot afternoon sun.\n2. Soak the coconut fibre in water until it is damp, then spread it in the top tray.\n3. Gently tip the worms onto the bedding.\n4. Cover them with the damp newspaper to keep them dark and moist.\n5. Wait one week, then add small amounts of fruit and vegetable scraps.\n\nTip: Do not feed your worms onion, citrus or meat. Worms dislike these foods, and meat will attract flies."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_easy', 1, 'In the poem, the sky ''growled across the hill''. What is the sky being made to sound like?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"an angry animal"},{"id":"b","text":"a happy child"},{"id":"c","text":"a quiet river"},{"id":"d","text":"a ringing bell"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Animals like dogs growl when they are angry, so the poet makes the stormy sky sound fierce and angry.', NULL, '{"title":"Storm Over the Paddock","text":"The sky pulled on its blackest coat\nand growled across the hill.\nThe gum trees bowed their heads in fear,\nthe magpies sat stock-still.\n\nThen lightning cracked its silver whip,\nthe rain came marching through,\nand drummed upon the iron roof\nuntil the world was new."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_easy', 1, '''Slim'' and ''skinny'' mean nearly the same thing, but ''slim'' usually sounds more positive.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Words can have the same meaning but a different feeling, called connotation. ''Slim'' sounds like a compliment; ''skinny'' can sound like too thin.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_easy', 1, '''Lanterns swung above us like tiny red moons.'' What kind of figurative language is this?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["simile","a simile"]'::jsonb, 'It compares the lanterns to moons using the word ''like'', so it is a simile.', NULL, '{"title":"The Night Market","text":"Mum gripped my hand as we squeezed into the night market. Lanterns swung above us like tiny red moons. Smoke curled from the satay stalls, and the air smelt of peanuts, chilli and burnt sugar.\n\nA man flipped noodles so high they almost touched the lanterns. Someone''s baby wailed. Somewhere, a drum thumped like a giant heartbeat.\n\nI bit into a hot dumpling and the juice burned my tongue, but I didn''t care. For the first time since we had moved to Cabramatta, the noise didn''t scare me. It felt like a party that had been waiting for us to arrive."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'easy', 1, 'Which phrases give the weather the actions of a person or animal? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The sky pulled on its blackest coat"},{"id":"b","text":"growled across the hill"},{"id":"c","text":"the rain came marching through"},{"id":"d","text":"upon the iron roof"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Pulling on a coat, growling and marching are things people or animals do. This is personification. ''Upon the iron roof'' just tells us where.', NULL, '{"title":"Storm Over the Paddock","text":"The sky pulled on its blackest coat\nand growled across the hill.\nThe gum trees bowed their heads in fear,\nthe magpies sat stock-still.\n\nThen lightning cracked its silver whip,\nthe rain came marching through,\nand drummed upon the iron roof\nuntil the world was new."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'easy', 1, 'What does the key stand for, or symbolise, for Nana?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"her memories of the farm and her old home"},{"id":"b","text":"a lost treasure chest"},{"id":"c","text":"a new house she will buy"},{"id":"d","text":"her sewing hobby"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Nana says she kept it ''so I''d never forget where I came from''. The key doesn''t open anything now; it stands for her memories of home.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Key","text":"Ellie found the key at the bottom of Nana''s sewing tin, wrapped in a faded hanky. It was heavy and brown with rust, and it did not fit any door in the house.\n\n\"That was the key to our farmhouse at Gundagai,\" Nana said softly, turning it over in her wrinkled hands. \"We had to sell the farm in the drought. I kept the key so I''d never forget where I came from.\"\n\nEllie watched Nana''s eyes go far away, as if she could see the paddocks and the windmill again.\n\nThat night, Ellie threaded the key onto a ribbon and hung it beside Nana''s bed. In the morning, Nana touched it and smiled."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'easy', 1, 'Put these parts of a narrative in the usual order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"orientation"},{"id":"b","text":"complication"},{"id":"c","text":"climax"},{"id":"d","text":"resolution"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A narrative introduces the characters and setting (orientation), then a problem appears (complication), builds to the most exciting point (climax), and is sorted out (resolution).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'medium', 1, 'What mood does the last line, ''until the world was new'', create?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"fresh and hopeful"},{"id":"b","text":"angry and frightening"},{"id":"c","text":"sad and lonely"},{"id":"d","text":"bored and sleepy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'After all the growling and cracking, the storm ends with the world feeling washed clean and ''new'', which is fresh and hopeful.', NULL, '{"title":"Storm Over the Paddock","text":"The sky pulled on its blackest coat\nand growled across the hill.\nThe gum trees bowed their heads in fear,\nthe magpies sat stock-still.\n\nThen lightning cracked its silver whip,\nthe rain came marching through,\nand drummed upon the iron roof\nuntil the world was new."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'medium', 1, '''The wind whispered through the trees'' gives the wind a human action. This technique is called ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"personification"'::jsonb, 'Whispering is something people do. Giving a non-human thing a human action is personification.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'medium', 1, '''Somewhere, a drum thumped like a giant heartbeat.'' What does this image suggest about the market?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It feels alive and full of energy."},{"id":"b","text":"It is quiet and empty."},{"id":"c","text":"Someone is sick."},{"id":"d","text":"The music is too soft to hear."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A heartbeat is the sign of something living. Comparing the drum to a giant heartbeat makes the whole market seem alive.', NULL, '{"title":"The Night Market","text":"Mum gripped my hand as we squeezed into the night market. Lanterns swung above us like tiny red moons. Smoke curled from the satay stalls, and the air smelt of peanuts, chilli and burnt sugar.\n\nA man flipped noodles so high they almost touched the lanterns. Someone''s baby wailed. Somewhere, a drum thumped like a giant heartbeat.\n\nI bit into a hot dumpling and the juice burned my tongue, but I didn''t care. For the first time since we had moved to Cabramatta, the noise didn''t scare me. It felt like a party that had been waiting for us to arrive."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'hard', 1, 'Ellie hangs the key on a ribbon beside Nana''s bed. What does this action show about Ellie?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She is caring and understands how much the key means to Nana."},{"id":"b","text":"She wants to keep the key for herself."},{"id":"c","text":"She thinks the key is worth a lot of money."},{"id":"d","text":"She is bored and looking for something to do."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Writers show character through actions. Ellie puts the key where Nana can see it every day, which shows she cares and understands.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Key","text":"Ellie found the key at the bottom of Nana''s sewing tin, wrapped in a faded hanky. It was heavy and brown with rust, and it did not fit any door in the house.\n\n\"That was the key to our farmhouse at Gundagai,\" Nana said softly, turning it over in her wrinkled hands. \"We had to sell the farm in the drought. I kept the key so I''d never forget where I came from.\"\n\nEllie watched Nana''s eyes go far away, as if she could see the paddocks and the windmill again.\n\nThat night, Ellie threaded the key onto a ribbon and hung it beside Nana''s bed. In the morning, Nana touched it and smiled."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'hard', 1, 'Which word gives the most positive feeling about someone who is careful with money?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"thrifty"},{"id":"b","text":"stingy"},{"id":"c","text":"mean"},{"id":"d","text":"tight-fisted"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'All four words describe someone who doesn''t spend much, but ''thrifty'' sounds sensible and good. The others sound unkind.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'hard', 1, 'A writer wants readers to feel that the bush at night is scary. Which words would help? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"looming"},{"id":"b","text":"gnarled"},{"id":"c","text":"shadowy"},{"id":"d","text":"sunny"},{"id":"e","text":"cheerful"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Looming, gnarled and shadowy all have dark, uneasy connotations. Sunny and cheerful would make the bush feel friendly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_hard', 1, 'How has the narrator changed by the end of the story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"At first the noise of the new place scared them, but now it feels welcoming."},{"id":"b","text":"They started off happy but end up scared."},{"id":"c","text":"They have learnt how to cook dumplings."},{"id":"d","text":"They want to move away from Cabramatta."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''For the first time'' the noise didn''t scare them, so it used to. Now it feels like a party ''waiting for us'', which shows they feel welcome.', NULL, '{"title":"The Night Market","text":"Mum gripped my hand as we squeezed into the night market. Lanterns swung above us like tiny red moons. Smoke curled from the satay stalls, and the air smelt of peanuts, chilli and burnt sugar.\n\nA man flipped noodles so high they almost touched the lanterns. Someone''s baby wailed. Somewhere, a drum thumped like a giant heartbeat.\n\nI bit into a hot dumpling and the juice burned my tongue, but I didn''t care. For the first time since we had moved to Cabramatta, the noise didn''t scare me. It felt like a party that had been waiting for us to arrive."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why might the writer have made the key rusty and not able to fit any door?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to show that the farm is gone, but Nana''s memories of it remain"},{"id":"b","text":"to show that Nana is careless with her things"},{"id":"c","text":"to make the story about a mystery door"},{"id":"d","text":"to show that keys are hard to look after"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The key can''t open anything now because the farm was sold long ago, yet Nana still treasures it. That makes it a symbol of the past living on in her memory.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Key","text":"Ellie found the key at the bottom of Nana''s sewing tin, wrapped in a faded hanky. It was heavy and brown with rust, and it did not fit any door in the house.\n\n\"That was the key to our farmhouse at Gundagai,\" Nana said softly, turning it over in her wrinkled hands. \"We had to sell the farm in the drought. I kept the key so I''d never forget where I came from.\"\n\nEllie watched Nana''s eyes go far away, as if she could see the paddocks and the windmill again.\n\nThat night, Ellie threaded the key onto a ribbon and hung it beside Nana''s bed. In the morning, Nana touched it and smiled."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_hard', 1, 'You are rewriting ''The Old Key'' from Nana''s point of view. Which opening sentence fits best?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"When my granddaughter found the old key in my sewing tin, I could almost smell the paddocks again."},{"id":"b","text":"Ellie found a key and gave it to her nana."},{"id":"c","text":"Once upon a time there was a magic key that opened every door."},{"id":"d","text":"Keys are made of metal and can rust if they get wet."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Nana''s point of view needs ''I'' and ''my'', and it should keep the same idea of the key bringing back memories of the farm.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Key","text":"Ellie found the key at the bottom of Nana''s sewing tin, wrapped in a faded hanky. It was heavy and brown with rust, and it did not fit any door in the house.\n\n\"That was the key to our farmhouse at Gundagai,\" Nana said softly, turning it over in her wrinkled hands. \"We had to sell the farm in the drought. I kept the key so I''d never forget where I came from.\"\n\nEllie watched Nana''s eyes go far away, as if she could see the paddocks and the windmill again.\n\nThat night, Ellie threaded the key onto a ribbon and hung it beside Nana''s bed. In the morning, Nana touched it and smiled."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_easy', 1, 'In the poem, the wind ''came prowling after dark''. What is the wind being made to seem like?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a hunting animal, like a cat"},{"id":"b","text":"a sleepy baby"},{"id":"c","text":"a gentle stream"},{"id":"d","text":"a ringing phone"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Cats and other hunters prowl, which means move around quietly looking for something. The poet makes the wind seem like a creature on the hunt.', NULL, '{"title":"The Wind at Night","text":"The wind came prowling after dark\nand rattled every door.\nIt sniffed along the window frames\nand moaned beneath the floor.\n\nIt kicked the wheelie bins about\nlike toys it didn''t need,\nthen curled up in the jacaranda\nas soft as thistle seed."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_easy', 1, '''Chuckle'' and ''cackle'' both describe laughing, but ''cackle'' usually sounds more unpleasant.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Words can mean nearly the same thing but carry different feelings, called connotation. A chuckle is soft and friendly; a cackle sounds harsh, like a witch.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_easy', 1, '''Mist hung over the lake like a grey blanket.'' What kind of figurative language is this?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["simile","a simile"]'::jsonb, 'It compares the mist to a blanket using the word ''like'', so it is a simile.', NULL, '{"title":"Dawn at Camp","text":"I hated the idea of camping. No wi-fi, no fridge, and a toilet that was really just a hole with a seat. On our first night at Myall Lakes I lay in my sleeping bag, listening to every rustle and creak, sure that something with teeth was circling the tent.\n\nAt dawn, Aunty Jo shook my foot. \"Come and see,\" she whispered.\n\nMist hung over the lake like a grey blanket. The water was so still it looked like glass, and a pelican slid across it without making a ripple. Somewhere a kookaburra started laughing, then another, until the whole bush seemed to be in on the joke.\n\nAunty Jo handed me a mug of hot chocolate. I wrapped my cold fingers around it and didn''t say a word. I didn''t want to break the spell."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'easy', 1, 'Which phrases give the wind the actions of a person or animal? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"It sniffed along the window frames"},{"id":"b","text":"and moaned beneath the floor"},{"id":"c","text":"It kicked the wheelie bins about"},{"id":"d","text":"in the jacaranda"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Sniffing, moaning and kicking are things people or animals do. This is personification. ''In the jacaranda'' just tells us where.', NULL, '{"title":"The Wind at Night","text":"The wind came prowling after dark\nand rattled every door.\nIt sniffed along the window frames\nand moaned beneath the floor.\n\nIt kicked the wheelie bins about\nlike toys it didn''t need,\nthen curled up in the jacaranda\nas soft as thistle seed."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'easy', 1, 'Why did Zara take a small branch from the lemon tree?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to grow a new lemon tree at the new house"},{"id":"b","text":"to make lemonade for the trip"},{"id":"c","text":"to show her teacher"},{"id":"d","text":"because Dad told her to"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'She tells Dad it is ''For the new house'', and she keeps the cut end in wet paper towel so it stays alive for planting.', NULL, '{"title":"The Lemon Tree","text":"Dad planted the lemon tree on the day Zara was born. Every birthday, she stood beside it for a photo, and every year it was a little taller than the year before.\n\nNow the house was sold. Boxes filled the hallway, and the empty rooms echoed when anyone spoke.\n\nOn the last morning, Zara walked out to the backyard with Mum''s garden scissors. She snipped a small branch with three leaves and a tiny green bud, wrapped the end in wet paper towel and tucked it into her lunchbox.\n\n\"What''s that for?\" asked Dad.\n\n\"For the new house,\" Zara said.\n\nDad was quiet for a moment. Then he went to the shed and came back with a small pot."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'easy', 1, 'Put these events from the story in the order they happened.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Dad planted the lemon tree."},{"id":"b","text":"Zara had a photo beside the tree each birthday."},{"id":"c","text":"The house was sold."},{"id":"d","text":"Zara snipped a branch from the tree."},{"id":"e","text":"Dad came back from the shed with a pot."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'The tree was planted when Zara was born, photos were taken every birthday, then the house was sold, Zara took a cutting, and Dad fetched a pot.', NULL, '{"title":"The Lemon Tree","text":"Dad planted the lemon tree on the day Zara was born. Every birthday, she stood beside it for a photo, and every year it was a little taller than the year before.\n\nNow the house was sold. Boxes filled the hallway, and the empty rooms echoed when anyone spoke.\n\nOn the last morning, Zara walked out to the backyard with Mum''s garden scissors. She snipped a small branch with three leaves and a tiny green bud, wrapped the end in wet paper towel and tucked it into her lunchbox.\n\n\"What''s that for?\" asked Dad.\n\n\"For the new house,\" Zara said.\n\nDad was quiet for a moment. Then he went to the shed and came back with a small pot."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'medium', 1, 'How does the mood change in the last two lines of the poem?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"from wild and noisy to calm and gentle"},{"id":"b","text":"from calm to angry"},{"id":"c","text":"from happy to frightening"},{"id":"d","text":"It does not change at all."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The wind rattles, moans and kicks, but then it ''curled up'' and became ''as soft as thistle seed''. The mood turns calm and gentle.', NULL, '{"title":"The Wind at Night","text":"The wind came prowling after dark\nand rattled every door.\nIt sniffed along the window frames\nand moaned beneath the floor.\n\nIt kicked the wheelie bins about\nlike toys it didn''t need,\nthen curled up in the jacaranda\nas soft as thistle seed."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'medium', 1, '''Somewhere a kookaburra started laughing, then another, until the whole bush seemed to be in on the joke.'' What feeling does this create?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The bush feels friendly, cheerful and full of life."},{"id":"b","text":"The narrator is being laughed at by the birds."},{"id":"c","text":"The kookaburras are angry and dangerous."},{"id":"d","text":"The bush is silent and empty."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Laughing and sharing a joke are happy, friendly things. Imagining the whole bush laughing together makes it feel warm and alive.', NULL, '{"title":"Dawn at Camp","text":"I hated the idea of camping. No wi-fi, no fridge, and a toilet that was really just a hole with a seat. On our first night at Myall Lakes I lay in my sleeping bag, listening to every rustle and creak, sure that something with teeth was circling the tent.\n\nAt dawn, Aunty Jo shook my foot. \"Come and see,\" she whispered.\n\nMist hung over the lake like a grey blanket. The water was so still it looked like glass, and a pelican slid across it without making a ripple. Somewhere a kookaburra started laughing, then another, until the whole bush seemed to be in on the joke.\n\nAunty Jo handed me a mug of hot chocolate. I wrapped my cold fingers around it and didn''t say a word. I didn''t want to break the spell."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'medium', 1, 'Words like ''buzz'', ''crash'' and ''sizzle'' sound like the noise they describe. What is this technique called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"b","text":"alliteration"},{"id":"c","text":"simile"},{"id":"d","text":"rhyme"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Onomatopoeia is when a word imitates a sound. Alliteration is repeating the first sound, like ''slippery slimy snake''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'hard', 1, 'What does the lemon tree most likely stand for, or symbolise, in this story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Zara''s family home and all the years she has grown up there"},{"id":"b","text":"how much Zara loves cooking"},{"id":"c","text":"that the new house will be bigger"},{"id":"d","text":"Mum''s garden scissors"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The tree is as old as Zara and grew with her every year. Taking a piece of it means taking a piece of home and her memories with her.', NULL, '{"title":"The Lemon Tree","text":"Dad planted the lemon tree on the day Zara was born. Every birthday, she stood beside it for a photo, and every year it was a little taller than the year before.\n\nNow the house was sold. Boxes filled the hallway, and the empty rooms echoed when anyone spoke.\n\nOn the last morning, Zara walked out to the backyard with Mum''s garden scissors. She snipped a small branch with three leaves and a tiny green bud, wrapped the end in wet paper towel and tucked it into her lunchbox.\n\n\"What''s that for?\" asked Dad.\n\n\"For the new house,\" Zara said.\n\nDad was quiet for a moment. Then he went to the shed and came back with a small pot."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'hard', 1, 'On the first night, the narrator was ''sure that something with teeth was circling the tent''. What does this phrase show?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The narrator''s fear made ordinary night sounds seem dangerous."},{"id":"b","text":"A dingo really was circling the tent."},{"id":"c","text":"The narrator loves wild animals."},{"id":"d","text":"The tent had a hole in it."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The narrator only heard rustles and creaks. Imagining ''something with teeth'' shows how nervous they were about sleeping in the bush.', NULL, '{"title":"Dawn at Camp","text":"I hated the idea of camping. No wi-fi, no fridge, and a toilet that was really just a hole with a seat. On our first night at Myall Lakes I lay in my sleeping bag, listening to every rustle and creak, sure that something with teeth was circling the tent.\n\nAt dawn, Aunty Jo shook my foot. \"Come and see,\" she whispered.\n\nMist hung over the lake like a grey blanket. The water was so still it looked like glass, and a pelican slid across it without making a ripple. Somewhere a kookaburra started laughing, then another, until the whole bush seemed to be in on the joke.\n\nAunty Jo handed me a mug of hot chocolate. I wrapped my cold fingers around it and didn''t say a word. I didn''t want to break the spell."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'hard', 1, 'A writer wants a character to seem kind and gentle. Which words would help? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"soft-spoken"},{"id":"b","text":"warm-hearted"},{"id":"c","text":"patient"},{"id":"d","text":"snarling"},{"id":"e","text":"sly"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Soft-spoken, warm-hearted and patient all have kind, gentle connotations. Snarling sounds angry and sly sounds sneaky.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_hard', 1, 'Dad ''was quiet for a moment'' and then came back with a small pot. What does this suggest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He was moved by Zara''s idea and wanted to help her keep the tree''s memory alive."},{"id":"b","text":"He was annoyed that Zara had cut the tree."},{"id":"c","text":"He wanted to plant flowers instead."},{"id":"d","text":"He did not hear what Zara said."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Dad planted the tree, so it means a lot to him too. His silence shows he is touched, and fetching a pot shows he supports her plan.', NULL, '{"title":"The Lemon Tree","text":"Dad planted the lemon tree on the day Zara was born. Every birthday, she stood beside it for a photo, and every year it was a little taller than the year before.\n\nNow the house was sold. Boxes filled the hallway, and the empty rooms echoed when anyone spoke.\n\nOn the last morning, Zara walked out to the backyard with Mum''s garden scissors. She snipped a small branch with three leaves and a tiny green bud, wrapped the end in wet paper towel and tucked it into her lunchbox.\n\n\"What''s that for?\" asked Dad.\n\n\"For the new house,\" Zara said.\n\nDad was quiet for a moment. Then he went to the shed and came back with a small pot."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_hard', 1, 'How has the narrator changed by the end of the story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"They go from hating camping to being amazed by the beauty of the bush."},{"id":"b","text":"They go from loving camping to wanting to go home."},{"id":"c","text":"They go from liking pelicans to being scared of them."},{"id":"d","text":"They don''t change at all."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'At first the narrator ''hated the idea of camping''. At dawn they stay silent because they ''didn''t want to break the spell'', which shows they are now enchanted.', NULL, '{"title":"Dawn at Camp","text":"I hated the idea of camping. No wi-fi, no fridge, and a toilet that was really just a hole with a seat. On our first night at Myall Lakes I lay in my sleeping bag, listening to every rustle and creak, sure that something with teeth was circling the tent.\n\nAt dawn, Aunty Jo shook my foot. \"Come and see,\" she whispered.\n\nMist hung over the lake like a grey blanket. The water was so still it looked like glass, and a pelican slid across it without making a ripple. Somewhere a kookaburra started laughing, then another, until the whole bush seemed to be in on the joke.\n\nAunty Jo handed me a mug of hot chocolate. I wrapped my cold fingers around it and didn''t say a word. I didn''t want to break the spell."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051302', 'very_hard', 1, 'The poet could simply have written ''The wind blew hard all night.'' Why is the poem more effective?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Turning the wind into a restless creature helps readers see, hear and feel the windy night."},{"id":"b","text":"It is shorter and quicker to read."},{"id":"c","text":"It gives more scientific facts about the weather."},{"id":"d","text":"It tells us exactly how fast the wind blew."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Prowling, sniffing, moaning and curling up give the wind a personality. That imagery lets readers picture the night instead of just being told about it.', NULL, '{"title":"The Wind at Night","text":"The wind came prowling after dark\nand rattled every door.\nIt sniffed along the window frames\nand moaned beneath the floor.\n\nIt kicked the wheelie bins about\nlike toys it didn''t need,\nthen curled up in the jacaranda\nas soft as thistle seed."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_easy', 1, 'What kind of story is this most like?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a fable"},{"id":"b","text":"a news report"},{"id":"c","text":"a recipe"},{"id":"d","text":"a diary entry"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A fable is a short story with talking animals that teaches a lesson, just like this one.', NULL, '{"title":"The Kookaburra and the Emu","text":"Kookaburra loved to laugh at the other animals. \"Look at Emu,\" he cackled from his branch. \"Such long legs, and she can''t even fly!\"\n\nEmu said nothing. She kept walking, day after day, searching for water as the dry season dragged on.\n\nWeeks later, the creek beside Kookaburra''s tree dried up. He flew in circles, thirsty and lost, until he spotted Emu striding across the plain. He followed her all the way to a hidden waterhole.\n\nAs he drank, Kookaburra did not laugh at all."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_easy', 1, 'A recount usually tells events in the order they happened.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Recounts retell what happened, so they go in time order, often with words like first, next and later.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_easy', 1, 'A story set in the future with robots and spaceships belongs to which genre?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["science fiction","sci-fi","scifi","sci fi"]'::jsonb, 'Stories about the future, space travel and imagined technology are science fiction.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'easy', 1, 'What is the theme, or message, of this fable?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Don''t make fun of others, because everyone has their own strengths."},{"id":"b","text":"Birds are better than emus."},{"id":"c","text":"Always fly when you are thirsty."},{"id":"d","text":"Creeks dry up in summer."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Kookaburra mocks Emu for not flying, but Emu''s long walking legs are what save him. The lesson is not to make fun of others.', NULL, '{"title":"The Kookaburra and the Emu","text":"Kookaburra loved to laugh at the other animals. \"Look at Emu,\" he cackled from his branch. \"Such long legs, and she can''t even fly!\"\n\nEmu said nothing. She kept walking, day after day, searching for water as the dry season dragged on.\n\nWeeks later, the creek beside Kookaburra''s tree dried up. He flew in circles, thirsty and lost, until he spotted Emu striding across the plain. He followed her all the way to a hidden waterhole.\n\nAs he drank, Kookaburra did not laugh at all."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'easy', 1, 'Which reasons does Leo give for liking the skate park? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"It gives kids a safe place to skate."},{"id":"b","text":"Kids are making new friends."},{"id":"c","text":"It is very quiet."},{"id":"d","text":"It keeps rubbish out of yards."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Leo says it is a safe place to practise and kids are making new friends. The other two are not his points (Mrs Barker says the opposite).', NULL, '{"title":"Two Views of the Skate Park","text":"Leo, aged 11: The new skate park is the best thing to happen to Tamworth in years. Before, we skated in the shopping centre car park and got told off. Now we have a safe place to practise, and kids from all over town are making new friends.\n\nMrs Barker, neighbour: Since the skate park opened beside my house, I cannot sit in my garden in peace. The clatter of boards goes on until dark, and rubbish blows into my yard. I am not against young people having fun, but it should have been built away from homes."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'easy', 1, 'Put the parts of a persuasive text in the usual order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"a statement of your opinion"},{"id":"b","text":"arguments with evidence"},{"id":"c","text":"a conclusion that restates your opinion"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'A persuasive text states the writer''s position, backs it up with arguments, then sums up by repeating the position strongly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'medium', 1, 'Why does the story end with ''Kookaburra did not laugh at all''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to show that Kookaburra has learnt his lesson"},{"id":"b","text":"to show that Kookaburra has a sore throat"},{"id":"c","text":"to show that the water tasted bad"},{"id":"d","text":"to show that Emu told a joke"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Kookaburra laughed at Emu at the start. Not laughing at the end shows he now respects her and has learnt the fable''s lesson.', NULL, '{"title":"The Kookaburra and the Emu","text":"Kookaburra loved to laugh at the other animals. \"Look at Emu,\" he cackled from his branch. \"Such long legs, and she can''t even fly!\"\n\nEmu said nothing. She kept walking, day after day, searching for water as the dry season dragged on.\n\nWeeks later, the creek beside Kookaburra''s tree dried up. He flew in circles, thirsty and lost, until he spotted Emu striding across the plain. He followed her all the way to a hidden waterhole.\n\nAs he drank, Kookaburra did not laugh at all."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'medium', 1, 'Why do Leo and Mrs Barker see the skate park so differently?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Leo uses the park, while Mrs Barker lives right next to it and has to put up with the noise."},{"id":"b","text":"Leo is telling the truth and Mrs Barker is not."},{"id":"c","text":"They are talking about two different skate parks."},{"id":"d","text":"Mrs Barker has never seen the skate park."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A person''s perspective depends on their situation. Leo gains a safe place to skate; Mrs Barker loses her peace and quiet.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Views of the Skate Park","text":"Leo, aged 11: The new skate park is the best thing to happen to Tamworth in years. Before, we skated in the shopping centre car park and got told off. Now we have a safe place to practise, and kids from all over town are making new friends.\n\nMrs Barker, neighbour: Since the skate park opened beside my house, I cannot sit in my garden in peace. The clatter of boards goes on until dark, and rubbish blows into my yard. I am not against young people having fun, but it should have been built away from homes."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'medium', 1, 'Why does the writer include the words of ranger Sam Ellis?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A ranger is an expert, so his words make the club sound more believable."},{"id":"b","text":"Sam Ellis wrote the whole advertisement."},{"id":"c","text":"to tell readers where to buy a hat"},{"id":"d","text":"to make the ad longer"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Quoting someone with authority, like a ranger who works in the bush, makes readers trust the claims more.', NULL, '{"title":"Join the Junior Landcare Club!","text":"Do you love the bush? Do you want to make a real difference? Join the Junior Landcare Club!\n\nEvery second Saturday we plant native trees along Wollombi Creek. Last year our members planted over 2000 seedlings, and platypus have now been seen in the creek for the first time in ten years.\n\nAccording to local ranger Sam Ellis, ''Young volunteers have done more for this creek than anyone expected.''\n\nBring a hat, a water bottle and your friends. The bush needs you!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'hard', 1, 'Mrs Barker says young people should not be allowed to have fun.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'She says ''I am not against young people having fun''. Her complaint is about where the park was built, not about fun.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Views of the Skate Park","text":"Leo, aged 11: The new skate park is the best thing to happen to Tamworth in years. Before, we skated in the shopping centre car park and got told off. Now we have a safe place to practise, and kids from all over town are making new friends.\n\nMrs Barker, neighbour: Since the skate park opened beside my house, I cannot sit in my garden in peace. The clatter of boards goes on until dark, and rubbish blows into my yard. I am not against young people having fun, but it should have been built away from homes."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'hard', 1, 'Which persuasive techniques does the advertisement use? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"rhetorical questions"},{"id":"b","text":"a quote from an expert"},{"id":"c","text":"facts and figures"},{"id":"d","text":"a funny joke"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'It opens with questions (''Do you love the bush?''), quotes a ranger, and gives figures like 2000 seedlings. There is no joke.', NULL, '{"title":"Join the Junior Landcare Club!","text":"Do you love the bush? Do you want to make a real difference? Join the Junior Landcare Club!\n\nEvery second Saturday we plant native trees along Wollombi Creek. Last year our members planted over 2000 seedlings, and platypus have now been seen in the creek for the first time in ten years.\n\nAccording to local ranger Sam Ellis, ''Young volunteers have done more for this creek than anyone expected.''\n\nBring a hat, a water bottle and your friends. The bush needs you!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'hard', 1, 'You are writing a report about how many koalas live in NSW. Which source would be most reliable?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a NSW Government wildlife survey"},{"id":"b","text":"a comment on a social media post"},{"id":"c","text":"a picture book about a koala"},{"id":"d","text":"an advertisement for a koala toy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A government survey is written by experts who count koalas carefully. The others are opinions, stories or ads trying to sell something.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence from the advertisement is a fact that could be checked, rather than an opinion?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Last year our members planted over 2000 seedlings."},{"id":"b","text":"The bush needs you!"},{"id":"c","text":"Do you want to make a real difference?"},{"id":"d","text":"Bring a hat, a water bottle and your friends."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'You could check the club''s records to see how many seedlings were planted. The others are a feeling, a question and an instruction.', NULL, '{"title":"Join the Junior Landcare Club!","text":"Do you love the bush? Do you want to make a real difference? Join the Junior Landcare Club!\n\nEvery second Saturday we plant native trees along Wollombi Creek. Last year our members planted over 2000 seedlings, and platypus have now been seen in the creek for the first time in ten years.\n\nAccording to local ranger Sam Ellis, ''Young volunteers have done more for this creek than anyone expected.''\n\nBring a hat, a water bottle and your friends. The bush needs you!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_hard', 1, 'You want to retell this fable as a modern story set at school, with the same theme. Which plot fits best?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A girl teases a classmate for being slow at running, then needs his help when she can''t solve a maths problem."},{"id":"b","text":"Two friends go to the beach and build a sandcastle."},{"id":"c","text":"A boy finds a lost dog and returns it to its owner."},{"id":"d","text":"A class goes on an excursion to a museum."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The theme is not to mock others because everyone has strengths. Only the first plot has someone teasing another person, then needing that person''s strength.', NULL, '{"title":"The Kookaburra and the Emu","text":"Kookaburra loved to laugh at the other animals. \"Look at Emu,\" he cackled from his branch. \"Such long legs, and she can''t even fly!\"\n\nEmu said nothing. She kept walking, day after day, searching for water as the dry season dragged on.\n\nWeeks later, the creek beside Kookaburra''s tree dried up. He flew in circles, thirsty and lost, until he spotted Emu striding across the plain. He followed her all the way to a hidden waterhole.\n\nAs he drank, Kookaburra did not laugh at all."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_hard', 1, 'A story about the First Fleet arriving at Sydney Cove in 1788 is told by a Gadigal boy watching from the shore. How would it most likely differ from the same event told by a British sailor?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It would show strangers arriving uninvited on his people''s land."},{"id":"b","text":"It would describe the long voyage from England."},{"id":"c","text":"It would be exactly the same."},{"id":"d","text":"It would only list the names of the ships."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Perspective depends on who is telling. The Gadigal people already lived at Sydney Cove, so for the boy this was strangers arriving on his Country, not the end of a voyage.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which features show that this story is a fable?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"animals that talk and a lesson at the end"},{"id":"b","text":"a list of ingredients and a method"},{"id":"c","text":"dates, facts and a glossary"},{"id":"d","text":"numbered steps and a diagram"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Fables are short stories where animals talk and act like people, and they end with a lesson for the reader.', NULL, '{"title":"The Galah and the Lyrebird","text":"Galah thought her pink and grey feathers were plain. Every day she watched Lyrebird dance on his mound, his lacy tail shimmering in the light.\n\n\"If only I could sing like him,\" she sighed. So she practised. She tried to copy the kookaburra, the whipbird and the bellbird, but every sound came out as a screech. The other birds covered their ears.\n\nOne evening a hawk swooped low over the forest. Lyrebird froze, but Galah screeched so loudly that every bird heard the warning and dived for cover.\n\nThe next morning, the birds gathered around Galah. \"Your voice saved us,\" said Lyrebird. \"Never wish it away.\""}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_easy', 1, 'A procedure is a text that tells you how to do or make something.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Recipes, game rules and instructions for building something are all procedures. They have steps to follow in order.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_easy', 1, 'A story about a detective who solves a crime belongs to which genre?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["mystery","a mystery","crime","detective","crime fiction"]'::jsonb, 'Stories with clues, suspects and a puzzle to solve are mysteries, also called crime or detective stories.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'easy', 1, 'What is the theme, or lesson, of this fable?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Be proud of who you are, because your own strengths matter."},{"id":"b","text":"Hawks are the most dangerous birds."},{"id":"c","text":"Singing is the most important skill."},{"id":"d","text":"Pink feathers are better than grey ones."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Galah wished she was like Lyrebird, but her own loud voice saved everyone. The lesson is to value who you are.', NULL, '{"title":"The Galah and the Lyrebird","text":"Galah thought her pink and grey feathers were plain. Every day she watched Lyrebird dance on his mound, his lacy tail shimmering in the light.\n\n\"If only I could sing like him,\" she sighed. So she practised. She tried to copy the kookaburra, the whipbird and the bellbird, but every sound came out as a screech. The other birds covered their ears.\n\nOne evening a hawk swooped low over the forest. Lyrebird froze, but Galah screeched so loudly that every bird heard the warning and dived for cover.\n\nThe next morning, the birds gathered around Galah. \"Your voice saved us,\" said Lyrebird. \"Never wish it away.\""}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'easy', 1, 'Which reasons does Dr Hassan give against fireworks? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Frightened dogs run away and get hit by cars."},{"id":"b","text":"Birds and flying-foxes panic at the bangs."},{"id":"c","text":"Families bring picnics."},{"id":"d","text":"The whole town comes together."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Dr Hassan talks about dogs running away and birds and flying-foxes panicking. Picnics and the town coming together are Chloe''s reasons for fireworks.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Views on Fireworks","text":"Chloe, aged 10: The fireworks over the river are the best night of the year in Port Macquarie. Families bring picnics, and everyone counts down together. When the sky lights up, even grumpy teenagers say ''wow''. It is one of the few times the whole town comes together.\n\nDr Sara Hassan, local vet: Every New Year''s Day, my clinic treats dogs that ran away in fright and were hit by cars. Birds and flying-foxes also panic at the loud bangs. I enjoy celebrations too, but I would like the council to try a quiet drone light show instead."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'easy', 1, 'Put the parts of an information report in the usual order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"a general statement introducing the topic"},{"id":"b","text":"paragraphs describing different features"},{"id":"c","text":"a conclusion that sums up"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'An information report opens by saying what the topic is, then each paragraph describes one feature, and it finishes by summing up.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'medium', 1, 'Why does the writer show Galah''s screech first as a problem and then as a strength?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to show that something you dislike about yourself can turn out to be valuable"},{"id":"b","text":"to prove that singing lessons always work"},{"id":"c","text":"to make the hawk the hero of the story"},{"id":"d","text":"to explain how galahs build nests"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'At first the screech makes birds cover their ears; later it saves them. The change carries the fable''s message about valuing yourself.', NULL, '{"title":"The Galah and the Lyrebird","text":"Galah thought her pink and grey feathers were plain. Every day she watched Lyrebird dance on his mound, his lacy tail shimmering in the light.\n\n\"If only I could sing like him,\" she sighed. So she practised. She tried to copy the kookaburra, the whipbird and the bellbird, but every sound came out as a screech. The other birds covered their ears.\n\nOne evening a hawk swooped low over the forest. Lyrebird froze, but Galah screeched so loudly that every bird heard the warning and dived for cover.\n\nThe next morning, the birds gathered around Galah. \"Your voice saved us,\" said Lyrebird. \"Never wish it away.\""}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'medium', 1, 'Why do Chloe and Dr Hassan see fireworks so differently?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Chloe sees the fun for families, while Dr Hassan sees the harm to animals in her work."},{"id":"b","text":"Chloe is telling the truth and Dr Hassan is not."},{"id":"c","text":"They are talking about two different towns."},{"id":"d","text":"Dr Hassan has never seen fireworks."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A person''s perspective depends on their experience. Chloe enjoys the night with her town; Dr Hassan treats the hurt animals the next day.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Views on Fireworks","text":"Chloe, aged 10: The fireworks over the river are the best night of the year in Port Macquarie. Families bring picnics, and everyone counts down together. When the sky lights up, even grumpy teenagers say ''wow''. It is one of the few times the whole town comes together.\n\nDr Sara Hassan, local vet: Every New Year''s Day, my clinic treats dogs that ran away in fright and were hit by cars. Birds and flying-foxes also panic at the loud bangs. I enjoy celebrations too, but I would like the council to try a quiet drone light show instead."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'medium', 1, 'Why does the ad mention that all money raised buys new books for the school library?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to make readers feel that buying a book also helps their school"},{"id":"b","text":"to explain how to borrow a library book"},{"id":"c","text":"to show that the fair is free"},{"id":"d","text":"to list the price of every book"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Knowing your money helps the library gives readers an extra good reason to come and spend. It is a persuasive appeal.', NULL, '{"title":"Come to the Wattle Creek Book Fair!","text":"Are you looking for your next favourite book? Do you want to meet a real author? Then don''t miss the Wattle Creek Book Fair!\n\nWith over 3000 books for sale, there is something for every reader, from comics to chapter books. Award-winning author Mei Lin will read from her new adventure novel at 11 am.\n\n\"The fair is the highlight of our school year,\" says librarian Mr Daniel Ortiz.\n\nAll money raised buys new books for our school library. Grab your friends and your pocket money. Reading has never been so much fun!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'hard', 1, 'Dr Hassan wants to stop all New Year''s Eve celebrations.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'She says ''I enjoy celebrations too'' and suggests a quiet drone light show instead. She wants a different kind of celebration, not none.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Views on Fireworks","text":"Chloe, aged 10: The fireworks over the river are the best night of the year in Port Macquarie. Families bring picnics, and everyone counts down together. When the sky lights up, even grumpy teenagers say ''wow''. It is one of the few times the whole town comes together.\n\nDr Sara Hassan, local vet: Every New Year''s Day, my clinic treats dogs that ran away in fright and were hit by cars. Birds and flying-foxes also panic at the loud bangs. I enjoy celebrations too, but I would like the council to try a quiet drone light show instead."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'hard', 1, 'Which persuasive techniques does the advertisement use? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"rhetorical questions"},{"id":"b","text":"a number fact"},{"id":"c","text":"a quote from someone at the school"},{"id":"d","text":"an exclamation to finish"},{"id":"e","text":"a scary warning"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'It opens with questions, says there are over 3000 books, quotes the librarian and ends with ''Reading has never been so much fun!'' There is no scary warning.', NULL, '{"title":"Come to the Wattle Creek Book Fair!","text":"Are you looking for your next favourite book? Do you want to meet a real author? Then don''t miss the Wattle Creek Book Fair!\n\nWith over 3000 books for sale, there is something for every reader, from comics to chapter books. Award-winning author Mei Lin will read from her new adventure novel at 11 am.\n\n\"The fair is the highlight of our school year,\" says librarian Mr Daniel Ortiz.\n\nAll money raised buys new books for our school library. Grab your friends and your pocket money. Reading has never been so much fun!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'hard', 1, 'You are researching how long platypuses live in the wild. Which source would be most reliable?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a book about platypuses written by a university scientist"},{"id":"b","text":"a cartoon about a talking platypus"},{"id":"c","text":"a stranger''s comment under an online video"},{"id":"d","text":"a poster selling a platypus soft toy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A scientist who studies platypuses has expert knowledge and checks facts. Cartoons are made up, comments may be guesses, and posters are trying to sell.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence from the ad is an opinion, not a fact that could be checked?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Reading has never been so much fun!"},{"id":"b","text":"Award-winning author Mei Lin will read from her new adventure novel at 11 am."},{"id":"c","text":"With over 3000 books for sale, there is something for every reader."},{"id":"d","text":"All money raised buys new books for our school library."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'You could check the time of the reading, count the books or look at where the money goes. Whether reading is ''so much fun'' is a feeling, so it is an opinion.', NULL, '{"title":"Come to the Wattle Creek Book Fair!","text":"Are you looking for your next favourite book? Do you want to meet a real author? Then don''t miss the Wattle Creek Book Fair!\n\nWith over 3000 books for sale, there is something for every reader, from comics to chapter books. Award-winning author Mei Lin will read from her new adventure novel at 11 am.\n\n\"The fair is the highlight of our school year,\" says librarian Mr Daniel Ortiz.\n\nAll money raised buys new books for our school library. Grab your friends and your pocket money. Reading has never been so much fun!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these story ideas shares the same theme as the fable?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A shy girl who thinks her quiet voice is boring finds she is the best at calming scared Kindergarten kids."},{"id":"b","text":"A boy wins a race because he trained every morning."},{"id":"c","text":"A family goes on a holiday to the snow."},{"id":"d","text":"A dog digs up a bone in the backyard."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Like Galah, the shy girl dislikes something about herself and then discovers it is a strength. The race story is about hard work, a different theme.', NULL, '{"title":"The Galah and the Lyrebird","text":"Galah thought her pink and grey feathers were plain. Every day she watched Lyrebird dance on his mound, his lacy tail shimmering in the light.\n\n\"If only I could sing like him,\" she sighed. So she practised. She tried to copy the kookaburra, the whipbird and the bellbird, but every sound came out as a screech. The other birds covered their ears.\n\nOne evening a hawk swooped low over the forest. Lyrebird froze, but Galah screeched so loudly that every bird heard the warning and dived for cover.\n\nThe next morning, the birds gathered around Galah. \"Your voice saved us,\" said Lyrebird. \"Never wish it away.\""}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005130330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051303', 'very_hard', 1, 'After a big storm, a farmer writes that it was ''the best day in years'' because her dam filled up. A family whose roof blew off calls it ''the worst night of our lives''. Why are their recounts so different?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Each writer''s situation shapes how they see the same event."},{"id":"b","text":"One of them must be lying."},{"id":"c","text":"They must be writing about different storms."},{"id":"d","text":"Farmers always like storms."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Same storm, different perspectives. The farmer gained water she needed, while the family lost their roof, so each feels very differently about it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'medium', 1, 'One dog has a bone. Which is correct?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the dogs bone"},{"id":"b","text":"the dog''s bone"},{"id":"c","text":"the dogs'' bone"},{"id":"d","text":"the dog,s bone"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'To show something belongs to one owner, add ''s: the dog''s bone.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'medium', 1, 'Write the short form (contraction) of ''do not'': ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"don''t"'::jsonb, '''Do not'' becomes ''don''t''. The apostrophe takes the place of the missing o.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'medium', 1, '''Its'' with no apostrophe means ''belonging to it''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''Its'' means belonging to it (the dog wagged its tail). ''It''s'' is short for ''it is'' or ''it has''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentences use apostrophes correctly? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"I can''t come."},{"id":"b","text":"The cat''s tail is long."},{"id":"c","text":"I like apple''s."},{"id":"d","text":"We''re late!"},{"id":"e","text":"The boy''s are playing."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '''Can''t'' and ''we''re'' are contractions, and ''cat''s'' shows the tail belongs to the cat. Plurals like ''apples'' and ''boys'' don''t need an apostrophe.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'medium', 1, 'Write the short form (contraction) of ''they are'': ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"they''re"'::jsonb, '''They are'' becomes ''they''re''. The apostrophe takes the place of the missing a.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the short form (contraction) of ''I am''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I''m"},{"id":"b","text":"Im''"},{"id":"c","text":"I''am"},{"id":"d","text":"Ia''m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The apostrophe goes where the letter a is left out: I am → I''m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_easy', 1, 'In ''Lucy''s hat'', the apostrophe shows that the hat belongs to Lucy.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Adding ''s to a name shows that something belongs to that person.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the short form (contraction) of ''is not'': ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"isn''t"'::jsonb, 'Join ''is'' and ''not'', and put an apostrophe where the o is left out: isn''t.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'easy', 1, 'The ball belongs to Jack. Which is correct?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Jack''s ball"},{"id":"b","text":"Jacks ball"},{"id":"c","text":"Jacks'' ball"},{"id":"d","text":"Ja''cks ball"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Jack is one owner, so add ''s: Jack''s ball.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these words are contractions? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"won''t"},{"id":"b","text":"she''ll"},{"id":"c","text":"cats"},{"id":"d","text":"you''ve"},{"id":"e","text":"happy"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'won''t (will not), she''ll (she will) and you''ve (you have) are short forms. ''Cats'' is just a plural.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'easy', 1, 'Write the long form of ''couldn''t''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"could not"'::jsonb, 'The apostrophe stands for the missing o in ''not'', so couldn''t means ''could not''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'medium', 1, 'The nests belong to several birds. Which is correct?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the birds'' nests"},{"id":"b","text":"the bird''s nests"},{"id":"c","text":"the birds nests"},{"id":"d","text":"the birds''s nests"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There is more than one bird, and ''birds'' already ends in s. So just add an apostrophe after the s: the birds'' nests.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'medium', 1, '''Its'' and ''it''s'' mean the same thing.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '''It''s'' is short for ''it is'' or ''it has''. ''Its'' (no apostrophe) means belonging to it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence uses the apostrophe correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The children''s toys were everywhere."},{"id":"b","text":"The childrens'' toys were everywhere."},{"id":"c","text":"The childrens toys'' were everywhere."},{"id":"d","text":"The children,s toys were everywhere."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Children'' is already plural and doesn''t end in s, so add ''s: children''s.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'hard', 1, 'One word in this sentence needs an apostrophe: ''Its going to rain today.'' Write that word correctly.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"it''s"'::jsonb, 'The sentence means ''It is going to rain'', so it needs the contraction it''s.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence is correct?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The team won its first game."},{"id":"b","text":"The team won it''s first game."},{"id":"c","text":"The team won its'' first game."},{"id":"d","text":"The team won i''ts first game."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The game belongs to the team, so use ''its'' with no apostrophe. ''It''s'' would mean ''it is''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these show something belonging to more than one owner? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"the girls'' change room"},{"id":"b","text":"the girl''s bag"},{"id":"c","text":"the teachers'' lounge"},{"id":"d","text":"the men''s toilets"},{"id":"e","text":"the dog''s lead"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'girls'' and teachers'' have the apostrophe after the plural s. ''Men'' is already plural, so men''s is many owners too.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_hard', 1, '''The Smiths'' car is red.'' What does the apostrophe tell you?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The car belongs to the Smith family."},{"id":"b","text":"The car belongs to one person called Smith."},{"id":"c","text":"There are many cars."},{"id":"d","text":"The Smiths own several cars."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Smiths'' means the whole Smith family. The apostrophe after the s shows the car belongs to them all.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_hard', 1, 'How many apostrophes are missing from this sentence? ''Wed love to see Sams new puppy, but its asleep.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"3"'::jsonb, 'We''d (we would), Sam''s (the puppy belongs to Sam) and it''s (it is). That is 3 apostrophes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Write the short form (contraction) of ''they had'': ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"they''d"'::jsonb, '''Had'' shortens to ''d, so they had → they''d. (They''d can also mean ''they would''.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the short form (contraction) of ''we will''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"we''ll"},{"id":"b","text":"wel''l"},{"id":"c","text":"we''ill"},{"id":"d","text":"weel"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The ''wi'' of ''will'' is left out, and the apostrophe goes in its place: we''ll.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the short form (contraction) of ''has not'': ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"hasn''t"'::jsonb, 'Join the words and put the apostrophe where the o of ''not'' is left out: hasn''t.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_easy', 1, 'The word ''apples'' in ''I bought three apples.'' needs an apostrophe.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '''Apples'' just means more than one apple. Plurals don''t need an apostrophe.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'easy', 1, 'Write the long form of ''you''re''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"you are"'::jsonb, 'The apostrophe stands for the missing a: you''re means ''you are''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'easy', 1, 'The tail belongs to the kangaroo. Which is correct?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the kangaroo''s tail"},{"id":"b","text":"the kangaroos tail"},{"id":"c","text":"the kangaroos'' tail"},{"id":"d","text":"the kangaroo,s tail"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There is one kangaroo, so add ''s: the kangaroo''s tail.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these sentences are correct? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mum''s car is blue."},{"id":"b","text":"I''ve finished."},{"id":"c","text":"The cat''s are hungry."},{"id":"d","text":"She''s my sister."},{"id":"e","text":"The bus''es are late."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Mum''s shows belonging, and I''ve and she''s are contractions. ''Cats'' and ''buses'' are just plurals, so no apostrophe.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'medium', 1, 'Which word fills the gap? ''___ coming to the sausage sizzle?''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Who''s"},{"id":"b","text":"Whose"},{"id":"c","text":"Whos"},{"id":"d","text":"Who''se"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The sentence means ''Who is coming?'', so use the contraction who''s. ''Whose'' asks who owns something.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'medium', 1, 'The lockers belong to all the students. Which is correct?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the students'' lockers"},{"id":"b","text":"the student''s lockers"},{"id":"c","text":"the students lockers"},{"id":"d","text":"the students''s lockers"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Many students own them, and ''students'' ends in s, so the apostrophe goes after the s: the students'' lockers.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'medium', 1, 'The apostrophe is used correctly in ''The dog''s are barking.''', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Nothing belongs to the dogs here; there are just many dogs. It should be ''The dogs are barking.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'hard', 1, 'Which word fills the gap? ''The koala hugged ___ baby.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"its"},{"id":"b","text":"it''s"},{"id":"c","text":"its''"},{"id":"d","text":"it is"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The baby belongs to the koala, so use ''its'' with no apostrophe. ''It''s'' means ''it is''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140131', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'hard', 1, 'Which word in this sentence needs an apostrophe? ''The womens team won the netball final.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '["women''s","womens"]'::jsonb, 'The team belongs to the women. ''Women'' is already plural, so add ''s: the women''s team.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140132', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these contractions are written correctly? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"should''ve"},{"id":"b","text":"would''nt"},{"id":"c","text":"they''re"},{"id":"d","text":"does''nt"},{"id":"e","text":"I''d"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'The apostrophe goes where letters are missing. In wouldn''t and doesn''t, the o of ''not'' is missing, so it goes between n and t.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140133', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_hard', 1, '''The players'' boots were muddy.'' How many players is this about?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"more than one player"},{"id":"b","text":"exactly one player"},{"id":"c","text":"no players"},{"id":"d","text":"you can''t tell"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The apostrophe comes after the s in ''players'', which shows the boots belong to more than one player.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140134', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_hard', 1, 'The toys belong to the babies. Write it with an apostrophe: the ___ toys', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"babies''"'::jsonb, '''Babies'' is plural and ends in s, so just add an apostrophe after it: the babies'' toys.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005140135', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses apostrophes correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"James''s brother doesn''t like the twins'' dog."},{"id":"b","text":"James brother does''nt like the twins dog."},{"id":"c","text":"James''s brother doesnt like the twin''s'' dog."},{"id":"d","text":"Jame''s brother doesn''t like the twins dog."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'James''s shows the brother is his, doesn''t is ''does not'', and twins'' shows the dog belongs to both twins.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is the conjunction in this sentence? ''I was hungry, so I made a sandwich.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"hungry"},{"id":"b","text":"so"},{"id":"c","text":"made"},{"id":"d","text":"sandwich"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A conjunction joins two ideas. ''So'' joins ''I was hungry'' and ''I made a sandwich''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_easy', 1, '''It''s'' is short for ''it is''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The apostrophe in ''it''s'' shows a letter is missing: it is becomes it''s.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_easy', 1, 'The dog belongs to Sam. It is ___ dog.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["Sam''s","Sam’s"]'::jsonb, 'To show something belongs to one person, add an apostrophe and s: Sam''s dog.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"\"Let''s go to the beach,\" said Dad."},{"id":"b","text":"\"Let''s go to the beach\" said Dad."},{"id":"c","text":"Let''s go to the beach, said Dad."},{"id":"d","text":"\"Let''s go to the beach,\" said Dad"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Speech marks go around the words spoken, a comma goes before the closing speech mark, and the sentence ends with a full stop.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'easy', 1, 'Which word is the modal verb in this sentence? ''You must wear a hat at recess.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"wear"},{"id":"b","text":"must"},{"id":"c","text":"hat"},{"id":"d","text":"recess"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Modal verbs show how certain or necessary something is, like must, should, could and might. Here it is ''must''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'easy', 1, 'Which feature belongs in an information report rather than a narrative?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"subheadings and facts"},{"id":"b","text":"a complication and resolution"},{"id":"c","text":"a main character''s feelings"},{"id":"d","text":"a ''once upon a time'' opening"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Information reports give facts and are organised with subheadings. The other features belong in stories.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'medium', 1, 'The bags belong to three girls. Which is correct?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the girl''s bags"},{"id":"b","text":"the girls'' bags"},{"id":"c","text":"the girls bags"},{"id":"d","text":"the girls''s bags"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Girls'' is a plural ending in s, so just add an apostrophe after the s: the girls'' bags.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'medium', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to make a paragraph.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Kangaroos are well suited to life in the Australian outback."},{"id":"b","text":"Their powerful back legs let them hop long distances using little energy."},{"id":"c","text":"They can also go for a long time without drinking water."},{"id":"d","text":"These features help kangaroos survive in a hot, dry land."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A paragraph starts with a topic sentence, adds supporting details (also is a clue it comes later), and ends by summing up.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'medium', 1, 'Change the verb to past tense: ''Yesterday she ___ to the bus stop.'' (run)', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"ran"'::jsonb, '''Run'' is an irregular verb. Its past tense is ''ran'', not ''runned''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'hard', 1, 'Which are complex sentences (they have a main clause and a dependent clause)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Because it was raining, we stayed inside."},{"id":"b","text":"We stayed inside."},{"id":"c","text":"The dog barked when the postie arrived."},{"id":"d","text":"I like apples and pears."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, '''Because it was raining'' and ''when the postie arrived'' are dependent clauses that can''t stand alone, so those sentences are complex.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'hard', 1, 'Tom said, "I am tired." Which sentence reports this correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Tom said that he was tired."},{"id":"b","text":"Tom said that I am tired."},{"id":"c","text":"Tom said that \"he was tired.\""},{"id":"d","text":"Tom said, he is tired."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Reported speech has no speech marks, changes ''I'' to ''he'', and usually moves the verb back in time: am becomes was.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'hard', 1, 'Which connective best fills the gap? ''Koalas eat eucalyptus leaves. ___, these leaves give them very little energy, so they sleep for up to 20 hours a day.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"However"},{"id":"b","text":"For example"},{"id":"c","text":"Firstly"},{"id":"d","text":"Meanwhile"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The second sentence turns to a problem with the leaves, so a contrast word is needed: However.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these modal verbs in order from least certain to most certain.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"might"},{"id":"b","text":"should"},{"id":"c","text":"must"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '''Might'' means it is possible, ''should'' means it is expected, and ''must'' means it is certain or required.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_hard', 1, 'In ''Although the bus was late, Priya made it to school on time,'' which part is the main clause?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Although the bus was late"},{"id":"b","text":"Priya made it to school on time"},{"id":"c","text":"the bus was late"},{"id":"d","text":"Although"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The main clause makes sense on its own: ''Priya made it to school on time.'' ''Although the bus was late'' needs the rest of the sentence.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"\"Have you seen the twins'' bikes?\" asked Mr Lee."},{"id":"b","text":"\"Have you seen the twin''s bikes\"? asked Mr Lee."},{"id":"c","text":"\"Have you seen the twins bikes?\" asked Mr Lee."},{"id":"d","text":"\"Have you seen the twins'' bikes,\" asked Mr Lee?"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The bikes belong to two twins, so the apostrophe goes after the s (twins''). The question mark belongs inside the speech marks because the spoken words are the question.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is the conjunction in this sentence? ''We could go to the pool or we could stay home.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"pool"},{"id":"b","text":"or"},{"id":"c","text":"stay"},{"id":"d","text":"home"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A conjunction joins two ideas. ''Or'' joins ''We could go to the pool'' and ''we could stay home'' and shows a choice.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_easy', 1, '''They''re'' is short for ''they are''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The apostrophe in ''they''re'' shows where the a of ''are'' is missing: they are becomes they''re.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_easy', 1, 'The ball belongs to the dog. It is the ___ ball.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["dog''s","dog’s"]'::jsonb, 'To show something belongs to one owner, add an apostrophe and s: the dog''s ball.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"\"Where are my shoes?\" asked Ella."},{"id":"b","text":"\"Where are my shoes\" asked Ella?"},{"id":"c","text":"\"Where are my shoes,\" asked Ella."},{"id":"d","text":"Where are my shoes? asked Ella."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The spoken words are a question, so the question mark goes inside the speech marks, and the sentence ends with a full stop.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'easy', 1, 'Which words are modal verbs? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"could"},{"id":"b","text":"should"},{"id":"c","text":"jumped"},{"id":"d","text":"might"},{"id":"e","text":"table"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Modal verbs show how likely or necessary something is: could, should and might. ''Jumped'' is an action verb and ''table'' is a noun.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'easy', 1, 'Which feature belongs in a procedure?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"numbered steps and a list of materials"},{"id":"b","text":"a complication and resolution"},{"id":"c","text":"lines that rhyme"},{"id":"d","text":"a character''s feelings"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A procedure tells you how to make or do something, so it lists what you need and gives steps in order.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'medium', 1, 'The hats belong to the men. Which is correct?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the men''s hats"},{"id":"b","text":"the mens'' hats"},{"id":"c","text":"the mens hats"},{"id":"d","text":"the man''s hats"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Men'' is a plural that doesn''t end in s, so add an apostrophe and s: the men''s hats.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'medium', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to make a paragraph.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"The platypus is one of Australia''s most unusual animals."},{"id":"b","text":"It has a bill like a duck and a flat tail like a beaver."},{"id":"c","text":"It also lays eggs, even though it is a mammal."},{"id":"d","text":"All these features make the platypus truly one of a kind."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Start with the topic sentence, add details (''also'' shows it comes after another detail), and finish with a sentence that sums up.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'medium', 1, 'Change the verb to past tense: ''Last week we ___ a koala in the tree.'' (see)', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"saw"'::jsonb, '''See'' is an irregular verb. Its past tense is ''saw'', not ''seed'' or ''seen''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'hard', 1, 'Which are complex sentences (they have a main clause and a dependent clause)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"After the bell rang, we lined up."},{"id":"b","text":"We lined up."},{"id":"c","text":"I will read to you if you bring the book."},{"id":"d","text":"She sang and he danced."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, '''After the bell rang'' and ''if you bring the book'' are dependent clauses that can''t stand alone. ''She sang and he danced'' joins two main clauses, so it is compound.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'hard', 1, 'Lily said, "I am going to the library." Which sentence reports this correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Lily said that she was going to the library."},{"id":"b","text":"Lily said that I am going to the library."},{"id":"c","text":"Lily said that \"she was going to the library.\""},{"id":"d","text":"Lily said, she is going to the library."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Reported speech has no speech marks, changes ''I'' to ''she'', and moves the verb back in time: am going becomes was going.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'hard', 1, 'Which connective best fills the gap? ''It had not rained for months. ___, the farmers'' dams were almost empty.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"As a result"},{"id":"b","text":"For example"},{"id":"c","text":"However"},{"id":"d","text":"Meanwhile"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The empty dams are caused by the lack of rain, so we need a cause-and-effect connective: As a result.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these sentences in order from least certain to most certain.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"It might rain tomorrow."},{"id":"b","text":"It will probably rain tomorrow."},{"id":"c","text":"It will definitely rain tomorrow."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '''Might'' means it is only possible, ''probably'' means it is likely, and ''definitely'' means it is certain.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_hard', 1, 'In ''Before the match started, the netball team warmed up on the court,'' which part is the main clause?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Before the match started"},{"id":"b","text":"the netball team warmed up on the court"},{"id":"c","text":"the match started"},{"id":"d","text":"Before"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The main clause makes sense on its own: ''the netball team warmed up on the court.'' ''Before the match started'' is a dependent clause that tells when.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000005150130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000051501', 'very_hard', 1, 'The esky belongs to the Jackson family. Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"\"Don''t forget the Jacksons'' esky,\" said Mum."},{"id":"b","text":"\"Don''t forget the Jackson''s esky\", said Mum."},{"id":"c","text":"\"Dont forget the Jacksons'' esky,\" said Mum."},{"id":"d","text":"\"Don''t forget the Jacksons esky\" said Mum."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Don''t'' needs an apostrophe for the missing o. The esky belongs to the whole Jackson family, a plural ending in s, so the apostrophe goes after the s. The comma goes inside the speech marks.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is the subordinating conjunction in this sentence? ''We went inside because it started to hail.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"went"},{"id":"b","text":"inside"},{"id":"c","text":"because"},{"id":"d","text":"hail"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, '''Because'' starts a dependent clause that gives a reason: because it started to hail.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_easy', 1, 'A paragraph usually begins with a topic sentence that tells what the paragraph is about.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The topic sentence introduces the main idea, and the sentences after it give details.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the contraction: would not = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["wouldn''t","wouldn’t"]'::jsonb, 'Join the words and put an apostrophe where the o of ''not'' is missing: wouldn''t.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'easy', 1, 'The toys belong to the children. Which is correct?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the childrens'' toys"},{"id":"b","text":"the children''s toys"},{"id":"c","text":"the childrens toys"},{"id":"d","text":"the childs'' toys"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Children'' is a plural that doesn''t end in s, so add apostrophe s: the children''s toys.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"\"If we hurry,\" said Noah, \"we can still catch the ferry.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"If we hurry,\" said Noah, \"We can still catch the ferry.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"If we hurry\" said Noah \"we can still catch the ferry.\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"If we hurry, said Noah, we can still catch the ferry.\""}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'When one spoken sentence is split, put commas around ''said Noah'' and start the second part with a lower-case letter, because it is the same sentence.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'easy', 1, 'Which are features of persuasive writing? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"high-modality words like ''must''"},{"id":"b","text":"rhetorical questions"},{"id":"c","text":"a clear position on an issue"},{"id":"d","text":"characters who face a complication"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Persuasive texts take a clear position and use strong modal words and rhetorical questions. Characters and complications belong in narratives.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'medium', 1, 'Mia said, "I will finish my project tomorrow." Which sentence reports this correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mia said that she would finish her project the next day."},{"id":"b","text":"Mia said that I will finish my project tomorrow."},{"id":"c","text":"Mia said, she will finish her project tomorrow."},{"id":"d","text":"Mia said that \"she would finish her project.\""}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'In reported speech, ''I'' becomes ''she'', ''will'' becomes ''would'', ''tomorrow'' becomes ''the next day'', and there are no speech marks.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence joins ''It was raining.'' and ''We went to the beach.'' using ''although'', and keeps the meaning?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Although it was raining, we went to the beach."},{"id":"b","text":"Although it was raining we, went to the beach."},{"id":"c","text":"It was raining although we went to the beach."},{"id":"d","text":"Although, it was raining we went to the beach."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Although it was raining'' is the dependent clause. When it comes first, put a comma after it, then the main clause.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'medium', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to make a cohesive paragraph.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Wombats are surprisingly well built for digging."},{"id":"b","text":"Their short, powerful legs and long claws let them shift soil quickly."},{"id":"c","text":"In fact, some wombat burrows are longer than a cricket pitch."},{"id":"d","text":"These tunnels keep wombats cool during hot Australian days."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Start with the topic sentence. ''Their'' refers back to wombats, ''In fact'' adds a stronger detail, and ''These tunnels'' refers back to the burrows.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'hard', 1, 'In ''The girl who won the race is my cousin'', which part is the relative clause?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The girl"},{"id":"b","text":"who won the race"},{"id":"c","text":"is my cousin"},{"id":"d","text":"the race is my cousin"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A relative clause gives more information about a noun and often starts with who, which or that: ''who won the race'' tells us which girl.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'hard', 1, '''Ella told Zara that she had won the prize.'' It is unclear who won. Which revision makes it clear that Zara won?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Ella told Zara, \"You have won the prize.\""},{"id":"b","text":"Ella told Zara that she won the prize."},{"id":"c","text":"She told her that she had won the prize."},{"id":"d","text":"Ella told Zara she had won it."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''She'' could mean Ella or Zara. Direct speech with ''You'' makes it clear that Zara is the winner.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentences use an apostrophe correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The dog wagged its tail."},{"id":"b","text":"The dog wagged it''s tail."},{"id":"c","text":"It''s going to rain."},{"id":"d","text":"The Smiths'' car is red."},{"id":"e","text":"Pie''s are $5 each."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, '''Its'' (belonging to it) has no apostrophe, ''it''s'' means it is, and the Smiths'' car belongs to the Smith family. Plain plurals like pies need no apostrophe.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_hard', 1, 'In ''When the siren sounded, the players who had been arguing finally shook hands,'' which part is the main clause?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the players who had been arguing finally shook hands"},{"id":"b","text":"When the siren sounded"},{"id":"c","text":"who had been arguing"},{"id":"d","text":"the siren sounded"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The main clause can stand alone: ''the players ... finally shook hands''. ''Who had been arguing'' sits inside it to describe the players, and ''When the siren sounded'' is a dependent clause.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_hard', 1, 'A Year 6 student is writing to the principal to ask for a school vegetable garden. Which sentence best suits this audience and purpose?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A vegetable garden would allow students to learn about science and healthy eating, so I respectfully ask you to consider it."},{"id":"b","text":"We totally need a veggie garden, it''d be awesome!"},{"id":"c","text":"Give us a vegetable garden now."},{"id":"d","text":"Vegetables are green and grow in dirt."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Writing to a principal needs a polite, formal tone, a clear request and a reason. The others are too casual, too rude or don''t persuade.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Reported: ''Coach asked whether we had packed our boots.'' Which is the direct speech version?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Coach asked, \"Have you packed your boots?\""},{"id":"b","text":"Coach asked, \"Whether we had packed our boots?\""},{"id":"c","text":"Coach asked \"have you packed your boots\"?"},{"id":"d","text":"Coach asked, have you packed your boots."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Whether'' becomes a question, ''we'' becomes ''you'', ''had packed'' becomes ''have packed'', and the question mark goes inside the speech marks.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is the subordinating conjunction in this sentence? ''We waited at the gate until the bus arrived.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"waited"},{"id":"b","text":"gate"},{"id":"c","text":"until"},{"id":"d","text":"arrived"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, '''Until'' starts a dependent clause that tells us when: until the bus arrived.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_easy', 1, '''Its'' and ''it''s'' mean the same thing.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '''It''s'' means it is or it has. ''Its'' shows belonging, like ''The koala hugged its baby.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the contraction: they will = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["they''ll","they’ll"]'::jsonb, 'Join the words and put an apostrophe where the letters ''wi'' are missing: they''ll.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'easy', 1, 'The bike belongs to James. Which is correct?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"James''s bike"},{"id":"b","text":"Jame''s bike"},{"id":"c","text":"James bike"},{"id":"d","text":"Jameses'' bike"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'James is one person, so add an apostrophe and s after his whole name: James''s bike. (Some writers use James'' bike, which is also accepted.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'easy', 1, 'Which is punctuated correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"\"Hurry up!\" shouted Coach. \"The bus is leaving.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Hurry up!\" Shouted Coach. \"The bus is leaving.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Hurry up\"! shouted Coach. \"The bus is leaving.\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"Hurry up!\" shouted Coach, \"the bus is leaving\"."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The exclamation mark goes inside the speech marks, ''shouted'' stays lower case, and ''The bus is leaving'' is a new sentence, so it starts with a capital and ends with a full stop inside the speech marks.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'easy', 1, 'Which are features of a formal letter? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"a polite greeting such as ''Dear Mr Lee''"},{"id":"b","text":"a clear purpose stated early"},{"id":"c","text":"a formal sign-off such as ''Yours sincerely''"},{"id":"d","text":"slang like ''heaps good''"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Formal letters greet politely, get to the point and sign off formally. Slang belongs in casual writing to friends.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'medium', 1, 'Jack said, "We are playing cricket today." Which sentence reports this correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Jack said that they were playing cricket that day."},{"id":"b","text":"Jack said that we are playing cricket today."},{"id":"c","text":"Jack said that \"they were playing cricket.\""},{"id":"d","text":"Jack said, they are playing cricket today."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'In reported speech, ''we'' becomes ''they'', ''are'' becomes ''were'', ''today'' becomes ''that day'', and there are no speech marks.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence correctly uses ''unless'' to join ''You will miss the bus.'' and ''You leave now.''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"You will miss the bus unless you leave now."},{"id":"b","text":"Unless you will miss the bus, you leave now."},{"id":"c","text":"You leave now unless you will miss the bus."},{"id":"d","text":"Unless, you leave now you will miss the bus."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Unless'' means ''if not''. You will miss the bus if you don''t leave now, so ''unless you leave now'' is the dependent clause.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'medium', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to make a cohesive paragraph.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Uluru is a huge sandstone rock in the Northern Territory."},{"id":"b","text":"It is sacred to the Anangu people, the Traditional Owners of the land."},{"id":"c","text":"For this reason, climbing Uluru was banned in 2019."},{"id":"d","text":"Today, visitors are invited to walk around its base instead."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Begin with the topic sentence. ''It'' refers back to Uluru, ''For this reason'' links the ban to its sacred meaning, and ''Today ... instead'' follows on from the ban.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence is written in the passive voice?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The window was broken by the cricket ball."},{"id":"b","text":"The cricket ball broke the window."},{"id":"c","text":"Sam hit the ball over the fence."},{"id":"d","text":"The ball flew high into the air."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'In the passive voice, the thing affected (the window) comes first and the doer comes after ''by''. In the active voice, the doer comes first: the ball broke the window.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'hard', 1, '''I saw the koala with binoculars.'' This could mean the koala had binoculars. Which revision makes it clear that the speaker had them?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Using binoculars, I saw the koala."},{"id":"b","text":"I saw the koala that had binoculars."},{"id":"c","text":"With binoculars, the koala was seen."},{"id":"d","text":"I saw the koala with the binoculars."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Putting ''Using binoculars'' right next to ''I'' shows the speaker was using them. The other versions are still unclear or say the koala had them.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentences use apostrophes correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The team celebrated its win."},{"id":"b","text":"The Joneses'' house is next door."},{"id":"c","text":"Your''s is the blue bag."},{"id":"d","text":"Who''s coming to the sausage sizzle?"},{"id":"e","text":"Theirs'' is the red tent."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '''Its'' and possessive pronouns like yours and theirs never take an apostrophe. ''The Joneses'' house'' belongs to the Jones family, and ''who''s'' means who is.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_hard', 1, 'In ''Even though the rain hadn''t stopped, the umpires, who were tired of waiting, restarted the match,'' which is the main clause?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the umpires restarted the match"},{"id":"b","text":"Even though the rain hadn''t stopped"},{"id":"c","text":"who were tired of waiting"},{"id":"d","text":"the rain hadn''t stopped"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Take away the dependent clause at the start and the relative clause in the middle, and what stands alone is ''the umpires restarted the match''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_hard', 1, 'A Year 6 student is giving a speech to persuade Kindergarten children to put rubbish in the bin. Which sentence best suits this audience?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"If we all put our rubbish in the bin, our playground will stay clean and the birds will stay safe!"},{"id":"b","text":"Litter constitutes a significant environmental hazard requiring immediate action."},{"id":"c","text":"Put your stuff in the bin or else."},{"id":"d","text":"Rubbish is made of many materials."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Five-year-olds need simple words, a friendly tone and a reason they care about. The second is too hard, the third is rude, and the last doesn''t persuade.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006110330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Mum asked, "Where did you leave your hat?" Which sentence reports this correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mum asked where I had left my hat."},{"id":"b","text":"Mum asked where did I leave my hat?"},{"id":"c","text":"Mum asked, where I had left my hat."},{"id":"d","text":"Mum asked where had I left my hat?"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A reported question becomes a statement: no question mark, normal word order (I had left), ''you'' becomes ''I'', and ''did leave'' moves back to ''had left''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence uses a simile?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The wind howled all night."},{"id":"b","text":"She was as quiet as a mouse."},{"id":"c","text":"The classroom was a zoo."},{"id":"d","text":"Buzz went the bee."}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'A simile compares two things using ''like'' or ''as'': as quiet as a mouse.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'medium', 1, '''The classroom was a zoo.'' What kind of figurative language is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"simile"},{"id":"b","text":"metaphor"},{"id":"c","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"d","text":"alliteration"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'It''s a metaphor: it says the classroom was a zoo, without ''like'' or ''as''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'medium', 1, 'A simile compares two things using ''like'' or ''as''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'That''s what makes it a simile, for example ''as brave as a lion''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these words are onomatopoeia? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"buzz"},{"id":"b","text":"sizzle"},{"id":"c","text":"happy"},{"id":"d","text":"crash"},{"id":"e","text":"slowly"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Onomatopoeia words sound like what they describe: buzz, sizzle and crash.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'medium', 1, '''The sun smiled down on us.'' What kind of figurative language is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"personification"},{"id":"b","text":"simile"},{"id":"c","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"d","text":"alliteration"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The sun can''t really smile. Giving human actions to things that aren''t human is personification.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is an example of onomatopoeia?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"bang"},{"id":"b","text":"table"},{"id":"c","text":"quickly"},{"id":"d","text":"blue"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Onomatopoeia is a word that sounds like the noise it names. ''Bang'' sounds like a bang.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_easy', 1, 'A metaphor says one thing IS another thing, without using ''like'' or ''as''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'For example, ''My bedroom is a pigsty'' is a metaphor. With ''like'' or ''as'', it would be a simile.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_easy', 1, '''He was as tall as a tree.'' What kind of figurative language is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"simile"},{"id":"b","text":"metaphor"},{"id":"c","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"d","text":"personification"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It compares him to a tree using ''as'', so it is a simile.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'easy', 1, '''Purple clouds rolled in like a herd of giant buffalo.'' What technique is the writer using?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"simile"},{"id":"b","text":"metaphor"},{"id":"c","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"d","text":"alliteration"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The clouds are compared to buffalo using the word ''like'', so it is a simile.', NULL, '{"title":"The Storm","text":"The afternoon had been as still as a held breath. Then, out over the ranges, the sky began to bruise. Purple clouds rolled in like a herd of giant buffalo, shouldering each other across the horizon.\n\nNina dragged the washing off the line as the first drops spat against the tin roof. Plink. Plonk. Then a thousand drummers joined in. The old gum tree by the shed groaned and waved its arms, begging the wind to leave it alone.\n\nInside, the house was a cave. The power had gone, and the candles flickered nervously on the kitchen table. Dad said the rain was heavy enough to fill the Pacific Ocean twice. Nina laughed, but she kept one eye on the window.\n\nBy morning, the storm had crept away. The paddocks sparkled, and the air smelt brand new. A magpie sat on the fence, warbling as if nothing had happened at all."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'easy', 1, 'What is the name for repeating the same first sound in words close together, as in ''slippery snakes slither silently''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"alliteration"'::jsonb, 'Repeating the first sound (here the s sound) is called alliteration.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'easy', 1, '''I''ve told you a million times!'' What kind of figurative language is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"hyperbole"},{"id":"b","text":"simile"},{"id":"c","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"d","text":"alliteration"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Nobody has said something a million times. A huge exaggeration for effect is called hyperbole.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'medium', 1, 'Which phrases from the story use personification? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"the old gum tree groaned and waved its arms"},{"id":"b","text":"the candles flickered nervously"},{"id":"c","text":"the house was a cave"},{"id":"d","text":"Plink. Plonk."},{"id":"e","text":"the storm had crept away"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, 'Trees can''t really groan and beg, candles can''t feel nervous, and storms can''t creep. These give human actions to things. ''The house was a cave'' is a metaphor and ''Plink. Plonk.'' is onomatopoeia.', NULL, '{"title":"The Storm","text":"The afternoon had been as still as a held breath. Then, out over the ranges, the sky began to bruise. Purple clouds rolled in like a herd of giant buffalo, shouldering each other across the horizon.\n\nNina dragged the washing off the line as the first drops spat against the tin roof. Plink. Plonk. Then a thousand drummers joined in. The old gum tree by the shed groaned and waved its arms, begging the wind to leave it alone.\n\nInside, the house was a cave. The power had gone, and the candles flickered nervously on the kitchen table. Dad said the rain was heavy enough to fill the Pacific Ocean twice. Nina laughed, but she kept one eye on the window.\n\nBy morning, the storm had crept away. The paddocks sparkled, and the air smelt brand new. A magpie sat on the fence, warbling as if nothing had happened at all."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'medium', 1, '''My brother is a couch potato.'' What does this metaphor mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He is lazy and sits around watching TV a lot."},{"id":"b","text":"He likes eating potatoes."},{"id":"c","text":"He is shaped like a potato."},{"id":"d","text":"He sleeps outside."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A couch potato is someone who lazes on the couch. The metaphor doesn''t mean he is really a potato.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'medium', 1, 'Add one word to turn this metaphor into a simile: Her smile was ___ sunshine.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"like"'::jsonb, 'Adding ''like'' makes a comparison: ''Her smile was like sunshine.'' Similes use ''like'' or ''as''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses personification?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The angry ocean swallowed the sandcastle."},{"id":"b","text":"The ocean was as blue as a sapphire."},{"id":"c","text":"The ocean is a giant bathtub."},{"id":"d","text":"Splash went the ocean."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'An ocean can''t feel angry or swallow, so the writer gives it human qualities. The others are a simile, a metaphor and onomatopoeia.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these sentences contain a metaphor? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Time is a thief."},{"id":"b","text":"The car was as red as a tomato."},{"id":"c","text":"Mr Baker is a walking dictionary."},{"id":"d","text":"The wind whispered through the trees."},{"id":"e","text":"Our backyard was a jungle after the rain."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'These say one thing IS another. ''As red as a tomato'' is a simile, and a whispering wind is personification.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'hard', 1, 'Which word in this sentence is onomatopoeia? ''The door creaked open slowly in the dark.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '["creaked","creak"]'::jsonb, '''Creaked'' sounds like the noise an old door makes, so it is onomatopoeia.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does the writer say the sky ''began to bruise''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The sky was turning dark purple like a bruise, hinting that something rough was coming."},{"id":"b","text":"Something had hit the sky and hurt it."},{"id":"c","text":"The sun was setting and the sky was turning orange."},{"id":"d","text":"Nina had hurt herself while the storm was coming."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A bruise is dark purple and comes after a hit. The metaphor shows the colour of the storm clouds and builds a feeling that the storm will be rough.', NULL, '{"title":"The Storm","text":"The afternoon had been as still as a held breath. Then, out over the ranges, the sky began to bruise. Purple clouds rolled in like a herd of giant buffalo, shouldering each other across the horizon.\n\nNina dragged the washing off the line as the first drops spat against the tin roof. Plink. Plonk. Then a thousand drummers joined in. The old gum tree by the shed groaned and waved its arms, begging the wind to leave it alone.\n\nInside, the house was a cave. The power had gone, and the candles flickered nervously on the kitchen table. Dad said the rain was heavy enough to fill the Pacific Ocean twice. Nina laughed, but she kept one eye on the window.\n\nBy morning, the storm had crept away. The paddocks sparkled, and the air smelt brand new. A magpie sat on the fence, warbling as if nothing had happened at all."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Name the technique in this sentence: ''Lightning is the sky''s camera flash.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"metaphor"'::jsonb, 'It says lightning IS a camera flash, without ''like'' or ''as'', so it is a metaphor.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which two techniques are both used in ''The hungry fire roared like a lion''? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"personification"},{"id":"b","text":"simile"},{"id":"c","text":"alliteration"},{"id":"d","text":"hyperbole"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'A fire can''t be hungry, so that is personification. ''Like a lion'' compares the fire to a lion, so that is a simile.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is a simile?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The puppy was as soft as a cloud."},{"id":"b","text":"The puppy is soft."},{"id":"c","text":"The puppy went ''woof''."},{"id":"d","text":"The puppy was a cloud."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A simile compares two things using ''like'' or ''as'': as soft as a cloud.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_easy', 1, '''Crunch'' is an example of onomatopoeia.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''Crunch'' sounds like the noise of biting into something crisp, so it is onomatopoeia.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is personification?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"giving human actions or feelings to something that isn''t human"},{"id":"b","text":"comparing two things using ''like'' or ''as''"},{"id":"c","text":"a word that sounds like the noise it names"},{"id":"d","text":"repeating the same first sound in nearby words"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Personification makes a thing act like a person, as in ''the trees danced in the wind''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'easy', 1, 'Grandpa says his ute is ''older than the hills''. What kind of figurative language is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"hyperbole"},{"id":"b","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"c","text":"simile"},{"id":"d","text":"alliteration"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A ute can''t really be older than hills. It is a big exaggeration to show the ute is very old, which is hyperbole.', NULL, '{"title":"Grandpa''s Ute","text":"Grandpa''s ute was older than the hills, or so he liked to tell us. It lived in the shed at the end of the driveway, sleeping under a blanket of dust.\n\nEvery Saturday he would wake it up. The engine coughed and spluttered, then growled like a grumpy old dog before it finally settled into a steady purr. Rust freckled its doors, and one headlight winked because the bulb had blown years ago.\n\nWe bounced along the dirt road to the river, the ute rattling and clanking over every rut. Grandpa drove with one elbow out the window, humming tunes that were as ancient as the ute itself.\n\nAt the river, the water was a mirror, holding the clouds perfectly still. Grandpa would switch off the engine, and the ute would sigh, tick, tick, tick, as it cooled down in the shade of the river gums.\n\n''She''s a beauty,'' Grandpa would say, patting the bonnet. To him, she was."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'easy', 1, 'Name the technique: ''Pete picked a pile of purple plums.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"alliteration"'::jsonb, 'Most of the words start with the same p sound. Repeating the first sound is alliteration.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'easy', 1, 'A word like ''whoosh'' that sounds like the noise it names is called ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["onomatopoeia","onomatopoeic"]'::jsonb, 'Words that copy sounds, like whoosh, thud and splash, are called onomatopoeia.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'medium', 1, 'Which phrases from the story use personification? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"sleeping under a blanket of dust"},{"id":"b","text":"one headlight winked"},{"id":"c","text":"the ute would sigh"},{"id":"d","text":"the water was a mirror"},{"id":"e","text":"rattling and clanking"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Sleeping, winking and sighing are things people do, so they give the ute human actions. ''The water was a mirror'' is a metaphor and ''rattling and clanking'' is onomatopoeia.', NULL, '{"title":"Grandpa''s Ute","text":"Grandpa''s ute was older than the hills, or so he liked to tell us. It lived in the shed at the end of the driveway, sleeping under a blanket of dust.\n\nEvery Saturday he would wake it up. The engine coughed and spluttered, then growled like a grumpy old dog before it finally settled into a steady purr. Rust freckled its doors, and one headlight winked because the bulb had blown years ago.\n\nWe bounced along the dirt road to the river, the ute rattling and clanking over every rut. Grandpa drove with one elbow out the window, humming tunes that were as ancient as the ute itself.\n\nAt the river, the water was a mirror, holding the clouds perfectly still. Grandpa would switch off the engine, and the ute would sigh, tick, tick, tick, as it cooled down in the shade of the river gums.\n\n''She''s a beauty,'' Grandpa would say, patting the bonnet. To him, she was."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'medium', 1, '''It''s raining cats and dogs.'' What does this saying mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It is raining very heavily."},{"id":"b","text":"Animals are falling from the sky."},{"id":"c","text":"It is only drizzling."},{"id":"d","text":"Pets don''t like the rain."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'This is an idiom. It doesn''t mean real animals. It means the rain is very heavy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'medium', 1, '''The wind was like a howling wolf'' is a metaphor.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'It uses ''like'' to compare the wind to a wolf, so it is a simile, not a metaphor.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'hard', 1, '''At the river, the water was a mirror.'' What does this tell you about the river?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It was calm and smooth enough to reflect the sky."},{"id":"b","text":"It was made of glass."},{"id":"c","text":"It was very deep and dangerous."},{"id":"d","text":"It was frozen solid."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A mirror is flat and shows reflections. The metaphor tells us the water was so still that the clouds showed in it.', NULL, '{"title":"Grandpa''s Ute","text":"Grandpa''s ute was older than the hills, or so he liked to tell us. It lived in the shed at the end of the driveway, sleeping under a blanket of dust.\n\nEvery Saturday he would wake it up. The engine coughed and spluttered, then growled like a grumpy old dog before it finally settled into a steady purr. Rust freckled its doors, and one headlight winked because the bulb had blown years ago.\n\nWe bounced along the dirt road to the river, the ute rattling and clanking over every rut. Grandpa drove with one elbow out the window, humming tunes that were as ancient as the ute itself.\n\nAt the river, the water was a mirror, holding the clouds perfectly still. Grandpa would switch off the engine, and the ute would sigh, tick, tick, tick, as it cooled down in the shade of the river gums.\n\n''She''s a beauty,'' Grandpa would say, patting the bonnet. To him, she was."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120131', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'hard', 1, 'Put the words in order to make a simile.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"The"},{"id":"b","text":"moon"},{"id":"c","text":"hung"},{"id":"d","text":"like"},{"id":"e","text":"a"},{"id":"f","text":"silver"},{"id":"g","text":"coin."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e","f","g"]'::jsonb, '''The'' has the capital and ''coin.'' has the full stop: The moon hung like a silver coin.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120132', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses a metaphor to show that Mia is very brave?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mia was a lion on the rugby field."},{"id":"b","text":"Mia was as brave as a lion."},{"id":"c","text":"Mia was brave."},{"id":"d","text":"Mia roared at the crowd."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Mia was a lion'' says she IS a lion, without ''like'' or ''as''. ''As brave as a lion'' is a simile.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120133', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_hard', 1, 'A writer wants readers to feel that a forest is scary. Which sentence does this best?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Twisted branches clawed at the darkening sky."},{"id":"b","text":"The tall trees swayed in the breeze."},{"id":"c","text":"The forest was as green as grass."},{"id":"d","text":"Birds tweeted happily in the trees."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Clawed'' makes the branches seem like a monster''s claws, and ''darkening'' adds a gloomy mood. The others feel calm or happy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120134', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Name the technique in this sentence: ''The streetlights blinked awake one by one.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"personification"'::jsonb, 'Streetlights can''t blink or wake up like a person, so this is personification.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006120135', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these sentences use hyperbole? Choose all that apply.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"I''m so hungry I could eat a horse."},{"id":"b","text":"This homework will take a hundred years!"},{"id":"c","text":"The cake was delicious."},{"id":"d","text":"My backpack weighs more than an elephant."},{"id":"e","text":"The dog ran quickly."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'These are huge exaggerations nobody means literally. ''The cake was delicious'' and ''The dog ran quickly'' are plain statements.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"definately"},{"id":"b","text":"definitely"},{"id":"c","text":"definitly"},{"id":"d","text":"defanitely"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Definitely'' has the word ''finite'' hiding inside it: de-finite-ly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_easy', 1, 'The w in ''wrestle'' is silent.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'We say ''ressle'', so the w is silent. The t is silent too.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the plural of ''knife''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"knives"'::jsonb, 'Many words ending in f or fe change to v and add -es: knife becomes knives.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'easy', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''The school ___ spoke at assembly.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"principle"},{"id":"b","text":"principal"},{"id":"c","text":"principel"},{"id":"d","text":"prinsipal"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The principal is the head of a school. A principle is a rule or belief. Tip: the principal is your pal.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'easy', 1, 'Which words drop the final e before adding -able? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"lovable"},{"id":"b","text":"usable"},{"id":"c","text":"noticeable"},{"id":"d","text":"adorable"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'love, use and adore drop the e. ''Noticeable'' keeps the e so the c still sounds like s.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'easy', 1, 'Adding the prefix mis- to ''spell'' makes the word ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"misspell"'::jsonb, 'A prefix doesn''t change the base word, so mis + spell keeps both s''s: misspell.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'medium', 1, 'What is the plural of ''crisis''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"crisises"},{"id":"b","text":"crises"},{"id":"c","text":"crisis''s"},{"id":"d","text":"crisi"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Words ending in -is from Greek change to -es in the plural: crisis becomes crises, like oasis becomes oases.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'medium', 1, 'The Latin root ''aud'' means hear. Which word means ''able to be heard''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"audible"},{"id":"b","text":"auditorium"},{"id":"c","text":"audience"},{"id":"d","text":"audition"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'aud (hear) + ible (able to be) = audible. An auditorium is a place, an audience is people, and an audition is a tryout.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'medium', 1, 'Add the suffix -ly to ''gentle''. Write the new word.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"gently"'::jsonb, 'When a word ends in -le, replace the e with y: gentle becomes gently, like simple becomes simply.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'hard', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''The medicine had no ___ on my cough.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"effect"},{"id":"b","text":"affect"},{"id":"c","text":"affeckt"},{"id":"d","text":"efect"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Here we need a noun (the result), which is ''effect''. ''Affect'' is usually a verb: the cold can affect you.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'hard', 1, 'Put these word parts in order to build the word ''disagreements''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"dis"},{"id":"b","text":"agree"},{"id":"c","text":"ment"},{"id":"d","text":"s"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Prefix first, then the base word, then the suffixes: dis + agree + ment + s.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'hard', 1, 'In ''occurred'', the r is doubled because ''occur'' ends in one vowel and one consonant, and the stress is on the last syllable.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Say oc-CUR: the stress is at the end, so the final r doubles before -ed. Compare ''visited'', where the stress is at the start.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these words are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"accommodation"},{"id":"b","text":"embarrass"},{"id":"c","text":"conscience"},{"id":"d","text":"privelege"},{"id":"e","text":"mischievous"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Accommodation has double c and double m, embarrass has double r and double s, and conscience has ''science'' in it. The correct spelling is ''privilege''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does ''magician'' end in -cian instead of -tion?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It names a person with a skill, and the base word ''magic'' ends in c."},{"id":"b","text":"All words about tricks end in -cian."},{"id":"c","text":"It is a mistake; it should be ''magition''."},{"id":"d","text":"-cian is used for every word with three syllables."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '-cian is used for people''s jobs or skills when the base word ends in c: magic → magician, music → musician, electric → electrician.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_hard', 1, 'The Latin root ''bene'' means good and ''fact'' means do. A person who does good by giving money to help others is a ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"benefactor"'::jsonb, 'bene (good) + fact (do) + or (a person who) = benefactor.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"enviroment"},{"id":"b","text":"environment"},{"id":"c","text":"envirnment"},{"id":"d","text":"enviornment"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Environment'' is the word ''environ'' plus the suffix -ment. Say it slowly, en-vi-ron-ment, so you don''t lose the n.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_easy', 1, 'The h in ''honest'' is silent.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'We say ''onest'', so the h is silent, just like in ''hour'' and ''honour''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the plural of ''thief''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"thieves"'::jsonb, 'Many words ending in f change the f to v and add -es: thief becomes thieves, like shelf becomes shelves.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'easy', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''Please ___ my apology.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"except"},{"id":"b","text":"accept"},{"id":"c","text":"acept"},{"id":"d","text":"exept"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '''Accept'' means to receive or agree to something. ''Except'' means not including: everyone except Sam.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'easy', 1, 'Which words keep the final e of the base word when -ment is added? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"excitement"},{"id":"b","text":"amazement"},{"id":"c","text":"argument"},{"id":"d","text":"management"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '-ment starts with a consonant, so excite, amaze and manage keep their e. ''Argument'' is a well-known exception: argue drops its e.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'easy', 1, 'Adding the prefix dis- to ''satisfied'' makes the word ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"dissatisfied"'::jsonb, 'A prefix doesn''t change the base word, so dis + satisfied keeps both s''s: dissatisfied.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'medium', 1, 'What is the plural of ''larva'' (a young insect)?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"larvas"},{"id":"b","text":"larvae"},{"id":"c","text":"larvies"},{"id":"d","text":"larvi"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Some words from Latin ending in -a change to -ae in the plural: larva becomes larvae, like antenna becomes antennae.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'medium', 1, 'The Greek root ''chron'' means time. Which word means ''arranged in the order that things happened in time''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"chronological"},{"id":"b","text":"chorus"},{"id":"c","text":"chlorine"},{"id":"d","text":"character"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'chron (time) + logical = chronological. A timeline is in chronological order. The other words only look similar.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'medium', 1, 'Add the suffix -ous to ''fame''. Write the new word.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"famous"'::jsonb, 'The suffix -ous starts with a vowel, so drop the e from ''fame'': fame + ous = famous.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'hard', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''The car remained ___ at the red light.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"stationary"},{"id":"b","text":"stationery"},{"id":"c","text":"stationairy"},{"id":"d","text":"stashionary"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Stationary'' with an a means not moving. ''Stationery'' with an e means paper and pens. Tip: e for envelopes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'hard', 1, 'Put these word parts in order to build the word ''internationally''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"inter"},{"id":"b","text":"nation"},{"id":"c","text":"al"},{"id":"d","text":"ly"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Prefix first, then the base word, then the suffixes: inter (between) + nation + al + ly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'hard', 1, 'The word ''weird'' follows the rule ''i before e except after c''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '''Weird'' has e before i with no c in front, so it breaks the rule. Other exceptions include ''their'', ''seize'' and ''protein''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these words are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"conscientious"},{"id":"b","text":"guarantee"},{"id":"c","text":"queue"},{"id":"d","text":"occassion"},{"id":"e","text":"parliament"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Conscientious has ''science'' inside it, guarantee has a silent u, queue has ''ueue'', and parliament has a hidden i. The correct spelling is ''occasion'', with two c''s and one s.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does ''courageous'' keep the e from ''courage'' before adding -ous?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The e keeps the g soft, so it still sounds like j."},{"id":"b","text":"Every word ending in -ous keeps an e."},{"id":"c","text":"It is a mistake; it should be ''couragous''."},{"id":"d","text":"Words with three syllables always keep the e."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Without the e, ''gou'' would make a hard g sound, as in ''gout''. Keeping the e keeps the soft j sound, like ''outrageous'' and ''gorgeous''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006130130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061301', 'very_hard', 1, 'The Latin root ''manu'' means hand and ''script'' means write. A document written by hand is a ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"manuscript"'::jsonb, 'manu (hand) + script (write) = manuscript. The same ''manu'' is in manual, meaning done by hand.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the same as ''reluctant''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"unwilling"},{"id":"b","text":"eager"},{"id":"c","text":"angry"},{"id":"d","text":"quick"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'If you are reluctant to do something, you don''t really want to: you are unwilling.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Something ''transparent'' can be seen through.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Glass and clear plastic are transparent: light passes through them so you can see what''s behind.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the opposite of ''temporary''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"permanent"},{"id":"b","text":"short"},{"id":"c","text":"brief"},{"id":"d","text":"quick"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Temporary means lasting only a short time. Its opposite is permanent, lasting for ever.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'easy', 1, '''The evidence was conclusive, so the case was closed.'' What does ''conclusive'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"proving something without doubt"},{"id":"b","text":"confusing"},{"id":"c","text":"missing"},{"id":"d","text":"very old"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The case was closed because the evidence settled it. Conclusive means it proves something so there is no doubt left.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'easy', 1, 'The prefix ''anti-'' means against. Write the word that means ''against bacteria''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["antibacterial","anti-bacterial"]'::jsonb, 'anti (against) + bacterial = antibacterial, like antibacterial hand wash.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'easy', 1, 'Which words mean ''to make something smaller or less''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"diminish"},{"id":"b","text":"reduce"},{"id":"c","text":"amplify"},{"id":"d","text":"lessen"},{"id":"e","text":"expand"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Diminish, reduce and lessen all mean make smaller. Amplify and expand mean make bigger.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from least angry to most angry.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"irritated"},{"id":"b","text":"angry"},{"id":"c","text":"furious"},{"id":"d","text":"livid"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Irritated is a little cross, angry is stronger, furious is very angry, and livid is so angry you might shake.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'medium', 1, 'In geography, what does ''erosion'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the wearing away of land by water, wind or ice"},{"id":"b","text":"the building of new mountains"},{"id":"c","text":"the planting of trees on bare land"},{"id":"d","text":"a sudden shaking of the ground"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Erosion is when water, wind or ice slowly wear away soil and rock, like waves wearing away a cliff.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'medium', 1, 'In which sentence does ''current'' mean a flow of water?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The swimmer was pulled out by a strong current."},{"id":"b","text":"Read the current issue of the magazine."},{"id":"c","text":"Our current teacher is Mr Jones."},{"id":"d","text":"Check the current time."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Only the swimming sentence is about moving water. In the others, ''current'' means happening now.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'hard', 1, '''Photo'' means light and ''synthesis'' means putting together. What is photosynthesis?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"how plants use light to make their own food"},{"id":"b","text":"taking photographs of plants"},{"id":"c","text":"how animals see in the dark"},{"id":"d","text":"the way light bounces off water"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'photo (light) + synthesis (putting together): plants use light energy to put together food from water and carbon dioxide.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'hard', 1, '''Legible'' means able to be read. Handwriting that cannot be read is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"illegible"'::jsonb, 'The prefix in- (meaning not) becomes il- before an l: il + legible = illegible.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'hard', 1, 'Which word most precisely describes a scientist who checks every tiny detail very carefully?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"meticulous"},{"id":"b","text":"careless"},{"id":"c","text":"nice"},{"id":"d","text":"busy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Meticulous means showing great care and attention to detail. ''Nice'' and ''busy'' are too vague.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_hard', 1, '''The mayor''s answer was ambiguous; reporters could not tell whether she supported the plan.'' What does ''ambiguous'' mean here?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"able to be understood in more than one way"},{"id":"b","text":"very angry"},{"id":"c","text":"completely clear"},{"id":"d","text":"very long"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The reporters couldn''t tell what she meant, so her answer could be read more than one way. That is ambiguous.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_hard', 1, 'The Latin root ''spect'' means look. In which words does it carry that meaning? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"inspect"},{"id":"b","text":"spectator"},{"id":"c","text":"spectacles"},{"id":"d","text":"speckled"},{"id":"e","text":"special"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'To inspect is to look closely, a spectator looks on, and spectacles help you look. Speckled and special just share some letters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_hard', 1, '''Humpback whales migrate north each winter.'' Which is the most precise meaning of ''migrate'' here?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"travel from one region to another at a particular time of year"},{"id":"b","text":"swim fast"},{"id":"c","text":"move house permanently"},{"id":"d","text":"go somewhere once by accident"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The whales go north ''each winter'', so the move is seasonal and repeated. That is what migrate means for animals.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the same as ''abundant''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"plentiful"},{"id":"b","text":"scarce"},{"id":"c","text":"tiny"},{"id":"d","text":"empty"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'If something is abundant, there is lots of it, like an abundant harvest. The closest word is plentiful.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_easy', 1, '''Hostile'' means friendly and welcoming.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '''Hostile'' means unfriendly or aggressive, like a hostile crowd booing the umpire.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word means the opposite of ''optimistic''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"pessimistic"},{"id":"b","text":"hopeful"},{"id":"c","text":"cheerful"},{"id":"d","text":"positive"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'An optimistic person expects good things to happen. A pessimistic person expects the worst.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'easy', 1, '''The bushwalkers were cautious near the cliff edge, testing each step before trusting it.'' What does ''cautious'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"careful to avoid danger"},{"id":"b","text":"careless"},{"id":"c","text":"in a hurry"},{"id":"d","text":"excited"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Testing each step near a cliff shows they were being careful. Cautious means taking care to avoid danger.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'easy', 1, 'The prefix ''inter-'' means between. Write the word that means ''between nations''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"international"'::jsonb, 'inter (between) + national = international, like an international cricket match between Australia and India.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'easy', 1, 'Which words mean ''very clever''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"brilliant"},{"id":"b","text":"ingenious"},{"id":"c","text":"dull"},{"id":"d","text":"gifted"},{"id":"e","text":"clumsy"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Brilliant, ingenious and gifted all describe great cleverness or talent. Dull and clumsy do not.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from quietest to loudest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"silent"},{"id":"b","text":"murmuring"},{"id":"c","text":"loud"},{"id":"d","text":"deafening"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Silent has no sound, murmuring is a soft low sound, loud is easy to hear, and deafening is so loud it could hurt your ears.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'medium', 1, 'In science, what does ''habitat'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the natural home where a plant or animal lives"},{"id":"b","text":"the food an animal eats"},{"id":"c","text":"a group of the same kind of animal"},{"id":"d","text":"the way an animal moves"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A habitat is the place that gives a living thing what it needs to survive, like a tree hollow and forest for a sugar glider.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'medium', 1, 'In which sentence does ''pitch'' mean the strip of ground in the middle of a cricket field?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The groundskeeper rolled the pitch flat before the match."},{"id":"b","text":"She can sing every note at the right pitch."},{"id":"c","text":"We helped Dad pitch the tent."},{"id":"d","text":"The night was pitch black."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Only the groundskeeper sentence is about cricket ground. In the others, pitch means how high a sound is, to set up, or completely.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'hard', 1, '''Omni'' means all and ''-vore'' means eater. What is an omnivore?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"an animal that eats both plants and meat"},{"id":"b","text":"an animal that eats only plants"},{"id":"c","text":"an animal that eats only meat"},{"id":"d","text":"an animal that eats only insects"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'omni (all) + vore (eater): an omnivore eats all kinds of food, plants and meat. Humans and possums are omnivores.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'hard', 1, '''Responsible'' means able to be trusted to do the right thing. A person who cannot be trusted to act sensibly is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"irresponsible"'::jsonb, 'The prefix in- (meaning not) becomes ir- before an r: ir + responsible = irresponsible, with a double r.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'hard', 1, 'Which word most precisely describes a speaker who expresses ideas clearly and fluently?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"articulate"},{"id":"b","text":"loud"},{"id":"c","text":"talkative"},{"id":"d","text":"nice"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Articulate means able to put ideas into clear, well-chosen words. A loud or talkative person isn''t always clear.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_hard', 1, '''Despite losing their captain to injury, the team remained resilient and won their next three games.'' What does ''resilient'' mean here?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"able to recover quickly from difficulties"},{"id":"b","text":"very tall and strong"},{"id":"c","text":"unwilling to play"},{"id":"d","text":"easily upset"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The team had a setback but bounced back to win. Being able to recover quickly from difficulties is being resilient.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_hard', 1, 'The Latin root ''struct'' means build. In which words does it carry that meaning? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"construct"},{"id":"b","text":"structure"},{"id":"c","text":"destruction"},{"id":"d","text":"strict"},{"id":"e","text":"struggle"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'To construct is to build, a structure is something built, and destruction is un-building or wrecking. Strict and struggle only share some letters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006140130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061401', 'very_hard', 1, '''The drought had a profound effect on the town; many families left and the school lost half its students.'' What does ''profound'' mean here?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"very great and far-reaching"},{"id":"b","text":"small and unimportant"},{"id":"c","text":"funny and surprising"},{"id":"d","text":"short and quick"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Families leaving and the school halving show a huge, deep change. Profound means very great or intense.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Why was Kirra''s father worried at the start of the story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The supply boat was late."},{"id":"b","text":"The lighthouse was broken."},{"id":"c","text":"Kirra was lost."},{"id":"d","text":"It was too hot."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first paragraph says the supply boat ''failed to appear'', so he paced the jetty checking his watch.', NULL, '{"title":"Good Eyes","text":"Nobody on Montague Island could remember the last time the supply boat had been late. So when it failed to appear on Friday morning, Kirra''s father paced the jetty, checking his watch every few minutes.\n\nBy afternoon, the wind had swung to the south and the sea had turned the colour of slate. Kirra watched her father climb the lighthouse stairs two at a time. She knew what he was thinking, though he would never say it aloud in front of her.\n\nJust after six, as the light began its slow sweep across the water, Kirra spotted something: a tiny orange flare, low on the horizon, then another.\n\n\"Dad!\" she shouted up the stairwell. \"Flares! North-east!\"\n\nHer father was already reaching for the radio. Within an hour, the rescue boat from Narooma had found the supply boat drifting with a broken engine, its crew cold, soaked and very relieved.\n\nThat night, Kirra''s father made her a hot chocolate and said only, \"Good eyes.\" But he kept his hand on her shoulder for a long time."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_easy', 1, 'What do microbats use to find insects in the dark?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"echolocation"'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says microbats hunt using echolocation: they listen for the echoes of their calls.', NULL, '{"title":"Small Bats, Big Help","text":"When most people picture an Australian bat, they imagine a large flying-fox hanging in a fig tree. Yet most of Australia''s bat species are microbats, tiny creatures that can weigh less than a twenty-cent coin.\n\nMicrobats hunt at night using echolocation. A microbat sends out high-pitched calls, too high for humans to hear, and listens for the echoes that bounce back. From these echoes it can work out the size, shape and distance of an insect, even in total darkness.\n\nThis skill makes microbats valuable to farmers and city dwellers alike. A single microbat can eat hundreds of mosquitoes and moths in one night, reducing pests without chemicals.\n\nUnfortunately, many microbat species are under threat. Old trees with hollows, where microbats roost during the day, are often cleared for housing and farming. Some councils now encourage residents to put up bat boxes, which act as artificial hollows.\n\nProtecting microbats, then, is not just good for bats. It is good for anyone who would rather not be bitten by a mosquito."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_easy', 1, 'The student council decided that students could bring their phones on camp.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The last paragraph says phones will stay at home. Students may bring a disposable camera instead.', NULL, '{"title":"Should Students Bring Phones on Camp?","text":"Each year, Year 6 at Riverside Public School spends three days at a camp in the Blue Mountains. This year, the student council was asked whether students should be allowed to bring phones.\n\nSupporters argue that phones help students keep in touch with family, especially students who are nervous about being away from home for the first time. They also point out that phones have cameras, so students can record memories of the trip.\n\nOthers disagree. Teachers note that the camp has a landline and that staff can contact families at any time. They worry that phones would distract students from the activities, such as abseiling and bushwalking, that make camp special. Some parents are also concerned that expensive devices could be lost or broken.\n\nAfter weighing up both sides, the student council recommended a compromise: students may bring a disposable camera, but phones will stay at home. Families will receive a daily update and photos through the school app."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'easy', 1, '''She knew what he was thinking, though he would never say it aloud in front of her.'' What was he most likely thinking?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"that the supply boat might be in trouble at sea"},{"id":"b","text":"that it was time for dinner"},{"id":"c","text":"that the lighthouse needed painting"},{"id":"d","text":"that Kirra should do her homework"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The boat was late and a storm was coming in, so he was probably afraid it was in danger. He didn''t say it so Kirra wouldn''t worry.', NULL, '{"title":"Good Eyes","text":"Nobody on Montague Island could remember the last time the supply boat had been late. So when it failed to appear on Friday morning, Kirra''s father paced the jetty, checking his watch every few minutes.\n\nBy afternoon, the wind had swung to the south and the sea had turned the colour of slate. Kirra watched her father climb the lighthouse stairs two at a time. She knew what he was thinking, though he would never say it aloud in front of her.\n\nJust after six, as the light began its slow sweep across the water, Kirra spotted something: a tiny orange flare, low on the horizon, then another.\n\n\"Dad!\" she shouted up the stairwell. \"Flares! North-east!\"\n\nHer father was already reaching for the radio. Within an hour, the rescue boat from Narooma had found the supply boat drifting with a broken engine, its crew cold, soaked and very relieved.\n\nThat night, Kirra''s father made her a hot chocolate and said only, \"Good eyes.\" But he kept his hand on her shoulder for a long time."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'easy', 1, 'Which are arguments FOR letting students bring phones? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Students can keep in touch with family."},{"id":"b","text":"Students can take photos of the trip."},{"id":"c","text":"Phones might distract students from activities."},{"id":"d","text":"Expensive phones could be lost or broken."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Paragraph two gives the supporters'' reasons: keeping in touch and recording memories. The other two are arguments against.', NULL, '{"title":"Should Students Bring Phones on Camp?","text":"Each year, Year 6 at Riverside Public School spends three days at a camp in the Blue Mountains. This year, the student council was asked whether students should be allowed to bring phones.\n\nSupporters argue that phones help students keep in touch with family, especially students who are nervous about being away from home for the first time. They also point out that phones have cameras, so students can record memories of the trip.\n\nOthers disagree. Teachers note that the camp has a landline and that staff can contact families at any time. They worry that phones would distract students from the activities, such as abseiling and bushwalking, that make camp special. Some parents are also concerned that expensive devices could be lost or broken.\n\nAfter weighing up both sides, the student council recommended a compromise: students may bring a disposable camera, but phones will stay at home. Families will receive a daily update and photos through the school app."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'easy', 1, 'Put these building stages in the order they happened.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Foundations and pylons were built."},{"id":"b","text":"Two halves of the arch were built out from each shore."},{"id":"c","text":"The two halves of the arch met in the middle."},{"id":"d","text":"The road deck was hung from the arch."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The signal words First, Next, In August 1930 and Finally show the order of the stages.', NULL, '{"title":"Building the Harbour Bridge","text":"The Sydney Harbour Bridge, opened in 1932, is one of the most famous steel arch bridges in the world. Building it was a remarkable feat of engineering.\n\nFirst, workers dug deep foundations on both sides of the harbour and built huge granite-faced pylons. Surprisingly, the pylons are mostly decorative; the real weight of the bridge rests on giant steel pins at the base of the arch.\n\nNext, the two halves of the arch were built outwards from each shore at the same time. Because there was nothing underneath to hold them up, each half was held back by strong steel cables fixed into tunnels dug in the rock behind it.\n\nAs the halves crept closer, engineers checked their measurements every day. Heat from the sun made the steel expand, so the gap between the halves changed size from morning to afternoon. In August 1930, the cables were slowly let out and the two halves met in the middle.\n\nFinally, the road deck was hung from the arch, and trains, cars and people could at last cross the harbour without a ferry."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'medium', 1, 'What is the main idea of the third paragraph?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Microbats help people by eating pests."},{"id":"b","text":"Microbats are smaller than flying-foxes."},{"id":"c","text":"Microbats sleep in tree hollows."},{"id":"d","text":"Farmers use chemicals to kill moths."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The paragraph says microbats are valuable because one can eat hundreds of mosquitoes and moths a night. That is the main idea.', NULL, '{"title":"Small Bats, Big Help","text":"When most people picture an Australian bat, they imagine a large flying-fox hanging in a fig tree. Yet most of Australia''s bat species are microbats, tiny creatures that can weigh less than a twenty-cent coin.\n\nMicrobats hunt at night using echolocation. A microbat sends out high-pitched calls, too high for humans to hear, and listens for the echoes that bounce back. From these echoes it can work out the size, shape and distance of an insect, even in total darkness.\n\nThis skill makes microbats valuable to farmers and city dwellers alike. A single microbat can eat hundreds of mosquitoes and moths in one night, reducing pests without chemicals.\n\nUnfortunately, many microbat species are under threat. Old trees with hollows, where microbats roost during the day, are often cleared for housing and farming. Some councils now encourage residents to put up bat boxes, which act as artificial hollows.\n\nProtecting microbats, then, is not just good for bats. It is good for anyone who would rather not be bitten by a mosquito."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'medium', 1, 'Why did Sofia most likely give three different answers about why she moved?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She didn''t want to share the real reason with people she didn''t know."},{"id":"b","text":"She couldn''t remember why she moved."},{"id":"c","text":"She was trying to be funny."},{"id":"d","text":"She had moved three times."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'She also turned her jumper inside out to hide her old school. She seems to want to keep her past private, so she avoided the real answer.', NULL, '{"title":"The Sketchbook","text":"On her first day at Lithgow Public, Sofia wore her old school jumper inside out so nobody would see the crest. It didn''t work. By recess, three people had asked her why she''d moved, and she had given three different answers.\n\nAt lunch she sat on the bench near the library, pretending to read. A boy with paint on his fingers sat down at the other end. He didn''t say anything. He just opened a sketchbook and started drawing the gum tree in front of them.\n\nAfter a while, Sofia leaned over. \"The branch on the left is wrong,\" she said. \"It bends the other way.\"\n\nThe boy looked up at the tree, then at his drawing, and laughed. \"You''re right.\" He tore out a page and handed it to her with a pencil. \"Show me.\"\n\nThe bell rang twenty minutes later. Sofia was surprised. She had forgotten to check the time even once."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'medium', 1, 'Put the parts of this text in the order they appear.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"background about the camp"},{"id":"b","text":"arguments for bringing phones"},{"id":"c","text":"arguments against bringing phones"},{"id":"d","text":"the council''s decision"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'This is a discussion text: it sets the scene, gives one side, then the other side, and ends with a recommendation.', NULL, '{"title":"Should Students Bring Phones on Camp?","text":"Each year, Year 6 at Riverside Public School spends three days at a camp in the Blue Mountains. This year, the student council was asked whether students should be allowed to bring phones.\n\nSupporters argue that phones help students keep in touch with family, especially students who are nervous about being away from home for the first time. They also point out that phones have cameras, so students can record memories of the trip.\n\nOthers disagree. Teachers note that the camp has a landline and that staff can contact families at any time. They worry that phones would distract students from the activities, such as abseiling and bushwalking, that make camp special. Some parents are also concerned that expensive devices could be lost or broken.\n\nAfter weighing up both sides, the student council recommended a compromise: students may bring a disposable camera, but phones will stay at home. Families will receive a daily update and photos through the school app."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'hard', 1, 'What does the last sentence suggest about Kirra''s father?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He is proud and grateful, but shows it through actions more than words."},{"id":"b","text":"He is angry that Kirra shouted."},{"id":"c","text":"He is too tired to talk."},{"id":"d","text":"He doesn''t care what Kirra did."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'He only says ''Good eyes'', but keeping his hand on her shoulder ''for a long time'' shows deep feelings he doesn''t put into words.', NULL, '{"title":"Good Eyes","text":"Nobody on Montague Island could remember the last time the supply boat had been late. So when it failed to appear on Friday morning, Kirra''s father paced the jetty, checking his watch every few minutes.\n\nBy afternoon, the wind had swung to the south and the sea had turned the colour of slate. Kirra watched her father climb the lighthouse stairs two at a time. She knew what he was thinking, though he would never say it aloud in front of her.\n\nJust after six, as the light began its slow sweep across the water, Kirra spotted something: a tiny orange flare, low on the horizon, then another.\n\n\"Dad!\" she shouted up the stairwell. \"Flares! North-east!\"\n\nHer father was already reaching for the radio. Within an hour, the rescue boat from Narooma had found the supply boat drifting with a broken engine, its crew cold, soaked and very relieved.\n\nThat night, Kirra''s father made her a hot chocolate and said only, \"Good eyes.\" But he kept his hand on her shoulder for a long time."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'hard', 1, '''She had forgotten to check the time even once.'' What does this suggest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She was enjoying herself and no longer felt so alone."},{"id":"b","text":"Her watch was broken."},{"id":"c","text":"She wanted to be late for class."},{"id":"d","text":"She didn''t hear the bell."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'When we are bored or uncomfortable we keep checking the time. Forgetting to shows she was absorbed and happy drawing with the boy.', NULL, '{"title":"The Sketchbook","text":"On her first day at Lithgow Public, Sofia wore her old school jumper inside out so nobody would see the crest. It didn''t work. By recess, three people had asked her why she''d moved, and she had given three different answers.\n\nAt lunch she sat on the bench near the library, pretending to read. A boy with paint on his fingers sat down at the other end. He didn''t say anything. He just opened a sketchbook and started drawing the gum tree in front of them.\n\nAfter a while, Sofia leaned over. \"The branch on the left is wrong,\" she said. \"It bends the other way.\"\n\nThe boy looked up at the tree, then at his drawing, and laughed. \"You''re right.\" He tore out a page and handed it to her with a pencil. \"Show me.\"\n\nThe bell rang twenty minutes later. Sofia was surprised. She had forgotten to check the time even once."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'hard', 1, 'Why did the engineers need to check their measurements every day?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The sun''s heat made the steel expand, so the gap kept changing."},{"id":"b","text":"The cables kept snapping."},{"id":"c","text":"Workers kept losing their rulers."},{"id":"d","text":"The harbour water was rising."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The text explains that heat made the steel expand, so the gap was a different size in the morning and the afternoon.', NULL, '{"title":"Building the Harbour Bridge","text":"The Sydney Harbour Bridge, opened in 1932, is one of the most famous steel arch bridges in the world. Building it was a remarkable feat of engineering.\n\nFirst, workers dug deep foundations on both sides of the harbour and built huge granite-faced pylons. Surprisingly, the pylons are mostly decorative; the real weight of the bridge rests on giant steel pins at the base of the arch.\n\nNext, the two halves of the arch were built outwards from each shore at the same time. Because there was nothing underneath to hold them up, each half was held back by strong steel cables fixed into tunnels dug in the rock behind it.\n\nAs the halves crept closer, engineers checked their measurements every day. Heat from the sun made the steel expand, so the gap between the halves changed size from morning to afternoon. In August 1930, the cables were slowly let out and the two halves met in the middle.\n\nFinally, the road deck was hung from the arch, and trains, cars and people could at last cross the harbour without a ferry."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which best sums up the author''s purpose in this text?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to inform readers about microbats and persuade them that microbats are worth protecting"},{"id":"b","text":"to entertain readers with a story about a bat"},{"id":"c","text":"to explain how to build a bat box"},{"id":"d","text":"to warn readers that bats are dangerous"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Most of the text gives facts, but the last part argues that protecting microbats is ''good for anyone''. It informs and persuades.', NULL, '{"title":"Small Bats, Big Help","text":"When most people picture an Australian bat, they imagine a large flying-fox hanging in a fig tree. Yet most of Australia''s bat species are microbats, tiny creatures that can weigh less than a twenty-cent coin.\n\nMicrobats hunt at night using echolocation. A microbat sends out high-pitched calls, too high for humans to hear, and listens for the echoes that bounce back. From these echoes it can work out the size, shape and distance of an insect, even in total darkness.\n\nThis skill makes microbats valuable to farmers and city dwellers alike. A single microbat can eat hundreds of mosquitoes and moths in one night, reducing pests without chemicals.\n\nUnfortunately, many microbat species are under threat. Old trees with hollows, where microbats roost during the day, are often cleared for housing and farming. Some councils now encourage residents to put up bat boxes, which act as artificial hollows.\n\nProtecting microbats, then, is not just good for bats. It is good for anyone who would rather not be bitten by a mosquito."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which details suggest Sofia was trying to hide or avoid attention at first? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"She wore her jumper inside out."},{"id":"b","text":"She gave three different answers."},{"id":"c","text":"She sat pretending to read."},{"id":"d","text":"She told the boy his drawing was wrong."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Hiding the crest, dodging questions and pretending to read all keep people away. Speaking up about the drawing is the moment she stops hiding.', NULL, '{"title":"The Sketchbook","text":"On her first day at Lithgow Public, Sofia wore her old school jumper inside out so nobody would see the crest. It didn''t work. By recess, three people had asked her why she''d moved, and she had given three different answers.\n\nAt lunch she sat on the bench near the library, pretending to read. A boy with paint on his fingers sat down at the other end. He didn''t say anything. He just opened a sketchbook and started drawing the gum tree in front of them.\n\nAfter a while, Sofia leaned over. \"The branch on the left is wrong,\" she said. \"It bends the other way.\"\n\nThe boy looked up at the tree, then at his drawing, and laughed. \"You''re right.\" He tore out a page and handed it to her with a pencil. \"Show me.\"\n\nThe bell rang twenty minutes later. Sofia was surprised. She had forgotten to check the time even once."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which statement would the author agree with?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The pylons look strong, but they do not carry the main weight of the bridge."},{"id":"b","text":"The arch was built from one side of the harbour to the other."},{"id":"c","text":"The road deck was built before the arch."},{"id":"d","text":"The cables held up the bridge after it was finished."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Paragraph two says the pylons are ''mostly decorative'' and the weight rests on steel pins. The other statements contradict the text.', NULL, '{"title":"Building the Harbour Bridge","text":"The Sydney Harbour Bridge, opened in 1932, is one of the most famous steel arch bridges in the world. Building it was a remarkable feat of engineering.\n\nFirst, workers dug deep foundations on both sides of the harbour and built huge granite-faced pylons. Surprisingly, the pylons are mostly decorative; the real weight of the bridge rests on giant steel pins at the base of the arch.\n\nNext, the two halves of the arch were built outwards from each shore at the same time. Because there was nothing underneath to hold them up, each half was held back by strong steel cables fixed into tunnels dug in the rock behind it.\n\nAs the halves crept closer, engineers checked their measurements every day. Heat from the sun made the steel expand, so the gap between the halves changed size from morning to afternoon. In August 1930, the cables were slowly let out and the two halves met in the middle.\n\nFinally, the road deck was hung from the arch, and trains, cars and people could at last cross the harbour without a ferry."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many runs did Kurrajong need at the start of the story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"six"},{"id":"b","text":"four"},{"id":"c","text":"two"},{"id":"d","text":"one"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The very first words are ''Six runs. That was all Kurrajong needed to win the final.''', NULL, '{"title":"The Last Over","text":"Six runs. That was all Kurrajong needed to win the final. They had one wicket left, and Amira had the ball.\n\nShe walked back to her mark slowly, rubbing the ball on her trousers until one side shone. Behind the stumps, her brother Zaid crouched low and banged his gloves together. The crowd at the Wagga oval had gone strangely quiet.\n\nHer first ball was a full toss. The batter swung and missed. Her second was smashed straight back past her for four.\n\nTwo runs needed. Four balls left.\n\nAmira could feel every eye on her. She thought about Coach Davies telling her last season that she was ''too small to bowl at the end''. She thought about the hours in the nets after school, with Zaid fetching ball after ball without complaining.\n\nShe ran in and bowled. It was slower than anything she had bowled all day. The batter, expecting pace, swung far too early. The ball floated past the bat and clipped the top of the off stump.\n\nZaid was the first to reach her. He didn''t cheer. He just lifted her right off the ground."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_easy', 1, 'What was the turtle tangled in?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["fishing line","line","old fishing line","a fishing line"]'::jsonb, 'The first paragraph says the turtle was caught in fishing line, and the second says its flipper was wrapped in it.', NULL, '{"title":"Students Help Rescue Tangled Turtle","text":"A green sea turtle is recovering at a wildlife hospital after two Year 6 students from Yamba spotted it caught in fishing line on Tuesday afternoon.\n\nLiam Carter and Isla Reeve, both 11, were walking along Main Beach after school when they noticed the turtle struggling in the shallows. Its front flipper was wrapped tightly in a tangle of old fishing line.\n\n\"At first we thought it was a rock,\" Isla said. \"Then it moved, and we could see it was in trouble.\"\n\nInstead of trying to free the turtle themselves, the students called a wildlife rescue hotline and stayed with the animal until volunteers arrived about half an hour later.\n\nVolunteer coordinator Pam Walsh said the students had done exactly the right thing. \"Turtles can bite, and pulling on the line can cut deeper into the flipper,\" she said. \"By calling us and keeping watch, these two gave the turtle its best chance.\"\n\nVets expect the turtle to be released within a month. The rescue group is now urging people who fish to take all their line home with them."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_easy', 1, 'According to the text, a bleached coral is already dead.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The third paragraph says ''A bleached coral is not dead, but it is starving.'' It can recover if the water cools.', NULL, '{"title":"Coral Bleaching","text":"Coral reefs look like colourful rocks, but they are built by millions of tiny animals called polyps. Each polyp has a partner: microscopic algae that live inside its body. The algae use sunlight to make food, and they share it with the coral. In return, the coral gives the algae shelter. The algae also give the coral most of its colour.\n\nWhen the ocean becomes too warm for too long, this partnership breaks down. The stressed coral pushes the algae out, and its white skeleton shows through its clear flesh. This is called coral bleaching.\n\nA bleached coral is not dead, but it is starving. If the water cools within a few weeks, the algae can return and the coral may recover. If the heat lasts, the coral is likely to die.\n\nThe Great Barrier Reef has suffered several major bleaching events since 1998. Scientists warn that as ocean temperatures rise, bleaching may happen more often, giving reefs less time to recover in between.\n\nThe good news is that some corals are tougher than others, and researchers are studying them closely. What they learn may help the reef survive in a warmer world."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'easy', 1, 'According to Pam Walsh, why was it right for the students not to free the turtle themselves?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Turtles can bite, and pulling the line can cut deeper into the flipper."},{"id":"b","text":"The students were too young to go into the water."},{"id":"c","text":"The turtle was too heavy to lift."},{"id":"d","text":"It is against the law to touch the beach."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Pam Walsh says ''Turtles can bite, and pulling on the line can cut deeper into the flipper.'' Calling experts kept both the children and the turtle safe.', NULL, '{"title":"Students Help Rescue Tangled Turtle","text":"A green sea turtle is recovering at a wildlife hospital after two Year 6 students from Yamba spotted it caught in fishing line on Tuesday afternoon.\n\nLiam Carter and Isla Reeve, both 11, were walking along Main Beach after school when they noticed the turtle struggling in the shallows. Its front flipper was wrapped tightly in a tangle of old fishing line.\n\n\"At first we thought it was a rock,\" Isla said. \"Then it moved, and we could see it was in trouble.\"\n\nInstead of trying to free the turtle themselves, the students called a wildlife rescue hotline and stayed with the animal until volunteers arrived about half an hour later.\n\nVolunteer coordinator Pam Walsh said the students had done exactly the right thing. \"Turtles can bite, and pulling on the line can cut deeper into the flipper,\" she said. \"By calling us and keeping watch, these two gave the turtle its best chance.\"\n\nVets expect the turtle to be released within a month. The rescue group is now urging people who fish to take all their line home with them."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'easy', 1, 'What is the main idea of the first paragraph?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Coral polyps and algae live together and help each other."},{"id":"b","text":"Coral reefs are made of rocks."},{"id":"c","text":"The Great Barrier Reef is in danger."},{"id":"d","text":"Scientists are studying tough corals."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first paragraph explains the partnership: the algae make food for the coral, and the coral gives the algae shelter.', NULL, '{"title":"Coral Bleaching","text":"Coral reefs look like colourful rocks, but they are built by millions of tiny animals called polyps. Each polyp has a partner: microscopic algae that live inside its body. The algae use sunlight to make food, and they share it with the coral. In return, the coral gives the algae shelter. The algae also give the coral most of its colour.\n\nWhen the ocean becomes too warm for too long, this partnership breaks down. The stressed coral pushes the algae out, and its white skeleton shows through its clear flesh. This is called coral bleaching.\n\nA bleached coral is not dead, but it is starving. If the water cools within a few weeks, the algae can return and the coral may recover. If the heat lasts, the coral is likely to die.\n\nThe Great Barrier Reef has suffered several major bleaching events since 1998. Scientists warn that as ocean temperatures rise, bleaching may happen more often, giving reefs less time to recover in between.\n\nThe good news is that some corals are tougher than others, and researchers are studying them closely. What they learn may help the reef survive in a warmer world."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'easy', 1, 'Put these stages in the order they happen.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"A bill is introduced into one house."},{"id":"b","text":"Members of that house debate and vote on the bill."},{"id":"c","text":"The Senate debates and votes on the bill."},{"id":"d","text":"The Governor-General gives royal assent."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The signal words First, Next and Finally show the order: introduced, voted on in the first house, passed to the Senate, then signed by the Governor-General.', NULL, '{"title":"How a Federal Law Is Made","text":"In Australia, new national laws are made by the Federal Parliament in Canberra. Before an idea can become a law, it must pass through several stages.\n\nFirst, a proposed law, called a bill, is introduced into one of the two houses of Parliament, usually the House of Representatives. The members debate the bill and may suggest changes, called amendments, to improve it.\n\nNext, the members vote. If most of them vote in favour, the bill passes that house and moves to the other house, the Senate. There, senators debate the bill again and hold their own vote.\n\nIf the Senate wants changes, the bill is sent back to the first house to consider them. A bill cannot become law until both houses agree on exactly the same version.\n\nFinally, the bill goes to the Governor-General, who represents the King. Once the Governor-General signs it, which is called royal assent, the bill becomes an Act of Parliament: a law that everyone in Australia must follow.\n\nThis process can take weeks or even years. It may seem slow, but it gives many people the chance to examine a law carefully before it affects millions of Australians."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'medium', 1, 'Why does the writer put ''Two runs needed. Four balls left.'' in a paragraph of its own?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to slow the story down and build tension"},{"id":"b","text":"to explain the rules of cricket"},{"id":"c","text":"to show that Amira is bad at maths"},{"id":"d","text":"to introduce a new character"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Short, separate sentences make the reader pause, like the crowd holding its breath. It reminds us how close the game is.', NULL, '{"title":"The Last Over","text":"Six runs. That was all Kurrajong needed to win the final. They had one wicket left, and Amira had the ball.\n\nShe walked back to her mark slowly, rubbing the ball on her trousers until one side shone. Behind the stumps, her brother Zaid crouched low and banged his gloves together. The crowd at the Wagga oval had gone strangely quiet.\n\nHer first ball was a full toss. The batter swung and missed. Her second was smashed straight back past her for four.\n\nTwo runs needed. Four balls left.\n\nAmira could feel every eye on her. She thought about Coach Davies telling her last season that she was ''too small to bowl at the end''. She thought about the hours in the nets after school, with Zaid fetching ball after ball without complaining.\n\nShe ran in and bowled. It was slower than anything she had bowled all day. The batter, expecting pace, swung far too early. The ball floated past the bat and clipped the top of the off stump.\n\nZaid was the first to reach her. He didn''t cheer. He just lifted her right off the ground."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'medium', 1, 'Why did nobody else ever sit in Mr Kowalski''s seat?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The regular passengers knew it was his seat and left it for him."},{"id":"b","text":"The seat was broken."},{"id":"c","text":"The driver had put a sign on it."},{"id":"d","text":"It was saved for school students."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The text says ''The regular passengers just seemed to know.'' They had seen him there every day and left it free for him.', NULL, '{"title":"The Window Seat","text":"Every morning, Mr Kowalski caught the 7:40 bus into town, and every morning he sat in the same seat: third row, left side, by the window. Nobody else ever sat there. The regular passengers just seemed to know.\n\nTom had noticed him for months: an old man in a neat grey coat, who nodded to the driver and hummed quietly to himself all the way into town.\n\nOne Monday, the seat was empty. Tuesday, too. By Friday, Tom found himself staring at it the whole trip.\n\nThe next week, a woman Tom had never seen before got on at his stop. She had the same sharp grey eyes as Mr Kowalski. She walked to the third row, but she didn''t sit by the window. She sat on the aisle side, took a single yellow rose from her bag and laid it gently on the window seat.\n\nThe bus driver caught Tom''s eye in the mirror and gave a small, sad nod. Tom looked down at his shoes. For the rest of the trip, nobody on the bus said a word."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'medium', 1, 'This text is a news report. What does the first paragraph do?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It sums up the main event: what happened, to whom, and when."},{"id":"b","text":"It gives the opinion of the newspaper editor."},{"id":"c","text":"It describes the life cycle of sea turtles."},{"id":"d","text":"It tells readers what to do next time."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'News reports open with a lead paragraph that sums up the whole story. Details and quotes come later.', NULL, '{"title":"Students Help Rescue Tangled Turtle","text":"A green sea turtle is recovering at a wildlife hospital after two Year 6 students from Yamba spotted it caught in fishing line on Tuesday afternoon.\n\nLiam Carter and Isla Reeve, both 11, were walking along Main Beach after school when they noticed the turtle struggling in the shallows. Its front flipper was wrapped tightly in a tangle of old fishing line.\n\n\"At first we thought it was a rock,\" Isla said. \"Then it moved, and we could see it was in trouble.\"\n\nInstead of trying to free the turtle themselves, the students called a wildlife rescue hotline and stayed with the animal until volunteers arrived about half an hour later.\n\nVolunteer coordinator Pam Walsh said the students had done exactly the right thing. \"Turtles can bite, and pulling on the line can cut deeper into the flipper,\" she said. \"By calling us and keeping watch, these two gave the turtle its best chance.\"\n\nVets expect the turtle to be released within a month. The rescue group is now urging people who fish to take all their line home with them."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'hard', 1, 'Why did Amira''s last ball work?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The batter expected a fast ball and swung too early."},{"id":"b","text":"The ball hit a bump in the pitch."},{"id":"c","text":"The batter was not watching."},{"id":"d","text":"Zaid caught the ball behind the stumps."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The ball was ''slower than anything she had bowled all day''. The batter was ''expecting pace'', so swung too early and missed.', NULL, '{"title":"The Last Over","text":"Six runs. That was all Kurrajong needed to win the final. They had one wicket left, and Amira had the ball.\n\nShe walked back to her mark slowly, rubbing the ball on her trousers until one side shone. Behind the stumps, her brother Zaid crouched low and banged his gloves together. The crowd at the Wagga oval had gone strangely quiet.\n\nHer first ball was a full toss. The batter swung and missed. Her second was smashed straight back past her for four.\n\nTwo runs needed. Four balls left.\n\nAmira could feel every eye on her. She thought about Coach Davies telling her last season that she was ''too small to bowl at the end''. She thought about the hours in the nets after school, with Zaid fetching ball after ball without complaining.\n\nShe ran in and bowled. It was slower than anything she had bowled all day. The batter, expecting pace, swung far too early. The ball floated past the bat and clipped the top of the off stump.\n\nZaid was the first to reach her. He didn''t cheer. He just lifted her right off the ground."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'hard', 1, 'What has most likely happened to Mr Kowalski?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He has died, and a relative is honouring him."},{"id":"b","text":"He has moved to another city."},{"id":"c","text":"He has bought a car."},{"id":"d","text":"He is sitting in a different seat on the bus."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'His seat is empty for a week, a woman with his same eyes leaves a rose there, the driver nods sadly and nobody speaks. These clues suggest he has died.', NULL, '{"title":"The Window Seat","text":"Every morning, Mr Kowalski caught the 7:40 bus into town, and every morning he sat in the same seat: third row, left side, by the window. Nobody else ever sat there. The regular passengers just seemed to know.\n\nTom had noticed him for months: an old man in a neat grey coat, who nodded to the driver and hummed quietly to himself all the way into town.\n\nOne Monday, the seat was empty. Tuesday, too. By Friday, Tom found himself staring at it the whole trip.\n\nThe next week, a woman Tom had never seen before got on at his stop. She had the same sharp grey eyes as Mr Kowalski. She walked to the third row, but she didn''t sit by the window. She sat on the aisle side, took a single yellow rose from her bag and laid it gently on the window seat.\n\nThe bus driver caught Tom''s eye in the mirror and gave a small, sad nod. Tom looked down at his shoes. For the rest of the trip, nobody on the bus said a word."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'hard', 1, 'Which statements does the text support? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Algae give coral most of its colour."},{"id":"b","text":"Bleaching happens when water stays too warm for too long."},{"id":"c","text":"A bleached coral may recover if the water cools in time."},{"id":"d","text":"All corals are equally tough."},{"id":"e","text":"Bleaching has happened only once, in 1998."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The text says algae give the colour, warm water causes bleaching, and coral may recover if it cools. It says some corals are tougher than others and there have been several events since 1998.', NULL, '{"title":"Coral Bleaching","text":"Coral reefs look like colourful rocks, but they are built by millions of tiny animals called polyps. Each polyp has a partner: microscopic algae that live inside its body. The algae use sunlight to make food, and they share it with the coral. In return, the coral gives the algae shelter. The algae also give the coral most of its colour.\n\nWhen the ocean becomes too warm for too long, this partnership breaks down. The stressed coral pushes the algae out, and its white skeleton shows through its clear flesh. This is called coral bleaching.\n\nA bleached coral is not dead, but it is starving. If the water cools within a few weeks, the algae can return and the coral may recover. If the heat lasts, the coral is likely to die.\n\nThe Great Barrier Reef has suffered several major bleaching events since 1998. Scientists warn that as ocean temperatures rise, bleaching may happen more often, giving reefs less time to recover in between.\n\nThe good news is that some corals are tougher than others, and researchers are studying them closely. What they learn may help the reef survive in a warmer world."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does the writer remind us that Coach Davies once called Amira ''too small to bowl at the end''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"so that her winning wicket also proves her doubter wrong, making the moment more satisfying"},{"id":"b","text":"to show that Coach Davies is the main character"},{"id":"c","text":"to explain why Amira bowled a full toss"},{"id":"d","text":"to show that Amira is still too small"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Remembering the coach''s doubt raises the stakes. When she bowls the winning ball at the end, she proves him wrong, so the victory means even more.', NULL, '{"title":"The Last Over","text":"Six runs. That was all Kurrajong needed to win the final. They had one wicket left, and Amira had the ball.\n\nShe walked back to her mark slowly, rubbing the ball on her trousers until one side shone. Behind the stumps, her brother Zaid crouched low and banged his gloves together. The crowd at the Wagga oval had gone strangely quiet.\n\nHer first ball was a full toss. The batter swung and missed. Her second was smashed straight back past her for four.\n\nTwo runs needed. Four balls left.\n\nAmira could feel every eye on her. She thought about Coach Davies telling her last season that she was ''too small to bowl at the end''. She thought about the hours in the nets after school, with Zaid fetching ball after ball without complaining.\n\nShe ran in and bowled. It was slower than anything she had bowled all day. The batter, expecting pace, swung far too early. The ball floated past the bat and clipped the top of the off stump.\n\nZaid was the first to reach her. He didn''t cheer. He just lifted her right off the ground."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_hard', 1, 'The author says the process ''may seem slow''. What is the author''s view of this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The slowness is worthwhile because it lets laws be checked carefully."},{"id":"b","text":"The process is a waste of time and should be scrapped."},{"id":"c","text":"Only the Senate should vote on laws."},{"id":"d","text":"The Governor-General should make laws alone."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Straight after ''may seem slow'', the author says ''but it gives many people the chance to examine a law carefully''. The word ''but'' shows the author thinks it is worth it.', NULL, '{"title":"How a Federal Law Is Made","text":"In Australia, new national laws are made by the Federal Parliament in Canberra. Before an idea can become a law, it must pass through several stages.\n\nFirst, a proposed law, called a bill, is introduced into one of the two houses of Parliament, usually the House of Representatives. The members debate the bill and may suggest changes, called amendments, to improve it.\n\nNext, the members vote. If most of them vote in favour, the bill passes that house and moves to the other house, the Senate. There, senators debate the bill again and hold their own vote.\n\nIf the Senate wants changes, the bill is sent back to the first house to consider them. A bill cannot become law until both houses agree on exactly the same version.\n\nFinally, the bill goes to the Governor-General, who represents the King. Once the Governor-General signs it, which is called royal assent, the bill becomes an Act of Parliament: a law that everyone in Australia must follow.\n\nThis process can take weeks or even years. It may seem slow, but it gives many people the chance to examine a law carefully before it affects millions of Australians."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does the woman sit on the aisle side and lay the rose on the window seat?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to leave Mr Kowalski''s seat for him, as a sign of love and respect"},{"id":"b","text":"because the window seat was dirty"},{"id":"c","text":"so she could get off the bus quickly"},{"id":"d","text":"because she didn''t know it was his seat"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'She chooses his row but leaves his exact seat empty, with a rose on it. Like the regular passengers, she keeps his place for him to honour his memory.', NULL, '{"title":"The Window Seat","text":"Every morning, Mr Kowalski caught the 7:40 bus into town, and every morning he sat in the same seat: third row, left side, by the window. Nobody else ever sat there. The regular passengers just seemed to know.\n\nTom had noticed him for months: an old man in a neat grey coat, who nodded to the driver and hummed quietly to himself all the way into town.\n\nOne Monday, the seat was empty. Tuesday, too. By Friday, Tom found himself staring at it the whole trip.\n\nThe next week, a woman Tom had never seen before got on at his stop. She had the same sharp grey eyes as Mr Kowalski. She walked to the third row, but she didn''t sit by the window. She sat on the aisle side, took a single yellow rose from her bag and laid it gently on the window seat.\n\nThe bus driver caught Tom''s eye in the mirror and gave a small, sad nod. Tom looked down at his shoes. For the rest of the trip, nobody on the bus said a word."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_easy', 1, '''grey as a grandfather''s beard'' — what kind of figurative language is this?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["simile","a simile"]'::jsonb, 'It compares the jetty to a beard using ''as'', so it is a simile.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Jetty","text":"The old jetty kneels on splintered legs,\ngrey as a grandfather''s beard.\nEach tide that slides beneath its boards\nsteals one more plank, as it feared.\n\nThe children who once cannonballed here\nare grey themselves, in town.\nThe jetty listens for their shouts\nwhile the river pulls it down."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_easy', 1, '''Smirk'' and ''smile'' have the same connotation.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Both describe a mouth turning up, but ''smirk'' suggests someone is smug or mean, while ''smile'' feels friendly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_easy', 1, 'What had happened to the hillside before the story begins?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A bushfire had burnt it."},{"id":"b","text":"A flood had washed it away."},{"id":"c","text":"It had been covered in snow."},{"id":"d","text":"Builders had cleared it."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first words are ''After the fires'', and the trees are black and the air tastes of ash.', NULL, '{"title":"Waiting","text":"After the fires, the hillside behind our house looked like the surface of the moon. Every tree was black. The air tasted of ash for weeks, and Mum stopped opening the curtains on that side of the house.\n\nIn spring, Dad dragged me up the hill to see something. I didn''t want to go. The burnt trunks stood like rows of broken matchsticks, and our shoes sank into grey powder.\n\nThen he pointed. Halfway up a scorched blackbutt, a ribbon of bright green leaves had burst straight out of the bark, so green it almost hurt to look at.\n\n\"Epicormic growth,\" Dad said, as if he were a scientist. Then, in his normal voice: \"It''s not dead, mate. It was just waiting.\"\n\nThat afternoon, Mum opened the curtains."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'easy', 1, 'Which words or phrases make the jetty seem like an old person? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"kneels on splintered legs"},{"id":"b","text":"grey as a grandfather''s beard"},{"id":"c","text":"listens for their shouts"},{"id":"d","text":"the river"},{"id":"e","text":"beneath its boards"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Kneeling, having a grey beard and listening are things an old person does. The river and the boards are just parts of the setting.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Jetty","text":"The old jetty kneels on splintered legs,\ngrey as a grandfather''s beard.\nEach tide that slides beneath its boards\nsteals one more plank, as it feared.\n\nThe children who once cannonballed here\nare grey themselves, in town.\nThe jetty listens for their shouts\nwhile the river pulls it down."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'easy', 1, '''The burnt trunks stood like rows of broken matchsticks.'' What picture does this create?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"thin, blackened trees, snapped and lined up"},{"id":"b","text":"tall, healthy green trees"},{"id":"c","text":"trees that are on fire right now"},{"id":"d","text":"a box of new matches"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A used matchstick is thin and black at the top. The simile helps us see burnt, broken trunks standing in rows.', NULL, '{"title":"Waiting","text":"After the fires, the hillside behind our house looked like the surface of the moon. Every tree was black. The air tasted of ash for weeks, and Mum stopped opening the curtains on that side of the house.\n\nIn spring, Dad dragged me up the hill to see something. I didn''t want to go. The burnt trunks stood like rows of broken matchsticks, and our shoes sank into grey powder.\n\nThen he pointed. Halfway up a scorched blackbutt, a ribbon of bright green leaves had burst straight out of the bark, so green it almost hurt to look at.\n\n\"Epicormic growth,\" Dad said, as if he were a scientist. Then, in his normal voice: \"It''s not dead, mate. It was just waiting.\"\n\nThat afternoon, Mum opened the curtains."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'easy', 1, 'Put these stages of a narrative in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"orientation"},{"id":"b","text":"rising action"},{"id":"c","text":"climax"},{"id":"d","text":"falling action"},{"id":"e","text":"resolution"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'A story sets the scene, builds tension through rising action, reaches the climax, then winds down and ends with a resolution.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'medium', 1, 'How does Ravi change during the story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He goes from relying on his notes to trusting himself."},{"id":"b","text":"He goes from confident to giving up."},{"id":"c","text":"He becomes angry with Grace."},{"id":"d","text":"He doesn''t change at all."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'At first he depends on his colour-coded cards. Without them, he speaks from what he believes and discovers he can do it.', NULL, '{"title":"The Debate","text":"Ravi had prepared for the debate for two weeks. He had index cards colour-coded by argument and a joke he''d practised in the mirror. When the teacher called his name, he strode to the front and smiled at the audience.\n\nThen he looked down. His cards were gone.\n\nFor a moment, the hall was silent. Ravi could hear the hum of the ceiling fans and someone''s chair creaking. He saw his opponent, Grace, glance at her own neat pile of notes.\n\nRavi took a breath. \"I was going to read you my notes,\" he said, \"but I think I''ll just tell you what I believe.\" And he did. His voice shook at first, then steadied.\n\nAfterwards, Grace found his cards under her chair. She handed them back with a grin. \"You didn''t need these,\" she said. Ravi looked at the neat colours and, for the first time, wasn''t sure that he ever had."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'medium', 1, 'Why does the writer mention the hum of the ceiling fans and a chair creaking?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to show how silent and tense the hall became"},{"id":"b","text":"to show that the hall was too hot"},{"id":"c","text":"to describe the school building"},{"id":"d","text":"to show that the audience was bored"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'You only hear tiny sounds like a fan humming when everything else is silent. This builds tension at the moment Ravi loses his cards.', NULL, '{"title":"The Debate","text":"Ravi had prepared for the debate for two weeks. He had index cards colour-coded by argument and a joke he''d practised in the mirror. When the teacher called his name, he strode to the front and smiled at the audience.\n\nThen he looked down. His cards were gone.\n\nFor a moment, the hall was silent. Ravi could hear the hum of the ceiling fans and someone''s chair creaking. He saw his opponent, Grace, glance at her own neat pile of notes.\n\nRavi took a breath. \"I was going to read you my notes,\" he said, \"but I think I''ll just tell you what I believe.\" And he did. His voice shook at first, then steadied.\n\nAfterwards, Grace found his cards under her chair. She handed them back with a grin. \"You didn''t need these,\" she said. Ravi looked at the neat colours and, for the first time, wasn''t sure that he ever had."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'medium', 1, 'Which words have a negative connotation? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"stubborn"},{"id":"b","text":"determined"},{"id":"c","text":"nosy"},{"id":"d","text":"curious"},{"id":"e","text":"scrawny"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Stubborn, nosy and scrawny sound like criticisms. Determined and curious describe similar things in a positive way.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'hard', 1, 'What could the old jetty symbolise in this poem?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the passing of time and memories slowly fading"},{"id":"b","text":"a new beginning"},{"id":"c","text":"the danger of swimming in rivers"},{"id":"d","text":"how to build a jetty"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The jetty is losing planks with each tide while the children who played there have grown old. It stands for time passing and the past fading.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Jetty","text":"The old jetty kneels on splintered legs,\ngrey as a grandfather''s beard.\nEach tide that slides beneath its boards\nsteals one more plank, as it feared.\n\nThe children who once cannonballed here\nare grey themselves, in town.\nThe jetty listens for their shouts\nwhile the river pulls it down."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'hard', 1, 'What do the bright green leaves symbolise?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"hope and recovery after disaster"},{"id":"b","text":"danger of another fire"},{"id":"c","text":"the coming of winter"},{"id":"d","text":"Dad''s love of science"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'New life bursting from a burnt tree shows that things can recover. The leaves stand for hope.', NULL, '{"title":"Waiting","text":"After the fires, the hillside behind our house looked like the surface of the moon. Every tree was black. The air tasted of ash for weeks, and Mum stopped opening the curtains on that side of the house.\n\nIn spring, Dad dragged me up the hill to see something. I didn''t want to go. The burnt trunks stood like rows of broken matchsticks, and our shoes sank into grey powder.\n\nThen he pointed. Halfway up a scorched blackbutt, a ribbon of bright green leaves had burst straight out of the bark, so green it almost hurt to look at.\n\n\"Epicormic growth,\" Dad said, as if he were a scientist. Then, in his normal voice: \"It''s not dead, mate. It was just waiting.\"\n\nThat afternoon, Mum opened the curtains."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'hard', 1, 'A writer wants to show a character is nervous without using the word ''nervous''. Which sentence does this best?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Tom wiped his damp palms on his shorts for the third time."},{"id":"b","text":"Tom was very nervous."},{"id":"c","text":"Tom felt a bit worried and nervous."},{"id":"d","text":"Tom went to the front of the class."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Show, don''t tell'': sweaty palms and a repeated action let readers work out he''s nervous without being told.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Ravi ''wasn''t sure that he ever had'' needed the cards. What does he realise?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"His real belief mattered more than all his careful preparation."},{"id":"b","text":"He should have used more colours."},{"id":"c","text":"Grace had stolen his cards on purpose."},{"id":"d","text":"He will never prepare for a debate again."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Grace says he didn''t need the cards today. Ravi goes further: maybe he never needed them, because speaking from belief was his real strength.', NULL, '{"title":"The Debate","text":"Ravi had prepared for the debate for two weeks. He had index cards colour-coded by argument and a joke he''d practised in the mirror. When the teacher called his name, he strode to the front and smiled at the audience.\n\nThen he looked down. His cards were gone.\n\nFor a moment, the hall was silent. Ravi could hear the hum of the ceiling fans and someone''s chair creaking. He saw his opponent, Grace, glance at her own neat pile of notes.\n\nRavi took a breath. \"I was going to read you my notes,\" he said, \"but I think I''ll just tell you what I believe.\" And he did. His voice shook at first, then steadied.\n\nAfterwards, Grace found his cards under her chair. She handed them back with a grin. \"You didn''t need these,\" she said. Ravi looked at the neat colours and, for the first time, wasn''t sure that he ever had."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does the story end with ''That afternoon, Mum opened the curtains''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It shows the family is ready to face the hillside again, because hope has returned."},{"id":"b","text":"It shows Mum wanted more sunlight to dry the washing."},{"id":"c","text":"It shows the fire had started again."},{"id":"d","text":"It shows Mum didn''t care about the trees."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Mum closed the curtains so she wouldn''t see the burnt hill. Opening them, after the news of new growth, symbolises moving on with hope.', NULL, '{"title":"Waiting","text":"After the fires, the hillside behind our house looked like the surface of the moon. Every tree was black. The air tasted of ash for weeks, and Mum stopped opening the curtains on that side of the house.\n\nIn spring, Dad dragged me up the hill to see something. I didn''t want to go. The burnt trunks stood like rows of broken matchsticks, and our shoes sank into grey powder.\n\nThen he pointed. Halfway up a scorched blackbutt, a ribbon of bright green leaves had burst straight out of the bark, so green it almost hurt to look at.\n\n\"Epicormic growth,\" Dad said, as if he were a scientist. Then, in his normal voice: \"It''s not dead, mate. It was just waiting.\"\n\nThat afternoon, Mum opened the curtains."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A writer retells ''The Three Little Pigs'' so the wolf is a kind character who has been misunderstood. What has the writer changed?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the perspective and how the character is represented"},{"id":"b","text":"only the setting"},{"id":"c","text":"only the title"},{"id":"d","text":"nothing important"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The events may be similar, but showing the wolf as kind changes whose side we see and how he is represented to readers.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_easy', 1, '''The dam''s a dish of drying mud'' says the dam IS a dish, without using ''like'' or ''as''. What kind of figurative language is this?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["metaphor","a metaphor"]'::jsonb, 'A metaphor says one thing is another. A simile would use ''like'' or ''as'': the dam is like a dish.', NULL, '{"title":"Drought","text":"The paddocks lie like old brown paper,\ncreased and cracked and torn.\nThe dam''s a dish of drying mud,\nthe sky''s a sheet of scorn.\n\nThe cattle stand with lowered heads\nand count their ribs for rain.\nThe windmill creaks a rusty prayer\nand turns, and turns again.\n\nThen one night on the iron roof,\na tap, a tap, a drum —\nand Dad stands barefoot in the yard\nand laughs, for rain has come."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_easy', 1, '''Confident'' and ''arrogant'' have the same connotation.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Both describe someone who believes in themselves, but ''confident'' sounds positive, while ''arrogant'' suggests they think they are better than others.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_easy', 1, 'What happened to the clock the week after Grandpa''s funeral?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It stopped."},{"id":"b","text":"It was sold."},{"id":"c","text":"It fell over."},{"id":"d","text":"It started chiming loudly."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says, in one short line, ''The week after Grandpa''s funeral, the clock stopped.''', NULL, '{"title":"Grandpa''s Clock","text":"Grandpa''s clock had stood in the hallway for as long as anyone could remember. It was taller than Dad, made of dark wood, and its brass pendulum swung with a slow, steady tock that you could hear from every room.\n\nThe week after Grandpa''s funeral, the clock stopped.\n\nNobody touched it. Mum walked around it as if it were asleep. Dad said he''d get it fixed, but the weeks became months, and the hallway grew so quiet that I started humming just to fill it.\n\nOne Saturday, I found a small brass winder in Grandpa''s desk drawer, wrapped in a note: Wind me every Sunday, or I''ll sulk.\n\nI laughed out loud for the first time since winter.\n\nThe next morning, I opened the little glass door, fitted the winder and turned it, carefully, seven times. For a moment nothing happened. Then the pendulum shivered, swung, and the old sound filled the house again.\n\nFrom the kitchen, Mum called, \"Is that Grandpa''s clock?\" Her voice sounded like she was smiling."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'easy', 1, 'Which lines give an animal or object a human action? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The windmill creaks a rusty prayer"},{"id":"b","text":"and count their ribs for rain"},{"id":"c","text":"The dam''s a dish of drying mud"},{"id":"d","text":"a tap, a tap, a drum"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Praying and counting are things people do, so a praying windmill and cattle counting their ribs are personification. The dam line is a metaphor and ''a tap, a tap'' imitates the sound of rain.', NULL, '{"title":"Drought","text":"The paddocks lie like old brown paper,\ncreased and cracked and torn.\nThe dam''s a dish of drying mud,\nthe sky''s a sheet of scorn.\n\nThe cattle stand with lowered heads\nand count their ribs for rain.\nThe windmill creaks a rusty prayer\nand turns, and turns again.\n\nThen one night on the iron roof,\na tap, a tap, a drum —\nand Dad stands barefoot in the yard\nand laughs, for rain has come."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'easy', 1, '''Her silver medal sat in her pocket like a stone.'' What does this simile suggest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The medal felt heavy and disappointing, not something to celebrate."},{"id":"b","text":"The medal was made of rock."},{"id":"c","text":"Priya likes collecting stones."},{"id":"d","text":"The medal was worth a lot of money."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A stone is heavy, cold and dull. Comparing the medal to one shows Priya feels weighed down by coming second.', NULL, '{"title":"Second Place","text":"When Mai spelt ''conscientious'' perfectly and the judges announced her the winner of the regional spelling bee, the audience clapped. Priya clapped too. Her hands moved, but she could not feel them.\n\nShe had studied every night for three months. Mai had started two weeks ago, between soccer games.\n\nOn the bus home, Priya sat at the back and pressed her forehead against the cold window. Her silver medal sat in her pocket like a stone. When Mai turned around and waved, Priya pretended to be asleep.\n\nThat night, she took out her word list and almost tore it in half. Then she noticed something written in the margin, in Mai''s round handwriting: Priya, you''re going to win this! I''ll quiz you on Thursday.\n\nPriya remembered Thursday. Mai had quizzed her for an hour and missed her own soccer training to do it.\n\nPriya picked up her phone. Her thumb hovered over the screen for a long time. Then she typed: Congratulations. You earned it. Want to study for the state final together?"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'easy', 1, 'Put these events from the story in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Grandpa''s funeral took place."},{"id":"b","text":"The clock stopped."},{"id":"c","text":"The narrator found the winder and a note."},{"id":"d","text":"The narrator wound the clock."},{"id":"e","text":"Mum called out from the kitchen."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'The clock stopped after the funeral, months later the narrator found the winder, wound the clock the next morning, and then Mum heard it.', NULL, '{"title":"Grandpa''s Clock","text":"Grandpa''s clock had stood in the hallway for as long as anyone could remember. It was taller than Dad, made of dark wood, and its brass pendulum swung with a slow, steady tock that you could hear from every room.\n\nThe week after Grandpa''s funeral, the clock stopped.\n\nNobody touched it. Mum walked around it as if it were asleep. Dad said he''d get it fixed, but the weeks became months, and the hallway grew so quiet that I started humming just to fill it.\n\nOne Saturday, I found a small brass winder in Grandpa''s desk drawer, wrapped in a note: Wind me every Sunday, or I''ll sulk.\n\nI laughed out loud for the first time since winter.\n\nThe next morning, I opened the little glass door, fitted the winder and turned it, carefully, seven times. For a moment nothing happened. Then the pendulum shivered, swung, and the old sound filled the house again.\n\nFrom the kitchen, Mum called, \"Is that Grandpa''s clock?\" Her voice sounded like she was smiling."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'medium', 1, 'How does the mood change in the last stanza?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"from hopeless and dry to joyful and relieved"},{"id":"b","text":"from happy to frightened"},{"id":"c","text":"from calm to angry"},{"id":"d","text":"It stays sad all the way through."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first two stanzas are full of cracked land, thin cattle and scorn. In the last one rain drums on the roof and Dad laughs, so the mood turns joyful.', NULL, '{"title":"Drought","text":"The paddocks lie like old brown paper,\ncreased and cracked and torn.\nThe dam''s a dish of drying mud,\nthe sky''s a sheet of scorn.\n\nThe cattle stand with lowered heads\nand count their ribs for rain.\nThe windmill creaks a rusty prayer\nand turns, and turns again.\n\nThen one night on the iron roof,\na tap, a tap, a drum —\nand Dad stands barefoot in the yard\nand laughs, for rain has come."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'medium', 1, '''Her hands moved, but she could not feel them.'' What does this show about Priya?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She was so shocked and disappointed that she clapped without really feeling it."},{"id":"b","text":"Her hands were frozen from the cold hall."},{"id":"c","text":"She was too excited to think."},{"id":"d","text":"She wasn''t watching the spelling bee."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'She claps because she should, but she is numb with disappointment. The writer shows her feelings instead of telling us.', NULL, '{"title":"Second Place","text":"When Mai spelt ''conscientious'' perfectly and the judges announced her the winner of the regional spelling bee, the audience clapped. Priya clapped too. Her hands moved, but she could not feel them.\n\nShe had studied every night for three months. Mai had started two weeks ago, between soccer games.\n\nOn the bus home, Priya sat at the back and pressed her forehead against the cold window. Her silver medal sat in her pocket like a stone. When Mai turned around and waved, Priya pretended to be asleep.\n\nThat night, she took out her word list and almost tore it in half. Then she noticed something written in the margin, in Mai''s round handwriting: Priya, you''re going to win this! I''ll quiz you on Thursday.\n\nPriya remembered Thursday. Mai had quizzed her for an hour and missed her own soccer training to do it.\n\nPriya picked up her phone. Her thumb hovered over the screen for a long time. Then she typed: Congratulations. You earned it. Want to study for the state final together?"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'medium', 1, '''Slim'' and ''skinny'' mean nearly the same thing, but ''skinny'' feels more negative. The feeling a word carries is called its ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["connotation","connotations"]'::jsonb, 'A word''s dictionary meaning is its denotation. The feeling or idea it suggests, positive or negative, is its connotation.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'hard', 1, 'What does the clock most likely symbolise in this story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Grandpa''s presence and the heartbeat of the family home"},{"id":"b","text":"how old-fashioned the house is"},{"id":"c","text":"the narrator''s fear of the dark"},{"id":"d","text":"the start of the school term"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The clock stops when Grandpa dies and the house falls silent. When it starts again, life returns to the house, so it stands for Grandpa and the family''s life together.', NULL, '{"title":"Grandpa''s Clock","text":"Grandpa''s clock had stood in the hallway for as long as anyone could remember. It was taller than Dad, made of dark wood, and its brass pendulum swung with a slow, steady tock that you could hear from every room.\n\nThe week after Grandpa''s funeral, the clock stopped.\n\nNobody touched it. Mum walked around it as if it were asleep. Dad said he''d get it fixed, but the weeks became months, and the hallway grew so quiet that I started humming just to fill it.\n\nOne Saturday, I found a small brass winder in Grandpa''s desk drawer, wrapped in a note: Wind me every Sunday, or I''ll sulk.\n\nI laughed out loud for the first time since winter.\n\nThe next morning, I opened the little glass door, fitted the winder and turned it, carefully, seven times. For a moment nothing happened. Then the pendulum shivered, swung, and the old sound filled the house again.\n\nFrom the kitchen, Mum called, \"Is that Grandpa''s clock?\" Her voice sounded like she was smiling."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'hard', 1, 'Why does the writer include the note in Mai''s handwriting?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It makes Priya, and the reader, realise that Mai had been a loyal friend all along."},{"id":"b","text":"It shows that Mai has neat handwriting."},{"id":"c","text":"It proves that Mai cheated."},{"id":"d","text":"It explains the rules of the spelling bee."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The note shows Mai believed in Priya and gave up training to help her. It turns Priya''s jealousy around and is the turning point of the story.', NULL, '{"title":"Second Place","text":"When Mai spelt ''conscientious'' perfectly and the judges announced her the winner of the regional spelling bee, the audience clapped. Priya clapped too. Her hands moved, but she could not feel them.\n\nShe had studied every night for three months. Mai had started two weeks ago, between soccer games.\n\nOn the bus home, Priya sat at the back and pressed her forehead against the cold window. Her silver medal sat in her pocket like a stone. When Mai turned around and waved, Priya pretended to be asleep.\n\nThat night, she took out her word list and almost tore it in half. Then she noticed something written in the margin, in Mai''s round handwriting: Priya, you''re going to win this! I''ll quiz you on Thursday.\n\nPriya remembered Thursday. Mai had quizzed her for an hour and missed her own soccer training to do it.\n\nPriya picked up her phone. Her thumb hovered over the screen for a long time. Then she typed: Congratulations. You earned it. Want to study for the state final together?"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'hard', 1, 'A writer wants to show that a character is angry without using the word ''angry''. Which sentences do this? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Her jaw tightened and she slammed the cupboard door."},{"id":"b","text":"He was really, really angry."},{"id":"c","text":"She stabbed at her dinner with her fork and didn''t eat a bite."},{"id":"d","text":"He smiled and waved to his friends."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, '''Show, don''t tell'': a tight jaw, a slammed door and stabbing at food let readers work out the anger. The second sentence tells us directly, and the last shows happiness.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does the poet end with Dad standing barefoot in the yard and laughing?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"One person''s joyful action captures how much the rain means to the whole family."},{"id":"b","text":"It shows that Dad forgot to put on his shoes."},{"id":"c","text":"It warns readers about the danger of lightning."},{"id":"d","text":"It shows that the drought is still going on."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'After two stanzas of suffering, a single picture of Dad rushing out barefoot and laughing shows the relief better than any explanation could.', NULL, '{"title":"Drought","text":"The paddocks lie like old brown paper,\ncreased and cracked and torn.\nThe dam''s a dish of drying mud,\nthe sky''s a sheet of scorn.\n\nThe cattle stand with lowered heads\nand count their ribs for rain.\nThe windmill creaks a rusty prayer\nand turns, and turns again.\n\nThen one night on the iron roof,\na tap, a tap, a drum —\nand Dad stands barefoot in the yard\nand laughs, for rain has come."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why is the note ''Wind me every Sunday, or I''ll sulk'' important to the story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Grandpa''s playful humour lives on in the note, and it helps the narrator begin to heal."},{"id":"b","text":"It proves the clock was broken all along."},{"id":"c","text":"It shows Grandpa was strict and bossy."},{"id":"d","text":"It tells the reader how clocks work."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The joke sounds just like Grandpa talking. It makes the narrator laugh ''for the first time since winter'', which starts the family''s recovery.', NULL, '{"title":"Grandpa''s Clock","text":"Grandpa''s clock had stood in the hallway for as long as anyone could remember. It was taller than Dad, made of dark wood, and its brass pendulum swung with a slow, steady tock that you could hear from every room.\n\nThe week after Grandpa''s funeral, the clock stopped.\n\nNobody touched it. Mum walked around it as if it were asleep. Dad said he''d get it fixed, but the weeks became months, and the hallway grew so quiet that I started humming just to fill it.\n\nOne Saturday, I found a small brass winder in Grandpa''s desk drawer, wrapped in a note: Wind me every Sunday, or I''ll sulk.\n\nI laughed out loud for the first time since winter.\n\nThe next morning, I opened the little glass door, fitted the winder and turned it, carefully, seven times. For a moment nothing happened. Then the pendulum shivered, swung, and the old sound filled the house again.\n\nFrom the kitchen, Mum called, \"Is that Grandpa''s clock?\" Her voice sounded like she was smiling."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Priya''s thumb ''hovered over the screen for a long time''. What does this pause show, and how is her conflict resolved?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She is torn between hurt pride and friendship, and in the end she chooses friendship."},{"id":"b","text":"She has forgotten how to spell ''congratulations'', so she gives up."},{"id":"c","text":"She wants to tell Mai the judges were unfair."},{"id":"d","text":"She is too tired to type, so she goes to sleep."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The pause shows her inner struggle. Her message, congratulating Mai and offering to study together, shows she has chosen friendship over jealousy.', NULL, '{"title":"Second Place","text":"When Mai spelt ''conscientious'' perfectly and the judges announced her the winner of the regional spelling bee, the audience clapped. Priya clapped too. Her hands moved, but she could not feel them.\n\nShe had studied every night for three months. Mai had started two weeks ago, between soccer games.\n\nOn the bus home, Priya sat at the back and pressed her forehead against the cold window. Her silver medal sat in her pocket like a stone. When Mai turned around and waved, Priya pretended to be asleep.\n\nThat night, she took out her word list and almost tore it in half. Then she noticed something written in the margin, in Mai''s round handwriting: Priya, you''re going to win this! I''ll quiz you on Thursday.\n\nPriya remembered Thursday. Mai had quizzed her for an hour and missed her own soccer training to do it.\n\nPriya picked up her phone. Her thumb hovered over the screen for a long time. Then she typed: Congratulations. You earned it. Want to study for the state final together?"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which genre is this story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"fantasy"},{"id":"b","text":"biography"},{"id":"c","text":"information report"},{"id":"d","text":"recount"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A river that talks and stones that move by themselves could not happen in real life. Magic like this belongs to fantasy.', NULL, '{"title":"The River Stones","text":"The day Wren turned twelve, the river spoke to her.\n\nIt was not a loud voice. It came up through the soles of her bare feet as she waded across the shallows below Ironbark Falls: a cold, slow whisper that seemed to know her name.\n\n''The stones are moving,'' it said. ''Go to the old woman on the ridge before the moon is full.''\n\nWren stumbled backwards onto the bank, heart hammering. Behind her, the water ran on as if nothing had happened. But on the flat rock where she had been standing, five smooth river stones now lay in a perfect circle."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_easy', 1, 'A myth often explains how something in nature or the world came to be.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Myths are traditional stories that often explain natural things, like why the sun rises or how a mountain was formed.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_easy', 1, 'A true story of a real person''s life, written by someone else, is called a ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["biography","a biography"]'::jsonb, 'bio (life) + graphy (writing) = biography. If you write your own life story, it is an autobiography.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'easy', 1, 'In Text B, what is the shop owner worried about?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"losing customers when travellers no longer drive through town"},{"id":"b","text":"trucks crashing into the bakery"},{"id":"c","text":"the price of flour"},{"id":"d","text":"the new road being too narrow"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'She says travellers used to stop for a pie, but cars will ''race past''. She fears her business won''t survive.', NULL, '{"title":"The Highway Bypass","text":"Text A – Council media release\n\nThe new highway bypass will open next month, cutting travel time between the two towns by twenty minutes. Mayor Linda Okafor said the bypass would ''remove heavy trucks from our main street and make it safer for families''. Construction created 300 local jobs.\n\nText B – Letter from a shop owner\n\nFor thirty-one years my bakery has stood on Main Street. Every holiday, travellers stopped for a pie and a chat. Once the bypass opens, those cars will race past without even seeing our town''s name. The council talks about safety, but it hasn''t said how small businesses like mine will survive."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'easy', 1, 'What is the writer''s position?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"We must act now to protect bilbies and bring them back."},{"id":"b","text":"Bilbies are not worth saving."},{"id":"c","text":"Chocolate bilbies should be banned."},{"id":"d","text":"Foxes and cats should be kept as pets."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The writer states the position at the end of the first paragraph: ''We must act now to bring them back.''', NULL, '{"title":"Bring Back the Bilby","text":"Bilbies once lived across most of mainland Australia. Today they survive in only a few small areas. We must act now to bring them back.\n\nFirstly, bilbies are ecosystem engineers. As they dig for food, they turn over large amounts of soil, which helps rain soak in and seeds sprout.\n\nSecondly, the main threats are known and can be managed. Feral cats and foxes hunt bilbies, so fenced reserves that keep these predators out give bilbies a safe place to breed.\n\nSome people say the money would be better spent elsewhere. However, protecting one species also protects the whole habitat it shares with many others.\n\nEvery Australian who has ever eaten a chocolate Easter Bilby should ask: will the real one still be here for our grandchildren?"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'easy', 1, 'Put the parts of a persuasive exposition in the usual order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"a thesis statement giving the writer''s position"},{"id":"b","text":"first argument with evidence"},{"id":"c","text":"second argument with evidence"},{"id":"d","text":"a rebuttal of the other side"},{"id":"e","text":"a conclusion that restates the position"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'An exposition states its thesis, backs it up with arguments, answers the other side, then finishes by restating the position strongly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'medium', 1, 'Which details in Text A are used to make the bypass sound positive? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Travel time is cut by twenty minutes."},{"id":"b","text":"Heavy trucks will be removed from the main street."},{"id":"c","text":"Construction created 300 local jobs."},{"id":"d","text":"Small businesses will survive."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Text A mentions time saved, safer streets and jobs. It says nothing about small businesses; that worry comes from Text B.', NULL, '{"title":"The Highway Bypass","text":"Text A – Council media release\n\nThe new highway bypass will open next month, cutting travel time between the two towns by twenty minutes. Mayor Linda Okafor said the bypass would ''remove heavy trucks from our main street and make it safer for families''. Construction created 300 local jobs.\n\nText B – Letter from a shop owner\n\nFor thirty-one years my bakery has stood on Main Street. Every holiday, travellers stopped for a pie and a chat. Once the bypass opens, those cars will race past without even seeing our town''s name. The council talks about safety, but it hasn''t said how small businesses like mine will survive."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'medium', 1, 'Which features show that this story is fantasy? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"a river that speaks"},{"id":"b","text":"stones that arrange themselves"},{"id":"c","text":"a girl who has a birthday"},{"id":"d","text":"bare feet in shallow water"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'A speaking river and stones forming a circle by magic are impossible in real life. Birthdays and wading happen in everyday stories too.', NULL, '{"title":"The River Stones","text":"The day Wren turned twelve, the river spoke to her.\n\nIt was not a loud voice. It came up through the soles of her bare feet as she waded across the shallows below Ironbark Falls: a cold, slow whisper that seemed to know her name.\n\n''The stones are moving,'' it said. ''Go to the old woman on the ridge before the moon is full.''\n\nWren stumbled backwards onto the bank, heart hammering. Behind her, the water ran on as if nothing had happened. But on the flat rock where she had been standing, five smooth river stones now lay in a perfect circle."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'medium', 1, 'Why does the writer mention that ''some people say the money would be better spent elsewhere''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to answer an opposing argument and show why it is weaker"},{"id":"b","text":"because the writer agrees with them"},{"id":"c","text":"to change the topic to money"},{"id":"d","text":"to end the text"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'This is a rebuttal. The writer raises the other side, then uses ''However'' to argue back, which makes the writer''s case stronger.', NULL, '{"title":"Bring Back the Bilby","text":"Bilbies once lived across most of mainland Australia. Today they survive in only a few small areas. We must act now to bring them back.\n\nFirstly, bilbies are ecosystem engineers. As they dig for food, they turn over large amounts of soil, which helps rain soak in and seeds sprout.\n\nSecondly, the main threats are known and can be managed. Feral cats and foxes hunt bilbies, so fenced reserves that keep these predators out give bilbies a safe place to breed.\n\nSome people say the money would be better spent elsewhere. However, protecting one species also protects the whole habitat it shares with many others.\n\nEvery Australian who has ever eaten a chocolate Easter Bilby should ask: will the real one still be here for our grandchildren?"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'hard', 1, 'A story written in 1890 shows a girl who leaves school at ten to work on the family farm, and the narrator thinks this is normal. Why might a reader today react differently?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Today''s readers live in a different context, where all children are expected to finish school."},{"id":"b","text":"Today''s readers don''t know what farms are."},{"id":"c","text":"The story must have been written badly."},{"id":"d","text":"Readers in 1890 could not read."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Context shapes how we read. In 1890 this was common; today, readers expect children to stay at school, so they may feel the girl was treated unfairly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'hard', 1, 'How does the author create suspense at the end?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"by ending with a strange sign that raises questions without answering them"},{"id":"b","text":"by explaining exactly what will happen next"},{"id":"c","text":"by describing a happy birthday party"},{"id":"d","text":"by giving facts about rivers"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The circle of stones appears with no explanation. Readers want to know who the old woman is and what the stones mean, so they keep reading.', NULL, '{"title":"The River Stones","text":"The day Wren turned twelve, the river spoke to her.\n\nIt was not a loud voice. It came up through the soles of her bare feet as she waded across the shallows below Ironbark Falls: a cold, slow whisper that seemed to know her name.\n\n''The stones are moving,'' it said. ''Go to the old woman on the ridge before the moon is full.''\n\nWren stumbled backwards onto the bank, heart hammering. Behind her, the water ran on as if nothing had happened. But on the flat rock where she had been standing, five smooth river stones now lay in a perfect circle."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'hard', 1, 'Which persuasive devices does the writer use? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"a rhetorical question"},{"id":"b","text":"high-modality language such as ''must''"},{"id":"c","text":"a counter-argument followed by a rebuttal"},{"id":"d","text":"a personal story from the writer''s childhood"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'It ends with a rhetorical question, says ''We must act now'', and answers the money argument. There is no childhood story.', NULL, '{"title":"Bring Back the Bilby","text":"Bilbies once lived across most of mainland Australia. Today they survive in only a few small areas. We must act now to bring them back.\n\nFirstly, bilbies are ecosystem engineers. As they dig for food, they turn over large amounts of soil, which helps rain soak in and seeds sprout.\n\nSecondly, the main threats are known and can be managed. Feral cats and foxes hunt bilbies, so fenced reserves that keep these predators out give bilbies a safe place to breed.\n\nSome people say the money would be better spent elsewhere. However, protecting one species also protects the whole habitat it shares with many others.\n\nEvery Australian who has ever eaten a chocolate Easter Bilby should ask: will the real one still be here for our grandchildren?"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which source would have the most authority on how many bilbies are left in the wild?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a government environment report based on wildlife surveys"},{"id":"b","text":"an advertisement for chocolate Easter Bilbies"},{"id":"c","text":"a social media post with no source"},{"id":"d","text":"a novel with a bilby as the main character"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Authority comes from expertise and evidence. A report based on surveys by scientists is far more reliable than an ad, an unsourced post or fiction.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_hard', 1, 'One story is about a girl who keeps falling off her surfboard but refuses to give up. Another is about an old farmer who rebuilds his farm after a flood. What theme do they most likely share?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"persistence in the face of difficulty"},{"id":"b","text":"the dangers of the ocean"},{"id":"c","text":"growing up in the city"},{"id":"d","text":"the importance of money"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Different characters, settings and problems, but both keep going after setbacks. A theme is the big idea a text shares, here persistence.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Because Text A was written by the council that built the bypass, readers should consider that it may present mainly the positive side.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Who writes a text shapes its perspective. The council wants the bypass to look good, so it leaves out problems like the one Text B raises.', NULL, '{"title":"The Highway Bypass","text":"Text A – Council media release\n\nThe new highway bypass will open next month, cutting travel time between the two towns by twenty minutes. Mayor Linda Okafor said the bypass would ''remove heavy trucks from our main street and make it safer for families''. Construction created 300 local jobs.\n\nText B – Letter from a shop owner\n\nFor thirty-one years my bakery has stood on Main Street. Every holiday, travellers stopped for a pie and a chat. Once the bypass opens, those cars will race past without even seeing our town''s name. The council talks about safety, but it hasn''t said how small businesses like mine will survive."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which genre is ''Gold Fever''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"historical fiction"},{"id":"b","text":"science fiction"},{"id":"c","text":"fantasy"},{"id":"d","text":"information report"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It is an invented story with made-up characters, but it is set in a real time and place from the past: the 1851 gold rush. That is historical fiction.', NULL, '{"title":"Gold Fever","text":"Near Bathurst, 1851\n\nThomas had never seen so many people in one place. Men in mud-stiff trousers pushed past him, swinging picks and pans. Tents had sprouted along the creek like mushrooms after rain, and the air rang with shouting in a dozen languages.\n\n\"Stay close,\" Father said, gripping the handle of their cart. They had walked for nine days from Sydney, and Thomas''s boots had split at the toes.\n\nThat afternoon, Father paid thirty shillings for a mining licence. Thomas stared. That was nearly all the money they had left.\n\n\"What if we don''t find any gold?\" he whispered.\n\nFather looked at the crowded creek, then at Thomas, and for a moment his face looked older than Thomas had ever seen it. Then he smiled and handed Thomas a pan.\n\n\"Then at least we''ll know we tried,\" he said. \"That''s worth something too.\""}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_easy', 1, 'A legend is a traditional story that may be based partly on real people or events.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Legends, like the stories of Ned Kelly or Robin Hood, mix real people or events with parts that have grown bigger over many retellings.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_easy', 1, 'A persuasive text that argues for one side of an issue, with a thesis, arguments and a conclusion, is called an ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["exposition","exposition text"]'::jsonb, 'An exposition puts forward one point of view and backs it up with arguments. A discussion looks at both sides.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'easy', 1, 'What is the main purpose of Text A?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to persuade readers to book a whale-watching tour"},{"id":"b","text":"to explain the rules about boats and whales"},{"id":"c","text":"to describe the life cycle of humpback whales"},{"id":"d","text":"to warn readers that whale watching is dangerous"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Text A is a brochure that ends with ''Book today: seats sell out fast!'' Its purpose is to sell tours.', NULL, '{"title":"Whale Watching: Two Texts","text":"Text A – Tour company brochure\n\nSee the giants of the deep up close! Every winter, thousands of humpback whales swim past Eden on their journey north. Our fast, modern boats will take you right into the action, so you can feel the spray as a 40-tonne whale leaps from the water beside you. Book today: seats sell out fast!\n\nText B – Article by a marine scientist\n\nWhale watching can help people care about the ocean. However, boats that approach too quickly or too closely can stress whales, especially mothers with young calves. In NSW, rules say most boats must stay at least 100 metres from a whale, and even further from a calf. Visitors should choose operators who follow these rules and who treat whales as wild animals, not as a show."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence is the writer''s thesis, the main position the whole text argues for?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Swimming lessons should be part of every school year, from Kindergarten to Year 6."},{"id":"b","text":"Australia is surrounded by ocean and dotted with rivers, dams and backyard pools."},{"id":"c","text":"Private lessons can be expensive."},{"id":"d","text":"Some people argue that schools are already too busy."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The thesis comes at the end of the first paragraph and is repeated in the conclusion. The others are background, evidence or the opposing view.', NULL, '{"title":"Every Child Should Learn to Swim at School","text":"Australia is surrounded by ocean and dotted with rivers, dams and backyard pools. Yet many children leave primary school unable to swim fifty metres. Swimming lessons should be part of every school year, from Kindergarten to Year 6.\n\nThe most important reason is safety. Water is part of Australian life, and a child who can swim and float is far more likely to survive a fall into a river or a rip at the beach.\n\nIn addition, lessons through school reach every child. Private lessons can be expensive, and families who live far from a pool may find it hard to get there each week. School lessons make sure no child misses out because of money or distance.\n\nSome people argue that schools are already too busy and that swimming takes time away from maths and English. However, a skill that could save a life deserves a place in the timetable. After all, a child cannot learn fractions if they never get the chance to grow up.\n\nIf we truly care about children''s safety, we must make swimming lessons part of every school year."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'easy', 1, 'Put the parts of a news report in the usual order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"a headline"},{"id":"b","text":"a lead paragraph that sums up who, what, when and where"},{"id":"c","text":"more details and quotes from people involved"},{"id":"d","text":"a final paragraph about what will happen next"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A news report grabs attention with a headline, sums up the event in the lead, adds details and quotes, and often ends with what happens next.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'medium', 1, 'Which words or phrases in Text A are chosen to excite readers? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"giants of the deep"},{"id":"b","text":"right into the action"},{"id":"c","text":"feel the spray"},{"id":"d","text":"at least 100 metres"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '''Giants of the deep'', ''right into the action'' and ''feel the spray'' make the tour sound thrilling. ''At least 100 metres'' comes from Text B and is a rule, not an exciting phrase.', NULL, '{"title":"Whale Watching: Two Texts","text":"Text A – Tour company brochure\n\nSee the giants of the deep up close! Every winter, thousands of humpback whales swim past Eden on their journey north. Our fast, modern boats will take you right into the action, so you can feel the spray as a 40-tonne whale leaps from the water beside you. Book today: seats sell out fast!\n\nText B – Article by a marine scientist\n\nWhale watching can help people care about the ocean. However, boats that approach too quickly or too closely can stress whales, especially mothers with young calves. In NSW, rules say most boats must stay at least 100 metres from a whale, and even further from a calf. Visitors should choose operators who follow these rules and who treat whales as wild animals, not as a show."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'medium', 1, 'Which details show that the story is set in the past? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"the year 1851"},{"id":"b","text":"paying in shillings for a mining licence"},{"id":"c","text":"walking nine days from Sydney with a cart"},{"id":"d","text":"Thomas feels nervous"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The date, the old money and walking from Sydney with a cart all belong to the 1850s. Feeling nervous could happen in any time.', NULL, '{"title":"Gold Fever","text":"Near Bathurst, 1851\n\nThomas had never seen so many people in one place. Men in mud-stiff trousers pushed past him, swinging picks and pans. Tents had sprouted along the creek like mushrooms after rain, and the air rang with shouting in a dozen languages.\n\n\"Stay close,\" Father said, gripping the handle of their cart. They had walked for nine days from Sydney, and Thomas''s boots had split at the toes.\n\nThat afternoon, Father paid thirty shillings for a mining licence. Thomas stared. That was nearly all the money they had left.\n\n\"What if we don''t find any gold?\" he whispered.\n\nFather looked at the crowded creek, then at Thomas, and for a moment his face looked older than Thomas had ever seen it. Then he smiled and handed Thomas a pan.\n\n\"Then at least we''ll know we tried,\" he said. \"That''s worth something too.\""}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'medium', 1, 'Which words does the writer use to signal a second, extra argument?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"In addition"},{"id":"b","text":"However"},{"id":"c","text":"Yet"},{"id":"d","text":"After all"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''In addition'' adds another argument. ''However'' and ''Yet'' signal a contrast, and ''After all'' introduces a final reason.', NULL, '{"title":"Every Child Should Learn to Swim at School","text":"Australia is surrounded by ocean and dotted with rivers, dams and backyard pools. Yet many children leave primary school unable to swim fifty metres. Swimming lessons should be part of every school year, from Kindergarten to Year 6.\n\nThe most important reason is safety. Water is part of Australian life, and a child who can swim and float is far more likely to survive a fall into a river or a rip at the beach.\n\nIn addition, lessons through school reach every child. Private lessons can be expensive, and families who live far from a pool may find it hard to get there each week. School lessons make sure no child misses out because of money or distance.\n\nSome people argue that schools are already too busy and that swimming takes time away from maths and English. However, a skill that could save a life deserves a place in the timetable. After all, a child cannot learn fractions if they never get the chance to grow up.\n\nIf we truly care about children''s safety, we must make swimming lessons part of every school year."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'hard', 1, 'Why is Text B''s information about boats and whales more trustworthy than Text A''s?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It is written by a marine scientist, it refers to official rules, and the writer isn''t selling anything."},{"id":"b","text":"It is shorter than Text A."},{"id":"c","text":"It uses more exclamation marks."},{"id":"d","text":"It mentions the town of Eden."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Authority comes from expertise and evidence. A scientist quoting the rules has nothing to gain, while a tour company wants your money.', NULL, '{"title":"Whale Watching: Two Texts","text":"Text A – Tour company brochure\n\nSee the giants of the deep up close! Every winter, thousands of humpback whales swim past Eden on their journey north. Our fast, modern boats will take you right into the action, so you can feel the spray as a 40-tonne whale leaps from the water beside you. Book today: seats sell out fast!\n\nText B – Article by a marine scientist\n\nWhale watching can help people care about the ocean. However, boats that approach too quickly or too closely can stress whales, especially mothers with young calves. In NSW, rules say most boats must stay at least 100 metres from a whale, and even further from a calf. Visitors should choose operators who follow these rules and who treat whales as wild animals, not as a show."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'hard', 1, 'Which theme does ''Gold Fever'' explore?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"taking a brave risk in the hope of a better life"},{"id":"b","text":"the dangers of the ocean"},{"id":"c","text":"friendship between animals"},{"id":"d","text":"how gold is formed underground"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Father spends nearly all their money on a chance to find gold, and says trying is ''worth something too''. The story is about courage and hope when taking a risk.', NULL, '{"title":"Gold Fever","text":"Near Bathurst, 1851\n\nThomas had never seen so many people in one place. Men in mud-stiff trousers pushed past him, swinging picks and pans. Tents had sprouted along the creek like mushrooms after rain, and the air rang with shouting in a dozen languages.\n\n\"Stay close,\" Father said, gripping the handle of their cart. They had walked for nine days from Sydney, and Thomas''s boots had split at the toes.\n\nThat afternoon, Father paid thirty shillings for a mining licence. Thomas stared. That was nearly all the money they had left.\n\n\"What if we don''t find any gold?\" he whispered.\n\nFather looked at the crowded creek, then at Thomas, and for a moment his face looked older than Thomas had ever seen it. Then he smiled and handed Thomas a pan.\n\n\"Then at least we''ll know we tried,\" he said. \"That''s worth something too.\""}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'hard', 1, 'Which persuasive devices does the writer use? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"high-modality words such as ''must''"},{"id":"b","text":"a rebuttal of the opposing view"},{"id":"c","text":"an emotional appeal about children''s safety"},{"id":"d","text":"quotes from named experts"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The writer says ''we must'', answers the ''schools are too busy'' argument, and appeals to our feelings about children growing up. No experts are quoted.', NULL, '{"title":"Every Child Should Learn to Swim at School","text":"Australia is surrounded by ocean and dotted with rivers, dams and backyard pools. Yet many children leave primary school unable to swim fifty metres. Swimming lessons should be part of every school year, from Kindergarten to Year 6.\n\nThe most important reason is safety. Water is part of Australian life, and a child who can swim and float is far more likely to survive a fall into a river or a rip at the beach.\n\nIn addition, lessons through school reach every child. Private lessons can be expensive, and families who live far from a pool may find it hard to get there each week. School lessons make sure no child misses out because of money or distance.\n\nSome people argue that schools are already too busy and that swimming takes time away from maths and English. However, a skill that could save a life deserves a place in the timetable. After all, a child cannot learn fractions if they never get the chance to grow up.\n\nIf we truly care about children''s safety, we must make swimming lessons part of every school year."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Text A promises to take you ''right into the action''. After reading Text B, what might a careful reader question?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"whether the tour boats keep the required distance from the whales"},{"id":"b","text":"whether humpback whales really swim past Eden"},{"id":"c","text":"whether the boats are modern"},{"id":"d","text":"whether it is cold in winter"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Text B says boats must stay at least 100 metres away. ''Right into the action'' and ''beside you'' make you wonder if the tour gets too close.', NULL, '{"title":"Whale Watching: Two Texts","text":"Text A – Tour company brochure\n\nSee the giants of the deep up close! Every winter, thousands of humpback whales swim past Eden on their journey north. Our fast, modern boats will take you right into the action, so you can feel the spray as a 40-tonne whale leaps from the water beside you. Book today: seats sell out fast!\n\nText B – Article by a marine scientist\n\nWhale watching can help people care about the ocean. However, boats that approach too quickly or too closely can stress whales, especially mothers with young calves. In NSW, rules say most boats must stay at least 100 metres from a whale, and even further from a calf. Visitors should choose operators who follow these rules and who treat whales as wild animals, not as a show."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_hard', 1, 'How does knowing about the 1851 gold rush help a reader understand Father''s choice?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Thousands of people risked everything because finding gold could change a family''s life overnight."},{"id":"b","text":"Gold was lying on the ground for anyone to pick up."},{"id":"c","text":"Mining licences were free in 1851."},{"id":"d","text":"Everyone in 1851 was already rich."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Context shapes meaning. In the gold rush, a lucky find could make a poor family rich, so many people, like Father, gambled nearly all they had.', NULL, '{"title":"Gold Fever","text":"Near Bathurst, 1851\n\nThomas had never seen so many people in one place. Men in mud-stiff trousers pushed past him, swinging picks and pans. Tents had sprouted along the creek like mushrooms after rain, and the air rang with shouting in a dozen languages.\n\n\"Stay close,\" Father said, gripping the handle of their cart. They had walked for nine days from Sydney, and Thomas''s boots had split at the toes.\n\nThat afternoon, Father paid thirty shillings for a mining licence. Thomas stared. That was nearly all the money they had left.\n\n\"What if we don''t find any gold?\" he whispered.\n\nFather looked at the crowded creek, then at Thomas, and for a moment his face looked older than Thomas had ever seen it. Then he smiled and handed Thomas a pan.\n\n\"Then at least we''ll know we tried,\" he said. \"That''s worth something too.\""}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000006150330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000061503', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which piece of evidence would most strengthen the writer''s safety argument?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"figures from a water-safety organisation on how many children drown each year"},{"id":"b","text":"the writer''s opinion that swimming is fun"},{"id":"c","text":"a joke about fish"},{"id":"d","text":"a photo of the writer''s backyard pool"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Facts from an expert organisation give the argument authority. An opinion, a joke or a photo would not prove that lessons save lives.', NULL, '{"title":"Every Child Should Learn to Swim at School","text":"Australia is surrounded by ocean and dotted with rivers, dams and backyard pools. Yet many children leave primary school unable to swim fifty metres. Swimming lessons should be part of every school year, from Kindergarten to Year 6.\n\nThe most important reason is safety. Water is part of Australian life, and a child who can swim and float is far more likely to survive a fall into a river or a rip at the beach.\n\nIn addition, lessons through school reach every child. Private lessons can be expensive, and families who live far from a pool may find it hard to get there each week. School lessons make sure no child misses out because of money or distance.\n\nSome people argue that schools are already too busy and that swimming takes time away from maths and English. However, a skill that could save a life deserves a place in the timetable. After all, a child cannot learn fractions if they never get the chance to grow up.\n\nIf we truly care about children''s safety, we must make swimming lessons part of every school year."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_easy', 1, 'What integer is the dot on?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["−4","-4"]'::jsonb, 'Each jump is 1. The dot is 2 jumps to the right of −6, so it is on −4.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":-6,"to":4,"jumps":10,"dotAt":2,"labelEvery":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_easy', 1, '−12 is less than −3.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'On a number line −12 is further to the left than −3, so −12 is the smaller number.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_easy', 1, '−5 + 8 = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"3"'::jsonb, 'Start at −5 and move 8 to the right: 5 steps gets you to 0, and 3 more steps gets you to 3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'easy', 1, 'Put these integers in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"−11"},{"id":"b","text":"−7"},{"id":"c","text":"−2"},{"id":"d","text":"0"},{"id":"e","text":"5"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Negative numbers with a bigger number after the minus sign are further left on the number line, so −11 is smallest. Then −7, −2, 0 and 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'easy', 1, 'What is 4 − 9?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5"},{"id":"b","text":"−5"},{"id":"c","text":"13"},{"id":"d","text":"−13"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Start at 4 and move 9 to the left: 4 steps gets you to 0, and 5 more steps gets you to −5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'easy', 1, 'What is −6 × 3?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["−18","-18"]'::jsonb, '6 × 3 = 18. A negative times a positive gives a negative, so the answer is −18.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'medium', 1, '−7 − (−10) = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3","+3"]'::jsonb, 'Subtracting a negative is the same as adding: −7 − (−10) = −7 + 10 = 3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these have an answer of −12? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"−3 × 4"},{"id":"b","text":"(−3) × (−4)"},{"id":"c","text":"24 ÷ (−2)"},{"id":"d","text":"−6 + 6"},{"id":"e","text":"−20 + 8"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, '−3 × 4 = −12, 24 ÷ (−2) = −12 and −20 + 8 = −12. But (−3) × (−4) = 12, because two negatives make a positive, and −6 + 6 = 0.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'medium', 1, 'At midnight in Cooma it was −2°C. By 4 am the temperature had dropped 7 degrees. Use the number line to find the temperature at 4 am.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5°C"},{"id":"b","text":"−5°C"},{"id":"c","text":"−9°C"},{"id":"d","text":"9°C"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'Dropping means moving left. Start at −2 and move 7 to the left: −2 − 7 = −9, so it was −9°C.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":-10,"to":2,"jumps":12,"labelEvery":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'hard', 1, 'Work out −4 + 3 × (−5).', 'short_answer', NULL, '["−19","-19"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply first: 3 × (−5) = −15. Then −4 + (−15) = −19.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'hard', 1, '(−2) × (−3) × (−5) is a positive number.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '(−2) × (−3) = 6, which is positive. Then 6 × (−5) = −30, which is negative. An odd number of negative signs gives a negative answer.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'hard', 1, 'What is (−48) ÷ (−6) − 10?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"−18"},{"id":"b","text":"−2"},{"id":"c","text":"2"},{"id":"d","text":"18"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Divide first: (−48) ÷ (−6) = 8, because a negative divided by a negative is positive. Then 8 − 10 = −2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Two integers add to −3 and multiply to −40. What is the smaller of the two integers?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["−8","-8"]'::jsonb, 'The product is negative, so one number is negative and one is positive. Pairs that multiply to 40 include 5 and 8. 5 + (−8) = −3 and 5 × (−8) = −40, so the numbers are 5 and −8. The smaller one is −8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_hard', 1, 'A submarine is at −120 m (120 m below sea level). It rises 45 m, then dives 3 times as far as it rose. Where is it now?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"−210 m"},{"id":"b","text":"−30 m"},{"id":"c","text":"−120 m"},{"id":"d","text":"−255 m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Rising 45 m: −120 + 45 = −75. Diving 3 × 45 = 135 m: −75 − 135 = −210. It is at −210 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_hard', 1, '(−3)² − 4 × (−2) = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"17"'::jsonb, 'Square first: (−3)² = (−3) × (−3) = 9. Then multiply: 4 × (−2) = −8. Finally 9 − (−8) = 9 + 8 = 17.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_easy', 1, 'What integer is the dot on?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"−3"},{"id":"b","text":"−7"},{"id":"c","text":"7"},{"id":"d","text":"−6"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'The line goes from −10 to 0 in jumps of 1. The dot is 3 jumps to the right of −10, so it is on −7.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":-10,"to":0,"jumps":10,"dotAt":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is −8 + 3?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["−5","-5"]'::jsonb, 'Start at −8 and move 3 to the right: −7, −6, −5. The answer is −5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_easy', 1, '−1 is greater than −10.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '−1 is much closer to 0 and is further right on the number line than −10, so −1 is greater.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'easy', 1, 'Which integer is 4 less than −2?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2"},{"id":"b","text":"−6"},{"id":"c","text":"−2"},{"id":"d","text":"6"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '4 less means move 4 to the left: −2 − 4 = −6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'easy', 1, '−9 − 4 = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["−13","-13"]'::jsonb, 'Start at −9 and move 4 more to the left: −10, −11, −12, −13.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'easy', 1, 'Put these Snowy Mountains temperatures in order from warmest to coldest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"6°C"},{"id":"b","text":"0°C"},{"id":"c","text":"−4°C"},{"id":"d","text":"−9°C"},{"id":"e","text":"−15°C"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Positive temperatures are warmest, then 0°C. Below zero, the bigger the number after the minus sign, the colder it is, so −15°C is the coldest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'medium', 1, 'The dot shows where Tama starts. He moves 7 units right, then 2 units left. Which integer does he finish on?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1","+1"]'::jsonb, 'The dot is on −4. Moving 7 right: −4 + 7 = 3. Then 2 left: 3 − 2 = 1.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":-6,"to":6,"jumps":12,"dotAt":2,"labelEvery":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'medium', 1, 'What is (−56) ÷ 8?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["−7","-7"]'::jsonb, '56 ÷ 8 = 7. A negative divided by a positive is negative, so the answer is −7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these give a positive answer? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−5) × (−4)"},{"id":"b","text":"−5 × 4"},{"id":"c","text":"(−36) ÷ (−9)"},{"id":"d","text":"−3 − (−8)"},{"id":"e","text":"−10 + 2"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, '(−5) × (−4) = 20 and (−36) ÷ (−9) = 4, because two negatives give a positive. −3 − (−8) = −3 + 8 = 5. But −5 × 4 = −20 and −10 + 2 = −8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'hard', 1, '18 ÷ (−3) + (−2) × 7 = ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["-20","−20"]'::jsonb, 'Divide and multiply first: 18 ÷ (−3) = −6 and (−2) × 7 = −14. Then −6 + (−14) = −20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'hard', 1, '−2 − 5 × (−3) = 13', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Multiply first: 5 × (−3) = −15. Then −2 − (−15) = −2 + 15 = 13. So it is true.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'hard', 1, 'Overnight the temperature at Perisher fell from 3°C to −11°C. By how many degrees did it fall?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8"},{"id":"b","text":"14"},{"id":"c","text":"−8"},{"id":"d","text":"11"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'From 3 down to 0 is 3 degrees, and from 0 down to −11 is 11 degrees. 3 + 11 = 14 degrees.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_hard', 1, 'In a quiz, a right answer scores 4 points and a wrong answer scores −2 points. Mia answered 12 questions and got 5 right. What was her total score?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, '5 right: 5 × 4 = 20. 12 − 5 = 7 wrong: 7 × (−2) = −14. Total: 20 + (−14) = 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which expression has the smallest value?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−2)³"},{"id":"b","text":"−2 × 5"},{"id":"c","text":"(−3)²"},{"id":"d","text":"−4 − 5"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '(−2)³ = (−2) × (−2) × (−2) = −8. −2 × 5 = −10. (−3)² = 9. −4 − 5 = −9. The smallest is −10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007010130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070101', 'very_hard', 1, '−24 ÷ ___ = 6', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["−4","-4","(−4)","(-4)"]'::jsonb, 'The answer 6 is positive, so we must divide −24 by a negative number. 24 ÷ 4 = 6, so the missing number is −4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_easy', 1, 'What fraction of the bar is shaded? Write it in simplest form.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3/4","three quarters","three-quarters"]'::jsonb, '6 of the 8 parts are shaded, which is 6/8. Divide the top and bottom by 2 to get 3/4.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"bar","parts":8,"shaded":6}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_easy', 1, '2/5 is equivalent to 6/15.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Multiply the top and bottom of 2/5 by 3: 2 × 3 = 6 and 5 × 3 = 15. So 2/5 = 6/15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write 0.7 as a percentage.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7%"},{"id":"b","text":"70%"},{"id":"c","text":"0.7%"},{"id":"d","text":"700%"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'To change a decimal to a percentage, multiply by 100: 0.7 × 100 = 70, so 0.7 = 70%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'easy', 1, 'What is 3/8 + 1/8? Give your answer in simplest form.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/2","one half","a half"]'::jsonb, 'The denominators are the same, so add the numerators: 3/8 + 1/8 = 4/8. Then simplify: 4/8 = 1/2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'easy', 1, 'Put these in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.3"},{"id":"b","text":"1/3"},{"id":"c","text":"35%"},{"id":"d","text":"2/5"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to decimals: 0.3, 1/3 = 0.333…, 35% = 0.35 and 2/5 = 0.4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'easy', 1, 'Find 25% of $64.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["16","$16","$16.00"]'::jsonb, '25% is one quarter, so divide by 4: $64 ÷ 4 = $16.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'medium', 1, 'What is 2 1/3 + 1 1/2?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3 2/5"},{"id":"b","text":"3 5/6"},{"id":"c","text":"3 2/6"},{"id":"d","text":"4 1/6"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Add the whole numbers: 2 + 1 = 3. Then add the fractions using sixths: 1/3 + 1/2 = 2/6 + 3/6 = 5/6. So the answer is 3 5/6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'medium', 1, 'Write the number the dot is on as a decimal.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.6",".6"]'::jsonb, 'The line from 0 to 1 is cut into 5 equal jumps, so each jump is 1/5. The dot is on 3/5, and 3/5 = 6/10 = 0.6.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":0,"to":1,"jumps":5,"dotAt":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these fractions give a recurring decimal? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/3"},{"id":"b","text":"1/8"},{"id":"c","text":"2/11"},{"id":"d","text":"3/20"},{"id":"e","text":"5/6"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, '1/3 = 0.333…, 2/11 = 0.1818… and 5/6 = 0.8333… go on forever. 1/8 = 0.125 and 3/20 = 0.15 stop, because 8 and 20 have only 2s and 5s as prime factors.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'hard', 1, 'What is 3/4 × 2 2/3? Give your answer in simplest form.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2","2/1"]'::jsonb, 'Change the mixed numeral to an improper fraction: 2 2/3 = 8/3. Then 3/4 × 8/3 = 24/12 = 2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'hard', 1, 'What is 2 1/4 ÷ 3/8?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"27/32"},{"id":"b","text":"6"},{"id":"c","text":"1 1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"2/3"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Change 2 1/4 to 9/4. To divide by 3/8, multiply by its reciprocal 8/3: 9/4 × 8/3 = 72/12 = 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'hard', 1, 'At a school sausage sizzle, 240 sausage sandwiches were sold and 35% of them had onions. That is ___ sandwiches with onions.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"84"'::jsonb, '10% of 240 is 24, so 30% is 72 and 5% is 12. 35% is 72 + 12 = 84.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Liam spent 2/5 of his money on a cricket bat, then 1/4 of what was left on a ball. He has $54 left. How many dollars did he start with?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["120","$120"]'::jsonb, 'After the bat he had 3/5 left. The ball cost 1/4 of 3/5 = 3/20, so he has 3/5 − 3/20 = 12/20 − 3/20 = 9/20 left. 9/20 of his money is $54, so 1/20 is $6 and all of it is 20 × $6 = $120.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_hard', 1, 'An $80 netball is on sale for 15% off. Then a further 10% is taken off the sale price. What is the final price?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$60.00"},{"id":"b","text":"$61.20"},{"id":"c","text":"$62.00"},{"id":"d","text":"$58.80"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '15% of $80 is $12, so the sale price is $68. 10% of $68 is $6.80, so the final price is $68 − $6.80 = $61.20. (It is not 25% off, because the 10% comes off the smaller price.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_hard', 1, '7/9 is greater than 0.78.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '7/9 = 0.777…, a recurring decimal. Compare 0.777… with 0.780: the hundredths digit 7 is less than 8, so 7/9 is less than 0.78.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_easy', 1, 'What fraction of the circle is shaded, in simplest form?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2/5"},{"id":"b","text":"4/6"},{"id":"c","text":"2/3"},{"id":"d","text":"3/5"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '4 of the 10 parts are shaded, which is 4/10. Divide the top and bottom by 2 to get 2/5.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"circle","parts":10,"shaded":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_easy', 1, '3/4 = ___/20', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"15"'::jsonb, '4 × 5 = 20, so multiply the top by 5 too: 3 × 5 = 15. So 3/4 = 15/20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write 45% as a decimal.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.45",".45"]'::jsonb, 'Per cent means out of 100, so 45% = 45/100 = 0.45.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'easy', 1, 'What is 5/6 − 1/3?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4/3"},{"id":"b","text":"1/2"},{"id":"c","text":"4/6"},{"id":"d","text":"2/3"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Make the denominators the same: 1/3 = 2/6. Then 5/6 − 2/6 = 3/6, which simplifies to 1/2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'easy', 1, '1/8 written as a decimal is 0.125, which is a terminating decimal.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '1 ÷ 8 = 0.125. The digits stop after the 5, so it is a terminating decimal.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 3/5? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.6"},{"id":"b","text":"60%"},{"id":"c","text":"6/10"},{"id":"d","text":"0.35"},{"id":"e","text":"9/15"},{"id":"f","text":"35%"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","e"]'::jsonb, '3/5 = 6/10 = 0.6 = 60%, and 9/15 also simplifies to 3/5. 0.35 and 35% just use the digits 3 and 5, but are not equal to 3/5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'medium', 1, 'A farm in Dubbo harvested 450 kg of oranges. 30% were sold at the markets. How many kilograms were sold at the markets?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["135","135 kg"]'::jsonb, '10% of 450 is 45, so 30% is 3 × 45 = 135 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'medium', 1, 'Write the number the dot is on as a mixed numeral.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1 3/4","7/4","1.75"]'::jsonb, 'The line from 0 to 2 has 8 jumps, so each jump is 2 ÷ 8 = 1/4. The dot is 7 jumps along, at 7/4 = 1 3/4.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":0,"to":2,"jumps":8,"dotAt":7}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'medium', 1, 'What is 2/3 × 9/10? Give the answer in simplest form.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/5"},{"id":"b","text":"11/13"},{"id":"c","text":"27/20"},{"id":"d","text":"2/5"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Multiply tops and bottoms: 2 × 9 = 18 and 3 × 10 = 30, so 18/30. Divide both by 6 to get 3/5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'hard', 1, 'What is 3 1/4 − 1 2/3? Write your answer as a mixed numeral.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1 7/12","19/12"]'::jsonb, 'Change to improper fractions: 13/4 − 5/3. Use twelfths: 39/12 − 20/12 = 19/12 = 1 7/12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'hard', 1, 'Put these in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"5/8"},{"id":"b","text":"0.65"},{"id":"c","text":"2/3"},{"id":"d","text":"67%"},{"id":"e","text":"0.7"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Change each to a decimal: 5/8 = 0.625, 0.65, 2/3 = 0.666…, 67% = 0.67 and 0.7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'hard', 1, 'Ava scored 42 out of 56 on a maths test. What percentage did she get?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"75%"},{"id":"b","text":"42%"},{"id":"c","text":"56%"},{"id":"d","text":"70%"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '42/56 simplifies to 3/4 (divide both by 14). 3/4 = 75%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_hard', 1, 'A ribbon 5 1/4 m long is cut into pieces that are each 3/8 m long. How many pieces are there?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"14"'::jsonb, 'Divide: 5 1/4 ÷ 3/8 = 21/4 × 8/3 = 168/12 = 14 pieces.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which fraction gives a recurring decimal?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7/20"},{"id":"b","text":"3/16"},{"id":"c","text":"5/12"},{"id":"d","text":"9/25"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'A fraction in simplest form terminates only if its denominator has no prime factors except 2 and 5. 20, 16 and 25 pass, but 12 = 2 × 2 × 3 has a 3, so 5/12 = 0.41666… recurs.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007020130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070201', 'very_hard', 1, 'At a school fete, 3/8 of the cakes were sold in the morning. Then 2/5 of the cakes that were left were sold in the afternoon. 36 cakes were not sold. How many cakes were there at the start?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"96"'::jsonb, 'After the morning 5/8 were left. The afternoon sold 2/5 of 5/8 = 2/8, so 5/8 − 2/8 = 3/8 were not sold. 3/8 of the cakes is 36, so 1/8 is 12 and all the cakes are 8 × 12 = 96.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which expression means ''a number n increased by 7''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7n"},{"id":"b","text":"n + 7"},{"id":"c","text":"n − 7"},{"id":"d","text":"n ÷ 7"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Increased by 7 means add 7, so the expression is n + 7. 7n means 7 × n.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_easy', 1, 'If x = 6, what is the value of 4x?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"24"'::jsonb, '4x means 4 × x. Replace x with 6: 4 × 6 = 24.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_easy', 1, '3a and 5a are like terms.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Like terms have exactly the same pronumeral part. Both terms have just a, so they are like terms.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'easy', 1, 'Simplify 5m + 3m.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8m","8 m"]'::jsonb, '5m and 3m are like terms, so add the numbers in front: 5 + 3 = 8. The answer is 8m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'easy', 1, 'If p = 9, then p − 4 = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"5"'::jsonb, 'Replace p with 9: 9 − 4 = 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are like terms with 4y? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"−2y"},{"id":"b","text":"4x"},{"id":"c","text":"y"},{"id":"d","text":"4y²"},{"id":"e","text":"10y"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Like terms must have exactly y as the pronumeral part. −2y, y and 10y all do. 4x has x instead, and 4y² has y², which is different from y.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'medium', 1, 'Simplify 6a + 2b − 4a + 5b.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2a + 7b"},{"id":"b","text":"10a + 7b"},{"id":"c","text":"9ab"},{"id":"d","text":"2a + 3b"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Group the like terms: 6a − 4a = 2a and 2b + 5b = 7b. So the answer is 2a + 7b.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'medium', 1, 'Simplify 3 × 4k.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12k","12 k"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply the numbers: 3 × 4 = 12, and keep the k. So 3 × 4k = 12k.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. Write its perimeter in simplest form.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6a","6 a"]'::jsonb, 'The perimeter is all four sides added: 2a + a + 2a + a = 6a.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":6,"height":3,"top":"2a","side":"a"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'hard', 1, 'Find the value of 3a + 2b when a = 4 and b = −5.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"2"'::jsonb, '3a = 3 × 4 = 12 and 2b = 2 × (−5) = −10. Then 12 + (−10) = 2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'hard', 1, 'Simplify 12xy ÷ 4x.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3y"},{"id":"b","text":"3xy"},{"id":"c","text":"8y"},{"id":"d","text":"3"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Write it as a fraction: 12xy/4x. Divide the numbers: 12 ÷ 4 = 3. The x on top and bottom cancel, leaving y. So the answer is 3y.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'hard', 1, 'Ben has d dollars. He spends $15, then shares what is left equally with his sister. Which expression shows how much each of them gets?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(d − 15) ÷ 2"},{"id":"b","text":"d − 15 ÷ 2"},{"id":"c","text":"d ÷ 2 − 15"},{"id":"d","text":"2(d − 15)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'First take away 15: d − 15. Then share it between 2 people by dividing by 2. The brackets make sure the subtraction happens first: (d − 15) ÷ 2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Simplify 5x × 3y × 2x.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"30x²y"},{"id":"b","text":"10x²y"},{"id":"c","text":"30xy"},{"id":"d","text":"15x²y"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Multiply the numbers: 5 × 3 × 2 = 30. Then the pronumerals: x × y × x = x²y. So the answer is 30x²y.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_hard', 1, 'When n = −3, what is the value of n² − 2n + 5?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"20"'::jsonb, 'n² = (−3)² = 9. 2n = 2 × (−3) = −6, so −2n = +6. Then 9 + 6 + 5 = 20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which expressions simplify to 6x? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2x + 4x"},{"id":"b","text":"3x × 2x"},{"id":"c","text":"12x² ÷ 2x"},{"id":"d","text":"8x − 2"},{"id":"e","text":"3 × 2x"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, '2x + 4x = 6x. 12x² ÷ 2x = 6x, because 12 ÷ 2 = 6 and x² ÷ x = x. 3 × 2x = 6x. But 3x × 2x = 6x², and 8x − 2 cannot be simplified because 8x and 2 are not like terms.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_easy', 1, 'If y = 5, what is the value of y + 12?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"17"'::jsonb, 'Replace y with 5: 5 + 12 = 17.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_easy', 1, 'What does 3t mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3 + t"},{"id":"b","text":"3 × t"},{"id":"c","text":"t ÷ 3"},{"id":"d","text":"t − 3"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'When a number is written right next to a pronumeral, it means multiply. 3t means 3 × t.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_easy', 1, '2x and 2y are like terms.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Like terms need the same pronumeral part. 2x has x and 2y has y, so they are not like terms, even though both have a 2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'easy', 1, 'Which expression means ''double a number w, then subtract 3''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2w − 3"},{"id":"b","text":"2(w − 3)"},{"id":"c","text":"w² − 3"},{"id":"d","text":"3 − 2w"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Double w is 2w. Then subtract 3: 2w − 3. In 2(w − 3) the 3 is taken away before doubling, and w² means w × w, not double.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'easy', 1, 'Simplify 9c − 4c.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5c","5 c"]'::jsonb, '9c and 4c are like terms, so subtract the numbers in front: 9 − 4 = 5. The answer is 5c.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the table. What number goes where the ? is?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"16"'::jsonb, 'Substitute n = 5 into 3n + 1: 3 × 5 + 1 = 15 + 1 = 16.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["n","3n + 1"],"rows":[["1",4],["2",7],["3",10],["5","?"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'medium', 1, '4h + 7 + 2h − 3 simplifies to ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["6h + 4","6h+4","4 + 6h","4+6h"]'::jsonb, 'Collect the h terms: 4h + 2h = 6h. Collect the numbers: 7 − 3 = 4. So the answer is 6h + 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'medium', 1, 'Find the value of 5m − 8 when m = 3.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"7"'::jsonb, '5m = 5 × 3 = 15. Then 15 − 8 = 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'medium', 1, 'When x = 4, put these expressions in order from smallest to largest value.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"x − 5"},{"id":"b","text":"x ÷ 2"},{"id":"c","text":"3x − 2"},{"id":"d","text":"x²"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = 4: x − 5 = −1, x ÷ 2 = 2, 3x − 2 = 10 and x² = 16.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'hard', 1, 'Simplify 7a × 3b.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"21ab"},{"id":"b","text":"10ab"},{"id":"c","text":"21a + b"},{"id":"d","text":"73ab"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Multiply the numbers: 7 × 3 = 21. Then multiply the pronumerals: a × b = ab. So 7a × 3b = 21ab.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'hard', 1, 'Find the value of 2(p + q) when p = 7 and q = −10.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["−6","-6"]'::jsonb, 'Work out the brackets first: 7 + (−10) = −3. Then 2 × (−3) = −6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'hard', 1, 'A phone plan costs $20 a month plus $5 for each gigabyte (GB) of data used. Which expression gives the cost in dollars for one month when g GB are used?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"20 + 5g"},{"id":"b","text":"25g"},{"id":"c","text":"5 + 20g"},{"id":"d","text":"20g + 5g"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The $20 is paid once. Each GB costs $5, so g GB cost 5 × g = 5g. The total is 20 + 5g.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. Which expression is its perimeter, in simplest form?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8x + 4"},{"id":"b","text":"4x + 2"},{"id":"c","text":"8x + 2"},{"id":"d","text":"3x² + 6x"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The perimeter is 3x + (x + 2) + 3x + (x + 2). Collect the x terms: 3x + x + 3x + x = 8x. Collect the numbers: 2 + 2 = 4. So it is 8x + 4. (3x² + 6x would be the area.)', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":9,"height":4,"top":"3x","side":"x + 2"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Simplify 18a²b ÷ 6ab.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3a","3 a"]'::jsonb, 'Divide the numbers: 18 ÷ 6 = 3. Then a²b ÷ ab: a² ÷ a = a and b ÷ b = 1. So the answer is 3a.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Tickets to the Royal Easter Show cost a dollars for an adult and c dollars for a child. A family buys 2 adult and 3 child tickets and pays with a $100 note. Which expression shows their change in dollars?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"100 − (2a + 3c)"},{"id":"b","text":"100 − 2a + 3c"},{"id":"c","text":"(2a + 3c) − 100"},{"id":"d","text":"100 − 5ac"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The tickets cost 2a + 3c dollars altogether. Change is what they paid minus the cost, so 100 − (2a + 3c). The brackets matter: 100 − 2a + 3c would add the child tickets back on.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which shows 5 × 5 × 5 written in index notation?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5³"},{"id":"b","text":"3⁵"},{"id":"c","text":"5 × 3"},{"id":"d","text":"15"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '5 is multiplied by itself 3 times. The base is 5 and the index (power) is 3, so it is 5³.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the value of 6²?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"36"'::jsonb, '6² means 6 × 6, which is 36.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_easy', 1, 'The dots make a square with 4 in each row and 4 rows. So 4² = ___ dots.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"16"'::jsonb, '4² means 4 × 4. There are 4 rows of 4 dots, which is 16 dots.', '{"kind":"array","rows":4,"columns":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'easy', 1, 'What is √81?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"9"'::jsonb, '√81 asks: what number times itself makes 81? 9 × 9 = 81, so √81 = 9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'easy', 1, 'True or false: 2⁴ is equal to 4².', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '2⁴ = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16 and 4² = 4 × 4 = 16. They are equal.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are square numbers? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"49"},{"id":"b","text":"64"},{"id":"c","text":"90"},{"id":"d","text":"121"},{"id":"e","text":"50"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '49 = 7², 64 = 8² and 121 = 11². 90 and 50 are not any whole number times itself.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'medium', 1, 'What is the cube root of 64 (∛64)?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"4"'::jsonb, 'The cube root asks: what number times itself three times makes 64? 4 × 4 × 4 = 64, so ∛64 = 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'medium', 1, 'Put these in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"2⁵"},{"id":"b","text":"6²"},{"id":"c","text":"3⁴"},{"id":"d","text":"10²"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Work each one out: 2⁵ = 32, 6² = 36, 3⁴ = 81 and 10² = 100. So the order is 2⁵, 6², 3⁴, 10².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the table of cube numbers. The cube of 4 is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"64"'::jsonb, 'The cube of 4 is 4³ = 4 × 4 × 4 = 16 × 4 = 64.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Number","Cube"],"rows":[["1",1],["2",8],["3",27],["4","?"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the prime factorisation of 72 written with indices?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2³ × 3²"},{"id":"b","text":"2² × 3³"},{"id":"c","text":"2 × 36"},{"id":"d","text":"8 × 9"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '72 = 2 × 36 = 2 × 2 × 18 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 9 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × 3. That is three 2s and two 3s, so 2³ × 3². The others are not all primes, or give 108.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'hard', 1, 'Work out √49 + 3².', 'short_answer', NULL, '"16"'::jsonb, '√49 = 7 and 3² = 9. Then 7 + 9 = 16.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'hard', 1, '√30 is not a whole number. It lies between 5 and ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, '5² = 25 and 6² = 36. 30 is between 25 and 36, so √30 is between 5 and 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_hard', 1, 'A square vegetable garden at a school in Dubbo has an area of 144 m². What is its perimeter?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"48 m"},{"id":"b","text":"36 m"},{"id":"c","text":"72 m"},{"id":"d","text":"12 m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The side length is √144 = 12 m, because 12 × 12 = 144. A square has 4 equal sides, so the perimeter is 4 × 12 = 48 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_hard', 1, 'Write 2³ × 5² as an ordinary number.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"200"'::jsonb, '2³ = 8 and 5² = 25. Then 8 × 25 = 200.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the smallest whole number you can multiply 18 by to make a square number?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"2"'::jsonb, '18 = 2 × 3². The 3s are already a pair, but the 2 is on its own. Multiply by one more 2: 18 × 2 = 36 = 2² × 3² = 6².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_easy', 1, 'In 7⁴, what number is the base?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"7"'::jsonb, 'In index notation the big number is the base and the small raised number is the index. In 7⁴ the base is 7 and the index is 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the square on the grid. Its area is 3² square units. How many square units is that?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9","9 square units"]'::jsonb, 'The square is 3 units long and 3 units wide. 3² = 3 × 3 = 9, and you can count 9 small squares inside it.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[3,0],[3,3],[0,3]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_easy', 1, 'True or false: 3² means 3 × 2.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '3² means 3 multiplied by itself: 3 × 3 = 9. It does not mean 3 × 2, which is 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'easy', 1, 'What is the value of 4³?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"64"'::jsonb, '4³ = 4 × 4 × 4. 4 × 4 = 16, and 16 × 4 = 64.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'easy', 1, 'Which number is the same as 10⁴?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"10 000"},{"id":"b","text":"1000"},{"id":"c","text":"40"},{"id":"d","text":"100 000"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '10⁴ = 10 × 10 × 10 × 10 = 10 000. The index 4 tells you there are 4 zeros.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'easy', 1, 'What is √144?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"12"'::jsonb, '12 × 12 = 144, so the square root of 144 is 12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'medium', 1, '∛125 = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"5"'::jsonb, 'Find the number that times itself three times makes 125. 5 × 5 × 5 = 125, so ∛125 = 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are cube numbers? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"8"},{"id":"b","text":"27"},{"id":"c","text":"36"},{"id":"d","text":"100"},{"id":"e","text":"125"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, '8 = 2³, 27 = 3³ and 125 = 5³. 36 and 100 are square numbers, but not cube numbers.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the array of dots. Which shows the number of dots in index notation?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7²"},{"id":"b","text":"2⁷"},{"id":"c","text":"7 × 2"},{"id":"d","text":"14"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 7 rows with 7 dots in each, so 7 × 7 = 49 dots. 7 × 7 is written 7². (2⁷ would be 128.)', '{"kind":"array","rows":7,"columns":7}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the prime factorisation of 60 written with indices?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2² × 3 × 5"},{"id":"b","text":"2 × 3 × 10"},{"id":"c","text":"2² × 15"},{"id":"d","text":"4 × 3 × 5"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '60 = 2 × 30 = 2 × 2 × 15 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 5. Two 2s make 2², so 60 = 2² × 3 × 5. The other answers still have numbers that are not prime (10, 15 and 4).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'hard', 1, 'Put these in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"√64"},{"id":"b","text":"∛1000"},{"id":"c","text":"2⁴"},{"id":"d","text":"5²"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, '√64 = 8, ∛1000 = 10, 2⁴ = 16 and 5² = 25. So the order is √64, ∛1000, 2⁴, 5².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'hard', 1, 'Work out 2⁵ − 5².', 'short_answer', NULL, '"7"'::jsonb, '2⁵ = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32 and 5² = 25. Then 32 − 25 = 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_hard', 1, 'A cube-shaped gift box has a volume of 216 cm³. How long is each edge, in centimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","6 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The volume of a cube is edge × edge × edge, so the edge is ∛216. 6 × 6 × 6 = 216, so each edge is 6 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_hard', 1, 'True or false: √(9 + 16) is equal to √9 + √16.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '√(9 + 16) = √25 = 5. But √9 + √16 = 3 + 4 = 7. They are not equal, so you can''t split a square root across a plus sign.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007030230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070302', 'very_hard', 1, 'If 2ⁿ = 128, what is the value of n?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","n = 7","n=7"]'::jsonb, 'Keep doubling from 2: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128. That is 7 twos multiplied together, so 2⁷ = 128 and n = 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Solve x + 7 = 12.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","x = 5","x=5"]'::jsonb, 'Take 7 from both sides to get x on its own: x = 12 − 7 = 5. Check: 5 + 7 = 12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_easy', 1, 'True or false: x = 4 is the solution of 3x = 12.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = 4: 3 × 4 = 12. Both sides match, so it is the solution.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_easy', 1, 'If y − 6 = 10, then y = ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"16"'::jsonb, 'Add 6 to both sides: y = 10 + 6 = 16. Check: 16 − 6 = 10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'easy', 1, 'Solve 2x + 3 = 11.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"x = 4"},{"id":"b","text":"x = 7"},{"id":"c","text":"x = 5.5"},{"id":"d","text":"x = 16"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'First take 3 from both sides: 2x = 8. Then divide both sides by 2: x = 4. Check: 2 × 4 + 3 = 11.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the table. For which value of x does 3x − 2 = 10?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4","x = 4","x=4"]'::jsonb, 'Find 10 in the 3x − 2 column. It is next to x = 4. Check: 3 × 4 − 2 = 12 − 2 = 10.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["x","3x − 2"],"rows":[["1",1],["2",4],["3",7],["4",10]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'easy', 1, 'Solve n ÷ 5 = 6.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30","n = 30","n=30"]'::jsonb, 'Undo dividing by 5 by multiplying both sides by 5: n = 6 × 5 = 30. Check: 30 ÷ 5 = 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'medium', 1, 'Mia buys 4 meat pies at the school canteen and pays $14 altogether. Which equation shows the cost, p dollars, of one pie?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4p = 14"},{"id":"b","text":"p + 4 = 14"},{"id":"c","text":"p ÷ 4 = 14"},{"id":"d","text":"14p = 4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '4 pies at p dollars each cost 4 × p = 4p dollars, and that equals $14. So 4p = 14 (and each pie costs $3.50).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'medium', 1, 'Which equations have x = 3 as their solution? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2x + 1 = 7"},{"id":"b","text":"5x = 20"},{"id":"c","text":"x − 8 = −5"},{"id":"d","text":"4x − 2 = 10"},{"id":"e","text":"x ÷ 3 = 9"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = 3 in each: 2 × 3 + 1 = 7 ✓, 5 × 3 = 15 not 20, 3 − 8 = −5 ✓, 4 × 3 − 2 = 10 ✓, 3 ÷ 3 = 1 not 9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'medium', 1, 'Put the steps for solving 3x − 4 = 11 in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Add 4 to both sides: 3x = 15"},{"id":"b","text":"Divide both sides by 3: x = 5"},{"id":"c","text":"Check: 3 × 5 − 4 = 11"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Undo the operations in reverse order: first undo − 4 by adding 4, then undo × 3 by dividing by 3. Last, substitute to check.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'hard', 1, 'Solve 5x − 8 = 27.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","x = 7","x=7"]'::jsonb, 'Add 8 to both sides: 5x = 35. Divide both sides by 5: x = 7. Check: 5 × 7 − 8 = 35 − 8 = 27.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'hard', 1, 'Solve (x + 4) ÷ 3 = 6.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["14","x = 14","x=14"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply both sides by 3: x + 4 = 18. Take 4 from both sides: x = 14. Check: (14 + 4) ÷ 3 = 18 ÷ 3 = 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'hard', 1, 'The perimeter of this rectangle is 30 cm. What is the value of x?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","x = 6","x=6"]'::jsonb, 'Perimeter = 2 lengths + 2 widths: (x + 3) + (x + 3) + x + x = 4x + 6. So 4x + 6 = 30, 4x = 24 and x = 6. Check: the sides are 9 cm and 6 cm, and 9 + 9 + 6 + 6 = 30.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":9,"height":6,"top":"(x + 3) cm","side":"x cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Jack is 3 times as old as his sister Ruby. Their ages add to 48. How old is Jack?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["36","36 years"]'::jsonb, 'Let Ruby be r years old, so Jack is 3r. Then r + 3r = 48, so 4r = 48 and r = 12. Jack is 3 × 12 = 36. Check: 12 + 36 = 48.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Solve 7 − 2x = 15.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"x = −4"},{"id":"b","text":"x = 4"},{"id":"c","text":"x = 11"},{"id":"d","text":"x = −11"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Take 7 from both sides: −2x = 8. Divide both sides by −2: x = −4. Check: 7 − 2 × (−4) = 7 + 8 = 15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_hard', 1, 'A taxi in Newcastle charges $4 to start plus $2.50 for each kilometre. Lena''s trip cost $29. How many kilometres did she travel?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["10","10 km"]'::jsonb, 'Let k be the kilometres: 4 + 2.5k = 29. Take 4 from both sides: 2.5k = 25. Divide by 2.5: k = 10. Check: 4 + 2.50 × 10 = 29.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Solve 4x = 28.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","x = 7","x=7"]'::jsonb, '4x means 4 × x. Divide both sides by 4: x = 28 ÷ 4 = 7. Check: 4 × 7 = 28.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Solve m − 9 = 15.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"m = 24"},{"id":"b","text":"m = 6"},{"id":"c","text":"m = −6"},{"id":"d","text":"m = 135"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Add 9 to both sides: m = 15 + 9 = 24. Check: 24 − 9 = 15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_easy', 1, 'True or false: if 2 + k = 9, then k = 11.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Substitute k = 11: 2 + 11 = 13, not 9. The solution is k = 9 − 2 = 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'easy', 1, 'If 3y + 2 = 20, then y = ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, 'Take 2 from both sides: 3y = 18. Divide by 3: y = 6. Check: 3 × 6 + 2 = 20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'easy', 1, 'Sam thinks of a number n, doubles it and then adds 5. The answer is 17. Which equation matches?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2n + 5 = 17"},{"id":"b","text":"n + 2 + 5 = 17"},{"id":"c","text":"2(n + 5) = 17"},{"id":"d","text":"5n + 2 = 17"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Doubling n gives 2n. Adding 5 after that gives 2n + 5. That equals 17, so 2n + 5 = 17.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'easy', 1, 'Solve x ÷ 4 = 9.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["36","x = 36","x=36"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply both sides by 4: x = 9 × 4 = 36. Check: 36 ÷ 4 = 9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'medium', 1, 'The perimeter of this triangle is 24 cm. What is the value of x?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","x = 7","x=7"]'::jsonb, 'Add the sides: x + x + 10 = 24, so 2x + 10 = 24. Take 10 from both sides: 2x = 14. Divide by 2: x = 7.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[11,1],[6,6]],"sideLabels":["10 cm","x cm","x cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'medium', 1, 'Which value of p makes 9p − 7 = 47 true?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"p = 6"},{"id":"b","text":"p = 5"},{"id":"c","text":"p = 4"},{"id":"d","text":"p = 7"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Substitute each: 9 × 6 − 7 = 54 − 7 = 47 ✓. 9 × 5 − 7 = 38, 9 × 4 − 7 = 29 and 9 × 7 − 7 = 56.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'medium', 1, 'Put these equations in order of their solutions, from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"x + 5 = 6"},{"id":"b","text":"2x = 8"},{"id":"c","text":"x − 3 = 4"},{"id":"d","text":"3x = 30"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Solve each one: x + 5 = 6 gives x = 1, 2x = 8 gives x = 4, x − 3 = 4 gives x = 7, and 3x = 30 gives x = 10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'hard', 1, 'Solve 4(x − 2) = 20.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","x = 7","x=7"]'::jsonb, 'Divide both sides by 4: x − 2 = 5. Add 2 to both sides: x = 7. Check: 4 × (7 − 2) = 4 × 5 = 20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'hard', 1, 'True or false: x = −2 is the solution of 3x + 10 = 4.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = −2: 3 × (−2) + 10 = −6 + 10 = 4. Both sides match, so it is the solution.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'hard', 1, 'A netball club made $180 at a sausage sizzle. It keeps $30 and shares the rest equally, giving $25 to each team. How many teams are there?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","6 teams"]'::jsonb, 'Let t be the number of teams: 30 + 25t = 180. Take 30 from both sides: 25t = 150. Divide by 25: t = 6. Check: 30 + 25 × 6 = 180.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_hard', 1, 'The sum of three consecutive whole numbers is 87. What is the smallest of the three numbers?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"28"'::jsonb, 'Call the numbers n, n + 1 and n + 2. Then 3n + 3 = 87, so 3n = 84 and n = 28. Check: 28 + 29 + 30 = 87.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_hard', 1, 'Solve (2x − 3) ÷ 5 = −1.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"x = −1"},{"id":"b","text":"x = 1"},{"id":"c","text":"x = 4"},{"id":"d","text":"x = −4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Multiply both sides by 5: 2x − 3 = −5. Add 3: 2x = −2. Divide by 2: x = −1. Check: (2 × (−1) − 3) ÷ 5 = −5 ÷ 5 = −1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007040130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070401', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangular paddock near Tamworth is 3 times as long as it is wide. Its perimeter is 96 m. How long is the paddock?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["36","36 m"]'::jsonb, 'Perimeter = 3w + w + 3w + w = 8w. So 8w = 96 and w = 12 m. The length is 3 × 12 = 36 m. Check: 36 + 12 + 36 + 12 = 96.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":9,"height":3,"top":"3w m","side":"w m"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Convert 3.5 m to centimetres.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["350","350 cm"]'::jsonb, 'There are 100 cm in 1 m, so multiply by 100: 3.5 × 100 = 350 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_easy', 1, 'The ruler is marked in centimetres. How long is the ribbon in millimetres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["75","75 mm"]'::jsonb, 'The ribbon reaches halfway between 7 and 8, so it is 7.5 cm. There are 10 mm in 1 cm, so 7.5 × 10 = 75 mm.', '{"kind":"ruler","cm":10,"length":7.5,"object":"ribbon"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the name of a straight line from the centre of a circle to its edge?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Radius"},{"id":"b","text":"Diameter"},{"id":"c","text":"Circumference"},{"id":"d","text":"Chord"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The radius goes from the centre out to the edge. The diameter goes right across through the centre, and the circumference is the distance around.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'easy', 1, 'How many metres are there in 2.4 km?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2400","2400 m"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1000 m in 1 km, so multiply by 1000: 2.4 × 1000 = 2400 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'easy', 1, 'What is the perimeter of this rectangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["26","26 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Perimeter is the distance all the way around: 8 + 5 + 8 + 5 = 26 cm.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":8,"height":5,"top":"8 cm","side":"5 cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'easy', 1, 'True or false: the diameter of a circle is twice as long as its radius.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The diameter goes from the edge through the centre to the other edge, which is two radii. So d = 2r.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'medium', 1, 'All the corners of this shape are right angles. What is its perimeter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["28","28 m"]'::jsonb, 'Find the missing sides first. The short top side is 8 − 4 = 4 m and the inside upright side is 6 − 3 = 3 m. Then add all six: 8 + 3 + 4 + 3 + 4 + 6 = 28 m.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[8,3],[4,3],[4,6],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["8 m","3 m","","","4 m","6 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'medium', 1, 'A circle has a diameter of 10 cm. Using π ≈ 3.14, what is its circumference?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"31.4 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"15.7 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"62.8 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"78.5 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Use C = πd: C = 3.14 × 10 = 31.4 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'medium', 1, 'Put these lengths in order from shortest to longest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"450 mm"},{"id":"b","text":"0.5 m"},{"id":"c","text":"62 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"1.2 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to centimetres: 450 mm = 45 cm, 0.5 m = 50 cm, 62 cm, and 1.2 m = 120 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'hard', 1, 'A circle has a radius of 7 cm. Find its circumference, correct to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["44.0","44","44.0 cm","43.96","43.98"]'::jsonb, 'The diameter is 2 × 7 = 14 cm. C = πd = 3.14 × 14 = 43.96, which rounds to 44.0 cm. (Using the π button gives 43.98..., also 44.0 cm.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are the same length as 1.5 km? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"1500 m"},{"id":"b","text":"150 000 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"15 000 mm"},{"id":"d","text":"1 500 000 mm"},{"id":"e","text":"150 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '1.5 km = 1.5 × 1000 = 1500 m. Then 1500 m × 100 = 150 000 cm, and 1500 m × 1000 = 1 500 000 mm. 15 000 mm is only 15 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'hard', 1, 'All the corners of this shape are right angles. What is its perimeter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["32","32 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The missing middle step is 9 − 3 − 3 = 3 cm across. The missing left side is 7 − 3 − 2 = 2 cm up. Add all eight sides: 9 + 7 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 2 + 3 + 2 = 32 cm.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[9,0],[9,7],[6,7],[6,4],[3,4],[3,2],[0,2]],"sideLabels":["9 cm","7 cm","3 cm","3 cm","","2 cm","3 cm",""]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bike wheel has a diameter of 60 cm. Using π ≈ 3.14, how many metres does the bike travel in 50 full turns of the wheel?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["94.2","94.2 m","94.25"]'::jsonb, 'One turn moves the bike one circumference: C = 3.14 × 60 = 188.4 cm. 50 turns: 188.4 × 50 = 9420 cm. Divide by 100 to get metres: 94.2 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_hard', 1, 'A semicircle has a diameter of 20 cm. Using π ≈ 3.14, what is its perimeter (curved edge plus straight edge)?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"51.4 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"31.4 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"62.8 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"41.4 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The curved edge is half a circumference: 3.14 × 20 ÷ 2 = 31.4 cm. Add the straight diameter: 31.4 + 20 = 51.4 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_hard', 1, 'A circular running track has a circumference of 400 m. What is its diameter, to the nearest metre?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["127","127 m"]'::jsonb, 'C = πd, so d = C ÷ π = 400 ÷ 3.14 ≈ 127.4, which rounds to 127 m. (The π button gives 127.3, also 127 m.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Convert 64 mm to centimetres.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6.4","6.4 cm"]'::jsonb, 'There are 10 mm in 1 cm, so divide by 10: 64 ÷ 10 = 6.4 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the distance all the way around a circle called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Circumference"},{"id":"b","text":"Radius"},{"id":"c","text":"Diameter"},{"id":"d","text":"Area"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The perimeter of a circle has a special name: the circumference.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the perimeter of this triangle?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["18","18 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Add the three sides: 7 + 5 + 6 = 18 cm.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[8,1],[5.3,5.2]],"sideLabels":["7 cm","5 cm","6 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'easy', 1, 'Convert 7250 m to kilometres.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7.25","7.25 km"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1000 m in 1 km, so divide by 1000: 7250 ÷ 1000 = 7.25 km.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'easy', 1, 'Look at this L-shaped garden bed. What is its perimeter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["26","26 m"]'::jsonb, 'Add all six sides: 6 + 2 + 4 + 5 + 2 + 7 = 26 m.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[6,2],[2,2],[2,7],[0,7]],"sideLabels":["6 m","2 m","4 m","5 m","2 m","7 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'easy', 1, 'True or false: a chord of a circle always passes through the centre.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'A chord is any straight line joining two points on the circle. Only the longest chord, the diameter, passes through the centre.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'medium', 1, 'A round backyard trampoline has a diameter of 4 m. Using π ≈ 3.14, what is its circumference?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"12.56 m"},{"id":"b","text":"6.28 m"},{"id":"c","text":"25.12 m"},{"id":"d","text":"8 m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Use C = πd: C = 3.14 × 4 = 12.56 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'medium', 1, 'Put these lengths in order from longest to shortest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"3.2 km"},{"id":"b","text":"850 m"},{"id":"c","text":"8500 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"0.9 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to metres: 3.2 km = 3200 m, 850 m, 8500 cm = 85 m, and 0.9 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are the same length as 45 cm? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"450 mm"},{"id":"b","text":"0.45 m"},{"id":"c","text":"4.5 m"},{"id":"d","text":"0.00045 km"},{"id":"e","text":"4500 mm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '45 cm × 10 = 450 mm. 45 cm ÷ 100 = 0.45 m. 0.45 m ÷ 1000 = 0.00045 km. 4.5 m is 450 cm and 4500 mm is 450 cm, so those are too long.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'hard', 1, 'Find the circumference of a circle with a radius of 4.5 cm, correct to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["28.3","28.3 cm","28.26","28.27"]'::jsonb, 'The diameter is 2 × 4.5 = 9 cm. C = πd = 3.14 × 9 = 28.26, which rounds to 28.3 cm. (The π button gives 28.27..., also 28.3 cm.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'hard', 1, 'All the corners of this shape are right angles. What is its perimeter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["36","36 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The short top side is 11 − 7 = 4 cm and the inside upright side is 7 − 3 = 4 cm. Add all six: 11 + 3 + 4 + 4 + 7 + 7 = 36 cm.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[11,0],[11,3],[7,3],[7,7],[0,7]],"sideLabels":["11 cm","3 cm","","","7 cm","7 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'hard', 1, 'A square tile has a perimeter of 3.6 m. How long is each side in centimetres?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"90 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"9 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"36 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"900 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each side is 3.6 ÷ 4 = 0.9 m. Change to centimetres: 0.9 × 100 = 90 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_hard', 1, 'A circle has a circumference of 50.24 cm. Using π ≈ 3.14, what is its radius?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","8 cm"]'::jsonb, 'C = πd, so d = 50.24 ÷ 3.14 = 16 cm. The radius is half the diameter: 16 ÷ 2 = 8 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_hard', 1, 'All the corners of this stepped shape are right angles, but only two sides are marked. What is its perimeter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["32","32 m"]'::jsonb, 'The steps across the top add up to the 10 m bottom, and the steps going up add up to the 6 m side. So the perimeter is 10 + 10 + 6 + 6 = 32 m.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[10,6],[7,6],[7,4],[4,4],[4,2],[0,2]],"sideLabels":["10 m","6 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070501', 'very_hard', 1, 'A quarter circle has a radius of 10 cm. Using π ≈ 3.14, what is its perimeter (curved edge plus the two straight edges)?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"35.7 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"15.7 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"31.4 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"25.7 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The full circumference is 3.14 × 20 = 62.8 cm, so the curved edge is 62.8 ÷ 4 = 15.7 cm. Add the two radii: 15.7 + 10 + 10 = 35.7 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["45","45 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'Area of a rectangle = length × width = 9 × 5 = 45 cm².', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":9,"height":5,"top":"9 cm","side":"5 cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_easy', 1, 'A triangle has a base of 8 cm and a perpendicular height of 5 cm. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"20 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"40 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"13 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"26 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area of a triangle = ½ × base × height = ½ × 8 × 5 = 20 cm². 40 cm² forgets to halve.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_easy', 1, '1 m² is the same as 100 cm².', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '1 m = 100 cm, so a 1 m by 1 m square is 100 cm by 100 cm. That is 100 × 100 = 10 000 cm², not 100 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the parallelogram. Each square is 1 m². What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["35","35 m²"]'::jsonb, 'The base is 7 squares long and the perpendicular height is 5 squares. Area = base × height = 7 × 5 = 35 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[8,1],[10,6],[3,6]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'easy', 1, '1 hectare = ___ m²', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["10 000","10000"]'::jsonb, 'A hectare is a square 100 m by 100 m, so it is 100 × 100 = 10 000 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these area formulas are correct? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rectangle: A = l × w"},{"id":"b","text":"Triangle: A = ½ × b × h"},{"id":"c","text":"Parallelogram: A = b × slanting side"},{"id":"d","text":"Trapezium: A = ½ × (a + b) × h"},{"id":"e","text":"Kite: A = x + y, where x and y are the diagonals"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'A parallelogram uses the perpendicular height, not the slanting side. A kite''s area is ½ × x × y, half the product of its diagonals. The other three are right.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'medium', 1, 'This trapezium has parallel sides of 8 m and 4 m, and a perpendicular height of 4 m. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["24","24 m²"]'::jsonb, 'Area of a trapezium = ½ × (a + b) × h = ½ × (8 + 4) × 4 = ½ × 12 × 4 = 24 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[2,1],[10,1],[8,5],[4,5]],"sideLabels":["8 m","","4 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'medium', 1, 'A rhombus has diagonals of 10 cm and 6 cm. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"30 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"60 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"16 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"32 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area of a rhombus = ½ × diagonal × diagonal = ½ × 10 × 6 = 30 cm². 60 cm² forgets to halve.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'medium', 1, '3.5 cm² = ___ mm²', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"350"'::jsonb, '1 cm = 10 mm, so 1 cm² = 10 × 10 = 100 mm². 3.5 × 100 = 350 mm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the shape. All its corners are right angles. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["46","46 m²"]'::jsonb, 'Split it into two rectangles. The bottom one is 10 × 3 = 30 m². The top one is 4 × 4 = 16 m². 30 + 16 = 46 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[10,3],[4,3],[4,7],[0,7]],"sideLabels":["10 m","3 m","6 m","4 m","4 m","7 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'hard', 1, 'Put these areas in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"40 000 mm²"},{"id":"b","text":"2500 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"0.3 m²"},{"id":"d","text":"5000 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to m². 40 000 mm² = 400 cm² = 0.04 m². 2500 cm² = 0.25 m². 5000 cm² = 0.5 m². So 0.04, 0.25, 0.3, 0.5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'hard', 1, 'A triangle with a base of 12 cm and a height of 7 cm has the same area as a parallelogram with a base of 6 cm and a height of 7 cm.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Triangle: ½ × 12 × 7 = 42 cm². Parallelogram: 6 × 7 = 42 cm². They are the same.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A paddock near Dubbo is a rectangle 400 m long and 250 m wide. What is its area in hectares?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["10","10 ha"]'::jsonb, 'Area = 400 × 250 = 100 000 m². Each hectare is 10 000 m², so 100 000 ÷ 10 000 = 10 ha.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A trapezium has an area of 60 cm². Its parallel sides are 7 cm and 13 cm. What is its perpendicular height in cm?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","6 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Area = ½ × (7 + 13) × h = 10 × h. So 10 × h = 60, which gives h = 6 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the end wall of this shed. The top of the roof is 7 m above the ground. What is the area of the wall?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"44 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"56 m²"},{"id":"c","text":"60 m²"},{"id":"d","text":"32 m²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Split it into a rectangle and a triangle. Rectangle: 8 × 4 = 32 m². The triangle''s height is 7 − 4 = 3 m, so it is ½ × 8 × 3 = 12 m². 32 + 12 = 44 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,0],[9,0],[9,4],[5,7],[1,4]],"sideLabels":["8 m","4 m","","","4 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the triangle. Each square is 1 cm². What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["20","20 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'The base is 8 squares and the perpendicular height is 5 squares. Area = ½ × 8 × 5 = 20 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[2,5]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Area is measured in square units, such as cm², m² and hectares.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Area counts how many squares cover a surface, so it uses square units like cm² and m². A hectare is 10 000 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_easy', 1, 'A parallelogram has a base of 9 cm and a perpendicular height of 4 cm. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"36 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"18 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"26 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"13 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height = 9 × 4 = 36 cm². It is not halved like a triangle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'easy', 1, '1 cm² = ___ mm²', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"100"'::jsonb, '1 cm = 10 mm, so a 1 cm square is 10 mm by 10 mm. 10 × 10 = 100 mm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the kite. Each square is 1 cm². Its diagonals are 7 cm and 6 cm. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["21","21 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'Area of a kite = ½ × diagonal × diagonal = ½ × 7 × 6 = 21 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[5,0],[8,5],[5,7],[2,5]],"grid":true}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'easy', 1, 'Put these units of area in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"mm²"},{"id":"b","text":"cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"m²"},{"id":"d","text":"ha"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, '1 cm² = 100 mm², 1 m² = 10 000 cm², and 1 ha = 10 000 m². So mm² is the smallest and the hectare is the largest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the trapezium. The 5 cm side is at right angles to the parallel sides. What is its area?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["40","40 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'The parallel sides are 10 cm and 6 cm and the height is 5 cm. Area = ½ × (10 + 6) × 5 = 8 × 5 = 40 cm².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[10,5],[4,5]],"sideLabels":["10 cm","5 cm","6 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these shapes have an area of 24 cm²? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"A rectangle 6 cm by 4 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"A triangle with base 6 cm and height 8 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"A parallelogram with base 12 cm and height 2 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"A rhombus with diagonals 6 cm and 4 cm"},{"id":"e","text":"A square with sides of 6 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Rectangle: 6 × 4 = 24. Triangle: ½ × 6 × 8 = 24. Parallelogram: 12 × 2 = 24. Rhombus: ½ × 6 × 4 = 12. Square: 6 × 6 = 36.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'medium', 1, 'A vineyard in the Hunter Valley covers 4.2 hectares. How many square metres is that?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["42 000","42000","42 000 m²"]'::jsonb, '1 ha = 10 000 m², so 4.2 ha = 4.2 × 10 000 = 42 000 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the shape. All its corners are right angles. What is its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"57 m²"},{"id":"b","text":"66 m²"},{"id":"c","text":"48 m²"},{"id":"d","text":"63 m²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The whole rectangle would be 11 × 6 = 66 m². The gap cut out of the top is 3 × 3 = 9 m². 66 − 9 = 57 m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[11,0],[11,6],[7,6],[7,3],[4,3],[4,6],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["11 m","6 m","4 m","3 m","3 m","3 m","4 m","6 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'hard', 1, 'A rectangle measuring 2 m by 50 cm has an area of 100 cm².', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Use the same units first. 2 m = 200 cm, so the area is 200 × 50 = 10 000 cm², which is 1 m². 100 cm² multiplies 2 by 50 without changing units.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'hard', 1, 'This triangle has an area of 36 cm² and a base of 9 cm. What is its perpendicular height?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"4 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"18 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"27 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area = ½ × base × height, so 36 = ½ × 9 × h. Double the area: 72 = 9 × h, so h = 72 ÷ 9 = 8 cm. 4 cm forgets the ½.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[9,0],[3,8]],"sideLabels":["9 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A school oval is a rectangle 120 m long and 75 m wide. What is its area in hectares?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.9","0.9 ha",".9"]'::jsonb, 'Area = 120 × 75 = 9000 m². Divide by 10 000 to get hectares: 9000 ÷ 10 000 = 0.9 ha.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rhombus has diagonals of 8 cm and 16 cm. A square has the same area as the rhombus. How long is each side of the square?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","8 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Area of the rhombus = ½ × 8 × 16 = 64 cm². A square with area 64 cm² has sides of 8 cm, because 8 × 8 = 64.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007050230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A garden bed is a trapezium with parallel sides of 6 m and 10 m and a perpendicular height of 4 m. A rectangular pond 2 m by 1.5 m sits inside it. What area of the bed (in m²) needs mulch?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["29","29 m²"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezium: ½ × (6 + 10) × 4 = 32 m². Pond: 2 × 1.5 = 3 m². Area to mulch = 32 − 3 = 29 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_easy', 1, 'What type of angle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Acute"},{"id":"b","text":"Right"},{"id":"c","text":"Obtuse"},{"id":"d","text":"Reflex"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'The angle is bigger than a right angle (90°) but smaller than a straight angle (180°), so it is obtuse.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":125}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the complement of 35°?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["55","55°"]'::jsonb, 'Complementary angles add to 90°. 90 − 35 = 55°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_easy', 1, 'When two straight lines cross, the vertically opposite angles are equal.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The angles straight across from each other at the crossing are always the same size.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'easy', 1, 'The flat arm of this angle is extended to make a straight line. The new angle next to the 112° angle is ___°.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["68","68°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles on a straight line add to 180°. 180 − 112 = 68°.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":112,"label":"112°"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the triangle. Find the size of angle x in degrees.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["70","70°"]'::jsonb, 'The angles in a triangle add to 180°. 65 + 45 = 110, and 180 − 110 = 70°.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[3,6]],"cornerLabels":["65°","45°","x"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these pairs of angles are supplementary? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"100° and 80°"},{"id":"b","text":"45° and 45°"},{"id":"c","text":"130° and 50°"},{"id":"d","text":"90° and 90°"},{"id":"e","text":"120° and 70°"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Supplementary angles add to 180°. 100 + 80, 130 + 50 and 90 + 90 all make 180. 45 + 45 = 90 (complementary) and 120 + 70 = 190.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'medium', 1, 'Three angles meet at a point. Two of them are 140° and 95°. What is the third angle in degrees?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["125","125°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles at a point add to 360°. 140 + 95 = 235, and 360 − 235 = 125°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'medium', 1, 'Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. What is true about the alternate angles?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"They are equal"},{"id":"b","text":"They add to 180°"},{"id":"c","text":"They add to 90°"},{"id":"d","text":"They add to 360°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Alternate angles sit on opposite sides of the transversal, between the parallel lines, making a Z shape. They are equal.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the quadrilateral. Find the size of angle x in degrees.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["120","120°"]'::jsonb, 'The angles in a quadrilateral add to 360°. 70 + 80 + 90 = 240, and 360 − 240 = 120°.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[9,0],[8,6],[2,5]],"cornerLabels":["70°","80°","90°","x"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'hard', 1, 'An isosceles triangle has an angle of 40° between its two equal sides. What size is each of the other two angles?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"70°"},{"id":"b","text":"140°"},{"id":"c","text":"40°"},{"id":"d","text":"50°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The other two angles are equal and share 180 − 40 = 140°. 140 ÷ 2 = 70° each.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'hard', 1, 'A triangle can have two obtuse angles.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Two obtuse angles would each be more than 90°, so together they would be more than 180°. That leaves nothing for the third angle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'hard', 1, 'Put these types of angle in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Acute"},{"id":"b","text":"Right"},{"id":"c","text":"Obtuse"},{"id":"d","text":"Straight"},{"id":"e","text":"Reflex"},{"id":"f","text":"Revolution"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e","f"]'::jsonb, 'Acute is less than 90°, right is 90°, obtuse is between 90° and 180°, straight is 180°, reflex is between 180° and 360°, and a revolution is 360°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Two co-interior angles between parallel lines are 2x° and (x + 30)°. Find x.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"50"'::jsonb, 'Co-interior angles add to 180°. 2x + x + 30 = 180, so 3x = 150 and x = 50. (The angles are 100° and 80°.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Two straight lines cross. One of the angles is 3 times the size of the angle next to it. What is the smaller angle in degrees?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["45","45°"]'::jsonb, 'Angles next to each other at a crossing lie on a straight line, so they add to 180°. x + 3x = 180, so 4x = 180 and x = 45°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Lines AB and CD are parallel. A transversal crosses AB, making a 62° angle. Which of these are sizes of angles where the transversal crosses CD? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"62°"},{"id":"b","text":"118°"},{"id":"c","text":"28°"},{"id":"d","text":"180°"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'At CD, the corresponding and alternate angles equal 62°. The angles next to them on a straight line are 180 − 62 = 118°. 28° is the complement, which isn''t formed here.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_easy', 1, 'What type of angle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Reflex"},{"id":"b","text":"Obtuse"},{"id":"c","text":"Straight"},{"id":"d","text":"Acute"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The angle goes past a straight line (180°) but not all the way round (360°), so it is a reflex angle.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":230}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the supplement of 64°?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["116","116°"]'::jsonb, 'Supplementary angles add to 180°. 180 − 64 = 116°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_easy', 1, 'The three angles in a triangle add up to 360°.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The angles in a triangle add up to 180°. It is the angles in a quadrilateral that add up to 360°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'easy', 1, 'What is the complement of this angle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"18°"},{"id":"b","text":"108°"},{"id":"c","text":"28°"},{"id":"d","text":"288°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Complementary angles add to 90°. 90 − 72 = 18°. 108° is the supplement, which adds to 180°.', '{"kind":"angle","degrees":72,"label":"72°"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the triangle. Find the size of angle x in degrees.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["70","70°"]'::jsonb, 'The angles in a triangle add to 180°. 55 + 55 = 110, and 180 − 110 = 70°.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[4,6]],"cornerLabels":["55°","55°","x"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'easy', 1, 'Angles at a point add up to ___°.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["360","360°"]'::jsonb, 'Going all the way around a point is one full turn, which is 360°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'medium', 1, 'Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Corresponding angles are equal"},{"id":"b","text":"Alternate angles are equal"},{"id":"c","text":"Co-interior angles are equal"},{"id":"d","text":"Co-interior angles add to 180°"},{"id":"e","text":"Vertically opposite angles add to 180°"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Corresponding (F shape) and alternate (Z shape) angles are equal. Co-interior angles (C shape) add to 180°, so they are not usually equal. Vertically opposite angles are equal.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'medium', 1, 'This kite has two equal angles marked x. Find x in degrees.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["82","82°"]'::jsonb, 'The angles in a quadrilateral add to 360°. 90 + 106 = 196, so the two x angles share 360 − 196 = 164°. 164 ÷ 2 = 82°.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[4,0],[8,4],[4,7],[0,4]],"cornerLabels":["90°","x","106°","x"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'medium', 1, 'Two straight lines cross. One of the angles is 38°. What are the other three angles?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"38°, 142°, 142°"},{"id":"b","text":"38°, 52°, 52°"},{"id":"c","text":"142°, 142°, 142°"},{"id":"d","text":"38°, 38°, 142°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The angle opposite 38° is also 38°. The two angles next to it are on a straight line with it, so each is 180 − 38 = 142°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'hard', 1, 'In triangle ABC, angle A is 48° and angle B is twice angle A. What is angle C in degrees?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["36","36°"]'::jsonb, 'Angle B = 2 × 48 = 96°. The angles add to 180°, so angle C = 180 − 48 − 96 = 36°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'hard', 1, 'If two angles are vertically opposite and also supplementary, they must both be right angles.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Vertically opposite angles are equal. Two equal angles that add to 180° must each be 90°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'hard', 1, 'One angle of a parallelogram is 65°. What are the other three angles?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"65°, 115°, 115°"},{"id":"b","text":"115°, 115°, 115°"},{"id":"c","text":"65°, 65°, 65°"},{"id":"d","text":"25°, 65°, 25°"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal, so one is 65°. Angles next to each other are co-interior between parallel sides, so they add to 180°: 180 − 65 = 115°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Four angles meet at a point. They are x°, 2x°, 3x° and 90°. Find x.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"45"'::jsonb, 'Angles at a point add to 360°. x + 2x + 3x + 90 = 360, so 6x = 270 and x = 45.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_hard', 1, 'Lines AB and CD are parallel. A pair of alternate angles between them are (4x − 20)° and (2x + 30)°. Find x.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"25"'::jsonb, 'Alternate angles are equal, so 4x − 20 = 2x + 30. Take 2x from both sides: 2x − 20 = 30, so 2x = 50 and x = 25. (Both angles are 80°.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070601', 'very_hard', 1, 'In a triangle, the second angle is twice the first, and the third angle is three times the first. What kind of triangle is it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Right-angled"},{"id":"b","text":"Acute-angled"},{"id":"c","text":"Obtuse-angled"},{"id":"d","text":"Equilateral"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'x + 2x + 3x = 180, so 6x = 180 and x = 30. The angles are 30°, 60° and 90°, so it is right-angled.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the side lengths. What type of triangle is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Isosceles"},{"id":"b","text":"Equilateral"},{"id":"c","text":"Scalene"},{"id":"d","text":"Right-angled"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Two of its sides are 7 cm, so exactly two sides are equal. That makes it isosceles.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[4,6]],"sideLabels":["8 cm","7 cm","7 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_easy', 1, 'A square is a special kind of rectangle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A rectangle is a quadrilateral with four right angles. A square has four right angles too, plus four equal sides.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many lines of symmetry does an equilateral triangle have?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3"},{"id":"b","text":"1"},{"id":"c","text":"0"},{"id":"d","text":"6"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A line from each corner to the middle of the opposite side cuts it into two matching halves. There are 3 corners, so 3 lines.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these does every rhombus have? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"All four sides equal"},{"id":"b","text":"Diagonals that cross at right angles"},{"id":"c","text":"Four right angles"},{"id":"d","text":"Opposite angles equal"},{"id":"e","text":"Diagonals of equal length"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'A rhombus has four equal sides, equal opposite angles, and diagonals that bisect each other at right angles. Only a square rhombus has right angles and equal diagonals.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'easy', 1, 'Point A is translated 3 units right and 4 units up. What are the coordinates of its image?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["(5, 5)","(5,5)","5, 5","5,5"]'::jsonb, 'A is at (2, 1). Moving 3 right adds 3 to x: 2 + 3 = 5. Moving 4 up adds 4 to y: 1 + 4 = 5. The image is at (5, 5).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":2,"y":1,"label":"A"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'easy', 1, 'A triangle with one angle of exactly 90° is called a ___ triangle.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["right-angled","right angled","right"]'::jsonb, 'A triangle with a right angle (90°) is a right-angled triangle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'medium', 1, 'The point (3, 2) is reflected in the y-axis. Where is its image?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−3, 2)"},{"id":"b","text":"(3, −2)"},{"id":"c","text":"(−3, −2)"},{"id":"d","text":"(2, 3)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Reflecting in the y-axis flips the point from right to left, so the x-coordinate changes sign and y stays the same: (−3, 2).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'medium', 1, 'This trapezium has its two slanting sides the same length. How many lines of symmetry does it have?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1"},{"id":"b","text":"2"},{"id":"c","text":"0"},{"id":"d","text":"4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The only fold that matches it up is the vertical line through the middle of the two parallel sides. So it has 1 line of symmetry.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,0],[9,0],[7,4],[3,4]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'medium', 1, 'The diagonals of a rectangle are equal in length.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Each diagonal of a rectangle joins opposite corners across the same length and width, so both diagonals are the same length.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'hard', 1, 'In which of these quadrilaterals do the diagonals always cross at right angles? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Square"},{"id":"b","text":"Rhombus"},{"id":"c","text":"Kite"},{"id":"d","text":"Rectangle"},{"id":"e","text":"Parallelogram"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Squares, rhombuses and kites all have diagonals that are perpendicular. A rectangle or parallelogram that is not a square or rhombus does not.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'hard', 1, 'Put these shapes in order from the most general to the most special.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Quadrilateral"},{"id":"b","text":"Parallelogram"},{"id":"c","text":"Rectangle"},{"id":"d","text":"Square"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Every square is a rectangle, every rectangle is a parallelogram, and every parallelogram is a quadrilateral. Each one adds more rules.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'hard', 1, 'The point P(4, 1) is rotated 90° anticlockwise about the origin. Where is its image?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−1, 4)"},{"id":"b","text":"(1, −4)"},{"id":"c","text":"(−4, −1)"},{"id":"d","text":"(4, −1)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A quarter turn anticlockwise about the origin takes (x, y) to (−y, x). So (4, 1) goes to (−1, 4). Check: P starts to the right, and a quarter turn anticlockwise points it up.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_hard', 1, 'Every square is a rhombus, but not every rhombus is a square.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A square has four equal sides, so it is a rhombus. A rhombus need not have right angles, so it is not always a square.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_hard', 1, 'A shape is reflected in the x-axis and then the image is reflected in the y-axis. This is the same as a rotation about the origin through how many degrees?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["180","180°"]'::jsonb, '(x, y) goes to (x, −y), then to (−x, −y). Changing the sign of both coordinates is a half turn, which is 180°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_hard', 1, 'I am a quadrilateral. My diagonals are equal in length and bisect each other, but they do not cross at right angles. What am I?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A rectangle"},{"id":"b","text":"A square"},{"id":"c","text":"A rhombus"},{"id":"d","text":"A kite"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Diagonals that are equal and bisect each other mean a rectangle. A square''s diagonals cross at right angles, so it can''t be a square. A rhombus or kite (not a square) has unequal diagonals.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_easy', 1, 'Classify this triangle by its side lengths.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Scalene"},{"id":"b","text":"Isosceles"},{"id":"c","text":"Equilateral"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The sides are 8 cm, 10 cm and 6 cm. No two are equal, so it is scalene.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["8 cm","10 cm","6 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_easy', 1, 'A rectangle that is not a square has 4 lines of symmetry.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'It has only 2: one across the middle and one down the middle. Folding along a diagonal does not match it up.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which transformation slides a shape without turning or flipping it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Translation"},{"id":"b","text":"Reflection"},{"id":"c","text":"Rotation"},{"id":"d","text":"Enlargement"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A translation moves every point the same distance in the same direction. A reflection flips and a rotation turns.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these does every parallelogram have? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Opposite sides parallel"},{"id":"b","text":"Opposite sides equal"},{"id":"c","text":"Four right angles"},{"id":"d","text":"Diagonals that bisect each other"},{"id":"e","text":"All sides equal"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Every parallelogram has opposite sides parallel and equal, and its diagonals cut each other in half. Right angles and four equal sides only belong to special parallelograms.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'easy', 1, 'Point A is translated to point B. Describe the translation.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5 units right"},{"id":"b","text":"5 units left"},{"id":"c","text":"6 units right"},{"id":"d","text":"2 units up"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A is at (1, 2) and B is at (6, 2). The y-coordinate stays the same and x goes from 1 to 6, which is 5 units right.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":1,"y":2,"label":"A"},{"x":6,"y":2,"label":"B"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'easy', 1, 'Each angle in an equilateral triangle is ___°.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["60","60°"]'::jsonb, 'All three angles are equal and add to 180°. 180 ÷ 3 = 60°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'medium', 1, 'Point A is reflected in the vertical line x = 5. What are the coordinates of its image?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["(8, 3)","(8,3)","8, 3","8,3"]'::jsonb, 'A is at (2, 3), which is 3 units left of the line x = 5. Its image is 3 units right of the line, at x = 8. The y-coordinate stays 3, so the image is (8, 3).', '{"kind":"grid","columns":10,"rows":8,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":2,"y":3,"label":"A"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'medium', 1, 'How many lines of symmetry does a kite have (one that is not a rhombus)?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1"},{"id":"b","text":"2"},{"id":"c","text":"0"},{"id":"d","text":"4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A kite has one line of symmetry: the diagonal that joins the corners where the equal sides meet.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'medium', 1, 'In an isosceles triangle, the two angles opposite the equal sides are equal.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'An isosceles triangle has a line of symmetry down the middle, so the two base angles match.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'hard', 1, 'Put these shapes in order from fewest lines of symmetry to most.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Parallelogram (not a rectangle or rhombus)"},{"id":"b","text":"Kite (not a rhombus)"},{"id":"c","text":"Rectangle (not a square)"},{"id":"d","text":"Equilateral triangle"},{"id":"e","text":"Square"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'A general parallelogram has 0 lines of symmetry, a kite has 1, a rectangle has 2, an equilateral triangle has 3, and a square has 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these quadrilaterals always have two pairs of parallel sides? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rhombus"},{"id":"b","text":"Rectangle"},{"id":"c","text":"Trapezium"},{"id":"d","text":"Kite"},{"id":"e","text":"Square"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, 'Rhombuses, rectangles and squares are all parallelograms, so both pairs of opposite sides are parallel. A trapezium has only one pair, and a kite usually has none.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'hard', 1, 'The point (2, 5) is rotated 180° about the origin. Where is its image?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−2, −5)"},{"id":"b","text":"(−5, 2)"},{"id":"c","text":"(2, −5)"},{"id":"d","text":"(5, 2)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A half turn about the origin changes the sign of both coordinates: (x, y) goes to (−x, −y). So (2, 5) goes to (−2, −5).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_hard', 1, 'A quadrilateral has exactly one pair of parallel sides and exactly one line of symmetry. What is it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"An isosceles trapezium"},{"id":"b","text":"A kite"},{"id":"c","text":"A parallelogram"},{"id":"d","text":"A rhombus"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'One pair of parallel sides makes it a trapezium. A trapezium with a line of symmetry has equal slanting sides, so it is an isosceles trapezium. A kite has no parallel sides, and parallelograms have two pairs.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_hard', 1, 'The point (−3, 4) is translated 5 units right, then reflected in the x-axis. Where does it end up?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["(2, −4)","(2, -4)","(2,−4)","(2,-4)","2, −4","2, -4"]'::jsonb, 'Translating 5 right: −3 + 5 = 2, so it moves to (2, 4). Reflecting in the x-axis changes the sign of y: (2, −4).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007060230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070602', 'very_hard', 1, 'A triangle can be both isosceles and right-angled.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A triangle with angles of 90°, 45° and 45° has a right angle and two equal sides, so it is both.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which of these is categorical data?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Favourite sport"},{"id":"b","text":"Height in centimetres"},{"id":"c","text":"Number of siblings"},{"id":"d","text":"Time to run 100 m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Categorical data is sorted into groups or names, not measured or counted. Favourite sport gives answers like ''cricket'' or ''netball'', so it is categorical. The others are numbers.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_easy', 1, 'The number of goals scored in a netball game is discrete numerical data.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Goals are counted in whole numbers (you can''t score 12.5 goals), so the data is discrete.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the dot plot. How many students own exactly 2 pets?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"4"'::jsonb, 'Each dot is one student. There are 4 dots above the 2, so 4 students own exactly 2 pets.', '{"kind":"dot_plot","title":"Pets owned by students in 7B","columns":[{"label":"0","count":3},{"label":"1","count":5},{"label":"2","count":4},{"label":"3","count":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are continuous numerical data? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mass of a newborn joey"},{"id":"b","text":"Number of cars in a car park"},{"id":"c","text":"Temperature at noon in Dubbo"},{"id":"d","text":"Brand of shoe"},{"id":"e","text":"Time taken to swim 50 m"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous data is measured and can take any value in a range, like 2.35 kg or 31.4 seconds. Mass, temperature and time are measured. Number of cars is counted (discrete) and shoe brand is categorical.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'easy', 1, 'This stem-and-leaf plot shows quiz scores, where 1 | 2 means 12. What is the highest score?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"34"'::jsonb, 'The highest score is on the last stem with the biggest leaf. Stem 3 and leaf 4 make 34.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Stem","Leaf"],"rows":[["1","2 5 8"],["2","0 3 3 7"],["3","1 4"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'easy', 1, 'A school asks every one of its 820 students which sport they play. Is this a census or a sample?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Census"},{"id":"b","text":"Sample"},{"id":"c","text":"Neither"},{"id":"d","text":"Both"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A census collects data from every member of the group. All 820 students were asked, so it is a census.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the column graph. How many students were surveyed altogether?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"30"'::jsonb, 'Add the heights of all the columns: 12 + 8 + 6 + 4 = 30 students.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"How students travel to school","columns":[{"label":"Bus","value":12},{"label":"Car","value":8},{"label":"Walk","value":6},{"label":"Bike","value":4}],"step":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'medium', 1, 'In a sector graph of 40 students, the sector for cricket has an angle of 90°. The number of students who chose cricket is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"10"'::jsonb, 'A full circle is 360°. 90° is 90/360 = 1/4 of the circle, so 1/4 of 40 students = 10 students.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'medium', 1, 'Put these steps for building a stem-and-leaf plot in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Find the smallest and largest values"},{"id":"b","text":"Write the stems (tens digits) in a column"},{"id":"c","text":"Write each leaf (ones digit) next to its stem"},{"id":"d","text":"Rewrite the leaves in order from smallest to largest"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'First see what range the stems must cover, then list the stems, then add every leaf, and finally sort the leaves on each stem so the plot is ordered.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the dot plot. What percentage of the students read 4 or more books?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["25%","25"]'::jsonb, 'Count all the dots: 2 + 4 + 6 + 3 + 1 = 16 students. 4 or more books: 3 + 1 = 4 students. 4/16 = 1/4 = 25%.', '{"kind":"dot_plot","title":"Books read in Term 3","columns":[{"label":"1","count":2},{"label":"2","count":4},{"label":"3","count":6},{"label":"4","count":3},{"label":"5","count":1}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'hard', 1, 'In a survey of 60 students, 15 chose swimming. What angle should the swimming sector have in a sector graph?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"15°"},{"id":"b","text":"90°"},{"id":"c","text":"60°"},{"id":"d","text":"25°"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Swimming is 15/60 = 1/4 of the students. A quarter of 360° is 90°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'hard', 1, 'Asking 50 people at Central Station whether they like catching trains gives a fair sample of what all Sydney residents think.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'People at a train station are more likely to use trains, so the sample is biased. A fair sample should be chosen at random from all Sydney residents.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_hard', 1, 'This stem-and-leaf plot shows test marks, where 4 | 1 means 41. What percentage of the marks are greater than 50?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["60%","60"]'::jsonb, 'There are 3 + 5 + 2 = 10 marks. Greater than 50: 52, 52, 56, 59, 63, 67, which is 6 marks (50 itself is not greater than 50). 6/10 = 60%.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Stem","Leaf"],"rows":[["4","1 5 8"],["5","0 2 2 6 9"],["6","3 7"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_hard', 1, '72 students chose their favourite sport. In a sector graph, the angles are Netball 120°, Cricket 90°, Soccer 100° and Other 50°. Choose all that are true.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"24 students chose netball"},{"id":"b","text":"Cricket was chosen by a quarter of the students"},{"id":"c","text":"20 students chose soccer"},{"id":"d","text":"Other was chosen by 12 students"},{"id":"e","text":"More students chose soccer than netball"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Each degree stands for 72 ÷ 360 = 0.2 students. Netball 120 × 0.2 = 24, cricket 90 × 0.2 = 18 (90° is a quarter), soccer 100 × 0.2 = 20, other 50 × 0.2 = 10. Netball (24) beats soccer (20).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_hard', 1, 'In a survey, 18 students caught the bus, which was 30% of everyone surveyed. In a sector graph of the results, the bus sector has an angle of ___°.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"108"'::jsonb, 'The bus sector is 30% of the circle, no matter how many were surveyed. 30% of 360° = 0.3 × 360° = 108°. (There were 60 students, and 18/60 × 360° = 108° too.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which of these is numerical data?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Favourite colour"},{"id":"b","text":"Number of pets"},{"id":"c","text":"Hair colour"},{"id":"d","text":"Favourite AFL team"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Numerical data is made of numbers that are counted or measured. Number of pets is counted. The others are names or groups, so they are categorical.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the column graph. Which fruit is the most popular?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Apple"},{"id":"b","text":"Banana"},{"id":"c","text":"Mango"},{"id":"d","text":"Grapes"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'The tallest column shows the most popular choice. Mango has the tallest column, at 9.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Favourite fruit in 7C","columns":[{"label":"Apple","value":7},{"label":"Banana","value":5},{"label":"Mango","value":9},{"label":"Grapes","value":3}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_easy', 1, 'Collecting data from every member of a whole group is called a census.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A census includes everyone in the group. A sample only uses part of the group.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'easy', 1, 'These entries come from a stem-and-leaf plot, where 3 | 7 means 37. Put them in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"2 | 9"},{"id":"b","text":"3 | 2"},{"id":"c","text":"3 | 7"},{"id":"d","text":"4 | 0"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Turn each entry into a number: 29, 32, 37 and 40. Compare stems first, then leaves.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the dot plot. How many students have at least 2 siblings?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"8"'::jsonb, '''At least 2'' means 2, 3 or 4 siblings. Add those dots: 5 + 2 + 1 = 8 students.', '{"kind":"dot_plot","title":"Siblings of students in 7D","columns":[{"label":"0","count":4},{"label":"1","count":7},{"label":"2","count":5},{"label":"3","count":2},{"label":"4","count":1}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these is discrete numerical data?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Height of a gum tree"},{"id":"b","text":"Number of kookaburras in a tree"},{"id":"c","text":"Mass of a wombat"},{"id":"d","text":"Favourite Australian bird"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Discrete data is counted in whole numbers. You count kookaburras. Height and mass are measured (continuous), and favourite bird is categorical.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are better done with a sample than a census? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Testing how long batteries from a factory last (testing uses the battery up)"},{"id":"b","text":"Finding out what all Australians think of a new coin design"},{"id":"c","text":"Recording the ages of the 25 students in your class"},{"id":"d","text":"Checking whether each runner in a 4-person relay team has their uniform"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'A sample is best when the group is huge or testing destroys the item. Testing every battery would leave none to sell, and asking all 27 million Australians is too costly. Your class and a relay team are small, so a census is easy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'medium', 1, 'This stem-and-leaf plot shows times in seconds to run around the oval, where 2 | 1 means 21. How many runners took less than 20 seconds?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"3"'::jsonb, 'Times less than 20 seconds are on stem 1: 14, 16 and 19. That is 3 runners.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Stem","Leaf"],"rows":[["1","4 6 9"],["2","1 1 5 8"],["3","0 2"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'medium', 1, 'A sector graph shows how 120 students get to school. The Walk sector has an angle of 60°, so ___ students walk.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"20"'::jsonb, '60° out of 360° is 60/360 = 1/6 of the circle. 1/6 of 120 students = 20 students.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'hard', 1, 'Of 45 students surveyed, 9 chose basketball. What angle should the basketball sector have in a sector graph?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"9°"},{"id":"b","text":"72°"},{"id":"c","text":"45°"},{"id":"d","text":"20°"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Basketball is 9/45 = 1/5 of the students. 1/5 of 360° = 72°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the column graph. What percentage of all the cans were collected on Wednesday?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30%","30"]'::jsonb, 'Total cans: 25 + 15 + 30 + 10 + 20 = 100. Wednesday had 30 out of 100, which is 30%.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Cans collected for recycling","columns":[{"label":"Mon","value":25},{"label":"Tue","value":15},{"label":"Wed","value":30},{"label":"Thu","value":10},{"label":"Fri","value":20}],"step":5}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'hard', 1, 'A dot plot is a good way to show 30 students'' exact heights measured to the nearest millimetre.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Exact heights are continuous and almost every value would be different, so each dot would sit alone above its own label. A stem-and-leaf plot or grouped column graph works better.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_hard', 1, 'Each dot is one soccer game. How many goals did the team score altogether over the season?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"23"'::jsonb, 'Multiply each number of goals by its number of games: 0 × 3 + 1 × 5 + 2 × 4 + 3 × 2 + 4 × 1 = 0 + 5 + 8 + 6 + 4 = 23 goals.', '{"kind":"dot_plot","title":"Goals per game for the Wollongong under-13s","columns":[{"label":"0","count":3},{"label":"1","count":5},{"label":"2","count":4},{"label":"3","count":2},{"label":"4","count":1}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_hard', 1, 'This stem-and-leaf plot shows students'' resting heart rates in beats per minute, where 5 | 2 means 52. Choose all that are true.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"There are 12 values"},{"id":"b","text":"The largest value is 78"},{"id":"c","text":"Five values are in the 60s"},{"id":"d","text":"Exactly half of the values are less than 62"},{"id":"e","text":"Two values are 70 or more"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, 'The values are 52, 54, 54, 57, 60, 63, 65, 65, 68, 71, 76, 84: that is 12 values. The largest is 84, not 78. The 60s hold 60, 63, 65, 65, 68 (five). Only 5 values are less than 62, not 6. Three values (71, 76, 84) are 70 or more.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Stem","Leaf"],"rows":[["5","2 4 4 7"],["6","0 3 5 5 8"],["7","1 6"],["8","4"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070701', 'very_hard', 1, 'A sector graph shows how the 30 students in 7E get to school. The Car sector is 84° and the Bus sector is twice as big. How many more students travel by bus than by car?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"7"'::jsonb, 'Each student is 360° ÷ 30 = 12°. Car: 84 ÷ 12 = 7 students. Bus: 168° ÷ 12 = 14 students. 14 − 7 = 7 more students.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the sample space for one spin of this spinner?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"{red, blue, green, yellow}"},{"id":"b","text":"{red, blue}"},{"id":"c","text":"{1, 2, 3, 4}"},{"id":"d","text":"{red}"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The sample space lists every possible outcome. The spinner can land on red, blue, green or yellow.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","blue","green","yellow"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_easy', 1, 'A fair six-sided die is rolled. What is the probability of rolling a 4? Write it as a fraction.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"1/6"'::jsonb, 'There are 6 equally likely outcomes and only one of them is a 4, so P(4) = 1/6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_easy', 1, 'A probability of 0.5 is the same as a probability of 50%.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '0.5 = 50/100 = 50%. Both mean an even chance.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'easy', 1, 'One counter is taken from this bag without looking. What is the probability it is blue? Write it as a fraction.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/2","5/10"]'::jsonb, 'There are 3 + 5 + 2 = 10 counters and 5 are blue. P(blue) = 5/10 = 1/2.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":3},{"colour":"blue","count":5},{"colour":"green","count":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'easy', 1, 'The probability of rain tomorrow in Bathurst is 0.3. The probability of no rain is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"0.7"'::jsonb, 'Rain and no rain are complementary events, so their probabilities add to 1. 1 − 0.3 = 0.7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'easy', 1, 'A card is picked at random from cards numbered 1 to 10. Which events have a probability of 1/2? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Picking an even number"},{"id":"b","text":"Picking an odd number"},{"id":"c","text":"Picking a number greater than 5"},{"id":"d","text":"Picking a multiple of 3"},{"id":"e","text":"Picking a prime number"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Even (2, 4, 6, 8, 10), odd (1, 3, 5, 7, 9) and greater than 5 (6 to 10) each have 5 of the 10 cards, so 5/10 = 1/2. Multiples of 3 are 3, 6, 9 (3/10) and primes are 2, 3, 5, 7 (4/10).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'medium', 1, 'A normal die is rolled once. Put these events from least likely to most likely.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rolling a 7"},{"id":"b","text":"Rolling a 6"},{"id":"c","text":"Rolling an even number"},{"id":"d","text":"Rolling a number less than 6"},{"id":"e","text":"Rolling a number from 1 to 6"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'The probabilities are 0 (impossible), 1/6, 3/6, 5/6 and 1 (certain).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'medium', 1, 'Jarrah tossed a coin 50 times. What is the relative frequency of heads? Write it as a decimal.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.46","23/50","46%"]'::jsonb, 'Relative frequency = number of times it happened ÷ number of trials = 23 ÷ 50 = 0.46.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Result","Frequency"],"rows":[["Heads",23],["Tails",27]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'medium', 1, 'Two coins are tossed together. How many outcomes are in the sample space?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2"},{"id":"b","text":"3"},{"id":"c","text":"4"},{"id":"d","text":"6"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'List them: HH, HT, TH, TT. Heads-tails and tails-heads are different outcomes, so there are 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'hard', 1, 'What is the probability that this spinner does NOT land on blue? Write it as a percentage.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["75%","75"]'::jsonb, '2 of the 8 sections are blue, so 6 are not blue. P(not blue) = 6/8 = 3/4 = 75%.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","red","red","blue","blue","green","green","green"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'hard', 1, 'A bag holds only red and green marbles. The probability of picking red is 2/5. There are 12 green marbles. How many red marbles are there?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8"},{"id":"b","text":"12"},{"id":"c","text":"20"},{"id":"d","text":"5"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'P(green) = 1 − 2/5 = 3/5, so 12 marbles are 3/5 of the bag. 1/5 is 4 marbles, so the bag holds 20. Red = 2/5 of 20 = 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'hard', 1, 'Luca tossed a coin 10 times and got 7 heads. This proves the coin is unfair.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'With only 10 tosses, results often wander away from half heads by chance. You need many more trials before saying a coin is unfair.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_hard', 1, 'A die was rolled 60 times and a 6 came up 18 times. Choose all that are true.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The relative frequency of a 6 is 0.3"},{"id":"b","text":"The theoretical probability of a 6 on a fair die is 1/6"},{"id":"c","text":"The die must be biased"},{"id":"d","text":"For a fair die, the relative frequency of a 6 should get closer to 1/6 with many more rolls"},{"id":"e","text":"The relative frequency of not rolling a 6 is 0.6"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '18/60 = 0.3. A fair die gives P(6) = 1/6. 60 rolls is not enough to be sure it is biased. More trials bring relative frequency closer to the true probability. Not a 6: 42/60 = 0.7, not 0.6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_hard', 1, 'A drawing pin landed point up 36 times in 120 throws. Using this result, about how many times would you expect it to land point up in 500 throws?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"150"'::jsonb, 'Relative frequency = 36 ÷ 120 = 0.3. Expected number = 0.3 × 500 = 150.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_hard', 1, 'A spinner with equal sections was spun 80 times. Which spinner most likely gave these results?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1 red, 2 blue and 2 green sections"},{"id":"b","text":"2 red, 1 blue and 1 green section"},{"id":"c","text":"1 red, 1 blue and 1 green section"},{"id":"d","text":"1 red, 3 blue and 1 green section"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Red came up about 18/80 ≈ 1/5 of the time, and blue and green each about 2/5. A spinner with 1 red, 2 blue and 2 green sections gives 1/5, 2/5, 2/5, which would expect 16, 32 and 32 out of 80.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Colour","Times landed"],"rows":[["Red",18],["Blue",32],["Green",30]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_easy', 1, 'How many outcomes are in the sample space when you roll a normal six-sided die once?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"6"'::jsonb, 'The sample space is {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, which has 6 outcomes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the probability that this spinner lands on blue?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/2"},{"id":"b","text":"1/4"},{"id":"c","text":"2/3"},{"id":"d","text":"1/3"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '2 of the 4 equal sections are blue, so P(blue) = 2/4 = 1/2.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["blue","blue","red","yellow"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which number is the probability of an impossible event?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"0"},{"id":"b","text":"1"},{"id":"c","text":"0.5"},{"id":"d","text":"−1"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Probabilities go from 0 to 1. An impossible event has probability 0, and a certain event has probability 1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'easy', 1, 'One counter is picked from this bag without looking. What is the probability it is yellow? Write it as a fraction in simplest form.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/3","4/12"]'::jsonb, 'There are 4 + 6 + 2 = 12 counters and 4 are yellow. P(yellow) = 4/12 = 1/3.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"yellow","count":4},{"colour":"purple","count":6},{"colour":"orange","count":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'easy', 1, 'When a die is rolled, ''rolling an even number'' and ''rolling an odd number'' are complementary events.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Every roll is either even or odd, never both, so together they cover the whole sample space. That makes them complementary.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'easy', 1, 'Put these probabilities in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/5"},{"id":"b","text":"0.25"},{"id":"c","text":"30%"},{"id":"d","text":"2/5"},{"id":"e","text":"0.45"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Write them all as decimals: 1/5 = 0.2, 0.25, 30% = 0.3, 2/5 = 0.4, 0.45.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'medium', 1, 'The probability that a netball team wins its next game is 35%. The probability that it does not win is ___%.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"65"'::jsonb, 'Winning and not winning are complementary, so they add to 100%. 100% − 35% = 65%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'medium', 1, 'Mei recorded her school bus for 40 days. What is the relative frequency of the bus being late? Write it as a fraction in simplest form.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3/10","12/40","0.3","30%"]'::jsonb, 'The bus was late 12 times in 40 days. 12/40 = 3/10.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Bus arrival","Days"],"rows":[["On time",28],["Late",12]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'medium', 1, 'One letter is picked at random from the word KANGAROO. Choose all that are true.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"P(A) = 1/4"},{"id":"b","text":"P(vowel) = 1/2"},{"id":"c","text":"P(K) = 1/7"},{"id":"d","text":"P(O) = P(A)"},{"id":"e","text":"P(Z) = 1/8"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'KANGAROO has 8 letters. A appears 2 times (2/8 = 1/4) and O appears 2 times, so P(O) = P(A). Vowels A, A, O, O give 4/8 = 1/2. P(K) = 1/8, and P(Z) = 0.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'hard', 1, 'A spinner has red, blue and green sections. P(red) = 0.45 and P(blue) = 0.3. What is P(green)?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.25"},{"id":"b","text":"0.75"},{"id":"c","text":"0.15"},{"id":"d","text":"0.55"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'All the probabilities add to 1. 0.45 + 0.3 = 0.75, so P(green) = 1 − 0.75 = 0.25.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'hard', 1, 'A coin is tossed and a six-sided die is rolled at the same time. How many outcomes are in the sample space?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"12"'::jsonb, 'Each of the 2 coin results (H, T) goes with each of the 6 die results: H1 to H6 and T1 to T6. 2 × 6 = 12 outcomes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'hard', 1, 'This spinner is spun 200 times. About how many times would you expect it to land on yellow?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"20"},{"id":"b","text":"10"},{"id":"c","text":"40"},{"id":"d","text":"1"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1 of the 10 equal sections is yellow, so P(yellow) = 1/10. Expected number = 1/10 × 200 = 20.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","red","red","red","blue","blue","blue","green","green","yellow"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bag holds 40 lollies. P(red) = 0.15 and P(green) = 3/8. How many lollies are neither red nor green?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"19"'::jsonb, 'Red: 0.15 × 40 = 6. Green: 3/8 × 40 = 15. Neither: 40 − 6 − 15 = 19.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_hard', 1, 'Ella rolled a die 50 times. Choose all that are true about her results.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The relative frequency of a 4 is 0.2"},{"id":"b","text":"The relative frequency of an even number is 0.58"},{"id":"c","text":"The relative frequency of a 1 is 0.12"},{"id":"d","text":"These results prove the die is biased"},{"id":"e","text":"The relative frequency of a 2 is 9%"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '4: 10/50 = 0.2. Even: (9 + 10 + 10)/50 = 29/50 = 0.58. 1: 6/50 = 0.12. 2: 9/50 = 18%, not 9%. 50 rolls are too few to prove bias.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Number rolled","Frequency"],"rows":[["1",6],["2",9],["3",8],["4",10],["5",7],["6",10]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007070230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000070702', 'very_hard', 1, 'Mia says: ''When I roll a die I either get a 6 or I don''t, so P(6) = 1/2.'' What is wrong with her thinking?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The two outcomes are not equally likely: P(6) = 1/6 and P(not 6) = 5/6"},{"id":"b","text":"Nothing, she is right"},{"id":"c","text":"P(6) is 1/2 only on a fair die"},{"id":"d","text":"P(not 6) should be 1/6"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''6'' is 1 outcome and ''not 6'' is 5 outcomes out of the 6 equally likely outcomes. Two possible events does not mean each has probability 1/2. P(6) = 1/6, P(not 6) = 5/6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Why did Jun miss the 9:15 ferry?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He stayed too long at the food court with his teammates."},{"id":"b","text":"His soccer game finished late."},{"id":"c","text":"The ferry left early."},{"id":"d","text":"He went to the wrong wharf."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first paragraph says that at 9:10 he was still in the food court laughing with his teammates, so he was too late.', NULL, '{"title":"Missed Ferry","text":"Jun had promised his mum he would catch the 9:15 ferry home from Circular Quay. At 9:10 he was still in the food court, laughing with his teammates about the penalty he had nearly missed.\n\nHe ran the whole way down George Street, his soccer bag thumping against his back. When he reached the wharf, the ferry was already pulling away, its wake curling white under the lights. The next one was not for another hour.\n\nJun stared at his phone. Mum was working a night shift. That left Hana. That morning he had told his sister she was the most annoying person in New South Wales, and he had meant it. She had borrowed his headphones without asking, again.\n\nHe typed three different messages and deleted them all. Finally he sent just two words: ''Missed ferry.''\n\nThe reply came almost at once. ''Stay where you are. Twenty minutes.''\n\nHana arrived in their mum''s old hatchback, the radio playing the kind of music he always complained about. She didn''t mention the morning. She didn''t mention the time. She just passed him a paper bag with a warm sausage roll inside and turned the music down a little.\n\nHalfway across the Harbour Bridge, Jun pulled his headphones out of his bag and held them out to her.\n\n''Keep them for a while,'' he said.\n\nHana smiled at the road ahead. ''I was going to buy my own,'' she said. ''But thanks.''"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_easy', 1, 'The pencil-like roots of the grey mangrove are called ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["pneumatophores","pneumatophore"]'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph names them: ''pencil-like roots called pneumatophores''.', NULL, '{"title":"The Hardest-Working Forest","text":"Along the edges of many NSW estuaries, from the Hawkesbury River to Botany Bay, grows a strange kind of forest. Its trees stand in salty mud that floods twice a day with the tide. These are mangroves, and they do far more work than most people realise.\n\nMangroves have special roots to survive where other trees would die. The grey mangrove, the most common species in NSW, sends up hundreds of pencil-like roots called pneumatophores. These poke out of the mud and take in air, because the waterlogged mud itself holds very little oxygen.\n\nThe tangle of roots also slows down moving water. As the water slows, mud and sand settle out of it instead of being carried away. In this way, mangroves hold the shoreline together and protect riverbanks from erosion during storms.\n\nMangroves are nurseries, too. Young fish, prawns and crabs shelter among the roots, where larger predators cannot easily reach them. Many of the fish that people catch in NSW, such as bream and mullet, spend part of their lives in estuaries like these.\n\nDespite all this, mangroves were once seen as smelly, useless swamps. Many were cleared for ports, rubbish tips and housing. Today, mangroves in NSW are protected by law, and it is illegal to damage them without a permit.\n\nSo next time you smell that salty, muddy air on a walk by the river, remember: you are standing beside one of the hardest-working ecosystems on the coast."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Priya wants the street outside her school closed to cars all day.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'She only wants it closed for thirty minutes before and after school, as she says at the end of the first paragraph.', NULL, '{"title":"Let Us Walk: The Case for a Car-Free School Street","text":"Every school morning, the street outside our school becomes a car park. Engines idle, horns beep and parents double-park while students squeeze between bumpers. I believe it is time our council closed the street to cars for thirty minutes before and after school.\n\nFirstly, a car-free street would be safer. Last term alone, our school newsletter reported two near misses at the crossing. Fewer cars means fewer chances for an accident, and no student should have to dodge a reversing four-wheel drive to get to class.\n\nSecondly, it would be healthier. When cars cannot drop students at the gate, families are more likely to walk, ride or scoot. Even a ten-minute walk each way adds up to more than an hour and a half of exercise a week.\n\nSome people argue that closing the street would be inconvenient for busy parents. That is true, for a few minutes. But parents could park one block away, and the short walk would give them a chance to talk with their children before the day begins. Surely a little inconvenience is worth a lot of safety.\n\nOur council should give it a try. Write to your local councillor today, and let''s make our street a place for people, not traffic.\n\n— Priya Sharma, Year 7 student representative"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'easy', 1, 'Which mangrove species does the text say is the most common in NSW?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["grey mangrove","the grey mangrove","grey mangroves","grey"]'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says ''The grey mangrove, the most common species in NSW''.', NULL, '{"title":"The Hardest-Working Forest","text":"Along the edges of many NSW estuaries, from the Hawkesbury River to Botany Bay, grows a strange kind of forest. Its trees stand in salty mud that floods twice a day with the tide. These are mangroves, and they do far more work than most people realise.\n\nMangroves have special roots to survive where other trees would die. The grey mangrove, the most common species in NSW, sends up hundreds of pencil-like roots called pneumatophores. These poke out of the mud and take in air, because the waterlogged mud itself holds very little oxygen.\n\nThe tangle of roots also slows down moving water. As the water slows, mud and sand settle out of it instead of being carried away. In this way, mangroves hold the shoreline together and protect riverbanks from erosion during storms.\n\nMangroves are nurseries, too. Young fish, prawns and crabs shelter among the roots, where larger predators cannot easily reach them. Many of the fish that people catch in NSW, such as bream and mullet, spend part of their lives in estuaries like these.\n\nDespite all this, mangroves were once seen as smelly, useless swamps. Many were cleared for ports, rubbish tips and housing. Today, mangroves in NSW are protected by law, and it is illegal to damage them without a permit.\n\nSo next time you smell that salty, muddy air on a walk by the river, remember: you are standing beside one of the hardest-working ecosystems on the coast."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'easy', 1, 'Which reasons does Priya give for closing the street? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"It would be safer for students."},{"id":"b","text":"Families would walk, ride or scoot more, which is healthier."},{"id":"c","text":"It would save the council money."},{"id":"d","text":"It would make parking easier for parents."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Her ''Firstly'' paragraph is about safety and her ''Secondly'' paragraph is about health. She never mentions money, and she admits parking would be less convenient.', NULL, '{"title":"Let Us Walk: The Case for a Car-Free School Street","text":"Every school morning, the street outside our school becomes a car park. Engines idle, horns beep and parents double-park while students squeeze between bumpers. I believe it is time our council closed the street to cars for thirty minutes before and after school.\n\nFirstly, a car-free street would be safer. Last term alone, our school newsletter reported two near misses at the crossing. Fewer cars means fewer chances for an accident, and no student should have to dodge a reversing four-wheel drive to get to class.\n\nSecondly, it would be healthier. When cars cannot drop students at the gate, families are more likely to walk, ride or scoot. Even a ten-minute walk each way adds up to more than an hour and a half of exercise a week.\n\nSome people argue that closing the street would be inconvenient for busy parents. That is true, for a few minutes. But parents could park one block away, and the short walk would give them a chance to talk with their children before the day begins. Surely a little inconvenience is worth a lot of safety.\n\nOur council should give it a try. Write to your local councillor today, and let''s make our street a place for people, not traffic.\n\n— Priya Sharma, Year 7 student representative"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'easy', 1, 'In the second stanza, Grandad names the pools that have dried up. What does this show?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He has known and loved the creek since he was young."},{"id":"b","text":"He wants to sell the farm."},{"id":"c","text":"He has forgotten where the pools were."},{"id":"d","text":"He is teaching the speaker to swim."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'He remembers the Deep Hole where he learnt to swim and the Rocks where yabbies hid. These memories go back to his childhood, so the creek means a lot to him.', NULL, '{"title":"Dry Creek","text":"The creek bed is a cracked old plate\nset out for rain that comes too late.\nThe river gums lean in to see\nwhere all the water used to be.\n\nMy grandfather walks here each day\nand names the pools that went away:\nthe Deep Hole, where he learnt to swim,\nthe Rocks, where yabbies hid from him.\n\nHe kicks the dust. He doesn''t speak.\nHe looks a long time at the creek,\nthen up, beyond the paddock line,\nat clouds that never give a sign.\n\nMum reads the forecast every night,\nthen folds the paper, lips pressed tight.\nThe dam is mud, the sheep are thin,\nand every week less hay comes in.\n\nBut last night, long after bed,\nI heard a sound above my head:\na tap, a drum, a rush, a roar,\nand Grandad laughing at the door.\n\nThis morning, where the cracks had been,\nthe creek runs brown, the hills run green,\nand Grandad stands there, boots and all,\nand lets the rain go on and fall."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'medium', 1, 'Why does Jun type three different messages and delete them all?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He feels awkward asking Hana for help after being rude to her that morning."},{"id":"b","text":"His phone battery is almost flat."},{"id":"c","text":"He can''t remember Hana''s number."},{"id":"d","text":"He wants to message his mum instead."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The paragraph before tells us he called Hana ''the most annoying person in New South Wales'' that morning. Asking her for help now feels embarrassing, so he struggles to find the words.', NULL, '{"title":"Missed Ferry","text":"Jun had promised his mum he would catch the 9:15 ferry home from Circular Quay. At 9:10 he was still in the food court, laughing with his teammates about the penalty he had nearly missed.\n\nHe ran the whole way down George Street, his soccer bag thumping against his back. When he reached the wharf, the ferry was already pulling away, its wake curling white under the lights. The next one was not for another hour.\n\nJun stared at his phone. Mum was working a night shift. That left Hana. That morning he had told his sister she was the most annoying person in New South Wales, and he had meant it. She had borrowed his headphones without asking, again.\n\nHe typed three different messages and deleted them all. Finally he sent just two words: ''Missed ferry.''\n\nThe reply came almost at once. ''Stay where you are. Twenty minutes.''\n\nHana arrived in their mum''s old hatchback, the radio playing the kind of music he always complained about. She didn''t mention the morning. She didn''t mention the time. She just passed him a paper bag with a warm sausage roll inside and turned the music down a little.\n\nHalfway across the Harbour Bridge, Jun pulled his headphones out of his bag and held them out to her.\n\n''Keep them for a while,'' he said.\n\nHana smiled at the road ahead. ''I was going to buy my own,'' she said. ''But thanks.''"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'medium', 1, 'What is the main idea of ''The Hardest-Working Forest''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mangroves are valuable because they protect the coast and shelter young sea creatures."},{"id":"b","text":"Mangroves smell bad and should be cleared."},{"id":"c","text":"Bream and mullet are the most popular fish in NSW."},{"id":"d","text":"The Hawkesbury River is longer than Botany Bay."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Every paragraph explains a job mangroves do (surviving in mud, stopping erosion, acting as nurseries), and the title calls them hard-working. The other options are small details or are wrong.', NULL, '{"title":"The Hardest-Working Forest","text":"Along the edges of many NSW estuaries, from the Hawkesbury River to Botany Bay, grows a strange kind of forest. Its trees stand in salty mud that floods twice a day with the tide. These are mangroves, and they do far more work than most people realise.\n\nMangroves have special roots to survive where other trees would die. The grey mangrove, the most common species in NSW, sends up hundreds of pencil-like roots called pneumatophores. These poke out of the mud and take in air, because the waterlogged mud itself holds very little oxygen.\n\nThe tangle of roots also slows down moving water. As the water slows, mud and sand settle out of it instead of being carried away. In this way, mangroves hold the shoreline together and protect riverbanks from erosion during storms.\n\nMangroves are nurseries, too. Young fish, prawns and crabs shelter among the roots, where larger predators cannot easily reach them. Many of the fish that people catch in NSW, such as bream and mullet, spend part of their lives in estuaries like these.\n\nDespite all this, mangroves were once seen as smelly, useless swamps. Many were cleared for ports, rubbish tips and housing. Today, mangroves in NSW are protected by law, and it is illegal to damage them without a permit.\n\nSo next time you smell that salty, muddy air on a walk by the river, remember: you are standing beside one of the hardest-working ecosystems on the coast."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'medium', 1, 'Who is Priya mainly writing for?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"people in her local community, whom she wants to write to the council"},{"id":"b","text":"students at a school in another state"},{"id":"c","text":"the drivers of school buses"},{"id":"d","text":"people who sell four-wheel drives"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'She ends with ''Write to your local councillor today'', so she is speaking to local families and students and asking them to act.', NULL, '{"title":"Let Us Walk: The Case for a Car-Free School Street","text":"Every school morning, the street outside our school becomes a car park. Engines idle, horns beep and parents double-park while students squeeze between bumpers. I believe it is time our council closed the street to cars for thirty minutes before and after school.\n\nFirstly, a car-free street would be safer. Last term alone, our school newsletter reported two near misses at the crossing. Fewer cars means fewer chances for an accident, and no student should have to dodge a reversing four-wheel drive to get to class.\n\nSecondly, it would be healthier. When cars cannot drop students at the gate, families are more likely to walk, ride or scoot. Even a ten-minute walk each way adds up to more than an hour and a half of exercise a week.\n\nSome people argue that closing the street would be inconvenient for busy parents. That is true, for a few minutes. But parents could park one block away, and the short walk would give them a chance to talk with their children before the day begins. Surely a little inconvenience is worth a lot of safety.\n\nOur council should give it a try. Write to your local councillor today, and let''s make our street a place for people, not traffic.\n\n— Priya Sharma, Year 7 student representative"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence shows Priya answering an argument against her view?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Some people argue that closing the street would be inconvenient for busy parents."},{"id":"b","text":"Firstly, a car-free street would be safer."},{"id":"c","text":"Engines idle, horns beep and parents double-park."},{"id":"d","text":"Our council should give it a try."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Here she raises the other side''s point (it''s inconvenient) and then answers it: parents could park a block away. This is called a rebuttal.', NULL, '{"title":"Let Us Walk: The Case for a Car-Free School Street","text":"Every school morning, the street outside our school becomes a car park. Engines idle, horns beep and parents double-park while students squeeze between bumpers. I believe it is time our council closed the street to cars for thirty minutes before and after school.\n\nFirstly, a car-free street would be safer. Last term alone, our school newsletter reported two near misses at the crossing. Fewer cars means fewer chances for an accident, and no student should have to dodge a reversing four-wheel drive to get to class.\n\nSecondly, it would be healthier. When cars cannot drop students at the gate, families are more likely to walk, ride or scoot. Even a ten-minute walk each way adds up to more than an hour and a half of exercise a week.\n\nSome people argue that closing the street would be inconvenient for busy parents. That is true, for a few minutes. But parents could park one block away, and the short walk would give them a chance to talk with their children before the day begins. Surely a little inconvenience is worth a lot of safety.\n\nOur council should give it a try. Write to your local councillor today, and let''s make our street a place for people, not traffic.\n\n— Priya Sharma, Year 7 student representative"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'hard', 1, 'Put these moments from the poem in the order they happen.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Grandad walks the dry creek and names the old pools."},{"id":"b","text":"Mum reads the forecast with her lips pressed tight."},{"id":"c","text":"The speaker hears rain on the roof at night."},{"id":"d","text":"The creek runs brown and the hills turn green."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The poem moves through the drought (stanzas 1–4), the rain at night (stanza 5) and the next morning (stanza 6).', NULL, '{"title":"Dry Creek","text":"The creek bed is a cracked old plate\nset out for rain that comes too late.\nThe river gums lean in to see\nwhere all the water used to be.\n\nMy grandfather walks here each day\nand names the pools that went away:\nthe Deep Hole, where he learnt to swim,\nthe Rocks, where yabbies hid from him.\n\nHe kicks the dust. He doesn''t speak.\nHe looks a long time at the creek,\nthen up, beyond the paddock line,\nat clouds that never give a sign.\n\nMum reads the forecast every night,\nthen folds the paper, lips pressed tight.\nThe dam is mud, the sheep are thin,\nand every week less hay comes in.\n\nBut last night, long after bed,\nI heard a sound above my head:\na tap, a drum, a rush, a roar,\nand Grandad laughing at the door.\n\nThis morning, where the cracks had been,\nthe creek runs brown, the hills run green,\nand Grandad stands there, boots and all,\nand lets the rain go on and fall."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'hard', 1, 'Why does Hana not mention the argument or how late it is?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She wants to help Jun without making him feel worse."},{"id":"b","text":"She has forgotten about the argument."},{"id":"c","text":"She is too angry to speak to him."},{"id":"d","text":"She doesn''t know what time it is."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'She replies at once, brings him a warm sausage roll and turns the music down. These kind actions suggest she is choosing not to make him feel guilty.', NULL, '{"title":"Missed Ferry","text":"Jun had promised his mum he would catch the 9:15 ferry home from Circular Quay. At 9:10 he was still in the food court, laughing with his teammates about the penalty he had nearly missed.\n\nHe ran the whole way down George Street, his soccer bag thumping against his back. When he reached the wharf, the ferry was already pulling away, its wake curling white under the lights. The next one was not for another hour.\n\nJun stared at his phone. Mum was working a night shift. That left Hana. That morning he had told his sister she was the most annoying person in New South Wales, and he had meant it. She had borrowed his headphones without asking, again.\n\nHe typed three different messages and deleted them all. Finally he sent just two words: ''Missed ferry.''\n\nThe reply came almost at once. ''Stay where you are. Twenty minutes.''\n\nHana arrived in their mum''s old hatchback, the radio playing the kind of music he always complained about. She didn''t mention the morning. She didn''t mention the time. She just passed him a paper bag with a warm sausage roll inside and turned the music down a little.\n\nHalfway across the Harbour Bridge, Jun pulled his headphones out of his bag and held them out to her.\n\n''Keep them for a while,'' he said.\n\nHana smiled at the road ahead. ''I was going to buy my own,'' she said. ''But thanks.''"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_hard', 1, 'What is the main message (theme) of ''Missed Ferry''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Small acts of kindness can repair a relationship better than words."},{"id":"b","text":"You should always catch public transport on time."},{"id":"c","text":"Older sisters are always right."},{"id":"d","text":"Sport is more important than family."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Neither Jun nor Hana says sorry. Instead Hana brings food and Jun offers his headphones. The story shows that actions can mend a fight. The first option is a lesson about the plot, not the deeper theme.', NULL, '{"title":"Missed Ferry","text":"Jun had promised his mum he would catch the 9:15 ferry home from Circular Quay. At 9:10 he was still in the food court, laughing with his teammates about the penalty he had nearly missed.\n\nHe ran the whole way down George Street, his soccer bag thumping against his back. When he reached the wharf, the ferry was already pulling away, its wake curling white under the lights. The next one was not for another hour.\n\nJun stared at his phone. Mum was working a night shift. That left Hana. That morning he had told his sister she was the most annoying person in New South Wales, and he had meant it. She had borrowed his headphones without asking, again.\n\nHe typed three different messages and deleted them all. Finally he sent just two words: ''Missed ferry.''\n\nThe reply came almost at once. ''Stay where you are. Twenty minutes.''\n\nHana arrived in their mum''s old hatchback, the radio playing the kind of music he always complained about. She didn''t mention the morning. She didn''t mention the time. She just passed him a paper bag with a warm sausage roll inside and turned the music down a little.\n\nHalfway across the Harbour Bridge, Jun pulled his headphones out of his bag and held them out to her.\n\n''Keep them for a while,'' he said.\n\nHana smiled at the road ahead. ''I was going to buy my own,'' she said. ''But thanks.''"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does the writer mention that mangroves were once seen as ''smelly, useless swamps''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to contrast old attitudes with what we now know, so readers value mangroves more"},{"id":"b","text":"to prove that mangroves really are useless"},{"id":"c","text":"to explain how to clear mangroves"},{"id":"d","text":"to describe the smell of the Hawkesbury River"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'After four paragraphs of benefits, the old view sounds wrong. The contrast shows how much our understanding has changed and persuades readers to protect mangroves.', NULL, '{"title":"The Hardest-Working Forest","text":"Along the edges of many NSW estuaries, from the Hawkesbury River to Botany Bay, grows a strange kind of forest. Its trees stand in salty mud that floods twice a day with the tide. These are mangroves, and they do far more work than most people realise.\n\nMangroves have special roots to survive where other trees would die. The grey mangrove, the most common species in NSW, sends up hundreds of pencil-like roots called pneumatophores. These poke out of the mud and take in air, because the waterlogged mud itself holds very little oxygen.\n\nThe tangle of roots also slows down moving water. As the water slows, mud and sand settle out of it instead of being carried away. In this way, mangroves hold the shoreline together and protect riverbanks from erosion during storms.\n\nMangroves are nurseries, too. Young fish, prawns and crabs shelter among the roots, where larger predators cannot easily reach them. Many of the fish that people catch in NSW, such as bream and mullet, spend part of their lives in estuaries like these.\n\nDespite all this, mangroves were once seen as smelly, useless swamps. Many were cleared for ports, rubbish tips and housing. Today, mangroves in NSW are protected by law, and it is illegal to damage them without a permit.\n\nSo next time you smell that salty, muddy air on a walk by the river, remember: you are standing beside one of the hardest-working ecosystems on the coast."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_hard', 1, 'How does the mood of ''Dry Creek'' change, and what causes the change?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It moves from worry and sadness to joy and relief because the drought breaks."},{"id":"b","text":"It moves from happiness to anger because the sheep are sold."},{"id":"c","text":"It stays calm all the way through because nothing happens."},{"id":"d","text":"It moves from fear to boredom because Grandad stops talking."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Early stanzas show silence, tight lips and thin sheep. Then the rain comes, Grandad laughs and stands in the rain, so the mood turns joyful and relieved.', NULL, '{"title":"Dry Creek","text":"The creek bed is a cracked old plate\nset out for rain that comes too late.\nThe river gums lean in to see\nwhere all the water used to be.\n\nMy grandfather walks here each day\nand names the pools that went away:\nthe Deep Hole, where he learnt to swim,\nthe Rocks, where yabbies hid from him.\n\nHe kicks the dust. He doesn''t speak.\nHe looks a long time at the creek,\nthen up, beyond the paddock line,\nat clouds that never give a sign.\n\nMum reads the forecast every night,\nthen folds the paper, lips pressed tight.\nThe dam is mud, the sheep are thin,\nand every week less hay comes in.\n\nBut last night, long after bed,\nI heard a sound above my head:\na tap, a drum, a rush, a roar,\nand Grandad laughing at the door.\n\nThis morning, where the cracks had been,\nthe creek runs brown, the hills run green,\nand Grandad stands there, boots and all,\nand lets the rain go on and fall."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which Australian coin shows the superb lyrebird?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the ten-cent coin"},{"id":"b","text":"the twenty-cent coin"},{"id":"c","text":"the one-dollar coin"},{"id":"d","text":"the fifty-cent coin"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The last paragraph says the lyrebird ''appears on the Australian ten-cent coin''.', NULL, '{"title":"The Superb Lyrebird","text":"Walk through the wet forests of south-eastern Australia and you might hear a kookaburra laughing, a whipbird cracking, a currawong calling and a camera shutter clicking, all within a minute. Look closer, and you may find that every one of those sounds is coming from a single bird: the superb lyrebird.\n\nThe superb lyrebird is one of the world''s best mimics. Males learn the calls of dozens of other species and weave them into long, complicated songs. Some lyrebirds living near people have even been recorded copying chainsaws, car alarms and the beeping of reversing trucks.\n\nWhy go to so much trouble? The answer is courtship. In winter, each male clears a small patch of ground called a display mound. Standing on it, he throws his long, lacy tail feathers forward over his head like a shimmering silver veil, then sings and dances to attract a female. The more varied and accurate his song, the more impressive he seems.\n\nLyrebirds are also the gardeners of the forest. As they scratch for worms and insects, they turn over huge amounts of leaf litter and soil. Scientists have found that this helps break down fallen leaves, mixes nutrients into the soil and may even reduce the fuel that feeds bushfires.\n\nThe superb lyrebird appears on the Australian ten-cent coin. Next time you hold one, remember the remarkable bird behind it."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Campers need to bring their own surfboard.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The ad says boards, wetsuits and rash vests are provided. Campers only need sunscreen, a hat and a big smile.', NULL, '{"title":"Saltwater Surf Camp","text":"Are you aged 11 to 15? Have you always wanted to stand up on a board but never known where to start? Then Saltwater Surf Camp at Port Macquarie is for you!\n\nOur five-day January camp runs from Monday to Friday. Each day includes two surf lessons with qualified coaches, beach safety training and fun activities like beach cricket, sandcastle challenges and a sunset barbecue on the final night. On the last afternoon, families are invited down to the beach to watch a mini surf competition, with prizes for the best wave, the biggest wipeout and the most improved surfer.\n\nAll lessons take place on a patrolled beach in small groups of no more than six surfers per coach. Boards, wetsuits and rash vests are provided, so all you need to bring is sunscreen, a hat and a big smile.\n\n''I was scared of the waves before camp, but by Thursday I was catching waves on my own!'' — Mia, 13, Tamworth\n\nCost: $495 per camper, including lunch and snacks each day. Book before 1 December and save $50!\n\nSpaces are strictly limited. Visit our booth at the Port Macquarie markets, or ask your parents to call us to secure your spot.\n\nSaltwater Surf Camp: where every wave is a new adventure."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Where are Aisha and Leo walking to?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Pulpit Rock"},{"id":"b","text":"the Grose River"},{"id":"c","text":"the car park"},{"id":"d","text":"a camping ground"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first paragraph says they are on the track to Pulpit Rock, a lookout above the Grose Valley.', NULL, '{"title":"Pulpit Rock","text":"Aisha had wanted to turn back an hour ago. The track to Pulpit Rock was steeper than the sign had promised, and the steps cut into the cliff seemed to go on for ever. Her cousin Leo, who was fifteen and knew everything, kept striding ahead.\n\n''Nearly there,'' he called, for the fourth time.\n\nAisha stopped to catch her breath and noticed a small grey-brown bird scratching in the leaf litter beside the track. It flicked aside a whole pile of leaves with one foot, then froze and looked at her with a bright black eye. She held her breath. The bird held its ground. For a moment there was nothing in the world but the two of them.\n\n''Aisha! Come on!''\n\nThe bird vanished into the ferns.\n\nWhen she finally reached the lookout, Leo was already taking photos, his phone held high above the railing. Below them, the Grose Valley spread out in every direction, blue and enormous and silent. Leo turned the camera on himself and grinned.\n\n''Worth it, right?'' he said, already scrolling to see how the picture had turned out.\n\nAisha looked out at the valley. It was beautiful, she had to admit. But she was thinking about a bright black eye in the ferns, and the way the whole forest had seemed to hold its breath with her.\n\n''Yeah,'' she said. ''It was worth it.''\n\nShe didn''t tell him which part she meant."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'easy', 1, 'How much does one camper pay if they book before 1 December? Write the amount in dollars.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["445","$445","445 dollars"]'::jsonb, 'The full price is $495 and booking early saves $50: $495 − $50 = $445.', NULL, '{"title":"Saltwater Surf Camp","text":"Are you aged 11 to 15? Have you always wanted to stand up on a board but never known where to start? Then Saltwater Surf Camp at Port Macquarie is for you!\n\nOur five-day January camp runs from Monday to Friday. Each day includes two surf lessons with qualified coaches, beach safety training and fun activities like beach cricket, sandcastle challenges and a sunset barbecue on the final night. On the last afternoon, families are invited down to the beach to watch a mini surf competition, with prizes for the best wave, the biggest wipeout and the most improved surfer.\n\nAll lessons take place on a patrolled beach in small groups of no more than six surfers per coach. Boards, wetsuits and rash vests are provided, so all you need to bring is sunscreen, a hat and a big smile.\n\n''I was scared of the waves before camp, but by Thursday I was catching waves on my own!'' — Mia, 13, Tamworth\n\nCost: $495 per camper, including lunch and snacks each day. Book before 1 December and save $50!\n\nSpaces are strictly limited. Visit our booth at the Port Macquarie markets, or ask your parents to call us to secure your spot.\n\nSaltwater Surf Camp: where every wave is a new adventure."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'easy', 1, 'In winter, a male lyrebird clears a small patch of ground called a display ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["mound","mounds"]'::jsonb, 'The third paragraph says each male clears ''a small patch of ground called a display mound''.', NULL, '{"title":"The Superb Lyrebird","text":"Walk through the wet forests of south-eastern Australia and you might hear a kookaburra laughing, a whipbird cracking, a currawong calling and a camera shutter clicking, all within a minute. Look closer, and you may find that every one of those sounds is coming from a single bird: the superb lyrebird.\n\nThe superb lyrebird is one of the world''s best mimics. Males learn the calls of dozens of other species and weave them into long, complicated songs. Some lyrebirds living near people have even been recorded copying chainsaws, car alarms and the beeping of reversing trucks.\n\nWhy go to so much trouble? The answer is courtship. In winter, each male clears a small patch of ground called a display mound. Standing on it, he throws his long, lacy tail feathers forward over his head like a shimmering silver veil, then sings and dances to attract a female. The more varied and accurate his song, the more impressive he seems.\n\nLyrebirds are also the gardeners of the forest. As they scratch for worms and insects, they turn over huge amounts of leaf litter and soil. Scientists have found that this helps break down fallen leaves, mixes nutrients into the soil and may even reduce the fuel that feeds bushfires.\n\nThe superb lyrebird appears on the Australian ten-cent coin. Next time you hold one, remember the remarkable bird behind it."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'easy', 1, 'What is the mother''s job most likely to be?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a nurse in a hospital"},{"id":"b","text":"a bus driver"},{"id":"c","text":"a teacher"},{"id":"d","text":"a shop assistant"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The poem never says ''nurse'', but she wears a name badge, works all night, checks drips ''from bed to bed'' and comforts a frightened patient. Westmead has a big hospital.', NULL, '{"title":"Night Shift","text":"At seven, when the streetlights wake,\nmy mother pins her name badge on,\nties back her hair and finds her keys,\nand checks the clock, and then she''s gone.\n\nShe drives to Westmead through the dark\nwhile Dad and I wash up and dry;\nwe leave the porch light burning bright,\na small gold star against the sky.\n\nAll night, while I am fast asleep,\nshe checks the drips from bed to bed;\nshe holds a frightened stranger''s hand\nand smooths the hair back from his head.\n\nAt six, the key turns in the lock.\nShe doesn''t wake us. Still, I hear\nthe kettle and the kitchen chair,\nthe sigh she lets out, long and clear.\n\nI bring her toast before the bus.\nHer eyes are heavy, ringed with grey.\nShe says, ''How did you sleep, my love?''\nas if she''d never been away."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'medium', 1, '''Her cousin Leo, who was fifteen and knew everything'' — what does this suggest about how Aisha sees Leo?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She finds him a bit of a know-it-all."},{"id":"b","text":"She thinks he is the smartest person she has met."},{"id":"c","text":"She is scared of him."},{"id":"d","text":"She thinks he is younger than her."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'No one really ''knows everything''. The phrase is gently sarcastic: it tells us Aisha thinks Leo acts as if he knows best, and he keeps calling ''Nearly there''.', NULL, '{"title":"Pulpit Rock","text":"Aisha had wanted to turn back an hour ago. The track to Pulpit Rock was steeper than the sign had promised, and the steps cut into the cliff seemed to go on for ever. Her cousin Leo, who was fifteen and knew everything, kept striding ahead.\n\n''Nearly there,'' he called, for the fourth time.\n\nAisha stopped to catch her breath and noticed a small grey-brown bird scratching in the leaf litter beside the track. It flicked aside a whole pile of leaves with one foot, then froze and looked at her with a bright black eye. She held her breath. The bird held its ground. For a moment there was nothing in the world but the two of them.\n\n''Aisha! Come on!''\n\nThe bird vanished into the ferns.\n\nWhen she finally reached the lookout, Leo was already taking photos, his phone held high above the railing. Below them, the Grose Valley spread out in every direction, blue and enormous and silent. Leo turned the camera on himself and grinned.\n\n''Worth it, right?'' he said, already scrolling to see how the picture had turned out.\n\nAisha looked out at the valley. It was beautiful, she had to admit. But she was thinking about a bright black eye in the ferns, and the way the whole forest had seemed to hold its breath with her.\n\n''Yeah,'' she said. ''It was worth it.''\n\nShe didn''t tell him which part she meant."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'medium', 1, 'Put the steps of the male lyrebird''s courtship display in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"He clears a small patch of ground."},{"id":"b","text":"He stands on the display mound."},{"id":"c","text":"He throws his tail feathers forward over his head."},{"id":"d","text":"He sings and dances to attract a female."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The third paragraph describes the display in this order: clear the mound, stand on it, throw the tail forward, then sing and dance.', NULL, '{"title":"The Superb Lyrebird","text":"Walk through the wet forests of south-eastern Australia and you might hear a kookaburra laughing, a whipbird cracking, a currawong calling and a camera shutter clicking, all within a minute. Look closer, and you may find that every one of those sounds is coming from a single bird: the superb lyrebird.\n\nThe superb lyrebird is one of the world''s best mimics. Males learn the calls of dozens of other species and weave them into long, complicated songs. Some lyrebirds living near people have even been recorded copying chainsaws, car alarms and the beeping of reversing trucks.\n\nWhy go to so much trouble? The answer is courtship. In winter, each male clears a small patch of ground called a display mound. Standing on it, he throws his long, lacy tail feathers forward over his head like a shimmering silver veil, then sings and dances to attract a female. The more varied and accurate his song, the more impressive he seems.\n\nLyrebirds are also the gardeners of the forest. As they scratch for worms and insects, they turn over huge amounts of leaf litter and soil. Scientists have found that this helps break down fallen leaves, mixes nutrients into the soil and may even reduce the fuel that feeds bushfires.\n\nThe superb lyrebird appears on the Australian ten-cent coin. Next time you hold one, remember the remarkable bird behind it."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'medium', 1, 'Which details are there to reassure parents that the camp is safe? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Lessons are on a patrolled beach."},{"id":"b","text":"There are no more than six surfers per coach."},{"id":"c","text":"The coaches are qualified."},{"id":"d","text":"There is a sunset barbecue on the final night."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'A patrolled beach, small groups and qualified coaches all speak to safety. The barbecue is there to make the camp sound fun.', NULL, '{"title":"Saltwater Surf Camp","text":"Are you aged 11 to 15? Have you always wanted to stand up on a board but never known where to start? Then Saltwater Surf Camp at Port Macquarie is for you!\n\nOur five-day January camp runs from Monday to Friday. Each day includes two surf lessons with qualified coaches, beach safety training and fun activities like beach cricket, sandcastle challenges and a sunset barbecue on the final night. On the last afternoon, families are invited down to the beach to watch a mini surf competition, with prizes for the best wave, the biggest wipeout and the most improved surfer.\n\nAll lessons take place on a patrolled beach in small groups of no more than six surfers per coach. Boards, wetsuits and rash vests are provided, so all you need to bring is sunscreen, a hat and a big smile.\n\n''I was scared of the waves before camp, but by Thursday I was catching waves on my own!'' — Mia, 13, Tamworth\n\nCost: $495 per camper, including lunch and snacks each day. Book before 1 December and save $50!\n\nSpaces are strictly limited. Visit our booth at the Port Macquarie markets, or ask your parents to call us to secure your spot.\n\nSaltwater Surf Camp: where every wave is a new adventure."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'hard', 1, 'Why does the writer call lyrebirds ''the gardeners of the forest''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Their scratching turns over soil and leaves, which helps the forest the way a gardener helps a garden."},{"id":"b","text":"They plant seeds in neat rows."},{"id":"c","text":"They eat the flowers in the forest."},{"id":"d","text":"They live in people''s gardens."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The paragraph explains that their scratching breaks down leaves and mixes nutrients into the soil. That is like a gardener digging and composting.', NULL, '{"title":"The Superb Lyrebird","text":"Walk through the wet forests of south-eastern Australia and you might hear a kookaburra laughing, a whipbird cracking, a currawong calling and a camera shutter clicking, all within a minute. Look closer, and you may find that every one of those sounds is coming from a single bird: the superb lyrebird.\n\nThe superb lyrebird is one of the world''s best mimics. Males learn the calls of dozens of other species and weave them into long, complicated songs. Some lyrebirds living near people have even been recorded copying chainsaws, car alarms and the beeping of reversing trucks.\n\nWhy go to so much trouble? The answer is courtship. In winter, each male clears a small patch of ground called a display mound. Standing on it, he throws his long, lacy tail feathers forward over his head like a shimmering silver veil, then sings and dances to attract a female. The more varied and accurate his song, the more impressive he seems.\n\nLyrebirds are also the gardeners of the forest. As they scratch for worms and insects, they turn over huge amounts of leaf litter and soil. Scientists have found that this helps break down fallen leaves, mixes nutrients into the soil and may even reduce the fuel that feeds bushfires.\n\nThe superb lyrebird appears on the Australian ten-cent coin. Next time you hold one, remember the remarkable bird behind it."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'hard', 1, 'Why do Dad and the speaker leave the porch light on, ''a small gold star against the sky''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It welcomes Mum home and shows the family is thinking of her while she is away."},{"id":"b","text":"They are scared of the dark."},{"id":"c","text":"They forgot to turn it off."},{"id":"d","text":"It helps the neighbours find their house."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A star guides travellers home and stands for hope. The light shows the family''s love and that they are waiting for her.', NULL, '{"title":"Night Shift","text":"At seven, when the streetlights wake,\nmy mother pins her name badge on,\nties back her hair and finds her keys,\nand checks the clock, and then she''s gone.\n\nShe drives to Westmead through the dark\nwhile Dad and I wash up and dry;\nwe leave the porch light burning bright,\na small gold star against the sky.\n\nAll night, while I am fast asleep,\nshe checks the drips from bed to bed;\nshe holds a frightened stranger''s hand\nand smooths the hair back from his head.\n\nAt six, the key turns in the lock.\nShe doesn''t wake us. Still, I hear\nthe kettle and the kitchen chair,\nthe sigh she lets out, long and clear.\n\nI bring her toast before the bus.\nHer eyes are heavy, ringed with grey.\nShe says, ''How did you sleep, my love?''\nas if she''d never been away."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'hard', 1, 'Why does the ad include Mia''s comment?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to show nervous beginners that a real camper went from scared to surfing on her own"},{"id":"b","text":"to tell readers where Tamworth is"},{"id":"c","text":"to explain the price of the camp"},{"id":"d","text":"to warn readers that the waves are dangerous"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A comment from a happy customer is called a testimonial. Mia admits she was scared, so readers who are nervous can picture themselves succeeding too.', NULL, '{"title":"Saltwater Surf Camp","text":"Are you aged 11 to 15? Have you always wanted to stand up on a board but never known where to start? Then Saltwater Surf Camp at Port Macquarie is for you!\n\nOur five-day January camp runs from Monday to Friday. Each day includes two surf lessons with qualified coaches, beach safety training and fun activities like beach cricket, sandcastle challenges and a sunset barbecue on the final night. On the last afternoon, families are invited down to the beach to watch a mini surf competition, with prizes for the best wave, the biggest wipeout and the most improved surfer.\n\nAll lessons take place on a patrolled beach in small groups of no more than six surfers per coach. Boards, wetsuits and rash vests are provided, so all you need to bring is sunscreen, a hat and a big smile.\n\n''I was scared of the waves before camp, but by Thursday I was catching waves on my own!'' — Mia, 13, Tamworth\n\nCost: $495 per camper, including lunch and snacks each day. Book before 1 December and save $50!\n\nSpaces are strictly limited. Visit our booth at the Port Macquarie markets, or ask your parents to call us to secure your spot.\n\nSaltwater Surf Camp: where every wave is a new adventure."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_hard', 1, '''She didn''t tell him which part she meant.'' Which part of the walk was most worth it to Aisha?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the quiet moment with the bird, which Leo missed while rushing ahead"},{"id":"b","text":"the photo Leo took at the lookout"},{"id":"c","text":"reaching the end of the steep steps"},{"id":"d","text":"seeing the whole Grose Valley from the railing"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'At the lookout she is still ''thinking about a bright black eye in the ferns''. Leo only cares about the view and his photo, so she keeps her special moment to herself.', NULL, '{"title":"Pulpit Rock","text":"Aisha had wanted to turn back an hour ago. The track to Pulpit Rock was steeper than the sign had promised, and the steps cut into the cliff seemed to go on for ever. Her cousin Leo, who was fifteen and knew everything, kept striding ahead.\n\n''Nearly there,'' he called, for the fourth time.\n\nAisha stopped to catch her breath and noticed a small grey-brown bird scratching in the leaf litter beside the track. It flicked aside a whole pile of leaves with one foot, then froze and looked at her with a bright black eye. She held her breath. The bird held its ground. For a moment there was nothing in the world but the two of them.\n\n''Aisha! Come on!''\n\nThe bird vanished into the ferns.\n\nWhen she finally reached the lookout, Leo was already taking photos, his phone held high above the railing. Below them, the Grose Valley spread out in every direction, blue and enormous and silent. Leo turned the camera on himself and grinned.\n\n''Worth it, right?'' he said, already scrolling to see how the picture had turned out.\n\nAisha looked out at the valley. It was beautiful, she had to admit. But she was thinking about a bright black eye in the ferns, and the way the whole forest had seemed to hold its breath with her.\n\n''Yeah,'' she said. ''It was worth it.''\n\nShe didn''t tell him which part she meant."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which ideas does ''Pulpit Rock'' suggest? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rushing towards a goal can mean missing small, special moments."},{"id":"b","text":"People can experience the same place in very different ways."},{"id":"c","text":"Bushwalking is too dangerous for young people."},{"id":"d","text":"Photos are the best way to remember a place."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Leo rushes and photographs the view, while Aisha treasures a quiet moment with a bird. The story questions photos rather than praising them, and it never says walking is dangerous.', NULL, '{"title":"Pulpit Rock","text":"Aisha had wanted to turn back an hour ago. The track to Pulpit Rock was steeper than the sign had promised, and the steps cut into the cliff seemed to go on for ever. Her cousin Leo, who was fifteen and knew everything, kept striding ahead.\n\n''Nearly there,'' he called, for the fourth time.\n\nAisha stopped to catch her breath and noticed a small grey-brown bird scratching in the leaf litter beside the track. It flicked aside a whole pile of leaves with one foot, then froze and looked at her with a bright black eye. She held her breath. The bird held its ground. For a moment there was nothing in the world but the two of them.\n\n''Aisha! Come on!''\n\nThe bird vanished into the ferns.\n\nWhen she finally reached the lookout, Leo was already taking photos, his phone held high above the railing. Below them, the Grose Valley spread out in every direction, blue and enormous and silent. Leo turned the camera on himself and grinned.\n\n''Worth it, right?'' he said, already scrolling to see how the picture had turned out.\n\nAisha looked out at the valley. It was beautiful, she had to admit. But she was thinking about a bright black eye in the ferns, and the way the whole forest had seemed to hold its breath with her.\n\n''Yeah,'' she said. ''It was worth it.''\n\nShe didn''t tell him which part she meant."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071101', 'very_hard', 1, 'What does the last line, ''as if she''d never been away'', suggest about the mother?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She hides how tired she is so she can put her child first."},{"id":"b","text":"She has forgotten that she went to work."},{"id":"c","text":"She did not really go to work that night."},{"id":"d","text":"She is cross that the toast is late."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Her eyes are heavy and ringed with grey, yet she asks about her child''s sleep. Putting her own tiredness aside shows her love and the sacrifices she makes.', NULL, '{"title":"Night Shift","text":"At seven, when the streetlights wake,\nmy mother pins her name badge on,\nties back her hair and finds her keys,\nand checks the clock, and then she''s gone.\n\nShe drives to Westmead through the dark\nwhile Dad and I wash up and dry;\nwe leave the porch light burning bright,\na small gold star against the sky.\n\nAll night, while I am fast asleep,\nshe checks the drips from bed to bed;\nshe holds a frightened stranger''s hand\nand smooths the hair back from his head.\n\nAt six, the key turns in the lock.\nShe doesn''t wake us. Still, I hear\nthe kettle and the kitchen chair,\nthe sigh she lets out, long and clear.\n\nI bring her toast before the bus.\nHer eyes are heavy, ringed with grey.\nShe says, ''How did you sleep, my love?''\nas if she''d never been away."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_easy', 1, '''the heat had pressed down on Wollongong like a heavy hand'' — what kind of figurative language is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"simile"},{"id":"b","text":"metaphor"},{"id":"c","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"d","text":"alliteration"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It compares the heat to a heavy hand using the word ''like'', so it is a simile.', NULL, '{"title":"The Southerly Buster","text":"All afternoon the heat had pressed down on Wollongong like a heavy hand. The air was thick and still. Even the seagulls had given up, slumped on the sand with their beaks open. Lena lay on the verandah, sure she would melt into a puddle before dinner.\n\nThen, at five o''clock, the sky to the south turned the colour of a bruise. A long roll of cloud came racing up the coast, swallowing the escarpment as it went. The wind arrived first. It snatched the washing off the line, flung the beach umbrellas into the dunes and whistled through every crack in the old house.\n\nThunder grumbled, low and lazy at first, then cracked overhead so loudly that Lena''s dog, Biscuit, shot under the bed. Rain hammered the tin roof, rat-a-tat-tat, and the gutters gurgled and overflowed.\n\nWithin ten minutes the temperature had dropped by fifteen degrees. Lena stood at the screen door, breathing in the smell of wet bitumen and crushed gum leaves. The storm was a broom, sweeping the whole sticky day out to sea.\n\nBy seven it was over. The clouds had shuffled off to the north, grumbling to themselves, and the first stars blinked awake in a sky that had been washed clean."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_easy', 1, '''The old car coughed and spluttered up the hill'' uses personification.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Coughing and spluttering are things people do. Giving a car human actions is personification.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word in this sentence is onomatopoeia? ''The bees buzzed around the bottlebrush.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '["buzzed","buzz"]'::jsonb, '''Buzzed'' sounds like the noise bees make, so it is onomatopoeia.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'easy', 1, '''Rain hammered the tin roof, rat-a-tat-tat.'' What technique is ''rat-a-tat-tat''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["onomatopoeia","onomatopoeic"]'::jsonb, '''Rat-a-tat-tat'' imitates the sound of rain drumming on tin, so it is onomatopoeia.', NULL, '{"title":"The Southerly Buster","text":"All afternoon the heat had pressed down on Wollongong like a heavy hand. The air was thick and still. Even the seagulls had given up, slumped on the sand with their beaks open. Lena lay on the verandah, sure she would melt into a puddle before dinner.\n\nThen, at five o''clock, the sky to the south turned the colour of a bruise. A long roll of cloud came racing up the coast, swallowing the escarpment as it went. The wind arrived first. It snatched the washing off the line, flung the beach umbrellas into the dunes and whistled through every crack in the old house.\n\nThunder grumbled, low and lazy at first, then cracked overhead so loudly that Lena''s dog, Biscuit, shot under the bed. Rain hammered the tin roof, rat-a-tat-tat, and the gutters gurgled and overflowed.\n\nWithin ten minutes the temperature had dropped by fifteen degrees. Lena stood at the screen door, breathing in the smell of wet bitumen and crushed gum leaves. The storm was a broom, sweeping the whole sticky day out to sea.\n\nBy seven it was over. The clouds had shuffled off to the north, grumbling to themselves, and the first stars blinked awake in a sky that had been washed clean."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'easy', 1, '''the gulls go squabbling, squawking, screeching'' — what is the repeated s sound at the start of words called?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"alliteration"},{"id":"b","text":"simile"},{"id":"c","text":"hyperbole"},{"id":"d","text":"metaphor"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Repeating the same first sound in words close together is alliteration. Here it imitates the harsh noise of the gulls.', NULL, '{"title":"Harbour at Dawn","text":"The harbour is a sleeping cat\ncurled grey beneath the sky,\nuntil the sun comes creeping up\nand opens one gold eye.\n\nThe ferries grumble from their wharves,\ntheir engines churn and chug;\nthe gulls go squabbling, squawking, screeching\nround every fishing tug.\n\nThe Bridge, a coat-hanger of steel,\nholds up the morning air,\nwhile joggers pound across its back\nwith not a breath to spare.\n\nDown at the Quay a busker plays,\nhis guitar case open wide;\nhis song floats out across the water\nand rides the morning tide.\n\nBy eight the cat is wide awake:\nit purrs and hums and roars,\nten thousand footsteps, horns and bells\nand ferries at the doors."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence uses hyperbole?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"My school bag weighs a tonne."},{"id":"b","text":"My school bag is heavy."},{"id":"c","text":"My school bag is blue."},{"id":"d","text":"My school bag is as old as my shoes."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration. A school bag can''t really weigh a tonne (1000 kg); the writer exaggerates to show how heavy it feels.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'medium', 1, '''The storm was a broom, sweeping the whole sticky day out to sea.'' What does this metaphor suggest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The storm quickly cleared away the heat and discomfort."},{"id":"b","text":"The storm left a big mess on the beach."},{"id":"c","text":"Lena had to sweep the verandah."},{"id":"d","text":"The storm was slow and gentle."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A broom sweeps dirt away. The metaphor shows the storm getting rid of the sticky heat, leaving everything fresh.', NULL, '{"title":"The Southerly Buster","text":"All afternoon the heat had pressed down on Wollongong like a heavy hand. The air was thick and still. Even the seagulls had given up, slumped on the sand with their beaks open. Lena lay on the verandah, sure she would melt into a puddle before dinner.\n\nThen, at five o''clock, the sky to the south turned the colour of a bruise. A long roll of cloud came racing up the coast, swallowing the escarpment as it went. The wind arrived first. It snatched the washing off the line, flung the beach umbrellas into the dunes and whistled through every crack in the old house.\n\nThunder grumbled, low and lazy at first, then cracked overhead so loudly that Lena''s dog, Biscuit, shot under the bed. Rain hammered the tin roof, rat-a-tat-tat, and the gutters gurgled and overflowed.\n\nWithin ten minutes the temperature had dropped by fifteen degrees. Lena stood at the screen door, breathing in the smell of wet bitumen and crushed gum leaves. The storm was a broom, sweeping the whole sticky day out to sea.\n\nBy seven it was over. The clouds had shuffled off to the north, grumbling to themselves, and the first stars blinked awake in a sky that had been washed clean."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'medium', 1, 'Why does the poet call the Harbour Bridge ''a coat-hanger of steel''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Its curved arch and flat deck look like the shape of a coat-hanger."},{"id":"b","text":"People hang their coats on it."},{"id":"c","text":"It is very small and light."},{"id":"d","text":"It is made of wire."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The metaphor is based on shape: the arch with the road hanging below looks like a coat-hanger. Sydneysiders even nickname it ''the Coathanger''.', NULL, '{"title":"Harbour at Dawn","text":"The harbour is a sleeping cat\ncurled grey beneath the sky,\nuntil the sun comes creeping up\nand opens one gold eye.\n\nThe ferries grumble from their wharves,\ntheir engines churn and chug;\nthe gulls go squabbling, squawking, screeching\nround every fishing tug.\n\nThe Bridge, a coat-hanger of steel,\nholds up the morning air,\nwhile joggers pound across its back\nwith not a breath to spare.\n\nDown at the Quay a busker plays,\nhis guitar case open wide;\nhis song floats out across the water\nand rides the morning tide.\n\nBy eight the cat is wide awake:\nit purrs and hums and roars,\nten thousand footsteps, horns and bells\nand ferries at the doors."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from the most positive connotation to the most negative.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"slender"},{"id":"b","text":"thin"},{"id":"c","text":"skinny"},{"id":"d","text":"scrawny"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'All four mean ''not wide'', but ''slender'' sounds graceful, ''thin'' is neutral, ''skinny'' is a bit negative and ''scrawny'' suggests weak and unhealthy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'hard', 1, 'What does the jacaranda symbolise for the narrator?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"their mother and the memories of her"},{"id":"b","text":"the end of the school year"},{"id":"c","text":"the hot weather"},{"id":"d","text":"the family''s new house"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Mum planted it the year the narrator was born and called it her exam tree. Now that she has died, the tree stands for her.', NULL, '{"title":"The Jacaranda","text":"The jacaranda in our backyard was planted the year I was born. Mum dug the hole herself, Dad says, with me asleep in a basket on the grass beside her. For most of the year it is an ordinary tree, a tangle of grey branches and feathery leaves that drop into the gutters.\n\nBut every November it explodes into purple.\n\nMum used to call it her exam tree. When she was at school in Grafton, she said, the jacarandas bloomed just as the exams began, and students joked that if a flower landed on your head, you would pass.\n\nThis is the first November without her. When the first buds opened, I couldn''t look at them. I kept the blinds in my room closed and walked to the bus stop the long way, so I wouldn''t have to pass under the branches.\n\nThen, one morning, Dad called me outside. The whole lawn was a purple carpet, and he was standing in the middle of it in his work boots, holding the old basket.\n\n''Your mum would have wanted a photo,'' he said.\n\nI stood under the branches while he fumbled with his phone. A single flower drifted down and landed in my hair. Dad laughed, a real laugh, the first one I''d heard in months.\n\n''Well,'' he said, ''looks like you''ll pass.''"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'hard', 1, 'Which phrases use personification? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"the clouds had shuffled off to the north, grumbling to themselves"},{"id":"b","text":"the first stars blinked awake"},{"id":"c","text":"the sky to the south turned the colour of a bruise"},{"id":"d","text":"Lena''s dog, Biscuit, shot under the bed"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Shuffling, grumbling and blinking awake are human actions given to clouds and stars. The bruise is a colour comparison, and a dog running under a bed is just what dogs do.', NULL, '{"title":"The Southerly Buster","text":"All afternoon the heat had pressed down on Wollongong like a heavy hand. The air was thick and still. Even the seagulls had given up, slumped on the sand with their beaks open. Lena lay on the verandah, sure she would melt into a puddle before dinner.\n\nThen, at five o''clock, the sky to the south turned the colour of a bruise. A long roll of cloud came racing up the coast, swallowing the escarpment as it went. The wind arrived first. It snatched the washing off the line, flung the beach umbrellas into the dunes and whistled through every crack in the old house.\n\nThunder grumbled, low and lazy at first, then cracked overhead so loudly that Lena''s dog, Biscuit, shot under the bed. Rain hammered the tin roof, rat-a-tat-tat, and the gutters gurgled and overflowed.\n\nWithin ten minutes the temperature had dropped by fifteen degrees. Lena stood at the screen door, breathing in the smell of wet bitumen and crushed gum leaves. The storm was a broom, sweeping the whole sticky day out to sea.\n\nBy seven it was over. The clouds had shuffled off to the north, grumbling to themselves, and the first stars blinked awake in a sky that had been washed clean."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'hard', 1, 'The feelings and ideas a word suggests, beyond its dictionary meaning, are called its ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["connotation","connotations"]'::jsonb, '''Home'' and ''house'' have similar dictionary meanings, but ''home'' carries feelings of warmth and belonging. Those extra feelings are its connotations.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_hard', 1, 'The poem begins with the metaphor ''The harbour is a sleeping cat''. Which other lines continue this same comparison? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"and opens one gold eye"},{"id":"b","text":"it purrs and hums and roars"},{"id":"c","text":"the gulls go squabbling, squawking, screeching"},{"id":"d","text":"his guitar case open wide"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'A cat opens its eye when it wakes and purrs when content. These lines extend the cat metaphor through the poem, which is called an extended metaphor. The gulls and busker are not part of it.', NULL, '{"title":"Harbour at Dawn","text":"The harbour is a sleeping cat\ncurled grey beneath the sky,\nuntil the sun comes creeping up\nand opens one gold eye.\n\nThe ferries grumble from their wharves,\ntheir engines churn and chug;\nthe gulls go squabbling, squawking, screeching\nround every fishing tug.\n\nThe Bridge, a coat-hanger of steel,\nholds up the morning air,\nwhile joggers pound across its back\nwith not a breath to spare.\n\nDown at the Quay a busker plays,\nhis guitar case open wide;\nhis song floats out across the water\nand rides the morning tide.\n\nBy eight the cat is wide awake:\nit purrs and hums and roars,\nten thousand footsteps, horns and bells\nand ferries at the doors."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_hard', 1, 'How have the narrator''s feelings about the jacaranda changed by the end?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"At first it is too painful to look at, but by the end it brings comfort and a link to Mum."},{"id":"b","text":"At first they love it, but by the end they want it cut down."},{"id":"c","text":"They never notice the tree at all."},{"id":"d","text":"They are annoyed that its leaves fill the gutters."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The narrator closes the blinds and walks the long way to avoid it. At the end they stand under the branches for a photo while Dad laughs, so the tree now brings comfort.', NULL, '{"title":"The Jacaranda","text":"The jacaranda in our backyard was planted the year I was born. Mum dug the hole herself, Dad says, with me asleep in a basket on the grass beside her. For most of the year it is an ordinary tree, a tangle of grey branches and feathery leaves that drop into the gutters.\n\nBut every November it explodes into purple.\n\nMum used to call it her exam tree. When she was at school in Grafton, she said, the jacarandas bloomed just as the exams began, and students joked that if a flower landed on your head, you would pass.\n\nThis is the first November without her. When the first buds opened, I couldn''t look at them. I kept the blinds in my room closed and walked to the bus stop the long way, so I wouldn''t have to pass under the branches.\n\nThen, one morning, Dad called me outside. The whole lawn was a purple carpet, and he was standing in the middle of it in his work boots, holding the old basket.\n\n''Your mum would have wanted a photo,'' he said.\n\nI stood under the branches while he fumbled with his phone. A single flower drifted down and landed in my hair. Dad laughed, a real laugh, the first one I''d heard in months.\n\n''Well,'' he said, ''looks like you''ll pass.''"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why is the flower landing in the narrator''s hair such a powerful ending?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It echoes Mum''s exam-tree joke, so she feels present in a moment of shared hope."},{"id":"b","text":"It shows the tree is dying."},{"id":"c","text":"It means the narrator will fail their exams."},{"id":"d","text":"It shows that Dad is bad at taking photos."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Mum said a flower landing on your head means you''ll pass. When Dad repeats her joke, the flower links the family to Mum, and they laugh together for the first time in months.', NULL, '{"title":"The Jacaranda","text":"The jacaranda in our backyard was planted the year I was born. Mum dug the hole herself, Dad says, with me asleep in a basket on the grass beside her. For most of the year it is an ordinary tree, a tangle of grey branches and feathery leaves that drop into the gutters.\n\nBut every November it explodes into purple.\n\nMum used to call it her exam tree. When she was at school in Grafton, she said, the jacarandas bloomed just as the exams began, and students joked that if a flower landed on your head, you would pass.\n\nThis is the first November without her. When the first buds opened, I couldn''t look at them. I kept the blinds in my room closed and walked to the bus stop the long way, so I wouldn''t have to pass under the branches.\n\nThen, one morning, Dad called me outside. The whole lawn was a purple carpet, and he was standing in the middle of it in his work boots, holding the old basket.\n\n''Your mum would have wanted a photo,'' he said.\n\nI stood under the branches while he fumbled with his phone. A single flower drifted down and landed in my hair. Dad laughed, a real laugh, the first one I''d heard in months.\n\n''Well,'' he said, ''looks like you''ll pass.''"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_easy', 1, '''Her smile was as bright as the sun'' is a metaphor.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'It compares using ''as … as'', so it is a simile. A metaphor would say ''Her smile was the sun''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sense does the first paragraph mostly appeal to?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"smell"},{"id":"b","text":"touch"},{"id":"c","text":"hearing"},{"id":"d","text":"taste"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The paragraph begins ''First comes the smell'' and lists hot chips, fairy floss and the scent of the animal pavilions.', NULL, '{"title":"Show Day","text":"The Easter Show hits you before you even reach the gates. First comes the smell: hot chips, fairy floss and the earthy, grassy scent of the animal pavilions, all mixed together into one big, delicious cloud.\n\nThen comes the noise. Sideshow spruikers bellow over the crowd, rides clank and whirr, and somewhere a rooster is crowing as if the whole showground belongs to him. Every few seconds a scream rises from one of the big rides and floats away across the sky.\n\nInside, the crowd is a river, and you are a leaf caught in its current. You are swept past the woodchopping arena, where axes thud into logs, and on past the dog high jump, where a kelpie flies over the bar like a furry rocket.\n\nMy little brother Theo has spent all his pocket money on a showbag the size of a small car. He clutches it to his chest, sticky with fairy floss, his eyes as round as dinner plates.\n\nBy late afternoon the sun is sinking, and the Ferris wheel lights flicker on one by one, a necklace of coloured jewels against the darkening sky. My feet ache, my hands are sticky and my ears are ringing. Theo is asleep on Dad''s shoulder, still holding his showbag.\n\nIt is, without any doubt, the best day of the year."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_easy', 1, '''Sizzle'', ''crash'' and ''hiss'' are examples of ___, words that sound like the noise they describe.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["onomatopoeia","onomatopoeic words"]'::jsonb, 'When a word imitates the sound it names, it is onomatopoeia. Say ''hiss'' slowly and it sounds like a snake.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'easy', 1, '''his eyes as round as dinner plates'' — what technique compares two things using ''as''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["simile","a simile"]'::jsonb, 'A comparison using ''like'' or ''as'' is a simile. Theo''s eyes are compared to dinner plates.', NULL, '{"title":"Show Day","text":"The Easter Show hits you before you even reach the gates. First comes the smell: hot chips, fairy floss and the earthy, grassy scent of the animal pavilions, all mixed together into one big, delicious cloud.\n\nThen comes the noise. Sideshow spruikers bellow over the crowd, rides clank and whirr, and somewhere a rooster is crowing as if the whole showground belongs to him. Every few seconds a scream rises from one of the big rides and floats away across the sky.\n\nInside, the crowd is a river, and you are a leaf caught in its current. You are swept past the woodchopping arena, where axes thud into logs, and on past the dog high jump, where a kelpie flies over the bar like a furry rocket.\n\nMy little brother Theo has spent all his pocket money on a showbag the size of a small car. He clutches it to his chest, sticky with fairy floss, his eyes as round as dinner plates.\n\nBy late afternoon the sun is sinking, and the Ferris wheel lights flicker on one by one, a necklace of coloured jewels against the darkening sky. My feet ache, my hands are sticky and my ears are ringing. Theo is asleep on Dad''s shoulder, still holding his showbag.\n\nIt is, without any doubt, the best day of the year."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'easy', 1, 'Which technique is used most throughout the poem ''Old Gum''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"personification"},{"id":"b","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"c","text":"rhetorical questions"},{"id":"d","text":"alliteration"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The tree is called ''she'', holds up her arms to pray, has skin and hair, doesn''t flinch and has a heartbeat. These are all human qualities, so it is personification.', NULL, '{"title":"Old Gum","text":"The old gum stands beside the road,\nher arms held up to pray;\nshe''s watched the coaches come and go\nfor a hundred years and a day.\n\nHer skin peels off in ribbons, grey\nand cream and salmon-pink;\nshe drops her branches without warning,\nquicker than you''d think.\n\nThe cockatoos come screeching in\nand gossip in her hair;\nthe wind runs whispering through her leaves\nand leaves its secrets there.\n\nThey want to widen the old road.\nThe surveyors'' pegs are in.\nShe doesn''t flinch. She''s seen it all:\nthe drought, the flood, the din.\n\nBut I have hugged her rough, warm trunk\nand felt her heartbeat slow,\nand I will stand beside her, too,\nthe day they say she''ll go."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence uses alliteration?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Seven sleepy seals sunbaked on the sand."},{"id":"b","text":"The seals lay on the beach in the sun."},{"id":"c","text":"The seal was as fat as a pillow."},{"id":"d","text":"The seal barked loudly at the boat."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Seven, sleepy, seals, sunbaked and sand all begin with s. Repeating the first sound is alliteration.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'medium', 1, '''the crowd is a river, and you are a leaf caught in its current'' — what feeling does this metaphor create?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"being swept along with no control over where you go"},{"id":"b","text":"feeling cool and refreshed"},{"id":"c","text":"being bored and lonely"},{"id":"d","text":"being lost in the bush"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A leaf in a river can''t choose its path; it is carried by the current. The metaphor shows how the huge crowd pushes you along.', NULL, '{"title":"Show Day","text":"The Easter Show hits you before you even reach the gates. First comes the smell: hot chips, fairy floss and the earthy, grassy scent of the animal pavilions, all mixed together into one big, delicious cloud.\n\nThen comes the noise. Sideshow spruikers bellow over the crowd, rides clank and whirr, and somewhere a rooster is crowing as if the whole showground belongs to him. Every few seconds a scream rises from one of the big rides and floats away across the sky.\n\nInside, the crowd is a river, and you are a leaf caught in its current. You are swept past the woodchopping arena, where axes thud into logs, and on past the dog high jump, where a kelpie flies over the bar like a furry rocket.\n\nMy little brother Theo has spent all his pocket money on a showbag the size of a small car. He clutches it to his chest, sticky with fairy floss, his eyes as round as dinner plates.\n\nBy late afternoon the sun is sinking, and the Ferris wheel lights flicker on one by one, a necklace of coloured jewels against the darkening sky. My feet ache, my hands are sticky and my ears are ringing. Theo is asleep on Dad''s shoulder, still holding his showbag.\n\nIt is, without any doubt, the best day of the year."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'medium', 1, '''The cockatoos come screeching in / and gossip in her hair.'' What is ''her hair''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the gum tree''s leaves and branches"},{"id":"b","text":"the poet''s hair"},{"id":"c","text":"the cockatoos'' feathers"},{"id":"d","text":"the long grass by the road"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The tree is personified as a woman, so her leafy branches at the top become her ''hair'', where the cockatoos sit and chatter.', NULL, '{"title":"Old Gum","text":"The old gum stands beside the road,\nher arms held up to pray;\nshe''s watched the coaches come and go\nfor a hundred years and a day.\n\nHer skin peels off in ribbons, grey\nand cream and salmon-pink;\nshe drops her branches without warning,\nquicker than you''d think.\n\nThe cockatoos come screeching in\nand gossip in her hair;\nthe wind runs whispering through her leaves\nand leaves its secrets there.\n\nThey want to widen the old road.\nThe surveyors'' pegs are in.\nShe doesn''t flinch. She''s seen it all:\nthe drought, the flood, the din.\n\nBut I have hugged her rough, warm trunk\nand felt her heartbeat slow,\nand I will stand beside her, too,\nthe day they say she''ll go."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'medium', 1, 'Which word gives the most positive impression of a person who won''t give up?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"determined"},{"id":"b","text":"stubborn"},{"id":"c","text":"pig-headed"},{"id":"d","text":"obstinate"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'All four describe someone who sticks to something. ''Determined'' has a positive connotation (admirable), while the others suggest the person is difficult.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'hard', 1, 'Which phrases use hyperbole? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"a showbag the size of a small car"},{"id":"b","text":"his eyes as round as dinner plates"},{"id":"c","text":"axes thud into logs"},{"id":"d","text":"My feet ache"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'A showbag can''t be as big as a car, and eyes can''t be as big as plates; these exaggerate for effect. Axes thudding and aching feet are simply true.', NULL, '{"title":"Show Day","text":"The Easter Show hits you before you even reach the gates. First comes the smell: hot chips, fairy floss and the earthy, grassy scent of the animal pavilions, all mixed together into one big, delicious cloud.\n\nThen comes the noise. Sideshow spruikers bellow over the crowd, rides clank and whirr, and somewhere a rooster is crowing as if the whole showground belongs to him. Every few seconds a scream rises from one of the big rides and floats away across the sky.\n\nInside, the crowd is a river, and you are a leaf caught in its current. You are swept past the woodchopping arena, where axes thud into logs, and on past the dog high jump, where a kelpie flies over the bar like a furry rocket.\n\nMy little brother Theo has spent all his pocket money on a showbag the size of a small car. He clutches it to his chest, sticky with fairy floss, his eyes as round as dinner plates.\n\nBy late afternoon the sun is sinking, and the Ferris wheel lights flicker on one by one, a necklace of coloured jewels against the darkening sky. My feet ache, my hands are sticky and my ears are ringing. Theo is asleep on Dad''s shoulder, still holding his showbag.\n\nIt is, without any doubt, the best day of the year."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'hard', 1, 'Which words from the ad could be a positive way of saying the rooms are small? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"cosy"},{"id":"b","text":"intimate"},{"id":"c","text":"towering"},{"id":"d","text":"rambling"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '''Cosy'' and ''intimate'' make small spaces sound warm and friendly. ''Towering'' describes the tall gums, and ''rambling'' describes a large, spreading garden.', NULL, '{"title":"For Sale: 14 Banksia Lane, Katoomba","text":"Escape to the mountains in this charming, cosy cottage, nestled among towering gums just minutes from Katoomba''s bustling main street.\n\nBursting with character, this classic 1920s home boasts original timber floors, a quaint kitchen and a sun-drenched reading nook, perfect for lazy Sunday afternoons. The intimate living room centres on a crackling open fireplace, ideal for those crisp mountain winters.\n\nOutside, a rambling cottage garden overflows with roses, lavender and hydrangeas, while birdsong drifts from the lush bushland next door. With a little loving care, this hidden gem could become the mountain retreat of your dreams.\n\nInside, generous built-in cupboards keep everything neat and tidy, and a charming claw-foot bath invites you to soak away the stresses of city life after a day exploring the mountains. Opportunities like this are rare. Inspect this Saturday from 10 to 10:30 am, before someone else snaps it up!\n\nJust a short stroll from the famous Three Sisters lookout and the bushwalking tracks of the national park, this home offers the best of both worlds: peaceful village living with every convenience close at hand. Whether you are searching for a weekend escape, a first home or a creative studio, this is a property you simply have to see to believe.\n\nContact Gumleaf Realty for details."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'hard', 1, 'In many stories, a wild storm arrives at an important moment. What does such a storm most often symbolise?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"conflict or trouble"},{"id":"b","text":"peace and calm"},{"id":"c","text":"wealth"},{"id":"d","text":"a holiday"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Storms are violent and frightening, so writers often use them to stand for conflict, danger or strong emotions in the characters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_hard', 1, 'What does the old gum most likely symbolise in the poem?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the long history of the place, which is now threatened by change"},{"id":"b","text":"the need for wider roads"},{"id":"c","text":"how dangerous gum trees are"},{"id":"d","text":"the speed of modern life"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'She has stood for ''a hundred years and a day'' through drought and flood, but now surveyors'' pegs mark the road widening. She stands for the past and for nature under threat.', NULL, '{"title":"Old Gum","text":"The old gum stands beside the road,\nher arms held up to pray;\nshe''s watched the coaches come and go\nfor a hundred years and a day.\n\nHer skin peels off in ribbons, grey\nand cream and salmon-pink;\nshe drops her branches without warning,\nquicker than you''d think.\n\nThe cockatoos come screeching in\nand gossip in her hair;\nthe wind runs whispering through her leaves\nand leaves its secrets there.\n\nThey want to widen the old road.\nThe surveyors'' pegs are in.\nShe doesn''t flinch. She''s seen it all:\nthe drought, the flood, the din.\n\nBut I have hugged her rough, warm trunk\nand felt her heartbeat slow,\nand I will stand beside her, too,\nthe day they say she''ll go."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_hard', 1, '''With a little loving care, this hidden gem could become the mountain retreat of your dreams.'' What is the writer most likely hinting?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The house needs repairs, but this is described with positive words."},{"id":"b","text":"The house is brand new."},{"id":"c","text":"The house is hard to find because it has no street address."},{"id":"d","text":"The owners will look after the house for you."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Could become'' and ''a little loving care'' suggest work is needed. ''Hidden gem'' and ''dreams'' have positive connotations that make the repairs sound appealing.', NULL, '{"title":"For Sale: 14 Banksia Lane, Katoomba","text":"Escape to the mountains in this charming, cosy cottage, nestled among towering gums just minutes from Katoomba''s bustling main street.\n\nBursting with character, this classic 1920s home boasts original timber floors, a quaint kitchen and a sun-drenched reading nook, perfect for lazy Sunday afternoons. The intimate living room centres on a crackling open fireplace, ideal for those crisp mountain winters.\n\nOutside, a rambling cottage garden overflows with roses, lavender and hydrangeas, while birdsong drifts from the lush bushland next door. With a little loving care, this hidden gem could become the mountain retreat of your dreams.\n\nInside, generous built-in cupboards keep everything neat and tidy, and a charming claw-foot bath invites you to soak away the stresses of city life after a day exploring the mountains. Opportunities like this are rare. Inspect this Saturday from 10 to 10:30 am, before someone else snaps it up!\n\nJust a short stroll from the famous Three Sisters lookout and the bushwalking tracks of the national park, this home offers the best of both worlds: peaceful village living with every convenience close at hand. Whether you are searching for a weekend escape, a first home or a creative studio, this is a property you simply have to see to believe.\n\nContact Gumleaf Realty for details."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071102', 'very_hard', 1, 'In the last stanza, the speaker ''felt her heartbeat slow''. What effect does this personification have?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It makes the tree feel like an ageing, living friend, so the reader cares whether it is cut down."},{"id":"b","text":"It shows the tree is about to fall on the road."},{"id":"c","text":"It explains how trees take in water."},{"id":"d","text":"It makes the poem funny."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A slow heartbeat belongs to someone old and alive. Feeling it while hugging the tree makes it seem like a person, which builds sympathy before ''the day they say she''ll go''.', NULL, '{"title":"Old Gum","text":"The old gum stands beside the road,\nher arms held up to pray;\nshe''s watched the coaches come and go\nfor a hundred years and a day.\n\nHer skin peels off in ribbons, grey\nand cream and salmon-pink;\nshe drops her branches without warning,\nquicker than you''d think.\n\nThe cockatoos come screeching in\nand gossip in her hair;\nthe wind runs whispering through her leaves\nand leaves its secrets there.\n\nThey want to widen the old road.\nThe surveyors'' pegs are in.\nShe doesn''t flinch. She''s seen it all:\nthe drought, the flood, the din.\n\nBut I have hugged her rough, warm trunk\nand felt her heartbeat slow,\nand I will stand beside her, too,\nthe day they say she''ll go."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_easy', 1, 'From which point of view is ''Ms Kovac'' told?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"first person"},{"id":"b","text":"second person"},{"id":"c","text":"third person"},{"id":"d","text":"no point of view"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Max tells his own story using ''I'', ''me'' and ''my'', so it is first person.', NULL, '{"title":"Ms Kovac","text":"I knew Ms Kovac was going to be trouble the moment she walked into 7B. She wore a grey jacket buttoned to the top, she didn''t smile, and she wrote her name on the whiteboard in square capital letters, as if she were carving it into stone.\n\n''Phones in the tub, please,'' she said. ''You''ll get them back at the bell.''\n\nEveryone groaned. I groaned the loudest, which turned out to be a mistake, because she looked straight at me for a long, slow second.\n\nAll through maths I kept my head down and doodled in the margin of my book: a dragon, then a dragon eating a calculator. When the bell went for lunch, she said, ''Max, could you stay back a moment?''\n\nHere it comes, I thought. Detention on day one. A note home.\n\nWhen the room was empty, Ms Kovac picked up my book and studied the dragon for so long that I started counting the ceiling tiles.\n\n''Who taught you to shade like this?'' she asked.\n\n''Nobody,'' I mumbled.\n\nShe nodded slowly. Then she took a flyer from her folder and slid it across the desk. ART CLUB, it said. TUESDAYS, ROOM 14. TEACHER: MS KOVAC.\n\n''We could use someone who draws dragons,'' she said. ''Bring your book. And Max? Do the maths first next time.''\n\nI walked out into the sunshine still holding the flyer. Maybe she wasn''t trouble after all. Or maybe she was just a different kind of trouble."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_easy', 1, '''Canberra is the best city in Australia'' is a fact.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, '''Best'' is a judgement that people can disagree with, so it is an opinion. ''Canberra is the capital of Australia'' would be a fact.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_easy', 1, 'A story told by a narrator outside the story, using ''he'', ''she'' and ''they'', is written in ___ person.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["third","3rd","the third"]'::jsonb, 'When the narrator isn''t a character and refers to everyone as he, she or they, the story is in third person.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence from the article is a fact?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Hillview Council has voted 6–3 to build a skate park in Rosella Reserve."},{"id":"b","text":"This is the worst decision the council has made in years."},{"id":"c","text":"Rosella Reserve is the most beautiful park in the district."},{"id":"d","text":"Hillview deserves better."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A council vote can be checked in the meeting records, so it is a fact. The others are the writer''s judgements.', NULL, '{"title":"Skate Park Plan a Disaster for Hillview","text":"Hillview Council has voted 6–3 to build a $450 000 skate park in Rosella Reserve, the quiet green space beside the community hall. The park is due to open in March next year.\n\nThis is, quite simply, the worst decision the council has made in years. Rosella Reserve is the most beautiful park in the district, and it will be ruined by concrete ramps and noisy teenagers. Residents who have enjoyed peaceful picnics there for decades will be driven away.\n\nThe council claims the skate park will ''give young people somewhere safe to go''. But everyone knows skate parks attract graffiti and rubbish. The money would be far better spent fixing the potholes on Wattle Road. Young people already have the school oval and the youth centre. Why should the rest of the community lose its favourite park for a fad that will be forgotten in five years?\n\nNot one resident I spoke to supports the plan. Mrs Joan Pettigrew, who has lived beside the reserve for forty years, said she was ''heartbroken''.\n\nHillview deserves better. Come to the community meeting at the hall on Thursday at 7 pm and tell the council what you really think.\n\n— Written by the Friends of Rosella Reserve"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'easy', 1, '''You hear footsteps behind you. You turn around slowly.'' Is this written in first, second or third person?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["second","second person","2nd","2nd person"]'::jsonb, 'The writer speaks directly to the reader as ''you'', so it is second person. It puts you inside the story.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'easy', 1, 'What kind of text is Edie''s writing, and who is it mainly written for?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a diary, written mainly for herself"},{"id":"b","text":"a newspaper report, written for all of Sydney"},{"id":"c","text":"a speech, written for her class"},{"id":"d","text":"an advertisement, written for tourists"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It is dated and records her own day and feelings in the first person. Diaries are private records, so she is mainly writing for herself.', NULL, '{"title":"From the Diary of Edie Walsh, aged 12, Balmain, 19 March 1932","text":"Today they opened the Bridge at last. Dad woke us before sunrise and we walked all the way from Balmain to find a good spot, because we haven''t the money for the tram. Mum packed bread and dripping and a bottle of cold tea.\n\nThere were more people than I have ever seen in my whole life. Some had climbed onto rooftops and some were up in the trees. Dad lifted Tommy onto his shoulders so he could see.\n\nJust before the ribbon was cut, a man on a horse rode up and slashed it with a sword! He wore an army uniform. People gasped and the police pulled him off his horse. Dad said he was making a point about who should have opened the Bridge, but I didn''t really understand. They tied the ribbon back together and cut it properly after that.\n\nDad was quiet on the walk home. He worked on the Bridge for nearly a year before they put him off, and he hasn''t found steady work since. He looked up at the arch for a long time and said, ''I put some of my sweat into that.'' Mum squeezed his arm.\n\nI think the Bridge is the most beautiful thing in Sydney. Tommy says it looks like a coat-hanger. Tommy is six and doesn''t know anything."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'medium', 1, 'Why does Max decide that Ms Kovac is ''trouble'' at the start?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"because of how she looks and acts: the buttoned jacket, no smile and the square letters"},{"id":"b","text":"because she gives him a detention"},{"id":"c","text":"because she tears up his drawing"},{"id":"d","text":"because other students warned him about her"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'He judges her in the first paragraph from her appearance and manner, before she has done anything to him.', NULL, '{"title":"Ms Kovac","text":"I knew Ms Kovac was going to be trouble the moment she walked into 7B. She wore a grey jacket buttoned to the top, she didn''t smile, and she wrote her name on the whiteboard in square capital letters, as if she were carving it into stone.\n\n''Phones in the tub, please,'' she said. ''You''ll get them back at the bell.''\n\nEveryone groaned. I groaned the loudest, which turned out to be a mistake, because she looked straight at me for a long, slow second.\n\nAll through maths I kept my head down and doodled in the margin of my book: a dragon, then a dragon eating a calculator. When the bell went for lunch, she said, ''Max, could you stay back a moment?''\n\nHere it comes, I thought. Detention on day one. A note home.\n\nWhen the room was empty, Ms Kovac picked up my book and studied the dragon for so long that I started counting the ceiling tiles.\n\n''Who taught you to shade like this?'' she asked.\n\n''Nobody,'' I mumbled.\n\nShe nodded slowly. Then she took a flyer from her folder and slid it across the desk. ART CLUB, it said. TUESDAYS, ROOM 14. TEACHER: MS KOVAC.\n\n''We could use someone who draws dragons,'' she said. ''Bring your book. And Max? Do the maths first next time.''\n\nI walked out into the sunshine still holding the flyer. Maybe she wasn''t trouble after all. Or maybe she was just a different kind of trouble."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these sentences from the article are opinions? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"This is, quite simply, the worst decision the council has made in years."},{"id":"b","text":"Rosella Reserve is the most beautiful park in the district."},{"id":"c","text":"The park is due to open in March next year."},{"id":"d","text":"Mrs Joan Pettigrew has lived beside the reserve for forty years."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '''Worst'' and ''most beautiful'' are judgements people could disagree with. An opening date and how long someone has lived somewhere can be checked, so they are facts.', NULL, '{"title":"Skate Park Plan a Disaster for Hillview","text":"Hillview Council has voted 6–3 to build a $450 000 skate park in Rosella Reserve, the quiet green space beside the community hall. The park is due to open in March next year.\n\nThis is, quite simply, the worst decision the council has made in years. Rosella Reserve is the most beautiful park in the district, and it will be ruined by concrete ramps and noisy teenagers. Residents who have enjoyed peaceful picnics there for decades will be driven away.\n\nThe council claims the skate park will ''give young people somewhere safe to go''. But everyone knows skate parks attract graffiti and rubbish. The money would be far better spent fixing the potholes on Wattle Road. Young people already have the school oval and the youth centre. Why should the rest of the community lose its favourite park for a fad that will be forgotten in five years?\n\nNot one resident I spoke to supports the plan. Mrs Joan Pettigrew, who has lived beside the reserve for forty years, said she was ''heartbroken''.\n\nHillview deserves better. Come to the community meeting at the hall on Thursday at 7 pm and tell the council what you really think.\n\n— Written by the Friends of Rosella Reserve"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'medium', 1, 'Which details show that Edie''s family was short of money during the Great Depression? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"They walked because they didn''t have the money for the tram."},{"id":"b","text":"Mum packed bread and dripping."},{"id":"c","text":"Dad hasn''t found steady work since he was put off."},{"id":"d","text":"Dad lifted Tommy onto his shoulders."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Walking to save the fare, a cheap meal of bread and dripping, and Dad losing his job all show hard times. Lifting Tommy up shows care, not money troubles.', NULL, '{"title":"From the Diary of Edie Walsh, aged 12, Balmain, 19 March 1932","text":"Today they opened the Bridge at last. Dad woke us before sunrise and we walked all the way from Balmain to find a good spot, because we haven''t the money for the tram. Mum packed bread and dripping and a bottle of cold tea.\n\nThere were more people than I have ever seen in my whole life. Some had climbed onto rooftops and some were up in the trees. Dad lifted Tommy onto his shoulders so he could see.\n\nJust before the ribbon was cut, a man on a horse rode up and slashed it with a sword! He wore an army uniform. People gasped and the police pulled him off his horse. Dad said he was making a point about who should have opened the Bridge, but I didn''t really understand. They tied the ribbon back together and cut it properly after that.\n\nDad was quiet on the walk home. He worked on the Bridge for nearly a year before they put him off, and he hasn''t found steady work since. He looked up at the arch for a long time and said, ''I put some of my sweat into that.'' Mum squeezed his arm.\n\nI think the Bridge is the most beautiful thing in Sydney. Tommy says it looks like a coat-hanger. Tommy is six and doesn''t know anything."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'hard', 1, 'Whose point of view is missing from the article?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the young people who would use the skate park"},{"id":"b","text":"Mrs Joan Pettigrew"},{"id":"c","text":"residents who have picnics in the reserve"},{"id":"d","text":"the Friends of Rosella Reserve"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The article quotes and speaks for residents who oppose the plan, but never asks a single skater or teenager. Leaving out one side is a sign of bias.', NULL, '{"title":"Skate Park Plan a Disaster for Hillview","text":"Hillview Council has voted 6–3 to build a $450 000 skate park in Rosella Reserve, the quiet green space beside the community hall. The park is due to open in March next year.\n\nThis is, quite simply, the worst decision the council has made in years. Rosella Reserve is the most beautiful park in the district, and it will be ruined by concrete ramps and noisy teenagers. Residents who have enjoyed peaceful picnics there for decades will be driven away.\n\nThe council claims the skate park will ''give young people somewhere safe to go''. But everyone knows skate parks attract graffiti and rubbish. The money would be far better spent fixing the potholes on Wattle Road. Young people already have the school oval and the youth centre. Why should the rest of the community lose its favourite park for a fad that will be forgotten in five years?\n\nNot one resident I spoke to supports the plan. Mrs Joan Pettigrew, who has lived beside the reserve for forty years, said she was ''heartbroken''.\n\nHillview deserves better. Come to the community meeting at the hall on Thursday at 7 pm and tell the council what you really think.\n\n— Written by the Friends of Rosella Reserve"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'hard', 1, 'Max is sure he is getting a detention, but he is wrong. What does this show about first-person narrators?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"We only see events through the narrator''s fears and guesses, which can be wrong."},{"id":"b","text":"First-person narrators always know what other characters are thinking."},{"id":"c","text":"First-person narrators never make mistakes."},{"id":"d","text":"The story must be told by the teacher."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A first-person narrator can only tell us what they see and think. Max''s worry colours his view, so the reader is surprised along with him.', NULL, '{"title":"Ms Kovac","text":"I knew Ms Kovac was going to be trouble the moment she walked into 7B. She wore a grey jacket buttoned to the top, she didn''t smile, and she wrote her name on the whiteboard in square capital letters, as if she were carving it into stone.\n\n''Phones in the tub, please,'' she said. ''You''ll get them back at the bell.''\n\nEveryone groaned. I groaned the loudest, which turned out to be a mistake, because she looked straight at me for a long, slow second.\n\nAll through maths I kept my head down and doodled in the margin of my book: a dragon, then a dragon eating a calculator. When the bell went for lunch, she said, ''Max, could you stay back a moment?''\n\nHere it comes, I thought. Detention on day one. A note home.\n\nWhen the room was empty, Ms Kovac picked up my book and studied the dragon for so long that I started counting the ceiling tiles.\n\n''Who taught you to shade like this?'' she asked.\n\n''Nobody,'' I mumbled.\n\nShe nodded slowly. Then she took a flyer from her folder and slid it across the desk. ART CLUB, it said. TUESDAYS, ROOM 14. TEACHER: MS KOVAC.\n\n''We could use someone who draws dragons,'' she said. ''Bring your book. And Max? Do the maths first next time.''\n\nI walked out into the sunshine still holding the flyer. Maybe she wasn''t trouble after all. Or maybe she was just a different kind of trouble."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'hard', 1, 'Put these steps for checking a text for bias in a sensible order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Find out who wrote the text."},{"id":"b","text":"Work out what the writer wants readers to think or do."},{"id":"c","text":"Look for views or facts that have been left out."},{"id":"d","text":"Decide how far you can trust the text."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'First know the writer, then their purpose. Knowing their purpose helps you spot what is missing, and only then can you judge how reliable the text is.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_hard', 1, 'The article was written by the Friends of Rosella Reserve. How does this help explain its bias?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The group exists to protect the reserve, so it only presents arguments against the skate park."},{"id":"b","text":"The group is part of the council, so it supports the council''s decision."},{"id":"c","text":"The group is made up of skaters, so it wants the park built."},{"id":"d","text":"The group has no interest in the reserve, so it is completely neutral."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A writer''s context shapes a text. A group set up to protect the reserve wants to stop the skate park, so it uses strong opinions and leaves out the other side.', NULL, '{"title":"Skate Park Plan a Disaster for Hillview","text":"Hillview Council has voted 6–3 to build a $450 000 skate park in Rosella Reserve, the quiet green space beside the community hall. The park is due to open in March next year.\n\nThis is, quite simply, the worst decision the council has made in years. Rosella Reserve is the most beautiful park in the district, and it will be ruined by concrete ramps and noisy teenagers. Residents who have enjoyed peaceful picnics there for decades will be driven away.\n\nThe council claims the skate park will ''give young people somewhere safe to go''. But everyone knows skate parks attract graffiti and rubbish. The money would be far better spent fixing the potholes on Wattle Road. Young people already have the school oval and the youth centre. Why should the rest of the community lose its favourite park for a fad that will be forgotten in five years?\n\nNot one resident I spoke to supports the plan. Mrs Joan Pettigrew, who has lived beside the reserve for forty years, said she was ''heartbroken''.\n\nHillview deserves better. Come to the community meeting at the hall on Thursday at 7 pm and tell the council what you really think.\n\n— Written by the Friends of Rosella Reserve"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_hard', 1, 'How does Edie''s family situation shape the way she writes about the Bridge?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Because her dad helped build it and then lost his job, the Bridge stands for both pride and hard times."},{"id":"b","text":"Because her family is rich, she sees the Bridge as just another building."},{"id":"c","text":"Because she lives far from Sydney, she has never seen the Bridge."},{"id":"d","text":"Because she dislikes her brother, she writes only about him."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Dad says ''I put some of my sweat into that'', yet he was put off and can''t find work. Edie''s pride in the ''beautiful'' Bridge is mixed with the family''s struggle.', NULL, '{"title":"From the Diary of Edie Walsh, aged 12, Balmain, 19 March 1932","text":"Today they opened the Bridge at last. Dad woke us before sunrise and we walked all the way from Balmain to find a good spot, because we haven''t the money for the tram. Mum packed bread and dripping and a bottle of cold tea.\n\nThere were more people than I have ever seen in my whole life. Some had climbed onto rooftops and some were up in the trees. Dad lifted Tommy onto his shoulders so he could see.\n\nJust before the ribbon was cut, a man on a horse rode up and slashed it with a sword! He wore an army uniform. People gasped and the police pulled him off his horse. Dad said he was making a point about who should have opened the Bridge, but I didn''t really understand. They tied the ribbon back together and cut it properly after that.\n\nDad was quiet on the walk home. He worked on the Bridge for nearly a year before they put him off, and he hasn''t found steady work since. He looked up at the arch for a long time and said, ''I put some of my sweat into that.'' Mum squeezed his arm.\n\nI think the Bridge is the most beautiful thing in Sydney. Tommy says it looks like a coat-hanger. Tommy is six and doesn''t know anything."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_hard', 1, 'If the story were retold in third person from Ms Kovac''s point of view, what would the reader most likely learn that Max cannot tell us?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"what she was thinking when she looked at Max and noticed his drawing"},{"id":"b","text":"what Max drew in his maths book"},{"id":"c","text":"which room the art club meets in"},{"id":"d","text":"what time the lunch bell rings"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Max can only guess at her thoughts. A narrator inside her head could reveal why she stared at him and what she saw in his dragon. The other details Max already tells us.', NULL, '{"title":"Ms Kovac","text":"I knew Ms Kovac was going to be trouble the moment she walked into 7B. She wore a grey jacket buttoned to the top, she didn''t smile, and she wrote her name on the whiteboard in square capital letters, as if she were carving it into stone.\n\n''Phones in the tub, please,'' she said. ''You''ll get them back at the bell.''\n\nEveryone groaned. I groaned the loudest, which turned out to be a mistake, because she looked straight at me for a long, slow second.\n\nAll through maths I kept my head down and doodled in the margin of my book: a dragon, then a dragon eating a calculator. When the bell went for lunch, she said, ''Max, could you stay back a moment?''\n\nHere it comes, I thought. Detention on day one. A note home.\n\nWhen the room was empty, Ms Kovac picked up my book and studied the dragon for so long that I started counting the ceiling tiles.\n\n''Who taught you to shade like this?'' she asked.\n\n''Nobody,'' I mumbled.\n\nShe nodded slowly. Then she took a flyer from her folder and slid it across the desk. ART CLUB, it said. TUESDAYS, ROOM 14. TEACHER: MS KOVAC.\n\n''We could use someone who draws dragons,'' she said. ''Bring your book. And Max? Do the maths first next time.''\n\nI walked out into the sunshine still holding the flyer. Maybe she wasn''t trouble after all. Or maybe she was just a different kind of trouble."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Is ''The Final Quarter'' written in first, second or third person?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["third","third person","3rd","3rd person"]'::jsonb, 'The narrator is not a character and calls the girls ''she'' and ''her'', so the story is in third person.', NULL, '{"title":"The Final Quarter","text":"With two minutes left in the under-13 netball grand final, the scores were level at 24 all. Ruby stood at goal shooter, her heart thudding so hard she was sure the umpire could hear it. She had missed her last two shots. If the ball came to her again, she thought, she would miss again. She almost hoped it wouldn''t.\n\nAt centre, her older sister Grace was thinking something quite different. Grace had watched Ruby practise in the backyard every night for a month, shooting until it was too dark to see the ring. She knew her sister could do this. She just wasn''t sure Ruby knew it.\n\nThe whistle blew. Grace took the centre pass, spun past her defender and fired the ball down the court. It zipped through the hands of the wing attack and landed in Ruby''s.\n\nRuby froze. The crowd went quiet. Then, from somewhere up the court, she heard a single voice: ''Backyard, Rubes! Just like the backyard!'' It was Grace, who had somehow found the breath to shout as she ran.\n\nRuby bent her knees, lifted the ball and let it go.\n\nSwish.\n\nLater, on the drive home, Ruby told everyone it had been the easiest shot of her life. Grace, sitting beside her with the trophy on her lap, said nothing at all. She just smiled out of the window."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_easy', 1, 'The pronouns ''I'', ''me'' and ''we'' show that a text is written in ___ person.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["first","1st","the first"]'::jsonb, 'When the narrator talks about themselves using I, me, my or we, the text is in first person.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the main purpose of the ZapCharge article?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to persuade readers to buy ZapCharge"},{"id":"b","text":"to explain the rules of netball"},{"id":"c","text":"to tell a story about a football coach"},{"id":"d","text":"to warn readers about sugar"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It praises the drink, tells you where to buy it and is sponsored by the company that makes it. Its purpose is to sell.', NULL, '{"title":"Why Every Young Athlete Needs ZapCharge","text":"Saturday sport is tough. Early starts, cold mornings and long matches can leave even the fittest players feeling flat by the final whistle. Whether you''re running the wing in rugby league or defending the circle in netball, your body works hard, and it needs the right fuel.\n\nThat''s where ZapCharge comes in. Packed with electrolytes and a burst of fruity flavour, ZapCharge helps young athletes stay at their best from the first whistle to the last. Water is fine, of course, but why settle for fine when you could be fantastic?\n\nTop coaches know the difference. ''My players perform better when they''re properly hydrated,'' says junior football coach Dave Morris.\n\nBest of all, ZapCharge comes in five exciting flavours, including Tropical Thunder and Berry Blast, and every bottle contains 30 grams of energy-boosting sugar to keep you going.\n\nPlayers across NSW are already making the switch. ''I drink one before every game and one at half-time,'' says Jayden, 12, a winger from Parramatta. ''I feel unstoppable.'' Mums and dads love it too, because the bright, resealable bottle fits neatly into any kit bag.\n\nDon''t let your team down. Grab a ZapCharge before your next game, available at supermarkets and canteens everywhere.\n\nThis article is sponsored by ZapCharge Drinks."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'easy', 1, 'Which account is more objective, sticking to facts without personal feelings?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Account 1, the surf club incident report"},{"id":"b","text":"Account 2, Ollie''s post"},{"id":"c","text":"Both are equally objective."},{"id":"d","text":"Neither gives any facts."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The report uses exact times and plain facts and never says how anyone felt. Ollie''s post is full of feelings like ''so scared'' and ''so embarrassing''.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Accounts of the Same Rescue","text":"Account 1: Surf Club Incident Report\n\nDate: Sunday 12 January. Location: North Beach, southern end. At approximately 2:15 pm, a male swimmer aged about 14 was observed drifting south of the flags in a rip current. Lifeguard J. Tran entered the water with a rescue board and reached the swimmer at 2:17 pm. The swimmer was conscious and was returned to shore at 2:22 pm. First aid was provided for mild exhaustion. No further treatment was required. Recommendation: increase rip-current signage at the southern end of the beach.\n\nAccount 2: Posted by Ollie_K\n\nWorst. Day. Ever. I honestly thought I was going to die out there. One minute I was bodysurfing with my cousins, the next the beach was getting smaller and smaller and I couldn''t swim back no matter how hard I tried. My arms felt like concrete. I was so scared I forgot everything they teach you at school about rips.\n\nThen this lifeguard just appeared out of nowhere on a board, like a superhero. She was so calm. She said, ''Grab on, mate, I''ve got you,'' and paddled me back like it was nothing. Everyone on the beach was staring. So embarrassing!\n\nMassive thanks to Jess at North Beach. Seriously, swim between the flags, people. And if you get caught, don''t panic: float and put your hand up."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence is an opinion?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Netball is the most exciting sport to watch."},{"id":"b","text":"A netball team has seven players on the court."},{"id":"c","text":"The Sydney Opera House opened in 1973."},{"id":"d","text":"Cricket is usually played in summer in Australia."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Most exciting'' is a personal judgement. The other sentences can be checked, so they are facts.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentences show that Account 2 is written from a personal point of view? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"I honestly thought I was going to die out there."},{"id":"b","text":"My arms felt like concrete."},{"id":"c","text":"At approximately 2:15 pm, a male swimmer aged about 14 was observed."},{"id":"d","text":"First aid was provided for mild exhaustion."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Ollie uses ''I'' and ''my'' and describes his own fear and tiredness. The other two sentences come from the report, which is impersonal.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Accounts of the Same Rescue","text":"Account 1: Surf Club Incident Report\n\nDate: Sunday 12 January. Location: North Beach, southern end. At approximately 2:15 pm, a male swimmer aged about 14 was observed drifting south of the flags in a rip current. Lifeguard J. Tran entered the water with a rescue board and reached the swimmer at 2:17 pm. The swimmer was conscious and was returned to shore at 2:22 pm. First aid was provided for mild exhaustion. No further treatment was required. Recommendation: increase rip-current signage at the southern end of the beach.\n\nAccount 2: Posted by Ollie_K\n\nWorst. Day. Ever. I honestly thought I was going to die out there. One minute I was bodysurfing with my cousins, the next the beach was getting smaller and smaller and I couldn''t swim back no matter how hard I tried. My arms felt like concrete. I was so scared I forgot everything they teach you at school about rips.\n\nThen this lifeguard just appeared out of nowhere on a board, like a superhero. She was so calm. She said, ''Grab on, mate, I''ve got you,'' and paddled me back like it was nothing. Everyone on the beach was staring. So embarrassing!\n\nMassive thanks to Jess at North Beach. Seriously, swim between the flags, people. And if you get caught, don''t panic: float and put your hand up."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'medium', 1, 'The narrator of ''The Final Quarter'' tells us what both Ruby and Grace are thinking.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'We learn Ruby fears she will miss and Grace knows her sister can do it. A narrator who can see into several characters'' minds is called omniscient.', NULL, '{"title":"The Final Quarter","text":"With two minutes left in the under-13 netball grand final, the scores were level at 24 all. Ruby stood at goal shooter, her heart thudding so hard she was sure the umpire could hear it. She had missed her last two shots. If the ball came to her again, she thought, she would miss again. She almost hoped it wouldn''t.\n\nAt centre, her older sister Grace was thinking something quite different. Grace had watched Ruby practise in the backyard every night for a month, shooting until it was too dark to see the ring. She knew her sister could do this. She just wasn''t sure Ruby knew it.\n\nThe whistle blew. Grace took the centre pass, spun past her defender and fired the ball down the court. It zipped through the hands of the wing attack and landed in Ruby''s.\n\nRuby froze. The crowd went quiet. Then, from somewhere up the court, she heard a single voice: ''Backyard, Rubes! Just like the backyard!'' It was Grace, who had somehow found the breath to shout as she ran.\n\nRuby bent her knees, lifted the ball and let it go.\n\nSwish.\n\nLater, on the drive home, Ruby told everyone it had been the easiest shot of her life. Grace, sitting beside her with the trophy on her lap, said nothing at all. She just smiled out of the window."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'medium', 1, 'Coach Dave Morris says, ''My players perform better when they''re properly hydrated.'' Why is this weak evidence that ZapCharge works?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He talks about being hydrated, which water can do too. He never mentions ZapCharge."},{"id":"b","text":"He is not a real person."},{"id":"c","text":"He coaches football, not netball, so he knows nothing about sport."},{"id":"d","text":"It is a fact, so it can''t be used as evidence."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The quote only says hydration helps. The article places it next to ZapCharge so readers assume he means the drink, but he doesn''t say so.', NULL, '{"title":"Why Every Young Athlete Needs ZapCharge","text":"Saturday sport is tough. Early starts, cold mornings and long matches can leave even the fittest players feeling flat by the final whistle. Whether you''re running the wing in rugby league or defending the circle in netball, your body works hard, and it needs the right fuel.\n\nThat''s where ZapCharge comes in. Packed with electrolytes and a burst of fruity flavour, ZapCharge helps young athletes stay at their best from the first whistle to the last. Water is fine, of course, but why settle for fine when you could be fantastic?\n\nTop coaches know the difference. ''My players perform better when they''re properly hydrated,'' says junior football coach Dave Morris.\n\nBest of all, ZapCharge comes in five exciting flavours, including Tropical Thunder and Berry Blast, and every bottle contains 30 grams of energy-boosting sugar to keep you going.\n\nPlayers across NSW are already making the switch. ''I drink one before every game and one at half-time,'' says Jayden, 12, a winger from Parramatta. ''I feel unstoppable.'' Mums and dads love it too, because the bright, resealable bottle fits neatly into any kit bag.\n\nDon''t let your team down. Grab a ZapCharge before your next game, available at supermarkets and canteens everywhere.\n\nThis article is sponsored by ZapCharge Drinks."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'medium', 1, 'Put these headlines in order from the most neutral to the most biased.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Council approves new bus route"},{"id":"b","text":"Council finally approves long-awaited bus route"},{"id":"c","text":"Clueless council''s bus route set to be a disaster"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The first just states what happened. ''Finally'' and ''long-awaited'' add a feeling of approval. ''Clueless'' and ''disaster'' are strong, one-sided opinions.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'hard', 1, 'Why does the incident report give exact times and avoid words like ''scared''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It is an official record, so it must be accurate and objective for the surf club."},{"id":"b","text":"The lifeguard did not care about the swimmer."},{"id":"c","text":"Reports are meant to entertain readers."},{"id":"d","text":"The writer forgot how the swimmer felt."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A report''s purpose is to record what happened so the club can learn from it, such as adding more signs. Feelings would not help that purpose.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Accounts of the Same Rescue","text":"Account 1: Surf Club Incident Report\n\nDate: Sunday 12 January. Location: North Beach, southern end. At approximately 2:15 pm, a male swimmer aged about 14 was observed drifting south of the flags in a rip current. Lifeguard J. Tran entered the water with a rescue board and reached the swimmer at 2:17 pm. The swimmer was conscious and was returned to shore at 2:22 pm. First aid was provided for mild exhaustion. No further treatment was required. Recommendation: increase rip-current signage at the southern end of the beach.\n\nAccount 2: Posted by Ollie_K\n\nWorst. Day. Ever. I honestly thought I was going to die out there. One minute I was bodysurfing with my cousins, the next the beach was getting smaller and smaller and I couldn''t swim back no matter how hard I tried. My arms felt like concrete. I was so scared I forgot everything they teach you at school about rips.\n\nThen this lifeguard just appeared out of nowhere on a board, like a superhero. She was so calm. She said, ''Grab on, mate, I''ve got you,'' and paddled me back like it was nothing. Everyone on the beach was staring. So embarrassing!\n\nMassive thanks to Jess at North Beach. Seriously, swim between the flags, people. And if you get caught, don''t panic: float and put your hand up."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'hard', 1, '''every bottle contains 30 grams of energy-boosting sugar'' — how does this word choice show bias?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It describes a large amount of sugar as a good thing and hides the downside."},{"id":"b","text":"It gives an exact number, so it can''t be biased."},{"id":"c","text":"It warns readers to drink less."},{"id":"d","text":"It compares ZapCharge fairly with water."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Thirty grams is a lot of sugar for one drink. Calling it ''energy-boosting'' gives it a positive connotation, so readers think it is healthy.', NULL, '{"title":"Why Every Young Athlete Needs ZapCharge","text":"Saturday sport is tough. Early starts, cold mornings and long matches can leave even the fittest players feeling flat by the final whistle. Whether you''re running the wing in rugby league or defending the circle in netball, your body works hard, and it needs the right fuel.\n\nThat''s where ZapCharge comes in. Packed with electrolytes and a burst of fruity flavour, ZapCharge helps young athletes stay at their best from the first whistle to the last. Water is fine, of course, but why settle for fine when you could be fantastic?\n\nTop coaches know the difference. ''My players perform better when they''re properly hydrated,'' says junior football coach Dave Morris.\n\nBest of all, ZapCharge comes in five exciting flavours, including Tropical Thunder and Berry Blast, and every bottle contains 30 grams of energy-boosting sugar to keep you going.\n\nPlayers across NSW are already making the switch. ''I drink one before every game and one at half-time,'' says Jayden, 12, a winger from Parramatta. ''I feel unstoppable.'' Mums and dads love it too, because the bright, resealable bottle fits neatly into any kit bag.\n\nDon''t let your team down. Grab a ZapCharge before your next game, available at supermarkets and canteens everywhere.\n\nThis article is sponsored by ZapCharge Drinks."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'hard', 1, 'Why does Ruby ''almost hope'' the ball won''t come to her?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She is afraid she will miss again and let the team down."},{"id":"b","text":"She is tired and wants a rest."},{"id":"c","text":"She wants Grace to shoot instead."},{"id":"d","text":"She thinks the umpire is unfair."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The narrator shows her thoughts: she had missed her last two shots and thought she ''would miss again''. Her fear makes her want to avoid the ball.', NULL, '{"title":"The Final Quarter","text":"With two minutes left in the under-13 netball grand final, the scores were level at 24 all. Ruby stood at goal shooter, her heart thudding so hard she was sure the umpire could hear it. She had missed her last two shots. If the ball came to her again, she thought, she would miss again. She almost hoped it wouldn''t.\n\nAt centre, her older sister Grace was thinking something quite different. Grace had watched Ruby practise in the backyard every night for a month, shooting until it was too dark to see the ring. She knew her sister could do this. She just wasn''t sure Ruby knew it.\n\nThe whistle blew. Grace took the centre pass, spun past her defender and fired the ball down the court. It zipped through the hands of the wing attack and landed in Ruby''s.\n\nRuby froze. The crowd went quiet. Then, from somewhere up the court, she heard a single voice: ''Backyard, Rubes! Just like the backyard!'' It was Grace, who had somehow found the breath to shout as she ran.\n\nRuby bent her knees, lifted the ball and let it go.\n\nSwish.\n\nLater, on the drive home, Ruby told everyone it had been the easiest shot of her life. Grace, sitting beside her with the trophy on her lap, said nothing at all. She just smiled out of the window."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Ruby later says it was ''the easiest shot of her life''. Why does Grace say nothing and just smile?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"She knows how scared Ruby really was, but lets her enjoy the moment."},{"id":"b","text":"She is angry that Ruby took the winning shot."},{"id":"c","text":"She didn''t see the shot."},{"id":"d","text":"She agrees that the shot was easy."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Because the narrator showed us both sisters'' thoughts, we know Ruby froze with fear. Grace knows it too but kindly lets Ruby tell her version.', NULL, '{"title":"The Final Quarter","text":"With two minutes left in the under-13 netball grand final, the scores were level at 24 all. Ruby stood at goal shooter, her heart thudding so hard she was sure the umpire could hear it. She had missed her last two shots. If the ball came to her again, she thought, she would miss again. She almost hoped it wouldn''t.\n\nAt centre, her older sister Grace was thinking something quite different. Grace had watched Ruby practise in the backyard every night for a month, shooting until it was too dark to see the ring. She knew her sister could do this. She just wasn''t sure Ruby knew it.\n\nThe whistle blew. Grace took the centre pass, spun past her defender and fired the ball down the court. It zipped through the hands of the wing attack and landed in Ruby''s.\n\nRuby froze. The crowd went quiet. Then, from somewhere up the court, she heard a single voice: ''Backyard, Rubes! Just like the backyard!'' It was Grace, who had somehow found the breath to shout as she ran.\n\nRuby bent her knees, lifted the ball and let it go.\n\nSwish.\n\nLater, on the drive home, Ruby told everyone it had been the easiest shot of her life. Grace, sitting beside her with the trophy on her lap, said nothing at all. She just smiled out of the window."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_hard', 1, 'The two accounts agree on what happened, yet they read very differently. What best explains this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"They have different purposes and audiences: an official record versus a personal post to friends."},{"id":"b","text":"One of the accounts is about a different rescue."},{"id":"c","text":"The lifeguard and Ollie disagree about the facts."},{"id":"d","text":"Ollie was not really in the water."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The report records facts for the surf club. Ollie writes to his followers to share his feelings, thank Jess and warn others. Purpose and audience shape the language.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Accounts of the Same Rescue","text":"Account 1: Surf Club Incident Report\n\nDate: Sunday 12 January. Location: North Beach, southern end. At approximately 2:15 pm, a male swimmer aged about 14 was observed drifting south of the flags in a rip current. Lifeguard J. Tran entered the water with a rescue board and reached the swimmer at 2:17 pm. The swimmer was conscious and was returned to shore at 2:22 pm. First aid was provided for mild exhaustion. No further treatment was required. Recommendation: increase rip-current signage at the southern end of the beach.\n\nAccount 2: Posted by Ollie_K\n\nWorst. Day. Ever. I honestly thought I was going to die out there. One minute I was bodysurfing with my cousins, the next the beach was getting smaller and smaller and I couldn''t swim back no matter how hard I tried. My arms felt like concrete. I was so scared I forgot everything they teach you at school about rips.\n\nThen this lifeguard just appeared out of nowhere on a board, like a superhero. She was so calm. She said, ''Grab on, mate, I''ve got you,'' and paddled me back like it was nothing. Everyone on the beach was staring. So embarrassing!\n\nMassive thanks to Jess at North Beach. Seriously, swim between the flags, people. And if you get caught, don''t panic: float and put your hand up."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007110330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which information would a balanced article about sports drinks include that this one leaves out? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"that water is enough for most children playing weekend sport"},{"id":"b","text":"the health risks of drinking a lot of sugar"},{"id":"c","text":"the names of the drink''s flavours"},{"id":"d","text":"where you can buy the drink"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'The sponsored article skips anything that might stop you buying, such as water being enough or sugar being unhealthy. The flavours and shops are already included because they help sell it.', NULL, '{"title":"Why Every Young Athlete Needs ZapCharge","text":"Saturday sport is tough. Early starts, cold mornings and long matches can leave even the fittest players feeling flat by the final whistle. Whether you''re running the wing in rugby league or defending the circle in netball, your body works hard, and it needs the right fuel.\n\nThat''s where ZapCharge comes in. Packed with electrolytes and a burst of fruity flavour, ZapCharge helps young athletes stay at their best from the first whistle to the last. Water is fine, of course, but why settle for fine when you could be fantastic?\n\nTop coaches know the difference. ''My players perform better when they''re properly hydrated,'' says junior football coach Dave Morris.\n\nBest of all, ZapCharge comes in five exciting flavours, including Tropical Thunder and Berry Blast, and every bottle contains 30 grams of energy-boosting sugar to keep you going.\n\nPlayers across NSW are already making the switch. ''I drink one before every game and one at half-time,'' says Jayden, 12, a winger from Parramatta. ''I feel unstoppable.'' Mums and dads love it too, because the bright, resealable bottle fits neatly into any kit bag.\n\nDon''t let your team down. Grab a ZapCharge before your next game, available at supermarkets and canteens everywhere.\n\nThis article is sponsored by ZapCharge Drinks."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_easy', 1, 'In the sentence ''The hikers were famished after the long walk, so they ate everything in the hut'', what does ''famished'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"very hungry"},{"id":"b","text":"very tired"},{"id":"c","text":"very lost"},{"id":"d","text":"very cold"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The clue is that they ''ate everything in the hut''. People eat everything when they are very hungry, so famished means very hungry.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_easy', 1, '''Ancient'' and ''modern'' are antonyms.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Antonyms are words with opposite meanings. Ancient means very old and modern means new, so they are opposites.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_easy', 1, 'The prefix ''un-'' means not, so ''unlikely'' means ___ likely.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"not"'::jsonb, 'un- at the start of a word means ''not'': unlikely = not likely, unhappy = not happy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'easy', 1, 'The Greek root ''auto'' means self and ''graph'' means write. Which word means a signature that a famous person writes themselves?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"autograph"},{"id":"b","text":"photograph"},{"id":"c","text":"paragraph"},{"id":"d","text":"geography"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'auto (self) + graph (write) = autograph, something written by the person themselves.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'easy', 1, 'Which words are synonyms for ''brave''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"courageous"},{"id":"b","text":"fearless"},{"id":"c","text":"timid"},{"id":"d","text":"valiant"},{"id":"e","text":"cautious"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Synonyms have similar meanings. Courageous, fearless and valiant all mean brave. Timid means shy and easily scared, and cautious means careful.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'easy', 1, 'The suffix ''-less'' means without. Write the one word that means ''without fear''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"fearless"'::jsonb, 'fear + less (without) = fearless.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'medium', 1, 'In the first paragraph, what does ''dilapidated'' most likely mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"falling apart from age and neglect"},{"id":"b","text":"freshly painted"},{"id":"c","text":"very large"},{"id":"d","text":"hidden from view"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The clues come straight after the colon: the roof sagged, the windows were smashed and the door hung from one rusty hinge. The shed was falling apart.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Boatshed","text":"Every summer, Grandpa Theo promised to fix the boatshed at the bottom of the garden, and every summer he found a reason not to. By the time Mia turned twelve, the building was completely dilapidated: the roof sagged, two windows were smashed and the door hung from a single rusty hinge.\n\n\"This year will be different,\" Mia announced on the first day of the holidays. Grandpa raised an eyebrow but reluctantly agreed to help, muttering that his knees were far too old for ladders.\n\nTheir supplies were meagre. They had half a tin of green paint, a box of bent nails and a saw so blunt it barely scratched the timber. Mia spent her own pocket money at the hardware store in Kiama on new hinges and a sheet of corrugated iron.\n\nFor a week they worked from breakfast until the light faded. Grandpa scrutinised every board Mia nailed, running his thumb along the edges and pointing out each gap. At first his comments annoyed her, but slowly she realised he was teaching her, not criticising her.\n\nOn the final afternoon, they stood back to admire the shed. It was not perfect. The paint was patchy and the door still stuck in wet weather. But it was sturdy, and it was theirs.\n\nGrandpa wiped his hands on his overalls. \"Next summer,\" he said, \"we build the boat.\""}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'medium', 1, 'In the fourth paragraph, what does ''scrutinised'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"looked at very closely and carefully"},{"id":"b","text":"threw away"},{"id":"c","text":"quickly ignored"},{"id":"d","text":"praised loudly"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Grandpa ran his thumb along the edges and pointed out each gap, so he was examining every board closely.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Boatshed","text":"Every summer, Grandpa Theo promised to fix the boatshed at the bottom of the garden, and every summer he found a reason not to. By the time Mia turned twelve, the building was completely dilapidated: the roof sagged, two windows were smashed and the door hung from a single rusty hinge.\n\n\"This year will be different,\" Mia announced on the first day of the holidays. Grandpa raised an eyebrow but reluctantly agreed to help, muttering that his knees were far too old for ladders.\n\nTheir supplies were meagre. They had half a tin of green paint, a box of bent nails and a saw so blunt it barely scratched the timber. Mia spent her own pocket money at the hardware store in Kiama on new hinges and a sheet of corrugated iron.\n\nFor a week they worked from breakfast until the light faded. Grandpa scrutinised every board Mia nailed, running his thumb along the edges and pointing out each gap. At first his comments annoyed her, but slowly she realised he was teaching her, not criticising her.\n\nOn the final afternoon, they stood back to admire the shed. It was not perfect. The paint was patchy and the door still stuck in wet weather. But it was sturdy, and it was theirs.\n\nGrandpa wiped his hands on his overalls. \"Next summer,\" he said, \"we build the boat.\""}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence is written in a formal register?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"We request that all students arrive by 8:30 am."},{"id":"b","text":"Hey guys, get here by half eight, yeah?"},{"id":"c","text":"Don''t be late or you''ll be in big trouble!"},{"id":"d","text":"Rock up early, legends."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Formal language is polite and impersonal, with no slang. ''We request that...'' is formal. ''Hey guys'', ''yeah'', ''rock up'' and ''legends'' are informal.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'hard', 1, 'The Latin root ''port'' means carry. In which words does ''port'' have this meaning? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"transport"},{"id":"b","text":"portable"},{"id":"c","text":"import"},{"id":"d","text":"portrait"},{"id":"e","text":"export"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Transport (carry across), portable (able to be carried), import (carry in) and export (carry out) all use ''port'' meaning carry. A portrait is a picture of a person, so it does not mean carry.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'hard', 1, 'Put these words in order from coldest to hottest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"freezing"},{"id":"b","text":"lukewarm"},{"id":"c","text":"hot"},{"id":"d","text":"scorching"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'These words show shades of meaning. Freezing is extremely cold, lukewarm is slightly warm, hot is very warm and scorching is extremely hot.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'hard', 1, 'The Greek root ''chron'' means time. When events are listed in the order they happened in time, they are in ___ order.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["chronological","chronologic"]'::jsonb, 'chron (time) + logical = chronological, meaning arranged in time order, like a timeline.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which is the most formal way to rewrite: ''Heaps of kids reckon the canteen food is pretty ordinary.''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Many students believe the canteen food is of poor quality."},{"id":"b","text":"Lots of kids think the canteen food is bad."},{"id":"c","text":"Heaps of students reckon canteen food is ordinary."},{"id":"d","text":"Many students believe the canteen food is pretty ordinary."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A formal version replaces every piece of slang: ''heaps'' becomes ''many'', ''kids'' becomes ''students'', ''reckon'' becomes ''believe'' and ''pretty ordinary'' becomes ''of poor quality''. Only the first option changes all of them.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_hard', 1, '''Thrifty'' and ''stingy'' both describe someone who spends little money. Why would a writer praising their grandmother choose ''thrifty''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"''Thrifty'' has a positive connotation (careful and sensible), while ''stingy'' sounds mean."},{"id":"b","text":"''Thrifty'' is a longer word, so it sounds smarter."},{"id":"c","text":"''Stingy'' is only used for animals."},{"id":"d","text":"The two words mean exactly the same thing, so it does not matter."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Synonyms can carry different feelings, called connotations. ''Thrifty'' suggests someone wise with money; ''stingy'' suggests someone selfish who won''t share.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_hard', 1, 'The Latin root ''bene'' means good and ''mal'' means bad. ''Benevolent'' means kind and wishing good to others. Write its antonym, built with ''mal''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"malevolent"'::jsonb, 'Swap bene (good) for mal (bad): malevolent means wishing harm to others, the opposite of benevolent.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is a synonym for ''enormous''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"huge"},{"id":"b","text":"tiny"},{"id":"c","text":"quick"},{"id":"d","text":"noisy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Enormous means very big. Huge also means very big, so they are synonyms. Tiny is an antonym.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_easy', 1, 'The prefix ''pre-'' means before, so to preview a film is to view it ___ it is released.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"before"'::jsonb, 'pre- means before: preview = view before, prepare = get ready before, prehistoric = before written history.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_easy', 1, '''Hey, what''s up?'' is an example of informal language.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''Hey'' and ''what''s up'' are casual words we use with friends, so this is informal. A formal greeting would be ''Good morning, how are you?''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'easy', 1, 'The Latin root ''aqua'' means water. Which word names a glass tank where fish are kept?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"aquarium"},{"id":"b","text":"auditorium"},{"id":"c","text":"planetarium"},{"id":"d","text":"gymnasium"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'aqua (water) + -arium (a place for) = aquarium, a place for water and the animals that live in it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'easy', 1, 'Which pairs of words are antonyms? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"generous – selfish"},{"id":"b","text":"rapid – swift"},{"id":"c","text":"victory – defeat"},{"id":"d","text":"timid – shy"},{"id":"e","text":"expand – shrink"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Antonyms are opposites: generous/selfish, victory/defeat and expand/shrink. Rapid and swift both mean fast, and timid and shy are similar, so those pairs are synonyms.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'easy', 1, 'The suffix ''-ful'' means full of. Write the one word that means ''full of hope''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"hopeful"'::jsonb, 'hope + ful (full of) = hopeful. Notice the suffix has only one l.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'medium', 1, 'In the first paragraph, what does ''elusive'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"hard to find or catch"},{"id":"b","text":"dangerous to touch"},{"id":"c","text":"very common"},{"id":"d","text":"brightly coloured"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The next sentence is the clue: many people have never seen one, even after years of looking. So elusive means hard to find.', NULL, '{"title":"The Elusive Platypus","text":"Few Australian animals are as elusive as the platypus. Many people who live beside rivers in the Blue Mountains or on the New England Tablelands have never seen one, even after years of looking. The platypus is mostly nocturnal, feeding at dawn, at dusk and through the night, and it spends the hot part of the day resting in a burrow dug into the riverbank.\n\nWhen it dives, the platypus closes its eyes, ears and nostrils. Instead of sight, it relies on its bill, which is covered in thousands of tiny sensors that pick up the faint electric signals given off by shrimp, insect larvae and worms. In a single night, a platypus can eat food equal to about a fifth of its own body weight.\n\nSadly, platypuses are vulnerable to many threats. Droughts dry out the pools they depend on, dams change the flow of rivers, and bushfires strip away the plants that hold riverbanks together. Litter is another danger: a platypus can become tangled in a discarded hair tie or fishing line and be unable to free itself.\n\nThere is good news, though. Volunteers across NSW are replanting creek banks, removing rubbish and reporting sightings to scientists. After heavy rain helps to replenish dry rivers, some groups have even been able to return rescued platypuses to the wild.\n\nIf you are lucky enough to spot one, stay quiet, keep your distance and enjoy the moment."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'medium', 1, 'The passage says the platypus is ''mostly nocturnal''. What does ''nocturnal'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"active mainly at night"},{"id":"b","text":"able to swim underwater"},{"id":"c","text":"living alone"},{"id":"d","text":"eating only plants"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The passage says it feeds at dawn, at dusk and through the night, and rests during the day. Nocturnal comes from the Latin ''nox'', meaning night.', NULL, '{"title":"The Elusive Platypus","text":"Few Australian animals are as elusive as the platypus. Many people who live beside rivers in the Blue Mountains or on the New England Tablelands have never seen one, even after years of looking. The platypus is mostly nocturnal, feeding at dawn, at dusk and through the night, and it spends the hot part of the day resting in a burrow dug into the riverbank.\n\nWhen it dives, the platypus closes its eyes, ears and nostrils. Instead of sight, it relies on its bill, which is covered in thousands of tiny sensors that pick up the faint electric signals given off by shrimp, insect larvae and worms. In a single night, a platypus can eat food equal to about a fifth of its own body weight.\n\nSadly, platypuses are vulnerable to many threats. Droughts dry out the pools they depend on, dams change the flow of rivers, and bushfires strip away the plants that hold riverbanks together. Litter is another danger: a platypus can become tangled in a discarded hair tie or fishing line and be unable to free itself.\n\nThere is good news, though. Volunteers across NSW are replanting creek banks, removing rubbish and reporting sightings to scientists. After heavy rain helps to replenish dry rivers, some groups have even been able to return rescued platypuses to the wild.\n\nIf you are lucky enough to spot one, stay quiet, keep your distance and enjoy the moment."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'medium', 1, 'The Greek root ''tele'' means far and ''scope'' means look. A ___ is an instrument for looking at things that are far away, such as stars.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["telescope","telescopes"]'::jsonb, 'tele (far) + scope (look) = telescope.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'hard', 1, 'Put these greetings in order from most informal to most formal.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Hey!"},{"id":"b","text":"Hi there,"},{"id":"c","text":"Good morning,"},{"id":"d","text":"Dear Sir or Madam,"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, '''Hey!'' is for close friends, ''Hi there'' is friendly, ''Good morning'' is polite, and ''Dear Sir or Madam'' is used in formal letters to people you don''t know.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'hard', 1, 'In the fourth paragraph, ''heavy rain helps to replenish dry rivers''. What does ''replenish'' mean here?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"fill up again"},{"id":"b","text":"wash away"},{"id":"c","text":"dry out"},{"id":"d","text":"make dirty"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The prefix re- means again, and the rivers were dry, so rain fills them up again. That is why rescued platypuses could be returned.', NULL, '{"title":"The Elusive Platypus","text":"Few Australian animals are as elusive as the platypus. Many people who live beside rivers in the Blue Mountains or on the New England Tablelands have never seen one, even after years of looking. The platypus is mostly nocturnal, feeding at dawn, at dusk and through the night, and it spends the hot part of the day resting in a burrow dug into the riverbank.\n\nWhen it dives, the platypus closes its eyes, ears and nostrils. Instead of sight, it relies on its bill, which is covered in thousands of tiny sensors that pick up the faint electric signals given off by shrimp, insect larvae and worms. In a single night, a platypus can eat food equal to about a fifth of its own body weight.\n\nSadly, platypuses are vulnerable to many threats. Droughts dry out the pools they depend on, dams change the flow of rivers, and bushfires strip away the plants that hold riverbanks together. Litter is another danger: a platypus can become tangled in a discarded hair tie or fishing line and be unable to free itself.\n\nThere is good news, though. Volunteers across NSW are replanting creek banks, removing rubbish and reporting sightings to scientists. After heavy rain helps to replenish dry rivers, some groups have even been able to return rescued platypuses to the wild.\n\nIf you are lucky enough to spot one, stay quiet, keep your distance and enjoy the moment."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'hard', 1, 'The Latin root ''spect'' means look. Which words contain this root? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"inspect"},{"id":"b","text":"spectator"},{"id":"c","text":"spectacles"},{"id":"d","text":"spend"},{"id":"e","text":"spear"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'To inspect is to look into something, a spectator looks on, and spectacles help you look. ''Spend'' and ''spear'' just happen to start with sp-; they have no ''spect'' root.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_hard', 1, '''The council''s decision was met with a cacophony of car horns, shouting and whistles.'' What does ''cacophony'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a harsh mix of loud sounds"},{"id":"b","text":"a quiet agreement"},{"id":"c","text":"a beautiful melody"},{"id":"d","text":"a long silence"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The context lists car horns, shouting and whistles, which are loud and unpleasant together. The Greek parts help too: ''kakos'' means bad and ''phone'' means sound.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_hard', 1, 'A student is writing to the local member of parliament asking for a new skate park. Which sentence best suits the purpose and audience?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I am writing to ask you to consider funding a skate park for young people in our suburb."},{"id":"b","text":"Yo, we really need a skate park, it''d be sick."},{"id":"c","text":"Give us a skate park now or else!"},{"id":"d","text":"Skate parks are cool and you should totally build one."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The audience is an important adult the writer doesn''t know, so the language should be formal and polite. The other options use slang (''yo'', ''sick'', ''totally'') or sound rude.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071201', 'very_hard', 1, '''Legible'' means clear enough to read. Add a prefix to write its antonym.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"illegible"'::jsonb, 'The prefix ''in-'' (not) changes to ''il-'' before a word starting with l: il + legible = illegible, with a double l. Compare illegal and illogical.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_easy', 1, 'In ''The tired players walked slowly to the bus'', which word is an adverb?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"slowly"},{"id":"b","text":"tired"},{"id":"c","text":"players"},{"id":"d","text":"walked"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'An adverb tells us how, when or where an action happens. ''Slowly'' tells us how they walked. ''Tired'' is an adjective, ''players'' is a noun and ''walked'' is a verb.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_easy', 1, '''The kookaburra laughed.'' is a simple sentence.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A simple sentence has one clause: one subject (the kookaburra) and one verb (laughed).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence uses an apostrophe correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It''s raining in Bathurst today."},{"id":"b","text":"Its raining in Bathurst today."},{"id":"c","text":"The cat licked it''s paw."},{"id":"d","text":"The apple''s are ripe."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''It''s'' is short for ''it is'', so ''It''s raining'' is correct. ''Its paw'' (belonging to it) has no apostrophe, and plain plurals like ''apples'' never need one.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'easy', 1, 'Which words are nouns in ''Our team won the trophy at the carnival in Dubbo''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"team"},{"id":"b","text":"trophy"},{"id":"c","text":"carnival"},{"id":"d","text":"Dubbo"},{"id":"e","text":"won"},{"id":"f","text":"our"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Nouns name people, places, things or groups: team, trophy, carnival and Dubbo (a proper noun). ''Won'' is a verb and ''our'' is a possessive word.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'easy', 1, 'In ''Jai ran to the canteen because he was hungry'', the conjunction is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"because"'::jsonb, 'A conjunction joins clauses. ''Because'' joins ''Jai ran to the canteen'' and ''he was hungry'' and gives the reason.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these is a compound sentence?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I wanted to swim, but the pool was closed."},{"id":"b","text":"Because the pool was closed, I went home."},{"id":"c","text":"The pool was closed."},{"id":"d","text":"Closed for repairs until Monday."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A compound sentence joins two main clauses with a coordinating conjunction such as and, but or so. Both ''I wanted to swim'' and ''the pool was closed'' make sense on their own.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'medium', 1, 'Which group of words is a phrase, not a clause?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"under the old gum tree"},{"id":"b","text":"the bus arrived"},{"id":"c","text":"when the bell rang"},{"id":"d","text":"she smiled"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A clause has a verb (and a subject). ''Under the old gum tree'' has no verb, so it is a phrase. The others contain verbs: arrived, rang, smiled.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"\"Hurry up,\" said Mum, \"or we''ll miss the train.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Hurry up\" said Mum, \"or we''ll miss the train\"."},{"id":"c","text":"\"Hurry up,\" said Mum \"or we''ll miss the train.\""},{"id":"d","text":"Hurry up, said Mum, \"or we''ll miss the train.\""}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The spoken words go inside quotation marks, with the comma inside before ''said Mum'', another comma after ''Mum'', and the full stop inside the closing marks.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'medium', 1, 'In ''The students'' lockers were painted blue'', the apostrophe shows that the lockers belong to more than one student.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The apostrophe comes after the s in ''students'', so it shows plural possession: the lockers of the students.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'hard', 1, 'In ''Although the forecast was for rain, the cricket match went ahead'', which part is the subordinate (dependent) clause?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Although the forecast was for rain"},{"id":"b","text":"the cricket match went ahead"},{"id":"c","text":"the forecast"},{"id":"d","text":"went ahead"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A subordinate clause can''t stand alone. ''Although the forecast was for rain'' leaves us waiting for more. ''The cricket match went ahead'' is the main clause.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses a colon correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Pack three things: a hat, a water bottle and sunscreen."},{"id":"b","text":"Pack: a hat, a water bottle and sunscreen."},{"id":"c","text":"Pack three things a hat: a water bottle and sunscreen."},{"id":"d","text":"Pack three: things a hat, a water bottle and sunscreen."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A colon comes after a complete idea to introduce a list. ''Pack three things'' is complete, so the colon goes after it. Don''t put a colon straight after a verb like ''Pack''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'hard', 1, 'Put these parts in order to make a complex sentence that begins with its subordinate clause.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"When the siren sounded,"},{"id":"b","text":"the swimmers"},{"id":"c","text":"raced back"},{"id":"d","text":"to the beach."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The subordinate clause ''When the siren sounded,'' comes first, followed by a comma, then the main clause ''the swimmers raced back to the beach.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses a semicolon correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The storm knocked out the power; we ate dinner by torchlight."},{"id":"b","text":"The storm knocked out the power; and we ate dinner by torchlight."},{"id":"c","text":"Because the storm knocked out the power; we ate dinner by torchlight."},{"id":"d","text":"The storm; knocked out the power and we ate dinner by torchlight."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A semicolon joins two closely linked main clauses that could each be a sentence. It replaces the conjunction, so we don''t add ''and'', and it can''t follow a subordinate clause starting with ''Because''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are complex sentences? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The koala slept while the tourists took photos."},{"id":"b","text":"The koala slept, and the tourists took photos."},{"id":"c","text":"If you finish early, you can read quietly."},{"id":"d","text":"The tourists who visited the sanctuary loved the koalas."},{"id":"e","text":"The koala and the wombat slept all day."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A complex sentence has a main clause plus a subordinate clause: ''while the tourists took photos'', ''If you finish early'' and ''who visited the sanctuary''. The second sentence is compound (joined by ''and'') and the last is simple.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_hard', 1, 'In the sentence ''The fast ended at sunset'', what word class is ''fast''?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["noun","a noun","common noun"]'::jsonb, 'The same word can belong to different classes. Here ''the fast'' is a thing (a time without eating) and it is the subject of ''ended'', so it is a noun, not an adjective.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is a verb in ''The wombat dug a deep burrow''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"dug"},{"id":"b","text":"wombat"},{"id":"c","text":"deep"},{"id":"d","text":"burrow"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A verb is an action or doing word. ''Dug'' is what the wombat did.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_easy', 1, 'A word that describes a noun, such as ''gigantic'' or ''muddy'', is called an ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["adjective","adjectives"]'::jsonb, 'Adjectives describe nouns: a gigantic wave, a muddy paddock.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_easy', 1, '''We''re'' is a contraction of ''we are''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'In a contraction the apostrophe shows where letters are left out. We''re = we are, with the a left out.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'easy', 1, 'Which word is a pronoun in ''Lena gave them the tickets''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"them"},{"id":"b","text":"gave"},{"id":"c","text":"tickets"},{"id":"d","text":"Lena"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A pronoun takes the place of a noun. ''Them'' stands in for the people Lena gave the tickets to.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentences use commas correctly in a list? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"We saw emus, kangaroos and wallabies."},{"id":"b","text":"We saw, emus kangaroos and wallabies."},{"id":"c","text":"Bring a pen, a ruler, a calculator and a protractor."},{"id":"d","text":"Bring a pen a ruler, a calculator, and, a protractor."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, 'Commas separate the items in a list, and the last two items are usually joined with ''and''. A comma doesn''t go straight after ''saw'' or after ''and''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these is a simple sentence?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The wind howled all night."},{"id":"b","text":"The wind howled, so we stayed inside."},{"id":"c","text":"Because the wind howled, we stayed inside."},{"id":"d","text":"The wind howled and the rain fell, but we slept."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A simple sentence has just one clause (one verb group). ''The wind howled all night'' has only one verb, ''howled''. The others have two or more clauses.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'medium', 1, 'Write the contraction for ''will not''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["won''t","won’t"]'::jsonb, '''Will not'' is an unusual contraction: it becomes won''t, with the apostrophe where the o of ''not'' was left out.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'medium', 1, 'In ''The lizard hid beneath the rock'', which word is a preposition?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"beneath"},{"id":"b","text":"hid"},{"id":"c","text":"lizard"},{"id":"d","text":"rock"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A preposition shows the position or relationship of one thing to another. ''Beneath'' tells us where the lizard hid in relation to the rock.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'medium', 1, 'Which correctly shows the uniforms belonging to several girls?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"the girls'' uniforms"},{"id":"b","text":"the girl''s uniforms"},{"id":"c","text":"the girls uniform''s"},{"id":"d","text":"the girls''s uniforms"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'For a plural ending in s, add the apostrophe after the s: girls''. ''The girl''s uniforms'' would mean only one girl.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'hard', 1, 'In ''A huge, hairy huntsman spider crept across the ceiling'', which noun group is the subject of the sentence?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A huge, hairy huntsman spider"},{"id":"b","text":"crept across"},{"id":"c","text":"across the ceiling"},{"id":"d","text":"huge, hairy"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The subject is who or what does the action. The noun group ''A huge, hairy huntsman spider'' did the creeping. ''Across the ceiling'' is a prepositional phrase saying where.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentences use a colon correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"There was only one thing left to do: run."},{"id":"b","text":"The recipe needs: flour, eggs and milk."},{"id":"c","text":"Coach gave us one rule: never give up."},{"id":"d","text":"My favourite: subject is maths."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, 'A colon follows a complete idea and introduces what comes next. ''There was only one thing left to do'' and ''Coach gave us one rule'' are complete, so the colons work. ''The recipe needs'' is not complete on its own.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'hard', 1, 'Put these sentences in order: simple sentence first, then compound, then complex.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"The bell rang."},{"id":"b","text":"The bell rang, and we went inside."},{"id":"c","text":"When the bell rang, we went inside."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Simple has one clause. Compound joins two main clauses with ''and''. Complex has a subordinate clause, ''When the bell rang'', plus a main clause.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses semicolons correctly to separate list items that already contain commas?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The team visited Orange, NSW; Ballarat, Victoria; and Mount Gambier, SA."},{"id":"b","text":"The team visited Orange; NSW, Ballarat; Victoria, and Mount Gambier; SA."},{"id":"c","text":"The team visited; Orange, NSW, Ballarat, Victoria, and Mount Gambier, SA."},{"id":"d","text":"The team visited Orange, NSW, Ballarat, Victoria; and Mount Gambier, SA."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'When each list item contains a comma (town, state), semicolons separate the items so the reader can tell where each one ends: Orange, NSW; Ballarat, Victoria; and Mount Gambier, SA.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_hard', 1, '''The bus was late, we missed the start of the film.'' This is a comma splice. Which is NOT a correct way to fix it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The bus was late, we missed, the start of the film."},{"id":"b","text":"The bus was late; we missed the start of the film."},{"id":"c","text":"The bus was late, so we missed the start of the film."},{"id":"d","text":"Because the bus was late, we missed the start of the film."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A comma alone can''t join two main clauses. A semicolon, a conjunction like ''so'', or turning one part into a subordinate clause all fix it. Adding another comma just makes it worse.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071202', 'very_hard', 1, 'How many clauses are in this sentence? ''When the rain stopped, the kids who had waited inside ran to the oval.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3","three"]'::jsonb, 'Count the verbs: ''When the rain stopped'' (1), ''who had waited inside'' (2) and the main clause ''the kids ran to the oval'' (3).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"necessary"},{"id":"b","text":"neccessary"},{"id":"c","text":"necesary"},{"id":"d","text":"neccesary"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Necessary has one c and two s''s. A tip: a shirt has one collar (c) and two sleeves (ss).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the plural of ''potato''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"potatoes"'::jsonb, 'Some words ending in o add -es in the plural: potato becomes potatoes, tomato becomes tomatoes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_easy', 1, '''Their'', ''there'' and ''they''re'' are homophones.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Homophones sound the same but have different spellings and meanings: their (belonging to them), there (a place) and they''re (they are).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'easy', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''We need to ___ our bags at the airport.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"weigh"},{"id":"b","text":"way"},{"id":"c","text":"whey"},{"id":"d","text":"wey"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Weigh'' means to measure how heavy something is. ''Way'' is a path or method, and ''whey'' is part of milk.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'easy', 1, 'Which plurals are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"churches"},{"id":"b","text":"babys"},{"id":"c","text":"boxes"},{"id":"d","text":"monkeys"},{"id":"e","text":"ladys"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Words ending in ch or x add -es (churches, boxes). A vowel before y means just add s (monkeys). A consonant before y means change y to i and add -es: babies, ladies.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'easy', 1, 'Adding -ing to ''hope'' gives ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"hoping"'::jsonb, 'When a word ends in a silent e, drop the e before adding a suffix that starts with a vowel: hope → hoping.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'medium', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"receive"},{"id":"b","text":"recieve"},{"id":"c","text":"receeve"},{"id":"d","text":"resieve"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Remember ''i before e except after c'' when the sound is ee: receive, ceiling, deceive.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'medium', 1, 'Add -ed to ''travel''. Write the word using Australian spelling.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"travelled"'::jsonb, 'In Australian English, a word ending in a single l after a single vowel doubles the l before -ed or -ing: travel → travelled.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'medium', 1, 'The plural of ''chief'' is ''chieves''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Not every word ending in f changes to v. Chief just adds s: chiefs. Others like this are roofs, beliefs and chefs.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'hard', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''The ___ of the story is that honesty matters most.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"moral"},{"id":"b","text":"morale"},{"id":"c","text":"morral"},{"id":"d","text":"moralle"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A moral is the lesson of a story. Morale (with an e) means the confidence or spirit of a group, as in ''team morale''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these commonly misspelt words are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"separate"},{"id":"b","text":"rhythm"},{"id":"c","text":"government"},{"id":"d","text":"tommorrow"},{"id":"e","text":"beginning"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Separate has ''a rat'' in it, rhythm has no vowels except y, and government has ''govern'' + ment. Tomorrow has one m and two r''s.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'hard', 1, 'Put these word parts in order to build the word ''unbelievably''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"un"},{"id":"b","text":"believe"},{"id":"c","text":"able"},{"id":"d","text":"ly"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Prefix first, then base word, then suffixes: un + believe + able + ly. The e of believe drops before -able, and -able + ly becomes -ably: unbelievably.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does ''beginning'' have a double n, while ''opening'' has a single n?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"In be-GIN the stress is on the last syllable, so the n doubles; in O-pen the stress is on the first syllable, so it doesn''t."},{"id":"b","text":"Longer words always double their last letter."},{"id":"c","text":"Words starting with a vowel never double letters."},{"id":"d","text":"There is no rule; both spellings are guesses."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Double the final consonant before a vowel suffix when the word ends in one vowel + one consonant and the last syllable is stressed: be-GIN → beginning, but O-pen → opening.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Write the plural of ''phenomenon''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"phenomena"'::jsonb, 'Phenomenon comes from Greek, and Greek words ending in -on often change to -a in the plural: phenomenon → phenomena, criterion → criteria.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses ''compliment'' and ''complement'' correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Her compliment about my scarf was kind, and its colour is a perfect complement to my coat."},{"id":"b","text":"Her complement about my scarf was kind, and its colour is a perfect compliment to my coat."},{"id":"c","text":"Her compliment about my scarf was kind, and its colour is a perfect compliment to my coat."},{"id":"d","text":"Her complement about my scarf was kind, and its colour is a perfect complement to my coat."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A compliment (with i) is praise: think ''I like your scarf''. A complement (with e) completes or goes well with something else.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"friend"},{"id":"b","text":"freind"},{"id":"c","text":"frend"},{"id":"d","text":"freynd"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A tip for friend: you are a friend to the end. The word ends in -end, with i before it: fr-i-end.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_easy', 1, 'The plural of ''mouse'' is ''mice''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Mouse has an irregular plural: mice. Others like it are goose → geese and foot → feet.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the plural of ''bus''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"buses"'::jsonb, 'Words ending in s, ss, sh, ch or x add -es in the plural, so bus becomes buses.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'easy', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''I ___ the ball over the fence by accident.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"threw"},{"id":"b","text":"through"},{"id":"c","text":"thorough"},{"id":"d","text":"though"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Threw'' is the past tense of throw. ''Through'' means in one side and out the other, ''thorough'' means careful and complete, and ''though'' means however.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'easy', 1, 'Add the suffix -ly to ''happy''. Write the new word.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"happily"'::jsonb, 'When a word ends in a consonant + y, change the y to i before adding a suffix: happy → happily.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'easy', 1, 'Which words keep the final e of the base word when -ment is added? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"achievement"},{"id":"b","text":"excitement"},{"id":"c","text":"argument"},{"id":"d","text":"movement"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '-ment starts with a consonant, so the e usually stays: achieve → achievement, excite → excitement, move → movement. ''Argument'' is a well-known exception: argue drops its e.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'medium', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"environment"},{"id":"b","text":"enviroment"},{"id":"c","text":"envirenment"},{"id":"d","text":"enviromnent"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Environment has ''iron'' hiding in the middle: env-iron-ment. Many people forget the n before -ment.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'medium', 1, 'Write the plural of ''echo''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"echoes"'::jsonb, 'Like potato and hero, echo adds -es in the plural: echoes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'medium', 1, '''Disappear'' is spelt with one s and two p''s.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Disappear is the prefix dis- + appear. The prefix has one s and appear has two p''s, so: dis + appear = disappear.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentences use ''lose'' or ''loose'' correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Don''t lose your hat at the beach."},{"id":"b","text":"My shoelace is loose."},{"id":"c","text":"We might loose the grand final."},{"id":"d","text":"The dog got lose from its lead."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Lose (one o) is a verb meaning to misplace or be beaten. Loose (two o''s) means not tight or free. So ''we might lose the final'' and ''the dog got loose''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'hard', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"responsible"},{"id":"b","text":"responsable"},{"id":"c","text":"resposible"},{"id":"d","text":"responsibel"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Responsible ends in -ible. A useful guide: if the part before the ending is not a whole word (respons), the ending is often -ible; if it is a whole word (depend), it is often -able.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'hard', 1, 'Add the suffix -ence to ''refer''. Write the new word.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"reference"'::jsonb, 'In re-FER the stress is at the end, but in REF-er-ence it moves to the start, so the r does not double: reference. Compare referred, where the stress stays on FER.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these words are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"conscious"},{"id":"b","text":"occasionally"},{"id":"c","text":"acommodate"},{"id":"d","text":"guarantee"},{"id":"e","text":"harrass"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Conscious, occasionally (two c''s, one s) and guarantee are correct. The right spellings are accommodate (double c, double m) and harass (one r, double s).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why is the plural of ''valley'' spelt ''valleys'', but the plural of ''city'' spelt ''cities''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"When y follows a vowel, just add s; when y follows a consonant, change y to i and add -es."},{"id":"b","text":"Words with two l''s always add s."},{"id":"c","text":"Longer words always change y to i."},{"id":"d","text":"There is no rule; you just have to guess."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'In valley, e (a vowel) comes before y, so add s: valleys. In city, t (a consonant) comes before y, so change y to i and add -es: cities.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007120330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these word parts in order to build the word ''irresponsibility''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"ir"},{"id":"b","text":"respons"},{"id":"c","text":"ibil"},{"id":"d","text":"ity"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'ir- (not) goes first and doubles the r because the base starts with r. Then respons, then -ible changes to -ibil before the suffix -ity: irresponsibility.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the job of a topic sentence?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It tells the reader the main idea of the paragraph."},{"id":"b","text":"It ends the whole essay."},{"id":"c","text":"It lists every source the writer used."},{"id":"d","text":"It must always be a question."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A topic sentence, usually the first sentence of a paragraph, tells the reader what the paragraph is about. The other sentences then support it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_easy', 1, 'You should start a new paragraph when you move to a new idea, time, place or speaker.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Remember TiPToP: start a new paragraph for a new TIme, PLace, TOpic or Person speaking.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which connective best fills the gap? ''The track was muddy. ___, the race went ahead.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"However"},{"id":"b","text":"Because"},{"id":"c","text":"Firstly"},{"id":"d","text":"Similarly"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The second sentence goes against what we''d expect from the first, so we need a contrast connective: However.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are persuasive techniques? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"rhetorical question"},{"id":"b","text":"emotive language"},{"id":"c","text":"repetition"},{"id":"d","text":"table of contents"},{"id":"e","text":"statistics"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Rhetorical questions, emotive language, repetition and statistics are all used to persuade readers. A table of contents just shows where things are in a book.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'easy', 1, 'A word or phrase such as ''therefore'', ''in addition'' or ''on the other hand'' that links ideas is called a ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["connective","connectives","cohesive connective","linking word","transition","transition word"]'::jsonb, 'Connectives (also called linking words) join ideas so writing flows and the reader can follow how ideas relate.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence has no errors?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Their going to the beach on Saturday."},{"id":"b","text":"They''re going to the beach on Saturday."},{"id":"c","text":"There going to the beach on saturday."},{"id":"d","text":"They''re going too the beach on Saturday."}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'They''re = they are, ''to'' shows direction, and days of the week need a capital letter. Only the second sentence gets all of these right.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'medium', 1, 'Which is the strongest revision of ''The dog was very scared''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The dog cowered under the table, trembling."},{"id":"b","text":"The dog was really, really scared."},{"id":"c","text":"The dog was very very scared."},{"id":"d","text":"The dog was scared a lot."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Show, don''t tell: a precise verb (cowered) and a detail (trembling) let the reader picture the fear, instead of just adding ''very'' or ''really''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'medium', 1, 'What persuasive techniques does the writer use in the first two sentences?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Asking the reader to imagine a scene, then a rhetorical question"},{"id":"b","text":"Quoting statistics from a study"},{"id":"c","text":"Telling a joke"},{"id":"d","text":"Giving the opposing argument"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Imagine walking past your classroom...'' puts the reader in the scene, and ''Wouldn''t that be better...?'' is a rhetorical question: the writer expects you to answer ''yes''.', NULL, '{"title":"Draft: Every School Needs a Vegie Garden","text":"Imagine walking past your classroom and picking a ripe tomato for lunch. Wouldn''t that be better than another soggy sandwich? Every school in NSW should have its own vegetable garden, and our school should be the first in Tamworth to start one.\n\nFirstly, a garden would help students eat better. Experts say that children who grow their own vegetables are far more likely to eat them. At the moment, many students throw away the carrot sticks their parents pack. If they had planted those carrots themselves, they would be proud to eat them.\n\nIn addition, a garden is an outdoor classroom. Students could measure garden beds in maths, test soil in science and write procedures in English. Learning would become hands-on, practical and fun.\n\nSome people argue that a garden costs too much. However, local hardware stores often donate seeds and tools, and the canteen could sell the extra produce at the school fete to raise money. The garden could pay for itself within a year.\n\nOur school has a sunny, empty corner beside the oval that is doing nothing. Let''s turn it into something that feeds us, teaches us and brings us together. Plant a garden today, and our whole school will reap the rewards tomorrow."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'medium', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to make a well-structured paragraph.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Koalas are in serious trouble in NSW."},{"id":"b","text":"Land clearing has destroyed much of the forest they need for food and shelter."},{"id":"c","text":"For example, many koalas have lost their homes as new housing estates have spread."},{"id":"d","text":"Unless we protect their habitat, koalas could disappear from the wild in NSW."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Start with the topic sentence (the main idea), then the reason, then an example (''For example'' points back to the reason), and finish with a concluding sentence.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'hard', 1, 'Which connective shows cause and effect?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"consequently"},{"id":"b","text":"meanwhile"},{"id":"c","text":"similarly"},{"id":"d","text":"for instance"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Consequently'' means ''as a result'', linking a cause to its effect. ''Meanwhile'' shows time, ''similarly'' compares and ''for instance'' gives an example.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'hard', 1, 'In the fourth paragraph, what is the job of the connective ''However''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It signals that the writer is about to argue against the opposing view."},{"id":"b","text":"It adds another reason that agrees with the one before."},{"id":"c","text":"It shows the order in which things happen."},{"id":"d","text":"It introduces an example."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The paragraph first gives the other side (''a garden costs too much''), then ''However'' turns to the writer''s rebuttal: donations and sales could pay for it.', NULL, '{"title":"Draft: Every School Needs a Vegie Garden","text":"Imagine walking past your classroom and picking a ripe tomato for lunch. Wouldn''t that be better than another soggy sandwich? Every school in NSW should have its own vegetable garden, and our school should be the first in Tamworth to start one.\n\nFirstly, a garden would help students eat better. Experts say that children who grow their own vegetables are far more likely to eat them. At the moment, many students throw away the carrot sticks their parents pack. If they had planted those carrots themselves, they would be proud to eat them.\n\nIn addition, a garden is an outdoor classroom. Students could measure garden beds in maths, test soil in science and write procedures in English. Learning would become hands-on, practical and fun.\n\nSome people argue that a garden costs too much. However, local hardware stores often donate seeds and tools, and the canteen could sell the extra produce at the school fete to raise money. The garden could pay for itself within a year.\n\nOur school has a sunny, empty corner beside the oval that is doing nothing. Let''s turn it into something that feeds us, teaches us and brings us together. Plant a garden today, and our whole school will reap the rewards tomorrow."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'hard', 1, 'Find the misspelt word and write it correctly: ''The council should definately build a new skate park.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"definitely"'::jsonb, 'Definitely is spelt with ''finite'' inside it: de-finite-ly. Proofreading for spelling is part of editing.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which revision of ''I think that maybe the canteen should possibly sell healthier food'' is the most persuasive?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The canteen must sell healthier food."},{"id":"b","text":"I think maybe the canteen should sell healthier food."},{"id":"c","text":"Maybe the canteen could possibly sell healthier food, I think."},{"id":"d","text":"The canteen should sell healthier food, if it wants to, maybe."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Words like ''I think'', ''maybe'' and ''possibly'' make the writer sound unsure. High-modality words like ''must'' sound confident and convincing.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence in the draft is the weakest evidence, because it needs a source to be convincing?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Experts say that children who grow their own vegetables are far more likely to eat them."},{"id":"b","text":"Students could measure garden beds in maths, test soil in science and write procedures in English."},{"id":"c","text":"Our school has a sunny, empty corner beside the oval that is doing nothing."},{"id":"d","text":"Plant a garden today, and our whole school will reap the rewards tomorrow."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Experts say'' doesn''t tell us which experts or what study. Naming the source (for example, a university study) would make this evidence much stronger. The other sentences are ideas, a fact the reader can check, or a call to action.', NULL, '{"title":"Draft: Every School Needs a Vegie Garden","text":"Imagine walking past your classroom and picking a ripe tomato for lunch. Wouldn''t that be better than another soggy sandwich? Every school in NSW should have its own vegetable garden, and our school should be the first in Tamworth to start one.\n\nFirstly, a garden would help students eat better. Experts say that children who grow their own vegetables are far more likely to eat them. At the moment, many students throw away the carrot sticks their parents pack. If they had planted those carrots themselves, they would be proud to eat them.\n\nIn addition, a garden is an outdoor classroom. Students could measure garden beds in maths, test soil in science and write procedures in English. Learning would become hands-on, practical and fun.\n\nSome people argue that a garden costs too much. However, local hardware stores often donate seeds and tools, and the canteen could sell the extra produce at the school fete to raise money. The garden could pay for itself within a year.\n\nOur school has a sunny, empty corner beside the oval that is doing nothing. Let''s turn it into something that feeds us, teaches us and brings us together. Plant a garden today, and our whole school will reap the rewards tomorrow."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which edit correctly fixes the run-on sentence ''We arrived late the bus had broken down''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"We arrived late because the bus had broken down."},{"id":"b","text":"We arrived late, the bus had broken down."},{"id":"c","text":"We arrived late the bus, had broken down."},{"id":"d","text":"We arrived, late the bus had broken down."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Two main clauses can''t just run together. Adding the conjunction ''because'' joins them correctly. A comma alone makes a comma splice.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_easy', 1, 'A concluding sentence sums up or rounds off the main idea of a paragraph.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The topic sentence opens the main idea and the concluding sentence wraps it up, often linking back to the topic sentence.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which verb is the most precise replacement for ''went'' in ''The kelpie went across the paddock''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"raced"},{"id":"b","text":"was"},{"id":"c","text":"did"},{"id":"d","text":"got"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Raced'' tells us exactly how the kelpie moved: fast. Precise verbs help the reader picture the action.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write a connective that adds another point: ''Swimming is good exercise. ___, it is a vital safety skill.''', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["Also","Furthermore","Moreover","In addition","Additionally","Besides"]'::jsonb, 'The second sentence adds another reason swimming is good, so an adding connective fits: Also, Furthermore, Moreover or In addition.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence would make the best topic sentence for a paragraph about why teenagers need more sleep?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Most teenagers need more sleep than they get."},{"id":"b","text":"I went to bed at ten o''clock last night."},{"id":"c","text":"Sleep."},{"id":"d","text":"Some people have bunk beds."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A topic sentence states the main idea of the whole paragraph. The others are a personal detail, a single word, or off topic.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are examples of emotive language? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"heartless cruelty"},{"id":"b","text":"a devastating loss"},{"id":"c","text":"a green bin"},{"id":"d","text":"innocent animals"},{"id":"e","text":"a Tuesday meeting"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Emotive language makes readers feel something strongly. ''Heartless'', ''devastating'' and ''innocent'' stir up feelings; ''a green bin'' and ''a Tuesday meeting'' are neutral facts.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence is correctly punctuated?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"My favourite sports are cricket, netball and swimming."},{"id":"b","text":"My favourite sports are, cricket netball and swimming."},{"id":"c","text":"my favourite sports are cricket, netball and swimming."},{"id":"d","text":"My favourite sports are cricket netball, and, swimming."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The sentence needs a capital letter at the start, a comma between list items, and no comma straight after ''are'' or after ''and''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence uses inclusive language to persuade?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"We all deserve clean, safe parks in our town."},{"id":"b","text":"The park is 2 kilometres from the station."},{"id":"c","text":"Parks have grass and trees."},{"id":"d","text":"The council meets on Mondays."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Inclusive language uses words like ''we'', ''us'' and ''our'' to make readers feel part of the same group as the writer, so they are more likely to agree.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'medium', 1, 'Put these connectives in the order a writer would usually use them to structure an argument.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Firstly"},{"id":"b","text":"Secondly"},{"id":"c","text":"Finally"},{"id":"d","text":"In conclusion"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Firstly, Secondly and Finally introduce the reasons in order, and ''In conclusion'' begins the last paragraph that sums up the argument.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'medium', 1, 'What is the topic sentence of the paragraph that begins ''For more than sixty years''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"For more than sixty years, the pool has been the heart of our town in summer."},{"id":"b","text":"Families cool off there on scorching afternoons."},{"id":"c","text":"Closing it would take away one of the few places where people of all ages can spend time together."},{"id":"d","text":"Our pool is worth saving."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first sentence states the paragraph''s main idea (the pool is the heart of the town). The examples about families, students and older residents all support it.', NULL, '{"title":"Letter to the Editor: Save Our Pool","text":"Dear Editor,\n\nI am writing to express my concern about the council''s plan to close the Riverside Pool at the end of this season.\n\nFor more than sixty years, the pool has been the heart of our town in summer. Families cool off there on scorching afternoons, primary school students take their first swimming lessons there, and older residents swim laps every morning to stay healthy. Closing it would take away one of the few places where people of all ages can spend time together.\n\nThe council claims the pool is too expensive to repair. I understand that money is tight. However, a community fundraiser and a grant from the state government could cover much of the cost. Other country towns have saved their pools in exactly this way.\n\nMost importantly, the pool keeps children safe. Learning to swim is an essential skill in a country with so many rivers, dams and beaches. My cousin has a trampoline in her backyard. Without a local pool, many families would have to drive over an hour for lessons, and some simply could not afford to.\n\nI urge the council to reconsider its decision and to meet with residents before it is too late. Our pool is worth saving.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nAva Nguyen, Year 7"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the strongest revision of ''It was a nice day at the beach''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Sunlight glittered on the waves as gulls wheeled above the sand."},{"id":"b","text":"It was a really nice day at the beach."},{"id":"c","text":"The day at the beach was nice and good."},{"id":"d","text":"It was a very nice, nice day at the beach."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Nice'' is vague. The first option uses precise verbs (glittered, wheeled) and sensory details so the reader can see the scene.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'hard', 1, 'In a persuasive text, admitting an opposing argument and then rebutting it can make your case stronger.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Showing that you understand the other side, then explaining why it is wrong, makes you seem fair and leaves the reader with your view.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'hard', 1, 'Which phrase best describes the tone of Ava''s letter?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"concerned but respectful"},{"id":"b","text":"angry and rude"},{"id":"c","text":"silly and joking"},{"id":"d","text":"bored and uninterested"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Ava shows concern (''express my concern'', ''before it is too late'') but stays polite: ''I understand that money is tight'' and ''I urge the council to reconsider''.', NULL, '{"title":"Letter to the Editor: Save Our Pool","text":"Dear Editor,\n\nI am writing to express my concern about the council''s plan to close the Riverside Pool at the end of this season.\n\nFor more than sixty years, the pool has been the heart of our town in summer. Families cool off there on scorching afternoons, primary school students take their first swimming lessons there, and older residents swim laps every morning to stay healthy. Closing it would take away one of the few places where people of all ages can spend time together.\n\nThe council claims the pool is too expensive to repair. I understand that money is tight. However, a community fundraiser and a grant from the state government could cover much of the cost. Other country towns have saved their pools in exactly this way.\n\nMost importantly, the pool keeps children safe. Learning to swim is an essential skill in a country with so many rivers, dams and beaches. My cousin has a trampoline in her backyard. Without a local pool, many families would have to drive over an hour for lessons, and some simply could not afford to.\n\nI urge the council to reconsider its decision and to meet with residents before it is too late. Our pool is worth saving.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nAva Nguyen, Year 7"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence should Ava delete because it does not support the main idea of its paragraph?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"My cousin has a trampoline in her backyard."},{"id":"b","text":"Learning to swim is an essential skill in a country with so many rivers, dams and beaches."},{"id":"c","text":"I understand that money is tight."},{"id":"d","text":"Families cool off there on scorching afternoons."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'That paragraph is about the pool keeping children safe. A cousin''s trampoline has nothing to do with swimming or safety, so it breaks the paragraph''s cohesion.', NULL, '{"title":"Letter to the Editor: Save Our Pool","text":"Dear Editor,\n\nI am writing to express my concern about the council''s plan to close the Riverside Pool at the end of this season.\n\nFor more than sixty years, the pool has been the heart of our town in summer. Families cool off there on scorching afternoons, primary school students take their first swimming lessons there, and older residents swim laps every morning to stay healthy. Closing it would take away one of the few places where people of all ages can spend time together.\n\nThe council claims the pool is too expensive to repair. I understand that money is tight. However, a community fundraiser and a grant from the state government could cover much of the cost. Other country towns have saved their pools in exactly this way.\n\nMost importantly, the pool keeps children safe. Learning to swim is an essential skill in a country with so many rivers, dams and beaches. My cousin has a trampoline in her backyard. Without a local pool, many families would have to drive over an hour for lessons, and some simply could not afford to.\n\nI urge the council to reconsider its decision and to meet with residents before it is too late. Our pool is worth saving.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nAva Nguyen, Year 7"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which revision combines these sentences most smoothly? ''The storm hit Coffs Harbour. It brought heavy rain. Many roads flooded.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The storm that hit Coffs Harbour brought such heavy rain that many roads flooded."},{"id":"b","text":"The storm hit Coffs Harbour, it brought heavy rain, many roads flooded."},{"id":"c","text":"The storm hit Coffs Harbour and it brought heavy rain and then many roads flooded."},{"id":"d","text":"The storm, hit Coffs Harbour bringing heavy rain, and many roads, flooded."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The first option joins the ideas with ''that'' and ''such... that'', showing cause and effect in one clear sentence. The second is a comma splice, the third overuses ''and'', and the fourth has commas in the wrong places.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000007130130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000071301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which is the correctly edited version of ''Me and Sam has went to the libary yesterday.''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Sam and I went to the library yesterday."},{"id":"b","text":"Me and Sam went to the library yesterday."},{"id":"c","text":"Sam and me has gone to the library yesterday."},{"id":"d","text":"Sam and I has went to the libary yesterday."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Three fixes: ''Sam and I'' (the subject form, with the other person first), ''went'' (not ''has went''), and the spelling ''library''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_easy', 1, 'A $80 jacket goes up in price by 10%. What is the new price?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$88"},{"id":"b","text":"$90"},{"id":"c","text":"$8"},{"id":"d","text":"$72"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '10% of $80 is $8 (divide by 10). The price goes up, so add it on: $80 + $8 = $88.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_easy', 1, 'To find the 10% GST on a price, you can divide the price by 10.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '10% means 10 out of 100, which is one tenth. Dividing by 10 gives one tenth of the price.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the table. How much profit is made?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["$15","15","15.00","$15.00"]'::jsonb, 'Profit = selling price − cost price = $55 − $40 = $15.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Item","Price"],"rows":[["Cost price","$40"],["Selling price","$55"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'easy', 1, 'A $60 pair of runners is 25% off. The sale price is $___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["45","45.00"]'::jsonb, '25% is one quarter. A quarter of $60 is $15. Take it off: $60 − $15 = $45.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these increase an amount by 20%? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Multiply it by 1.2"},{"id":"b","text":"Find 0.2 of it and add that on"},{"id":"c","text":"Multiply it by 0.8"},{"id":"d","text":"Add 20 to it"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing by 20% gives 100% + 20% = 120% of the amount, which is × 1.2. Finding 20% and adding it on does the same thing. × 0.8 is a 20% decrease, and adding 20 is not a percentage at all.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'easy', 1, 'The school canteen buys pies for $3.50 each and sells them for $3.00 each. What happens on each pie?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A loss of 50c"},{"id":"b","text":"A profit of 50c"},{"id":"c","text":"A loss of $3.00"},{"id":"d","text":"A profit of $6.50"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The canteen sells for less than it paid, so it is a loss. $3.50 − $3.00 = 50c.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'medium', 1, 'A cricket bat costs $150 before GST. What is its price including 10% GST?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["$165","165","165.00","$165.00"]'::jsonb, 'GST is 10% of $150, which is $15. Add it on: $150 + $15 = $165.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the graph. By what percentage was the price of the bike decreased?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["20%","20"]'::jsonb, 'The price fell by $500 − $400 = $100. As a percentage of the original price: 100 ÷ 500 × 100% = 20%.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Price of a bike ($)","columns":[{"label":"Before sale","value":500},{"label":"During sale","value":400}],"step":100}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'medium', 1, 'Put these steps in order to find the sale price of a $90 item with 30% off.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Find 10% of $90, which is $9"},{"id":"b","text":"Find 30%: 3 × $9 = $27"},{"id":"c","text":"Subtract: $90 − $27 = $63"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'First find 10%, then build up to 30%, then take the discount away from the original price.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'hard', 1, 'A phone costs $440 including GST. What was its price before GST was added?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["$400","400","400.00","$400.00"]'::jsonb, 'The price with GST is 110% of the price before GST, so divide by 1.1: $440 ÷ 1.1 = $400. Check: 10% of $400 is $40, and $400 + $40 = $440.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'hard', 1, 'Mia bought a surfboard for $250 and sold it for $300. What was her percentage profit?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"20%"},{"id":"b","text":"50%"},{"id":"c","text":"16.7%"},{"id":"d","text":"25%"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Profit = $300 − $250 = $50. Percentage profit is compared with what she paid: 50 ÷ 250 × 100% = 20%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'hard', 1, 'Increasing a price by 10% and then decreasing the new price by 10% brings it back to the original price.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Try $100. Up 10% gives $110. Down 10% of $110 is $11 off, giving $99, not $100. The second 10% is taken from a bigger amount.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_hard', 1, 'After a 20% discount, a tent costs $96. What was the original price?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["$120","120","120.00","$120.00"]'::jsonb, 'After 20% off you pay 80% of the original price. So 80% is $96, 1% is $1.20 and 100% is $120. Or: $96 ÷ 0.8 = $120.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_hard', 1, 'A shop buys a lamp for $50, marks the price up by 40%, then later sells it at 25% off the marked price. Which statements are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The marked price is $70"},{"id":"b","text":"The sale price is $52.50"},{"id":"c","text":"The shop makes a loss"},{"id":"d","text":"The profit is 5% of what the shop paid"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '40% of $50 is $20, so the marked price is $70. 25% of $70 is $17.50, so the sale price is $52.50. That is $2.50 more than $50, a profit, and 2.50 ÷ 50 × 100% = 5%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_hard', 1, 'The table shows how many members a netball club had. By what percentage did membership fall from June to December?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["20%","20"]'::jsonb, 'From June to December it fell by 300 − 240 = 60 members. Compare with June: 60 ÷ 300 × 100% = 20%.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Month","Members"],"rows":[["January",250],["June",300],["December",240]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_easy', 1, 'A cap costs $30 before GST. How much is the 10% GST?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["$3","3","3.00","$3.00"]'::jsonb, '10% is one tenth. One tenth of $30 is $30 ÷ 10 = $3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_easy', 1, 'A $40 shirt is 50% off. What is the sale price?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$20"},{"id":"b","text":"$35"},{"id":"c","text":"$10"},{"id":"d","text":"$4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '50% is half. Half of $40 is $20 off, so you pay $40 − $20 = $20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_easy', 1, 'If you sell something for less than you paid for it, you make a loss.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A loss means you got back less money than you spent. A profit means you got back more.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the table. What is the sale price of the skateboard?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["$102","102","102.00","$102.00"]'::jsonb, '10% of $120 is $12 and 5% is $6, so 15% is $18. The sale price is $120 − $18 = $102.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Skateboard","Amount"],"rows":[["Original price","$120"],["Discount","15%"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'easy', 1, 'Increasing $70 by 30% gives $___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["91","91.00"]'::jsonb, '10% of $70 is $7, so 30% is $21. Add it on: $70 + $21 = $91. Or: $70 × 1.3 = $91.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'easy', 1, 'Put these deals in order of final price, from cheapest to dearest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"$100 with 30% off"},{"id":"b","text":"$80 with 10% off"},{"id":"c","text":"$75 with no discount"},{"id":"d","text":"$90 with 5% off"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Work out each final price: $100 − $30 = $70, $80 − $8 = $72, $75, and $90 − $4.50 = $85.50.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'medium', 1, 'A school sausage sizzle spent $180 on supplies and took in $261. What was the percentage profit?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"45%"},{"id":"b","text":"31%"},{"id":"c","text":"81%"},{"id":"d","text":"55%"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Profit = $261 − $180 = $81. Compare it with the cost: 81 ÷ 180 × 100% = 45%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'medium', 1, 'A beach umbrella costs $70 before GST. Which of these give its price including 10% GST? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"$70 × 1.1"},{"id":"b","text":"$70 + $7"},{"id":"c","text":"$70 × 0.1"},{"id":"d","text":"$70 ÷ 1.1"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'The GST is 10% of $70, which is $7, so the price is $70 + $7 = $77. Multiplying by 1.1 (110%) gives the same answer. $70 × 0.1 is only the GST, and dividing by 1.1 takes GST off.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'medium', 1, 'A $200 scooter is on sale. The dot on the number line shows its sale price. What percentage discount is this?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30%","30"]'::jsonb, 'Each jump is 200 ÷ 10 = 20, so the dot is at 7 × 20 = 140. The discount is $200 − $140 = $60, and 60 ÷ 200 × 100% = 30%.', '{"kind":"number_line","from":0,"to":200,"jumps":10,"dotAt":7}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'hard', 1, 'A car bought for $24 000 is sold two years later for $18 000. What is the percentage loss?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["25%","25"]'::jsonb, 'The loss is $24 000 − $18 000 = $6000. Compare it with the buying price: 6000 ÷ 24 000 × 100% = 25%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'hard', 1, 'A 25% discount followed by another 20% discount is the same as a 45% discount.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Try $100. 25% off gives $75. 20% off $75 is $15 off, giving $60. That is 40% off in total, not 45%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'hard', 1, 'The price of a concert ticket rose from $80 to $92. What was the percentage increase?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"15%"},{"id":"b","text":"12%"},{"id":"c","text":"13%"},{"id":"d","text":"87%"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The increase is $92 − $80 = $12. Compare it with the old price: 12 ÷ 80 × 100% = 15%.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bookshop sells a novel for $33 including GST and pays the 10% GST to the government. The shop paid $20 for the book. How much profit does it make?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["$10","10","10.00","$10.00"]'::jsonb, 'The price before GST is $33 ÷ 1.1 = $30 (the other $3 is GST). Profit = $30 − $20 = $10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the graph. What was the percentage increase in koalas from 2024 to 2026?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["25%","25"]'::jsonb, 'The number went up by 100 − 80 = 20. Compare with the starting number: 20 ÷ 80 × 100% = 25%.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Koalas counted in a reserve","columns":[{"label":"2024","value":80},{"label":"2026","value":100}],"step":20}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080101', 'very_hard', 1, 'After a 15% pay rise, Jack earns $920 a week. What did he earn before the rise?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"$800"},{"id":"b","text":"$782"},{"id":"c","text":"$1058"},{"id":"d","text":"$905"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'After the rise he earns 115% of his old pay. So $920 ÷ 1.15 = $800. Check: 15% of $800 is $120, and $800 + $120 = $920.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_easy', 1, 'Simplify the ratio 12 : 18.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2 : 3"},{"id":"b","text":"3 : 2"},{"id":"c","text":"6 : 9"},{"id":"d","text":"4 : 6"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The highest common factor of 12 and 18 is 6. 12 ÷ 6 = 2 and 18 ÷ 6 = 3, so 12 : 18 = 2 : 3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the bar. What is the ratio of shaded parts to unshaded parts?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2 : 3"},{"id":"b","text":"2 : 5"},{"id":"c","text":"3 : 2"},{"id":"d","text":"3 : 5"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 2 shaded parts and 5 − 2 = 3 unshaded parts. Shaded to unshaded is 2 : 3.', '{"kind":"fraction","shape":"bar","parts":5,"shaded":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_easy', 1, 'A speed of 60 km/h means travelling 60 km in one hour.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'km/h means kilometres per hour, so 60 km/h is 60 kilometres every hour.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'easy', 1, 'A car travels 240 km in 3 hours. What is its average speed in km/h?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["80","80 km/h"]'::jsonb, 'Speed = distance ÷ time = 240 ÷ 3 = 80 km/h.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'easy', 1, 'Share $40 in the ratio 3 : 5. The larger share is $___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["25","25.00"]'::jsonb, 'There are 3 + 5 = 8 parts. One part is $40 ÷ 8 = $5. The larger share is 5 parts: 5 × $5 = $25.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'easy', 1, 'Which ratios are equivalent to 4 : 6? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2 : 3"},{"id":"b","text":"8 : 12"},{"id":"c","text":"6 : 4"},{"id":"d","text":"10 : 15"},{"id":"e","text":"4 : 9"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, '4 : 6 simplifies to 2 : 3. 8 : 12 and 10 : 15 also simplify to 2 : 3. 6 : 4 is the other way round, and 4 : 9 is different.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the table of rice prices. Which bag is the best buy?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"500 g"},{"id":"b","text":"750 g"},{"id":"c","text":"1 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'Find the price of 100 g in each: $4.00 ÷ 5 = 80c, $5.40 ÷ 7.5 = 72c and $7.50 ÷ 10 = 75c. The 750 g bag is cheapest per 100 g.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Bag of rice","Price"],"rows":[["500 g","$4.00"],["750 g","$5.40"],["1 kg","$7.50"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'medium', 1, 'Simplify the ratio 2.5 : 4.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5 : 8","5:8"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply both parts by 2 to remove the decimal: 5 : 8. 5 and 8 have no common factor, so it is fully simplified.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'medium', 1, 'Put these speeds in order from slowest to fastest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"15 km/h"},{"id":"b","text":"5 m/s"},{"id":"c","text":"20 km/h"},{"id":"d","text":"400 m/min"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to km/h. 5 m/s is 5 × 3600 = 18 000 m per hour, or 18 km/h. 400 m/min is 400 × 60 = 24 000 m per hour, or 24 km/h. So 15, 18, 20, 24.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'hard', 1, 'Simplify the ratio 45 minutes : 2 hours.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3 : 8","3:8"]'::jsonb, 'Use the same units first: 2 hours = 120 minutes. 45 : 120, then divide both by 15 to get 3 : 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the graph. At the same speed, how many hours would it take the cyclist to ride 75 km?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","5 hours","5 h"]'::jsonb, 'The graph goes up 15 km every hour, so the speed is 15 km/h. Time = distance ÷ speed = 75 ÷ 15 = 5 hours.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Distance ridden by a cyclist (km)","points":[{"label":"0 h","value":0},{"label":"1 h","value":15},{"label":"2 h","value":30},{"label":"3 h","value":45}],"step":15}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'hard', 1, 'Ava and Ben share 56 lamingtons in the ratio 3 : 4. How many more lamingtons does Ben get than Ava?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8"},{"id":"b","text":"1"},{"id":"c","text":"24"},{"id":"d","text":"14"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 3 + 4 = 7 parts, so one part is 56 ÷ 7 = 8. Ava gets 24 and Ben gets 32. Ben gets one part more: 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_hard', 1, 'A train travels at 90 km/h. How many minutes does it take to travel 60 km?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["40","40 minutes","40 min"]'::jsonb, 'Time = distance ÷ speed = 60 ÷ 90 = 2/3 of an hour. 2/3 of 60 minutes is 40 minutes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_hard', 1, 'The ratio of boys to girls in a class is 4 : 5. There are 12 boys. How many students are in the class?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"27"},{"id":"b","text":"15"},{"id":"c","text":"21"},{"id":"d","text":"36"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '4 parts are 12 boys, so one part is 3. Girls are 5 parts: 15. The class has 12 + 15 = 27 students.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_hard', 1, 'A runner covers 400 m in 50 seconds. Which statements are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Her speed is 8 m/s"},{"id":"b","text":"Her speed is 28.8 km/h"},{"id":"c","text":"At this speed she runs 1 km in 2 min 5 s"},{"id":"d","text":"Her speed is 20 km/h"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '400 ÷ 50 = 8 m/s. In an hour that is 8 × 3600 = 28 800 m, or 28.8 km/h. 1000 m ÷ 8 m/s = 125 s, which is 2 min 5 s.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_easy', 1, 'The ratio 10 : 15 in simplest form is 2 : ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"3"'::jsonb, 'Divide both parts by their highest common factor, 5: 10 ÷ 5 = 2 and 15 ÷ 5 = 3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the bag. What is the ratio of red counters to blue counters?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3 : 5"},{"id":"b","text":"5 : 3"},{"id":"c","text":"3 : 8"},{"id":"d","text":"5 : 8"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 3 red and 5 blue counters. Red comes first in the question, so the ratio is 3 : 5.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":3},{"colour":"blue","count":5}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_easy', 1, 'A tap fills 60 L in 4 minutes. How many litres does it fill per minute?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["15","15 L","15 L/min"]'::jsonb, 'Rate = 60 L ÷ 4 minutes = 15 L per minute.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'easy', 1, 'The ratio 3 : 7 is the same as 7 : 3.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Order matters in a ratio. 3 : 7 means 3 of the first thing for every 7 of the second; 7 : 3 is the other way round.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'easy', 1, 'Divide 72 kg of mulch in the ratio 5 : 3. What is the smaller share?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"27 kg"},{"id":"b","text":"45 kg"},{"id":"c","text":"24 kg"},{"id":"d","text":"9 kg"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 5 + 3 = 8 parts, so one part is 72 ÷ 8 = 9 kg. The smaller share is 3 parts: 3 × 9 = 27 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the grid. What is the ratio of shaded squares to unshaded squares, in simplest form?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2 : 3","2:3"]'::jsonb, 'There are 10 squares: 4 shaded and 6 unshaded. 4 : 6, divided by 2, is 2 : 3.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":5,"rows":2,"cells":[[0,0],[1,0],[2,0],[3,0]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'medium', 1, 'A cyclist rides at 24 km/h for 2.5 hours. How far does she ride, in kilometres?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["60","60 km"]'::jsonb, 'Distance = speed × time = 24 × 2.5 = 60 km.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are rates (comparing two different kinds of units)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"$4.50 per kg"},{"id":"b","text":"80 km/h"},{"id":"c","text":"3 boys to 5 girls"},{"id":"d","text":"72 heartbeats per minute"},{"id":"e","text":"2 cups of flour to 1 cup of sugar"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'A rate compares different units: dollars per kilogram, kilometres per hour, beats per minute. Boys to girls and cups to cups compare the same kind of thing, so they are ratios.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the table. Put the bottles in order from best buy to worst buy.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"2 L"},{"id":"b","text":"3 L"},{"id":"c","text":"1 L"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Find the price of 1 litre in each: 1 L is $3.20, 2 L is $5.80 ÷ 2 = $2.90, 3 L is $9.00 ÷ 3 = $3.00. Cheapest per litre is the best buy.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Juice bottle","Price"],"rows":[["1 L","$3.20"],["2 L","$5.80"],["3 L","$9.00"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'hard', 1, 'Simplify the ratio 1.2 m : 80 cm.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3 : 2"},{"id":"b","text":"2 : 3"},{"id":"c","text":"12 : 8"},{"id":"d","text":"1.2 : 80"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Use the same units: 1.2 m = 120 cm. 120 : 80, divided by 40, is 3 : 2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'hard', 1, 'Concrete is made from cement, sand and gravel in the ratio 1 : 2 : 3. How many kilograms of sand are in 300 kg of concrete?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["100","100 kg"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 parts, so one part is 300 ÷ 6 = 50 kg. Sand is 2 parts: 2 × 50 = 100 kg.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'hard', 1, 'Driving at 80 km/h for 45 minutes covers 60 km.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '45 minutes is 3/4 of an hour. Distance = 80 × 3/4 = 60 km.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the graph of a family''s car trip. What was the average speed for the whole trip from 9 am to 12 pm, in km/h?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["70","70 km/h"]'::jsonb, 'The family travelled 210 km in 3 hours, including the stop. Average speed = 210 ÷ 3 = 70 km/h.', '{"kind":"line_graph","title":"Distance from Sydney (km)","points":[{"label":"9 am","value":0},{"label":"10 am","value":90},{"label":"11 am","value":90},{"label":"12 pm","value":210}],"step":30}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_hard', 1, 'In a crate, the ratio of red apples to green apples is 5 : 2. There are 15 more red apples than green apples. How many apples are in the crate?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"35"'::jsonb, 'The difference is 5 − 2 = 3 parts, and that is 15 apples, so one part is 5. The crate holds 5 + 2 = 7 parts: 7 × 5 = 35 apples.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008010230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080102', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which is faster: 15 m/s or 50 km/h?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"15 m/s, because it equals 54 km/h"},{"id":"b","text":"50 km/h, because 50 is bigger than 15"},{"id":"c","text":"They are the same speed"},{"id":"d","text":"15 m/s, because it equals 90 km/h"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '15 m/s is 15 × 3600 = 54 000 m in an hour, which is 54 km/h. 54 km/h is faster than 50 km/h.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Expand 3(x + 4).', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3x + 12","3x+12","12 + 3x"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply each term inside the brackets by 3: 3 × x = 3x and 3 × 4 = 12. So 3(x + 4) = 3x + 12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Simplify 5a − 8a.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"−3a"},{"id":"b","text":"3a"},{"id":"c","text":"13a"},{"id":"d","text":"−13a"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'These are like terms. 5 − 8 = −3, so 5a − 8a = −3a.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_easy', 1, 'The highest common factor of 6x and 9 is 3.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The factors of 6 and 9 they share are 1 and 3, so the HCF of the numbers is 3. 9 has no x, so x is not a common factor.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. Which expression gives its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4x + 20"},{"id":"b","text":"4x + 5"},{"id":"c","text":"x + 20"},{"id":"d","text":"4x + 9"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area = length × width = 4(x + 5). Multiply both terms by 4: 4x + 20.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":8,"height":4,"top":"x + 5","side":"4"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'easy', 1, 'Factorise: 8y + 12 = 4(2y + ___)', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"3"'::jsonb, 'The HCF of 8y and 12 is 4. 8y ÷ 4 = 2y and 12 ÷ 4 = 3, so 8y + 12 = 4(2y + 3).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'easy', 1, 'Which expressions are equal to −2(x − 5)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"−2x + 10"},{"id":"b","text":"10 − 2x"},{"id":"c","text":"−2x − 10"},{"id":"d","text":"2x − 10"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '−2 × x = −2x and −2 × −5 = +10, so −2(x − 5) = −2x + 10, which can also be written 10 − 2x.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'medium', 1, 'Expand and simplify 2(x + 3) + 5x.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7x + 6","7x+6","6 + 7x"]'::jsonb, 'Expand: 2x + 6 + 5x. Collect the like terms 2x + 5x = 7x. Answer: 7x + 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'medium', 1, 'Factorise fully: 12ab − 18a', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6a(2b − 3)"},{"id":"b","text":"6(2ab − 3a)"},{"id":"c","text":"3a(4b − 6)"},{"id":"d","text":"6a(2b − 18)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The HCF of 12ab and 18a is 6a. 12ab ÷ 6a = 2b and 18a ÷ 6a = 3, so the answer is 6a(2b − 3). 6(2ab − 3a) and 3a(4b − 6) are equal to it, but still have a common factor left inside the brackets.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'medium', 1, 'Put the steps in order to simplify 4(2x − 1) − 3(x − 2).', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Expand the first bracket: 8x − 4"},{"id":"b","text":"Expand the second bracket: −3x + 6"},{"id":"c","text":"Group like terms: 8x − 3x − 4 + 6"},{"id":"d","text":"Simplify: 5x + 2"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Expand each bracket (−3 × −2 = +6), then collect the x terms and the number terms.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'hard', 1, 'Simplify x/3 + x/4.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7x/12"},{"id":"b","text":"2x/7"},{"id":"c","text":"x/12"},{"id":"d","text":"2x/12"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Use the common denominator 12: x/3 = 4x/12 and x/4 = 3x/12. Add the tops: 7x/12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the triangle. Which expression is its perimeter?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6x − 2"},{"id":"b","text":"6x + 4"},{"id":"c","text":"6x − 4"},{"id":"d","text":"5x − 2"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Add the three sides: 3x + (2x + 1) + (x − 3). The x terms make 6x and the numbers make 1 − 3 = −2. Perimeter = 6x − 2.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[11,1],[6,7]],"sideLabels":["3x","2x + 1","x − 3"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'hard', 1, 'Expand −x(x − 7).', 'short_answer', NULL, '["−x² + 7x","-x² + 7x","−x²+7x","-x²+7x","7x − x²","7x - x²","7x−x²","7x-x²"]'::jsonb, '−x × x = −x² and −x × −7 = +7x. So −x(x − 7) = −x² + 7x.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_hard', 1, '6x²y ÷ 3xy simplifies to 2x.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Divide the numbers: 6 ÷ 3 = 2. Cancel one x (x² ÷ x = x) and the y (y ÷ y = 1). That leaves 2x.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are correct ways to factorise −4x − 8? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"−4(x + 2)"},{"id":"b","text":"4(−x − 2)"},{"id":"c","text":"−4(x − 2)"},{"id":"d","text":"−2(2x + 4)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Expand each to check: −4(x + 2) = −4x − 8, 4(−x − 2) = −4x − 8 and −2(2x + 4) = −4x − 8. But −4(x − 2) = −4x + 8, which is wrong.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Simplify 2x/5 − (x − 1)/3.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(x + 5)/15"},{"id":"b","text":"(x − 5)/15"},{"id":"c","text":"(x − 1)/15"},{"id":"d","text":"(x + 1)/2"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The common denominator is 15: 2x/5 = 6x/15 and (x − 1)/3 = (5x − 5)/15. Subtract the whole top: 6x − (5x − 5) = 6x − 5x + 5 = x + 5. Answer: (x + 5)/15.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Expand: 5(y − 2) = 5y − ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"10"'::jsonb, 'Multiply each term by 5: 5 × y = 5y and 5 × 2 = 10. So 5(y − 2) = 5y − 10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_easy', 1, '−4m + 9m = 5m', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'These are like terms. −4 + 9 = 5, so −4m + 9m = 5m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the highest common factor of 10p and 15?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5"},{"id":"b","text":"10"},{"id":"c","text":"15"},{"id":"d","text":"5p"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The biggest number that divides both 10 and 15 is 5. 15 has no p, so p is not part of the HCF.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'easy', 1, 'Expand −2(a + 6).', 'short_answer', NULL, '["−2a − 12","-2a - 12","−2a−12","-2a-12"]'::jsonb, '−2 × a = −2a and −2 × 6 = −12. So −2(a + 6) = −2a − 12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. Which expression is its perimeter?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4y + 4"},{"id":"b","text":"4y + 2"},{"id":"c","text":"2y + 2"},{"id":"d","text":"6y − 3"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Perimeter = 2 × 3 + 2 × (2y − 1) = 6 + 4y − 2 = 4y + 4. (6y − 3 is the area, not the perimeter.)', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":4,"height":7,"top":"3","side":"2y − 1"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'easy', 1, 'When x = −2, put these expressions in order from smallest to largest value.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"3x"},{"id":"b","text":"x − 1"},{"id":"c","text":"−x"},{"id":"d","text":"x²"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = −2: 3x = −6, x − 1 = −3, −x = 2 and x² = (−2)² = 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'medium', 1, 'Which expressions simplify to 3a − 2b? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"5a − 2b − 2a"},{"id":"b","text":"a − 4b + 2a + 2b"},{"id":"c","text":"3a + 2b − 4b"},{"id":"d","text":"3a − 2b − b"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Collect like terms in each: 5a − 2a = 3a; a + 2a = 3a and −4b + 2b = −2b; 2b − 4b = −2b. The last one is 3a − 3b.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'medium', 1, 'Factorise 15x − 20.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5(3x − 4)","5(3x - 4)","5(3x-4)","5(3x−4)"]'::jsonb, 'The HCF of 15x and 20 is 5. 15x ÷ 5 = 3x and 20 ÷ 5 = 4, so 15x − 20 = 5(3x − 4).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'medium', 1, 'Simplify 3x/5 + x/5.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4x/5"},{"id":"b","text":"4x/10"},{"id":"c","text":"3x²/5"},{"id":"d","text":"4x/25"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The denominators are the same, so add the tops and keep the bottom: 3x + x = 4x, giving 4x/5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'hard', 1, 'Expand and simplify 3(2m − 1) − 2(m − 4).', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4m + 5","4m+5","5 + 4m"]'::jsonb, 'Expand: 6m − 3 − 2m + 8 (because −2 × −4 = +8). Collect: 6m − 2m = 4m and −3 + 8 = 5. Answer: 4m + 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. Which expression is its area, expanded?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2x² + 6x"},{"id":"b","text":"2x² + 3x"},{"id":"c","text":"2x + 6"},{"id":"d","text":"3x² + 6x"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area = 2x(x + 3). Multiply both terms by 2x: 2x × x = 2x² and 2x × 3 = 6x. Area = 2x² + 6x.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":8,"height":5,"top":"x + 3","side":"2x"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'hard', 1, 'Factorising 6a² − 9a fully gives 3a(2a − 3).', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The HCF of 6a² and 9a is 3a. 6a² ÷ 3a = 2a and 9a ÷ 3a = 3, so 6a² − 9a = 3a(2a − 3).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Simplify (x + 1)/2 + (x − 3)/4.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(3x − 1)/4"},{"id":"b","text":"(2x − 2)/6"},{"id":"c","text":"(3x − 5)/4"},{"id":"d","text":"(3x + 5)/4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Use the denominator 4: (x + 1)/2 = (2x + 2)/4. Add the tops: 2x + 2 + x − 3 = 3x − 1. Answer: (3x − 1)/4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_hard', 1, 'Expand and simplify: −3x(2x − 5) − x(4 − x) = −5x² + ___x', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"11"'::jsonb, '−3x(2x − 5) = −6x² + 15x and −x(4 − x) = −4x + x². Collect: −6x² + x² = −5x² and 15x − 4x = 11x.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080201', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangle has area 8x² − 12x. Which pairs could be its length and width? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"4x and 2x − 3"},{"id":"b","text":"2x and 4x − 6"},{"id":"c","text":"8x and x − 12"},{"id":"d","text":"4 and 2x² − 3x"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply each pair to check: 4x(2x − 3) = 8x² − 12x, 2x(4x − 6) = 8x² − 12x and 4(2x² − 3x) = 8x² − 12x. But 8x(x − 12) = 8x² − 96x.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Simplify x³ × x⁴.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"x⁷"},{"id":"b","text":"x¹²"},{"id":"c","text":"2x⁷"},{"id":"d","text":"2x¹²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'When you multiply powers with the same base, keep the base and add the indices: 3 + 4 = 7, so x³ × x⁴ = x⁷.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Any number except 0 raised to the power of 0 equals 1.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'This is the zero index law. For example 5³ ÷ 5³ = 5⁰, and any number divided by itself is 1, so 5⁰ = 1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_easy', 1, 'a⁹ ÷ a⁴ simplifies to a raised to the power ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"5"'::jsonb, 'When you divide powers with the same base, keep the base and subtract the indices: 9 − 4 = 5, so the answer is a⁵.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'easy', 1, 'Simplify (m²)⁵.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"m¹⁰"},{"id":"b","text":"m⁷"},{"id":"c","text":"m²⁵"},{"id":"d","text":"5m²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'For a power of a power, multiply the indices: 2 × 5 = 10, so (m²)⁵ = m¹⁰. Adding them to get m⁷ is a common mistake.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'easy', 1, 'Evaluate 5⁰ + 3⁰.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"2"'::jsonb, 'Any non-zero number to the power of 0 is 1. So 5⁰ = 1 and 3⁰ = 1, and 1 + 1 = 2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 2⁸? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2⁵ × 2³"},{"id":"b","text":"2¹⁰ ÷ 2²"},{"id":"c","text":"(2⁴)²"},{"id":"d","text":"2⁴ × 2²"},{"id":"e","text":"(2²)⁶"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '2⁵ × 2³ = 2⁸ (add 5 + 3), 2¹⁰ ÷ 2² = 2⁸ (subtract 10 − 2) and (2⁴)² = 2⁸ (multiply 4 × 2). 2⁴ × 2² = 2⁶ and (2²)⁶ = 2¹².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'medium', 1, 'Simplify 3a⁴ × 5a².', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"15a⁶"},{"id":"b","text":"8a⁶"},{"id":"c","text":"15a⁸"},{"id":"d","text":"8a⁸"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Multiply the numbers: 3 × 5 = 15. Then add the indices of a: 4 + 2 = 6. So the answer is 15a⁶.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the table. Use the index laws to write 4 × 32 as a single power of 2.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2⁷"},{"id":"b","text":"2¹⁰"},{"id":"c","text":"2⁶"},{"id":"d","text":"2⁸"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'From the table, 4 = 2² and 32 = 2⁵. Multiplying powers means adding indices: 2² × 2⁵ = 2⁷. Check: 4 × 32 = 128 = 2⁷.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Power of 2","Value"],"rows":[["2¹",2],["2²",4],["2³",8],["2⁴",16],["2⁵",32],["2⁶",64]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'medium', 1, 'Put these steps for simplifying (y³)⁴ ÷ y⁵ in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"(y³)⁴ = y¹²"},{"id":"b","text":"Now work out y¹² ÷ y⁵"},{"id":"c","text":"Subtract the indices: 12 − 5 = 7"},{"id":"d","text":"The answer is y⁷"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Deal with the power of a power first by multiplying 3 × 4 = 12. Then divide by subtracting the indices: 12 − 5 = 7, giving y⁷.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'hard', 1, 'Simplify 20x⁷ ÷ 4x³.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5x⁴"},{"id":"b","text":"16x⁴"},{"id":"c","text":"5x¹⁰"},{"id":"d","text":"80x⁴"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Divide the numbers: 20 ÷ 4 = 5. Subtract the indices of x: 7 − 3 = 4. So the answer is 5x⁴.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'hard', 1, '(3x)⁰ = 3', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The brackets mean the whole of 3x is raised to the power 0, so (3x)⁰ = 1. It is 3x⁰ (no brackets) that equals 3 × 1 = 3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'hard', 1, 'If 5ⁿ × 5³ = 5¹¹, then n = ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["8","n = 8"]'::jsonb, 'Multiplying powers adds the indices, so n + 3 = 11. That means n = 11 − 3 = 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Simplify (2a³)² × a⁴.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4a¹⁰"},{"id":"b","text":"2a¹⁰"},{"id":"c","text":"4a⁹"},{"id":"d","text":"4a²⁴"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '(2a³)² means 2a³ × 2a³ = 4a⁶. The 2 gets squared too. Then 4a⁶ × a⁴ = 4a¹⁰ by adding the indices 6 + 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Without a calculator, evaluate (3⁴ × 3⁵) ÷ 3⁷.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"9"'::jsonb, '3⁴ × 3⁵ = 3⁹ (add 4 + 5). Then 3⁹ ÷ 3⁷ = 3² (subtract 9 − 7). 3² = 9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these simplify to 6p⁵? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2p² × 3p³"},{"id":"b","text":"12p⁸ ÷ 2p³"},{"id":"c","text":"6p⁵ × p⁰"},{"id":"d","text":"(6p)⁵"},{"id":"e","text":"2p × 3p⁵"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '2p² × 3p³ = 6p⁵. 12p⁸ ÷ 2p³ = 6p⁵. 6p⁵ × p⁰ = 6p⁵ × 1 = 6p⁵. But (6p)⁵ = 7776p⁵ and 2p × 3p⁵ = 6p⁶.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Simplify y² × y⁶.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"y⁸"},{"id":"b","text":"y¹²"},{"id":"c","text":"2y⁸"},{"id":"d","text":"y⁴"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Same base, multiplying: keep the base and add the indices. 2 + 6 = 8, so y² × y⁶ = y⁸.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_easy', 1, 'k¹⁰ ÷ k² simplifies to k raised to the power ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"8"'::jsonb, 'Same base, dividing: keep the base and subtract the indices. 10 − 2 = 8, so the answer is k⁸.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_easy', 1, 'm⁰ = 0 for any number m that is not zero.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Any non-zero number to the power of 0 equals 1, not 0. So m⁰ = 1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'easy', 1, 'Evaluate 4⁰ × 9.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"9"'::jsonb, '4⁰ = 1 because any non-zero number to the power of 0 is 1. Then 1 × 9 = 9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'easy', 1, 'Simplify (b³)³.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"b⁹"},{"id":"b","text":"b⁶"},{"id":"c","text":"b²⁷"},{"id":"d","text":"3b³"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'For a power of a power, multiply the indices: 3 × 3 = 9, so (b³)³ = b⁹.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'easy', 1, 'Put these in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"2⁰"},{"id":"b","text":"2³"},{"id":"c","text":"3²"},{"id":"d","text":"2⁴"},{"id":"e","text":"5²"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'Work out each one: 2⁰ = 1, 2³ = 8, 3² = 9, 2⁴ = 16 and 5² = 25.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'medium', 1, 'Simplify 6x⁵ ÷ 2x².', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3x³"},{"id":"b","text":"4x³"},{"id":"c","text":"3x⁷"},{"id":"d","text":"12x³"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Divide the numbers: 6 ÷ 2 = 3. Subtract the indices of x: 5 − 2 = 3. So the answer is 3x³.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 1? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"7⁰"},{"id":"b","text":"100⁰"},{"id":"c","text":"1⁵"},{"id":"d","text":"0¹"},{"id":"e","text":"(−4)⁰"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Any non-zero number to the power of 0 is 1, so 7⁰, 100⁰ and (−4)⁰ are all 1. 1⁵ = 1 × 1 × 1 × 1 × 1 = 1. But 0¹ = 0.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'medium', 1, 'If (w⁴)ᵏ = w²⁰, then k = ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["5","k = 5"]'::jsonb, 'For a power of a power you multiply the indices, so 4 × k = 20. That means k = 20 ÷ 4 = 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'hard', 1, 'Simplify 4m³n² × 2mn⁵.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"8m⁴n⁷"},{"id":"b","text":"6m⁴n⁷"},{"id":"c","text":"8m³n¹⁰"},{"id":"d","text":"8m⁴n¹⁰"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Multiply the numbers: 4 × 2 = 8. Add the indices for each letter: m³ × m¹ = m⁴ and n² × n⁵ = n⁷. So the answer is 8m⁴n⁷.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the table. Use the index laws to work out 243 ÷ 9.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"27"'::jsonb, 'From the table, 243 = 3⁵ and 9 = 3². Dividing means subtracting indices: 3⁵ ÷ 3² = 3³, and the table shows 3³ = 27.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Power of 3","Value"],"rows":[["3¹",3],["3²",9],["3³",27],["3⁴",81],["3⁵",243]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'hard', 1, 'a³ + a³ = a⁶', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The index laws are for multiplying and dividing, not adding. a³ + a³ is two lots of a³, so it equals 2a³. It is a³ × a³ that equals a⁶.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Simplify (x⁵ × x³) ÷ (x²)³.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"x²"},{"id":"b","text":"x⁵"},{"id":"c","text":"x¹⁰"},{"id":"d","text":"x⁰"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Top: x⁵ × x³ = x⁸. Bottom: (x²)³ = x⁶. Then x⁸ ÷ x⁶ = x², by subtracting 8 − 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Without a calculator, evaluate 6⁹ ÷ (6⁴ × 6³).', 'short_answer', NULL, '"36"'::jsonb, '6⁴ × 6³ = 6⁷. Then 6⁹ ÷ 6⁷ = 6², and 6² = 36.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Simplify 18a⁶b⁴ ÷ 3a²b.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"6a⁴b³"},{"id":"b","text":"6a³b⁴"},{"id":"c","text":"15a⁴b³"},{"id":"d","text":"6a⁴b⁴"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Divide the numbers: 18 ÷ 3 = 6. Subtract indices for each letter: a⁶ ÷ a² = a⁴, and b⁴ ÷ b¹ = b³ (b on its own means b¹). So the answer is 6a⁴b³.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_easy', 1, 'Solve 3(x + 2) = 21.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","x = 5","x=5"]'::jsonb, 'Divide both sides by 3: x + 2 = 7. Then subtract 2: x = 5. Check: 3 × (5 + 2) = 21.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_easy', 1, 'Solve 5x = 2x + 12.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"x = 4"},{"id":"b","text":"x = 12"},{"id":"c","text":"x = 3"},{"id":"d","text":"x = 6"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Subtract 2x from both sides to get the pronumerals on one side: 3x = 12. Then divide by 3: x = 4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_easy', 1, 'x = 6 is a solution of x/2 + 1 = 4.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = 6: 6/2 + 1 = 3 + 1 = 4. Both sides are equal, so x = 6 is a solution.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'easy', 1, 'If 4(y − 3) = 20, then y = ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["8","y = 8"]'::jsonb, 'Divide both sides by 4: y − 3 = 5. Then add 3: y = 8. Check: 4 × (8 − 3) = 20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'easy', 1, 'Which numbers are solutions of x² = 49? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"7"},{"id":"b","text":"−7"},{"id":"c","text":"24.5"},{"id":"d","text":"49"},{"id":"e","text":"−49"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '7 × 7 = 49 and −7 × −7 = 49, so both 7 and −7 work. 24.5 is 49 ÷ 2, which is a common mistake.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'easy', 1, 'Put these steps for solving 3(x − 4) = 2x + 1 in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Expand: 3x − 12 = 2x + 1"},{"id":"b","text":"Subtract 2x: x − 12 = 1"},{"id":"c","text":"Add 12: x = 13"},{"id":"d","text":"Check: 3 × 9 = 27 and 26 + 1 = 27"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'First expand the brackets, then collect the x terms on one side, then get x on its own, and finally check by substituting x = 13 into both sides.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'medium', 1, 'Solve 7a − 4 = 3a + 16.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","a = 5","a=5"]'::jsonb, 'Subtract 3a from both sides: 4a − 4 = 16. Add 4: 4a = 20. Divide by 4: a = 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'medium', 1, 'Solve (x + 4)/3 = 5.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"x = 11"},{"id":"b","text":"x = 1"},{"id":"c","text":"x = 19"},{"id":"d","text":"x = 3"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Multiply both sides by 3 first: x + 4 = 15. Then subtract 4: x = 11. Check: (11 + 4)/3 = 15/3 = 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'medium', 1, 'Mia thinks of a number, doubles it and adds 9. Her answer is 35. What was her number?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"13"'::jsonb, 'Write the equation 2n + 9 = 35. Subtract 9: 2n = 26. Divide by 2: n = 13.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'hard', 1, 'Solve 5(x − 2) = 3(x + 4).', 'short_answer', NULL, '["11","x = 11","x=11"]'::jsonb, 'Expand both sides: 5x − 10 = 3x + 12. Subtract 3x: 2x − 10 = 12. Add 10: 2x = 22. So x = 11.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. Its perimeter is 46 cm. Find x.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"x = 8"},{"id":"b","text":"x = 9.75"},{"id":"c","text":"x = 16"},{"id":"d","text":"x = 10"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Perimeter = 2 × (length + width) = 2(x + 7 + x) = 4x + 14. So 4x + 14 = 46, 4x = 32 and x = 8. Check: 15 + 8 + 15 + 8 = 46.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":5,"height":3,"top":"(x + 7) cm","side":"x cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'hard', 1, 'The equation x² = 64 has two solutions, 8 and −8.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '8 × 8 = 64 and −8 × −8 = 64 (a negative times a negative is positive), so both are solutions.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Solve (2x − 1)/3 = (x + 4)/2.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["14","x = 14","x=14"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply both sides by 6 (or cross-multiply): 2(2x − 1) = 3(x + 4). Expand: 4x − 2 = 3x + 12. Subtract 3x and add 2: x = 14. Check: 27/3 = 9 and 18/2 = 9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_hard', 1, 'At a Penrith sausage sizzle, sausages cost $3 each and drinks cost $2 each. Liam bought 4 more drinks than sausages and paid $38. How many sausages did he buy?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["6","6 sausages"]'::jsonb, 'Let s be the number of sausages, so he bought s + 4 drinks. 3s + 2(s + 4) = 38, so 5s + 8 = 38, 5s = 30 and s = 6. Check: 6 sausages cost $18 and 10 drinks cost $20, total $38.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which equations have the solution x = −3? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2x + 9 = 3"},{"id":"b","text":"x² = 9"},{"id":"c","text":"5 − x = 2"},{"id":"d","text":"4(x + 5) = 8"},{"id":"e","text":"x/3 = 1"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = −3. 2 × (−3) + 9 = 3 ✓. (−3)² = 9 ✓. 5 − (−3) = 8, not 2. 4 × (−3 + 5) = 4 × 2 = 8 ✓. −3/3 = −1, not 1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_easy', 1, 'If 2(x + 5) = 18, then x = ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["4","x = 4"]'::jsonb, 'Divide both sides by 2: x + 5 = 9. Then subtract 5: x = 4. Check: 2 × (4 + 5) = 18.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_easy', 1, 'Solve 6m = m + 20.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"m = 4"},{"id":"b","text":"m = 5"},{"id":"c","text":"m = 20"},{"id":"d","text":"m = 3"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Subtract m from both sides: 5m = 20. Divide by 5: m = 4. Check: 6 × 4 = 24 and 4 + 20 = 24.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_easy', 1, 'x = 10 is a solution of x/5 − 1 = 3.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = 10: 10/5 − 1 = 2 − 1 = 1, not 3. So x = 10 is not a solution. (The solution is x = 20.)', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'easy', 1, 'Solve n/4 = 9.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["36","n = 36","n=36"]'::jsonb, 'n is being divided by 4, so do the opposite: multiply both sides by 4. n = 9 × 4 = 36.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'easy', 1, 'Solve 5(p − 2) = 35.', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"p = 9"},{"id":"b","text":"p = 5"},{"id":"c","text":"p = 7.4"},{"id":"d","text":"p = 7"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Divide both sides by 5: p − 2 = 7. Then add 2: p = 9. Check: 5 × (9 − 2) = 35.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'easy', 1, 'Which numbers are solutions of y² = 81? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"9"},{"id":"b","text":"−9"},{"id":"c","text":"40.5"},{"id":"d","text":"81"},{"id":"e","text":"3"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '9 × 9 = 81 and −9 × −9 = 81, so y = 9 or y = −9. 40.5 is 81 ÷ 2, a common mistake.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'medium', 1, 'Solve 9k + 2 = 4k + 27.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","k = 5","k=5"]'::jsonb, 'Subtract 4k from both sides: 5k + 2 = 27. Subtract 2: 5k = 25. Divide by 5: k = 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the triangle. Find the value of x.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["30","x = 30"]'::jsonb, 'The angles in a triangle add to 180°, so x + 2x + 3x = 180. That gives 6x = 180, so x = 30. The angles are 30°, 60° and 90°.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[1,1],[11,1],[3.5,5.33]],"cornerLabels":["2x°","x°","3x°"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'medium', 1, 'Ava is 3 times as old as her brother. Their ages add to 48. How old is her brother?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12 years"]'::jsonb, 'Let her brother be b years old, so Ava is 3b. b + 3b = 48, so 4b = 48 and b = 12. Check: 12 + 36 = 48.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'hard', 1, 'Solve 4(2x + 1) = 6(x + 3).', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7","x = 7","x=7"]'::jsonb, 'Expand both sides: 8x + 4 = 6x + 18. Subtract 6x: 2x + 4 = 18. Subtract 4: 2x = 14. So x = 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'hard', 1, 'Put these steps for solving (x − 4)/5 + 3 = 7 in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Subtract 3: (x − 4)/5 = 4"},{"id":"b","text":"Multiply by 5: x − 4 = 20"},{"id":"c","text":"Add 4: x = 24"},{"id":"d","text":"Check: 20/5 + 3 = 7"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Undo the operations in reverse order: first take away the 3, then undo the dividing by 5, then undo the subtracting 4. Finally check the answer.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the rectangle. Its perimeter is 54 m. Find x.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","x = 8"]'::jsonb, 'Perimeter = 2 × (2x + 3 + x) = 6x + 6. So 6x + 6 = 54, 6x = 48 and x = 8. Check: the sides are 19 m and 8 m, and 19 + 8 + 19 + 8 = 54.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":6,"height":2.5,"top":"(2x + 3) m","side":"x m"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Solve (3x + 2)/4 = (x + 6)/2.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["10","x = 10","x=10"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply both sides by 4: 3x + 2 = 2(x + 6) = 2x + 12. Subtract 2x: x + 2 = 12, so x = 10. Check: 32/4 = 8 and 16/2 = 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Netball tickets cost $12 for adults and $7 for children. A group had 3 more children than adults and paid $97 altogether. How many adults were in the group?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4","4 adults"]'::jsonb, 'Let a be the number of adults, so there were a + 3 children. 12a + 7(a + 3) = 97, so 19a + 21 = 97, 19a = 76 and a = 4. Check: 4 × $12 + 7 × $7 = $48 + $49 = $97.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080203', 'very_hard', 1, 'A square has an area of 144 cm². Which is the correct equation and answer for its side length s?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"s² = 144, so s = 12 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"4s = 144, so s = 36 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"s² = 144, so s = 12 cm or −12 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"2s = 144, so s = 72 cm"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area of a square is side × side, so s² = 144. The equation has solutions 12 and −12, but a length can''t be negative, so s = 12 cm. 4s is the perimeter, not the area.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020401', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_easy', 1, 'In which quadrant is the point (−3, 4)?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"First quadrant"},{"id":"b","text":"Second quadrant"},{"id":"c","text":"Third quadrant"},{"id":"d","text":"Fourth quadrant"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, 'x is negative (left of the y-axis) and y is positive (above the x-axis). Top left is the second quadrant.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020402', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_easy', 1, 'What are the coordinates of point A?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(5, 2)"},{"id":"b","text":"(2, 5)"},{"id":"c","text":"(5, 5)"},{"id":"d","text":"(2, 2)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Go across the x-axis first: A is 5 across. Then go up: A is 2 up. So A is (5, 2). The x-coordinate always comes first.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":5,"y":2,"label":"A"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020403', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_easy', 1, 'The point (0, −6) lies on the y-axis.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Its x-coordinate is 0, so it is not left or right of the y-axis. It sits on the y-axis, 6 units below the origin.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020404', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the table of values. If the pattern continues, what is y when x = 4?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"9"'::jsonb, 'Each time x goes up by 1, y goes up by 2. So when x = 4, y = 7 + 2 = 9. (The rule is y = 2x + 1.)', '{"kind":"table","headings":["x","y"],"rows":[["0",1],["1",3],["2",5],["3",7]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020405', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'easy', 1, 'For the rule y = 2x − 3, when x = −1, y = ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["−5","-5"]'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = −1: y = 2 × (−1) − 3 = −2 − 3 = −5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020406', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'easy', 1, 'Which points are in the fourth quadrant? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"(4, −2)"},{"id":"b","text":"(−4, 2)"},{"id":"c","text":"(1, −7)"},{"id":"d","text":"(−3, −3)"},{"id":"e","text":"(6, 0)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, 'The fourth quadrant is bottom right: x positive and y negative. (4, −2) and (1, −7) fit. (6, 0) sits on the x-axis, so it is in no quadrant.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020407', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the table of values. Which rule does it follow?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"y = 3x + 1"},{"id":"b","text":"y = x + 3"},{"id":"c","text":"y = 3x − 1"},{"id":"d","text":"y = 4x"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'y goes up by 3 each time x goes up by 1, so the rule starts y = 3x. When x = 0, y = 1, so add 1: y = 3x + 1. Check x = −1: 3 × (−1) + 1 = −2.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["x","y"],"rows":[["−1","−2"],["0","1"],["1","4"],["2","7"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020408', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'medium', 1, 'The point (2, 9) lies on the line y = 4x + 1.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = 2: y = 4 × 2 + 1 = 9. That matches the y-coordinate, so the point is on the line.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020409', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the straight-line graph. What is the value of y when x = 3?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4","y = 4"]'::jsonb, 'Go across to 3 on the x-axis, then up to the line. The line is at 4 there. (The line''s rule is y = x + 1.)', '{"kind":"grid","columns":7,"rows":7,"axes":true,"path":[[0,1],[5,6]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020410', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'hard', 1, 'At which point do the lines y = 2x + 1 and y = x + 4 cross?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(3, 7)"},{"id":"b","text":"(7, 3)"},{"id":"c","text":"(1, 3)"},{"id":"d","text":"(5, 9)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Where they cross, the y-values are equal: 2x + 1 = x + 4, so x = 3. Then y = 2 × 3 + 1 = 7. Check in the other line: 3 + 4 = 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020411', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'hard', 1, 'Put these steps for drawing the graph of y = x − 2 in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Choose some x-values for a table"},{"id":"b","text":"Use the rule to work out each y-value"},{"id":"c","text":"Plot each (x, y) point on the axes"},{"id":"d","text":"Rule a straight line through the points"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'First make a table of values using the rule, then plot the points, and finally join them with a ruled straight line.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020412', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'hard', 1, 'The point (k, 11) lies on the line y = 3x − 4. What is the value of k?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5","k = 5"]'::jsonb, 'Put y = 11 into the rule: 11 = 3k − 4. Add 4: 15 = 3k. Divide by 3: k = 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020413', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the table of values. Which rule does it follow?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"y = 9 − 2x"},{"id":"b","text":"y = 2x + 5"},{"id":"c","text":"y = −2x + 7"},{"id":"d","text":"y = x + 6"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'y goes down by 2 each time x goes up by 1, so the rule has −2x. Going back one step to x = 0 gives y = 9. So y = 9 − 2x. Check x = 3: 9 − 6 = 3.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["x","y"],"rows":[["1",7],["2",5],["3",3],["4",1]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020414', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which points lie on the line y = −3x + 2? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"(0, 2)"},{"id":"b","text":"(1, −1)"},{"id":"c","text":"(−2, 8)"},{"id":"d","text":"(2, −5)"},{"id":"e","text":"(−1, −1)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Substitute each x. x = 0 gives 2 ✓. x = 1 gives −1 ✓. x = −2 gives 6 + 2 = 8 ✓. x = 2 gives −4, not −5. x = −1 gives 5, not −1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020415', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_hard', 1, 'The lines y = x + 5 and y = 11 − x cross at the point P. What is the y-coordinate of P?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","y = 8"]'::jsonb, 'At P the y-values are equal: x + 5 = 11 − x, so 2x = 6 and x = 3. Then y = 3 + 5 = 8. Check: 11 − 3 = 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020416', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which point is in the third quadrant?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−2, −5)"},{"id":"b","text":"(2, −5)"},{"id":"c","text":"(−2, 5)"},{"id":"d","text":"(2, 5)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The third quadrant is bottom left, where both x and y are negative. Only (−2, −5) has both coordinates negative.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020417', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the grid. Which point is at (4, 1)?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"B"},{"id":"b","text":"A"},{"id":"c","text":"C"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Go 4 across the x-axis first, then 1 up. That is point B. Point A is at (1, 4), with the numbers swapped.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":6,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":1,"y":4,"label":"A"},{"x":4,"y":1,"label":"B"},{"x":4,"y":4,"label":"C"}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020418', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_easy', 1, 'The point (−5, 0) lies on the x-axis.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Its y-coordinate is 0, so it is neither above nor below the x-axis. It sits on the x-axis, 5 units left of the origin.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020419', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'easy', 1, 'For the rule y = 5 − x, when x = 7, y = ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["−2","-2"]'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = 7: y = 5 − 7 = −2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020420', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the table of values. Which rule does it follow?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"y = 2x + 4"},{"id":"b","text":"y = x + 4"},{"id":"c","text":"y = 4x + 2"},{"id":"d","text":"y = 2x"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'y goes up by 2 each time x goes up by 1, so the rule starts y = 2x. When x = 0, y = 4, so y = 2x + 4. Check x = 3: 6 + 4 = 10.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["x","y"],"rows":[["0",4],["1",6],["2",8],["3",10]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020421', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'easy', 1, 'The point (3, 2) is the same as the point (2, 3).', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Order matters. (3, 2) is 3 across and 2 up, but (2, 3) is 2 across and 3 up, so they are different points.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020422', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'medium', 1, 'Which points lie on the y-axis? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"(0, 4)"},{"id":"b","text":"(0, −7)"},{"id":"c","text":"(4, 0)"},{"id":"d","text":"(−7, 7)"},{"id":"e","text":"(0, 0)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, 'Points on the y-axis have an x-coordinate of 0. That is (0, 4), (0, −7) and the origin (0, 0). (4, 0) is on the x-axis.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020423', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the straight-line graph. Which rule does it show?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"y = 6 − x"},{"id":"b","text":"y = x + 6"},{"id":"c","text":"y = 6x"},{"id":"d","text":"y = x − 6"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The line passes through (0, 6), (3, 3) and (6, 0). In each point x + y = 6, so y = 6 − x. As x goes up, y goes down.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":7,"rows":7,"axes":true,"path":[[0,6],[6,0]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020424', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'medium', 1, 'The point (4, m) lies on the line y = 2x − 9. What is the value of m?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["−1","-1","m = −1","m = -1"]'::jsonb, 'Substitute x = 4: y = 2 × 4 − 9 = 8 − 9 = −1. So m = −1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020425', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'hard', 1, 'These points all lie on the line y = 2x − 1. Put them in order from lowest to highest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"(−2, −5)"},{"id":"b","text":"(−1, −3)"},{"id":"c","text":"(0, −1)"},{"id":"d","text":"(3, 5)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'For y = 2x − 1, a bigger x gives a bigger y, so the line rises from left to right. The y-values in order are −5, −3, −1 and 5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020426', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the table of values. Which rule does it follow?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"y = 1 − 3x"},{"id":"b","text":"y = 3x + 1"},{"id":"c","text":"y = −3x − 2"},{"id":"d","text":"y = x + 9"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'y goes down by 3 each time x goes up by 1, so the rule has −3x. When x = 0, y = 1. So y = 1 − 3x. Check x = −2: 1 + 6 = 7.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["x","y"],"rows":[["−2","7"],["−1","4"],["0","1"],["1","−2"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020427', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'hard', 1, 'At which point do the lines y = 3x − 2 and y = x + 6 meet?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(4, 10)"},{"id":"b","text":"(10, 4)"},{"id":"c","text":"(2, 4)"},{"id":"d","text":"(4, 8)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Set the rules equal: 3x − 2 = x + 6, so 2x = 8 and x = 4. Then y = 4 + 6 = 10. Check: 3 × 4 − 2 = 10.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020428', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the graph of y = 2x + 1. The line y = 7 − x is drawn on the same axes. At which point do the two lines cross?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"(2, 5)"},{"id":"b","text":"(5, 2)"},{"id":"c","text":"(3, 7)"},{"id":"d","text":"(1, 6)"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Set the rules equal: 2x + 1 = 7 − x, so 3x = 6 and x = 2. Then y = 2 × 2 + 1 = 5. The graph goes through (2, 5), and 7 − 2 = 5 too. (3, 7) is only on the first line and (1, 6) only on the second.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":8,"rows":10,"axes":true,"path":[[0,1],[4,9]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020429', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_hard', 1, 'A Newcastle taxi charges $4 to start plus $2 per kilometre, so the cost is C = 2d + 4 dollars for d kilometres. How many kilometres long is a trip that costs $30?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["13","13 km"]'::jsonb, 'Put C = 30 into the rule: 30 = 2d + 4. Subtract 4: 26 = 2d. Divide by 2: d = 13 km.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008020430', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080204', 'very_hard', 1, 'The points (−1, 5), (1, 1) and (3, −3) all lie on one straight line.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Each time x goes up by 2, y goes down by 4, so the points change at a steady rate. They all fit y = 3 − 2x: 3 + 2 = 5, 3 − 2 = 1 and 3 − 6 = −3.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_easy', 1, 'This triangle has a right angle at the bottom right corner. Which side is the hypotenuse?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"p"},{"id":"b","text":"q"},{"id":"c","text":"r"},{"id":"d","text":"It has no hypotenuse"}]'::jsonb, '"c"'::jsonb, 'The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle. The right angle is at the bottom right, so the side across from it is r, the sloping side.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[8,6]],"sideLabels":["p","q","r"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_easy', 1, 'The hypotenuse is always the longest side of a right-angled triangle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The hypotenuse is opposite the right angle, which is the biggest angle in the triangle, so it is always the longest side.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_easy', 1, 'A right-angled triangle has shorter sides a and b and hypotenuse c. Pythagoras'' theorem says a² + b² = ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["c²","c^2","c2","c squared"]'::jsonb, 'Pythagoras'' theorem: the squares of the two shorter sides add up to the square of the hypotenuse, so a² + b² = c².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the right-angled triangle. How long is side x?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["10","10 cm"]'::jsonb, 'x is the hypotenuse. x² = 8² + 6² = 64 + 36 = 100, so x = √100 = 10 cm.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[8,6]],"sideLabels":["8 cm","6 cm","x"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'easy', 1, 'A right-angled triangle has shorter sides of 5 m and 12 m. How long is the hypotenuse?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"13 m"},{"id":"b","text":"17 m"},{"id":"c","text":"7 m"},{"id":"d","text":"169 m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'c² = 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169, so c = √169 = 13 m. 17 m just adds the sides, and 169 forgets the square root.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are Pythagorean triads? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"3, 4, 5"},{"id":"b","text":"5, 12, 13"},{"id":"c","text":"4, 5, 6"},{"id":"d","text":"8, 15, 17"},{"id":"e","text":"2, 3, 4"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Check if the two smaller squares add to the biggest square. 9 + 16 = 25 ✓, 25 + 144 = 169 ✓, 64 + 225 = 289 ✓. But 16 + 25 = 41, not 36, and 4 + 9 = 13, not 16.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the right-angled triangle. How long is side x?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["15","15 cm"]'::jsonb, '17 cm is the hypotenuse, so x is a shorter side. x² = 17² − 8² = 289 − 64 = 225, so x = √225 = 15 cm.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[7.5,0],[7.5,4]],"sideLabels":["x","8 cm","17 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'medium', 1, 'A right-angled triangle has shorter sides of 4 cm and 7 cm. Find the hypotenuse, correct to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8.1","8.1 cm"]'::jsonb, 'c² = 4² + 7² = 16 + 49 = 65, so c = √65 ≈ 8.06, which rounds to 8.1 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'medium', 1, 'A triangle with sides 7 cm, 24 cm and 25 cm is right-angled.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Test with Pythagoras: 7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625, and 25² = 625. They are equal, so the triangle is right-angled.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'hard', 1, 'A 5 m ladder leans against a wall. Its foot is 1.4 m from the wall. How high up the wall does it reach?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4.8","4.8 m"]'::jsonb, 'The ladder is the hypotenuse. h² = 5² − 1.4² = 25 − 1.96 = 23.04, so h = √23.04 = 4.8 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'hard', 1, 'Put these steps in order to find a shorter side of a right-angled triangle.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Find the hypotenuse (opposite the right angle)"},{"id":"b","text":"Square the hypotenuse and the known side"},{"id":"c","text":"Subtract the smaller square from the larger one"},{"id":"d","text":"Take the square root of the result"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'First spot the hypotenuse. Then square both known sides, subtract (because the shorter side''s square is what''s left over), and finally square root to get the length.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'hard', 1, 'Is a triangle with sides 6 cm, 9 cm and 11 cm right-angled?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Yes, because 6² + 9² = 11²"},{"id":"b","text":"No, because 6² + 9² = 117 but 11² = 121"},{"id":"c","text":"Yes, because 6 + 9 is more than 11"},{"id":"d","text":"No, because 11 is an odd number"}]'::jsonb, '"b"'::jsonb, '6² + 9² = 36 + 81 = 117 and 11² = 121. They are not equal, so there is no right angle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Mia crosses this rectangular oval diagonally instead of walking along two sides. How many metres shorter is her walk?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["40","40 m"]'::jsonb, 'Along two sides is 120 + 50 = 170 m. The diagonal is √(120² + 50²) = √(14 400 + 2500) = √16 900 = 130 m. 170 − 130 = 40 m shorter.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":12,"height":5,"top":"120 m","side":"50 m"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_hard', 1, '9, 40 and x form a Pythagorean triad, with x the largest number. What is x?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"41"'::jsonb, 'x² = 9² + 40² = 81 + 1600 = 1681, and √1681 = 41 (because 41 × 41 = 1681).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_hard', 1, 'A right-angled triangle has a hypotenuse of 10 cm. Which could be its two shorter sides? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"6 cm and 8 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"5 cm and 5 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"√50 cm and √50 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"7 cm and 3 cm"},{"id":"e","text":"2 cm and √96 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'The squares of the shorter sides must add to 10² = 100. 36 + 64 = 100 ✓, 50 + 50 = 100 ✓, 4 + 96 = 100 ✓. But 25 + 25 = 50 and 49 + 9 = 58.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Triangle PQR has its right angle at Q. Which side is the hypotenuse?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"PR"},{"id":"b","text":"PQ"},{"id":"c","text":"QR"},{"id":"d","text":"It depends on the lengths"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The hypotenuse is opposite the right angle. The side that does not touch Q is PR.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the right-angled triangle. How long is the hypotenuse?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["10","10 m"]'::jsonb, 'The right angle is at the bottom left. The side opposite it is the sloping side, 10 m. It is also the longest side.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["8 m","10 m","6 m"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Pythagoras'' theorem works for every triangle, not just right-angled ones.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'a² + b² = c² is only true when the triangle has a right angle. In other triangles the squares don''t add up like that.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'easy', 1, 'Look at the right-angled triangle. How long is side h?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["15","15 cm"]'::jsonb, 'h is the hypotenuse. h² = 12² + 9² = 144 + 81 = 225, so h = √225 = 15 cm.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[8,6]],"sideLabels":["12 cm","9 cm","h"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'easy', 1, 'This triangle has a right angle at the bottom left corner. Which equation is true for it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"j² + k² = m²"},{"id":"b","text":"j² + m² = k²"},{"id":"c","text":"k² + m² = j²"},{"id":"d","text":"j + k = m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'm is opposite the right angle, so m is the hypotenuse. The two shorter sides are j and k, so j² + k² = m².', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["k","m","j"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'easy', 1, 'Doubling every number in the triad 3, 4, 5 gives another triad: 6, 8, ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"10"'::jsonb, '5 × 2 = 10. Check: 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100 = 10².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'medium', 1, 'A 13 m rope runs from the top of a flagpole to a peg in the ground 5 m from the base. How tall is the flagpole?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12 m"]'::jsonb, 'The rope is the hypotenuse. h² = 13² − 5² = 169 − 25 = 144, so h = √144 = 12 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'medium', 1, 'A right-angled triangle has shorter sides of 3 cm and 5 cm. Find the hypotenuse, correct to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["5.8","5.8 cm"]'::jsonb, 'c² = 3² + 5² = 9 + 25 = 34, so c = √34 ≈ 5.83, which rounds to 5.8 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these side lengths make a right-angled triangle? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"9, 12, 15"},{"id":"b","text":"10, 24, 26"},{"id":"c","text":"5, 6, 7"},{"id":"d","text":"7, 8, 10"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '81 + 144 = 225 = 15² ✓ and 100 + 576 = 676 = 26² ✓. But 25 + 36 = 61, not 49, and 49 + 64 = 113, not 100.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the right-angled triangle. Find x, correct to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["7.1","7.1 cm"]'::jsonb, '10 cm is the hypotenuse, so subtract. x² = 10² − 7² = 100 − 49 = 51, so x = √51 ≈ 7.14, which rounds to 7.1 cm.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[7,0],[7,7.1]],"sideLabels":["7 cm","x","10 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'hard', 1, 'Josh flies a kite on a 30 m string. The kite is directly above a point 18 m from Josh. How high is the kite? (Ignore Josh''s height.)', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"24 m"},{"id":"b","text":"35 m"},{"id":"c","text":"12 m"},{"id":"d","text":"48 m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The string is the hypotenuse. h² = 30² − 18² = 900 − 324 = 576, so h = √576 = 24 m. 35 m comes from adding the squares by mistake.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'hard', 1, 'Put these lengths in order from shortest to longest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"7"},{"id":"b","text":"√50"},{"id":"c","text":"7.2"},{"id":"d","text":"√53"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, '7 = √49. √50 ≈ 7.07, a little more than 7. 7.2 = √51.84. √53 ≈ 7.28, the longest.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Each square is 1 km. Ruby rides 7 km east, 5 km north, then 5 km east. How far is she from her start in a straight line?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["13","13 km"]'::jsonb, 'Altogether she is 7 + 5 = 12 km east and 5 km north of the start. Distance = √(12² + 5²) = √(144 + 25) = √169 = 13 km.', '{"kind":"grid","columns":12,"rows":6,"axes":true,"points":[{"x":0,"y":0,"label":"Start"},{"x":12,"y":5,"label":"End"}],"path":[[0,0],[7,0],[7,5],[12,5]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_hard', 1, 'A square has a diagonal of 10 cm. What is the area of the square in cm²?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["50","50 cm²","50 cm2"]'::jsonb, 'If the side is s, the diagonal makes a right-angled triangle: s² + s² = 10², so 2s² = 100 and s² = 50. The area of a square is s², so it is 50 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080301', 'very_hard', 1, 'A 2.5 m ladder reaches 2.4 m up a wall. Its foot slides 0.8 m further from the wall. How many metres does the top slide down?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.4","0.4 m","40 cm"]'::jsonb, 'At first the foot is √(2.5² − 2.4²) = √(6.25 − 5.76) = √0.49 = 0.7 m out. After sliding it is 0.7 + 0.8 = 1.5 m out, so the height is √(6.25 − 2.25) = √4 = 2 m. It slid 2.4 − 2 = 0.4 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_easy', 1, 'A circle has a radius of 6 cm. What is its diameter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["12","12 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The diameter goes right across the circle through the centre, so it is two radii: 2 × 6 = 12 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which formula gives the area of a circle with radius r?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A = πr²"},{"id":"b","text":"A = 2πr"},{"id":"c","text":"A = πd"},{"id":"d","text":"A = πr"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The area of a circle is π times the radius squared: A = πr². 2πr and πd give the circumference instead.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_easy', 1, 'The circumference of a circle is a little more than 3 times its diameter.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'C = π × d and π ≈ 3.14, so the circumference is about 3.14 times the diameter, a little more than 3 times.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'easy', 1, 'Using π ≈ 3.14, find the circumference of a circle with a diameter of 10 cm.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["31.4","31.4 cm"]'::jsonb, 'C = π × d = 3.14 × 10 = 31.4 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'easy', 1, 'Using π ≈ 3.14, find the area of a circular garden bed with a radius of 10 m, in m².', 'short_answer', NULL, '["314","314 m²","314 m2"]'::jsonb, 'A = πr² = 3.14 × 10² = 3.14 × 100 = 314 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'easy', 1, 'A circle has a radius of 4 cm. Its circumference is 2 × π × 4, which is ___ cm to 1 decimal place.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["25.1","25.12","25.13"]'::jsonb, '2 × π × 4 = 8π ≈ 25.13 (or 8 × 3.14 = 25.12), which rounds to 25.1 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'medium', 1, 'Find the area of a semicircle with a radius of 6 cm, in cm² to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["56.5","56.5 cm²","56.52","56.55"]'::jsonb, 'A whole circle would be π × 6² = 36π ≈ 113.1 cm². A semicircle is half: 113.1 ÷ 2 ≈ 56.5 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'medium', 1, 'Using a calculator, what is the area of a quadrant (quarter circle) with a radius of 8 cm?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"About 50.3 cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"About 201.1 cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"About 12.6 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"About 100.5 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A full circle is π × 8² = 64π ≈ 201.1 cm². A quadrant is a quarter of that: 201.1 ÷ 4 ≈ 50.3 cm². 201.1 is the whole circle and 100.5 is a semicircle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'medium', 1, 'A circle has a diameter of 14 cm. Which of these are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Its radius is 7 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"Its circumference is about 44 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"Its area is about 154 cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"Its area is about 616 cm²"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Radius = 14 ÷ 2 = 7 cm. C = π × 14 ≈ 44.0 cm. A = π × 7² = 49π ≈ 153.9 ≈ 154 cm². 616 cm² comes from squaring the diameter instead of the radius.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'hard', 1, 'Find the perimeter of a semicircle with a radius of 5 cm, to 1 decimal place. Remember the straight edge.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["25.7","25.7 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The curved part is half a circumference: π × 10 ÷ 2 ≈ 15.7 cm. The straight edge is the diameter, 10 cm. Perimeter ≈ 15.7 + 10 = 25.7 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'hard', 1, 'A garden is a 10 m by 6 m rectangle with a semicircle on one 6 m end. Find its total area in m², to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["74.1","74.1 m²","74.13","74.14"]'::jsonb, 'Rectangle: 10 × 6 = 60 m². The semicircle has radius 3 m: π × 3² ÷ 2 ≈ 14.1 m². Total ≈ 60 + 14.1 = 74.1 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'hard', 1, 'Put these shapes in order from smallest to largest area.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Circle, radius 3 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"Square, side 6 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"Semicircle, radius 5 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"Quadrant, radius 8 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Circle: π × 9 ≈ 28.3 cm². Square: 36 cm². Semicircle: π × 25 ÷ 2 ≈ 39.3 cm². Quadrant: π × 64 ÷ 4 ≈ 50.3 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_hard', 1, 'A bike wheel has a diameter of 70 cm. About how many turns does it make to travel 1 km? Give the nearest whole number.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["455","455 turns"]'::jsonb, 'One turn moves the bike one circumference: π × 70 ≈ 219.9 cm. 1 km = 100 000 cm. 100 000 ÷ 219.9 ≈ 454.7, so about 455 turns.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_hard', 1, 'Using π ≈ 3.14, a circle has a circumference of 62.8 cm. What is its area in cm²?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["314","314 cm²","314 cm2"]'::jsonb, 'C = π × d, so d = 62.8 ÷ 3.14 = 20 cm and r = 10 cm. A = 3.14 × 10² = 314 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_hard', 1, 'A round pond with a radius of 3 m has a path 1 m wide all the way around it. Find the area of the path in m², to the nearest whole number.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["22","22 m²","22 m2"]'::jsonb, 'Pond and path together make a circle of radius 4 m: π × 16. The pond alone is π × 9. Path = π × 16 − π × 9 = 7π ≈ 22.0, so about 22 m².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_easy', 1, 'A round trampoline has a diameter of 18 m. What is its radius?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9","9 m"]'::jsonb, 'The radius is half the diameter: 18 ÷ 2 = 9 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the circumference of a circle?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The distance around the outside"},{"id":"b","text":"The distance across through the centre"},{"id":"c","text":"The distance from the centre to the edge"},{"id":"d","text":"The space inside the circle"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The circumference is the perimeter of a circle: the distance all the way around it. Across is the diameter and centre to edge is the radius.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_easy', 1, 'A quadrant is one quarter of a circle.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Quad means four. A quadrant is a quarter of a circle, cut by two radii at right angles.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'easy', 1, 'Find the circumference of a circle with a radius of 7 cm, to the nearest whole centimetre.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["44","44 cm"]'::jsonb, 'C = 2 × π × r = 2 × π × 7 = 14π ≈ 43.98, which rounds to 44 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'easy', 1, 'Find the area of a circle with a radius of 4 cm, in cm² to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["50.3","50.2","50.24","50.27","50.3 cm²","50.2 cm²"]'::jsonb, 'A = πr² = π × 4² = 16π ≈ 50.3 cm² (or 3.14 × 16 = 50.24 ≈ 50.2 cm² using π ≈ 3.14).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'easy', 1, 'A pizza has a diameter of 30 cm. Which calculation gives its area?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"π × 15²"},{"id":"b","text":"π × 30²"},{"id":"c","text":"2 × π × 15"},{"id":"d","text":"π × 30"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Area uses the radius, which is 30 ÷ 2 = 15 cm, so A = π × 15². 2 × π × 15 and π × 30 both give the circumference.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'medium', 1, 'Find the area of a quadrant with a radius of 6 cm, in cm² to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["28.3","28.3 cm²","28.26","28.27"]'::jsonb, 'A full circle is π × 6² = 36π ≈ 113.1 cm². A quadrant is a quarter: 113.1 ÷ 4 ≈ 28.3 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'medium', 1, 'A semicircle has a diameter of 12 m. Its curved edge is half of π × 12, which is ___ m to 1 decimal place.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["18.8","18.84","18.85"]'::jsonb, 'π × 12 ≈ 37.7 m is the whole circumference. Half of it is about 18.85, which rounds to 18.8 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these have an area of about 78.5 cm²? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"A circle with radius 5 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"A quadrant with radius 10 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"A semicircle with radius 5 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"A circle with diameter 5 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Circle r = 5: π × 25 ≈ 78.5 ✓. Quadrant r = 10: π × 100 ÷ 4 ≈ 78.5 ✓. Semicircle r = 5: half of 78.5 ≈ 39.3. Diameter 5 means r = 2.5: π × 6.25 ≈ 19.6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'hard', 1, 'Find the perimeter of a quadrant with a radius of 4 m, to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["14.3","14.3 m","14.28"]'::jsonb, 'The curved edge is a quarter of the circumference: 2 × π × 4 ÷ 4 ≈ 6.28 m. Add the two straight radii: 6.28 + 4 + 4 ≈ 14.3 m.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'hard', 1, 'A shape is a square of side 8 cm with a semicircle stuck on one side (diameter 8 cm). Find its area in cm², to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["89.1","89.1 cm²","89.12","89.13"]'::jsonb, 'Square: 8 × 8 = 64 cm². Semicircle radius 4: π × 16 ÷ 2 ≈ 25.1 cm². Total ≈ 64 + 25.1 = 89.1 cm².', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'hard', 1, 'If you double the radius of a circle, its area doubles.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'A = πr². Doubling r makes it π × (2r)² = 4πr², so the area becomes 4 times as big, not 2 times.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_hard', 1, 'Put these shapes in order from smallest to largest perimeter.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Circle, radius 3 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"Semicircle, radius 4 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"Quadrant, radius 6 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"Square, side 6 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Circle: 2π × 3 ≈ 18.8 cm. Semicircle: π × 4 + 8 ≈ 20.6 cm. Quadrant: 2π × 6 ÷ 4 + 12 ≈ 21.4 cm. Square: 4 × 6 = 24 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_hard', 1, 'Using π ≈ 3.14, a circle has an area of 50.24 cm². What is its diameter?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["8","8 cm"]'::jsonb, 'A = 3.14 × r², so r² = 50.24 ÷ 3.14 = 16 and r = √16 = 4 cm. The diameter is 2 × 4 = 8 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080302', 'very_hard', 1, 'A running track has two straight sides of 100 m and two semicircular ends, each with a diameter of 60 m. About how long is one lap?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"About 388 m"},{"id":"b","text":"About 294 m"},{"id":"c","text":"About 577 m"},{"id":"d","text":"About 260 m"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The two semicircles make one whole circle of diameter 60 m: π × 60 ≈ 188.5 m. Add the straights: 188.5 + 100 + 100 ≈ 388 m. 294 m uses the radius by mistake.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_easy', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. What is the volume of the block?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["24","24 cm³","24 cm3"]'::jsonb, 'Each layer has 4 × 3 = 12 cubes and there are 2 layers: 12 × 2 = 24 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":4,"width":3,"height":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_easy', 1, 'A space of 1 cm³ holds exactly 1 mL of water.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '1 cm³ = 1 mL. That''s why a 1000 cm³ container holds 1 litre.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which rule gives the volume of any prism?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Area of the base × height"},{"id":"b","text":"Length + width + height"},{"id":"c","text":"Area of the base + height"},{"id":"d","text":"Perimeter of the base × height"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A prism has the same cross-section all the way through, so V = Ah: the area of the base (cross-section) times the height (length).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'easy', 1, 'A rectangular prism is 8 cm long, 5 cm wide and 3 cm high. What is its volume in cm³?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["120","120 cm³","120 cm3"]'::jsonb, 'V = l × w × h = 8 × 5 × 3 = 120 cm³.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"rectangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'easy', 1, 'A drink bottle with a volume of 750 cm³ holds ___ mL.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["750","750 mL"]'::jsonb, '1 cm³ holds 1 mL, so 750 cm³ holds 750 mL.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'easy', 1, 'How many litres fit in 1 m³?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1000 L"},{"id":"b","text":"100 L"},{"id":"c","text":"1 L"},{"id":"d","text":"1 000 000 L"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1 m³ = 100 cm × 100 cm × 100 cm = 1 000 000 cm³ = 1 000 000 mL, and 1 000 000 mL ÷ 1000 = 1000 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'medium', 1, 'A triangular prism has a triangle face with a base of 6 cm and a height of 4 cm. The prism is 9 cm long. What is its volume in cm³?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["108","108 cm³","108 cm3"]'::jsonb, 'Area of the triangle = ½ × 6 × 4 = 12 cm². Volume = 12 × 9 = 108 cm³.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'medium', 1, 'Using π ≈ 3.14, find the volume of a cylinder with a radius of 5 cm and a height of 10 cm, in cm³.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["785","785 cm³","785.4","785 cm3"]'::jsonb, 'Area of the circle = 3.14 × 5² = 78.5 cm². Volume = 78.5 × 10 = 785 cm³.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"cylinder"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these are equal to 2 L? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2000 mL"},{"id":"b","text":"2000 cm³"},{"id":"c","text":"0.002 m³"},{"id":"d","text":"200 cm³"},{"id":"e","text":"20 000 mL"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '2 L = 2000 mL = 2000 cm³. 1 m³ = 1000 L, so 2 L = 2 ÷ 1000 = 0.002 m³. 200 cm³ is only 0.2 L and 20 000 mL is 20 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'hard', 1, 'A fish tank is 60 cm long, 30 cm wide and 40 cm high. How many litres does it hold when full?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["72","72 L"]'::jsonb, 'V = 60 × 30 × 40 = 72 000 cm³ = 72 000 mL. Divide by 1000: 72 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'hard', 1, 'Put these solids in order from smallest to largest volume.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Cube with side 5 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"Rectangular prism 4 cm × 6 cm × 6 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"Cylinder with radius 3 cm and height 6 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"Triangular prism: triangle base 10 cm, height 6 cm, length 6 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Cube: 5 × 5 × 5 = 125 cm³. Rectangular prism: 4 × 6 × 6 = 144 cm³. Cylinder: π × 9 × 6 ≈ 169.6 cm³. Triangular prism: ½ × 10 × 6 × 6 = 180 cm³.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'hard', 1, 'A swimming pool is 25 m long, 10 m wide and 2 m deep. How many litres does it hold when full?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"500 000 L"},{"id":"b","text":"500 L"},{"id":"c","text":"50 000 L"},{"id":"d","text":"5 000 000 L"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'V = 25 × 10 × 2 = 500 m³. Each m³ holds 1000 L, so 500 × 1000 = 500 000 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_hard', 1, 'A cylinder-shaped rainwater tank has a diameter of 2 m and a height of 1.5 m. How many litres does it hold, to the nearest 10 L?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4710","4710 L"]'::jsonb, 'The radius is 1 m. V = π × 1² × 1.5 ≈ 4.71 m³. Each m³ is 1000 L, so 4.71 × 1000 ≈ 4710 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_hard', 1, 'A rectangular prism has a volume of 360 cm³ and a base 9 cm by 8 cm. Its height is ___ cm.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["5","5 cm"]'::jsonb, 'V = base area × height. Base area = 9 × 8 = 72 cm², so height = 360 ÷ 72 = 5 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_hard', 1, 'A full container 20 cm × 15 cm × 10 cm is tipped into an empty tank 30 cm long and 25 cm wide. How deep is the water in the tank, in cm?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4","4 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Water volume = 20 × 15 × 10 = 3000 cm³. The tank''s base is 30 × 25 = 750 cm². Depth = 3000 ÷ 750 = 4 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_easy', 1, 'Each small cube is 1 cm³. What is the volume of this cube?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["27","27 cm³","27 cm3"]'::jsonb, '3 × 3 = 9 cubes in each layer, and there are 3 layers: 9 × 3 = 27 cm³.', '{"kind":"cubes","length":3,"width":3,"height":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which unit is best for the volume of a shoebox?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"cm³"},{"id":"b","text":"cm"},{"id":"c","text":"cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"kg"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Volume is measured in cubic units like cm³. cm is for length, cm² is for area and kg is for mass.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_easy', 1, '5 L = ___ cm³', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["5000","5000 cm³"]'::jsonb, '1 L = 1000 mL = 1000 cm³, so 5 L = 5 × 1000 = 5000 cm³.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'easy', 1, 'A shipping container is 12 m long, 4 m wide and 2 m high. What is its volume in m³?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["96","96 m³","96 m3"]'::jsonb, 'V = l × w × h = 12 × 4 × 2 = 96 m³.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"rectangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'easy', 1, 'The volume of a cylinder is the area of its circular base multiplied by its height.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A cylinder is like a prism with a circle for a cross-section, so V = πr² × h.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'easy', 1, 'How many litres is 3.5 m³?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3500 L"},{"id":"b","text":"350 L"},{"id":"c","text":"35 000 L"},{"id":"d","text":"3.5 L"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '1 m³ = 1000 L, so 3.5 m³ = 3.5 × 1000 = 3500 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'medium', 1, 'This right-angled triangle is the cross-section of a prism 12 cm long. What is the volume of the prism in cm³?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["288","288 cm³","288 cm3"]'::jsonb, 'Area of the triangle = ½ × 8 × 6 = 24 cm². Volume = 24 × 12 = 288 cm³.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[0,6]],"sideLabels":["8 cm","","6 cm"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'medium', 1, 'A cylinder has a radius of 2 m and a height of 5 m. Find its volume in m³, to 1 decimal place.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["62.8","62.8 m³","62.83"]'::jsonb, 'Area of the circle = π × 2² = 4π ≈ 12.57 m². Volume = 4π × 5 = 20π ≈ 62.8 m³.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"cylinder"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'medium', 1, 'Which of these prisms have a volume of 60 cm³? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rectangular prism 5 cm × 4 cm × 3 cm"},{"id":"b","text":"Cube with side 6 cm"},{"id":"c","text":"Triangular prism: triangle base 5 cm, height 4 cm, length 6 cm"},{"id":"d","text":"Rectangular prism 10 cm × 6 cm × 2 cm"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c"]'::jsonb, '5 × 4 × 3 = 60 ✓. ½ × 5 × 4 = 10, then 10 × 6 = 60 ✓. The cube is 6 × 6 × 6 = 216 and 10 × 6 × 2 = 120.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'hard', 1, 'A milk carton 7 cm × 7 cm × 20 cm can hold 1 L of milk.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'V = 7 × 7 × 20 = 980 cm³ = 980 mL. That is less than 1 L (1000 mL), so it can''t hold a full litre.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'hard', 1, 'A cylinder-shaped bucket has a diameter of 20 cm and a height of 30 cm. How many litres does it hold, to 1 decimal place?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["9.4","9.4 L","9.42"]'::jsonb, 'Radius = 10 cm. V = π × 10² × 30 = 3000π ≈ 9425 cm³ = 9425 mL. Divide by 1000: about 9.4 L.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'hard', 1, 'Put these amounts in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"450 mL"},{"id":"b","text":"0.5 L"},{"id":"c","text":"600 cm³"},{"id":"d","text":"0.0008 m³"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Change them all to mL: 450 mL, 0.5 L = 500 mL, 600 cm³ = 600 mL, 0.0008 m³ = 0.8 L = 800 mL.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_hard', 1, 'A tent is a triangular prism. Its triangle end has a base of 2 m and a height of 1.5 m, and the tent is 3 m long. How many litres of air are inside?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4500","4500 L"]'::jsonb, 'Triangle area = ½ × 2 × 1.5 = 1.5 m². Volume = 1.5 × 3 = 4.5 m³. Each m³ is 1000 L, so 4.5 × 1000 = 4500 L.', '{"kind":"solid","shape":"triangular_prism"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_hard', 1, 'A cube-shaped box holds exactly 64 L. How long is each edge, in cm?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["40","40 cm"]'::jsonb, '64 L = 64 000 mL = 64 000 cm³. The edge is the number that cubes to 64 000: 40 × 40 × 40 = 64 000, so 40 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008030330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080303', 'very_hard', 1, 'Cylinder A has radius 4 cm and height 10 cm. Cylinder B has radius 8 cm and height 3 cm. Which holds more?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"B, by about 100 cm³"},{"id":"b","text":"A, by about 100 cm³"},{"id":"c","text":"They hold the same"},{"id":"d","text":"B, by about 32 cm³"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A: π × 4² × 10 = 160π. B: π × 8² × 3 = 192π. B is bigger by 32π ≈ 100.5 cm³, so about 100 cm³. 32 forgets to multiply by π.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Two shapes are congruent when they…', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"are exactly the same shape and size"},{"id":"b","text":"are the same shape but different sizes"},{"id":"c","text":"have the same area"},{"id":"d","text":"have the same perimeter"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Congruent means identical in shape and size, so one could sit exactly on top of the other. Same area or perimeter alone is not enough.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_easy', 1, 'If you reflect (flip) a triangle, the new triangle is congruent to the original.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A reflection does not change any side lengths or angles, so the image is the same shape and size: it is congruent.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_easy', 1, 'The symbol ≅ means ''is ___ to''.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"congruent"'::jsonb, '△ABC ≅ △PQR is read ''triangle ABC is congruent to triangle PQR''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'easy', 1, 'Look at triangle ABC. △ABC ≅ △PQR, with the letters matching in order. How long is side PQ?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["4","4 cm"]'::jsonb, 'The letters match in order: A with P, B with Q, C with R. So PQ matches AB, which is 4 cm.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[4,0],[0,3]],"sideLabels":["4 cm","5 cm","3 cm"],"cornerLabels":["A","B","C"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are tests that prove two triangles are congruent? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"SSS"},{"id":"b","text":"SAS"},{"id":"c","text":"AAS"},{"id":"d","text":"RHS"},{"id":"e","text":"AAA"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The four congruence tests are SSS, SAS, AAS and RHS. AAA only shows the triangles are the same shape; one could be bigger than the other.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'easy', 1, 'A quadrilateral has all four sides equal, but its angles are not right angles. What is it?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rhombus"},{"id":"b","text":"Square"},{"id":"c","text":"Rectangle"},{"id":"d","text":"Kite"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Four equal sides makes it a rhombus. A square also has four equal sides, but its angles are all 90°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'medium', 1, 'In △XYZ, XY = 6 cm, YZ = 8 cm and ∠XYZ = 50°. In △LMN, LM = 6 cm, MN = 8 cm and ∠LMN = 50°. Which test shows the triangles are congruent?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"SAS"},{"id":"b","text":"SSS"},{"id":"c","text":"AAS"},{"id":"d","text":"RHS"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Two pairs of sides are equal, and the 50° angle sits between them in both triangles (at Y and at M). That is Side, Angle, Side: SAS.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'medium', 1, 'The diagonals of a rectangle are equal in length.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Each diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle with the same two side lengths, so the two diagonals are equal.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'medium', 1, 'Put these from the most general to the most special. Each one is a special type of the one before it.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Quadrilateral"},{"id":"b","text":"Parallelogram"},{"id":"c","text":"Rectangle"},{"id":"d","text":"Square"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Every square is a rectangle (with equal sides), every rectangle is a parallelogram (with right angles), and every parallelogram is a quadrilateral.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'hard', 1, 'Look at right-angled triangle ABC. Triangle PQR also has a right angle, a hypotenuse of 13 cm and one other side of 5 cm. Which is true?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"They are congruent by RHS"},{"id":"b","text":"They are congruent by SAS"},{"id":"c","text":"They are not necessarily congruent"},{"id":"d","text":"They are congruent by AAA"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Both have a Right angle, the same Hypotenuse (13 cm) and another equal Side (5 cm). That is the RHS test, so they are congruent.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[12,0],[0,5]],"sideLabels":["12 cm","13 cm","5 cm"],"cornerLabels":["A","B","C"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'hard', 1, '△ABC ≅ △DEF, with the letters matching in order. ∠A = 40° and ∠B = 75°. What is the size of ∠F?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["65","65°"]'::jsonb, '∠F matches ∠C. The angles in a triangle add to 180°, so ∠C = 180° − 40° − 75° = 65°. So ∠F = 65°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'hard', 1, 'Look at rhombus ABCD. Which of these are true for every rhombus? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"All four sides are equal"},{"id":"b","text":"The diagonals cross at right angles"},{"id":"c","text":"The diagonals are equal in length"},{"id":"d","text":"Opposite angles are equal"},{"id":"e","text":"All angles are 90°"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'A rhombus has four equal sides, opposite angles equal, and diagonals that cross at right angles. Its diagonals are only equal (and its angles 90°) when it is a square.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[5,0],[8,4],[3,4]],"sideLabels":["5 cm","5 cm","5 cm","5 cm"],"cornerLabels":["A","B","C","D"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_hard', 1, 'In △ABC and △DEF, AB = DE, BC = EF and ∠A = ∠D. Why can''t you say the triangles are congruent from this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The equal angle is not between the two equal sides, so no test fits"},{"id":"b","text":"You always need three equal sides"},{"id":"c","text":"You always need two equal angles"},{"id":"d","text":"They are congruent by SAS, so you can"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'SAS needs the angle between the two sides. The angle between AB and BC is ∠B, not ∠A. Two sides and a non-included angle can make two different triangles, so it is not a test.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_hard', 1, 'ABCD is a parallelogram. The diagonal AC splits it into △ABC and △CDA. Which test proves these triangles are congruent?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"SSS"},{"id":"b","text":"RHS"},{"id":"c","text":"AAA"},{"id":"d","text":"They are not congruent"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal, so AB = CD and BC = DA. The diagonal AC is shared by both triangles. Three pairs of equal sides: SSS.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[6,0],[8,4],[2,4]],"cornerLabels":["A","B","C","D"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_hard', 1, 'In △ABC, AB = AC. M is the midpoint of BC, and AM is drawn. △ABM ≅ △ACM by SSS. What is the size of ∠AMB?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["90","90°"]'::jsonb, 'Because the triangles are congruent, ∠AMB = ∠AMC. These two angles sit on the straight line BC, so they add to 180°. Each one is 180° ÷ 2 = 90°.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[4,6],[0,0],[8,0]],"cornerLabels":["A","B","C"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which of these moves always give a shape congruent to the original? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Translating (sliding) it"},{"id":"b","text":"Rotating (turning) it"},{"id":"c","text":"Reflecting (flipping) it"},{"id":"d","text":"Enlarging it"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Sliding, turning and flipping keep every side and angle the same. Enlarging changes the size, so the new shape is not congruent.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_easy', 1, 'What does RHS stand for in the congruence tests?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Right angle, Hypotenuse, Side"},{"id":"b","text":"Right angle, Height, Side"},{"id":"c","text":"Rotation, Hypotenuse, Size"},{"id":"d","text":"Right angle, Half, Side"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'RHS means two right-angled triangles have equal hypotenuses and one other pair of equal sides.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_easy', 1, 'Any two squares with sides of 7 cm are congruent.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Both squares have four 7 cm sides and four 90° angles, so they are exactly the same shape and size.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'easy', 1, 'Look at rectangle ABCD. It is congruent to rectangle EFGH. What is the perimeter of EFGH?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["26","26 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Congruent shapes have the same side lengths, so EFGH is also 9 cm by 4 cm. Perimeter = 9 + 4 + 9 + 4 = 26 cm.', '{"kind":"rectangle","width":9,"height":4,"top":"9 cm","side":"4 cm"}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'easy', 1, '△ABC ≅ △XYZ, with the letters matching in order. Which side matches side BC?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"YZ"},{"id":"b","text":"XY"},{"id":"c","text":"XZ"},{"id":"d","text":"AC"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'B matches Y and C matches Z, so side BC matches side YZ.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'easy', 1, 'A quadrilateral with exactly one pair of parallel sides is called a ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["trapezium","trapezoid"]'::jsonb, 'A trapezium has exactly one pair of parallel sides. A parallelogram has two pairs.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'medium', 1, '△ABC has ∠A = 35°, ∠B = 80° and BC = 7 cm. △DEF has ∠D = 35°, ∠E = 80° and EF = 7 cm. Which test shows they are congruent?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"AAS"},{"id":"b","text":"SAS"},{"id":"c","text":"RHS"},{"id":"d","text":"SSS"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Two pairs of matching angles are equal, and a matching pair of sides (BC and EF) is equal. That is Angle, Angle, Side: AAS.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'medium', 1, 'The shape is a kite. The diagonals of a kite cross at right angles.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A kite has two pairs of equal sides next to each other. Its diagonals always meet at 90°.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[4,0],[7,5],[4,7],[1,5]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'medium', 1, 'Put the steps for proving two triangles congruent in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Match up the corresponding vertices"},{"id":"b","text":"List the pairs of equal sides and angles"},{"id":"c","text":"Choose the test that fits (SSS, SAS, AAS or RHS)"},{"id":"d","text":"Write the congruence statement, such as △ABC ≅ △PQR"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'First decide which corners match, then list the equal parts, then name the test they fit, and finish with the congruence statement written in matching order.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'hard', 1, '△PQR ≅ △STU, with the letters matching in order. PQ = 11 cm, QR = 7 cm and SU = 9 cm. What is the perimeter of △PQR?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["27","27 cm"]'::jsonb, 'SU matches PR, so PR = 9 cm. Perimeter = 11 + 7 + 9 = 27 cm.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'hard', 1, 'In which of these quadrilaterals do the diagonals always bisect each other (cut each other in half)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Parallelogram"},{"id":"b","text":"Rectangle"},{"id":"c","text":"Rhombus"},{"id":"d","text":"Square"},{"id":"e","text":"Kite"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The diagonals of every parallelogram bisect each other, and rectangles, rhombuses and squares are all parallelograms. In a kite only one diagonal is cut in half.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'hard', 1, 'In parallelogram ABCD, ∠ABC = 115°. What is the size of ∠BCD?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["65","65°"]'::jsonb, 'AB is parallel to DC, so ∠ABC and ∠BCD are co-interior angles that add to 180°. ∠BCD = 180° − 115° = 65°.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[7,0],[9,4],[2,4]],"cornerLabels":["A","B","C","D"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_hard', 1, 'In rectangle ABCD, the diagonal AC is drawn. Which statements are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"△ABC ≅ △CDA"},{"id":"b","text":"The triangles are congruent by SSS"},{"id":"c","text":"The triangles are congruent by RHS"},{"id":"d","text":"△ABC ≅ △ACD"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'AB = CD, BC = DA and AC is shared, so SSS works. ∠B = ∠D = 90° with the shared hypotenuse AC and AB = CD, so RHS works too. A matches C, B matches D and C matches A, so the statement is △ABC ≅ △CDA. Writing △ACD puts the letters in the wrong order.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,0],[8,0],[8,5],[0,5]],"cornerLabels":["A","B","C","D"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_hard', 1, 'In kite ABCD, AB = AD and CB = CD, so △ABC ≅ △ADC by SSS. If ∠BAD = 70° and ∠BCD = 50°, what is the size of ∠ABC?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["120","120°"]'::jsonb, 'The triangles are congruent, so ∠ABC = ∠ADC. The angles of a quadrilateral add to 360°, so these two share 360° − 70° − 50° = 240°. Each is 240° ÷ 2 = 120°.', '{"kind":"shape","points":[[0,4],[4,7],[10,4],[4,1]],"cornerLabels":["A","B","C","D"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008040130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080401', 'very_hard', 1, 'The diagonals of square ABCD meet at O. △AOB, △BOC, △COD and △DOA are all congruent, so ∠AOB = ___°.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["90","90°"]'::jsonb, 'The four triangles are congruent, so the four angles at O are equal. Together they make a full turn of 360°, so each is 360° ÷ 4 = 90°.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_easy', 1, 'The mean of 4, 7, 9 and 12 is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"8"'::jsonb, 'Add the numbers: 4 + 7 + 9 + 12 = 32. There are 4 numbers, so the mean is 32 ÷ 4 = 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the range of 15, 3, 22, 9, 11?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"19"},{"id":"b","text":"22"},{"id":"c","text":"11"},{"id":"d","text":"7"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Range = highest − lowest = 22 − 3 = 19.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the dot plot. What is the mode?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["2","2 goals"]'::jsonb, 'The mode is the value with the most dots. 2 goals has 4 dots, more than any other, so the mode is 2.', '{"kind":"dot_plot","title":"Goals scored per game","columns":[{"label":"1","count":2},{"label":"2","count":4},{"label":"3","count":1},{"label":"4","count":3},{"label":"5","count":1}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'easy', 1, 'Find the median of 13, 8, 21, 5, 17, 10.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"11.5"'::jsonb, 'In order: 5, 8, 10, 13, 17, 21. There are 6 numbers, so the median is halfway between the 3rd and 4th: (10 + 13) ÷ 2 = 11.5.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'easy', 1, 'An outlier of 95 is added to the scores 10, 12, 11, 13. This changes the mean much more than the median.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Before: mean = 46 ÷ 4 = 11.5 and median = 11.5. After: mean = 141 ÷ 5 = 28.2, but the median is only 12. Outliers pull the mean a long way.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'easy', 1, 'This stem-and-leaf plot shows the ages of volunteers at a sausage sizzle (2 | 1 means 21). What is the mode?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["35","35 years"]'::jsonb, 'The leaves give the ages 21, 24, 27, 30, 32, 35, 35, 36, 43, 48. Only 35 appears twice, so the mode is 35.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Stem","Leaf"],"rows":[["2","1 4 7"],["3","0 2 5 5 6"],["4","3 8"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'medium', 1, 'This stem-and-leaf plot shows the ages of volunteers at a sausage sizzle (2 | 1 means 21). What is the median age?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["33.5","33.5 years"]'::jsonb, 'The 10 ages in order are 21, 24, 27, 30, 32, 35, 35, 36, 43, 48. The median is halfway between the 5th and 6th: (32 + 35) ÷ 2 = 33.5.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Stem","Leaf"],"rows":[["2","1 4 7"],["3","0 2 5 5 6"],["4","3 8"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the histogram. Which class interval is the modal class?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"160–169"},{"id":"b","text":"150–159"},{"id":"c","text":"140–149"},{"id":"d","text":"180–189"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The modal class is the interval with the tallest column. 160–169 cm has 9 students, the most of any interval.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Heights of Year 8 students (cm)","columns":[{"label":"140–149","value":3},{"label":"150–159","value":7},{"label":"160–169","value":9},{"label":"170–179","value":4},{"label":"180–189","value":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'medium', 1, 'In a Sydney suburb, most houses sold for about $1 200 000, but one mansion sold for $9 000 000. Which measure best describes a typical sale price?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Median"},{"id":"b","text":"Mean"},{"id":"c","text":"Range"},{"id":"d","text":"Highest price"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The mansion is an outlier that drags the mean up. The median is the middle price, so it is hardly affected and better shows a typical sale.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'hard', 1, 'For the data 1, 3, 3, 5, 6, 12, put the mode, median, mean and range in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mode"},{"id":"b","text":"Median"},{"id":"c","text":"Mean"},{"id":"d","text":"Range"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Mode = 3 (it appears twice). Median = (3 + 5) ÷ 2 = 4. Mean = 30 ÷ 6 = 5. Range = 12 − 1 = 11. So 3, 4, 5, 11.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the histogram. How many students are 160 cm or taller?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["15","15 students"]'::jsonb, 'Add the columns from 160 cm up: 9 + 4 + 2 = 15 students.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Heights of Year 8 students (cm)","columns":[{"label":"140–149","value":3},{"label":"150–159","value":7},{"label":"160–169","value":9},{"label":"170–179","value":4},{"label":"180–189","value":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'hard', 1, 'In a maths test, 8A had a mean of 72 and a range of 10. 8B had a mean of 72 and a range of 40. Which statements are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"On average, both classes did equally well"},{"id":"b","text":"8B''s scores are more spread out"},{"id":"c","text":"8A''s scores are more consistent"},{"id":"d","text":"8B had the highest single score"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Equal means show the same average. A bigger range means more spread, so 8B is more spread out and 8A more consistent. The range does not tell you who got the top score.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_hard', 1, 'A cricketer''s mean score after 6 innings is 32 runs. After his 7th innings, his mean is 35 runs. How many runs did he score in the 7th innings?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["53","53 runs"]'::jsonb, 'Total after 6 innings = 6 × 32 = 192. Total after 7 innings = 7 × 35 = 245. So the 7th innings was 245 − 192 = 53 runs.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_hard', 1, 'The scores 48, 52, 55, 55, 60 and 2 are recorded. The 2 is removed as an outlier. Which statements are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The mean goes up"},{"id":"b","text":"The median goes up"},{"id":"c","text":"The mode stays the same"},{"id":"d","text":"The range stays the same"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Before: mean = 272 ÷ 6 ≈ 45.3, median = 53.5, mode = 55, range = 58. After: mean = 270 ÷ 5 = 54, median = 55, mode = 55, range = 12. So the range changes a lot.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the dot plot. What is the mean number of days absent?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1.4","1.4 days"]'::jsonb, 'There are 5 + 3 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 15 students. Total days = 0 × 5 + 1 × 3 + 2 × 4 + 3 × 2 + 4 × 1 = 21. Mean = 21 ÷ 15 = 1.4 days.', '{"kind":"dot_plot","title":"Days absent in Term 2","columns":[{"label":"0","count":5},{"label":"1","count":3},{"label":"2","count":4},{"label":"3","count":2},{"label":"4","count":1}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the median of 7, 3, 12, 9, 5?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"7"'::jsonb, 'Put them in order: 3, 5, 7, 9, 12. The middle number is 7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which of these data sets have a mode of 4? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"4, 4, 5, 6"},{"id":"b","text":"1, 4, 4, 4, 9"},{"id":"c","text":"2, 3, 4, 5"},{"id":"d","text":"4, 6, 6, 8"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'The mode is the value that appears most often. In the first two sets, 4 appears most. In 2, 3, 4, 5 nothing repeats, and in 4, 6, 6, 8 the mode is 6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the dot plot. What is the range of the number of pets?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"4"'::jsonb, 'The most pets anyone has is 4 and the fewest is 0. Range = 4 − 0 = 4.', '{"kind":"dot_plot","title":"Pets owned by each student","columns":[{"label":"0","count":3},{"label":"1","count":6},{"label":"2","count":4},{"label":"3","count":2},{"label":"4","count":1}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'easy', 1, 'The mean of 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 is equal to its median.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Mean = 30 ÷ 5 = 6. The middle number is also 6. The data is evenly spread, so they match.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'easy', 1, 'Which value is an outlier in 23, 25, 21, 24, 58, 22?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"58"},{"id":"b","text":"21"},{"id":"c","text":"25"},{"id":"d","text":"23"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Most values are between 21 and 25. 58 is far away from the rest, so it is the outlier.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'easy', 1, 'The five scores 15, 18, 20, 23 and ___ have a mean of 20.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"24"'::jsonb, 'A mean of 20 for 5 scores means a total of 5 × 20 = 100. The four known scores add to 76, so the last is 100 − 76 = 24.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'medium', 1, 'This stem-and-leaf plot shows how many minutes students took to walk to school (1 | 5 means 15 minutes). What is the range?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["24","24 minutes"]'::jsonb, 'The smallest value is 15 (stem 1, leaf 5) and the largest is 39 (stem 3, leaf 9). Range = 39 − 15 = 24 minutes.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Stem","Leaf"],"rows":[["1","5 8"],["2","0 3 3 7 9"],["3","1 9"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'medium', 1, 'Kaitlyn''s last five 100 m times were 14.2, 13.8, 14.0, 13.9 and 14.1 seconds. What was her mean time in seconds?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["14","14.0","14 seconds"]'::jsonb, 'Add the times: 14.2 + 13.8 + 14.0 + 13.9 + 14.1 = 70.0. Divide by 5: 70.0 ÷ 5 = 14.0 seconds.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'medium', 1, 'Look at the histogram. In how many innings did the batter score fewer than 20 runs?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7"},{"id":"b","text":"5"},{"id":"c","text":"15"},{"id":"d","text":"8"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Fewer than 20 means the 0–9 and 10–19 columns: 2 + 5 = 7 innings.', '{"kind":"column_graph","title":"Runs scored per innings","columns":[{"label":"0–9","value":2},{"label":"10–19","value":5},{"label":"20–29","value":8},{"label":"30–39","value":3},{"label":"40–49","value":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'hard', 1, 'This stem-and-leaf plot shows how many minutes students took to walk to school (1 | 5 means 15 minutes). What is the mean time?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["25","25 minutes"]'::jsonb, 'The times are 15, 18, 20, 23, 23, 27, 29, 31, 39. They add to 225. There are 9 times, so the mean is 225 ÷ 9 = 25 minutes.', '{"kind":"table","headings":["Stem","Leaf"],"rows":[["1","5 8"],["2","0 3 3 7 9"],["3","1 9"]]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'hard', 1, 'A data set has a mean of 40 and a median of 30. What is most likely true?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"A few very high values are pulling the mean up"},{"id":"b","text":"A few very low values are pulling the mean down"},{"id":"c","text":"The data is spread evenly on both sides of the middle"},{"id":"d","text":"The mode must be 35"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The mean is much bigger than the median. The median ignores how big the top values are, but the mean does not, so some very high values must be dragging the mean up.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'hard', 1, 'For the data 8, 2, 9, 5, 2, 10, put the mode, mean, median and range in order from smallest to largest.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mode"},{"id":"b","text":"Mean"},{"id":"c","text":"Median"},{"id":"d","text":"Range"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'In order: 2, 2, 5, 8, 9, 10. Mode = 2. Mean = 36 ÷ 6 = 6. Median = (5 + 8) ÷ 2 = 6.5. Range = 10 − 2 = 8. So 2, 6, 6.5, 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_hard', 1, 'The mean of 8 numbers is 15. One number is removed and the mean of the other 7 is 14. What number was removed?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"22"'::jsonb, 'Total of 8 numbers = 8 × 15 = 120. Total of 7 numbers = 7 × 14 = 98. The removed number is 120 − 98 = 22.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Two Year 8 classes sat the same test. 8C: mean 68, median 70, range 45. 8D: mean 68, median 66, range 20. Which statements are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"8D''s results are more consistent than 8C''s"},{"id":"b","text":"At least half of 8C scored 70 or more"},{"id":"c","text":"If the classes are the same size, their total scores are equal"},{"id":"d","text":"8D had the highest single score"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '8D''s smaller range means less spread. A median of 70 means at least half of 8C scored 70 or more. Equal means and equal class sizes give equal totals. The ranges don''t say who had the top score.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080501', 'very_hard', 1, 'Look at the dot plot. How much bigger is the mean than the median?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"0.5"'::jsonb, 'There are 12 students. Total = 0 × 2 + 1 × 5 + 2 × 3 + 3 × 1 + 4 × 1 = 18, so the mean is 18 ÷ 12 = 1.5. The 6th and 7th values are both 1, so the median is 1. 1.5 − 1 = 0.5.', '{"kind":"dot_plot","title":"Number of siblings","columns":[{"label":"0","count":2},{"label":"1","count":5},{"label":"2","count":3},{"label":"3","count":1},{"label":"4","count":1}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_easy', 1, 'A fair coin is tossed twice. How many outcomes are in the sample space?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"4"},{"id":"b","text":"2"},{"id":"c","text":"3"},{"id":"d","text":"6"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The outcomes are HH, HT, TH and TT. That is 2 × 2 = 4 outcomes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the spinner. What is the probability of NOT landing on red?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3/4","0.75","75%"]'::jsonb, 'P(red) = 1/4. Not landing on red is the complement, so P(not red) = 1 − 1/4 = 3/4.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","blue","green","yellow"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_easy', 1, 'The probability of an event and the probability of its complement always add to 1.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'An event either happens or it doesn''t, so between them they cover every outcome. P(A) + P(not A) = 1.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'easy', 1, 'A fair die is rolled. Put these events in order from least likely to most likely.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rolling a 7"},{"id":"b","text":"Rolling a 6"},{"id":"c","text":"Rolling an even number"},{"id":"d","text":"Rolling a number less than 7"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Rolling a 7 is impossible (0). Rolling a 6 is 1/6. Rolling an even number is 3/6 = 1/2. Every number on a die is less than 7, so that is certain (1).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'easy', 1, 'One counter is taken from the bag without looking. What is the probability it is blue or green?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"7/10"},{"id":"b","text":"1/2"},{"id":"c","text":"1/5"},{"id":"d","text":"3/10"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 3 + 5 + 2 = 10 counters. 5 blue and 2 green make 7 that are blue or green, so P = 7/10.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":3},{"colour":"blue","count":5},{"colour":"green","count":2}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'easy', 1, 'The probability of a spinner landing on yellow is 0.15. If it is spun 200 times, how many yellows would you expect?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"30"'::jsonb, 'Expected frequency = probability × number of trials = 0.15 × 200 = 30.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'medium', 1, 'A coin is tossed and a six-sided die is rolled. How many outcomes are in the sample space?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"12"'::jsonb, 'The coin has 2 outcomes and the die has 6. In a table with 2 rows and 6 columns there are 2 × 6 = 12 outcomes, from H1 to T6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'medium', 1, 'Two fair coins are tossed. What is the probability of getting one head and one tail?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/2"},{"id":"b","text":"1/4"},{"id":"c","text":"1/3"},{"id":"d","text":"3/4"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The outcomes are HH, HT, TH, TT. Two of the four (HT and TH) have one head and one tail, so P = 2/4 = 1/2.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'medium', 1, 'A die is rolled. Event A is ''rolling an even number''. Which of these describe the complement of A? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rolling an odd number"},{"id":"b","text":"Rolling a 1, 3 or 5"},{"id":"c","text":"Rolling a number less than 4"},{"id":"d","text":"Not rolling an even number"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'The complement is every outcome not in A: 1, 3 and 5. That is ''odd'', ''1, 3 or 5'' and ''not even''. ''Less than 4'' includes 2, which is even.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'hard', 1, 'Two fair dice are rolled and the numbers are added. What is the probability of a total of 7?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/6","6/36"]'::jsonb, 'A 6 × 6 table has 36 outcomes. A total of 7 comes from 1+6, 2+5, 3+4, 4+3, 5+2 and 6+1: 6 outcomes. P = 6/36 = 1/6.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'hard', 1, 'Look at the spinner. It is spun twice. What is the probability of blue, then blue again?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"1/4"},{"id":"b","text":"1/2"},{"id":"c","text":"1"},{"id":"d","text":"1/8"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Half the spinner is blue, so P(blue) = 1/2 each spin. On a tree diagram, multiply along the blue–blue path: 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","blue","blue","green"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'hard', 1, 'A counter is taken from the bag, put back, and a second counter is taken. P(red, then red) = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["4/25","16/100","0.16"]'::jsonb, 'P(red) = 4/10 each time because the counter goes back. Multiply along the path: 4/10 × 4/10 = 16/100 = 4/25.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":4},{"colour":"yellow","count":6}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_hard', 1, 'Two fair coins are tossed together 80 times. How many times would you expect to get two heads?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"20"},{"id":"b","text":"40"},{"id":"c","text":"10"},{"id":"d","text":"60"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'P(HH) = 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4. Expected frequency = 1/4 × 80 = 20.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A fair die is rolled twice. What is the probability of getting at least one 6?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"11/36"'::jsonb, 'Use the complement. P(no 6 on a roll) = 5/6, so P(no 6 on either roll) = 5/6 × 5/6 = 25/36. P(at least one 6) = 1 − 25/36 = 11/36.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A card is picked at random from ten cards numbered 1 to 10. Which statements are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"P(even or greater than 8) = 3/5"},{"id":"b","text":"P(even and greater than 8) = 1/10"},{"id":"c","text":"P(even or odd) = 1"},{"id":"d","text":"P(even) + P(greater than 8) = P(even or greater than 8)"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Even or greater than 8: 2, 4, 6, 8, 9, 10, which is 6/10 = 3/5. Even and greater than 8: only 10, so 1/10. Every card is even or odd, so 1. But P(even) + P(greater than 8) = 5/10 + 2/10 = 7/10, which counts the 10 twice, so the last one is false.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_easy', 1, 'The probability that it rains in Katoomba tomorrow is 0.3. What is the probability that it does not rain?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.7","7/10","70%"]'::jsonb, 'Not raining is the complement of raining. P(no rain) = 1 − 0.3 = 0.7.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_easy', 1, 'Look at the spinner. What is the probability of landing on blue?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"2/3"},{"id":"b","text":"1/3"},{"id":"c","text":"1/2"},{"id":"d","text":"3/2"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The spinner has 3 equal parts and 2 of them are blue, so P(blue) = 2/3.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["blue","blue","red"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_easy', 1, 'A die is rolled. Which outcomes are in the event ''rolling a prime number''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"2"},{"id":"b","text":"3"},{"id":"c","text":"5"},{"id":"d","text":"1"},{"id":"e","text":"4"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The prime numbers on a die are 2, 3 and 5. 1 is not prime, and 4 = 2 × 2 is composite.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'easy', 1, 'When a coin is tossed and a die is rolled, there are 8 possible outcomes.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'There are 2 coin outcomes and 6 die outcomes, so 2 × 6 = 12 outcomes, not 2 + 6 = 8.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'easy', 1, 'A counter is taken from the bag and put back, 60 times. How many times would you expect to get green?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"20"'::jsonb, 'There are 12 counters and 4 are green, so P(green) = 4/12 = 1/3. Expected frequency = 1/3 × 60 = 20.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"orange","count":3},{"colour":"purple","count":5},{"colour":"green","count":4}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'easy', 1, 'If the probability of an event is 2/9, the probability of its complement is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"7/9"'::jsonb, 'An event and its complement add to 1. 1 − 2/9 = 9/9 − 2/9 = 7/9.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'medium', 1, 'Two fair coins are tossed. What is the probability of getting at least one tail?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"3/4"},{"id":"b","text":"1/2"},{"id":"c","text":"1/4"},{"id":"d","text":"2/3"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The outcomes are HH, HT, TH, TT. Only HH has no tail, so 3 of the 4 have at least one tail: P = 3/4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'medium', 1, 'Put the steps for using a tree diagram to find probabilities for two coin tosses in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Draw two branches for the first toss, H and T"},{"id":"b","text":"From the end of each branch, draw two branches for the second toss"},{"id":"c","text":"Write the outcome at the end of each path: HH, HT, TH, TT"},{"id":"d","text":"Multiply the probabilities along each path"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A tree diagram is built one stage at a time: first toss, then second toss from each branch. Then list each path''s outcome and multiply along the path to get its probability.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'medium', 1, 'The spinner is spun and a coin is tossed. What is the probability of blue and a head?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["1/6"]'::jsonb, 'P(blue) = 1/3 and P(head) = 1/2. For ''and'' with two separate steps, multiply: 1/3 × 1/2 = 1/6.', '{"kind":"spinner","sections":["red","blue","yellow"]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'hard', 1, 'A card is picked at random from twelve cards numbered 1 to 12. Which statements are true? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"P(multiple of 3) = 1/3"},{"id":"b","text":"P(multiple of 3 or multiple of 4) = 1/2"},{"id":"c","text":"P(multiple of 3 and multiple of 4) = 1/12"},{"id":"d","text":"P(not a multiple of 4) = 1/4"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Multiples of 3: 3, 6, 9, 12 (4/12 = 1/3). Multiples of 3 or 4: 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12 (6/12 = 1/2). Both: only 12 (1/12). Multiples of 4 are 4, 8, 12, so P(not) = 9/12 = 3/4.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'hard', 1, 'Two dice are rolled and the numbers are added, 180 times. How many times would you expect a total of 6?', 'short_answer', NULL, '"25"'::jsonb, 'A total of 6 comes from 1+5, 2+4, 3+3, 4+2 and 5+1: 5 of the 36 outcomes. Expected frequency = 5/36 × 180 = 25.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'hard', 1, 'A netball goal shooter scores 0.8 of her shots. She takes two shots, and each shot does not affect the other. What is the probability she scores both?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"0.64"},{"id":"b","text":"1.6"},{"id":"c","text":"0.8"},{"id":"d","text":"0.16"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'On a tree diagram, follow the score–score path and multiply: 0.8 × 0.8 = 0.64.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A school bus is late on 0.1 of mornings, and each morning is separate. What is the probability it is late on at least one of two mornings?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["0.19","19/100","19%"]'::jsonb, 'Use the complement. P(on time both days) = 0.9 × 0.9 = 0.81. P(late at least once) = 1 − 0.81 = 0.19.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A fair die is rolled twice. What is the probability that the first roll is higher than the second?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"5/12"},{"id":"b","text":"1/2"},{"id":"c","text":"1/6"},{"id":"d","text":"7/12"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'There are 36 outcomes. 6 are doubles. The other 30 split evenly: first higher in 15, second higher in 15. P = 15/36 = 5/12.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008050230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000080502', 'very_hard', 1, 'A counter is taken from the bag, put back, and a second is taken. P(one red and one blue, in either order) = ___', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["12/25","0.48","48/100"]'::jsonb, 'P(red, blue) = 2/5 × 3/5 = 6/25 and P(blue, red) = 3/5 × 2/5 = 6/25. ''Or'' adds the two paths: 6/25 + 6/25 = 12/25.', '{"kind":"bag","counters":[{"colour":"red","count":2},{"colour":"blue","count":3}]}'::jsonb, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_easy', 1, 'According to the text, seagrasses are a type of seaweed.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph says ''Seagrasses are not seaweeds. They are flowering plants, with roots, leaves and tiny flowers.''', NULL, '{"title":"The Ocean''s Hidden Meadows","text":"Ask most people to name an important marine habitat and they will mention coral reefs. Few will think of seagrass. Yet the underwater meadows that grow in sheltered bays and estuaries along the New South Wales coast are among the most productive ecosystems on Earth.\n\nSeagrasses are not seaweeds. They are flowering plants, with roots, leaves and tiny flowers, whose ancestors lived on land millions of years ago before returning to the sea. Like plants on land, they need sunlight to grow, which is why they are found only in clear, shallow water.\n\nThese meadows act as nurseries. Young bream, whiting and snapper shelter among the blades, safe from larger predators, until they are big enough to move into open water. Seahorses, pipefish and cuttlefish live there too. In northern Australia, dugongs and green turtles graze on seagrass the way cattle graze on pasture.\n\nSeagrass also helps fight climate change. Its roots trap carbon in the mud of the seafloor, and a healthy meadow can store carbon far faster than the same area of forest.\n\nHowever, seagrass meadows are disappearing. Run-off from farms and cities clouds the water and blocks sunlight. Boat anchors and moorings tear up plants, leaving bare patches that can take decades to recover.\n\nSimple changes can help. Boaters can use seagrass-friendly moorings, and communities can reduce fertiliser run-off. Protecting these hidden meadows protects the fish we catch, the animals we love and the climate we all share."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_easy', 1, 'The sentence ''The fence was painted by Leo'' is in the passive voice. Who did the painting?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["Leo"]'::jsonb, 'In a passive sentence the doer comes after ''by''. ''By Leo'' tells us Leo did the painting. The active version is ''Leo painted the fence.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_easy', 1, 'In the sentence ''Students must wear a hat at lunch'', the word ''must'' shows ___ modality.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["high","strong"]'::jsonb, 'Modality is how certain or forceful a word is. ''Must'' leaves no choice, so it is high modality. ''Should'' is medium and ''might'' is low.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence best states the main idea of the text?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Seagrass meadows are valuable to sea life and the climate, but they are under threat and need protecting."},{"id":"b","text":"Dugongs and green turtles eat seagrass."},{"id":"c","text":"Coral reefs are the most important marine habitat."},{"id":"d","text":"Boat anchors can damage the seafloor."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The main idea covers the whole text: why seagrass matters (nursery, carbon) and why it needs protecting. The other options are small details or something the text argues against.', NULL, '{"title":"The Ocean''s Hidden Meadows","text":"Ask most people to name an important marine habitat and they will mention coral reefs. Few will think of seagrass. Yet the underwater meadows that grow in sheltered bays and estuaries along the New South Wales coast are among the most productive ecosystems on Earth.\n\nSeagrasses are not seaweeds. They are flowering plants, with roots, leaves and tiny flowers, whose ancestors lived on land millions of years ago before returning to the sea. Like plants on land, they need sunlight to grow, which is why they are found only in clear, shallow water.\n\nThese meadows act as nurseries. Young bream, whiting and snapper shelter among the blades, safe from larger predators, until they are big enough to move into open water. Seahorses, pipefish and cuttlefish live there too. In northern Australia, dugongs and green turtles graze on seagrass the way cattle graze on pasture.\n\nSeagrass also helps fight climate change. Its roots trap carbon in the mud of the seafloor, and a healthy meadow can store carbon far faster than the same area of forest.\n\nHowever, seagrass meadows are disappearing. Run-off from farms and cities clouds the water and blocks sunlight. Boat anchors and moorings tear up plants, leaving bare patches that can take decades to recover.\n\nSimple changes can help. Boaters can use seagrass-friendly moorings, and communities can reduce fertiliser run-off. Protecting these hidden meadows protects the fish we catch, the animals we love and the climate we all share."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'easy', 1, 'What is the main purpose of this letter?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"To persuade readers to oppose the Council''s plan to shorten the library''s hours"},{"id":"b","text":"To inform readers about the history of Riverbend Library"},{"id":"c","text":"To entertain readers with a story about studying"},{"id":"d","text":"To complain about the Council''s new signs"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The letter argues against closing at 5 pm and ends by urging readers to tell councillors the library must stay open until 8 pm. That is persuasion. The signs are only one piece of evidence.', NULL, '{"title":"Letter to the Editor: Keep Our Library Open","text":"To the Editor,\n\nThe Council''s plan to close Riverbend Library at 5 pm on weeknights, instead of 8 pm, may save $40 000 a year. But it will cost our community far more than that.\n\nFor many students, the library is not a luxury; it is the only quiet place they have. Not every teenager has a desk at home, a reliable internet connection or a room without younger siblings in it. After 5 pm, when parents are still at work and homes are at their noisiest, those students will have nowhere to go.\n\nThe library is also a lifeline for adults. Every Tuesday and Thursday evening, volunteers run free English conversation classes and help older residents fill in online forms. Surely a council that claims to value ''inclusion'' should be protecting these services, not cutting them.\n\nSome will argue that everything is online now, so libraries matter less. Yet the library recorded more than 30 000 evening visits last year. Those visitors were not imaginary. They were real people who chose to come through the doors.\n\nForty thousand dollars is less than the Council spent on new signage last year. If the Council can afford new signs, it can afford to keep the lights on.\n\nI urge every reader to attend the Council meeting on 14 March and to tell our councillors, clearly and politely, that Riverbend Library must stay open until 8 pm.\n\nYours sincerely,\nHannah Okafor, Year 8 student"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'easy', 1, 'Put these events from the story in the order they happen.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Pop joins Mia on the rocks without the bait bucket."},{"id":"b","text":"Pop calls Mia by her mother''s name."},{"id":"c","text":"Pop tells the story about the stingray and his sandwich."},{"id":"d","text":"Mia writes a message to her mum on the drive home."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Follow the story through the morning: the forgotten bucket, then ''Jenny'', then the stingray story, and finally the drive home.', NULL, '{"title":"Tide Line","text":"Every Saturday for as long as Mia could remember, Pop had driven them to the end of the breakwall at Swansea before sunrise. He was always first out of the car, rods over one shoulder, whistling something from the seventies.\n\nThis Saturday, Mia was first out of the car.\n\nPop sat behind the wheel for a long moment, frowning at the dashboard as if someone had rearranged it overnight. When he finally joined her on the rocks, he had forgotten the bait bucket. Mia said nothing. She walked back and fetched it herself.\n\nThey fished in silence for an hour. The sky turned from grey to apricot, and a pelican landed on a nearby post to supervise. Twice Pop cast out a hook with no bait on it at all.\n\n''Your mum used to love this spot, Jenny,'' he said suddenly, smiling at her.\n\nMia''s line went slack in her hands. Jenny was her mother''s name.\n\nShe opened her mouth to correct him, then closed it again. Instead she pointed at the pelican. ''Do you think he wants a fish?''\n\nPop laughed, the same big laugh as always, and for a moment everything was ordinary. He told her the story about the stingray that had stolen his sandwich in 1983. She had heard it a hundred times. She listened as if it were the first.\n\nOn the drive home, Mia wrote a message to her mum. She typed it three times before she was happy with the words. Then she put the phone face down on her knee and watched the lake slide past the window."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'medium', 1, 'What can you infer about Pop from the details in the story?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"His memory is failing, and he is becoming confused."},{"id":"b","text":"He is tired of fishing and wants to stop going."},{"id":"c","text":"He is angry with Mia for being first out of the car."},{"id":"d","text":"He is playing a joke by calling Mia ''Jenny''."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The writer never says it directly, but the clues add up: he stares at the dashboard, forgets the bait, casts empty hooks and calls Mia by her mother''s name. These are signs of memory loss.', NULL, '{"title":"Tide Line","text":"Every Saturday for as long as Mia could remember, Pop had driven them to the end of the breakwall at Swansea before sunrise. He was always first out of the car, rods over one shoulder, whistling something from the seventies.\n\nThis Saturday, Mia was first out of the car.\n\nPop sat behind the wheel for a long moment, frowning at the dashboard as if someone had rearranged it overnight. When he finally joined her on the rocks, he had forgotten the bait bucket. Mia said nothing. She walked back and fetched it herself.\n\nThey fished in silence for an hour. The sky turned from grey to apricot, and a pelican landed on a nearby post to supervise. Twice Pop cast out a hook with no bait on it at all.\n\n''Your mum used to love this spot, Jenny,'' he said suddenly, smiling at her.\n\nMia''s line went slack in her hands. Jenny was her mother''s name.\n\nShe opened her mouth to correct him, then closed it again. Instead she pointed at the pelican. ''Do you think he wants a fish?''\n\nPop laughed, the same big laugh as always, and for a moment everything was ordinary. He told her the story about the stingray that had stolen his sandwich in 1983. She had heard it a hundred times. She listened as if it were the first.\n\nOn the drive home, Mia wrote a message to her mum. She typed it three times before she was happy with the words. Then she put the phone face down on her knee and watched the lake slide past the window."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'medium', 1, 'Which persuasive techniques does the writer use? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Statistics, such as 30 000 evening visits"},{"id":"b","text":"Rebutting an opposing view (''Some will argue...'')"},{"id":"c","text":"A call to action at the end"},{"id":"d","text":"A quote from a famous expert"},{"id":"e","text":"A rhetorical question in the final paragraph"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The letter uses numbers ($40 000, 30 000 visits), answers the claim that ''everything is online now'', and ends by urging readers to attend the meeting. There is no expert quote, and the last paragraph is a statement, not a question.', NULL, '{"title":"Letter to the Editor: Keep Our Library Open","text":"To the Editor,\n\nThe Council''s plan to close Riverbend Library at 5 pm on weeknights, instead of 8 pm, may save $40 000 a year. But it will cost our community far more than that.\n\nFor many students, the library is not a luxury; it is the only quiet place they have. Not every teenager has a desk at home, a reliable internet connection or a room without younger siblings in it. After 5 pm, when parents are still at work and homes are at their noisiest, those students will have nowhere to go.\n\nThe library is also a lifeline for adults. Every Tuesday and Thursday evening, volunteers run free English conversation classes and help older residents fill in online forms. Surely a council that claims to value ''inclusion'' should be protecting these services, not cutting them.\n\nSome will argue that everything is online now, so libraries matter less. Yet the library recorded more than 30 000 evening visits last year. Those visitors were not imaginary. They were real people who chose to come through the doors.\n\nForty thousand dollars is less than the Council spent on new signage last year. If the Council can afford new signs, it can afford to keep the lights on.\n\nI urge every reader to attend the Council meeting on 14 March and to tell our councillors, clearly and politely, that Riverbend Library must stay open until 8 pm.\n\nYours sincerely,\nHannah Okafor, Year 8 student"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'medium', 1, 'Which word best describes the mood of most of the poem?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"weary and anxious"},{"id":"b","text":"joyful and excited"},{"id":"c","text":"angry and violent"},{"id":"d","text":"calm and carefree"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The cracked dam, the thin sheep, Mum turning away from the radio and the silence when the cloud passes all create a tired, worried mood as the family waits for rain.', NULL, '{"title":"Drought Year","text":"The dam is a cracked brown plate,\nlicked clean by a sun that will not leave.\nDad walks the fence line every evening,\ncounting what is left.\n\nThe sheep come to the trough before we call them,\ntheir ribs like the rungs of a ladder.\nMy brother names the thinnest one Hope\nand feeds her from his hand.\n\nThe radio talks of rain in other towns,\nof storms that turned around at Dubbo.\nMum stops listening halfway through\nand starts the washing-up again,\nscrubbing the same clean plate\nuntil the news is over.\n\nIn town the shop windows say CLOSING DOWN\nin letters the colour of dust.\nThe footy club has cancelled training;\nthe oval is too hard to fall on.\n\nAt school we write about the sea.\nI write about the sound of rain on the tin roof,\nlike a crowd that has come back\nto cheer for us.\n\nTonight a single cloud drifts over,\nfat and grey and full of nothing.\nDad watches it the whole way past.\nNobody says a word.\n\nBut in the morning, on the kitchen window,\nthere is a fog of breath and something else:\nthree small drops, running.\nMum leaves them there all day."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'hard', 1, 'Why does the writer begin the text by mentioning coral reefs?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"To contrast seagrass with a famous habitat and show that seagrass is often overlooked"},{"id":"b","text":"Because the whole text is about coral reefs"},{"id":"c","text":"To prove that coral reefs are disappearing faster than seagrass"},{"id":"d","text":"To tell readers that seagrass grows on coral reefs"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The writer starts with what readers already know (''they will mention coral reefs'') and then says ''Few will think of seagrass''. The contrast hooks the reader and sets up the idea that seagrass deserves more attention.', NULL, '{"title":"The Ocean''s Hidden Meadows","text":"Ask most people to name an important marine habitat and they will mention coral reefs. Few will think of seagrass. Yet the underwater meadows that grow in sheltered bays and estuaries along the New South Wales coast are among the most productive ecosystems on Earth.\n\nSeagrasses are not seaweeds. They are flowering plants, with roots, leaves and tiny flowers, whose ancestors lived on land millions of years ago before returning to the sea. Like plants on land, they need sunlight to grow, which is why they are found only in clear, shallow water.\n\nThese meadows act as nurseries. Young bream, whiting and snapper shelter among the blades, safe from larger predators, until they are big enough to move into open water. Seahorses, pipefish and cuttlefish live there too. In northern Australia, dugongs and green turtles graze on seagrass the way cattle graze on pasture.\n\nSeagrass also helps fight climate change. Its roots trap carbon in the mud of the seafloor, and a healthy meadow can store carbon far faster than the same area of forest.\n\nHowever, seagrass meadows are disappearing. Run-off from farms and cities clouds the water and blocks sunlight. Boat anchors and moorings tear up plants, leaving bare patches that can take decades to recover.\n\nSimple changes can help. Boaters can use seagrass-friendly moorings, and communities can reduce fertiliser run-off. Protecting these hidden meadows protects the fish we catch, the animals we love and the climate we all share."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'hard', 1, 'The cloud is ''fat and grey and full of nothing''. What does this description suggest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The cloud looks as if it will bring rain but brings none, which deepens the family''s disappointment."},{"id":"b","text":"The cloud is about to cause a dangerous storm."},{"id":"c","text":"The speaker thinks clouds are boring to look at."},{"id":"d","text":"The cloud is full of smoke from a bushfire."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Fat and grey'' makes the cloud look heavy with rain, but ''full of nothing'' tells us it is empty. Dad watching it pass in silence shows how let down the family feels.', NULL, '{"title":"Drought Year","text":"The dam is a cracked brown plate,\nlicked clean by a sun that will not leave.\nDad walks the fence line every evening,\ncounting what is left.\n\nThe sheep come to the trough before we call them,\ntheir ribs like the rungs of a ladder.\nMy brother names the thinnest one Hope\nand feeds her from his hand.\n\nThe radio talks of rain in other towns,\nof storms that turned around at Dubbo.\nMum stops listening halfway through\nand starts the washing-up again,\nscrubbing the same clean plate\nuntil the news is over.\n\nIn town the shop windows say CLOSING DOWN\nin letters the colour of dust.\nThe footy club has cancelled training;\nthe oval is too hard to fall on.\n\nAt school we write about the sea.\nI write about the sound of rain on the tin roof,\nlike a crowd that has come back\nto cheer for us.\n\nTonight a single cloud drifts over,\nfat and grey and full of nothing.\nDad watches it the whole way past.\nNobody says a word.\n\nBut in the morning, on the kitchen window,\nthere is a fog of breath and something else:\nthree small drops, running.\nMum leaves them there all day."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the strongest thesis statement for an essay on whether NSW high schools should start at 9:30 am?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"High schools should start at 9:30 am because later starts suit teenagers'' sleep patterns, improve concentration and reduce lateness."},{"id":"b","text":"This essay will talk about school start times."},{"id":"c","text":"Some people like a later start and some people don''t."},{"id":"d","text":"I think 9:30 am is good."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A strong thesis takes a clear position and previews the reasons that will be argued. The others only announce the topic, sit on the fence, or give a vague opinion with no reasons.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which details suggest that Mia is worried and upset, even though she never says so? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Her line goes slack in her hands when Pop calls her Jenny."},{"id":"b","text":"She types the message to her mum three times."},{"id":"c","text":"She puts the phone face down on her knee."},{"id":"d","text":"A pelican lands on a nearby post."},{"id":"e","text":"The sky turns from grey to apricot."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The writer shows Mia''s feelings through her actions: she freezes, chooses her words very carefully, and hides the phone as if she doesn''t want to think about it. The pelican and the sky are setting details.', NULL, '{"title":"Tide Line","text":"Every Saturday for as long as Mia could remember, Pop had driven them to the end of the breakwall at Swansea before sunrise. He was always first out of the car, rods over one shoulder, whistling something from the seventies.\n\nThis Saturday, Mia was first out of the car.\n\nPop sat behind the wheel for a long moment, frowning at the dashboard as if someone had rearranged it overnight. When he finally joined her on the rocks, he had forgotten the bait bucket. Mia said nothing. She walked back and fetched it herself.\n\nThey fished in silence for an hour. The sky turned from grey to apricot, and a pelican landed on a nearby post to supervise. Twice Pop cast out a hook with no bait on it at all.\n\n''Your mum used to love this spot, Jenny,'' he said suddenly, smiling at her.\n\nMia''s line went slack in her hands. Jenny was her mother''s name.\n\nShe opened her mouth to correct him, then closed it again. Instead she pointed at the pelican. ''Do you think he wants a fish?''\n\nPop laughed, the same big laugh as always, and for a moment everything was ordinary. He told her the story about the stingray that had stolen his sandwich in 1983. She had heard it a hundred times. She listened as if it were the first.\n\nOn the drive home, Mia wrote a message to her mum. She typed it three times before she was happy with the words. Then she put the phone face down on her knee and watched the lake slide past the window."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does the writer put quotation marks around the word ''inclusion''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"To suggest the Council says it values inclusion but its plan does the opposite"},{"id":"b","text":"Because ''inclusion'' is the name of a library program"},{"id":"c","text":"To show that she is quoting a dictionary definition"},{"id":"d","text":"Because ''inclusion'' is a word she has made up"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The quotation marks point to the Council''s own word and question it. Cutting classes for migrants and help for older residents doesn''t match a claim to value inclusion, so the marks make the claim sound hollow.', NULL, '{"title":"Letter to the Editor: Keep Our Library Open","text":"To the Editor,\n\nThe Council''s plan to close Riverbend Library at 5 pm on weeknights, instead of 8 pm, may save $40 000 a year. But it will cost our community far more than that.\n\nFor many students, the library is not a luxury; it is the only quiet place they have. Not every teenager has a desk at home, a reliable internet connection or a room without younger siblings in it. After 5 pm, when parents are still at work and homes are at their noisiest, those students will have nowhere to go.\n\nThe library is also a lifeline for adults. Every Tuesday and Thursday evening, volunteers run free English conversation classes and help older residents fill in online forms. Surely a council that claims to value ''inclusion'' should be protecting these services, not cutting them.\n\nSome will argue that everything is online now, so libraries matter less. Yet the library recorded more than 30 000 evening visits last year. Those visitors were not imaginary. They were real people who chose to come through the doors.\n\nForty thousand dollars is less than the Council spent on new signage last year. If the Council can afford new signs, it can afford to keep the lights on.\n\nI urge every reader to attend the Council meeting on 14 March and to tell our councillors, clearly and politely, that Riverbend Library must stay open until 8 pm.\n\nYours sincerely,\nHannah Okafor, Year 8 student"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_hard', 1, 'How does the final stanza change the poem?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"''But'' signals a turn from hopelessness to a fragile hope, shown by the drops Mum leaves on the window."},{"id":"b","text":"It proves the drought is over and the farm is saved."},{"id":"c","text":"It repeats the sadness of the earlier stanzas with no change."},{"id":"d","text":"It moves the setting from the farm to the sea."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The stanza opens with ''But'', marking a shift. Three small drops are not the end of the drought, yet Mum keeping them ''all day'' shows how much a tiny sign of rain means: hope, not rescue.', NULL, '{"title":"Drought Year","text":"The dam is a cracked brown plate,\nlicked clean by a sun that will not leave.\nDad walks the fence line every evening,\ncounting what is left.\n\nThe sheep come to the trough before we call them,\ntheir ribs like the rungs of a ladder.\nMy brother names the thinnest one Hope\nand feeds her from his hand.\n\nThe radio talks of rain in other towns,\nof storms that turned around at Dubbo.\nMum stops listening halfway through\nand starts the washing-up again,\nscrubbing the same clean plate\nuntil the news is over.\n\nIn town the shop windows say CLOSING DOWN\nin letters the colour of dust.\nThe footy club has cancelled training;\nthe oval is too hard to fall on.\n\nAt school we write about the sea.\nI write about the sound of rain on the tin roof,\nlike a crowd that has come back\nto cheer for us.\n\nTonight a single cloud drifts over,\nfat and grey and full of nothing.\nDad watches it the whole way past.\nNobody says a word.\n\nBut in the morning, on the kitchen window,\nthere is a fog of breath and something else:\nthree small drops, running.\nMum leaves them there all day."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_easy', 1, 'According to the text, all of the workers on the Snowy Mountains Scheme were born in Australia.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The text says more than 100 000 people from over 30 countries worked on it, and many had come from Europe after the Second World War.', NULL, '{"title":"The Snowy Mountains Scheme","text":"High in the Australian Alps, beneath some of the coldest and steepest country on the continent, lies one of the largest engineering projects ever completed in Australia: the Snowy Mountains Hydro-electric Scheme.\n\nThe Scheme was built between 1949 and 1974. Its purpose was to capture water from the Snowy River and other rivers that flowed east to the sea, and send it west through the mountains. On the way, the falling water would spin turbines to produce electricity. Once it reached the other side, it could be released into the Murray and Murrumbidgee rivers to irrigate farms.\n\nTo do this, workers built large dams, power stations deep underground, and many kilometres of tunnels blasted through solid rock. They worked through winter blizzards in remote camps far from any town.\n\nThe Scheme was not only an engineering achievement. More than 100 000 people from over 30 countries worked on it. Many had arrived in Australia after the Second World War, having lost their homes in Europe. Men who had been on opposite sides of the war now worked side by side in the same tunnels. Towns such as Cooma, once small and quiet, filled with new languages, foods and customs.\n\nThe work came at a cost. More than a hundred workers lost their lives during construction.\n\nToday, the Scheme still produces renewable electricity and supplies water to farms. Many historians also see it as a turning point that helped shape the multicultural nation Australia has become."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is written in the active voice?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Jaxon kicked the ball over the fence."},{"id":"b","text":"The ball was kicked over the fence by Jaxon."},{"id":"c","text":"The ball was kicked over the fence."},{"id":"d","text":"The goal was scored in the last minute."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'In the active voice the doer comes first and does the action: Jaxon (doer) kicked (action) the ball. The others use ''was kicked'' or ''was scored'', which is passive.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_easy', 1, '''Perhaps'', ''might'' and ''could'' are examples of low modality words.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'These words show that something is possible but not certain, so they are low modality. Words like ''must'', ''definitely'' and ''always'' are high modality.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'easy', 1, 'Who is the intended audience of this speech?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Students at Lakeside High''s assembly"},{"id":"b","text":"Lifeguards at Shellharbour beach"},{"id":"c","text":"Members of the NSW Parliament"},{"id":"d","text":"Tourists visiting Australia"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The title says it is a speech to assembly, and the speaker talks about ''every student at Lakeside High'' and asks listeners to sign a petition at the canteen.', NULL, '{"title":"Swim Safe: A Speech to Assembly","text":"Good morning, everyone.\n\nPicture this. It''s January. The sun is blazing, the sand is burning your feet, and the water at Shellharbour looks perfect. You run straight in. Ten seconds later, the bottom disappears and a current starts pulling you away from shore. Do you know what to do?\n\nIf you hesitated, you are not alone. Many Australian teenagers have never been taught how to spot a rip, how to float or how to signal for help. That is why I believe every student at Lakeside High should complete a free water-safety course before the end of Year 8.\n\nFirst, it saves lives. Rips are among the most dangerous hazards on Australian beaches, and many people caught in them are confident swimmers who simply did not recognise the danger.\n\nSecond, it is fair. Not every family can afford private lessons or lives near a pool. A course run through school means nobody misses out because of money or postcode.\n\nThird, it builds confidence. Students who know how to stay calm in the water are more likely to join in at the beach, at school carnivals and on camps.\n\nSome people say this is the job of parents, not schools. But schools already teach us to cross roads safely and to stay safe online. Why should the ocean be any different?\n\nWe live in a country surrounded by surf. Let''s make sure every one of us knows how to come home from the beach. Sign the petition at the canteen this week. Thank you."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'easy', 1, 'Put these events from the story in the order they happen.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Arlo finds a note taped beside the spare key."},{"id":"b","text":"Arlo sees boxes stacked on the kitchen table."},{"id":"c","text":"A man hammers a sign into the front lawn."},{"id":"d","text":"Arlo''s mum tells him Mrs Petrakis is moving to Wagga."},{"id":"e","text":"Arlo carries the basil home on his bike."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d","e"]'::jsonb, 'The story follows Arlo''s Sunday: the note at the back door, the boxes through the window, the sign out the front, then that evening''s news and the trip home with the basil.', NULL, '{"title":"The Spare Key","text":"For two years, Arlo had looked after Mrs Petrakis''s garden every Sunday. She paid him ten dollars and a slice of whatever she had baked, and she always stood at the back door telling him which tomatoes were ready, as if he hadn''t learnt that long ago.\n\nThis Sunday, the back door was closed. The spare key was under the pot of basil, where it always was, but a note was taped beside it: Arlo, take whatever you like from the garden. Love, E.\n\nHe picked the tomatoes anyway, then the beans. Through the kitchen window he could see cardboard boxes stacked on the table, each labelled in her careful capitals: KITCHEN, BOOKS, GIVE AWAY. The calendar on the fridge, usually crowded with appointments, had been taken down.\n\nWhen he came round the side of the house, a man in a blue suit was hammering a sign into the front lawn. Arlo didn''t need to read it. He had seen the same sign go up on three other houses in the street.\n\nThat evening his mum told him that Mrs Petrakis''s daughter was taking her to live near her in Wagga, somewhere with nurses and a lift. ''She asked if you would keep the basil,'' Mum said. ''She said you were the only one who ever listened to her about tomatoes.''\n\nArlo carried the pot home on his bike, one-handed, very slowly. He put it on his windowsill, where it would get the morning sun, and he watered it before he did anything else."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'easy', 1, 'Where is the poem set?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"On an early morning train travelling from Penrith to Central"},{"id":"b","text":"On a school bus at the end of the day"},{"id":"c","text":"In a library in the city"},{"id":"d","text":"At a cello concert"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The poem begins ''Six-ten from Penrith, still dark'' in a ''carriage'', passes Blacktown and Parramatta, and ends ''At Central''. The library is only a metaphor for the quiet passengers.', NULL, '{"title":"Western Line","text":"Six-ten from Penrith, still dark,\nthe carriage hums like a held breath.\nTradies with eyes closed,\na nurse in blue, her shoes in a bag,\na boy my age with a cello\ntaller than his mother.\n\nI know the rules of this carriage:\nno eye contact, no small talk,\nearbuds in, bag on your knees,\nwatch the window, not the faces.\nI have kept those rules for a whole year,\nsince the morning I started at my new school.\n\nNobody speaks. We are a library\ntravelling at a hundred kilometres an hour,\neach of us a closed book\nwith our titles turned to the wall.\n\nAt Blacktown the sun comes up\nlike someone switching on the house lights,\nand suddenly we can see each other:\nthe tiredness, the lunch boxes,\nthe hands holding on.\n\nSomewhere past Parramatta\nthe cello case tips and falls.\nThree strangers catch it at once.\nThe boy says sorry, sorry, thank you,\nand the nurse laughs, and the tradie laughs,\nand for one stop\nwe are not a library at all.\n\nAt Central we spill out\nonto the platform, into the city,\nback into our separate stories.\nBut I carry the laugh with me\nup the stairs, into the day,\nlike a coin found in a coat pocket."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'medium', 1, 'What can the reader infer from the boxes, the missing calendar and the sign on the lawn?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mrs Petrakis is moving out and her house is being sold."},{"id":"b","text":"Mrs Petrakis is having her kitchen renovated."},{"id":"c","text":"Mrs Petrakis has gone on a short holiday."},{"id":"d","text":"Mrs Petrakis is angry with Arlo."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Packed boxes labelled GIVE AWAY, a calendar taken down and a sign like the ones on other houses in the street all point to a move and a sale, which his mum confirms that evening.', NULL, '{"title":"The Spare Key","text":"For two years, Arlo had looked after Mrs Petrakis''s garden every Sunday. She paid him ten dollars and a slice of whatever she had baked, and she always stood at the back door telling him which tomatoes were ready, as if he hadn''t learnt that long ago.\n\nThis Sunday, the back door was closed. The spare key was under the pot of basil, where it always was, but a note was taped beside it: Arlo, take whatever you like from the garden. Love, E.\n\nHe picked the tomatoes anyway, then the beans. Through the kitchen window he could see cardboard boxes stacked on the table, each labelled in her careful capitals: KITCHEN, BOOKS, GIVE AWAY. The calendar on the fridge, usually crowded with appointments, had been taken down.\n\nWhen he came round the side of the house, a man in a blue suit was hammering a sign into the front lawn. Arlo didn''t need to read it. He had seen the same sign go up on three other houses in the street.\n\nThat evening his mum told him that Mrs Petrakis''s daughter was taking her to live near her in Wagga, somewhere with nurses and a lift. ''She asked if you would keep the basil,'' Mum said. ''She said you were the only one who ever listened to her about tomatoes.''\n\nArlo carried the pot home on his bike, one-handed, very slowly. He put it on his windowsill, where it would get the morning sun, and he watered it before he did anything else."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'medium', 1, 'Which rhetorical devices does the speaker use? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Rhetorical questions, such as ''Why should the ocean be any different?''"},{"id":"b","text":"Inclusive language, such as ''Let''s'' and ''every one of us''"},{"id":"c","text":"Rebutting an opposing view about whose job it is"},{"id":"d","text":"A quote from a famous Olympic swimmer"},{"id":"e","text":"An exact statistic about the number of drownings"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The speaker asks questions that don''t need answers, uses ''us'' and ''Let''s'' to include the audience, and answers the claim that it is parents'' job. There is no expert quote and no exact drowning figure.', NULL, '{"title":"Swim Safe: A Speech to Assembly","text":"Good morning, everyone.\n\nPicture this. It''s January. The sun is blazing, the sand is burning your feet, and the water at Shellharbour looks perfect. You run straight in. Ten seconds later, the bottom disappears and a current starts pulling you away from shore. Do you know what to do?\n\nIf you hesitated, you are not alone. Many Australian teenagers have never been taught how to spot a rip, how to float or how to signal for help. That is why I believe every student at Lakeside High should complete a free water-safety course before the end of Year 8.\n\nFirst, it saves lives. Rips are among the most dangerous hazards on Australian beaches, and many people caught in them are confident swimmers who simply did not recognise the danger.\n\nSecond, it is fair. Not every family can afford private lessons or lives near a pool. A course run through school means nobody misses out because of money or postcode.\n\nThird, it builds confidence. Students who know how to stay calm in the water are more likely to join in at the beach, at school carnivals and on camps.\n\nSome people say this is the job of parents, not schools. But schools already teach us to cross roads safely and to stay safe online. Why should the ocean be any different?\n\nWe live in a country surrounded by surf. Let''s make sure every one of us knows how to come home from the beach. Sign the petition at the canteen this week. Thank you."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'medium', 1, 'Why does the writer include the detail that men from opposite sides of the war worked side by side?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"To show that the Scheme also brought people together and helped change Australian society"},{"id":"b","text":"To explain how the tunnels were blasted through rock"},{"id":"c","text":"To prove that the war was still going on in the mountains"},{"id":"d","text":"To show that the workers did not get along"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'This detail sits in the paragraph that begins ''The Scheme was not only an engineering achievement''. It shows the Scheme''s human side: former enemies working together, and towns like Cooma becoming multicultural.', NULL, '{"title":"The Snowy Mountains Scheme","text":"High in the Australian Alps, beneath some of the coldest and steepest country on the continent, lies one of the largest engineering projects ever completed in Australia: the Snowy Mountains Hydro-electric Scheme.\n\nThe Scheme was built between 1949 and 1974. Its purpose was to capture water from the Snowy River and other rivers that flowed east to the sea, and send it west through the mountains. On the way, the falling water would spin turbines to produce electricity. Once it reached the other side, it could be released into the Murray and Murrumbidgee rivers to irrigate farms.\n\nTo do this, workers built large dams, power stations deep underground, and many kilometres of tunnels blasted through solid rock. They worked through winter blizzards in remote camps far from any town.\n\nThe Scheme was not only an engineering achievement. More than 100 000 people from over 30 countries worked on it. Many had arrived in Australia after the Second World War, having lost their homes in Europe. Men who had been on opposite sides of the war now worked side by side in the same tunnels. Towns such as Cooma, once small and quiet, filled with new languages, foods and customs.\n\nThe work came at a cost. More than a hundred workers lost their lives during construction.\n\nToday, the Scheme still produces renewable electricity and supplies water to farms. Many historians also see it as a turning point that helped shape the multicultural nation Australia has become."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'hard', 1, 'What does the poet mean by ''each of us a closed book / with our titles turned to the wall''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The passengers keep to themselves and don''t let others know who they are."},{"id":"b","text":"The passengers are all reading books on the train."},{"id":"c","text":"The passengers have forgotten their own names."},{"id":"d","text":"The train is carrying books to a library in the city."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'This extends the library metaphor. A closed book with its title hidden can''t be ''read'' by anyone, just as the silent passengers hide their lives from each other.', NULL, '{"title":"Western Line","text":"Six-ten from Penrith, still dark,\nthe carriage hums like a held breath.\nTradies with eyes closed,\na nurse in blue, her shoes in a bag,\na boy my age with a cello\ntaller than his mother.\n\nI know the rules of this carriage:\nno eye contact, no small talk,\nearbuds in, bag on your knees,\nwatch the window, not the faces.\nI have kept those rules for a whole year,\nsince the morning I started at my new school.\n\nNobody speaks. We are a library\ntravelling at a hundred kilometres an hour,\neach of us a closed book\nwith our titles turned to the wall.\n\nAt Blacktown the sun comes up\nlike someone switching on the house lights,\nand suddenly we can see each other:\nthe tiredness, the lunch boxes,\nthe hands holding on.\n\nSomewhere past Parramatta\nthe cello case tips and falls.\nThree strangers catch it at once.\nThe boy says sorry, sorry, thank you,\nand the nurse laughs, and the tradie laughs,\nand for one stop\nwe are not a library at all.\n\nAt Central we spill out\nonto the platform, into the city,\nback into our separate stories.\nBut I carry the laugh with me\nup the stairs, into the day,\nlike a coin found in a coat pocket."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence from the speech is its thesis statement?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"That is why I believe every student at Lakeside High should complete a free water-safety course before the end of Year 8."},{"id":"b","text":"Picture this."},{"id":"c","text":"Second, it is fair."},{"id":"d","text":"We live in a country surrounded by surf."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The thesis is the speaker''s main position, which the rest of the speech argues for. ''First'', ''Second'' and ''Third'' are reasons supporting it; ''Picture this'' is the hook.', NULL, '{"title":"Swim Safe: A Speech to Assembly","text":"Good morning, everyone.\n\nPicture this. It''s January. The sun is blazing, the sand is burning your feet, and the water at Shellharbour looks perfect. You run straight in. Ten seconds later, the bottom disappears and a current starts pulling you away from shore. Do you know what to do?\n\nIf you hesitated, you are not alone. Many Australian teenagers have never been taught how to spot a rip, how to float or how to signal for help. That is why I believe every student at Lakeside High should complete a free water-safety course before the end of Year 8.\n\nFirst, it saves lives. Rips are among the most dangerous hazards on Australian beaches, and many people caught in them are confident swimmers who simply did not recognise the danger.\n\nSecond, it is fair. Not every family can afford private lessons or lives near a pool. A course run through school means nobody misses out because of money or postcode.\n\nThird, it builds confidence. Students who know how to stay calm in the water are more likely to join in at the beach, at school carnivals and on camps.\n\nSome people say this is the job of parents, not schools. But schools already teach us to cross roads safely and to stay safe online. Why should the ocean be any different?\n\nWe live in a country surrounded by surf. Let''s make sure every one of us knows how to come home from the beach. Sign the petition at the canteen this week. Thank you."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'hard', 1, 'Which revision changes ''The council will close the pool'' from high to low modality, while keeping the council as the doer?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The council might close the pool."},{"id":"b","text":"The pool will be closed by the council."},{"id":"c","text":"The council must close the pool."},{"id":"d","text":"The council closed the pool."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Might'' lowers the certainty. ''Will be closed by'' changes the voice to passive, not the modality; ''must'' is still high; and ''closed'' just changes the tense.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_hard', 1, 'At the start, Mrs Petrakis tells Arlo about the tomatoes ''as if he hadn''t learnt that long ago''. Why is her message that he was ''the only one who ever listened to her about tomatoes'' important by the end?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It reveals that his patient listening, which seemed small to him, meant a great deal to her, and he honours that by caring for the basil."},{"id":"b","text":"It shows she thought Arlo knew nothing about gardening."},{"id":"c","text":"It proves Arlo never really listened to her."},{"id":"d","text":"It explains why she paid him only ten dollars."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The early detail hints Arlo already knew everything she told him, yet he kept listening. The ending shows she noticed and valued that kindness, and Arlo watering the basil ''before he did anything else'' shows he values her too.', NULL, '{"title":"The Spare Key","text":"For two years, Arlo had looked after Mrs Petrakis''s garden every Sunday. She paid him ten dollars and a slice of whatever she had baked, and she always stood at the back door telling him which tomatoes were ready, as if he hadn''t learnt that long ago.\n\nThis Sunday, the back door was closed. The spare key was under the pot of basil, where it always was, but a note was taped beside it: Arlo, take whatever you like from the garden. Love, E.\n\nHe picked the tomatoes anyway, then the beans. Through the kitchen window he could see cardboard boxes stacked on the table, each labelled in her careful capitals: KITCHEN, BOOKS, GIVE AWAY. The calendar on the fridge, usually crowded with appointments, had been taken down.\n\nWhen he came round the side of the house, a man in a blue suit was hammering a sign into the front lawn. Arlo didn''t need to read it. He had seen the same sign go up on three other houses in the street.\n\nThat evening his mum told him that Mrs Petrakis''s daughter was taking her to live near her in Wagga, somewhere with nurses and a lift. ''She asked if you would keep the basil,'' Mum said. ''She said you were the only one who ever listened to her about tomatoes.''\n\nArlo carried the pot home on his bike, one-handed, very slowly. He put it on his windowsill, where it would get the morning sun, and he watered it before he did anything else."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which ideas does the poem suggest? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Small, shared moments can break through the loneliness of city life."},{"id":"b","text":"The speaker treasures the shared laugh as something small but valuable."},{"id":"c","text":"The speaker may have felt lonely since starting at a new school."},{"id":"d","text":"The speaker is annoyed by the boy with the cello."},{"id":"e","text":"The poem warns that trains are dangerous."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The falling cello makes strangers laugh together, and the speaker keeps the laugh ''like a coin found in a coat pocket'', a small lucky treasure. Keeping the silent rules ''for a whole year'' since a new school hints at loneliness. Nothing suggests annoyance or danger.', NULL, '{"title":"Western Line","text":"Six-ten from Penrith, still dark,\nthe carriage hums like a held breath.\nTradies with eyes closed,\na nurse in blue, her shoes in a bag,\na boy my age with a cello\ntaller than his mother.\n\nI know the rules of this carriage:\nno eye contact, no small talk,\nearbuds in, bag on your knees,\nwatch the window, not the faces.\nI have kept those rules for a whole year,\nsince the morning I started at my new school.\n\nNobody speaks. We are a library\ntravelling at a hundred kilometres an hour,\neach of us a closed book\nwith our titles turned to the wall.\n\nAt Blacktown the sun comes up\nlike someone switching on the house lights,\nand suddenly we can see each other:\nthe tiredness, the lunch boxes,\nthe hands holding on.\n\nSomewhere past Parramatta\nthe cello case tips and falls.\nThree strangers catch it at once.\nThe boy says sorry, sorry, thank you,\nand the nurse laughs, and the tradie laughs,\nand for one stop\nwe are not a library at all.\n\nAt Central we spill out\nonto the platform, into the city,\nback into our separate stories.\nBut I carry the laugh with me\nup the stairs, into the day,\nlike a coin found in a coat pocket."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081101', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does the writer place the short paragraph ''The work came at a cost...'' just before the final paragraph?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"To balance the praise by acknowledging the human cost, giving a fuller and fairer picture before the conclusion"},{"id":"b","text":"To change the topic to a completely different project"},{"id":"c","text":"To argue that the Scheme should never have been built"},{"id":"d","text":"To give instructions for visiting the Snowy Mountains"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Most of the text celebrates the Scheme. A short, separate paragraph about the workers who died makes the reader pause, so the conclusion feels balanced rather than one-sided, without arguing against the Scheme.', NULL, '{"title":"The Snowy Mountains Scheme","text":"High in the Australian Alps, beneath some of the coldest and steepest country on the continent, lies one of the largest engineering projects ever completed in Australia: the Snowy Mountains Hydro-electric Scheme.\n\nThe Scheme was built between 1949 and 1974. Its purpose was to capture water from the Snowy River and other rivers that flowed east to the sea, and send it west through the mountains. On the way, the falling water would spin turbines to produce electricity. Once it reached the other side, it could be released into the Murray and Murrumbidgee rivers to irrigate farms.\n\nTo do this, workers built large dams, power stations deep underground, and many kilometres of tunnels blasted through solid rock. They worked through winter blizzards in remote camps far from any town.\n\nThe Scheme was not only an engineering achievement. More than 100 000 people from over 30 countries worked on it. Many had arrived in Australia after the Second World War, having lost their homes in Europe. Men who had been on opposite sides of the war now worked side by side in the same tunnels. Towns such as Cooma, once small and quiet, filled with new languages, foods and customs.\n\nThe work came at a cost. More than a hundred workers lost their lives during construction.\n\nToday, the Scheme still produces renewable electricity and supplies water to farms. Many historians also see it as a turning point that helped shape the multicultural nation Australia has become."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence uses a simile?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The classroom was as noisy as a zoo."},{"id":"b","text":"The classroom was a zoo."},{"id":"c","text":"The noisy classroom buzzed all day."},{"id":"d","text":"The classroom was very noisy."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A simile compares two things using ''like'' or ''as'': as noisy as a zoo. ''The classroom was a zoo'' says it IS a zoo, which is a metaphor.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_easy', 1, 'In ''The wind howled through the empty streets'', the wind is personified.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Howling is something a living creature does. Giving the wind this action is personification, and it makes the wind sound wild and lonely.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_easy', 1, 'Words that imitate the sounds they describe, such as ''buzz'', ''crackle'' and ''thud'', are examples of ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["onomatopoeia"]'::jsonb, 'Onomatopoeia is when a word sounds like the noise it names. Say ''crackle'' aloud and you can almost hear a fire.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'easy', 1, 'Put the steps of a TEE paragraph about language in the correct order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Name the technique the writer uses"},{"id":"b","text":"Quote an example from the text"},{"id":"c","text":"Explain the effect on the reader"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'TEE stands for Technique, Example, Effect. You name the technique, back it up with a quote, then explain what it makes the reader see or feel.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'easy', 1, '''The first gust slammed into the town like a door.'' Which technique is used here?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"simile"},{"id":"b","text":"metaphor"},{"id":"c","text":"alliteration"},{"id":"d","text":"onomatopoeia"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The gust is compared to a slamming door using ''like'', so it is a simile. It makes the wind feel sudden and forceful.', NULL, '{"title":"The Southerly","text":"All afternoon the heat had pressed down on Kiama like a hot iron. The bitumen shimmered. Dogs lay flat in the shade of parked cars, and the sea lay flat too, a sheet of tired blue glass.\n\nThen, just after five, the southerly arrived.\n\nIt came first as a smudge on the horizon, a bruise-coloured line of cloud swallowing the headland. The gulls noticed before the people did. They rose from the beach in a single shriek and wheeled inland.\n\nThe first gust slammed into the town like a door. Beach umbrellas cartwheeled down the sand. A boy''s hat leapt from his head and raced him to the water. The Norfolk pines bent and groaned, shaking their heavy arms at the sky as if they were furious at being woken.\n\nAnd the temperature fell. In ten minutes it dropped from thirty-eight degrees to twenty-two. People who had been hiding indoors spilled out onto their verandahs, faces turned up, eyes closed, drinking in the cool air.\n\nRain followed: fat, warm drops that hissed on the hot footpath and released that smell, the smell of dust and eucalyptus and relief, that every Australian child knows by heart.\n\nMy grandmother stood beside me at the window. ''There it is,'' she whispered, the way some people speak in church. ''The cool change.''\n\nOutside, the whole town seemed to breathe out."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'easy', 1, 'Write one example of onomatopoeia from the second paragraph (beginning ''Roller doors'').', 'short_answer', NULL, '["clatter","rattle","beeps","beep"]'::jsonb, 'The second paragraph has three sound words: doors ''clatter'', trolleys ''rattle'' and a forklift ''beeps''. Each word sounds like the noise it describes.', NULL, '{"title":"Saturday at the Market","text":"By seven o''clock, the Saturday market is wide awake.\n\nRoller doors clatter up. Trolleys rattle across the concrete. Somewhere a forklift beeps its way backwards, and the fruit sellers begin their morning song: ''Mangoes, mangoes, five for ten! Sweet as sugar, five for ten!''\n\nAt the bakery stall, crusty loaves are stacked like golden bricks, still breathing out warmth. Next door, a woman in a striped apron flips gozleme on a hot plate. The pastry sizzles and spits, and the smell of spinach, cheese and butter drifts down the aisle, tapping people on the shoulder and turning their heads.\n\nPyramids of purple plums, polished red apples and plump pale peaches crowd the tables. Mr Tran, who has run the herb stall for twenty years, crushes a leaf of Thai basil between his fingers and holds it out for me to smell. It is sharp and sweet and green, like summer squeezed into a single leaf.\n\nChildren dart between the legs of shoppers. A busker plays the violin beside the exit, her case open at her feet, and coins clink into it like small applause.\n\nBy eleven, the crowd thins. Stallholders pack away the leftovers and sweep up the squashed grapes and fallen lettuce leaves. The roller doors clang shut one by one.\n\nThe market goes quiet, but only until next Saturday, when the whole noisy, delicious show begins again."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'medium', 1, 'The storm is described as ''a bruise-coloured line of cloud''. What does ''bruise-coloured'' suggest?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The cloud is dark purple and grey, and threatening, as if the storm could hurt."},{"id":"b","text":"The cloud is bright and cheerful."},{"id":"c","text":"Someone in the town has been injured."},{"id":"d","text":"The cloud is the same colour as the sea."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A bruise is dark purple-blue and comes from being hurt. The word paints the colour and also gives the storm a sense of danger and violence.', NULL, '{"title":"The Southerly","text":"All afternoon the heat had pressed down on Kiama like a hot iron. The bitumen shimmered. Dogs lay flat in the shade of parked cars, and the sea lay flat too, a sheet of tired blue glass.\n\nThen, just after five, the southerly arrived.\n\nIt came first as a smudge on the horizon, a bruise-coloured line of cloud swallowing the headland. The gulls noticed before the people did. They rose from the beach in a single shriek and wheeled inland.\n\nThe first gust slammed into the town like a door. Beach umbrellas cartwheeled down the sand. A boy''s hat leapt from his head and raced him to the water. The Norfolk pines bent and groaned, shaking their heavy arms at the sky as if they were furious at being woken.\n\nAnd the temperature fell. In ten minutes it dropped from thirty-eight degrees to twenty-two. People who had been hiding indoors spilled out onto their verandahs, faces turned up, eyes closed, drinking in the cool air.\n\nRain followed: fat, warm drops that hissed on the hot footpath and released that smell, the smell of dust and eucalyptus and relief, that every Australian child knows by heart.\n\nMy grandmother stood beside me at the window. ''There it is,'' she whispered, the way some people speak in church. ''The cool change.''\n\nOutside, the whole town seemed to breathe out."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'medium', 1, 'What does the orange cross painted on the trunk symbolise?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The decision to cut the tree down"},{"id":"b","text":"A prize for the oldest tree"},{"id":"c","text":"A target for a school game"},{"id":"d","text":"The way to the classroom"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The council paints the cross because the tree is ''too old'' and ''too dangerous''. Marking it with a cross signals that it is going to be removed, so the cross stands for the threat to the tree.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Gum","text":"In the middle of the school oval\nstands the gum tree no one planted,\nolder than the classrooms,\nolder than the road.\n\nIts bark peels off in long grey ribbons\nlike pages torn from a diary,\nand underneath, the new skin glows\npink and cream, as soft as a promise.\n\nEvery recess it holds out its arms\nto whoever needs the shade:\nthe kids with no one to sit with,\nthe kids who need to cry in secret,\nthe kids who just want to read,\nthe kids who run rings round its trunk\nuntil the bell drags them inside.\n\nIt has heard every secret in this school\nand has never told a single one.\n\nLast spring the council came with orange paint\nand drew a cross upon its trunk.\nToo old, they said. Too dangerous.\nA branch might fall.\n\nWe signed petitions. We wrote letters.\nA Year 7 girl tied a yellow ribbon\nround its waist, and then another girl,\nand then the whole school.\n\nNow the tree wears a hundred ribbons\nthat flicker in the wind like little flames,\nand the orange cross is fading,\nand the gum tree is still standing,\nolder than the classrooms,\nolder than the road,\nholding out its arms."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'medium', 1, 'Which word has the most negative connotation when describing a thin person?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"scrawny"},{"id":"b","text":"slim"},{"id":"c","text":"slender"},{"id":"d","text":"lean"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'All four words mean thin, but ''scrawny'' suggests weak and unhealthy. ''Slim'' and ''slender'' sound attractive, and ''lean'' suggests fit and strong.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'hard', 1, 'Which techniques appear in the fourth paragraph (beginning ''Pyramids of purple plums'')? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"alliteration"},{"id":"b","text":"simile"},{"id":"c","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"d","text":"rhetorical question"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '''Purple plums'', ''polished'' and ''plump pale peaches'' repeat the p sound (alliteration), and ''like summer squeezed into a single leaf'' is a simile. There are no sound words or questions in that paragraph.', NULL, '{"title":"Saturday at the Market","text":"By seven o''clock, the Saturday market is wide awake.\n\nRoller doors clatter up. Trolleys rattle across the concrete. Somewhere a forklift beeps its way backwards, and the fruit sellers begin their morning song: ''Mangoes, mangoes, five for ten! Sweet as sugar, five for ten!''\n\nAt the bakery stall, crusty loaves are stacked like golden bricks, still breathing out warmth. Next door, a woman in a striped apron flips gozleme on a hot plate. The pastry sizzles and spits, and the smell of spinach, cheese and butter drifts down the aisle, tapping people on the shoulder and turning their heads.\n\nPyramids of purple plums, polished red apples and plump pale peaches crowd the tables. Mr Tran, who has run the herb stall for twenty years, crushes a leaf of Thai basil between his fingers and holds it out for me to smell. It is sharp and sweet and green, like summer squeezed into a single leaf.\n\nChildren dart between the legs of shoppers. A busker plays the violin beside the exit, her case open at her feet, and coins clink into it like small applause.\n\nBy eleven, the crowd thins. Stallholders pack away the leftovers and sweep up the squashed grapes and fallen lettuce leaves. The roller doors clang shut one by one.\n\nThe market goes quiet, but only until next Saturday, when the whole noisy, delicious show begins again."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'hard', 1, 'The tree ''holds out its arms'' and ''has heard every secret... and has never told a single one''. What is the effect of this personification?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It makes the tree seem like a kind, trustworthy friend, so losing it would feel like losing a person."},{"id":"b","text":"It makes the tree seem frightening and dangerous."},{"id":"c","text":"It shows the tree is too old and should be cut down."},{"id":"d","text":"It explains how gum trees grow."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Holding out arms is a welcoming gesture, and keeping secrets is what a loyal friend does. Giving the tree these human qualities makes readers care about it and support the students'' protest.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Gum","text":"In the middle of the school oval\nstands the gum tree no one planted,\nolder than the classrooms,\nolder than the road.\n\nIts bark peels off in long grey ribbons\nlike pages torn from a diary,\nand underneath, the new skin glows\npink and cream, as soft as a promise.\n\nEvery recess it holds out its arms\nto whoever needs the shade:\nthe kids with no one to sit with,\nthe kids who need to cry in secret,\nthe kids who just want to read,\nthe kids who run rings round its trunk\nuntil the bell drags them inside.\n\nIt has heard every secret in this school\nand has never told a single one.\n\nLast spring the council came with orange paint\nand drew a cross upon its trunk.\nToo old, they said. Too dangerous.\nA branch might fall.\n\nWe signed petitions. We wrote letters.\nA Year 7 girl tied a yellow ribbon\nround its waist, and then another girl,\nand then the whole school.\n\nNow the tree wears a hundred ribbons\nthat flicker in the wind like little flames,\nand the orange cross is fading,\nand the gum tree is still standing,\nolder than the classrooms,\nolder than the road,\nholding out its arms."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'hard', 1, '''My little brother is a tornado. He spins through the lounge room, flattens the cushions and leaves a trail of wreckage wherever he goes.'' Which technique is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"extended metaphor"},{"id":"b","text":"simile"},{"id":"c","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"d","text":"alliteration"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The brother is called a tornado (a metaphor), and the comparison keeps going with ''spins'', ''flattens'' and ''trail of wreckage''. A metaphor carried over several sentences is an extended metaphor.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_hard', 1, 'How does the imagery change across the passage, and why?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It moves from heavy, still images of heat to violent movement and finally relief, matching the shift from oppressive heat to the cool change."},{"id":"b","text":"It stays calm and peaceful throughout to show nothing changes."},{"id":"c","text":"It moves from happy images to sad ones to show the town has been destroyed."},{"id":"d","text":"It only describes sounds, so readers can''t picture the setting."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The opening uses heavy, flat images (hot iron, tired blue glass). The storm brings movement (slammed, cartwheeled, raced), and the ending brings relief (''drinking in the cool air'', ''breathe out''). The imagery tracks the town''s experience.', NULL, '{"title":"The Southerly","text":"All afternoon the heat had pressed down on Kiama like a hot iron. The bitumen shimmered. Dogs lay flat in the shade of parked cars, and the sea lay flat too, a sheet of tired blue glass.\n\nThen, just after five, the southerly arrived.\n\nIt came first as a smudge on the horizon, a bruise-coloured line of cloud swallowing the headland. The gulls noticed before the people did. They rose from the beach in a single shriek and wheeled inland.\n\nThe first gust slammed into the town like a door. Beach umbrellas cartwheeled down the sand. A boy''s hat leapt from his head and raced him to the water. The Norfolk pines bent and groaned, shaking their heavy arms at the sky as if they were furious at being woken.\n\nAnd the temperature fell. In ten minutes it dropped from thirty-eight degrees to twenty-two. People who had been hiding indoors spilled out onto their verandahs, faces turned up, eyes closed, drinking in the cool air.\n\nRain followed: fat, warm drops that hissed on the hot footpath and released that smell, the smell of dust and eucalyptus and relief, that every Australian child knows by heart.\n\nMy grandmother stood beside me at the window. ''There it is,'' she whispered, the way some people speak in church. ''The cool change.''\n\nOutside, the whole town seemed to breathe out."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which of these are reasonable readings of symbols in the poem? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The yellow ribbons represent the students'' care and their united protest."},{"id":"b","text":"The new pink and cream bark represents renewal and hope."},{"id":"c","text":"The fading orange cross suggests the threat to the tree is passing."},{"id":"d","text":"The road represents the students'' journey to university."},{"id":"e","text":"The diary pages represent the tree''s homework."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The ribbons spread from one girl to ''the whole school'', showing unity. The fresh bark ''as soft as a promise'' hints at new life, and a fading cross suggests the decision is losing its force. The road and diary readings have no support in the poem.', NULL, '{"title":"The Old Gum","text":"In the middle of the school oval\nstands the gum tree no one planted,\nolder than the classrooms,\nolder than the road.\n\nIts bark peels off in long grey ribbons\nlike pages torn from a diary,\nand underneath, the new skin glows\npink and cream, as soft as a promise.\n\nEvery recess it holds out its arms\nto whoever needs the shade:\nthe kids with no one to sit with,\nthe kids who need to cry in secret,\nthe kids who just want to read,\nthe kids who run rings round its trunk\nuntil the bell drags them inside.\n\nIt has heard every secret in this school\nand has never told a single one.\n\nLast spring the council came with orange paint\nand drew a cross upon its trunk.\nToo old, they said. Too dangerous.\nA branch might fall.\n\nWe signed petitions. We wrote letters.\nA Year 7 girl tied a yellow ribbon\nround its waist, and then another girl,\nand then the whole school.\n\nNow the tree wears a hundred ribbons\nthat flicker in the wind like little flames,\nand the orange cross is fading,\nand the gum tree is still standing,\nolder than the classrooms,\nolder than the road,\nholding out its arms."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_hard', 1, '''coins clink into it like small applause'' combines onomatopoeia and a simile. What effect does this create?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It turns the sound of money into praise, suggesting passers-by enjoy and appreciate the busker''s music."},{"id":"b","text":"It suggests the busker is rich and doesn''t need the money."},{"id":"c","text":"It shows the crowd is booing the busker."},{"id":"d","text":"It means the coins are too loud and annoying."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Clink'' lets us hear each coin, and comparing the sound to applause gives it meaning: every coin is like a small clap for her playing. Together they show the crowd''s appreciation.', NULL, '{"title":"Saturday at the Market","text":"By seven o''clock, the Saturday market is wide awake.\n\nRoller doors clatter up. Trolleys rattle across the concrete. Somewhere a forklift beeps its way backwards, and the fruit sellers begin their morning song: ''Mangoes, mangoes, five for ten! Sweet as sugar, five for ten!''\n\nAt the bakery stall, crusty loaves are stacked like golden bricks, still breathing out warmth. Next door, a woman in a striped apron flips gozleme on a hot plate. The pastry sizzles and spits, and the smell of spinach, cheese and butter drifts down the aisle, tapping people on the shoulder and turning their heads.\n\nPyramids of purple plums, polished red apples and plump pale peaches crowd the tables. Mr Tran, who has run the herb stall for twenty years, crushes a leaf of Thai basil between his fingers and holds it out for me to smell. It is sharp and sweet and green, like summer squeezed into a single leaf.\n\nChildren dart between the legs of shoppers. A busker plays the violin beside the exit, her case open at her feet, and coins clink into it like small applause.\n\nBy eleven, the crowd thins. Stallholders pack away the leftovers and sweep up the squashed grapes and fallen lettuce leaves. The roller doors clang shut one by one.\n\nThe market goes quiet, but only until next Saturday, when the whole noisy, delicious show begins again."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence uses a metaphor?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Her smile was sunshine."},{"id":"b","text":"Her smile was like sunshine."},{"id":"c","text":"She smiled brightly."},{"id":"d","text":"She smiled seven times."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A metaphor says one thing IS another: her smile was sunshine. Using ''like'' would make it a simile.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_easy', 1, 'Repeating the same first sound in nearby words, as in ''sizzling sausages sold on Saturday'', is called ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["alliteration"]'::jsonb, 'Alliteration repeats a beginning sound, here the s sound. It makes a phrase catchy and easy to remember.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_easy', 1, '''I''ve told you a million times to hang up your towel!'' is an example of hyperbole.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Nobody has really said it a million times. Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration used for effect, here to show frustration.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'easy', 1, '''My heart was a drum.'' Which technique is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"metaphor"},{"id":"b","text":"simile"},{"id":"c","text":"onomatopoeia"},{"id":"d","text":"hyperbole"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The heart is said to BE a drum, with no ''like'' or ''as'', so it is a metaphor. It suggests a loud, pounding heartbeat from fear.', NULL, '{"title":"Fog on the Ridge","text":"We set out from Wentworth Falls at dawn, and for the first hour the valley lay below us in perfect, crystal sunlight. The cliffs glowed orange. A lyrebird somewhere in the gully was running through its whole repertoire: whipbird, kookaburra, car alarm.\n\nThen the fog came.\n\nIt did not roll in the way fog does in films. It simply rose out of the valley, soft and silent, like breath on a mirror. Within minutes the cliffs were gone. Then the trees beside the track were gone. Then Dad, three steps ahead of me, became a grey shape, then a voice, then nothing at all.\n\n''Stay close,'' his voice said from somewhere.\n\nThe fog wrapped around us like a damp grey blanket. It beaded on my eyelashes and the hairs on my arms. Every sound was swallowed. Even the lyrebird had stopped, as if it too was holding its breath.\n\nI put my hand on the cold sandstone wall beside the track and followed it, step by step, the way you might follow a handrail in the dark. My heart was a drum.\n\nWe walked like that for twenty minutes, though it felt like a year. And then, as quickly as it had come, the fog thinned. A gum tree appeared, then a railing, then Dad, grinning, his beanie sparkling with tiny drops of water.\n\n''Well,'' he said. ''That was an adventure.''\n\nI didn''t tell him I had been counting my steps the whole way, just to hear a number that wasn''t silence."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'easy', 1, 'Which phrases from the ad are similes? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"glowing like honey in the afternoon light"},{"id":"b","text":"scattered like spilled sugar across a black tablecloth"},{"id":"c","text":"a sunrise that sets the desert on fire"},{"id":"d","text":"the sky melts from orange to violet"},{"id":"e","text":"Leave the traffic behind"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Only the first two compare using ''like''. ''Sets the desert on fire'' and ''the sky melts'' are metaphors, and ''Leave the traffic behind'' is a command, not a comparison.', NULL, '{"title":"Broken Hill: Where the Sky Never Ends","text":"Leave the traffic behind. Leave the noise behind. Out here, on the edge of the great red outback, you''ll find a city like no other.\n\nWake to a sunrise that sets the desert on fire, painting the plains in crimson and gold. Wander wide streets lined with grand old buildings from the silver-mining boom, their stone walls glowing like honey in the afternoon light.\n\nExplore galleries bursting with colour, created by artists who have fallen in love with this wild, untamed land. Drive half an hour to Silverton, a ghost town frozen in time, where the old hotel has starred in more films than most actors.\n\nAt dusk, climb to the Living Desert Sculptures and watch the sun sink behind the stone giants as the sky melts from orange to violet. Then, as darkness falls, look up. Far from city lights, the stars don''t twinkle. They blaze. Millions of them, scattered like spilled sugar across a black tablecloth.\n\nHere, the air is clean, the people are warm, and time moves at the pace of a lazy kangaroo. Share a yarn at a country pub, taste a dusty-red sunset from a camp chair, and remember what quiet sounds like.\n\nBroken Hill. Come for the sky. Stay for the stories.\n\nBook your outback escape today at the Visitor Information Centre."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'easy', 1, 'In many stories, what does a white dove usually symbolise?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"peace"},{"id":"b","text":"danger"},{"id":"c","text":"greed"},{"id":"d","text":"anger"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The white dove has long been used as a symbol of peace, so writers can use it to suggest calm or the end of a conflict.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'medium', 1, '''a rusted red beetle / asleep on four flat tyres, / dreaming of the Hume''. Which two techniques are used?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"metaphor and personification"},{"id":"b","text":"simile and onomatopoeia"},{"id":"c","text":"hyperbole and simile"},{"id":"d","text":"onomatopoeia and rhetorical question"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The ute is called a beetle without ''like'' (metaphor), and it is ''asleep'' and ''dreaming'', which are human actions (personification). Together they make the ute seem alive but waiting.', NULL, '{"title":"Pop''s Ute","text":"It sits in the shed now,\na rusted red beetle\nasleep on four flat tyres,\ndreaming of the Hume.\n\nPop drove it for forty years,\nto the sale yards, to the coast,\nto the hospital the night my mum was born,\none hand on the wheel, one arm out the window,\nbrowned like a saddle in the sun.\n\nThe seats still smell of him:\ndiesel and wool and peppermints.\nThe radio is stuck forever\non the cricket station,\ncrackling with the ghosts of summers.\n\nIn the glovebox: a road map of New South Wales\nfolded soft as cloth,\na pencil, a fuel receipt from 1991,\nand a photo of Nan, laughing,\nher hair blown sideways by the wind.\n\nDad says we should sell it.\nIt''s only scrap, he says.\nIt''s only taking up room.\n\nBut on Sundays, when no one is watching,\nI climb into the driver''s seat\nand put my hands where Pop''s hands were,\non the wheel worn smooth as a river stone.\n\nI can''t reach the pedals yet.\nI don''t know where I''m going.\nBut the key is in my pocket,\nwarm from my hand,\nand one day, I tell the ute,\none day we''ll take the long way home."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'medium', 1, 'In the fifth paragraph, the fog wraps around them like a damp grey ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["blanket"]'::jsonb, 'The simile ''like a damp grey blanket'' helps us feel the fog: thick, close and wet against the skin.', NULL, '{"title":"Fog on the Ridge","text":"We set out from Wentworth Falls at dawn, and for the first hour the valley lay below us in perfect, crystal sunlight. The cliffs glowed orange. A lyrebird somewhere in the gully was running through its whole repertoire: whipbird, kookaburra, car alarm.\n\nThen the fog came.\n\nIt did not roll in the way fog does in films. It simply rose out of the valley, soft and silent, like breath on a mirror. Within minutes the cliffs were gone. Then the trees beside the track were gone. Then Dad, three steps ahead of me, became a grey shape, then a voice, then nothing at all.\n\n''Stay close,'' his voice said from somewhere.\n\nThe fog wrapped around us like a damp grey blanket. It beaded on my eyelashes and the hairs on my arms. Every sound was swallowed. Even the lyrebird had stopped, as if it too was holding its breath.\n\nI put my hand on the cold sandstone wall beside the track and followed it, step by step, the way you might follow a handrail in the dark. My heart was a drum.\n\nWe walked like that for twenty minutes, though it felt like a year. And then, as quickly as it had come, the fog thinned. A gum tree appeared, then a railing, then Dad, grinning, his beanie sparkling with tiny drops of water.\n\n''Well,'' he said. ''That was an adventure.''\n\nI didn''t tell him I had been counting my steps the whole way, just to hear a number that wasn''t silence."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from the weakest to the strongest meaning.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"damp"},{"id":"b","text":"wet"},{"id":"c","text":"soaked"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Damp is slightly wet, wet is fully wet, and soaked means wet right through. Choosing the right strength of word makes writing precise.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'hard', 1, 'The ad describes the land as ''wild, untamed''. Why did the writer choose ''untamed'' rather than ''empty''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"''Untamed'' suggests exciting, natural beauty, while ''empty'' suggests there is nothing to see."},{"id":"b","text":"''Untamed'' is shorter than ''empty''."},{"id":"c","text":"''Untamed'' warns tourists that the outback is dangerous."},{"id":"d","text":"The two words have exactly the same connotation."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Both could describe a place with few people, but their connotations differ. An ad wants readers to visit, so it uses ''untamed'', which sounds adventurous, not ''empty'', which sounds dull.', NULL, '{"title":"Broken Hill: Where the Sky Never Ends","text":"Leave the traffic behind. Leave the noise behind. Out here, on the edge of the great red outback, you''ll find a city like no other.\n\nWake to a sunrise that sets the desert on fire, painting the plains in crimson and gold. Wander wide streets lined with grand old buildings from the silver-mining boom, their stone walls glowing like honey in the afternoon light.\n\nExplore galleries bursting with colour, created by artists who have fallen in love with this wild, untamed land. Drive half an hour to Silverton, a ghost town frozen in time, where the old hotel has starred in more films than most actors.\n\nAt dusk, climb to the Living Desert Sculptures and watch the sun sink behind the stone giants as the sky melts from orange to violet. Then, as darkness falls, look up. Far from city lights, the stars don''t twinkle. They blaze. Millions of them, scattered like spilled sugar across a black tablecloth.\n\nHere, the air is clean, the people are warm, and time moves at the pace of a lazy kangaroo. Share a yarn at a country pub, taste a dusty-red sunset from a camp chair, and remember what quiet sounds like.\n\nBroken Hill. Come for the sky. Stay for the stories.\n\nBook your outback escape today at the Visitor Information Centre."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'hard', 1, 'What does the key in the speaker''s pocket most likely symbolise?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The speaker''s connection to Pop and the hope of carrying his memory into the future"},{"id":"b","text":"The speaker''s plan to sell the ute"},{"id":"c","text":"The speaker''s fear of driving"},{"id":"d","text":"A key to Pop''s house"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The key is ''warm from my hand'' and linked to the promise ''one day we''ll take the long way home''. It stands for keeping Pop''s ute, and Pop''s memory, alive.', NULL, '{"title":"Pop''s Ute","text":"It sits in the shed now,\na rusted red beetle\nasleep on four flat tyres,\ndreaming of the Hume.\n\nPop drove it for forty years,\nto the sale yards, to the coast,\nto the hospital the night my mum was born,\none hand on the wheel, one arm out the window,\nbrowned like a saddle in the sun.\n\nThe seats still smell of him:\ndiesel and wool and peppermints.\nThe radio is stuck forever\non the cricket station,\ncrackling with the ghosts of summers.\n\nIn the glovebox: a road map of New South Wales\nfolded soft as cloth,\na pencil, a fuel receipt from 1991,\nand a photo of Nan, laughing,\nher hair blown sideways by the wind.\n\nDad says we should sell it.\nIt''s only scrap, he says.\nIt''s only taking up room.\n\nBut on Sundays, when no one is watching,\nI climb into the driver''s seat\nand put my hands where Pop''s hands were,\non the wheel worn smooth as a river stone.\n\nI can''t reach the pedals yet.\nI don''t know where I''m going.\nBut the key is in my pocket,\nwarm from my hand,\nand one day, I tell the ute,\none day we''ll take the long way home."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'hard', 1, '''Then Dad, three steps ahead of me, became a grey shape, then a voice, then nothing at all.'' What is the effect of this sentence''s structure?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The step-by-step list mirrors Dad slowly disappearing, building the narrator''s fear."},{"id":"b","text":"It shows that Dad is walking too fast."},{"id":"c","text":"It lists the different animals the narrator hears."},{"id":"d","text":"It slows the story down because nothing important happens."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Each ''then'' takes away more of Dad, from a shape to a sound to nothing. The reader experiences the loss in stages, just as the narrator does, which increases the tension.', NULL, '{"title":"Fog on the Ridge","text":"We set out from Wentworth Falls at dawn, and for the first hour the valley lay below us in perfect, crystal sunlight. The cliffs glowed orange. A lyrebird somewhere in the gully was running through its whole repertoire: whipbird, kookaburra, car alarm.\n\nThen the fog came.\n\nIt did not roll in the way fog does in films. It simply rose out of the valley, soft and silent, like breath on a mirror. Within minutes the cliffs were gone. Then the trees beside the track were gone. Then Dad, three steps ahead of me, became a grey shape, then a voice, then nothing at all.\n\n''Stay close,'' his voice said from somewhere.\n\nThe fog wrapped around us like a damp grey blanket. It beaded on my eyelashes and the hairs on my arms. Every sound was swallowed. Even the lyrebird had stopped, as if it too was holding its breath.\n\nI put my hand on the cold sandstone wall beside the track and followed it, step by step, the way you might follow a handrail in the dark. My heart was a drum.\n\nWe walked like that for twenty minutes, though it felt like a year. And then, as quickly as it had come, the fog thinned. A gum tree appeared, then a railing, then Dad, grinning, his beanie sparkling with tiny drops of water.\n\n''Well,'' he said. ''That was an adventure.''\n\nI didn''t tell him I had been counting my steps the whole way, just to hear a number that wasn''t silence."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_hard', 1, 'Dad calls the ute ''only scrap''. What does the contrast between Dad''s words and the speaker''s imagery suggest? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"To Dad the ute is an object, but to the speaker it holds memories of Pop."},{"id":"b","text":"The smells and sounds in the ute make Pop still feel present."},{"id":"c","text":"The speaker is not ready to let go of Pop."},{"id":"d","text":"The speaker agrees that the ute should be sold."},{"id":"e","text":"Dad never knew Pop."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Dad''s flat, repeated ''It''s only...'' contrasts with rich sensory imagery: Pop''s smell, the crackling radio, the worn wheel. The speaker secretly sits in the ute on Sundays, showing they want to hold on. Nothing suggests agreement or that Dad never knew Pop.', NULL, '{"title":"Pop''s Ute","text":"It sits in the shed now,\na rusted red beetle\nasleep on four flat tyres,\ndreaming of the Hume.\n\nPop drove it for forty years,\nto the sale yards, to the coast,\nto the hospital the night my mum was born,\none hand on the wheel, one arm out the window,\nbrowned like a saddle in the sun.\n\nThe seats still smell of him:\ndiesel and wool and peppermints.\nThe radio is stuck forever\non the cricket station,\ncrackling with the ghosts of summers.\n\nIn the glovebox: a road map of New South Wales\nfolded soft as cloth,\na pencil, a fuel receipt from 1991,\nand a photo of Nan, laughing,\nher hair blown sideways by the wind.\n\nDad says we should sell it.\nIt''s only scrap, he says.\nIt''s only taking up room.\n\nBut on Sundays, when no one is watching,\nI climb into the driver''s seat\nand put my hands where Pop''s hands were,\non the wheel worn smooth as a river stone.\n\nI can''t reach the pedals yet.\nI don''t know where I''m going.\nBut the key is in my pocket,\nwarm from my hand,\nand one day, I tell the ute,\none day we''ll take the long way home."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_hard', 1, 'How does the ad mainly use imagery to achieve its purpose?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Vivid colour, light and sky imagery helps readers picture being there, persuading them to book a trip."},{"id":"b","text":"It uses neutral facts and figures so readers can compare holiday prices."},{"id":"c","text":"It tells a story about a family to entertain readers."},{"id":"d","text":"It describes dangers to warn readers away from the outback."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Crimson and gold sunrises, honey-coloured walls and blazing stars appeal to the senses so readers imagine the experience. The ad ends with ''Book your outback escape today'', showing that the imagery serves persuasion.', NULL, '{"title":"Broken Hill: Where the Sky Never Ends","text":"Leave the traffic behind. Leave the noise behind. Out here, on the edge of the great red outback, you''ll find a city like no other.\n\nWake to a sunrise that sets the desert on fire, painting the plains in crimson and gold. Wander wide streets lined with grand old buildings from the silver-mining boom, their stone walls glowing like honey in the afternoon light.\n\nExplore galleries bursting with colour, created by artists who have fallen in love with this wild, untamed land. Drive half an hour to Silverton, a ghost town frozen in time, where the old hotel has starred in more films than most actors.\n\nAt dusk, climb to the Living Desert Sculptures and watch the sun sink behind the stone giants as the sky melts from orange to violet. Then, as darkness falls, look up. Far from city lights, the stars don''t twinkle. They blaze. Millions of them, scattered like spilled sugar across a black tablecloth.\n\nHere, the air is clean, the people are warm, and time moves at the pace of a lazy kangaroo. Share a yarn at a country pub, taste a dusty-red sunset from a camp chair, and remember what quiet sounds like.\n\nBroken Hill. Come for the sky. Stay for the stories.\n\nBook your outback escape today at the Visitor Information Centre."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081102', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which revision of ''It was a hot day'' creates the most vivid imagery and avoids cliché?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The heat pressed against the classroom windows, and the asphalt outside softened like chewing gum."},{"id":"b","text":"It was as hot as an oven."},{"id":"c","text":"It was a really, really hot day."},{"id":"d","text":"It was the hottest day in the history of the universe."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''As hot as an oven'' is a cliché, ''really, really'' adds no picture, and the last is tired hyperbole. The first uses fresh, specific images we can see and feel: heat pressing on windows and asphalt going soft.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is written in the first person?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I grabbed my towel and ran to the pool."},{"id":"b","text":"She grabbed her towel and ran to the pool."},{"id":"c","text":"You grab your towel and run to the pool."},{"id":"d","text":"They grabbed their towels and ran to the pool."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'First person uses ''I'', ''me'' and ''my''. ''She'' and ''they'' are third person, and ''you'' is second person.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_easy', 1, '''Mount Kosciuszko is the highest mountain on mainland Australia'' is a statement of fact.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'A fact can be checked and proven. You can look up the heights of mountains, so this is a fact, not an opinion.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_easy', 1, 'A text that speaks directly to the reader using ''you'', as in ''You open the door and step inside'', is written in the ___ person.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["second","2nd"]'::jsonb, 'First person is ''I'', second person is ''you'', and third person is ''he'', ''she'' or ''they''. Second person puts the reader inside the action.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'easy', 1, 'From which point of view is this story told?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"first person, by the player"},{"id":"b","text":"first person, by Coach Barnes"},{"id":"c","text":"second person"},{"id":"d","text":"third-person omniscient"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The narrator uses ''I'' and ''me'' and is the goal shooter who hurts her ankle, so it is first person from the player''s point of view.', NULL, '{"title":"Trial Day","text":"I knew Coach Barnes didn''t rate me the moment she called the names for the A team and mine wasn''t one of them.\n\nI''d been the best goal shooter at my old club in Orange. Everyone said so. But here in Bathurst, it was as if none of that counted. For three weeks I''d turned up early, stayed late and shot until my arms ached, and still Coach watched me with that same small frown.\n\nAt Saturday''s trial game I went up for a rebound and landed badly. My ankle twisted, but I didn''t say anything. I wasn''t going to give her another reason to drop me. I played the whole second half, limping only when I was sure she wasn''t looking.\n\nAfterwards she called me over. I was certain this was it.\n\n''How''s the ankle?'' she asked.\n\n''Fine,'' I said.\n\nShe looked at me for a long moment. ''I''ve had my eye on you since your first training session,'' she said. ''You''ve got the best shooting technique in this club. But you''ve been training with that left knee strapped for three weeks, and now this. I''m not putting you on court until a physio clears you.''\n\nI opened my mouth, then shut it.\n\n''The A team is set for the first round,'' she added. ''Not for the season.''\n\nI walked home very slowly, and it wasn''t only because of my ankle."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these sentences are opinions? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Netball is the best sport in the world."},{"id":"b","text":"Winter is a miserable season."},{"id":"c","text":"Our canteen sells the tastiest pies in Newcastle."},{"id":"d","text":"Sydney is the capital of New South Wales."},{"id":"e","text":"The Murray is Australia''s longest river."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '''Best'', ''miserable'' and ''tastiest'' are judgements people can disagree about, so those are opinions. The capital city and the longest river can be checked, so they are facts.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'easy', 1, 'Which side does this news report mostly favour?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The residents who oppose the skate park"},{"id":"b","text":"The council that approved the skate park"},{"id":"c","text":"The young people who want the skate park"},{"id":"d","text":"It treats every side equally."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Every person quoted is against the plan, the opening calls residents ''furious'', and the council''s view is given only one line beginning ''The council claims''. The report leans toward the residents.', NULL, '{"title":"Skate Park Plan Sparks Outrage","text":"Residents of quiet Elm Street are furious after Hillvale Council revealed plans to build a skate park on the much-loved Jacaranda Reserve.\n\nThe $600 000 project, approved at Tuesday night''s council meeting, will replace the reserve''s lawn and picnic tables with concrete ramps, rails and a bowl.\n\nElm Street has long been one of Hillvale''s most desirable addresses, with tidy gardens and tree-lined footpaths that residents have worked hard to maintain.\n\nLong-time resident Margaret Hill, 72, who has lived opposite the reserve for 40 years, said she was ''devastated''.\n\n''This is a peaceful place for families and older people,'' she said. ''Now we''ll have noise, graffiti and gangs of teenagers hanging around at all hours.''\n\nFellow resident Doug Pearce agreed. ''Nobody asked us,'' he said. ''The council has simply ignored the people who actually pay the rates.''\n\nLocal business owner Craig Dunn warned that property values in the street could plummet.\n\nThe council claims the skate park will provide ''a safe space for young people''. However, residents fear the reserve will become a magnet for trouble.\n\nA petition against the plan has already attracted more than 300 signatures. Residents will protest outside the council chambers on Friday, and many say they will keep fighting until the plan is scrapped."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'medium', 1, 'What does the reader learn by the end that the narrator misunderstood at the start?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Coach Barnes rated her highly and was worried about her injuries, not doubting her talent."},{"id":"b","text":"Coach Barnes really did think she was a poor player."},{"id":"c","text":"The narrator had never played netball before."},{"id":"d","text":"The A team had already been picked for the whole season."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The narrator reads Coach''s frown as disapproval, but Coach says she has ''the best shooting technique in this club'' and is protecting her strapped knee and ankle. A first-person narrator only knows what she thinks, so she can get things wrong.', NULL, '{"title":"Trial Day","text":"I knew Coach Barnes didn''t rate me the moment she called the names for the A team and mine wasn''t one of them.\n\nI''d been the best goal shooter at my old club in Orange. Everyone said so. But here in Bathurst, it was as if none of that counted. For three weeks I''d turned up early, stayed late and shot until my arms ached, and still Coach watched me with that same small frown.\n\nAt Saturday''s trial game I went up for a rebound and landed badly. My ankle twisted, but I didn''t say anything. I wasn''t going to give her another reason to drop me. I played the whole second half, limping only when I was sure she wasn''t looking.\n\nAfterwards she called me over. I was certain this was it.\n\n''How''s the ankle?'' she asked.\n\n''Fine,'' I said.\n\nShe looked at me for a long moment. ''I''ve had my eye on you since your first training session,'' she said. ''You''ve got the best shooting technique in this club. But you''ve been training with that left knee strapped for three weeks, and now this. I''m not putting you on court until a physio clears you.''\n\nI opened my mouth, then shut it.\n\n''The A team is set for the first round,'' she added. ''Not for the season.''\n\nI walked home very slowly, and it wasn''t only because of my ankle."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'medium', 1, 'Which words or phrases are loaded language, chosen to make readers feel a certain way? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"furious"},{"id":"b","text":"much-loved"},{"id":"c","text":"a magnet for trouble"},{"id":"d","text":"approved at Tuesday night''s council meeting"},{"id":"e","text":"concrete ramps, rails and a bowl"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '''Furious'', ''much-loved'' and ''magnet for trouble'' carry strong feelings that push readers against the skate park. The meeting and the list of equipment are neutral, factual details.', NULL, '{"title":"Skate Park Plan Sparks Outrage","text":"Residents of quiet Elm Street are furious after Hillvale Council revealed plans to build a skate park on the much-loved Jacaranda Reserve.\n\nThe $600 000 project, approved at Tuesday night''s council meeting, will replace the reserve''s lawn and picnic tables with concrete ramps, rails and a bowl.\n\nElm Street has long been one of Hillvale''s most desirable addresses, with tidy gardens and tree-lined footpaths that residents have worked hard to maintain.\n\nLong-time resident Margaret Hill, 72, who has lived opposite the reserve for 40 years, said she was ''devastated''.\n\n''This is a peaceful place for families and older people,'' she said. ''Now we''ll have noise, graffiti and gangs of teenagers hanging around at all hours.''\n\nFellow resident Doug Pearce agreed. ''Nobody asked us,'' he said. ''The council has simply ignored the people who actually pay the rates.''\n\nLocal business owner Craig Dunn warned that property values in the street could plummet.\n\nThe council claims the skate park will provide ''a safe space for young people''. However, residents fear the reserve will become a magnet for trouble.\n\nA petition against the plan has already attracted more than 300 signatures. Residents will protest outside the council chambers on Friday, and many say they will keep fighting until the plan is scrapped."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'medium', 1, 'Put these sentences in order from most objective to most biased.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"The train arrived 15 minutes late."},{"id":"b","text":"The train was late again, which was frustrating."},{"id":"c","text":"The train company is a hopeless joke run by clowns."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'The first is a checkable fact. The second adds a mild opinion (''frustrating''). The third uses insulting, exaggerated language and gives no evidence, so it is the most biased.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'hard', 1, 'Whose perspective is missing from the report, making it one-sided?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The young people who would use the skate park"},{"id":"b","text":"Long-time Elm Street residents"},{"id":"c","text":"Local business owners"},{"id":"d","text":"People who signed the petition"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The report quotes older residents and a business owner, but no young person is asked. Teenagers are only described as ''gangs'' by others, so they are represented without being given a voice.', NULL, '{"title":"Skate Park Plan Sparks Outrage","text":"Residents of quiet Elm Street are furious after Hillvale Council revealed plans to build a skate park on the much-loved Jacaranda Reserve.\n\nThe $600 000 project, approved at Tuesday night''s council meeting, will replace the reserve''s lawn and picnic tables with concrete ramps, rails and a bowl.\n\nElm Street has long been one of Hillvale''s most desirable addresses, with tidy gardens and tree-lined footpaths that residents have worked hard to maintain.\n\nLong-time resident Margaret Hill, 72, who has lived opposite the reserve for 40 years, said she was ''devastated''.\n\n''This is a peaceful place for families and older people,'' she said. ''Now we''ll have noise, graffiti and gangs of teenagers hanging around at all hours.''\n\nFellow resident Doug Pearce agreed. ''Nobody asked us,'' he said. ''The council has simply ignored the people who actually pay the rates.''\n\nLocal business owner Craig Dunn warned that property values in the street could plummet.\n\nThe council claims the skate park will provide ''a safe space for young people''. However, residents fear the reserve will become a magnet for trouble.\n\nA petition against the plan has already attracted more than 300 signatures. Residents will protest outside the council chambers on Friday, and many say they will keep fighting until the plan is scrapped."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'hard', 1, 'William writes that the land is ''empty and waiting for us''. How might a reader today view this differently?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Today we recognise it was Wiradjuri Country, already home to Aboriginal people, and that his view reflects colonial attitudes of his time."},{"id":"b","text":"Today we know there was never any gold on the Turon River."},{"id":"c","text":"Today we know the land really was empty in 1851."},{"id":"d","text":"A modern reader would agree that the land was made only for diggers."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Writers are shaped by their context. In 1851 many settlers described the land as empty, ignoring the Aboriginal people who lived there. A modern reader, in a different context, notices whose presence is left out.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter from the Turon, 1851","text":"Sofala, Turon River\n14th July, 1851\n\nMy dear Mother,\n\nI write to you by candlelight from a tent no bigger than our kitchen table, with my fingers so cold I can scarcely hold the pen. Do not be alarmed. I am well, and in better spirits than I have been in my life.\n\nYou will have read in the Sydney papers of the gold found on the Turon. Believe me, Mother, the papers do not tell the half of it. Every day more men arrive, clerks and sailors and shopkeepers, all with the same fever in their eyes. Yesterday a man not twenty yards from my claim washed out a nugget the size of a pigeon''s egg.\n\nI have not yet been so fortunate. Tom Jenkins and I have dug and washed from first light until dark these three weeks, and have only a little dust to show for it. But we are certain our luck will turn. This is a land made for men who are willing to work, and it is empty and waiting for us.\n\nThe prices here are a scandal. Flour costs four times what it does in Sydney, and the storekeepers grow fat while honest diggers go hungry. Still, I would not trade places with any clerk in George Street.\n\nPlease tell Father I have not forgotten my promise. When I have made enough, I shall come home and set up the shop as we planned.\n\nYour loving son,\nWilliam"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'hard', 1, 'How would the story change if it were told by a third-person omniscient narrator?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Readers would know Coach''s real reasons from the start, so the surprise at the end would be lost."},{"id":"b","text":"The story would have to be set in a different town."},{"id":"c","text":"The narrator would have to use ''you'' throughout."},{"id":"d","text":"Readers would know less about the player''s feelings than before."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'An omniscient narrator can see into every character''s mind, including Coach Barnes''s. The twist works because we only see the player''s mistaken view; knowing everything would remove that.', NULL, '{"title":"Trial Day","text":"I knew Coach Barnes didn''t rate me the moment she called the names for the A team and mine wasn''t one of them.\n\nI''d been the best goal shooter at my old club in Orange. Everyone said so. But here in Bathurst, it was as if none of that counted. For three weeks I''d turned up early, stayed late and shot until my arms ached, and still Coach watched me with that same small frown.\n\nAt Saturday''s trial game I went up for a rebound and landed badly. My ankle twisted, but I didn''t say anything. I wasn''t going to give her another reason to drop me. I played the whole second half, limping only when I was sure she wasn''t looking.\n\nAfterwards she called me over. I was certain this was it.\n\n''How''s the ankle?'' she asked.\n\n''Fine,'' I said.\n\nShe looked at me for a long moment. ''I''ve had my eye on you since your first training session,'' she said. ''You''ve got the best shooting technique in this club. But you''ve been training with that left knee strapped for three weeks, and now this. I''m not putting you on court until a physio clears you.''\n\nI opened my mouth, then shut it.\n\n''The A team is set for the first round,'' she added. ''Not for the season.''\n\nI walked home very slowly, and it wasn''t only because of my ankle."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which features of the letter are shaped by its context (who wrote it, when, and why)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"He reassures his mother (''Do not be alarmed'') because he is writing to worried family far away."},{"id":"b","text":"He mentions the Sydney papers because news of the gold rush travelled through newspapers in 1851."},{"id":"c","text":"His complaint about flour prices reflects the real hardships of life on the goldfields."},{"id":"d","text":"He writes formally because the letter is a school essay."},{"id":"e","text":"He describes the gold as worthless because nobody wanted it."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'His audience (his mother), his time (news by newspaper in 1851) and his situation (expensive supplies on the diggings) all shape what he writes. The letter is not a school essay, and he clearly values gold.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter from the Turon, 1851","text":"Sofala, Turon River\n14th July, 1851\n\nMy dear Mother,\n\nI write to you by candlelight from a tent no bigger than our kitchen table, with my fingers so cold I can scarcely hold the pen. Do not be alarmed. I am well, and in better spirits than I have been in my life.\n\nYou will have read in the Sydney papers of the gold found on the Turon. Believe me, Mother, the papers do not tell the half of it. Every day more men arrive, clerks and sailors and shopkeepers, all with the same fever in their eyes. Yesterday a man not twenty yards from my claim washed out a nugget the size of a pigeon''s egg.\n\nI have not yet been so fortunate. Tom Jenkins and I have dug and washed from first light until dark these three weeks, and have only a little dust to show for it. But we are certain our luck will turn. This is a land made for men who are willing to work, and it is empty and waiting for us.\n\nThe prices here are a scandal. Flour costs four times what it does in Sydney, and the storekeepers grow fat while honest diggers go hungry. Still, I would not trade places with any clerk in George Street.\n\nPlease tell Father I have not forgotten my promise. When I have made enough, I shall come home and set up the shop as we planned.\n\nYour loving son,\nWilliam"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_hard', 1, 'A TV news story about teenagers shows only footage of graffiti and fights, and interviews only adults who complain about young people. What is the main problem with how teenagers are represented?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It shows a whole group in a narrow, negative way and leaves out positive stories and teenagers'' own voices."},{"id":"b","text":"It is too short to be a news story."},{"id":"c","text":"It interviews too many teenagers."},{"id":"d","text":"It is a fact, so it cannot be biased."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Representation is how a text shows a group of people. By choosing only negative footage and only adult critics, the story suggests all teenagers are trouble, which is unfair and one-sided.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_hard', 1, 'A company statement says, ''Mistakes were made.'' Why might the writer have chosen the passive voice here?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It hides who made the mistakes, so nobody has to take responsibility."},{"id":"b","text":"It makes the sentence longer and more detailed."},{"id":"c","text":"It names exactly who made each mistake."},{"id":"d","text":"It is the only correct way to write about mistakes."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The active version would be ''We made mistakes'' or ''The manager made mistakes''. The passive leaves out the doer, which is a subtle way to avoid blame and shape how readers see the company.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which sentence is written in the third person?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He packed his bag for camp."},{"id":"b","text":"I packed my bag for camp."},{"id":"c","text":"You pack your bag for camp."},{"id":"d","text":"We packed our bags for camp."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Third person uses ''he'', ''she'', ''it'' or ''they''. ''I'' and ''we'' are first person, and ''you'' is second person.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_easy', 1, 'An opinion is a statement that can be proven true or false.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'That describes a fact. An opinion is a belief or judgement, like ''Pizza is the best food'', which people can disagree with but cannot prove.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_easy', 1, 'A narrator who is a character in the story and uses ''I'' and ''me'' is called a ___ person narrator.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["first","1st"]'::jsonb, 'When the storyteller is inside the story saying ''I'', it is a first person narrator. We only see what that character sees and thinks.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'easy', 1, 'Which account uses more emotive language?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Account A, by Priya"},{"id":"b","text":"Account B, by the referee"},{"id":"c","text":"Both use the same amount"},{"id":"d","text":"Neither uses any emotive language"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Priya uses words like ''took my legs clean out'', ''crack'', ''furious'' and ''I don''t know what game he was watching''. The referee uses calm, precise, formal language.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Accounts of the Final","text":"Account A: Priya, Year 8 striker\n\nWe were drawing 1–1 with two minutes left when I broke through. Their defender came sliding in from behind and took my legs clean out. Everybody heard the crack of his boot on my shin. I hit the ground hard and lay there, waiting for the whistle. It never came. The referee just waved play on, and thirty seconds later they scored at the other end. Coach was furious. Half our parents were on their feet. I still have the bruise. Honestly, I don''t know what game he was watching, but it wasn''t ours.\n\nAccount B: Referee''s match report, Mr D. Lowe\n\nIn the 58th minute, with the score at 1–1, the home striker (No. 9) entered the penalty area at speed. The defender (No. 4) made a sliding challenge from the side and clearly played the ball first, after which the players'' legs made contact. The striker fell. From my position approximately ten metres away, with a clear line of sight, I judged the challenge to be fair and allowed play to continue. The opposing team scored from the resulting counter-attack. The home coach approached me after the match to dispute the decision; the discussion was respectful."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'easy', 1, 'Which statements from the release are facts that could be checked? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The resort will feature 200 luxury rooms."},{"id":"b","text":"Community information sessions will be held at the Port Amber Bowling Club."},{"id":"c","text":"Port Amber deserves to be on the map."},{"id":"d","text":"Seabreeze is a world-class holiday destination."},{"id":"e","text":"This is an exciting new chapter in the town''s history."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'The number of rooms and the place of the sessions can be checked. ''Deserves'', ''world-class'' and ''exciting'' are judgements, so they are opinions.', NULL, '{"title":"Media Release: Seabreeze Resort","text":"EXCITING NEW RESORT TO BRING JOBS AND GROWTH TO PORT AMBER\n\nBluewater Developments is proud to announce Seabreeze Resort, a world-class holiday destination on the northern shores of Port Amber.\n\nThe resort will feature 200 luxury rooms, three restaurants, a day spa and a championship golf course. Construction will begin in March next year and is expected to take two years.\n\nBluewater estimates the project will create 350 construction jobs and 120 permanent positions, giving local young people the chance to build careers without leaving home.\n\n''Port Amber deserves to be on the map,'' said Bluewater''s managing director, Grant Fowler. ''This resort will bring visitors, money and pride back to a town that has been overlooked for far too long.''\n\nThe resort will be built on 40 hectares of currently unused land beside Amber Creek. Bluewater has committed to planting native gardens throughout the site and will sponsor the local surf club for five years. Guests will enjoy uninterrupted ocean views, private beach access and a lagoon-style pool surrounded by palms.\n\nCommunity information sessions will be held at the Port Amber Bowling Club throughout May. Bluewater looks forward to welcoming everyone to this exciting new chapter in the town''s history."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'easy', 1, 'What is the main purpose of this media release?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"To promote the resort and win the community''s support"},{"id":"b","text":"To give a balanced report of the resort''s benefits and problems"},{"id":"c","text":"To warn residents about environmental damage"},{"id":"d","text":"To advertise jobs at the surf club"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It is written by the company building the resort, uses positive words like ''exciting'' and ''world-class'', and only mentions benefits. Its purpose is to make people feel good about the project.', NULL, '{"title":"Media Release: Seabreeze Resort","text":"EXCITING NEW RESORT TO BRING JOBS AND GROWTH TO PORT AMBER\n\nBluewater Developments is proud to announce Seabreeze Resort, a world-class holiday destination on the northern shores of Port Amber.\n\nThe resort will feature 200 luxury rooms, three restaurants, a day spa and a championship golf course. Construction will begin in March next year and is expected to take two years.\n\nBluewater estimates the project will create 350 construction jobs and 120 permanent positions, giving local young people the chance to build careers without leaving home.\n\n''Port Amber deserves to be on the map,'' said Bluewater''s managing director, Grant Fowler. ''This resort will bring visitors, money and pride back to a town that has been overlooked for far too long.''\n\nThe resort will be built on 40 hectares of currently unused land beside Amber Creek. Bluewater has committed to planting native gardens throughout the site and will sponsor the local surf club for five years. Guests will enjoy uninterrupted ocean views, private beach access and a lagoon-style pool surrounded by palms.\n\nCommunity information sessions will be held at the Port Amber Bowling Club throughout May. Bluewater looks forward to welcoming everyone to this exciting new chapter in the town''s history."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'medium', 1, 'Why do the two accounts of the tackle differ so much?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Each writer''s position and purpose shape what they notice: Priya felt the hit and wanted a penalty, while the referee must explain and justify his decision."},{"id":"b","text":"One of them was not at the match."},{"id":"c","text":"They are describing two different matches."},{"id":"d","text":"The referee could not see the tackle at all."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Priya writes from the ground, hurt and upset. Mr Lowe writes an official report that explains why he allowed play to continue. Same event, different perspectives and purposes.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Accounts of the Final","text":"Account A: Priya, Year 8 striker\n\nWe were drawing 1–1 with two minutes left when I broke through. Their defender came sliding in from behind and took my legs clean out. Everybody heard the crack of his boot on my shin. I hit the ground hard and lay there, waiting for the whistle. It never came. The referee just waved play on, and thirty seconds later they scored at the other end. Coach was furious. Half our parents were on their feet. I still have the bruise. Honestly, I don''t know what game he was watching, but it wasn''t ours.\n\nAccount B: Referee''s match report, Mr D. Lowe\n\nIn the 58th minute, with the score at 1–1, the home striker (No. 9) entered the penalty area at speed. The defender (No. 4) made a sliding challenge from the side and clearly played the ball first, after which the players'' legs made contact. The striker fell. From my position approximately ten metres away, with a clear line of sight, I judged the challenge to be fair and allowed play to continue. The opposing team scored from the resulting counter-attack. The home coach approached me after the match to dispute the decision; the discussion was respectful."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'medium', 1, 'Put these modal words in order from lowest to highest modality.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"might"},{"id":"b","text":"should"},{"id":"c","text":"must"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, '''Might'' only suggests a possibility, ''should'' gives advice or expectation, and ''must'' shows certainty or obligation. Writers choose modality to sound more or less forceful.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'medium', 1, 'What does the letter suggest about life in country NSW in 1931?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Times were very hard, with low wool prices, little work and debt, but neighbours supported each other."},{"id":"b","text":"Farmers were becoming rich from high wool prices."},{"id":"c","text":"Everyone in the country had moved to Sydney."},{"id":"d","text":"Life was easy because there was plenty of paid work."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The letter was written during the Great Depression. It mentions men without work since Christmas, wool at less than half its price and a visit from the bank, but also swapping milk for eggs: ''nobody goes hungry while a neighbour has food''.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter Home, 1931","text":"Wattle Flat, via Bathurst\n3rd June, 1931\n\nDear Jack,\n\nYour letter came on Tuesday, and your mother read it aloud three times at the table. We are glad you have found work at the docks, even if it is only two days a week. Two days is better than none, and there are men in this district who have not had a day''s paid work since Christmas.\n\nI will not pretend things are easy here. Wool fetched less than half of what it did two years ago, and the bank manager came out again last week. He was polite enough, but we both knew what he meant. We have sold the good horse and the piano. Your mother says she does not miss the piano, but I notice she still dusts the place where it stood.\n\nStill, we are luckier than many. The garden gives us potatoes and cabbages, the hens are laying, and Mrs Doyle next door swaps us milk for eggs. Nobody round here has money, but nobody goes hungry while a neighbour has food. That is the country way.\n\nDo not send money home, son. Keep it for yourself and put a little aside. Times will get better. They must.\n\nYour sister sends her love and asks if Sydney is as grand as they say.\n\nYour loving father,\nTom Walsh"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'hard', 1, 'The release calls the site ''currently unused land beside Amber Creek''. What might this wording leave out?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The land may be bushland or wetland used by wildlife and locals; calling it ''unused'' makes it sound worthless."},{"id":"b","text":"The exact number of hotel rooms."},{"id":"c","text":"The name of the company''s managing director."},{"id":"d","text":"The date of the information sessions."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Bias can come from what is left out. Land beside a creek is rarely truly ''unused'', but the release never mentions the environment, so readers don''t think about what might be lost.', NULL, '{"title":"Media Release: Seabreeze Resort","text":"EXCITING NEW RESORT TO BRING JOBS AND GROWTH TO PORT AMBER\n\nBluewater Developments is proud to announce Seabreeze Resort, a world-class holiday destination on the northern shores of Port Amber.\n\nThe resort will feature 200 luxury rooms, three restaurants, a day spa and a championship golf course. Construction will begin in March next year and is expected to take two years.\n\nBluewater estimates the project will create 350 construction jobs and 120 permanent positions, giving local young people the chance to build careers without leaving home.\n\n''Port Amber deserves to be on the map,'' said Bluewater''s managing director, Grant Fowler. ''This resort will bring visitors, money and pride back to a town that has been overlooked for far too long.''\n\nThe resort will be built on 40 hectares of currently unused land beside Amber Creek. Bluewater has committed to planting native gardens throughout the site and will sponsor the local surf club for five years. Guests will enjoy uninterrupted ocean views, private beach access and a lagoon-style pool surrounded by palms.\n\nCommunity information sessions will be held at the Port Amber Bowling Club throughout May. Bluewater looks forward to welcoming everyone to this exciting new chapter in the town''s history."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'hard', 1, 'Read: ''Ella smiled at the new girl, though inside she was annoyed. Across the room, Mr Chen wondered why Ella was being so kind, while the new girl secretly hoped Ella would be her friend.'' Which point of view is used?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"third-person omniscient"},{"id":"b","text":"third-person limited"},{"id":"c","text":"first person"},{"id":"d","text":"second person"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The narrator uses ''she'' and ''he'' (third person) and reveals the private thoughts of three different characters. A narrator who knows everyone''s thoughts is omniscient.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'hard', 1, 'The father writes, ''Times will get better. They must.'' What does adding ''They must'' reveal?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"He is less certain than he sounds, and ''must'' reads more like a hope or a plea than a prediction."},{"id":"b","text":"He has secret information that the Depression is ending."},{"id":"c","text":"He is ordering Jack to make things better."},{"id":"d","text":"He thinks things are already better."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Will'' sounds confident, but the extra ''They must'' suggests he needs it to be true. It is high modality used to hold on to hope, hinting at worry he is trying not to show his son.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter Home, 1931","text":"Wattle Flat, via Bathurst\n3rd June, 1931\n\nDear Jack,\n\nYour letter came on Tuesday, and your mother read it aloud three times at the table. We are glad you have found work at the docks, even if it is only two days a week. Two days is better than none, and there are men in this district who have not had a day''s paid work since Christmas.\n\nI will not pretend things are easy here. Wool fetched less than half of what it did two years ago, and the bank manager came out again last week. He was polite enough, but we both knew what he meant. We have sold the good horse and the piano. Your mother says she does not miss the piano, but I notice she still dusts the place where it stood.\n\nStill, we are luckier than many. The garden gives us potatoes and cabbages, the hens are laying, and Mrs Doyle next door swaps us milk for eggs. Nobody round here has money, but nobody goes hungry while a neighbour has food. That is the country way.\n\nDo not send money home, son. Keep it for yourself and put a little aside. Times will get better. They must.\n\nYour sister sends her love and asks if Sydney is as grand as they say.\n\nYour loving father,\nTom Walsh"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which statements are reasonable conclusions about the two accounts? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Neither account should be treated as the whole truth on its own."},{"id":"b","text":"Account B''s precise details make it sound objective, but the referee is also defending his own decision."},{"id":"c","text":"Priya''s claim that ''everybody heard the crack'' cannot be checked and reflects her strong feelings."},{"id":"d","text":"Account B proves the tackle was definitely fair."},{"id":"e","text":"Priya is lying about being tackled."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Both writers have a stake in the event. Formal detail can make a text seem neutral, but the referee is still justifying himself, and Priya''s version is shaped by pain and frustration. Neither proves the other wrong, and nothing suggests she is lying.', NULL, '{"title":"Two Accounts of the Final","text":"Account A: Priya, Year 8 striker\n\nWe were drawing 1–1 with two minutes left when I broke through. Their defender came sliding in from behind and took my legs clean out. Everybody heard the crack of his boot on my shin. I hit the ground hard and lay there, waiting for the whistle. It never came. The referee just waved play on, and thirty seconds later they scored at the other end. Coach was furious. Half our parents were on their feet. I still have the bruise. Honestly, I don''t know what game he was watching, but it wasn''t ours.\n\nAccount B: Referee''s match report, Mr D. Lowe\n\nIn the 58th minute, with the score at 1–1, the home striker (No. 9) entered the penalty area at speed. The defender (No. 4) made a sliding challenge from the side and clearly played the ball first, after which the players'' legs made contact. The striker fell. From my position approximately ten metres away, with a clear line of sight, I judged the challenge to be fair and allowed play to continue. The opposing team scored from the resulting counter-attack. The home coach approached me after the match to dispute the decision; the discussion was respectful."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_hard', 1, '''The bank manager came out again last week... we both knew what he meant.'' What is the father hinting at, and why doesn''t he say it directly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The family might lose the farm because of debt; he hints rather than says it so he doesn''t worry Jack or make him send money."},{"id":"b","text":"The bank manager is a family friend who visits often; he leaves out details because they are boring."},{"id":"c","text":"The bank is giving the family a loan; he keeps it secret as a surprise."},{"id":"d","text":"The bank manager wants to buy the piano; he doesn''t mention it because it was already sold."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'In the Depression, banks often took farms from families who couldn''t repay loans. The father''s purpose is to reassure his son (''Do not send money home''), so he hints at the danger without spelling it out.', NULL, '{"title":"A Letter Home, 1931","text":"Wattle Flat, via Bathurst\n3rd June, 1931\n\nDear Jack,\n\nYour letter came on Tuesday, and your mother read it aloud three times at the table. We are glad you have found work at the docks, even if it is only two days a week. Two days is better than none, and there are men in this district who have not had a day''s paid work since Christmas.\n\nI will not pretend things are easy here. Wool fetched less than half of what it did two years ago, and the bank manager came out again last week. He was polite enough, but we both knew what he meant. We have sold the good horse and the piano. Your mother says she does not miss the piano, but I notice she still dusts the place where it stood.\n\nStill, we are luckier than many. The garden gives us potatoes and cabbages, the hens are laying, and Mrs Doyle next door swaps us milk for eggs. Nobody round here has money, but nobody goes hungry while a neighbour has food. That is the country way.\n\nDo not send money home, son. Keep it for yourself and put a little aside. Times will get better. They must.\n\nYour sister sends her love and asks if Sydney is as grand as they say.\n\nYour loving father,\nTom Walsh"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008110330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081103', 'very_hard', 1, 'Two headlines report the same event. A: ''Police officer injures teenager during arrest''. B: ''Teenager injured during arrest''. What does headline B do?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It uses the passive voice and leaves out who caused the injury, softening the police role."},{"id":"b","text":"It gives more detail about who was responsible."},{"id":"c","text":"It blames the teenager for the injury."},{"id":"d","text":"It reports a completely different event."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Headline A names the doer (the officer) in the active voice. Headline B is passive and drops the doer, so readers don''t learn who caused the injury. A small grammar choice changes how the event is represented.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_easy', 1, '''The ancient pottery was so fragile that visitors were not allowed to touch it.'' What does ''fragile'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"easily broken"},{"id":"b","text":"very heavy"},{"id":"c","text":"brightly coloured"},{"id":"d","text":"extremely valuable"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The clue is that visitors could not touch it: something fragile breaks easily, like glass or a thin eggshell.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_easy', 1, '''Benevolent'' and ''malevolent'' are antonyms.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'The Latin root ''bene'' means good and ''male'' means bad. Benevolent means kind and wishing good; malevolent means wishing harm. They are opposites.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the prefix in the word ''impossible'' that means ''not''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["im","im-"]'::jsonb, 'Impossible = im (not) + possible. The prefix im- is used before words starting with p, b or m: impatient, imbalance, immature.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'easy', 1, 'Which word is an antonym of ''scarce''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"abundant"},{"id":"b","text":"rare"},{"id":"c","text":"limited"},{"id":"d","text":"sparse"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Scarce means there is not enough of something. Abundant means there is plenty, so it is the opposite. Rare, limited and sparse are synonyms of scarce.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'easy', 1, 'The Latin root ''port'' means carry. Which words contain this root? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"transport"},{"id":"b","text":"portable"},{"id":"c","text":"export"},{"id":"d","text":"porcupine"},{"id":"e","text":"porridge"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Transport (carry across), portable (able to be carried) and export (carry out of a country) all come from ''port''. Porcupine and porridge just happen to start with the same letters.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'easy', 1, 'The suffix -ology means ''the study of'' and the Greek root ''bio'' means life, so the study of living things is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"biology"'::jsonb, 'bio (life) + ology (the study of) = biology. The same pattern gives geology (earth) and zoology (animals).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'medium', 1, 'In the first paragraph, what does ''meticulous'' most likely mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"very careful about details"},{"id":"b","text":"often late"},{"id":"c","text":"easily frightened"},{"id":"d","text":"relaxed and carefree"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The next sentence is the clue: Mia checks the timetable twice, sets two alarms and arrives early. Meticulous means very careful about every detail.', NULL, '{"title":"The Last Ferry","text":"Mia had always been meticulous about time. She checked the ferry timetable twice, set two alarms and still arrived at Circular Quay twenty minutes early. Tonight, though, a cancelled train had left her stranded at Central, and now she sprinted across the wharf as the last ferry to Manly began to pull away.\n\nThe deckhand, a weathered man with a grey beard, saw her coming. For a moment his expression was inscrutable; Mia could not tell whether he would wait or simply shrug. Then he raised a hand, and the engines dropped to a low murmur. It was a brief reprieve, but it was enough.\n\n''You''re lucky,'' he said gruffly as she leapt aboard, her chest heaving. ''Skipper''s usually not so lenient.''\n\nMia collapsed onto a bench by the window. Outside, the harbour was turning an ominous shade of slate. Thick clouds had smothered the last of the sunset, and a cold wind whipped spray across the deck. Somewhere behind the Opera House, thunder rumbled.\n\nBy the time the ferry crossed the open water near the Heads, the swell had grown relentless. The boat lurched and rolled, and several passengers clutched their seats. Mia, however, felt oddly calm. She had nearly missed this journey, and after the chaos of the afternoon, even a storm felt like a kind of welcome.\n\nWhen the ferry finally nudged against Manly Wharf, she thanked the deckhand. He only grunted, but she was almost certain she saw him smile."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'medium', 1, 'Find the word in paragraph 2 that means ''a short break or rescue from something bad''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"reprieve"'::jsonb, 'The deckhand holds the ferry for a moment, saving Mia from being left behind. ''It was a brief reprieve'' means a short rescue from a bad situation.', NULL, '{"title":"The Last Ferry","text":"Mia had always been meticulous about time. She checked the ferry timetable twice, set two alarms and still arrived at Circular Quay twenty minutes early. Tonight, though, a cancelled train had left her stranded at Central, and now she sprinted across the wharf as the last ferry to Manly began to pull away.\n\nThe deckhand, a weathered man with a grey beard, saw her coming. For a moment his expression was inscrutable; Mia could not tell whether he would wait or simply shrug. Then he raised a hand, and the engines dropped to a low murmur. It was a brief reprieve, but it was enough.\n\n''You''re lucky,'' he said gruffly as she leapt aboard, her chest heaving. ''Skipper''s usually not so lenient.''\n\nMia collapsed onto a bench by the window. Outside, the harbour was turning an ominous shade of slate. Thick clouds had smothered the last of the sunset, and a cold wind whipped spray across the deck. Somewhere behind the Opera House, thunder rumbled.\n\nBy the time the ferry crossed the open water near the Heads, the swell had grown relentless. The boat lurched and rolled, and several passengers clutched their seats. Mia, however, felt oddly calm. She had nearly missed this journey, and after the chaos of the afternoon, even a storm felt like a kind of welcome.\n\nWhen the ferry finally nudged against Manly Wharf, she thanked the deckhand. He only grunted, but she was almost certain she saw him smile."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'medium', 1, 'Put these requests in order from most informal to most formal register.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Hey, gimme a sec."},{"id":"b","text":"Hang on a minute."},{"id":"c","text":"Please wait a moment."},{"id":"d","text":"We kindly request your patience."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Slang like ''gimme'' is the most casual. ''Hang on'' is everyday speech, ''Please wait'' is polite, and ''We kindly request your patience'' is the formal language of official notices.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'hard', 1, 'The writer says the harbour was turning ''an ominous shade of slate''. Why choose the word ''ominous''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It suggests that something bad, the storm, is about to happen."},{"id":"b","text":"It shows the water is calm and peaceful."},{"id":"c","text":"It means the harbour is very large."},{"id":"d","text":"It shows Mia is happy to be on the ferry."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Ominous means giving a warning that something bad is coming. The next sentences describe thick clouds, cold wind and thunder, so the word builds tension before the storm.', NULL, '{"title":"The Last Ferry","text":"Mia had always been meticulous about time. She checked the ferry timetable twice, set two alarms and still arrived at Circular Quay twenty minutes early. Tonight, though, a cancelled train had left her stranded at Central, and now she sprinted across the wharf as the last ferry to Manly began to pull away.\n\nThe deckhand, a weathered man with a grey beard, saw her coming. For a moment his expression was inscrutable; Mia could not tell whether he would wait or simply shrug. Then he raised a hand, and the engines dropped to a low murmur. It was a brief reprieve, but it was enough.\n\n''You''re lucky,'' he said gruffly as she leapt aboard, her chest heaving. ''Skipper''s usually not so lenient.''\n\nMia collapsed onto a bench by the window. Outside, the harbour was turning an ominous shade of slate. Thick clouds had smothered the last of the sunset, and a cold wind whipped spray across the deck. Somewhere behind the Opera House, thunder rumbled.\n\nBy the time the ferry crossed the open water near the Heads, the swell had grown relentless. The boat lurched and rolled, and several passengers clutched their seats. Mia, however, felt oddly calm. She had nearly missed this journey, and after the chaos of the afternoon, even a storm felt like a kind of welcome.\n\nWhen the ferry finally nudged against Manly Wharf, she thanked the deckhand. He only grunted, but she was almost certain she saw him smile."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'hard', 1, 'The Greek prefix ''ana-'' can mean against and ''chron'' means time. A mobile phone in a story set in 1850 is an ''anachronism''. What is an anachronism?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"something placed in the wrong time period"},{"id":"b","text":"a story told in time order"},{"id":"c","text":"a very old object"},{"id":"d","text":"a clock that runs backwards"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'ana (against) + chron (time): something that goes against its time. Mobile phones did not exist in 1850, so the phone is out of place in time.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'hard', 1, 'Each word describes someone careful with money. Which have a negative connotation? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"stingy"},{"id":"b","text":"miserly"},{"id":"c","text":"thrifty"},{"id":"d","text":"frugal"},{"id":"e","text":"tight-fisted"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","e"]'::jsonb, 'Stingy, miserly and tight-fisted suggest someone mean who will not share. Thrifty and frugal are positive: they suggest someone sensible who avoids waste.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_hard', 1, 'The prefix ''intra-'' means within and ''inter-'' means between. A debating competition held between different schools is an ___ competition.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["interschool","inter-school"]'::jsonb, 'The competition is between schools, so it takes inter-: interschool. An intraschool competition would be held within one school, such as between houses.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_hard', 1, 'A student is writing to the local council. Which sentence is the best formal version of ''Heaps of kids reckon the skate park is pretty dodgy''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Many young people believe the skate park is unsafe."},{"id":"b","text":"Lots of kids think the skate park is kinda dangerous."},{"id":"c","text":"Heaps of young people reckon the skate park is unsafe."},{"id":"d","text":"The skate park is dodgy, according to many kids."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Formal register replaces slang: ''heaps'' becomes ''many'', ''kids'' becomes ''young people'', ''reckon'' becomes ''believe'' and ''dodgy'' becomes ''unsafe''. Every other option keeps at least one informal word.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_hard', 1, 'The Greek root ''pseudo'' means false and ''nym'' means name. Write the word for a false name an author writes under.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"pseudonym"'::jsonb, 'pseudo (false) + nym (name) = pseudonym. The p is silent. ''Nym'' also appears in synonym (same name) and antonym (opposite name).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is closest in meaning to ''commence''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"begin"},{"id":"b","text":"finish"},{"id":"c","text":"comment"},{"id":"d","text":"combine"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Commence is a formal word for begin: ''The ceremony will commence at 10 am.'' Finish is its antonym.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_easy', 1, 'The prefix ''sub-'' means under, so a submarine travels under the sea.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'sub (under) + marine (of the sea) = submarine. The same prefix is in subway and submerge.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_easy', 1, 'An antonym of ''ancient'' is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["modern","new","recent","contemporary"]'::jsonb, 'Ancient means very old, so its opposite is modern or new.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'easy', 1, 'The Greek root ''graph'' means write or draw. Which words contain this root? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"autograph"},{"id":"b","text":"paragraph"},{"id":"c","text":"photograph"},{"id":"d","text":"giraffe"},{"id":"e","text":"grapefruit"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'An autograph is a name written by yourself, a paragraph is a block of writing, and a photograph is ''drawn with light''. Giraffe and grapefruit only look similar.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence is written in an informal register?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"No way, the excursion''s off!"},{"id":"b","text":"The excursion has been postponed until further notice."},{"id":"c","text":"Students must bring a hat and water bottle."},{"id":"d","text":"Parents are advised to collect children at 3 pm."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''No way'' and the contraction ''excursion''s'' sound like casual speech between friends. The other sentences use the formal language of a school notice.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'easy', 1, 'Add a suffix to the verb ''employ'' to make the word for the person who is given a job.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"employee"'::jsonb, 'The suffix -ee names the person something is done to: an employee is employed. The suffix -er names the person who does it: the employer gives the job.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'medium', 1, '''Why has their number dwindled so dramatically?'' What does ''dwindled'' mean?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"become smaller bit by bit"},{"id":"b","text":"grown suddenly"},{"id":"c","text":"stayed the same"},{"id":"d","text":"moved to a new place"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The passage says there used to be flocks of hundreds and now only a few hundred birds survive at all. Dwindled means gradually became smaller.', NULL, '{"title":"The Honeyeater''s Last Song?","text":"Fifty years ago, the regent honeyeater was a common sight in the box-ironbark forests of New South Wales and Victoria. Flocks of hundreds of these striking black-and-gold birds would descend on flowering eucalypts, filling the bush with their chatter. Today, only a few hundred are thought to survive in the wild.\n\nWhy has their number dwindled so dramatically? The main cause is the loss of habitat. Over the past century, much of the woodland the birds depend on has been cleared for farms, roads and housing. Without enough flowering trees, the honeyeaters cannot find the nectar they need, and the few birds that remain are spread so thinly that young males struggle to hear older birds sing. Scientists have discovered that some young honeyeaters are now copying the songs of other species, because they have never heard their own.\n\nThe situation is precarious, but it is not hopeless. Zoo keepers breed honeyeaters and release them into protected forests. Volunteers plant thousands of seedlings each year to replenish lost woodland. Landholders who protect old trees on their properties play a vital role too.\n\nEvery one of us can help. Supporting local tree-planting days, donating to conservation groups and telling others about the honeyeater''s plight all make a difference. If we act now, these remarkable birds may flourish once more, and their song will again ring through the forests."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'medium', 1, '''Landholders who protect old trees on their properties play a vital role too.'' Which words could replace ''vital'' without changing the meaning? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"essential"},{"id":"b","text":"crucial"},{"id":"c","text":"optional"},{"id":"d","text":"lively"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, 'Here vital means extremely important, so essential and crucial fit. Optional is the opposite. ''Lively'' is another meaning of vital (a vital person), but it does not fit this sentence.', NULL, '{"title":"The Honeyeater''s Last Song?","text":"Fifty years ago, the regent honeyeater was a common sight in the box-ironbark forests of New South Wales and Victoria. Flocks of hundreds of these striking black-and-gold birds would descend on flowering eucalypts, filling the bush with their chatter. Today, only a few hundred are thought to survive in the wild.\n\nWhy has their number dwindled so dramatically? The main cause is the loss of habitat. Over the past century, much of the woodland the birds depend on has been cleared for farms, roads and housing. Without enough flowering trees, the honeyeaters cannot find the nectar they need, and the few birds that remain are spread so thinly that young males struggle to hear older birds sing. Scientists have discovered that some young honeyeaters are now copying the songs of other species, because they have never heard their own.\n\nThe situation is precarious, but it is not hopeless. Zoo keepers breed honeyeaters and release them into protected forests. Volunteers plant thousands of seedlings each year to replenish lost woodland. Landholders who protect old trees on their properties play a vital role too.\n\nEvery one of us can help. Supporting local tree-planting days, donating to conservation groups and telling others about the honeyeater''s plight all make a difference. If we act now, these remarkable birds may flourish once more, and their song will again ring through the forests."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from the mildest to the strongest feeling of happiness.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"content"},{"id":"b","text":"pleased"},{"id":"c","text":"delighted"},{"id":"d","text":"ecstatic"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Content is quietly satisfied, pleased is a little happier, delighted is very happy, and ecstatic is overwhelmingly, jump-up-and-down happy.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'hard', 1, '''The situation is precarious, but it is not hopeless.'' What does ''precarious'' mean here?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"unstable and at risk of getting worse"},{"id":"b","text":"completely safe"},{"id":"c","text":"over and finished"},{"id":"d","text":"easy to fix"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The word ''but'' sets up a contrast: the situation is dangerous and could collapse, yet there is still hope. Precarious means insecure and risky.', NULL, '{"title":"The Honeyeater''s Last Song?","text":"Fifty years ago, the regent honeyeater was a common sight in the box-ironbark forests of New South Wales and Victoria. Flocks of hundreds of these striking black-and-gold birds would descend on flowering eucalypts, filling the bush with their chatter. Today, only a few hundred are thought to survive in the wild.\n\nWhy has their number dwindled so dramatically? The main cause is the loss of habitat. Over the past century, much of the woodland the birds depend on has been cleared for farms, roads and housing. Without enough flowering trees, the honeyeaters cannot find the nectar they need, and the few birds that remain are spread so thinly that young males struggle to hear older birds sing. Scientists have discovered that some young honeyeaters are now copying the songs of other species, because they have never heard their own.\n\nThe situation is precarious, but it is not hopeless. Zoo keepers breed honeyeaters and release them into protected forests. Volunteers plant thousands of seedlings each year to replenish lost woodland. Landholders who protect old trees on their properties play a vital role too.\n\nEvery one of us can help. Supporting local tree-planting days, donating to conservation groups and telling others about the honeyeater''s plight all make a difference. If we act now, these remarkable birds may flourish once more, and their song will again ring through the forests."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'hard', 1, 'The Latin root ''dict'' means say or speak. Which word means ''to say what will happen before it happens''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"predict"},{"id":"b","text":"contradict"},{"id":"c","text":"dictate"},{"id":"d","text":"verdict"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'pre (before) + dict (say) = predict. Contradict means speak against, dictate means say aloud for someone to write, and a verdict is a jury''s decision.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'hard', 1, 'In formal English, ''disinterested'' and ''uninterested'' mean exactly the same thing.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'A disinterested judge is fair because they gain nothing from the result. An uninterested person is simply bored. The prefixes dis- and un- give different meanings.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_hard', 1, 'In the last paragraph the writer mentions ''the honeyeater''s plight'' instead of ''the honeyeater''s situation''. What is the effect of ''plight''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It suggests serious, unfair trouble, which stirs the reader''s sympathy and urge to help."},{"id":"b","text":"It makes the text sound more scientific and neutral."},{"id":"c","text":"It tells the reader that the birds are migrating."},{"id":"d","text":"It makes the problem sound small and easy to solve."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Situation'' is neutral, but ''plight'' means a dangerous or sad situation. Its emotional connotation helps persuade readers to act in the very next sentences.', NULL, '{"title":"The Honeyeater''s Last Song?","text":"Fifty years ago, the regent honeyeater was a common sight in the box-ironbark forests of New South Wales and Victoria. Flocks of hundreds of these striking black-and-gold birds would descend on flowering eucalypts, filling the bush with their chatter. Today, only a few hundred are thought to survive in the wild.\n\nWhy has their number dwindled so dramatically? The main cause is the loss of habitat. Over the past century, much of the woodland the birds depend on has been cleared for farms, roads and housing. Without enough flowering trees, the honeyeaters cannot find the nectar they need, and the few birds that remain are spread so thinly that young males struggle to hear older birds sing. Scientists have discovered that some young honeyeaters are now copying the songs of other species, because they have never heard their own.\n\nThe situation is precarious, but it is not hopeless. Zoo keepers breed honeyeaters and release them into protected forests. Volunteers plant thousands of seedlings each year to replenish lost woodland. Landholders who protect old trees on their properties play a vital role too.\n\nEvery one of us can help. Supporting local tree-planting days, donating to conservation groups and telling others about the honeyeater''s plight all make a difference. If we act now, these remarkable birds may flourish once more, and their song will again ring through the forests."}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_hard', 1, 'A student is writing to their Member of Parliament. Which sentence uses the most suitable formal register?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I am concerned that the closure of the bus route will make it difficult for students to reach school."},{"id":"b","text":"I''m pretty worried the bus thing is gonna wreck everyone''s mornings."},{"id":"c","text":"Seriously, cutting the bus route is the worst idea ever!!"},{"id":"d","text":"The bus route closure is a total disaster, to be honest."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Formal writing avoids slang (gonna, pretty), exaggeration, double exclamation marks and chatty phrases like ''to be honest''. It states the concern clearly and politely.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081201', 'very_hard', 1, 'The Latin prefix ''circum-'' means around and ''navigate'' means sail. To sail all the way around the world is to ___ it.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"circumnavigate"'::jsonb, 'circum (around) + navigate (sail) = circumnavigate. The same prefix is in circumference, the distance around a circle.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120201', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_easy', 1, 'In ''The exhausted players trudged slowly off the oval'', which word is an adverb?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"slowly"},{"id":"b","text":"exhausted"},{"id":"c","text":"trudged"},{"id":"d","text":"oval"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'An adverb tells how, when or where an action happens. ''Slowly'' tells how the players trudged. ''Exhausted'' is an adjective, ''trudged'' is a verb and ''oval'' is a noun.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120202', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_easy', 1, '''Although it rained, the match continued.'' is a complex sentence.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'It has a main clause (''the match continued'') and a dependent clause starting with the subordinating conjunction ''although''. That makes it complex.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120203', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the contraction of ''they are'' to complete the sentence: ''___ going to the athletics carnival on Friday.''', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["They''re","they''re"]'::jsonb, 'They + are = they''re, with an apostrophe where the a is left out. Don''t confuse it with ''their'' (belonging to them) or ''there'' (a place).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120204', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence is written in the passive voice?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The window was broken by the cricket ball."},{"id":"b","text":"The cricket ball broke the window."},{"id":"c","text":"Sam hit the ball over the fence."},{"id":"d","text":"The ball flew towards the classroom."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'In the passive voice, the thing receiving the action comes first and the verb uses a form of ''to be'' (was broken). The doer, the cricket ball, comes after ''by''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120205', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'easy', 1, 'Which words show high modality (strong certainty or obligation)? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"must"},{"id":"b","text":"certainly"},{"id":"c","text":"might"},{"id":"d","text":"definitely"},{"id":"e","text":"perhaps"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Must, certainly and definitely express no doubt. Might and perhaps are low modality: they show something is only possible.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120206', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence uses a semicolon correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The bus was late; we missed the start of the film."},{"id":"b","text":"The bus was late; because of the traffic."},{"id":"c","text":"We bought; apples, pears and bananas."},{"id":"d","text":"Although the bus was late; we arrived on time."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A semicolon joins two closely linked main clauses that could each stand alone. ''The bus was late'' and ''we missed the start of the film'' are both complete sentences.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120207', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'medium', 1, 'Write the subordinating conjunction in this sentence: ''We stayed inside because the storm was severe.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"because"'::jsonb, '''Because'' begins the dependent clause ''because the storm was severe'' and links it to the main clause ''We stayed inside''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120208', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'medium', 1, 'Put these words in order from lowest to highest modality.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"perhaps"},{"id":"b","text":"probably"},{"id":"c","text":"almost certainly"},{"id":"d","text":"definitely"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Perhaps shows only a chance, probably shows it is likely, almost certainly is very likely, and definitely shows complete certainty.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120209', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'medium', 1, 'The change rooms belong to all the girls in the team. Which word correctly fills the gap? ''The ___ change rooms were flooded.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"girls''"},{"id":"b","text":"girl''s"},{"id":"c","text":"girls"},{"id":"d","text":"girls''s"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'For a plural noun ending in s, add only an apostrophe after the s to show ownership: the girls'' change rooms. ''Girl''s'' would mean one girl.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120210', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'hard', 1, '''The council will repair the footpath.'' Which is the correct passive voice version?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The footpath will be repaired by the council."},{"id":"b","text":"The footpath will repair the council."},{"id":"c","text":"The council will be repaired by the footpath."},{"id":"d","text":"The footpath was repaired by the council."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Move the receiver (the footpath) to the front, use ''will be'' plus the past participle ''repaired'' to keep the future tense, and put the doer after ''by''. The last option wrongly changes the tense to past.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120211', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'hard', 1, 'Which of these are complex sentences? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"When the siren sounded, everyone left the pool."},{"id":"b","text":"I wanted to go, but I was sick."},{"id":"c","text":"The girl who won the race is my cousin."},{"id":"d","text":"We ate lunch and then played handball."},{"id":"e","text":"Unless you hurry, you''ll miss the bus."}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Complex sentences have a dependent clause: ''When the siren sounded'', ''who won the race'' and ''Unless you hurry''. The other two join main clauses or verbs with ''but'' and ''and''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120212', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'hard', 1, 'Name the punctuation mark that belongs in the gap: ''You will need three things_ a torch, a map and plenty of water.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '["colon",":"]'::jsonb, 'A colon introduces a list after a complete clause. ''You will need three things'' is complete, and the list explains what the things are.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120213', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_hard', 1, 'A politician says, ''Mistakes were made, and the money was lost.'' Why might they choose the passive voice?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"It avoids saying who made the mistakes and lost the money."},{"id":"b","text":"It makes the sentence shorter and more exciting."},{"id":"c","text":"It is the only correct way to talk about the past."},{"id":"d","text":"It shows the politician is taking full responsibility."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The passive voice lets a speaker leave out the doer. ''Mistakes were made'' hides who made them, which can be used to dodge blame. ''I made mistakes'' would take responsibility.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120214', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"“We need to leave now,” said Ms Tran, “or we''ll miss the train.”"},{"id":"b","text":"“We need to leave now” said Ms Tran, “or we''ll miss the train”."},{"id":"c","text":"“We need to leave now,” said Ms Tran. “or we''ll miss the train.”"},{"id":"d","text":"“We need to leave now, said Ms Tran, or we''ll miss the train.”"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The comma goes inside the first closing quotation mark, another comma follows ''Ms Tran'' because the sentence continues, the second part starts with a lower-case ''or'', and the full stop sits inside the final quotation mark.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120215', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_hard', 1, 'How many clauses are in this sentence? ''Although the forecast predicted rain, we packed a picnic because we were optimistic.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '["3","three"]'::jsonb, 'Each clause has its own verb: ''Although the forecast predicted rain'' (predicted), ''we packed a picnic'' (packed) and ''because we were optimistic'' (were). That is 3 clauses: one main and two dependent.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120216', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which words are nouns in this sentence? ''Courage helped the swimmer cross the river.'' Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"Courage"},{"id":"b","text":"swimmer"},{"id":"c","text":"river"},{"id":"d","text":"helped"},{"id":"e","text":"cross"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Nouns name people, places, things or ideas. ''Swimmer'' and ''river'' are common nouns, and ''courage'' is an abstract noun (an idea). ''Helped'' and ''cross'' are verbs.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120217', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which of these is a compound sentence?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I like netball, and my brother likes rugby league."},{"id":"b","text":"Because it was hot, we went swimming."},{"id":"c","text":"Running for the bus in the rain."},{"id":"d","text":"The dog that barked all night."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A compound sentence joins two main clauses with a coordinating conjunction such as and, but or so. Both ''I like netball'' and ''my brother likes rugby league'' make sense alone.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120218', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_easy', 1, 'In ''The children''s books were on the shelf'', the apostrophe is in the right place.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''Children'' is already plural and does not end in s, so we add ''s to show ownership: the children''s books.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120219', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'easy', 1, 'Which word has the lowest modality?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"may"},{"id":"b","text":"will"},{"id":"c","text":"must"},{"id":"d","text":"definitely"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''May'' only suggests something is possible. ''Will'', ''must'' and ''definitely'' all show strong certainty or obligation.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120220', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'easy', 1, 'Complete this passive sentence in the past tense: ''The trophy ___ presented by the principal.''', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"was"'::jsonb, 'The passive voice uses a form of ''to be'' plus the past participle. One trophy in the past needs ''was'': the trophy was presented.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120221', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'easy', 1, 'In ''The koala in the gum tree was asleep'', what is ''in the gum tree''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a prepositional phrase that describes the koala"},{"id":"b","text":"a main clause"},{"id":"c","text":"a dependent clause"},{"id":"d","text":"a verb group"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It starts with the preposition ''in'' and has no verb of its own, so it is a phrase, not a clause. It tells us which koala.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120222', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'medium', 1, 'Put these sentences in order: simple, then compound, then complex.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"The dog barked."},{"id":"b","text":"The dog barked, and the cat ran away."},{"id":"c","text":"When the dog barked, the cat ran away."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'A simple sentence has one clause. A compound sentence joins two main clauses with ''and''. A complex sentence has a dependent clause starting with ''When''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120223', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'medium', 1, 'In the passive sentence ''The essay was written by Priya'', who is doing the action? Write the name.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"Priya"'::jsonb, 'In a passive sentence the doer comes after ''by''. Priya did the writing. The active version is ''Priya wrote the essay.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120224', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence uses commas correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Mr Lee, our science teacher, ran the marathon."},{"id":"b","text":"Mr Lee our science teacher, ran the marathon."},{"id":"c","text":"Mr Lee, our science teacher ran the marathon."},{"id":"d","text":"Mr, Lee our science teacher ran the marathon."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Our science teacher'' is extra information about Mr Lee, so it needs a comma on both sides, like a pair of brackets.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120225', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses semicolons correctly in a list?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"We visited Dubbo, NSW; Bendigo, Victoria; and Mildura, Victoria."},{"id":"b","text":"We visited Dubbo; NSW, Bendigo; Victoria, and Mildura; Victoria."},{"id":"c","text":"We visited; Dubbo, NSW, Bendigo, Victoria and Mildura, Victoria."},{"id":"d","text":"We visited Dubbo, NSW, Bendigo, Victoria, and Mildura, Victoria;"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'When the items in a list already contain commas, semicolons separate the items so the reader can tell where each one ends: Dubbo, NSW; Bendigo, Victoria; Mildura, Victoria.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120226', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentences are in the passive voice? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The results were announced at assembly."},{"id":"b","text":"The cake was eaten before lunch."},{"id":"c","text":"The team celebrated its win."},{"id":"d","text":"The poem has been read aloud."},{"id":"e","text":"The kookaburra was laughing."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'In each passive sentence the subject receives the action: results were announced, cake was eaten, poem has been read. ''The kookaburra was laughing'' is active: the kookaburra is doing the laughing.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120227', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'hard', 1, 'Write the relative pronoun in this sentence: ''The student whose bag was stolen reported it to the office.''', 'short_answer', NULL, '"whose"'::jsonb, '''Whose'' begins the relative clause ''whose bag was stolen'', which adds information about the student. Other relative pronouns are who, whom, which and that.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120228', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_hard', 1, 'A school newsletter says: ''Students must wear hats at lunch. Sun damage could lead to skin cancer.'' Why does the writer use ''must'' rather than ''should''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"High modality makes it a firm rule that students have no choice about."},{"id":"b","text":"''Must'' is softer and more polite than ''should''."},{"id":"c","text":"''Should'' is grammatically wrong in a newsletter."},{"id":"d","text":"''Must'' shows the writer is unsure about the rule."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Must'' is high modality and shows obligation. ''Should'' is medium modality and sounds like advice. The writer wants students to know the rule is not optional.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120229', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_hard', 1, '''Running late for the bus, my lunch fell out of my bag.'' is correct because it contains a main clause.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'This is a dangling modifier: the opening phrase describes the person running late, but the subject is ''my lunch'', so it says the lunch was running late. Fix: ''Running late for the bus, I dropped my lunch.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120230', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081202', 'very_hard', 1, 'In the noun group ''the quiet student in the back row'', the head noun (the main noun) is ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"student"'::jsonb, 'Everything else in the group adds detail about the student: ''the'' and ''quiet'' come before it, and the phrase ''in the back row'' comes after it. ''Row'' belongs to the phrase, not the head.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120301', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"necessary"},{"id":"b","text":"neccessary"},{"id":"c","text":"necesary"},{"id":"d","text":"neccesary"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Necessary has one c and two s''s. A memory trick: a shirt has one collar and two sleeves.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120302', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_easy', 1, 'The plural of ''potato'' is ''potatoes''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Some words ending in o add -es in the plural: potatoes, tomatoes, heroes, echoes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120303', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the plural of ''wolf''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"wolves"'::jsonb, 'Many words ending in f change the f to v and add -es: wolf becomes wolves, like half becomes halves.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120304', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'easy', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''The team was not ___ to train on the wet oval.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"allowed"},{"id":"b","text":"aloud"},{"id":"c","text":"alowed"},{"id":"d","text":"alloud"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Allowed'' means permitted. ''Aloud'' is a homophone meaning out loud, as in reading aloud.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120305', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'easy', 1, 'Adding -ing to ''begin'' gives ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"beginning"'::jsonb, 'Begin ends in one vowel and one consonant, and the stress is on the last syllable (be-GIN), so double the n before adding -ing.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120306', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'easy', 1, 'Which words follow the rule ''i before e, except after c''? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"receive"},{"id":"b","text":"ceiling"},{"id":"c","text":"believe"},{"id":"d","text":"weird"},{"id":"e","text":"science"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Receive and ceiling have ei after c, and believe has ie with no c. Weird (ei, no c) and science (ie after c) are exceptions to the rule.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120307', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'medium', 1, 'What is the plural of ''phenomenon''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"phenomena"},{"id":"b","text":"phenomenas"},{"id":"c","text":"phenomenon''s"},{"id":"d","text":"phenomenies"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Phenomenon comes from Greek, and Greek words ending in -on often change to -a in the plural: phenomenon becomes phenomena, like criterion becomes criteria.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120308', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'medium', 1, 'Put these word parts in order to build the word ''disrespectfully''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"dis"},{"id":"b","text":"respect"},{"id":"c","text":"ful"},{"id":"d","text":"ly"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'Start with the prefix dis-, then the base word respect, then the suffixes -ful and -ly: dis + respect + ful + ly.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120309', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'medium', 1, 'Add the suffix -ful to ''beauty''. Write the new word.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"beautiful"'::jsonb, 'When a word ends in a consonant then y, change the y to i before adding the suffix: beauty becomes beautiful, like plenty becomes plentiful.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120310', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'hard', 1, 'Which sentence uses the correct homophones?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"They''re leaving their bags over there."},{"id":"b","text":"Their leaving there bags over they''re."},{"id":"c","text":"There leaving their bags over they''re."},{"id":"d","text":"They''re leaving there bags over their."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'They''re = they are, their = belonging to them, and there = a place. ''They are leaving their bags over there.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120311', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'hard', 1, '''Preferred'' doubles the r, but ''preference'' does not, because in ''preference'' the stress moves to the first syllable.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Say pre-FERRED (stress at the end, so double the r) and PREF-er-ence (stress at the start, so keep one r). The same happens with referred and reference.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120312', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'hard', 1, 'Which words are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"separate"},{"id":"b","text":"rhythm"},{"id":"c","text":"occassion"},{"id":"d","text":"conscious"},{"id":"e","text":"persistant"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Separate, rhythm and conscious are correct. The correct spellings of the others are ''occasion'' (one s) and ''persistent'' (-ent).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120313', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which is the correct Australian spelling?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"manoeuvre"},{"id":"b","text":"manouvre"},{"id":"c","text":"manuever"},{"id":"d","text":"manoevre"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Australian English uses the British spelling manoeuvre: m-a-n-o-e-u-v-r-e. The American spelling is maneuver.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120314', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Write the Australian spelling (practice or practise) that fits this sentence: ''The netball team will ___ every Tuesday after school.''', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"practise"'::jsonb, 'In Australian English, practice with a c is the noun (netball practice) and practise with an s is the verb. Here the team will do the action, so it is the verb: practise.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120315', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence is correct?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The criteria for the award are listed below."},{"id":"b","text":"The criteria for the award is listed below."},{"id":"c","text":"The criterias for the award are listed below."},{"id":"d","text":"The criterion for the award are listed below."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Criteria is the plural of criterion, so it needs the plural verb ''are''. ''Criterias'' is not a word, and one criterion would need ''is''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120316', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_easy', 1, 'You can remember how to spell ''separate'' because there is ''a rat'' in the middle: sep-a-rat-e.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Separate is often misspelt ''seperate''. Spotting ''a rat'' in the middle reminds you that the second vowel is an a.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120317', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''I would like a ___ of cake, please.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"piece"},{"id":"b","text":"peace"},{"id":"c","text":"peice"},{"id":"d","text":"pease"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A piece is a part of something: a piece of pie. Peace means calm or no war.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120318', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_easy', 1, 'Write the plural of ''baby''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"babies"'::jsonb, 'When a word ends in a consonant then y, change the y to i and add -es: baby becomes babies.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120319', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'easy', 1, 'Which word is spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"environment"},{"id":"b","text":"enviroment"},{"id":"c","text":"envirnment"},{"id":"d","text":"envronment"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Environment has ''iron'' hiding inside it: env-iron-ment. People often leave out the n before -ment.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120320', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'easy', 1, 'Which plurals are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"heroes"},{"id":"b","text":"pianos"},{"id":"c","text":"tomatos"},{"id":"d","text":"echoes"},{"id":"e","text":"radioes"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","d"]'::jsonb, 'Heroes and echoes add -es. Pianos and radios just add -s (musical words and words with a vowel before the o usually do). So ''tomatos'' should be tomatoes and ''radioes'' should be radios.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120321', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'easy', 1, 'Adding the suffix -ness to ''happy'' gives ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"happiness"'::jsonb, 'Change the y to i before adding the suffix, because happy ends in a consonant then y: happy becomes happiness.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120322', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'medium', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''The red scarf ___ her outfit perfectly.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"complements"},{"id":"b","text":"compliments"},{"id":"c","text":"complimints"},{"id":"d","text":"complaments"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Complement (with an e) means completes or goes well with. A compliment (with an i) is something nice you say. Tip: complEment completes.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120323', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'medium', 1, 'Add the suffix -ly to ''basic''. Write the new word.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"basically"'::jsonb, 'When a word ends in -ic, add -ally, not just -ly: basic becomes basically, like tragic becomes tragically. ''Publicly'' is the main exception.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120324', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'medium', 1, 'Put these word parts in order to build the word ''misunderstanding''.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"mis"},{"id":"b","text":"under"},{"id":"c","text":"stand"},{"id":"d","text":"ing"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'The prefix mis- comes first, then the compound base word under + stand, then the suffix -ing.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120325', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'hard', 1, 'Which word correctly fills the gap? ''The ___ of the story is that honesty matters.''', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"moral"},{"id":"b","text":"morale"},{"id":"c","text":"morall"},{"id":"d","text":"moralle"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A moral is the lesson of a story. Morale (with an e) means the confidence or spirit of a group: ''Team morale was high.''', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120326', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'hard', 1, 'When you add -able to ''manage'', you drop the e to make ''managable''.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'The correct spelling is manageable. The e stays so the g keeps its soft ''j'' sound, just like in noticeable, where the e keeps the c soft.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120327', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'hard', 1, 'Which words are spelt correctly? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"guarantee"},{"id":"b","text":"mischievious"},{"id":"c","text":"February"},{"id":"d","text":"embarassment"},{"id":"e","text":"questionnaire"}]'::jsonb, '["a","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'Guarantee, February and questionnaire are correct. The correct spellings of the others are ''mischievous'' (no i before -ous) and ''embarrassment'' (double r and double s).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120328', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Adding the suffix -ence to ''occur'' gives ___.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"occurrence"'::jsonb, 'Occur is stressed on the last syllable (oc-CUR) and ends in one vowel and one consonant, so double the r: occurrence. It keeps double c and double r.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120329', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_hard', 1, 'Which sentence has every word spelt correctly?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The government''s committee will receive the questionnaires on Wednesday."},{"id":"b","text":"The goverment''s committee will recieve the questionnaires on Wednesday."},{"id":"c","text":"The government''s commitee will receive the questionnaires on Wednesday."},{"id":"d","text":"The government''s committee will receive the questionaires on Wensday."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Watch for government (the n before -ment), receive (ei after c), committee (double m, double t, double e), questionnaire (double n) and Wednesday (silent d).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008120330', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081203', 'very_hard', 1, 'The Greek root ''psych'' means mind. Write the word for a person who studies the mind and behaviour (it begins with a silent p).', 'short_answer', NULL, '"psychologist"'::jsonb, 'psych (mind) + ology (study of) + ist (a person who) = psychologist. Remember the silent p and the ch that sounds like k.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130101', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_easy', 1, 'What is the main job of a topic sentence?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"to introduce the main idea of the paragraph"},{"id":"b","text":"to end the essay"},{"id":"c","text":"to list every piece of evidence"},{"id":"d","text":"to give the title of the text"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The topic sentence usually comes first in a paragraph and tells the reader what the paragraph is about. The other sentences support it.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130102', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_easy', 1, '''In addition'' is a connective used to add another point.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, '''In addition'', ''furthermore'' and ''moreover'' all signal that the writer is adding another point to the argument.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130103', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_easy', 1, 'A question asked for effect, where the writer does not expect an answer, is called a ___ question.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"rhetorical"'::jsonb, 'A rhetorical question like ''Who wants to breathe dirty air?'' makes the reader think the answer is obvious, which helps persuade them.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130104', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'easy', 1, 'Which of these are persuasive techniques? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"rhetorical question"},{"id":"b","text":"emotive language"},{"id":"c","text":"inclusive language (we, us, our)"},{"id":"d","text":"a table of contents"},{"id":"e","text":"a glossary"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Rhetorical questions, emotive language and inclusive language all try to win the reader over. A table of contents and a glossary help readers find information but do not persuade.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130105', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'easy', 1, 'Which is the strongest thesis statement for an essay about school uniforms?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"School uniforms should stay compulsory because they build school pride, reduce costs for families and keep students safe on excursions."},{"id":"b","text":"This essay is about school uniforms."},{"id":"c","text":"Some people like uniforms and some people don''t."},{"id":"d","text":"I am going to talk about uniforms and what people think."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'A strong thesis takes a clear position (uniforms should stay compulsory) and previews the reasons the essay will argue. The others only announce the topic.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130106', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'easy', 1, '''The bushfire season was severe. As a result, many families had to leave their homes.'' Write the connective that shows cause and effect.', 'short_answer', NULL, '["As a result","as a result,"]'::jsonb, '''As a result'' links the cause (a severe bushfire season) to its effect (families leaving their homes). Similar connectives are ''therefore'' and ''consequently''.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130107', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence is the thesis statement of this draft?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Our school should build a vegetable garden because it would teach valuable skills, improve our health and bring the whole community together."},{"id":"b","text":"It is time for a change."},{"id":"c","text":"Firstly, a garden is a living classroom."},{"id":"d","text":"Let''s dig in and start planting today!"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The thesis states the writer''s position and previews the three arguments. Each body paragraph then develops one of them: skills, health and community.', NULL, '{"title":"Draft: Our School Needs a Vegetable Garden","text":"Every day, students at Riverbank High throw away bags of uneaten, plastic-wrapped food. It is time for a change. Our school should build a vegetable garden because it would teach valuable skills, improve our health and bring the whole community together.\n\nFirstly, a garden is a living classroom. Students could measure garden beds in maths, study soil and insects in science and write about the changing seasons in English. Learning by doing is far more memorable than copying notes from a whiteboard.\n\nSecondly, fresh vegetables are good for us. Imagine biting into a crisp, sweet carrot you grew yourself. Its far tastier than anything sold in the canteen, and it costs almost nothing. Nutritionists agree that young people who eat more vegetables have more energy and concentrate better in class.\n\nFinally, a garden would bring people together. Grandparents could share their gardening knowledge, local businesses could donate tools, and students from every year group could work side by side.\n\nSome people argue that a garden would take too much time to look after. However, a roster of student volunteers, with each class caring for the garden for one week a term, would share the load fairly.\n\nSurely we all want a healthier, greener and more connected school. Let''s dig in and start planting today!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130108', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'medium', 1, 'Which technique is used in ''Imagine biting into a crisp, sweet carrot you grew yourself''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"sensory imagery that invites readers to picture the experience"},{"id":"b","text":"statistics that prove the point"},{"id":"c","text":"a rebuttal of the other side"},{"id":"d","text":"a rhetorical question"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Crisp'' and ''sweet'' appeal to touch and taste, and ''Imagine'' asks readers to picture themselves enjoying it, which makes the idea appealing.', NULL, '{"title":"Draft: Our School Needs a Vegetable Garden","text":"Every day, students at Riverbank High throw away bags of uneaten, plastic-wrapped food. It is time for a change. Our school should build a vegetable garden because it would teach valuable skills, improve our health and bring the whole community together.\n\nFirstly, a garden is a living classroom. Students could measure garden beds in maths, study soil and insects in science and write about the changing seasons in English. Learning by doing is far more memorable than copying notes from a whiteboard.\n\nSecondly, fresh vegetables are good for us. Imagine biting into a crisp, sweet carrot you grew yourself. Its far tastier than anything sold in the canteen, and it costs almost nothing. Nutritionists agree that young people who eat more vegetables have more energy and concentrate better in class.\n\nFinally, a garden would bring people together. Grandparents could share their gardening knowledge, local businesses could donate tools, and students from every year group could work side by side.\n\nSome people argue that a garden would take too much time to look after. However, a roster of student volunteers, with each class caring for the garden for one week a term, would share the load fairly.\n\nSurely we all want a healthier, greener and more connected school. Let''s dig in and start planting today!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130109', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'medium', 1, 'Put these sentences in order to make a well-structured paragraph.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"Regular exercise improves students'' concentration."},{"id":"b","text":"Students who exercise before class often find it easier to focus on their work."},{"id":"c","text":"This is because exercise increases blood flow to the brain."},{"id":"d","text":"Therefore, schools should timetable sport in the morning."}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'A strong paragraph moves from topic sentence, to evidence, to explanation (''This is because''), to a linking sentence that ties back to the argument (''Therefore'').', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130110', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the strongest revision of ''The storm was very bad and it was really scary.''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The storm raged through the night, its howling winds tearing tiles from rooftops."},{"id":"b","text":"The storm was extremely bad and very, very scary."},{"id":"c","text":"The storm was bad, it was scary."},{"id":"d","text":"It was a really bad storm and it was scary and bad."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Strong writing shows rather than tells. Precise verbs (raged, tearing) and vivid detail (howling winds, tiles from rooftops) replace vague words like ''very bad'' and ''scary''. The third option is also a comma splice.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130111', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'hard', 1, 'Which correction fixes the error in paragraph 3?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Change ''Its'' to ''It''s''."},{"id":"b","text":"Change ''tastier'' to ''more tastier''."},{"id":"c","text":"Change ''Nutritionists'' to ''Nutritionist''s''."},{"id":"d","text":"Change ''costs'' to ''cost''."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''Its far tastier'' means ''It is far tastier'', so it needs the contraction it''s. ''Its'' without an apostrophe means belonging to it.', NULL, '{"title":"Draft: Our School Needs a Vegetable Garden","text":"Every day, students at Riverbank High throw away bags of uneaten, plastic-wrapped food. It is time for a change. Our school should build a vegetable garden because it would teach valuable skills, improve our health and bring the whole community together.\n\nFirstly, a garden is a living classroom. Students could measure garden beds in maths, study soil and insects in science and write about the changing seasons in English. Learning by doing is far more memorable than copying notes from a whiteboard.\n\nSecondly, fresh vegetables are good for us. Imagine biting into a crisp, sweet carrot you grew yourself. Its far tastier than anything sold in the canteen, and it costs almost nothing. Nutritionists agree that young people who eat more vegetables have more energy and concentrate better in class.\n\nFinally, a garden would bring people together. Grandparents could share their gardening knowledge, local businesses could donate tools, and students from every year group could work side by side.\n\nSome people argue that a garden would take too much time to look after. However, a roster of student volunteers, with each class caring for the garden for one week a term, would share the load fairly.\n\nSurely we all want a healthier, greener and more connected school. Let''s dig in and start planting today!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130112', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'hard', 1, 'An editor is checking a report. Which sentences contain a grammar error? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"The team''s coach praised their effort."},{"id":"b","text":"Between you and I, the test was easy."},{"id":"c","text":"Neither of the boys have finished the task."},{"id":"d","text":"Each of the students has a laptop."},{"id":"e","text":"There''s lots of reasons to recycle."}]'::jsonb, '["b","c","e"]'::jsonb, 'After ''between'' we need ''me'': between you and me. ''Neither'' is singular, so ''neither of the boys has''. ''Reasons'' is plural, so ''there are lots of reasons''. The other two are correct.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130113', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Why does the writer include paragraph 5, which begins ''Some people argue...''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"To acknowledge an opposing view and rebut it, which makes the argument more convincing."},{"id":"b","text":"To admit that the garden is a bad idea."},{"id":"c","text":"To add a new reason why vegetables are healthy."},{"id":"d","text":"To give the conclusion before the end."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'This is a rebuttal. The writer raises a likely objection (the garden takes too much time) and answers it with a solution (a class roster), showing they have thought about both sides.', NULL, '{"title":"Draft: Our School Needs a Vegetable Garden","text":"Every day, students at Riverbank High throw away bags of uneaten, plastic-wrapped food. It is time for a change. Our school should build a vegetable garden because it would teach valuable skills, improve our health and bring the whole community together.\n\nFirstly, a garden is a living classroom. Students could measure garden beds in maths, study soil and insects in science and write about the changing seasons in English. Learning by doing is far more memorable than copying notes from a whiteboard.\n\nSecondly, fresh vegetables are good for us. Imagine biting into a crisp, sweet carrot you grew yourself. Its far tastier than anything sold in the canteen, and it costs almost nothing. Nutritionists agree that young people who eat more vegetables have more energy and concentrate better in class.\n\nFinally, a garden would bring people together. Grandparents could share their gardening knowledge, local businesses could donate tools, and students from every year group could work side by side.\n\nSome people argue that a garden would take too much time to look after. However, a roster of student volunteers, with each class caring for the garden for one week a term, would share the load fairly.\n\nSurely we all want a healthier, greener and more connected school. Let''s dig in and start planting today!"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130114', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_hard', 1, 'A speech ends: ''We will plant, we will grow, we will share.'' Which rhetorical devices does it use? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"repetition"},{"id":"b","text":"the rule of three"},{"id":"c","text":"simile"},{"id":"d","text":"hyperbole"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b"]'::jsonb, '''We will'' is repeated at the start of each part, and there are three parts, which makes the line rhythmic and memorable. There is no comparison (simile) or exaggeration (hyperbole).', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130115', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Type a one-word connective that shows a result to fill the gap: ''Many students arrive at school without breakfast. ___, a school garden could give them fresh food.''', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["Therefore","Consequently","Thus","Hence"]'::jsonb, 'The second sentence is a result of the first, so it needs a cause-and-effect connective such as ''Therefore'' or ''Consequently''. ''However'' would wrongly signal a contrast.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130116', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_easy', 1, 'A writer usually starts a new paragraph when moving to a new idea, time, place or speaker.', 'true_false', NULL, '"true"'::jsonb, 'Paragraphs group related sentences. Starting a new one signals a change, which helps the reader follow the text.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130117', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_easy', 1, 'Which connective shows contrast?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"however"},{"id":"b","text":"furthermore"},{"id":"c","text":"therefore"},{"id":"d","text":"firstly"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''However'' signals that the next idea is different from or opposite to the last one. Furthermore adds, therefore shows a result and firstly shows order.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130118', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_easy', 1, 'The sentence that tells the reader the main idea of a paragraph is called the ___ sentence.', 'fill_blank', NULL, '"topic"'::jsonb, 'The topic sentence states the paragraph''s main idea, usually at the very start.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130119', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'easy', 1, 'Which sentence uses emotive language?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"Helpless puppies are being abandoned on our streets every single day."},{"id":"b","text":"Some dogs live in animal shelters."},{"id":"c","text":"The shelter opens at 9 am."},{"id":"d","text":"Dogs need food, water and exercise."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'Words like ''helpless'' and ''abandoned'' are chosen to make readers feel sad or angry. The other sentences state facts without trying to stir feelings.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130120', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'easy', 1, 'Put these parts of a persuasive essay in order.', 'ordering', '[{"id":"a","text":"a hook to grab attention"},{"id":"b","text":"the thesis statement"},{"id":"c","text":"body paragraphs with arguments and evidence"},{"id":"d","text":"a conclusion that restates the thesis"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c","d"]'::jsonb, 'An introduction opens with a hook, then states the thesis. Body paragraphs develop the arguments, and the conclusion sums up and restates the position.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130121', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'easy', 1, 'What is the name of the persuasive technique where a writer uses ''we'', ''us'' and ''our'' to make readers feel part of a group?', 'short_answer', NULL, '["inclusive language","inclusive"]'::jsonb, 'Inclusive language puts the writer and reader on the same side: ''We all want a safer town.'' It makes readers more likely to agree.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130122', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'medium', 1, 'Which sentence states the writer''s main contention (thesis)?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"I am writing to urge Wattle Creek Council to reverse its plan to close our public pool."},{"id":"b","text":"Every summer, more than 400 local children learn to swim there."},{"id":"c","text":"The council claims that repairs would cost too much."},{"id":"d","text":"Together, we can save our pool."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The main contention is the writer''s overall position, stated in the first paragraph: the council should not close the pool. The other sentences are evidence, a counterargument or a rallying call.', NULL, '{"title":"Letter to the Editor: Save Our Pool","text":"To the Editor,\n\nI am writing to urge Wattle Creek Council to reverse its plan to close our public pool. The pool is far more than a place to cool off; it is a lifeline for our town.\n\nFirst, the pool keeps children safe. Every summer, more than 400 local children learn to swim there. Without lessons, these children are more likely to get into trouble in rivers, dams and at the beach. Can we really put a price on a child''s life?\n\nIn addition, the pool is a meeting place for the whole community. Older residents attend aqua-aerobics classes, teenagers has somewhere to go in the school holidays and families gather on the lawns for birthday parties. Closing it would leave a hole in the heart of our town.\n\nThe council claims that repairs would cost too much. However, a recent survey found that 87 per cent of residents would happily pay a small yearly fee to keep the pool open. Surely the council should listen to the people who elected it.\n\nOur parents swam in this pool, and our children deserve the same chance. I urge every reader to sign the petition at the library and to attend next Tuesday''s council meeting. Together, we can save our pool.\n\nYours sincerely,\nEllie Nguyen, Year 8"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130123', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'medium', 1, '''Can we really put a price on a child''s life?'' Which persuasive technique is this?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"rhetorical question"},{"id":"b","text":"statistic"},{"id":"c","text":"rebuttal"},{"id":"d","text":"alliteration"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The writer does not expect an answer; the obvious answer is ''no''. This rhetorical question also uses emotive language about children''s safety.', NULL, '{"title":"Letter to the Editor: Save Our Pool","text":"To the Editor,\n\nI am writing to urge Wattle Creek Council to reverse its plan to close our public pool. The pool is far more than a place to cool off; it is a lifeline for our town.\n\nFirst, the pool keeps children safe. Every summer, more than 400 local children learn to swim there. Without lessons, these children are more likely to get into trouble in rivers, dams and at the beach. Can we really put a price on a child''s life?\n\nIn addition, the pool is a meeting place for the whole community. Older residents attend aqua-aerobics classes, teenagers has somewhere to go in the school holidays and families gather on the lawns for birthday parties. Closing it would leave a hole in the heart of our town.\n\nThe council claims that repairs would cost too much. However, a recent survey found that 87 per cent of residents would happily pay a small yearly fee to keep the pool open. Surely the council should listen to the people who elected it.\n\nOur parents swam in this pool, and our children deserve the same chance. I urge every reader to sign the petition at the library and to attend next Tuesday''s council meeting. Together, we can save our pool.\n\nYours sincerely,\nEllie Nguyen, Year 8"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130124', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'medium', 1, 'Which connectives add information? Choose all that are right.', 'multi_select', '[{"id":"a","text":"furthermore"},{"id":"b","text":"moreover"},{"id":"c","text":"in addition"},{"id":"d","text":"nevertheless"},{"id":"e","text":"consequently"}]'::jsonb, '["a","b","c"]'::jsonb, 'Furthermore, moreover and in addition add another point. Nevertheless shows contrast and consequently shows a result.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130125', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'hard', 1, 'Which is the strongest, most concise revision of ''In my personal opinion, I think that the canteen should sell healthier food options.''?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"The canteen should sell healthier food."},{"id":"b","text":"In my opinion, I think the canteen should sell healthier food options."},{"id":"c","text":"I personally think, in my opinion, that the canteen should sell healthier food."},{"id":"d","text":"Healthier food options, in my personal opinion, I think the canteen should sell."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, '''In my personal opinion'' and ''I think'' say the same thing twice, and both weaken the claim. Stating the point directly is clearer and more persuasive.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130126', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'hard', 1, 'Paragraph 3 has a subject–verb agreement error. Type the word that should be changed to ''have''.', 'short_answer', NULL, '"has"'::jsonb, '''Teenagers'' is plural, so it needs the plural verb: teenagers have somewhere to go. ''Has'' goes with a singular subject, such as ''a teenager has''.', NULL, '{"title":"Letter to the Editor: Save Our Pool","text":"To the Editor,\n\nI am writing to urge Wattle Creek Council to reverse its plan to close our public pool. The pool is far more than a place to cool off; it is a lifeline for our town.\n\nFirst, the pool keeps children safe. Every summer, more than 400 local children learn to swim there. Without lessons, these children are more likely to get into trouble in rivers, dams and at the beach. Can we really put a price on a child''s life?\n\nIn addition, the pool is a meeting place for the whole community. Older residents attend aqua-aerobics classes, teenagers has somewhere to go in the school holidays and families gather on the lawns for birthday parties. Closing it would leave a hole in the heart of our town.\n\nThe council claims that repairs would cost too much. However, a recent survey found that 87 per cent of residents would happily pay a small yearly fee to keep the pool open. Surely the council should listen to the people who elected it.\n\nOur parents swam in this pool, and our children deserve the same chance. I urge every reader to sign the petition at the library and to attend next Tuesday''s council meeting. Together, we can save our pool.\n\nYours sincerely,\nEllie Nguyen, Year 8"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130127', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'hard', 1, '''Furthermore'' and ''however'' can be swapped in a paragraph without changing the meaning, because both are connectives.', 'true_false', NULL, '"false"'::jsonb, 'Connectives have different jobs. ''Furthermore'' adds a similar point, but ''however'' signals a contrast. Swapping them would confuse the reader about how the ideas link.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130128', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_hard', 1, 'How does the writer rebut the council''s argument in paragraph 4?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"With survey evidence that most residents would pay a fee to keep the pool open, so cost need not close it."},{"id":"b","text":"By agreeing that repairs cost too much and the pool should close."},{"id":"c","text":"By insulting the council members."},{"id":"d","text":"By changing the subject to birthday parties."}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'The writer states the council''s claim, then uses ''However'' and a statistic (87 per cent of residents would pay) to show there is a way to cover the cost. That is a rebuttal backed by evidence.', NULL, '{"title":"Letter to the Editor: Save Our Pool","text":"To the Editor,\n\nI am writing to urge Wattle Creek Council to reverse its plan to close our public pool. The pool is far more than a place to cool off; it is a lifeline for our town.\n\nFirst, the pool keeps children safe. Every summer, more than 400 local children learn to swim there. Without lessons, these children are more likely to get into trouble in rivers, dams and at the beach. Can we really put a price on a child''s life?\n\nIn addition, the pool is a meeting place for the whole community. Older residents attend aqua-aerobics classes, teenagers has somewhere to go in the school holidays and families gather on the lawns for birthday parties. Closing it would leave a hole in the heart of our town.\n\nThe council claims that repairs would cost too much. However, a recent survey found that 87 per cent of residents would happily pay a small yearly fee to keep the pool open. Surely the council should listen to the people who elected it.\n\nOur parents swam in this pool, and our children deserve the same chance. I urge every reader to sign the petition at the library and to attend next Tuesday''s council meeting. Together, we can save our pool.\n\nYours sincerely,\nEllie Nguyen, Year 8"}'::jsonb, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130129', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_hard', 1, '''Our parks are the lungs of our city.'' Which device is this, and what is its effect?', 'multiple_choice', '[{"id":"a","text":"a metaphor that suggests parks are as essential to the city as lungs are to a body"},{"id":"b","text":"a simile that compares parks to trees using ''like''"},{"id":"c","text":"personification that makes parks seem angry"},{"id":"d","text":"alliteration that makes the sentence sound musical"}]'::jsonb, '"a"'::jsonb, 'It says parks ARE lungs, without ''like'' or ''as'', so it is a metaphor. Lungs keep a body alive by bringing in clean air, so the image suggests parks are vital to a city''s health.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001'),
  ('61000000-0000-0000-0000-000008130130', '51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000081301', 'very_hard', 1, 'Type a one-word connective that shows contrast to fill the gap: ''Mobile phones can distract students. ___, they can be useful research tools when teachers set clear rules.''', 'fill_blank', NULL, '["However","Nevertheless","Nonetheless","Yet","Still","Conversely"]'::jsonb, 'The second sentence gives the other side of the argument, so it needs a contrast connective such as ''However'' or ''Nevertheless''. ''Therefore'' would wrongly signal a result.', NULL, NULL, '90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001')
